var e=[];e.push({id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification`,title:`Classification and diagnosis of diabetes: type 1, LADA, MODY, ketosis-prone and secondary diabetes`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Diabetes mellitus** is chronic hyperglycaemia diagnosed by **FPG 126 mg/dL or more, 2-hour OGTT glucose 200 mg/dL or more, HbA1c 6.5% or more** (two abnormal results unless symptomatic) or **random glucose 200 mg/dL or more with symptoms**, and classified by aetiology into **type 1 (autoimmune), type 2, specific types (MODY, LADA-spectrum, pancreatic, drug-induced) and gestational diabetes** using **islet autoantibodies and C-peptide** when the phenotype is unclear.`,keywords:[`diabetes classification`,`ADA diagnostic criteria`,`HbA1c pitfalls`,`OGTT`,`LADA`,`latent autoimmune diabetes in adults`,`MODY`,`HNF1A`,`GCK`,`ketosis-prone diabetes`,`Flatbush diabetes`,`GAD65 antibody`,`IA-2`,`ZnT8`,`C-peptide`,`type 5 diabetes`,`malnutrition-related diabetes`,`secondary diabetes`,`neonatal diabetes`,`fulminant type 1 diabetes`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Diabetes mellitus** is a group of metabolic disorders sharing **chronic hyperglycaemia** from defects in insulin secretion, insulin action or both; the diagnostic cut-offs are the glucose levels above which **retinopathy prevalence rises steeply**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Globally about **589 million adults (20-79 years)** live with diabetes, projected to **853 million by 2050**, and about 4 in 10 are undiagnosed. [IDF Atlas 2025]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB** (2023) found diabetes in **11.4%** (about **101 million**) and prediabetes in **15.3%** (about 136 million) of Indian adults, with the highest prevalence in Goa, Puducherry and Kerala. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`India has the **largest number of children and adolescents with type 1 diabetes** in the world, yet type 1 is still only about **5-10%** of all diabetes; **type 2 is over 90%**. [IDF Atlas 2025]`,`Asian Indians develop type 2 diabetes about **a decade earlier** and at a **lower BMI** than white Europeans - the **thin-fat phenotype** (more visceral fat, less muscle, smaller beta-cell reserve). [RSSDI 2022]`,`**MODY** accounts for roughly **1-5% of diabetes** in young people, and over 80% of cases are misdiagnosed as type 1 or type 2. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**LADA** is the commonest form of autoimmune diabetes in adults - about **2-12%** of adults labelled type 2 carry GAD antibodies, depending on the population. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology: the ADA classification and risk factors`,points:[`**Type 1 diabetes**: autoimmune (type 1A) or idiopathic (type 1B) **beta-cell destruction** leading to absolute insulin deficiency; includes LADA at the slow end of the spectrum. [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 2 diabetes**: progressive loss of adequate beta-cell secretion on a background of **insulin resistance**, usually with obesity, family history and sedentary life. [ADA 2026]`,`**Specific types**: monogenic diabetes (MODY, neonatal diabetes), diseases of the **exocrine pancreas** (FCPD, pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis, haemochromatosis, pancreatic cancer), **endocrinopathies** (Cushing, acromegaly, phaeochromocytoma, glucagonoma) and **drug- or chemical-induced** diabetes. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gestational diabetes**: diabetes first diagnosed in the 2nd or 3rd trimester that was not clearly overt before pregnancy (see the obstetrics GDM topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Drugs that cause hyperglycaemia**: glucocorticoids, thiazides, beta-blockers, **atypical antipsychotics** (olanzapine, clozapine), **calcineurin inhibitors** (tacrolimus), protease inhibitors, statins (small effect), niacin and **immune checkpoint inhibitors** (anti-PD-1 can cause fulminant autoimmune diabetes). [ADA 2026]`,`**Post-transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM)** follows tacrolimus and steroids and is diagnosed only once the patient is stable on maintenance immunosuppression. [ADA 2026]`,`In April 2025 the **IDF recognised malnutrition-related diabetes as "type 5 diabetes"**: lean young adults in low-income settings with early-life undernutrition, marked insulin-secretory deficiency but **no ketosis-proneness and no autoimmunity**. [IDF 2025]`,`Genetic syndromes with diabetes: **Down, Turner, Klinefelter, Prader-Willi**, **Wolfram (DIDMOAD)**, myotonic dystrophy and mitochondrial diabetes (**MIDD, m.3243A>G** with deafness). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why the types behave differently`,points:[`**Type 1**: T-cell mediated insulitis destroys beta cells; autoantibodies (GAD65, IA-2, ZnT8, insulin) are **markers, not causes**, and appear months to years before glucose rises. [Harrison 22e]`,`Symptoms appear when about **70-80% of beta-cell mass** is lost; residual secretion explains the **honeymoon phase** after insulin is started in type 1. [Harrison 22e]`,`Without insulin, lipolysis is unrestrained and free fatty acids go to the liver, where **glucagon-driven ketogenesis** produces beta-hydroxybutyrate - so ketosis marks **absolute insulin deficiency**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LADA** shares autoimmunity with type 1 but has **slower destruction** (often a single GAD antibody, lower titre), with features of insulin resistance - hence the hybrid phenotype. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`,`**MODY** is autosomal dominant **beta-cell dysfunction**: GCK mutations reset the **glucose sensor** to a higher set-point (mild stable hyperglycaemia), while HNF1A/HNF4A mutations cause progressive secretory failure that bypasses the K-ATP channel - hence **exquisite sulfonylurea sensitivity**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Ketosis-prone (A-beta+) diabetes**: acute **glucotoxicity and lipotoxicity** transiently stun beta cells, causing DKA; once glucose is controlled, secretion recovers and many come off insulin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes** and other pancreatogenic diabetes lose both insulin and **glucagon**, so ketosis is rare but **hypoglycaemia on insulin is brittle** (see the FCPD topic). [RSSDI 2022]`,`**HbA1c** forms by non-enzymatic glycation of the haemoglobin beta-chain N-terminal valine over the **120-day red-cell life**, so anything that changes red-cell age or haemoglobin structure distorts it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria (the table examiners ask)`,points:[`Diabetes: **FPG 126 mg/dL or more** (8 hours fasting), **2-hour plasma glucose 200 mg/dL or more** on a 75 g OGTT, **HbA1c 6.5% or more** (NGSP-certified, DCCT-standardised assay), or **random glucose 200 mg/dL or more with classic symptoms or crisis**. [ADA 2026]`,`Without unequivocal hyperglycaemia, diagnosis needs **two abnormal results** - two different tests on the **same sample** or the same test on **two occasions**. [ADA 2026]`,`If two different tests disagree, **repeat the test that was abnormal**; a value near the cut-off should be re-tested in 3-6 months. [ADA 2026]`,`**Prediabetes**: IFG **100-125 mg/dL** (WHO 110-125), IGT **140-199 mg/dL** at 2 hours, or HbA1c **5.7-6.4%** (see the Prediabetes topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Staging of type 1**: **stage 1** two or more autoantibodies with normoglycaemia; **stage 2** two or more antibodies with dysglycaemia; **stage 3** clinical diabetes - stages 1-2 are "presymptomatic type 1". [ADA 2026]`,`The IDF (2024) proposes a **1-hour post-load glucose of 155 mg/dL or more** for intermediate hyperglycaemia and **209 mg/dL or more** for diabetes; ADA has **not adopted** it. [IDF 2024]`,`**Ketosis-prone diabetes** is classified by the **A-beta system**: A+ or A- (autoantibody present or absent) and beta+ or beta- (C-peptide preserved or absent); **A-beta+** is the commonest and most likely to stop insulin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**LADA** criteria (Immunology of Diabetes Society): age **over 30**, positive islet antibody, and **no insulin needed for at least 6 months** after diagnosis. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history in the OPD`,points:[`**Osmotic symptoms**: polyuria, nocturia, thirst and weight loss appear once glucose exceeds the **renal threshold of about 180 mg/dL**; rapid loss over weeks suggests **insulin deficiency**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 1 / LADA clues**: young or lean, weight loss, **ketosis or DKA**, personal or family **autoimmunity** (thyroid, coeliac, vitiligo, Addison), and failure of oral drugs within months. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**MODY clues**: diabetes before **25-35 years** in two or three generations, non-obese, **no ketosis**, small insulin needs with **detectable C-peptide 3-5 years** after diagnosis, antibody-negative. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Ketosis-prone clue**: an overweight middle-aged man with **DKA as the first presentation**, often a strong family history of type 2 and no precipitant. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Secondary clues**: abdominal pain, steatorrhoea and alcohol (pancreatic); recent steroid, antipsychotic or checkpoint-inhibitor use; new diabetes **over 50 with weight loss and no family history** (pancreatic cancer). [ADA 2026]`,`**Neonatal diabetes**: onset **before 6 months** of age is monogenic until proven otherwise - test genes, not antibodies. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Many with type 2 are **asymptomatic** and found on screening or with a complication (infection, TB, foot ulcer, MI, blurred vision). [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**BMI and waist** with Asian cut-offs (overweight 23, obese 25; waist 90 cm men, 80 cm women): lean build favours type 1, LADA, FCPD, type 5 or MODY. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** and skin tags mark insulin resistance - against type 1 and MODY, for type 2 or PCOS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kussmaul breathing, dehydration and ketotic breath** at presentation mean insulin deficiency until proven otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitiligo, goitre, premature greying** suggest polyglandular autoimmunity and support type 1 or LADA. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Cushingoid habitus, acromegalic features, hyperpigmentation with hepatomegaly** (haemochromatosis) and **lipodystrophy** point to secondary diabetes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Sensorineural deafness** with maternal inheritance suggests **MIDD**; **optic atrophy** with diabetes insipidus suggests **Wolfram syndrome**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Parotid enlargement and a thin, malnourished young person** suggests FCPD or type 5 diabetes. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of the type`,points:[`**Type 1 vs type 2 in adults**: features favouring type 1 are age **under 35**, BMI **under 25**, unintentional weight loss, ketoacidosis and **glucose over 360 mg/dL** at presentation. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**LADA vs type 2**: LADA is leaner, has personal or family autoimmunity, fails oral drugs early and is **GAD-positive**. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`,`**MODY vs type 1**: MODY is **antibody-negative** with **persistent C-peptide** beyond the honeymoon; a child with type 1 has **at least one antibody in over 90%** at onset. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**MODY vs young type 2**: MODY patients are usually non-obese without acanthosis; **hsCRP is low in HNF1A-MODY**; a dominant three-generation history favours MODY. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Ketosis-prone vs type 1**: the ketosis-prone patient is overweight, antibody-negative and has **C-peptide that recovers** after the DKA settles. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**FCPD vs type 1**: FCPD has **pancreatic calculi on X-ray or ultrasound**, steatorrhoea, ketosis resistance and high insulin needs (see the FCPD topic). [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Stress hyperglycaemia** (sepsis, MI) and **steroid-induced** hyperglycaemia mimic new diabetes - an **HbA1c 6.5% or more** at admission says the diabetes predates the illness. [ADA 2026]`,`**Renal glycosuria** (and SGLT2 inhibitor use) gives glycosuria with **normal blood glucose** - never diagnose diabetes from urine. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line and their pitfalls`,points:[`Use **venous plasma glucose** in a **fluoride-oxalate tube**; glycolysis lowers glucose by about **5-7% per hour** if the sample sits unspun, so centrifuge within 30 minutes or put it on ice. [ADA 2026]`,`**Capillary glucometer readings are for screening and monitoring only**, never for diagnosis. [ICMR 2018]`,`**OGTT method**: 3 days of normal diet (150 g or more carbohydrate a day), **8-14 hour** overnight fast, **75 g anhydrous glucose** (82.5 g monohydrate) in 250-300 mL water over 5 minutes, seated and not smoking, sample at 2 hours. [WHO 2006]`,`**HbA1c falsely high**: **iron deficiency anaemia**, B12 or folate deficiency, asplenia, uraemia (carbamylation) and some assays with HbF. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c falsely low**: haemolysis, **recent blood loss or transfusion**, erythropoietin or iron therapy, **pregnancy (2nd-3rd trimester)**, dialysis, **G6PD deficiency**, HIV on NRTIs and dapsone. [ADA 2026]`,`**Haemoglobinopathies** common in India (**beta-thalassaemia trait, HbE in the North-East, HbS in central and tribal India, HbD-Punjab**) interfere depending on assay - an HPLC chromatogram flags the variant; then rely on **glucose criteria or fructosamine**. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**NFHS-5** found anaemia in **57% of women aged 15-49** - check haemoglobin before trusting an HbA1c in an Indian woman. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`**HbA1c is not used to diagnose** type 1 diabetes in symptomatic children, GDM, cystic fibrosis-related diabetes, early post-transplant diabetes or acute-onset hyperglycaemia - use plasma glucose. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations: antibodies, C-peptide and genetics`,points:[`**Islet autoantibodies**: test **GAD65 first** (most sensitive in adults), then **IA-2 and ZnT8**; **insulin autoantibodies** are valid only before or within about 10 days of starting insulin. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`Two or more antibodies give a near-certain diagnosis of type 1; a single low-titre GAD in an older obese adult has **lower positive predictive value**. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**C-peptide** (secreted 1:1 with insulin, not on assays of injected insulin) measures endogenous secretion: random **under 200 pmol/L (0.6 ng/mL) = type 1 pattern**, **200-600 pmol/L intermediate**, **over 600 pmol/L (1.8 ng/mL) = type 2 pattern**. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`Measure C-peptide with a **paired glucose over 144 mg/dL** (8 mmol/L), and ideally **3 or more years after diagnosis** - in the first years residual secretion can mislead. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`C-peptide is falsely high in **CKD** (renally cleared) and falsely low during **hypoglycaemia or immediately after DKA**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Genetic testing for MODY**: next-generation sequencing panel (GCK, HNF1A, HNF4A, HNF1B and others) when the **Exeter MODY probability calculator** is high, or antibody-negative with preserved C-peptide; available at tertiary centres in India. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Imaging**: plain X-ray or ultrasound abdomen for **pancreatic calculi** (FCPD); CT pancreas for new-onset diabetes over 50 with weight loss. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Baseline at diagnosis** in every type: creatinine-eGFR, **urine ACR**, lipids, ALT, TSH (type 1), coeliac serology (type 1), fundus and foot examination (see the Complication screening topic). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Management by type in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Type 1 / stage 3**: start **basal-bolus insulin** immediately at about **0.4-0.5 units/kg/day** in adults (half basal, half split across meals) - never trial oral drugs (see the Type 1 management topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**LADA**: use **C-peptide to choose** - under 0.3 nmol/L give insulin like type 1; 0.3-0.7 nmol/L insulin plus agents that do not exhaust beta cells (metformin, DPP-4i, SGLT2i, GLP-1 RA); over 0.7 nmol/L follow the type 2 algorithm and **avoid sulfonylureas**. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`,`**HNF1A and HNF4A MODY**: **low-dose sulfonylurea** - gliclazide **20-40 mg/day** (about a quarter of the usual starting dose) - often controls glucose for decades; watch for hypoglycaemia. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**GCK-MODY**: **no treatment** - HbA1c stays about **5.6-7.6%**, complications are rare, and drugs barely change glucose; stop unnecessary insulin. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**HNF1B-MODY (renal cysts and diabetes)** usually needs **insulin** and nephrology follow-up for cysts, hypomagnesaemia and genital tract anomalies. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Neonatal diabetes with KCNJ11/ABCC8 mutations**: switch from insulin to **oral glibenclamide** (start 0.2 mg/kg/day in two doses, titrate; most need about 0.5 mg/kg/day) at a specialist centre. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Ketosis-prone (A-beta+)**: insulin until glucose is controlled, then **taper insulin over weeks while monitoring**, often onto metformin; relapse of DKA occurs, so teach ketone checks. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Drug-induced**: review the need for the culprit, switch antipsychotic (olanzapine to aripiprazole) where psychiatry agrees, and treat glucose by the type 2 algorithm or insulin (see the Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Pancreatogenic diabetes**: insulin (metformin if mild), **pancreatic enzyme replacement** 40,000-50,000 lipase units with each meal, and vitamin D and calcium (see the FCPD topic). [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit any new diabetes with **ketonuria 2+ or more, blood ketone 3 mmol/L or more, vomiting, drowsiness or acidosis** - DKA can be the first presentation of type 1, LADA, ketosis-prone diabetes or checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes (see the DKA and HHS topic). [ADA 2024 hyperglycaemic crises consensus]`,`Send **islet antibodies before discharge**, but **C-peptide after recovery** (2-3 weeks or more) - C-peptide during DKA is falsely low. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**HbA1c on admission** separates pre-existing diabetes (6.5% or more) from **stress hyperglycaemia** in sepsis, MI or stroke. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fulminant type 1 diabetes** (East Asian, also reported from India): DKA within a week of symptoms with **HbA1c under 8.7%**, raised pancreatic enzymes and absent C-peptide - needs ICU-level care. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes** (anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1) is abrupt and permanent - check glucose before each cycle and treat with insulin, not steroids. [ADA 2026]`,`Every newly diagnosed insulin-requiring patient must leave with **insulin technique, glucose meter, hypoglycaemia plan and sick-day rules** taught. [ISPAD 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: overt diabetes in pregnancy is FPG **126 or more**, HbA1c **6.5% or more** or random **200 or more** at booking; **GCK-MODY** in a mother needs treatment only if the fetus has not inherited it (judged by **fetal growth**). [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Children**: type 1 dominates; consider **MODY** if antibody-negative, and **monogenic** if onset before 6 months; obesity has made **youth-onset type 2** common in urban India. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Elderly**: new diabetes with weight loss needs **pancreatic cancer** exclusion; HbA1c may read higher with age and anaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD**: HbA1c is unreliable in **stage 4-5 and dialysis** (ESA, transfusion, shortened red-cell life) - use glucose or CGM; C-peptide is falsely high. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Haemoglobinopathy or thalassaemia major**: transfusion makes HbA1c meaningless; iron overload causes **secondary diabetes** - use OGTT and fructosamine. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**HIV**: protease inhibitors and older NRTIs cause insulin resistance and **lower HbA1c** - diagnose on glucose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: venous FPG in a fluoride tube plus a clinical phenotype check (age, BMI, ketones) classifies most patients without antibodies. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications of misclassification and prognosis`,points:[`Labelling type 1 or LADA as type 2 leads to **DKA** and prolonged hyperglycaemia on oral drugs - LADA patients become insulin-requiring within **about 5 years** on average. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`,`Labelling MODY as type 1 condemns the patient to **unnecessary insulin injections**, and missing it misses **family screening** (50% risk to each child). [ISPAD 2022]`,`**HNF1A-MODY** carries the same microvascular and **cardiovascular risk** as type 1 and type 2 if poorly controlled; GCK-MODY has very low complication risk. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Type 1 diabetes shortens life expectancy by about **10-12 years** in high-income countries, more in India where insulin access is uneven. [Harrison 22e]`,`Undiagnosed type 2 diabetes runs for **4-7 years** before diagnosis, so complications are often present at diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ketosis-prone diabetes** relapses into DKA in a substantial proportion over the next years, especially if C-peptide falls. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, screening and the national programme`,points:[`Refer to an endocrinologist: **suspected MODY, LADA, neonatal, ketosis-prone, fulminant or pancreatogenic diabetes**, and any young patient where the type is unclear. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Reclassify** at follow-up: re-test C-peptide in LADA or unclear cases every **1-2 years** or when control slips. [Diabetes LADA expert panel 2020]`,`Screen **asymptomatic adults** from age **35** (ADA) or **30** (NP-NCD India), and earlier if overweight (BMI 23 or more in Asians) with a risk factor; repeat at least every **3 years** if normal. [ADA 2026]`,`Offer **autoantibody screening to first-degree relatives** of people with type 1 diabetes; those with two or more antibodies need regular glucose monitoring and education about DKA symptoms. [ADA 2026]`,`In confirmed MODY, offer **cascade genetic testing** to first-degree relatives. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**NP-NCD** (National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases) screens everyone **30 and over** for diabetes at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** using capillary random glucose, with confirmation at the PHC/CHC. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`A random capillary glucose **140 mg/dL or more** at population screening is referred for confirmatory venous testing. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Counsel at diagnosis about **what type of diabetes** they have and why, because the type decides insulin need, inheritance and family screening. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Free **metformin, glimepiride and insulin** are provided at government facilities under NP-NCD and Jan Aushadhi supplies cheap generics. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Prevent type 2 in high-risk relatives with lifestyle change (see the Prediabetes topic); presymptomatic type 1 can be **delayed with teplizumab** where available. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2026** keep the diagnostic thresholds unchanged and reiterate that HbA1c must be interpreted against **red-cell turnover and haemoglobin variants**. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025-2026** formalise **presymptomatic type 1 (stages 1 and 2)**, recommend antibody screening of relatives, and monitoring schedules for antibody-positive people. [ADA 2026]`,`**IDF 2024 position statement**: 1-hour post-load glucose **155 mg/dL** (intermediate hyperglycaemia) and **209 mg/dL** (diabetes) as a cheaper, more sensitive test - not yet adopted by ADA or WHO. [IDF 2024]`,`**IDF 2025**: malnutrition-related diabetes named **type 5 diabetes**, with an international working group to define criteria - highly relevant to rural India. [IDF 2025]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB 2023** updated Indian prevalence to **11.4% diabetes, 15.3% prediabetes**; **NCD-RisC (Lancet 2024)** estimated over a quarter of the world's adults with diabetes live in India, with low treatment coverage. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**ADA/EASD 2021 consensus on type 1 in adults** gave the antibody-first, then C-peptide algorithm now used worldwide for unclear adult diabetes. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**RSSDI-ESI 2022 clinical practice recommendations** endorse HbA1c for diagnosis in India only with an NGSP-certified method and after excluding anaemia and haemoglobinopathy. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: "**What is the diagnostic criterion for diabetes?**" - list all four, add "two abnormal results if asymptomatic", and mention **HbA1c pitfalls**.`,`Classic long question: **classify diabetes** - write the ADA aetiological classification as a **branch diagram**, then add Indian specials (LADA, FCPD, ketosis-prone, type 5).`,`Common mistake: diagnosing on a **glucometer** or on **urine sugar**.`,`Common mistake: trusting an HbA1c of 6.1% in a woman with **Hb 8 g/dL** from iron deficiency - it may be falsely high; with haemolysis it may be falsely low.`,`Common mistake: ordering C-peptide **during DKA** or in the first months after diagnosis.`,`Remember the **MODY genes by number**: MODY1 HNF4A, MODY2 GCK, MODY3 HNF1A, MODY5 HNF1B - **GCK = no treatment, HNF1A/4A = low-dose sulfonylurea, HNF1B = insulin + kidneys**.`,`**A-beta classification** of ketosis-prone diabetes and **LADA C-peptide cut-offs (0.3 and 0.7 nmol/L)** are favourite viva extensions.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Diagnostic criteria for diabetes and prediabetes (venous plasma, mg/dL)`,columns:[`Test`,`Normal`,`Prediabetes`,`Diabetes`,`Practical notes`],rows:[[`Fasting plasma glucose`,`Under 100`,`100-125 (IFG; WHO 110-125)`,`126 or more`,`8 hours fasting; fluoride tube, spin fast`],[`2-hour plasma glucose, 75 g OGTT`,`Under 140`,`140-199 (IGT)`,`200 or more`,`Best test for Indians - detects isolated post-load hyperglycaemia`],[`HbA1c`,`Under 5.7%`,`5.7-6.4%`,`6.5% or more`,`NGSP-certified assay; check Hb and variants first`],[`Random plasma glucose`,`-`,`-`,`200 or more with symptoms or crisis`,`Diagnostic alone only if symptomatic`],[`1-hour post-load glucose (IDF 2024)`,`Under 155`,`155-208`,`209 or more`,`Proposed by IDF; not ADA/WHO standard`]]},{heading:`Aetiological classification and how to recognise each type`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Typical patient`,`Antibodies / C-peptide`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Type 1 (autoimmune)`,`T-cell beta-cell destruction`,`Child or young adult, lean, weight loss, DKA`,`1 or more antibodies; C-peptide under 200 pmol/L later`,`Basal-bolus insulin`],[`LADA`,`Slow autoimmune destruction`,`Adult over 30, lean, oral drug failure in months`,`GAD positive; C-peptide falls over years`,`Insulin or non-SU agents by C-peptide`],[`Type 2`,`Insulin resistance plus progressive secretory failure`,`Overweight, family history, acanthosis`,`Negative; C-peptide over 600 pmol/L`,`Lifestyle plus metformin`],[`MODY (HNF1A, HNF4A, GCK, HNF1B)`,`Monogenic beta-cell defect, autosomal dominant`,`Young, non-obese, 3 generations, no ketosis`,`Negative; C-peptide preserved`,`By gene: none, low-dose SU or insulin`],[`Ketosis-prone (A-beta+)`,`Transient beta-cell stunning`,`Overweight man with DKA at onset`,`Negative; C-peptide recovers after DKA`,`Insulin, then taper`],[`Pancreatogenic (FCPD, type 3c)`,`Loss of exocrine and endocrine pancreas`,`Lean young adult, abdominal pain, steatorrhoea, calculi`,`Negative; C-peptide low-moderate`,`Insulin plus enzymes`],[`Drug-induced / endocrinopathy`,`Insulin resistance or reduced secretion`,`Steroids, antipsychotics, tacrolimus, Cushing, acromegaly`,`Negative; variable`,`Treat cause; insulin or orals`],[`Type 5 (malnutrition-related, IDF 2025)`,`Insulin-secretory deficiency after early undernutrition`,`BMI under 18.5, young, rural, poor`,`Negative; low C-peptide, no ketosis`,`Low-dose insulin or orals plus nutrition`]]},{heading:`MODY subtypes the examiner expects`,columns:[`Gene (MODY number)`,`Frequency`,`Clinical clues`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`HNF1A (MODY3)`,`Commonest symptomatic MODY`,`Young onset, glycosuria at low glucose, low hsCRP, progressive`,`Low-dose sulfonylurea (gliclazide 20-40 mg)`],[`GCK (MODY2)`,`Common; found on routine testing`,`Mild stable fasting glucose 99-144 mg/dL, HbA1c 5.6-7.6%, small OGTT rise`,`No treatment (except selected pregnancies)`],[`HNF4A (MODY1)`,`Less common`,`Neonatal macrosomia and hypoglycaemia, later diabetes`,`Low-dose sulfonylurea`],[`HNF1B (MODY5)`,`Less common`,`Renal cysts, genital anomalies, pancreatic atrophy, low magnesium, raised LFTs`,`Insulin; renal follow-up`],[`KCNJ11 / ABCC8 (neonatal)`,`Rare`,`Diabetes before 6 months, sometimes developmental delay (DEND)`,`High-dose glibenclamide`]]},{heading:`HbA1c pitfalls in Indian practice`,columns:[`Condition`,`Effect on HbA1c`,`Why`,`What to use instead`],rows:[[`Iron, B12 or folate deficiency`,`Falsely high`,`Older red cell population`,`Plasma glucose; recheck after correction`],[`Haemolysis, G6PD deficiency, malaria`,`Falsely low`,`Younger red cells`,`Plasma glucose, fructosamine`],[`Recent blood loss or transfusion`,`Falsely low / unreliable`,`New or donor red cells`,`Plasma glucose`],[`Pregnancy (2nd-3rd trimester)`,`Falsely low`,`Increased red-cell turnover`,`OGTT`],[`CKD 4-5, dialysis, ESA therapy`,`Usually falsely low`,`Shortened survival, erythropoietin`,`Glucose, CGM, glycated albumin`],[`HbS, HbE, HbD trait, beta-thalassaemia`,`Assay-dependent`,`Variant co-elutes or alters glycation`,`Assay free of interference; glucose criteria`],[`Asplenia, alcohol, uraemia (carbamylation)`,`Falsely high`,`Prolonged red-cell life or modified Hb`,`Plasma glucose`]]},{heading:`Drugs and treatments tied to diabetes type`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Basal-bolus insulin (type 1, LADA with low C-peptide)`,`0.4-0.5 units/kg/day SC; half basal, half prandial`,`Lifelong; titrate to SMBG/CGM`,`Hypoglycaemia, weight gain, lipohypertrophy`],[`Gliclazide (HNF1A / HNF4A MODY)`,`20-40 mg once daily orally`,`Lifelong; small increments`,`Hypoglycaemia at usual doses; avoid in LADA`],[`Glibenclamide (KCNJ11/ABCC8 neonatal diabetes)`,`Start 0.2 mg/kg/day orally in 2 doses`,`Increase to effect, usually about 0.5 mg/kg/day`,`Diarrhoea, hypoglycaemia; specialist only`],[`Metformin (type 2, ketosis-prone after remission, mild LADA)`,`500 mg once or twice daily with meals, up to 1 g twice daily`,`Increase every 1-2 weeks`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency; stop if eGFR under 30`],[`Pancreatic enzyme replacement (pancreatogenic diabetes)`,`40,000-50,000 lipase units with meals, half with snacks`,`Lifelong; adjust to steatorrhoea`,`Abdominal pain; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses`]]}],redFlags:[`New hyperglycaemia with **ketonuria 2+, vomiting, abdominal pain or Kussmaul breathing** - suspected DKA at first presentation.`,`Random glucose **over 300 mg/dL with drowsiness or dehydration** - HHS or DKA.`,`Lean young adult with **rapid weight loss** and glucose over 250 mg/dL - likely type 1 or LADA; start insulin, do not trial tablets.`,`Diabetes in an **infant under 6 months** - neonatal diabetes, urgent genetic testing.`,`New diabetes **over 50 with weight loss, jaundice or back pain** - exclude pancreatic cancer.`,`Sudden severe hyperglycaemia on **immune checkpoint inhibitor** therapy - fulminant autoimmune diabetes.`,`Pregnant woman with **overt diabetes** at booking - urgent joint obstetric-medical care.`],pearls:[`**Never diagnose on a glucometer or a urine dipstick** - venous plasma in a fluoride tube, spun promptly.`,`Asymptomatic? **Two abnormal results** - two tests on one sample counts.`,`Check **Hb before HbA1c**: iron deficiency raises it, haemolysis and transfusion lower it.`,`**Antibodies first, C-peptide later** (3 years after diagnosis, never during DKA).`,`C-peptide: **under 200 pmol/L = type 1 pattern; over 600 = type 2 pattern**.`,`**GCK = Give no treatment; HNF1A = Hypersensitive to sulfonylurea; HNF1B = kidney cysts, needs insulin**.`,`LADA rule of thirds: **over 30, antibody-positive, insulin-free for 6 months**.`,`Ketosis-prone **A-beta+**: DKA at onset but insulin can often be stopped.`,`**Type 5 diabetes** (IDF 2025) = malnutrition-related, lean, no ketosis, no autoimmunity.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-q1`,stem:`A 24-year-old non-obese woman has fasting glucose readings of 108-128 mg/dL on several occasions over 6 years, HbA1c 6.3%, and only a 30 mg/dL rise on a 75 g OGTT. Her mother has had 'borderline sugar' for 20 years without treatment. GAD antibodies are negative. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Start metformin 500 mg twice daily`,`Start basal insulin 0.2 units/kg at bedtime`,`Confirm GCK-MODY genetically and give no glucose-lowering drug`,`Start gliclazide 80 mg twice daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Mild, stable fasting hyperglycaemia for years, a small OGTT increment, HbA1c in the 5.6-7.6% band, antibody negativity and a dominant family history are classic for GCK-MODY. The glucose sensor is reset, complications are rare and drugs barely change glucose, so no treatment is needed. Metformin and insulin are unnecessary; full-dose gliclazide is the treatment for HNF1A-MODY, not GCK, and at that dose would cause hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-q2`,stem:`In which of the following situations is HbA1c most likely to be falsely LOW compared with true average glycaemia?`,options:[`Untreated iron deficiency anaemia`,`Two weeks after a two-unit blood transfusion`,`Vitamin B12 deficiency`,`Asplenia after trauma`],answer:1,explanation:`Transfused and newly formed red cells have had less time exposed to glucose, so HbA1c falls after transfusion, blood loss or haemolysis. Iron, B12 and folate deficiency increase the average red-cell age and raise HbA1c, as does asplenia, which prolongs red-cell survival. In these situations plasma glucose criteria should be used for diagnosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-q3`,stem:`A 38-year-old man diagnosed with 'type 2 diabetes' 4 years ago is now on three oral drugs with HbA1c 10.2%. BMI is 21 kg/m2 and he has vitiligo. GAD antibody is positive and random C-peptide (glucose 220 mg/dL) is 0.2 nmol/L. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Add a sulfonylurea at maximum dose`,`Start a basal-bolus insulin regimen`,`Add pioglitazone and recheck in 3 months`,`Order next-generation sequencing for MODY`],answer:1,explanation:`This is LADA (adult onset, antibody-positive, insulin-free initially) with C-peptide under 0.3 nmol/L, which means severe insulin deficiency - the expert panel advises insulin as in type 1, ideally basal-bolus. Sulfonylureas may hasten beta-cell exhaustion and are avoided in LADA; pioglitazone will not overcome insulin deficiency; MODY testing is irrelevant in an antibody-positive patient.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old overweight man presents with DKA without any precipitant. Islet antibodies are negative. Six weeks later, on insulin, fasting C-peptide is well preserved and he has had repeated low readings. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`He has type 1B idiopathic diabetes and needs lifelong insulin`,`C-peptide measured after recovery is meaningless; repeat it during the next DKA`,`He has A-beta+ ketosis-prone diabetes and insulin can be tapered with monitoring`,`He should receive teplizumab to preserve beta-cell function`],answer:2,explanation:`Antibody-negative with preserved beta-cell function after recovery is the A-beta+ (Flatbush) phenotype of ketosis-prone diabetes; most can taper insulin, often onto metformin, under ketone and glucose monitoring. C-peptide is unreliable during DKA, not after it. Type 1B implies absent C-peptide. Teplizumab is for presymptomatic autoimmune type 1, which this is not.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-q5`,stem:`An asymptomatic 42-year-old man has, on a single venous sample, fasting plasma glucose 132 mg/dL and HbA1c 6.7%. Haemoglobin is normal. What is the correct interpretation per ADA 2026?`,options:[`Diabetes is confirmed; no repeat test is needed`,`Prediabetes; repeat in one year`,`Diabetes cannot be diagnosed without an OGTT`,`Repeat both tests on another day before labelling`],answer:0,explanation:`ADA allows confirmation with two different abnormal tests from the same sample, so FPG 126 or more plus HbA1c 6.5% or more establishes diabetes. Repeating on another day is needed only when a single test is abnormal or the two disagree. An OGTT is not mandatory, and both values exceed the prediabetes ranges.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify diabetes mellitus. Give the diagnostic criteria and discuss how you will differentiate type 1 diabetes, LADA, MODY and type 2 diabetes in a young Indian adult.`,openingLines:[`Diabetes mellitus is chronic hyperglycaemia from defective insulin secretion, action or both, diagnosed by FPG 126 mg/dL or more, 2-hour OGTT 200 mg/dL or more, HbA1c 6.5% or more or random 200 mg/dL or more with symptoms (ADA 2026).`,`Correct classification matters because it decides insulin need, drug choice, prognosis and family screening.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification (ADA 2026 with Indian additions)`,points:[`Type 1 (autoimmune 1A, idiopathic 1B; stages 1-3), including LADA.`,`Type 2 - insulin resistance with progressive beta-cell failure.`,`Specific types: monogenic (MODY, neonatal), exocrine pancreas (FCPD, pancreatitis), endocrinopathies, drug-induced, post-transplant.`,`Gestational diabetes; Indian specials - ketosis-prone (A-beta), type 5 malnutrition-related (IDF 2025).`]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria`,points:[`FPG 126 or more, 2-hour OGTT 200 or more, HbA1c 6.5% or more, random 200 or more with symptoms.`,`Two abnormal results if asymptomatic (same sample allowed).`,`Prediabetes: IFG 100-125, IGT 140-199, HbA1c 5.7-6.4%.`,`HbA1c pitfalls: anaemia, haemoglobinopathy, transfusion, pregnancy, CKD.`]},{heading:`Clinical discriminators`,points:[`Type 1: under 35, lean, weight loss, ketosis, glucose over 360 mg/dL, autoimmunity.`,`LADA: over 30, lean, oral drug failure within months to years, GAD-positive.`,`MODY: three generations, non-obese, no ketosis, antibody-negative, C-peptide preserved.`,`Type 2: obesity, acanthosis, family history, metabolic syndrome.`]},{heading:`Laboratory approach`,points:[`GAD65 first, then IA-2 and ZnT8; insulin antibodies only before insulin.`,`C-peptide with glucose over 144 mg/dL, after 3 years: under 200 pmol/L type 1, over 600 type 2.`,`Exeter MODY calculator then gene panel (GCK, HNF1A, HNF4A, HNF1B).`,`X-ray/USG abdomen for pancreatic calculi.`]},{heading:`Treatment consequences`,points:[`Type 1 and LADA with low C-peptide - insulin; avoid sulfonylureas in LADA.`,`GCK-MODY - no drugs; HNF1A/4A - low-dose gliclazide; HNF1B - insulin.`,`Type 2 - lifestyle plus metformin, organ-protective drugs.`,`Family cascade testing in MODY; antibody screening of type 1 relatives.`]}],mustDraw:[`Branch diagram of the ADA aetiological classification`,`Flow chart: suspected adult type 1 - GAD antibody - C-peptide - genetic testing`,`Table comparing type 1, LADA, MODY and type 2`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria and HbA1c pitfalls`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical and laboratory differentiation`,marks:4},{part:`Treatment implications`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ADA classification`,`LADA`,`MODY`,`C-peptide`,`GAD antibody`,`HbA1c pitfalls`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-classification-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`HbA1c in the diagnosis of diabetes: advantages and pitfalls in Indian practice.`,openingLines:[`HbA1c reflects average glycaemia over 8-12 weeks through non-enzymatic glycation of the haemoglobin beta-chain; 6.5% or more diagnoses diabetes and 5.7-6.4% prediabetes (ADA 2026).`,`It must be done by an NGSP-certified, DCCT-standardised method and interpreted against red-cell turnover.`],answer:[{heading:`Advantages`,points:[`No fasting, single sample, low day-to-day variability, stable after collection.`,`Same test used for monitoring and predicts microvascular complications.`]},{heading:`Falsely high`,points:[`Iron, B12, folate deficiency; asplenia; uraemia; alcohol.`]},{heading:`Falsely low`,points:[`Haemolysis, G6PD deficiency, malaria, transfusion, blood loss, pregnancy, ESA, dialysis, HIV on NRTIs, dapsone.`]},{heading:`Indian issues and alternatives`,points:[`Anaemia in 57% of women (NFHS-5); HbE, HbS, HbD and beta-thalassaemia trait - assay-dependent.`,`Less sensitive than OGTT for isolated IGT, common in Asian Indians.`,`Alternatives: plasma glucose criteria, fructosamine, glycated albumin, CGM.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: condition, effect on HbA1c, alternative test`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and cut-offs`,marks:1},{part:`Advantages`,marks:1},{part:`Pitfalls with mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Alternatives`,marks:1}],keywords:[`HbA1c`,`NGSP`,`anaemia`,`haemoglobinopathy`,`fructosamine`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diabetes mellitus: diagnosis, classification and pathophysiology`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 2: Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes`,`Holt RIG et al. The management of type 1 diabetes in adults: ADA/EASD consensus report, Diabetes Care 2021`,`Buzzetti R et al. Management of latent autoimmune diabetes in adults: a consensus statement from an international expert panel, Diabetes 2020`,`ISPAD Clinical Practice Consensus Guidelines 2022: The diagnosis and management of monogenic diabetes in children and adolescents`,`Anjana RM et al. Metabolic NCD health report of India: ICMR-INDIAB national cross-sectional study 17, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023`,`RSSDI-ESI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus 2022`,`IDF Diabetes Atlas, 11th edition, 2025; IDF position statement on the 1-hour post-load plasma glucose, 2024`]}),e.push({id:`endocrine-t1dm-management`,title:`Type 1 diabetes: insulin regimens, carb counting, sick-day rules and transition care`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Type 1 diabetes** is autoimmune absolute insulin deficiency managed for life with **physiological basal-bolus insulin (multiple daily injections or a pump) at about 0.4-1 unit/kg/day**, matched to food by **carbohydrate counting (insulin-to-carb ratio, correction factor)**, guided by **CGM**, protected by **sick-day rules (never stop insulin, check ketones)**, and supported through school and **structured transition** to adult care, aiming for **HbA1c under 7% and time in range over 70%**.`,keywords:[`T1DM`,`type 1 diabetes management`,`basal-bolus`,`MDI`,`insulin pump`,`CSII`,`insulin glargine`,`insulin degludec`,`insulin aspart`,`insulin lispro`,`carbohydrate counting`,`insulin-to-carb ratio`,`correction factor`,`500 rule`,`1800 rule`,`sick-day rules`,`beta-hydroxybutyrate`,`teplizumab`,`honeymoon phase`,`transition care`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Type 1 diabetes** is chronic hyperglycaemia from **autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells**, producing absolute insulin deficiency that is fatal without insulin. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **9.5 million people** worldwide live with type 1 diabetes, and about one in five are under 20 years of age. [IDF Atlas 2025]`,`India has the **largest number of children and adolescents with type 1 diabetes** in the world - about **229,400 under 20 years** in the 2021 estimate. [IDF Atlas 2021]`,`Incidence peaks at **5-7 years and around puberty**, but over half of all new type 1 cases are now diagnosed **in adults**. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`Indian registries (ICMR Youth Onset Diabetes Registry) show many children present first in **DKA**, and **access to insulin, strips and education** decides survival in low-income families. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`Life expectancy is reduced by about **10-12 years**, mostly from cardiovascular disease, kidney disease and acute complications, and is far worse where insulin is interrupted. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**HLA genes** (DR3-DQ2 and DR4-DQ8) contribute about half the genetic risk; non-HLA genes include INS, PTPN22 and CTLA4. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk to a child is about **6% if the father**, about **3-4% if the mother** and about **5% if a sibling** has type 1, against about 0.3% in the general population. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Proposed environmental triggers include **enteroviral infection**, early-life diet and gut microbiome changes; none is a proven, preventable cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Associated autoimmunity**: autoimmune thyroid disease in about **15-30%**, **coeliac disease** in about 1-16%, plus vitiligo, Addison disease and pernicious anaemia. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Insulin need rises with **puberty** (growth hormone and sex steroids cause insulin resistance), **infection, steroids and pregnancy** and falls in the honeymoon phase, with exercise and with weight loss. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Psychosocial risk factors** for poor control: poverty, single caregiver, depression, **disordered eating (deliberate insulin omission)** and school or college exams. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why physiological insulin replacement`,points:[`A healthy pancreas secretes about **half its daily insulin as basal** (suppressing hepatic glucose output and lipolysis between meals) and half as **prandial bursts** that peak within 30-60 minutes of eating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Basal-bolus therapy** copies this: a long-acting analogue supplies basal needs and a rapid-acting analogue covers each meal and corrects highs. [ADA 2026]`,`Pancreatic insulin goes first to the **liver via the portal vein**; subcutaneous insulin reaches the periphery first, causing **peripheral hyperinsulinaemia** and weight gain with relative hepatic under-insulinisation. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Within a few years of diagnosis the **glucagon response to hypoglycaemia is lost** and later the adrenaline response blunts - explaining recurrent hypoglycaemia and **hypoglycaemia unawareness** (see the Hypoglycaemia topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`Even a little residual beta-cell function (detectable C-peptide) **lowers hypoglycaemia and complication risk** - the reason to protect the honeymoon and the aim of immunotherapy. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`With **insulin deficiency** fat breaks down to free fatty acids, which the liver turns into ketones under unopposed glucagon - **missing insulin for even a few hours** on a pump can start DKA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dawn phenomenon**: early-morning growth hormone and cortisol raise glucose between 3 and 8 a.m., worst in adolescents. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification of regimens and insulins`,points:[`**Multiple daily injections (MDI) basal-bolus**: one or two basal injections plus rapid-acting insulin before each meal - the standard of care. [ADA 2026]`,`**Continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII, pump)**: rapid-acting analogue only, with programmable basal rates and meal boluses; with a CGM and algorithm it becomes **automated insulin delivery (AID)** (see the Glucose monitoring topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Premixed or NPH-regular twice daily** regimens are **inferior** but are still used in India for cost or when a child cannot inject at school; they need fixed meal times and snacks. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`**Rapid-acting analogues** (lispro, aspart, glulisine): onset **10-15 minutes**, peak **1-2 hours**, duration **3-5 hours**; **faster aspart and ultra-rapid lispro** start in about 5 minutes. [ADA 2026]`,`**Regular (soluble) human insulin**: onset **30-60 minutes**, peak 2-4 hours, duration 6-8 hours - inject **30 minutes before meals**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NPH**: onset 1-2 hours, peak **4-10 hours**, duration 10-16 hours - its peak causes nocturnal hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Long-acting analogues**: glargine U100 about 24 hours, **glargine U300** and **degludec** (over 42 hours) are flatter with less nocturnal hypoglycaemia; **biosimilar glargine** is the cheapest analogue in India. [ADA 2026]`,`**Once-weekly insulin icodec** is approved for type 2 in several countries but **not recommended for type 1**, where it caused more hypoglycaemia (ONWARDS 6). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: what to ask at every visit`,points:[`**Glucose data review**: CGM or meter download - fasting, pre-meal, post-meal and night readings; **time in range** and hypoglycaemia frequency. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia history**: number per week, nocturnal events, severe episodes needing help, and **awareness** (Gold or Clarke score). [ADA 2026]`,`**Injection practice**: sites, rotation, needle length and reuse, pen or syringe, timing in relation to meals, missed doses. [FITTER Forward 2024]`,`**Food**: carbohydrate counting skill, meal pattern, snacks, festivals and fasting. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Growth and puberty** in children; **school performance, exams and sports**; missed school days. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Psychosocial screen**: depression, diabetes distress, **disordered eating** (insulin omission for weight loss), alcohol, recreational drugs - screen from age 10-12. [ADA 2026]`,`**Symptoms of associated disease**: weight loss with diarrhoea (coeliac), fatigue and hyperpigmentation (Addison), goitre or lethargy (thyroid), unexplained hypoglycaemia (Addison or coeliac). [ISPAD 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Height, weight and BMI on a growth chart** at every visit - faltering growth suggests poor control, coeliac disease or hypothyroidism. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Mauriac syndrome** (growth failure, hepatomegaly, delayed puberty, cushingoid face) signals chronic severe under-insulinisation. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Inspect and palpate injection sites for **lipohypertrophy** - rubbery lumps from repeated injections in one place that cause erratic absorption. [FITTER Forward 2024]`,`**Blood pressure** with age-centile charts in children; **fundus** and **foot** examination as screening schedules require. [ADA 2026]`,`**Limited joint mobility** (prayer sign) and **necrobiosis lipoidica** indicate long duration and microvascular risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Goitre, vitiligo and pubertal staging (Tanner)** at least yearly. [ISPAD 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of common problems on insulin`,points:[`**High fasting glucose** has three causes: **waning basal insulin**, the **dawn phenomenon** and, rarely, **rebound after nocturnal hypoglycaemia (Somogyi)** - CGM or a 3 a.m. check decides. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Unexplained erratic glucose**: lipohypertrophy, wrong technique, missed doses, gastroparesis, coeliac disease, Addison disease or hypothyroidism. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Rising insulin needs** in a teenager are usually **puberty and missed doses**, not insulin resistance; falling needs suggest **honeymoon, Addison, coeliac or renal failure**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Recurrent DKA** usually means **insulin omission** (cost, psychosocial, eating disorder) - not a technical failure. [ADA 2026]`,`In adults labelled type 1 with **preserved C-peptide years later**, reconsider **MODY** (see the Classification topic). [ADA/EASD 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring targets`,points:[`**HbA1c** every **3 months**; target **under 7%** for most children and adults, **under 6.5%** if achievable without hypoglycaemia, and looser (under 7.5-8%) with unawareness or limited resources. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**CGM targets**: time in range (70-180 mg/dL) **over 70%**, time below 70 **under 4%**, time below 54 **under 1%** (see the Glucose monitoring topic). [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**SMBG**: at least **4-6 times a day** (before meals, bedtime) and more with exercise, illness or driving if no CGM. [ADA 2026]`,`**Blood ketone (beta-hydroxybutyrate)**: under 0.6 mmol/L normal; **0.6-1.4 raised**; **1.5-2.9 high risk of DKA**; **3.0 or more** - DKA likely, urgent care. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**At diagnosis and yearly**: TSH (and anti-TPO at diagnosis), **coeliac serology (tTG-IgA with total IgA)** at diagnosis then at 2 and 5 years, lipids, urine ACR, creatinine. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Complication screening** in children: urine ACR and retinal screening from **age 11 or puberty with 2-5 years' duration**; in adults, retina and kidney **within 5 years of diagnosis**, then yearly (see the Complication screening topic). [ISPAD 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: insulin dosing and carbohydrate counting`,points:[`**Starting total daily dose (TDD)**: adults **0.4-0.5 units/kg/day**; prepubertal children **0.7-1.0**; puberty **1.2-1.5** or more; honeymoon often **under 0.5**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Give about **40-50% as basal** (glargine or degludec once daily, or detemir/NPH twice daily) and split the rest across meals by carbohydrate content. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**Insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio (ICR)** by the **500 rule**: 500 / TDD = grams of carbohydrate covered by 1 unit of rapid analogue (450 rule for regular insulin). [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**Correction (insulin sensitivity) factor** by the **1800 rule**: 1800 / TDD = mg/dL fall per unit of rapid analogue (1500 rule for regular insulin); correction dose = (current glucose minus target) / ISF. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`Worked example: TDD 40 units - ICR **1 unit per 12.5 g**, ISF **45 mg/dL per unit**; for 60 g carbohydrate at glucose 220 (target 120), bolus = 4.8 + 2.2 = **7 units**. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**Indian carbohydrate exchanges**: one medium **chapati about 15 g**, one small katori (about 100 g) **cooked rice about 28-30 g**, one medium idli about 15 g, one glass milk about 12 g - teach with local food. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`**Basal titration**: adjust by 10% (or 1-2 units) every 3 days to keep fasting and overnight glucose **80-130 mg/dL** without lows; **basal fixed if a skipped meal keeps glucose flat**. [ADA 2026]`,`Inject rapid analogues **0-15 minutes before meals** (regular insulin 30 minutes); in small children with unpredictable intake, give the bolus **immediately after the meal** based on what was eaten. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Injection technique**: **4 mm pen needle** at 90 degrees without a skin fold in most people, rotate within abdomen, thighs, buttocks and arms, and **do not reuse needles**. [FITTER Forward 2024]`,`**Storage in India**: unopened vials and pens in a **refrigerator at 2-8 C**; the in-use pen or vial at room temperature for up to **28-30 days**; where there is no fridge, an **earthen pot (matka) or wet-cloth cooler** keeps insulin effective. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Exercise, sick-day rules and hypoglycaemia plan`,points:[`**Before exercise** aim for glucose **126-180 mg/dL**; if under 90, take **10-20 g carbohydrate** and delay; if over 270, check ketones and **do not exercise if blood ketone is 1.5 mmol/L or more**. [Exercise in T1D consensus 2017]`,`**Reduce the pre-exercise meal bolus by 25-75%** if exercising within 90 minutes of eating, take **30-60 g carbohydrate per hour** during prolonged exercise, and reduce bedtime basal by about **20%** after afternoon or evening exercise to prevent **nocturnal hypoglycaemia**. [Exercise in T1D consensus 2017]`,`**Sick-day rule 1: never stop basal insulin**, even if not eating - illness raises insulin needs. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Rule 2**: check glucose every **2-3 hours** and **ketones** whenever glucose is over 250 mg/dL or the patient vomits. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Rule 3 - extra rapid insulin by ketones**: blood ketone **0.6-1.4** give **5-10% of TDD**; **1.5-2.9** give **10-20% of TDD**; repeat every 2-3 hours; **3.0 or more** - seek urgent care. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Rule 4**: fluids hourly - sugar-free if glucose is high, **sugar-containing (ORS, juice) if glucose is under 180** so insulin can continue without hypoglycaemia. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Rule 5 - go to hospital**: persistent vomiting, ketones rising or **3.0 or more**, deep breathing, abdominal pain, drowsiness, age under 2, or a carer unable to cope. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Hypoglycaemia (under 70 mg/dL)**: **15 g fast carbohydrate** (3 teaspoons sugar in water, 150 mL juice), recheck in 15 minutes; severe - **glucagon 1 mg IM/SC** (0.5 mg if under 25 kg) or 3 mg nasal; every patient on insulin should have glucagon (see the Hypoglycaemia topic). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit for **DKA** (see the DKA and HHS topic), severe hypoglycaemia with persistent neuroglycopenia, persistent vomiting with ketones, and new diagnosis in a young child or a family unable to manage at home. [ISPAD 2022]`,`New diagnosis without acidosis can be managed **as an outpatient or day-care** where a trained team can teach injections, SMBG and hypoglycaemia care within 24-48 hours. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Never omit basal insulin** in hospital; if nil by mouth, continue basal (about 80% of usual dose) or use a **variable-rate IV insulin infusion with dextrose**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pump users in hospital** may continue the pump if alert and capable with a written policy; otherwise convert to basal-bolus **before** the pump is removed, as the pump has no depot. [ADA 2026]`,`Before surgery, give the usual basal (reduce 20% the night before or morning of), monitor glucose hourly and keep **140-180 mg/dL** (see the Perioperative diabetes topic). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations: school, transition, pregnancy and others`,points:[`**School**: a written diabetes care plan, a trained teacher, permission to test, inject and eat in class; **CBSE allows children with type 1 to carry glucose, snacks, meter and insulin into the exam hall** (circular 2017). [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`**Transition** to adult care at about **16-18 years** (complete by 18-25): start preparing **from age 12-14**, hold joint paediatric-adult clinics, and track drop-out, which is when HbA1c and DKA admissions peak. [ADA 2026]`,`Topics to cover in transition: **driving, alcohol (delayed hypoglycaemia), contraception, smoking, recreational drugs, travel, careers and insurance**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: plan with HbA1c **under 6.5%**, folic acid before conception, retinal check; insulin needs fall in the first trimester and **rise 2-3-fold** by the third; CGM target **time in range 63-140 over 70%** (see the obstetrics topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Elderly with long-standing type 1**: relax HbA1c to **under 8%**, watch for unawareness, cognitive decline and kidney disease. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fasting (Ramadan, Navratri)**: possible with CGM, basal reduced by **20-40%** and a plan to break the fast below 70 or above 300 mg/dL (see the Diabetes in special situations topic). [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Adjuncts in adults**: **metformin** for overweight type 1 reduces insulin dose and weight but not HbA1c much; **SGLT2 inhibitors are not approved in type 1** because of **euglycaemic DKA**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: twice-daily human premixed or NPH plus regular with fixed meals, free insulin through state programmes (for example **Kerala Mittayi**) and NGO schemes, and at least **3 strips a day**. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**DCCT**: intensive therapy lowered HbA1c to about 7% and cut retinopathy by about **76%**, nephropathy by about 50% and neuropathy by about 60%. [DCCT 1993]`,`**EDIC follow-up**: early intensive therapy reduced cardiovascular events by about **42%** years later - **metabolic memory**. [DCCT/EDIC 2005]`,`**Severe hypoglycaemia** risk rises as HbA1c falls with injections; **CGM and AID** break this trade-off. [ADA 2026]`,`**Acute complications**: DKA (commonest cause of death in young type 1), severe hypoglycaemia, and **dead-in-bed syndrome** (nocturnal arrhythmia during hypoglycaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic complications** appear after about **5 years** of type 1 - which is why screening starts then (or at puberty). [ADA 2026]`,`**Psychiatric burden**: depression and **diabetes distress** are 2-3 times commoner; insulin omission for weight control triples mortality. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and counselling`,points:[`Every person with type 1 should be under a **specialist diabetes team** (physician or paediatrician, educator, dietitian, counsellor) at least once a year, with primary care in between. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Review **every 3 months**: HbA1c, CGM/SMBG data, weight/height, BP, injection sites, hypoglycaemia, psychosocial screen. [ADA 2026]`,`**Yearly**: TSH, lipids (from age 11 in children, then every 3 years if normal), urine ACR, eGFR, retina, feet, coeliac screen if symptomatic. [ISPAD 2022]`,`Refer urgently for **recurrent DKA, recurrent severe hypoglycaemia, unawareness, growth failure, suspected eating disorder** and pregnancy. [ADA 2026]`,`**Statin**: adults with type 1 over **40 years**, or 20-39 with additional risk factors or microvascular disease. [ADA 2026]`,`**Teplizumab** (anti-CD3 monoclonal, 14-day IV course) delays progression from **stage 2 to stage 3** type 1 by a median of about **2-3 years** in people aged **8 and over**; side effects are lymphopenia, rash and cytokine release; not marketed in India. [ADA 2026]`,`**Antibody screening of relatives** identifies presymptomatic type 1, halves DKA at diagnosis and allows education and trial entry. [ADA 2026]`,`There is **no proven primary prevention** - diet changes, vitamin D and oral insulin have not prevented type 1. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel families that type 1 is **not caused by sugar** or anything they did, that **insulin is lifelong**, and that "cures" sold by quacks kill by causing DKA. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`Under NP-NCD, type 1 children should be linked to the district **NCD clinic** for free insulin; insulin is on the **National List of Essential Medicines** and Jan Aushadhi stocks human insulin. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA 2026**: **CGM from diagnosis** for everyone with type 1, and **AID offered to all with type 1** who can use it safely, including young children and pregnancy. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025-2026**: consider **teplizumab** for stage 2 type 1 in those 8 and over; follow antibody-positive relatives with HbA1c or glucose every 6-12 months. [ADA 2026]`,`**ISPAD 2022**: HbA1c target **under 7%** for children (rather than 7.5%) with TIR over 70% as the CGM goal. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**ICMR Guidelines for Management of Type 1 Diabetes 2022** give Indian-adapted carb exchanges, insulin storage without refrigeration, school care and low-cost regimens. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`,`**ADA 2024 hyperglycaemic crises consensus**: DKA diagnosis now accepts **beta-hydroxybutyrate 3.0 mmol/L or more** and glucose 200 or more, or known diabetes, to catch euglycaemic DKA. [ADA 2024 hyperglycaemic crises consensus]`,`**Weekly insulin icodec**: not recommended for type 1 (excess hypoglycaemia in ONWARDS 6). [ADA 2026]`,`**Islet transplantation** (donislecel approved by FDA in 2023) and **stem-cell-derived islets** are reserved for severe hypoglycaemia unawareness and remain largely investigational. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: "**Calculate the bolus**" - know the **500 and 1800 rules** and do one worked example aloud.`,`Viva: "**What are sick-day rules?**" - never stop insulin, check glucose and ketones 2-3 hourly, extra 10-20% of TDD by ketones, fluids, when to come in.`,`Common mistake: **stopping insulin when a child is vomiting and not eating** - the commonest preventable cause of DKA.`,`Common mistake: increasing evening insulin for high fasting glucose **without excluding nocturnal hypoglycaemia**.`,`Remember the **honeymoon**: needs fall below 0.5 units/kg/day in the first months - reduce doses, do not stop insulin.`,`Long question favourite: **"Management of a 10-year-old with newly diagnosed type 1 diabetes"** - education, insulin, monitoring, school, family, follow-up.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Insulins used in type 1 diabetes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Insulin aspart / lispro / glulisine (rapid analogue)`,`Per ICR and ISF, SC 0-15 min before meals; pumps`,`Onset 10-15 min, peak 1-2 h, lasts 3-5 h`,`Hypoglycaemia, lipohypertrophy`],[`Regular human insulin`,`SC 30 min before meals; IV in DKA`,`Onset 30-60 min, peak 2-4 h, lasts 6-8 h`,`Late post-meal hypoglycaemia`],[`NPH (isophane) insulin`,`SC twice daily, about 2/3 morning in older regimens`,`Peak 4-10 h, lasts 10-16 h`,`Nocturnal hypoglycaemia; mix gently`],[`Insulin glargine U100 (incl. biosimilar)`,`SC once daily, about 40-50% of TDD`,`About 24 h; titrate by 10% every 3 days`,`Hypoglycaemia; do not mix with other insulins`],[`Insulin degludec / glargine U300`,`SC once daily`,`Degludec over 42 h; titrate every 3-4 days`,`Less nocturnal hypoglycaemia; cost`],[`Premixed human 30/70 or analogue 30/70`,`SC twice daily before breakfast and dinner`,`Fixed ratio; needs regular meals and snacks`,`Inferior control; hypoglycaemia`],[`Glucagon`,`1 mg IM/SC (0.5 mg if under 25 kg); nasal 3 mg`,`Single dose; repeat glucose after 10-15 min`,`Nausea, vomiting; ineffective in alcohol-induced or starved states`],[`Teplizumab`,`IV infusion daily for 14 days (body-surface dosing)`,`One course, stage 2, age 8 and over`,`Lymphopenia, rash, cytokine release; avoid in active infection`]]},{heading:`Insulin regimens compared`,columns:[`Regimen`,`How`,`Best for`,`Drawbacks`],rows:[[`MDI basal-bolus`,`Basal once/twice daily + rapid before each meal`,`Most people; flexible meals`,`4-5 injections a day; needs carb counting`],[`Pump (CSII)`,`Rapid analogue, programmable basal and boluses`,`Variable basal needs, young children, dawn phenomenon`,`Cost; DKA within hours if delivery fails`],[`Automated insulin delivery`,`Pump + CGM + algorithm adjusts basal`,`All with type 1 able to use it (ADA 2026)`,`Cost, training; still needs meal boluses`],[`Twice-daily premixed / split-mixed`,`NPH + regular or 30/70 before breakfast and dinner`,`Cost-limited, no lunch-time injection possible`,`Rigid meals, snacks needed, more hypoglycaemia`]]},{heading:`Sick-day insulin by blood ketones (ISPAD 2022)`,columns:[`Blood ketone (mmol/L)`,`Meaning`,`Extra rapid insulin`,`Action`],rows:[[`Under 0.6`,`Normal`,`Usual correction only`,`Glucose 2-3 hourly`],[`0.6-1.4`,`Raised`,`5-10% of TDD`,`Recheck glucose and ketones in 2 hours; fluids`],[`1.5-2.9`,`High risk of DKA`,`10-20% of TDD`,`Recheck in 2 hours; call the team`],[`3.0 or more`,`DKA likely`,`10-20% of TDD while arranging transfer`,`Hospital now`]]},{heading:`Morning hyperglycaemia: what is causing it?`,columns:[`Cause`,`3 a.m. glucose`,`Mechanism`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Waning basal insulin`,`High and rising`,`Basal runs out before morning`,`Increase or move basal; switch to degludec/glargine`],[`Dawn phenomenon`,`Normal, rises after 3-4 a.m.`,`Growth hormone and cortisol surge`,`Pump basal rise; later NPH dose`],[`Somogyi effect`,`Low (under 70)`,`Rebound after nocturnal hypoglycaemia`,`Reduce evening insulin; bedtime snack`],[`Bedtime snack or missed dinner bolus`,`High from bedtime`,`Uncovered carbohydrate`,`Bolus for snack; correct at bedtime`]]},{heading:`Exercise adjustments (international consensus 2017)`,columns:[`Situation`,`Glucose before`,`Action`,`After exercise`],rows:[[`Starting glucose low`,`Under 90 mg/dL`,`10-20 g carbohydrate, delay start`,`Check every 30 min`],[`Ideal`,`126-180 mg/dL`,`Start; 30-60 g carbohydrate per hour if prolonged`,`Reduce bedtime basal about 20%`],[`High without ketones`,`Over 270, ketone under 0.6`,`Light exercise allowed; small correction`,`Watch late hypoglycaemia`],[`High with ketones`,`Ketone 1.5 or more`,`Do not exercise; give insulin`,`Recheck ketones`]]}],redFlags:[`**Vomiting with blood ketone 1.5 mmol/L or more** (or urine ketones 2+), abdominal pain or deep breathing - DKA, hospital now.`,`Ketones **3.0 mmol/L or more** at any glucose, including normal glucose on a pump or SGLT2 inhibitor.`,`**Severe hypoglycaemia** needing help, seizure, or recurrent hypoglycaemia without warning symptoms.`,`**Recurrent DKA** - think insulin omission, eating disorder, poverty or abuse.`,`**Growth failure or delayed puberty** in a child with type 1.`,`**Pregnancy** in a woman with type 1 - urgent joint antenatal care.`,`Unexplained **falling insulin needs** with weight loss or hypotension - exclude Addison disease or coeliac disease.`],pearls:[`**Never stop basal insulin** - not for vomiting, not for fasting, not for surgery.`,`**500 rule** for carb ratio, **1800 rule** for correction factor (rapid analogue).`,`Starting dose: **0.5 units/kg/day adult, 0.7-1.0 child, up to 1.5 in puberty**, half basal.`,`Ketones: **0.6, 1.5, 3.0** - raised, danger, hospital.`,`**Rule of 15**: 15 g sugar, wait 15 minutes, recheck.`,`High fasting glucose? **Check 3 a.m.** before increasing the dose (Somogyi vs dawn).`,`**Lipohypertrophy** is the hidden cause of erratic sugars - feel the injection sites.`,`Screen with **TSH and tTG-IgA** - thyroid and coeliac travel with type 1.`,`**DCCT** gave microvascular benefit; **EDIC** gave metabolic memory and CV benefit.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-q1`,stem:`A 16-year-old on basal-bolus insulin has a total daily dose of 50 units of which 25 units is glargine. Using the 500 and 1800 rules, what is the correct pre-lunch bolus for 70 g carbohydrate when glucose is 228 mg/dL and target is 120 mg/dL?`,options:[`7 units`,`10 units`,`12 units`,`5 units`],answer:1,explanation:`The insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio is 500/50 = 1 unit per 10 g, so 70 g needs 7 units. The correction factor is 1800/50 = 36 mg/dL per unit, and (228 - 120)/36 = 3 units. Total bolus is 10 units. Seven units covers food but ignores the high glucose; 12 units overcorrects; 5 units would leave glucose high.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-q2`,stem:`A 9-year-old with type 1 diabetes has fever and has vomited twice. He has not eaten since morning. Glucose is 260 mg/dL and blood beta-hydroxybutyrate is 1.8 mmol/L. His total daily dose is 30 units. What should the parents do?`,options:[`Stop all insulin until he can eat`,`Give his usual basal plus 3-6 units of rapid-acting insulin, fluids, and recheck in 2 hours`,`Give only his usual basal dose and wait until morning`,`Give 30 units of rapid-acting insulin at once`],answer:1,explanation:`With ketones of 1.5-2.9 mmol/L ISPAD advises an extra 10-20% of the total daily dose (3-6 units here) as rapid-acting insulin, continued basal, fluids and recheck of glucose and ketones in 2 hours, with hospital if ketones rise or vomiting persists. Stopping insulin causes DKA. Basal alone undertreats ketosis. A full day's dose as rapid insulin risks severe hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-q3`,stem:`A 14-year-old on NPH and regular insulin twice daily has fasting glucose of 230-260 mg/dL. A 3 a.m. check shows 52 mg/dL. What is the correct change?`,options:[`Increase the evening NPH dose by 2 units`,`Add metformin at bedtime`,`Reduce the evening NPH dose or move it to bedtime`,`Increase the morning regular insulin`],answer:2,explanation:`A low 3 a.m. reading followed by high fasting glucose suggests nocturnal hypoglycaemia caused by the evening NPH peak, with a rebound (Somogyi) rise. Reduce or move the evening NPH to bedtime, or switch to a long-acting analogue. Increasing evening NPH would deepen the nocturnal hypoglycaemia; metformin and morning regular insulin do not address the overnight problem.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes plans a 60-minute run 60 minutes after lunch. Her pre-lunch glucose is 150 mg/dL. Which advice is most appropriate?`,options:[`Reduce the lunch bolus by about 25-75% and carry fast carbohydrate`,`Take an extra correction dose before running to prevent a post-exercise rise`,`Skip her basal insulin that day`,`Avoid all exercise because type 1 diabetes contraindicates running`],answer:0,explanation:`Aerobic exercise increases insulin-independent glucose uptake, so the consensus advises reducing the pre-exercise meal bolus by 25-75% when exercise follows within 90 minutes, carrying carbohydrate and monitoring for late and nocturnal lows. Extra insulin before running risks hypoglycaemia. Basal must never be skipped. Exercise is encouraged in type 1, not contraindicated.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-q5`,stem:`Which statement about teplizumab is correct?`,options:[`It reverses established stage 3 type 1 diabetes and allows insulin withdrawal`,`It is a once-weekly oral drug for type 2 diabetes`,`It delays progression from stage 2 to stage 3 type 1 diabetes by a median of about 2-3 years`,`It is an SGLT2 inhibitor approved as an adjunct in type 1 diabetes`],answer:2,explanation:`Teplizumab is an anti-CD3 monoclonal antibody given as a 14-day intravenous course to people aged 8 and over with stage 2 (presymptomatic, dysglycaemic) type 1 diabetes; it delayed clinical diagnosis by a median of roughly 2-3 years. It does not cure established stage 3 disease, is not oral and is unrelated to SGLT2 inhibitors, which are not approved for type 1.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`A 10-year-old boy is newly diagnosed with type 1 diabetes without ketoacidosis. Outline his management, including insulin regimen, carbohydrate counting, monitoring, sick-day rules and school care.`,openingLines:[`Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune absolute insulin deficiency; management is lifelong physiological insulin replacement with education of the child and family as its foundation.`,`Goals (ISPAD 2022): HbA1c under 7%, time in range over 70%, normal growth and school life, no severe hypoglycaemia or DKA.`],answer:[{heading:`Initial assessment and education`,points:[`Confirm no DKA (ketones, pH); weight, height on growth chart, Tanner stage.`,`Baseline TSH, anti-TPO, tTG-IgA, lipids; family counselling - not their fault, insulin lifelong.`,`Teach injection, SMBG, hypoglycaemia recognition within 48 hours.`]},{heading:`Insulin regimen`,points:[`TDD 0.7-1.0 units/kg/day (about 25 units at 30 kg); 40-50% basal glargine once daily.`,`Rapid analogue before meals by ICR (500 rule) and ISF (1800 rule); twice-daily premixed if cost-limited.`,`4 mm needles, rotate sites, store at 2-8 C or in an earthen pot.`]},{heading:`Carbohydrate counting and diet`,points:[`Indian exchanges: chapati 15 g, katori rice 28-30 g, idli 15 g.`,`Normal family diet, regular meals, no 'diabetic' sweets; dietitian review.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`CGM from diagnosis (ADA 2026) or SMBG 4-6 times daily.`,`HbA1c 3-monthly; thyroid yearly; coeliac at 2 and 5 years; ACR and retina from 11 years or puberty after 2-5 years.`]},{heading:`Sick-day rules and hypoglycaemia`,points:[`Never stop basal; glucose 2-3 hourly; ketones if over 250.`,`Extra 5-10% TDD (ketone 0.6-1.4) or 10-20% (1.5 or more); hospital at 3.0 or persistent vomiting.`,`Hypoglycaemia: 15 g sugar, recheck in 15 min; glucagon 0.5-1 mg IM at home.`]},{heading:`School, exercise and follow-up`,points:[`School care plan, trained teacher, CBSE exam-hall permission.`,`Exercise: glucose 126-180 before, snack, reduce bolus.`,`3-monthly review; plan transition from age 12-14; psychosocial screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Insulin action profiles: basal-bolus (flat basal, meal peaks)`,`Sick-day rules flow chart by ketone level`],markSplit:[{part:`Education and initial assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Insulin regimen with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Carb counting and diet`,marks:1},{part:`Monitoring and targets`,marks:2},{part:`Sick-day rules and hypoglycaemia`,marks:2},{part:`School and follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`basal-bolus`,`500 rule`,`1800 rule`,`sick-day rules`,`ketones`,`school care plan`]},{id:`endocrine-t1dm-management-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Sick-day rules in type 1 diabetes.`,openingLines:[`Sick-day rules are the instructions that keep a person with type 1 diabetes out of DKA during intercurrent illness, when stress hormones raise insulin needs even if food intake falls.`,`The cardinal rule is never to stop insulin.`],answer:[{heading:`Rules`,points:[`Never stop basal insulin.`,`Glucose every 2-3 hours; ketones if glucose over 250 or vomiting.`,`Fluids hourly - sugar-containing if glucose under 180.`,`Treat the illness (fever, infection).`]},{heading:`Extra insulin by ketones`,points:[`Under 0.6 - usual corrections; 0.6-1.4 - 5-10% of TDD; 1.5-2.9 - 10-20% of TDD; repeat 2-3 hourly.`,`3.0 or more - hospital.`]},{heading:`When to go to hospital`,points:[`Persistent vomiting, rising ketones, abdominal pain, deep breathing, drowsiness, age under 2, carer unable to cope.`]}],mustDraw:[`Ketone-based sick-day flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Principle and rule 1`,marks:1},{part:`Monitoring and fluids`,marks:1},{part:`Extra insulin by ketones`,marks:2},{part:`When to seek help`,marks:1}],keywords:[`sick-day rules`,`beta-hydroxybutyrate`,`TDD`,`DKA prevention`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diabetes mellitus: management and therapies`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Sections 6, 7, 9 and 14`,`ISPAD Clinical Practice Consensus Guidelines 2022 (insulin treatment, sick-day management, glycaemic targets, exercise, school)`,`Holt RIG et al. The management of type 1 diabetes in adults: ADA/EASD consensus report, Diabetes Care 2021`,`ICMR Guidelines for Management of Type 1 Diabetes, Indian Council of Medical Research, 2022`,`Riddell MC et al. Exercise management in type 1 diabetes: a consensus statement, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2017`,`Herold KC et al. An anti-CD3 antibody, teplizumab, in relatives at risk for type 1 diabetes, N Engl J Med 2019`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`]}),e.push({id:`endocrine-cgm-technology`,title:`Glucose monitoring: SMBG, CGM, time in range and the AGP report`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Glucose monitoring** ranges from **self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG)** with a glucometer to **continuous glucose monitoring (CGM)** of interstitial glucose, summarised over 14 days in the **ambulatory glucose profile (AGP)** whose core targets are **time in range 70-180 mg/dL over 70%, time below 70 under 4% (below 54 under 1%), time above 180 under 25% and CV 36% or less**, with the **glucose management indicator (GMI)** estimating HbA1c.`,keywords:[`SMBG`,`glucometer`,`CGM`,`continuous glucose monitoring`,`flash glucose monitoring`,`isCGM`,`rtCGM`,`professional CGM`,`FreeStyle Libre`,`time in range`,`TIR`,`TBR`,`TAR`,`GMI`,`glucose management indicator`,`AGP`,`ambulatory glucose profile`,`coefficient of variation`,`automated insulin delivery`,`hybrid closed loop`,`fructosamine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology of use`,points:[`**SMBG** measures capillary glucose with a meter and test strip at a moment in time; **CGM** uses a subcutaneous enzyme sensor to read **interstitial glucose every 1-15 minutes**, giving trends, alarms and 24-hour patterns. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c** gives a 2-3 month average but no information on **hypoglycaemia, variability or timing** - two people with HbA1c 7% can have very different glucose lives. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Time in range (TIR)** is now accepted as a clinical end-point: each **10% rise in TIR** corresponds to about **0.5-0.8% fall in HbA1c**, and lower TIR predicts retinopathy and microalbuminuria. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`CGM use has transformed type 1 care in high-income countries, where most people with type 1 now use it; in India, use is growing but limited by **cost of sensors** and is concentrated in urban specialist clinics. [RSSDI 2022]`,`In India, the most widely used systems are **intermittently scanned (flash) CGM** and **professional (blinded) 14-day CGM** used in clinics to make treatment decisions. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Accuracy is expressed as **MARD** (mean absolute relative difference); current sensors achieve about **8-10%**, close to good glucometers. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Indications and risk factors that drive monitoring need`,points:[`**CGM is recommended from diagnosis** for children and adults with **type 1** and anyone on **insulin**, and should be offered to those on **sulfonylureas or glinides** (hypoglycaemia risk). [ADA 2026]`,`CGM **can be considered** in type 2 on non-hypoglycaemic agents to guide lifestyle and drug choices; **over-the-counter CGM** devices for non-insulin users were approved in the USA in 2024. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia unawareness**, recurrent or nocturnal hypoglycaemia, and **HbA1c-glucose discordance** are strong indications for CGM. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy with type 1** - CGM improves neonatal outcomes (CONCEPTT trial: fewer large-for-gestational-age babies and NICU admissions). [ADA 2026]`,`**Unreliable HbA1c** (CKD on dialysis, haemoglobinopathy, transfusion, pregnancy) makes CGM the preferred measure of glycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Occupations** where hypoglycaemia endangers others (drivers, machine operators) and those who live alone need frequent monitoring or CGM alarms. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Mechanism: how meters and sensors work`,points:[`Meters and most sensors use **glucose oxidase or glucose dehydrogenase** to generate an electrical current proportional to glucose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Meter standard (ISO 15197:2013)**: 95% of readings within **15 mg/dL when glucose is under 100**, and within **15%** when 100 or more. [ISO 15197:2013]`,`CGM reads **interstitial fluid**, which lags blood glucose by about **5-15 minutes** - so during rapid change (after meals, exercise, hypoglycaemia treatment) sensor and finger-prick values differ. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Compression lows**: lying on the sensor at night reduces local blood flow and gives a falsely low reading. [ADA 2026]`,`**Interfering substances**: **high-dose vitamin C** (falsely high on some sensors), **paracetamol over about 1 g every 6 hours** and **hydroxyurea** (falsely high on some sensors) - check the device label. [ADA 2026]`,`**GMI** is calculated from CGM mean glucose: **GMI (%) = 3.31 + 0.02392 x mean glucose (mg/dL)**; mean 154 mg/dL gives GMI about 7%. [Bergenstal 2018]`,`**HbA1c and GMI differ** in many people because of red-cell lifespan and **glycation rate (glycation gap)** - a difference of 0.5% or more is common. [Bergenstal 2018]`]},{heading:`Classification of devices and CGM metrics`,points:[`**SMBG** with a glucometer: cheapest, needs finger-pricks, gives snapshots only. [ADA 2026]`,`**Real-time CGM (rtCGM)**: sends glucose to a reader or phone automatically every few minutes, with **high and low alarms** and trend arrows. [ADA 2026]`,`**Intermittently scanned CGM (isCGM, flash)**: newer versions also stream and alarm; older ones give data only when scanned. [ADA 2026]`,`**Professional (blinded) CGM**: worn for 10-14 days, data downloaded by the clinic - useful for diagnosis of patterns and widely used in India. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Implantable CGM** lasts months but needs a minor procedure; **automated insulin delivery (AID)** links a CGM to a pump via an algorithm. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia levels**: **level 1** 54-69 mg/dL; **level 2** under 54 mg/dL (clinically significant); **level 3** severe event needing help regardless of number. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Hyperglycaemia levels**: **level 1** 181-250 mg/dL; **level 2** over 250 mg/dL. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Glycaemic variability**: coefficient of variation (**CV = SD / mean x 100**) target **36% or less**; above this, hypoglycaemia risk rises sharply. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: what the patient reports and what the data show`,points:[`Ask about **symptoms that do not match readings** - sweating at a normal reading (lag or rapid fall) or no symptoms at 50 mg/dL (unawareness). [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **alarm fatigue** - frequent alarms lead people to switch them off; reset thresholds sensibly. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask **when and how often** the person tests or scans, and what they do with the result - monitoring without action does not lower HbA1c. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **skin reactions** to adhesive, sensor failures, and early sensor loss in hot, humid Indian summers or with sweating labourers. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **cost and supply** - many Indian patients ration strips or wear a sensor only intermittently. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Ask about **food, exercise, alcohol and sleep around each pattern** - the AGP is interpreted with the patient's diary. [AGP Report 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination relevant to monitoring`,points:[`Inspect **finger-tips** for callosities and soreness; teach side-of-finger pricking and site rotation. [ADA 2026]`,`Inspect the **sensor site** (back of upper arm or abdomen) for **contact dermatitis**, infection or bleeding. [ADA 2026]`,`Check **injection and sensor sites for lipohypertrophy** - sensors on lipohypertrophy read unreliably and insulin absorbs erratically. [FITTER Forward 2024]`,`Check **vision and hand dexterity** in the elderly - choose meters with large displays or CGM. [ADA 2026]`,`Look for **pallor** (anaemia) and features of CKD that make HbA1c unreliable and CGM more useful. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: when HbA1c and glucose readings disagree`,points:[`**HbA1c higher than GMI/SMBG** suggests iron deficiency, B12 deficiency, asplenia, a high glycation rate, or **poor-quality or missed monitoring** (testing only when fasting). [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c lower than GMI/SMBG** suggests haemolysis, recent bleeding or transfusion, CKD with ESA, pregnancy, G6PD deficiency or a haemoglobin variant. [ADA 2026]`,`**Faulty SMBG technique**: unwashed fingers (fruit sugar gives falsely high), expired or heat-damaged strips, wrong code, and altitude or haematocrit effects. [ADA 2026]`,`**Sensor error** vs real hypoglycaemia: confirm low CGM values with a **finger-prick** if symptoms do not match or before treating at night when compression is likely. [ADA 2026]`,`Use **fructosamine** (2-3 week average) or **glycated albumin** when HbA1c is unreliable; both are affected by low albumin and nephrotic syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: SMBG schedules and CGM targets`,points:[`**Type 1 or intensive insulin without CGM**: SMBG **6-10 times a day** - before meals and snacks, at bedtime, before exercise and driving, when hypoglycaemia is suspected. [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 2 on basal insulin**: daily **fasting** SMBG to titrate the dose, plus checks when symptomatic. [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 2 on non-hypoglycaemic drugs**: routine SMBG adds little; a **structured profile** (7-point or paired pre- and 2-hour post-meal) for a few days before visits is more useful. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Core CGM targets (type 1 and type 2)**: **TIR 70-180 over 70%**, **TBR under 70 below 4%**, **under 54 below 1%**, **TAR over 180 below 25%**, **over 250 below 5%**, **CV 36% or less**. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Older or high-risk people**: **TIR over 50%**, **TBR under 70 below 1%**, TAR over 250 below 10% - safety first. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Pregnancy with type 1**: TIR **63-140 mg/dL over 70%**, below 63 under 4%, below 54 under 1%, above 140 under 25%. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**Data sufficiency**: at least **14 days** with at least **70% sensor wear** (about 10 days) before drawing conclusions. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**TIR 70%** corresponds roughly to **HbA1c 7%**, and **TIR 50%** to about **HbA1c 8%**. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: reading the AGP report step by step`,points:[`**Step 1 - data sufficiency**: 14 days, 70% wear; if less, interpret with caution. [AGP Report 2023]`,`**Step 2 - look at TBR first**: if time below 70 is **4% or more** or below 54 is **1% or more**, fix hypoglycaemia before anything else. [AGP Report 2023]`,`**Step 3 - TIR and TAR**: if TIR is under 70%, find **when** glucose is high - overnight, fasting, or after a particular meal. [AGP Report 2023]`,`**Step 4 - read the curve**: the median line shows the average day, the **25-75% band** shows day-to-day consistency, and the **5-95% band** shows extremes; a wide band means inconsistency in food, insulin timing or activity. [AGP Report 2023]`,`**Overnight fall or night lows**: reduce **basal insulin by 10-20%** or the evening sulfonylurea; a rise after 3 a.m. suggests the dawn phenomenon. [ADA 2026]`,`**Post-meal spikes** with return to baseline: inject the rapid analogue **15-20 minutes before eating**, increase that meal's carb ratio, or reduce the carbohydrate load. [ADA 2026]`,`**Late post-meal lows** (3-5 hours): reduce that bolus, or switch from regular insulin to an analogue. [ADA 2026]`,`**Agree one or two changes** per visit, write them down, and review the next AGP in **2-4 weeks**. [AGP Report 2023]`,`Use **trend arrows** to adjust a meal bolus: a rising arrow before a meal may need a little more insulin, a falling arrow a little less (device-specific charts). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Automated insulin delivery and in-hospital monitoring`,points:[`**AID (hybrid closed loop)** adjusts basal insulin every 5 minutes from CGM data; the user still announces meals and boluses for carbohydrate. [ADA 2026]`,`AID raises TIR by about **10-15 percentage points** (roughly 2.5-3.5 hours a day) and lowers TBR, with the biggest gains overnight. [ADA 2026]`,`**AID failure**: an occluded cannula or dislodged set causes **DKA within hours** - teach users to check ketones and switch to pen injections if glucose stays over 250 mg/dL. [ADA 2026]`,`**In hospital**, patients may continue personal CGM if alert and capable, with **point-of-care glucose confirmation** for treatment decisions and hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`Remove CGM and pumps before **MRI, CT and diathermy**, and reapply afterwards. [ADA 2026]`,`In ICU, **arterial or venous** glucose is preferred over capillary readings in shock, oedema or vasopressor use, which make finger-prick values unreliable. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: CGM from diagnosis, with alerts shared to parents' phones; it reduces fear of night hypoglycaemia. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: CGM is recommended in type 1; HbA1c is a secondary measure because it falls in pregnancy. [ADA 2026]`,`**Elderly**: CGM reduces hypoglycaemia; aim TIR over 50% with TBR under 1%. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: HbA1c unreliable - use CGM; accuracy is acceptable in most dialysis patients. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Ramadan and other fasts**: CGM shows safe windows and when to break the fast. [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a **structured SMBG profile** for 3 days before review, or one **professional 14-day CGM** at diagnosis and when treatment is changed, gives most of the information for a fraction of continuous use cost. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and limitations`,points:[`**Skin reactions** to adhesive (acrylates) occur in a minority and may force a change of device. [ADA 2026]`,`**Alarm fatigue and anxiety** - some people check excessively; set alerts to clinically useful thresholds. [ADA 2026]`,`**Over-correction (stacking)**: treating a high reading again before the first correction has acted causes hypoglycaemia; wait 2-3 hours between corrections. [ADA 2026]`,`**Cost** limits continuous CGM in India; intermittent use still gives valuable pattern information. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**SMBG without structure** (random readings, no action) wastes strips and does not lower HbA1c in non-insulin type 2. [ADA 2026]`,`**Data overload** in clinic - use the one-page AGP and the TIR bar, not raw tracings. [AGP Report 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring schedule`,points:[`Review CGM data **at every visit** and remotely between visits where possible. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c**: every 3 months when changing therapy or not at target; every **6 months** when stable at target. [ADA 2026]`,`Refer for **AID or pump** assessment when type 1 TIR stays under 70% or hypoglycaemia persists despite MDI and CGM. [ADA 2026]`,`Re-teach **meter technique and strip storage** yearly - hot Indian summers damage strips. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Check **meter accuracy** against a laboratory venous glucose yearly or when readings seem odd. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Counselling and programme link`,points:[`Teach **hand-washing before every finger-prick**, use of the side of the fingertip, and disposal of lancets in a puncture-proof container. [ADA 2026]`,`Teach the patient to **read their own AGP**: green bar (TIR) up, red bars (TBR) down. [AGP Report 2023]`,`Advise **finger-prick confirmation** before treating symptoms that disagree with the sensor and before driving if the sensor shows under 90 mg/dL or a falling arrow. [ADA 2026]`,`Under **NP-NCD**, glucometers and strips are supplied at health facilities for screening; home supplies are not universal, so prioritise strips for **insulin users**. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Some state programmes for children with type 1 (for example **Kerala Mittayi**) supply meters and strips free. [ICMR T1D Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA 2026**: **CGM at diagnosis** for anyone on insulin or a hypoglycaemia-causing drug; CGM may be considered in type 2 on other agents. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025-2026**: **AID recommended for all with type 1** and to be considered in **insulin-treated type 2**; AID systems approved for type 2 in the USA in 2024-2025. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025-2026**: patients may continue personal CGM in hospital with point-of-care confirmation. [ADA 2026]`,`**AGP Report (2023 update)** standardises the one-page report with the stacked TIR bar showing five ranges. [AGP Report 2023]`,`**Over-the-counter CGM** for people not on insulin (USA, 2024) - adds consumer data that clinicians are increasingly asked to interpret. [ADA 2026]`,`Some guidelines now accept **TIR as a co-primary outcome** alongside HbA1c in trials. [International TIR Consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: "**What are the TIR targets?**" - **70-180 over 70%, under 70 below 4%, under 54 below 1%, over 180 below 25%, over 250 below 5%, CV 36% or less**.`,`Viva: "**What is GMI?**" - estimated HbA1c from CGM mean glucose (3.31 + 0.02392 x mean); needs 14 days.`,`Viva: "**How do you read an AGP?**" - data sufficiency, hypoglycaemia first, then hyperglycaemia by time of day, then variability.`,`Common mistake: **treating a high fasting glucose** from the AGP by increasing basal when the curve shows **overnight lows**.`,`Common mistake: assuming CGM and finger-prick should match exactly - **lag of 5-15 minutes** is normal.`,`Remember **"70-4-1-25-5"** for the core targets and **"14 days, 70% wear"** for sufficiency.`]}],tables:[{heading:`International consensus CGM targets (2019)`,columns:[`Group`,`Time in range`,`Time below range`,`Time above range`],rows:[[`Type 1 and type 2 adults`,`70-180 mg/dL over 70%`,`Under 70 below 4%; under 54 below 1%`,`Over 180 below 25%; over 250 below 5%`],[`Older / high-risk`,`70-180 over 50%`,`Under 70 below 1%`,`Over 250 below 10%`],[`Pregnancy with type 1`,`63-140 over 70%`,`Under 63 below 4%; under 54 below 1%`,`Over 140 below 25%`],[`Variability (all)`,`CV 36% or less`,`-`,`-`]]},{heading:`Monitoring methods compared`,columns:[`Method`,`What it measures`,`Strengths`,`Limitations`],rows:[[`SMBG (glucometer)`,`Capillary glucose at one moment`,`Cheap, widely available, confirms sensor readings`,`Finger-pricks, snapshots, technique errors`],[`Flash / intermittently scanned CGM`,`Interstitial glucose, 14-day sensor`,`Trend arrows, AGP, newer models alarm`,`Cost, lag, adhesive reactions`],[`Real-time CGM`,`Interstitial glucose streamed every few minutes`,`Alarms, data sharing, links to AID`,`Highest cost, alarm fatigue`],[`Professional (blinded) CGM`,`14-day retrospective record`,`Unbiased patterns for clinic decisions`,`No real-time feedback`],[`HbA1c`,`2-3 month average`,`Standardised, predicts complications`,`No hypoglycaemia or variability data; red-cell artefacts`],[`Fructosamine / glycated albumin`,`2-3 week average`,`Useful when HbA1c unreliable`,`Affected by low albumin, nephrotic syndrome`]]},{heading:`AGP patterns and what to change`,columns:[`AGP pattern`,`Likely cause`,`Action`,`Recheck`],rows:[[`Overnight lows (TBR at 1-4 a.m.)`,`Too much basal or evening SU/NPH`,`Reduce basal 10-20%; move NPH; stop evening SU`,`AGP in 2 weeks`],[`Fasting high, flat night`,`Too little basal`,`Increase basal 10%`,`Fasting values 3 days`],[`Rise after 3-4 a.m.`,`Dawn phenomenon`,`Later NPH, degludec, or pump basal rise`,`AGP`],[`Spike after one meal, returns to baseline`,`Bolus timing or too many carbs`,`Inject 15-20 min earlier; adjust carb ratio`,`Post-meal readings`],[`Low 3-5 h after meal`,`Bolus too large or regular insulin tail`,`Reduce bolus; switch to analogue`,`AGP`],[`Wide 5-95% band, CV over 36%`,`Inconsistent food, timing, lipohypertrophy`,`Education, site rotation, consider AID`,`AGP`]]},{heading:`Drugs and doses adjusted from monitoring data`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Basal insulin (glargine, degludec)`,`SC once daily`,`Change 10-20% by overnight and fasting pattern every 3 days`,`Hypoglycaemia; nocturnal lows signal excess`],[`Rapid-acting insulin (aspart, lispro)`,`SC before meals by carb ratio and correction factor`,`Adjust ratio by post-meal pattern every 1-2 weeks`,`Stacking corrections causes lows`],[`Sulfonylurea (gliclazide MR)`,`30-120 mg orally once daily`,`Reduce or stop if TBR 4% or more`,`Hypoglycaemia, weight gain`],[`Oral glucose for level 1-2 hypoglycaemia`,`15 g (3 teaspoons sugar or 150 mL juice) orally`,`Recheck in 15 min; repeat if under 70`,`Over-treatment rebound highs`],[`Glucagon for level 3 hypoglycaemia`,`1 mg IM/SC (0.5 mg if under 25 kg) or 3 mg nasal`,`Single dose; call for help`,`Nausea, vomiting`]]},{heading:`Recommended SMBG frequency`,columns:[`Patient`,`Frequency`,`When`,`Purpose`],rows:[[`Type 1 or MDI without CGM`,`6-10 times daily`,`Before meals, bedtime, exercise, driving, symptoms`,`Dose each meal; catch lows`],[`Type 2 on basal insulin`,`Once daily plus symptoms`,`Fasting`,`Basal titration`],[`Type 2 on SU`,`Few times a week plus symptoms`,`Fasting, before driving, symptoms`,`Detect hypoglycaemia`],[`Type 2 on non-hypoglycaemic drugs`,`Structured profile before visits`,`7-point or paired pre/post meal`,`Guide therapy change`],[`Pregnancy`,`4-7 times daily`,`Fasting and 1 or 2 h post-meal`,`Fasting under 95, 1 h under 140, 2 h under 120`]]}],redFlags:[`**TBR under 54 mg/dL of 1% or more**, or any level 3 (severe) hypoglycaemia - urgent therapy change.`,`CGM shows glucose **persistently over 250 mg/dL on a pump** - possible set failure; check ketones and switch to pen.`,`Recurrent **nocturnal lows with no symptoms** - hypoglycaemia unawareness.`,`Readings that do not match symptoms in a person **about to drive** - confirm by finger-prick, do not drive.`,`HbA1c and GMI differ widely with **unexplained anaemia** - investigate the anaemia (haemolysis, bleeding).`,`Sensor site **cellulitis or abscess**.`],pearls:[`**70-4-1-25-5**: TIR over 70%, under 70 below 4%, under 54 below 1%, over 180 below 25%, over 250 below 5%.`,`**CV 36% or less** = stable glucose.`,`**14 days, 70% wear** before trusting an AGP.`,`Read the AGP **lows first**, then highs, then variability.`,`**TIR 70% is about HbA1c 7%**; each 10% TIR is about 0.5-0.8% HbA1c.`,`**GMI = 3.31 + 0.02392 x mean glucose (mg/dL)**.`,`Interstitial glucose **lags 5-15 minutes** - confirm lows by finger-prick.`,`Elderly: **TIR over 50%, TBR under 1%** - safety over tightness.`,`Pregnancy type 1: **63-140 over 70%**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-q1`,stem:`According to the International Consensus on Time in Range, which set of targets is correct for most adults with type 1 or type 2 diabetes?`,options:[`TIR 70-180 mg/dL over 50%, time below 70 under 10%`,`TIR 70-180 mg/dL over 70%, time below 70 under 4%, time below 54 under 1%`,`TIR 63-140 mg/dL over 70%, time below 63 under 4%`,`TIR 80-130 mg/dL over 90%, time below 70 under 1%`],answer:1,explanation:`The core targets are TIR 70-180 mg/dL above 70%, time below 70 under 4% and below 54 under 1%, with time above 180 under 25%. TIR over 50% is the relaxed target for older or high-risk people (and they need TBR under 1%, not 10%). 63-140 mg/dL is the pregnancy range in type 1. The 80-130 range is a pre-meal SMBG target, not a CGM consensus metric.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-q2`,stem:`A 14-day CGM (90% wear) in a man on basal-bolus insulin shows TIR 58%, time below 70 of 7% clustered between 1 and 4 a.m., and high fasting values around 7 a.m. HbA1c is 7.6%. What is the first change?`,options:[`Increase the basal insulin by 20%`,`Reduce the basal insulin by 10-20% and recheck the AGP`,`Increase the breakfast bolus`,`Add a sulfonylurea at bedtime`],answer:1,explanation:`The AGP is read hypoglycaemia-first: TBR of 7% is well above the 4% limit and is clustered overnight, which points to excess basal insulin, possibly with rebound morning highs. Reducing basal by 10-20% is the first step. Increasing basal or adding a bedtime sulfonylurea would worsen nocturnal hypoglycaemia; the breakfast bolus does not explain overnight lows.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-q3`,stem:`A woman with type 2 diabetes has HbA1c 8.4%, but her 14-day CGM mean glucose is 140 mg/dL (GMI about 6.7%). Haemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL with low MCV and low ferritin. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Iron deficiency falsely raising HbA1c`,`Sensor interference from vitamin C`,`Haemolysis falsely raising HbA1c`,`Insulin resistance making GMI unreliable`],answer:0,explanation:`Iron deficiency prolongs red-cell age and raises HbA1c above what mean glucose predicts, so HbA1c exceeds GMI. Haemolysis does the opposite - it lowers HbA1c. Vitamin C interference raises sensor readings, which would make GMI higher, not lower. Insulin resistance does not affect the relationship between mean glucose and GMI.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-q4`,stem:`What is the minimum CGM data needed for a reliable AGP interpretation according to the international consensus?`,options:[`3 days with 50% wear`,`7 days with any wear time`,`14 days with at least 70% sensor wear`,`90 days with 100% wear`],answer:2,explanation:`The consensus requires 14 days of data with at least 70% sensor wear (about 10 days) to give metrics that correlate with 3-month glucose patterns. Three or seven days miss weekday-weekend and day-to-day variation. Ninety days of complete wear is unnecessary and unrealistic.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-q5`,stem:`A 70-year-old with type 2 diabetes, CKD stage 4 and a past fall from hypoglycaemia uses a CGM. Which target set is most appropriate?`,options:[`TIR over 70% and HbA1c under 6.5%`,`TIR 63-140 mg/dL over 70%`,`No targets, because CGM is inaccurate in CKD`,`TIR over 50% with time below 70 under 1%`],answer:3,explanation:`For older or high-risk people (falls, CKD, hypoglycaemia history) the consensus relaxes TIR to over 50% but tightens time below 70 to under 1%, putting safety first. TIR over 70% with HbA1c under 6.5% risks hypoglycaemia; 63-140 mg/dL is the pregnancy range; CGM remains useful in CKD, where HbA1c is less reliable.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the methods of glucose monitoring in diabetes. Discuss continuous glucose monitoring, time-in-range targets and how you would interpret an ambulatory glucose profile (AGP) report.`,openingLines:[`Glucose monitoring ranges from SMBG and HbA1c to continuous glucose monitoring of interstitial glucose, which reveals hypoglycaemia, variability and daily patterns hidden by HbA1c.`,`The International Consensus (2019) set time in range 70-180 mg/dL over 70% as the core CGM target, reported on the one-page AGP.`],answer:[{heading:`Methods of monitoring`,points:[`HbA1c (2-3 months), fructosamine/glycated albumin (2-3 weeks), SMBG, CGM (rtCGM, flash, professional), AID.`,`Meter standard ISO 15197:2013 - within 15 mg/dL or 15%.`]},{heading:`CGM: principle, indications, limitations`,points:[`Glucose oxidase sensor in interstitial fluid, lag 5-15 minutes, MARD 8-10%.`,`Indications (ADA 2026): type 1 from diagnosis, any insulin or SU user, unawareness, pregnancy, unreliable HbA1c.`,`Limitations: cost, lag, compression lows, vitamin C/paracetamol/hydroxyurea interference, adhesive reactions.`]},{heading:`CGM metrics and targets`,points:[`TIR 70-180 over 70%; TBR under 70 below 4%, under 54 below 1%; TAR over 180 below 25%, over 250 below 5%; CV 36% or less.`,`Older/high-risk TIR over 50%, TBR under 1%; pregnancy type 1 TIR 63-140 over 70%.`,`GMI = 3.31 + 0.02392 x mean glucose; TIR 70% about HbA1c 7%.`]},{heading:`Interpreting the AGP`,points:[`Data sufficiency: 14 days, 70% wear.`,`Hypoglycaemia first - TBR and its timing.`,`Hyperglycaemia by time of day - overnight, fasting, post-meal.`,`Variability - IQR band, CV; then agree 1-2 changes.`]},{heading:`Actions from patterns`,points:[`Night lows - reduce basal 10-20% or evening SU.`,`Post-meal spikes - pre-bolus 15-20 minutes, adjust carb ratio.`,`Wide variability - education, site rotation, consider AID.`]},{heading:`Indian context`,points:[`Cost limits continuous use - professional 14-day CGM at diagnosis and therapy change; structured SMBG.`,`Hot climate damages strips; NP-NCD prioritises supplies for insulin users.`]}],mustDraw:[`Sketch of an AGP: median line with 25-75% and 5-95% bands over 24 hours`,`Stacked TIR bar with the five ranges and targets`],markSplit:[{part:`Methods of monitoring`,marks:2},{part:`CGM principle and indications`,marks:2},{part:`Targets and GMI`,marks:3},{part:`AGP interpretation and actions`,marks:3}],keywords:[`CGM`,`time in range`,`AGP`,`GMI`,`TBR`,`coefficient of variation`]},{id:`endocrine-cgm-technology-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate HbA1c from CGM-derived metrics (time in range and GMI) in the monitoring of diabetes.`,openingLines:[`HbA1c is a laboratory marker of average glycaemia over about 3 months; CGM metrics describe the full 24-hour glucose profile over 14 days.`,`They complement each other: HbA1c predicts complications in trials, CGM shows hypoglycaemia, variability and timing.`],answer:[{heading:`Points of difference`,points:[`Period: HbA1c 8-12 weeks vs CGM 14 days.`,`Hypoglycaemia: not shown by HbA1c vs TBR quantified by CGM.`,`Variability: absent vs CV and AGP bands.`,`Interference: red-cell lifespan and variants vs sensor lag, compression, drugs.`,`Cost: low vs high; availability universal vs limited in India.`]},{heading:`Linking the two`,points:[`GMI estimates HbA1c from mean glucose; discordance of 0.5% or more is common.`,`TIR 70% is about HbA1c 7%; a large gap suggests anaemia, haemolysis or glycation differences.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Differences (5 points)`,marks:3},{part:`GMI and discordance`,marks:1}],keywords:[`HbA1c`,`GMI`,`time in range`,`discordance`]}],references:[`Battelino T et al. Clinical targets for continuous glucose monitoring data interpretation: recommendations from the International Consensus on Time in Range, Diabetes Care 2019`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 7: Diabetes Technology`,`Bergenstal RM et al. Glucose Management Indicator (GMI): a new term for estimating A1C from CGM, Diabetes Care 2018`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diabetes mellitus: management`,`ISPAD Clinical Practice Consensus Guidelines 2022: Diabetes technologies - glucose monitoring`,`RSSDI-ESI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus 2022`,`International Diabetes Center. Ambulatory Glucose Profile (AGP) Report, version 5, 2023`]}),e.push({id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention`,title:`Prediabetes and prevention of type 2 diabetes`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Prediabetes** (intermediate hyperglycaemia) is glucose above normal but below the diabetes threshold - **IFG 100-125 mg/dL, IGT 140-199 mg/dL at 2 hours or HbA1c 5.7-6.4%** - that progresses to diabetes in about **5-10% a year** and raises cardiovascular risk; **lifestyle change (7% weight loss, 150 minutes a week of activity)** cuts progression by about **58% (DPP) and 28.5% in Indians (IDPP-1)**, with **metformin** for selected high-risk people.`,keywords:[`prediabetes`,`intermediate hyperglycaemia`,`IFG`,`IGT`,`impaired fasting glucose`,`impaired glucose tolerance`,`IDRS`,`Indian Diabetes Risk Score`,`CBAC`,`DPP`,`Diabetes Prevention Program`,`IDPP`,`Da Qing`,`Finnish DPS`,`metformin prevention`,`lifestyle modification`,`yoga`,`NP-NCD`,`diabetes remission`,`prediabetes remission`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Prediabetes** is a state of **intermediate hyperglycaemia** - impaired fasting glucose (IFG), impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) or a raised HbA1c - that is a **risk factor**, not a disease label, for diabetes and cardiovascular disease. [ADA 2026]`,`Globally about **635 million adults have IGT** and about **488 million have IFG**, most in low- and middle-income countries. [IDF Atlas 2025]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB** found prediabetes in **15.3%** of Indian adults (about **136 million**) - more people than have diabetes (11.4%) - with the ratio of prediabetes to diabetes highest in **less-developed states**, signalling the next wave. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Progression to diabetes is about **5-10% a year**, and up to **70%** of people with prediabetes eventually develop diabetes; risk is highest with **combined IFG plus IGT**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Asian Indians progress faster** and at younger age and lower BMI than Europeans - the control arm of IDPP-1 reached a **3-year cumulative incidence of 55%**. [IDPP-1 2006]`,`About **5-10% of people with prediabetes revert to normal** each year, especially with weight loss - the basis of the "prediabetes remission" concept. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Non-modifiable**: age **over 30-35**, family history in a first-degree relative, South Asian ethnicity, past **gestational diabetes**, low birth weight (thin-fat baby). [ADA 2026]`,`**Modifiable**: **central obesity** (waist **90 cm or more in men, 80 cm or more in women** in Indians), overweight (**BMI 23 or more**), physical inactivity, refined-carbohydrate diet, sugary drinks, poor sleep and **tobacco**. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Associated conditions**: hypertension, dyslipidaemia (low HDL, high triglycerides), **PCOS**, **MASLD (fatty liver)**, acanthosis nigricans, obstructive sleep apnoea and HIV. [ADA 2026]`,`**Drugs**: glucocorticoids, atypical antipsychotics, thiazides, beta-blockers and statins (small rise in risk, outweighed by CV benefit). [ADA 2026]`,`**Developmental origins (DOHaD)**: maternal undernutrition followed by childhood catch-up growth and urban overnutrition programmes the Indian **thin-fat phenotype** (Pune Maternal Nutrition Study). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Urbanisation**: ICMR-INDIAB shows the highest prediabetes rates in urban and more-developed states, but rural rates are rising fast. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: IFG and IGT are different defects`,points:[`**IFG** reflects **hepatic insulin resistance** and loss of **first-phase insulin secretion** - the liver fails to suppress overnight glucose output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IGT** reflects **muscle (peripheral) insulin resistance** and impaired **late-phase secretion** - glucose taken in at a meal is cleared slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Combined IFG plus IGT** has both defects and roughly **double the progression risk** of either alone. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Beta-cell function is already reduced by **about 50%** in people with IGT at the upper end of the range - prevention is a race to protect the remaining beta cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ectopic fat** in liver and pancreas (the "twin cycle" hypothesis) drives both insulin resistance and beta-cell dysfunction; losing about **10-15 kg** removes this fat and can restore normal glucose. [DiRECT 2018]`,`**Asian Indians** have more visceral and liver fat and lower muscle mass at the same BMI, and **post-load (IGT) hyperglycaemia predominates** - so fasting glucose alone misses many. [RSSDI 2022]`,`IGT carries **cardiovascular risk** through post-prandial spikes, endothelial dysfunction and the accompanying metabolic syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**ADA**: IFG **100-125 mg/dL**, IGT **140-199 mg/dL** 2 hours after 75 g glucose, or **HbA1c 5.7-6.4%** - any one defines prediabetes. [ADA 2026]`,`**WHO** defines IFG as **110-125 mg/dL** and does not endorse HbA1c for prediabetes; the WHO term is **intermediate hyperglycaemia**. [WHO 2006]`,`**International Expert Committee / NICE**: HbA1c **6.0-6.4% (42-47 mmol/mol)** identifies the highest-risk group. [NICE PH38]`,`**IDF 2024**: a **1-hour post-load glucose of 155 mg/dL or more** identifies intermediate hyperglycaemia earlier and more sensitively than the 2-hour value. [IDF 2024]`,`**Isolated IFG, isolated IGT and combined IFG/IGT** are the three glucose categories; HbA1c and glucose tests identify **different, partly overlapping people**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Risk stratification** within prediabetes: HbA1c 6.0-6.4%, combined IFG and IGT, BMI over 35, age under 60, past GDM and metabolic syndrome mark the **highest risk** (and the best metformin responders). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history`,points:[`Prediabetes is **asymptomatic** - it is found by **screening** or incidentally on a test done for another reason. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **family history**, past **GDM or a baby over 4 kg**, PCOS, hypertension, and early heart disease in the family. [ICMR 2018]`,`Take a **24-hour diet recall** (rice/roti portions, sweets, sugary tea, fried snacks, eating out, late dinners) and a **physical activity** history (steps, occupation, screen time). [ICMR 2018]`,`Ask about **sleep duration and snoring** (OSA), stress, **tobacco and alcohol**. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **drugs** - steroids (including self-medication and Ayurvedic preparations with hidden steroids), antipsychotics, thiazides. [ADA 2026]`,`Assess **readiness to change** (stages of change) - prevention depends on behaviour, not prescriptions. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Weight, height, BMI** with Asian cut-offs: normal **18.5-22.9**, overweight **23-24.9**, obese **25 or more**. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Waist circumference** midway between the costal margin and iliac crest: **90 cm or more in men, 80 cm or more in women** = abdominal obesity in Indians. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** (neck, axillae) and **skin tags** indicate hyperinsulinaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood pressure** - hypertension coexists in about a third. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Hirsutism, acne** in women (PCOS); **hepatomegaly** (fatty liver). [ADA 2026]`,`Look for **Cushingoid features** if the history suggests steroid use. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Early diabetes** - repeat or add an OGTT when values are near cut-offs; a single FPG of 120 can coexist with a 2-hour value over 200. [ADA 2026]`,`**Stress hyperglycaemia** during acute illness - re-test after recovery. [ADA 2026]`,`**Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia** - typically normal fasting with high afternoon glucose (see the Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**GCK-MODY** - lifelong stable fasting glucose 99-144 mg/dL in a young, lean person with a similar parent; needs no treatment (see the Classification topic). [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Spurious results**: falsely low glucose from **delayed sample processing** (glycolysis), and falsely high HbA1c from **iron deficiency**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Early pancreatic disease** (chronic pancreatitis, FCPD) in a lean person with abdominal pain. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations: screening and confirmation`,points:[`**Who to screen (ADA)**: all adults from **age 35**; any adult with **BMI 25 or more (23 or more in Asians)** plus one risk factor; women with past GDM **every 1-3 years lifelong**; people with HIV; children after puberty or age 10 if overweight with risk factors. [ADA 2026]`,`**NP-NCD India**: everyone **30 and over** is screened at Ayushman Arogya Mandir using the **CBAC form** and a **random capillary glucose**; 140 mg/dL or more is referred for confirmation. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`**IDRS (Indian Diabetes Risk Score, MDRF)**: age, waist, physical activity and family history give a score out of 100; **60 or more = high risk** (test now), **30-50 = moderate**, **under 30 = low**. [Mohan 2005]`,`**75 g OGTT** is the most sensitive test in Indians because **isolated IGT** is common; fasting glucose and HbA1c miss many. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**HbA1c** is convenient but must be interpreted after checking **haemoglobin** (iron deficiency raises it). [ADA 2026]`,`**Baseline work-up once prediabetes is found**: lipid profile, BP, ALT and ultrasound for fatty liver if raised, TSH if symptomatic, and a **10-year CV risk** estimate. [ADA 2026]`,`**Repeat testing at least yearly** in people with prediabetes, and every **3 years** in people with normal results. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: lifestyle - the first-line treatment`,points:[`**Weight loss target**: at least **7% of body weight** (DPP), maintained; **5-10%** gives most of the benefit, and more weight loss gives more prevention. [ADA 2026]`,`**Physical activity**: at least **150 minutes a week of moderate activity** (brisk walking) over at least 3 days, plus **resistance training 2-3 times a week**, and break sitting **every 30 minutes**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diet (ICMR-NIN 2024)**: cereals to **no more than about 45% of energy**, more **pulses, vegetables, whole grains and millets**, **added sugar under 5% of energy**, limit fried and ultra-processed food, **salt under 5 g a day**. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`Use the **Indian plate method**: half the plate vegetables, a quarter protein (dal, paneer, egg, fish), a quarter cereal - and swap white rice for smaller portions or whole grains. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Stop smoking** - smoking itself increases diabetes risk; cessation may cause short-term weight gain that should be managed, not feared. [ADA 2026]`,`**Sleep 7-8 hours** and treat OSA; manage stress. [ADA 2026]`,`**Yoga**: an Indian multicentre cluster-randomised trial of a **yoga-based lifestyle protocol** (2021) reported a substantial reduction in progression from prediabetes; yoga is a culturally accepted way to meet activity targets. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Structured programmes** work better than advice alone - group sessions, a coach, and follow-up for at least 6-12 months; **SMS and mobile reminders** cut diabetes incidence by about **36%** in Indian men with IGT (IDPP-3). [IDPP-3 2013]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: drugs for prevention`,points:[`**Metformin** should be **considered** for prediabetes, especially with **BMI 35 or more, age under 60, past GDM**, HbA1c 6.0% or more, or rising glucose despite lifestyle change. [ADA 2026]`,`Dose: **850 mg twice daily** in DPP; Indian IDPP-1 used only **250 mg twice daily** with similar benefit (26.4%) - in practice **500 mg once or twice daily with meals**. [IDPP-1 2006]`,`With long-term metformin, check **vitamin B12** periodically, especially with neuropathy or anaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Acarbose** (STOP-NIDDM, 25% reduction), **pioglitazone** (ACT NOW, 72% reduction) and **orlistat** (XENDOS, 37%) prevent diabetes but are limited by side effects and are **not first-line**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide** for obesity markedly reduce progression (tirzepatide SURMOUNT-1 3-year: about **94% lower risk**); in prediabetes with obesity, **weight-management pharmacotherapy** may be considered (see the Obesity pharmacotherapy topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**No drug is approved specifically to prevent diabetes** in India; metformin is the only one with long-term safety and cost data (cheap via Jan Aushadhi). [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Vitamin D supplements do not prevent diabetes** in people with normal vitamin D (D2d trial). [ADA 2026]`,`Treat **cardiovascular risk factors** - BP and lipids - by standard thresholds; prediabetes alone is not a statin indication but adds to risk. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Bariatric surgery and when hospital care is needed`,points:[`Prediabetes itself never needs admission; admit only for a coexisting acute illness or newly found **diabetes with ketosis or hyperosmolar features**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Metabolic (bariatric) surgery** reduces incident diabetes by about **75-80%** (Swedish Obese Subjects) in severe obesity; in Asians it is considered at **BMI 35 or more**, or 32.5 with comorbidity. [IFSO-APC 2022]`,`Opportunistic testing in hospital (for example an **HbA1c on admission**) identifies prediabetes that should be handed over to primary care for follow-up. [ADA 2026]`,`Women with GDM must have a **75 g OGTT 4-12 weeks postpartum** - many are lost to follow-up in India; link them before discharge. [ADA 2026]`,`Post-transplant, antipsychotic-treated and steroid-treated patients need **glucose surveillance** because their progression is faster. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Past GDM**: lifelong testing every 1-3 years; lifestyle plus **metformin** reduced progression by about **50%** in women with past GDM in DPP. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy**: prediabetes found early in pregnancy (HbA1c 5.7-6.4%) needs early lifestyle care and GDM testing at 24-28 weeks (see the obstetrics GDM topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Children and adolescents**: screen overweight youth after puberty or from 10 years; family-based lifestyle change; **metformin is not effective** for prevention in youth (RISE). [ADA 2026]`,`**Elderly**: prediabetes over 70 progresses slowly and many revert - focus on activity, muscle strength and fall prevention, not drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**PCOS**: screen with OGTT; lifestyle first, metformin often helps menstrual and metabolic features (see the PCOS topic). [ADA 2026]`,`**Antipsychotic users**: baseline and 3-monthly glucose for the first year; prefer metabolically neutral agents where possible. [ADA 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: use **IDRS or CBAC** to pick who needs a venous test; group lifestyle sessions by ASHAs and CHOs are cheaper than individual counselling. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular disease** risk is raised in prediabetes - IGT and HbA1c over 6.0% carry the highest risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microvascular changes** begin before diabetes: about **8% of DPP participants** had retinopathy and small-fibre **neuropathy** and **albuminuria** are seen in prediabetes. [ADA 2026]`,`**Da Qing 30-year follow-up**: 6 years of lifestyle intervention delayed diabetes by about **4 years** and reduced CV events by about **26%** and all-cause mortality by about **26%**. [Da Qing 2019]`,`**DPP Outcomes Study (15 years)**: lifestyle reduced diabetes incidence by **27%** and metformin by **18%**; benefits waned but persisted. [DPPOS 2015]`,`**Prediabetes remission** (returning to normal glucose regulation) is linked to lower diabetes risk and, in recent post-hoc analyses of DPPOS and Da Qing (2023-2025), to **fewer cardiovascular events and deaths**. [ADA 2026]`,`Associated **MASLD, OSA, cognitive decline and some cancers** share the same insulin-resistant background. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review **every 3-6 months** for weight, waist, BP and adherence to the plan; retest glucose or HbA1c **at least yearly**. [ADA 2026]`,`Refer to a **dietitian** and a **structured lifestyle programme** where available; refer to an endocrinologist if MODY is suspected or glucose rises despite therapy. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Refer for **bariatric assessment** if BMI is 35 or more (32.5 with comorbidity) and lifestyle and drugs fail. [IFSO-APC 2022]`,`Screen and treat **BP, lipids and fatty liver**; calculate CV risk annually. [ADA 2026]`,`If diabetes develops, switch to the full diabetes care pathway (see the Type 2 diabetes topic). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**NP-NCD** (renamed from NPCDCS in 2023) runs population-based screening for diabetes, hypertension and common cancers in everyone **30 and over** at Ayushman Arogya Mandir, with treatment at PHC/CHC NCD clinics. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`The **75/25 initiative** (launched 2023) aims to put **75 million people with hypertension or diabetes** on standard care by 2025 - prediabetes counselling is part of the visit. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Fit India Movement**, **Eat Right India (FSSAI)** and school health under **Ayushman Bharat** support activity, less sugar and less trans fat at population level. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Counsel in **plain words**: "Your sugar is in the danger zone, but this stage can be reversed - losing 5-7 kg and walking 30 minutes 5 days a week halves your risk." [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Population prevention**: maternal nutrition, breastfeeding, healthy school meals and taxes on sugary drinks address the **thin-fat** origins of Indian diabetes. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA 2026**: lifestyle programme referral for all with prediabetes, metformin for high-risk groups, yearly testing, and attention to **CV risk**; weight-management drugs may be considered with obesity. [ADA 2026]`,`**IDF 2024**: 1-hour post-load glucose **155 mg/dL or more** proposed to diagnose intermediate hyperglycaemia. [IDF 2024]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians 2024**: limit cereals to about 45% of energy, added sugar under 5%, salt under 5 g. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB 2023**: prediabetes **15.3%**, exceeding diabetes in prevalence. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Prediabetes remission** is emerging (2023-2025 analyses) as a treatment goal linked to lower CV mortality. [ADA 2026]`,`**SURMOUNT-1 3-year (2024)**: tirzepatide reduced progression from prediabetes to diabetes by about 94% in obesity. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: "**Define prediabetes**" - give the ADA values, note WHO's 110 for IFG, and say HbA1c 5.7-6.4%.`,`Viva: "**What were the results of the DPP and IDPP?**" - DPP: lifestyle **58%**, metformin **31%**; IDPP-1: lifestyle **28.5%**, metformin **26.4%**, both 28.2%.`,`Viva: "**What is IDRS?**" - four items (age, waist, activity, family history), maximum 100, **60 or more high risk**.`,`Common mistake: relying on **fasting glucose alone** in Indians - isolated IGT is missed; use OGTT.`,`Common mistake: prescribing metformin **instead of** lifestyle - lifestyle is more effective, and drugs are add-ons.`,`Remember the **remission** definition for diabetes: HbA1c under 6.5% at least **3 months** after stopping all glucose-lowering drugs (ADA/EASD 2021).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Criteria for prediabetes by organisation`,columns:[`Test`,`ADA`,`WHO`,`IEC / NICE`,`IDF 2024`],rows:[[`Fasting plasma glucose (mg/dL)`,`100-125`,`110-125`,`110-125 (NICE)`,`-`],[`2-hour OGTT (mg/dL)`,`140-199`,`140-199`,`-`,`140-199`],[`HbA1c`,`5.7-6.4%`,`Not used`,`6.0-6.4%`,`-`],[`1-hour OGTT (mg/dL)`,`Not used`,`Not used`,`Not used`,`155-208`]]},{heading:`Indian Diabetes Risk Score (IDRS, MDRF)`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Category`,`Score`],rows:[[`Age`,`Under 35 / 35-49 / 50 or more`,`0 / 20 / 30`],[`Abdominal obesity (waist)`,`Women under 80, men under 90 / women 80-89, men 90-99 / women 90 or more, men 100 or more (cm)`,`0 / 10 / 20`],[`Physical activity`,`Vigorous exercise or strenuous work / moderate / mild / sedentary`,`0 / 10 / 20 / 30`],[`Family history of diabetes`,`None / one parent / both parents`,`0 / 10 / 20`],[`Interpretation`,`Under 30 low; 30-50 moderate; 60 or more high risk - test now`,`Maximum 100`]]},{heading:`Landmark prevention trials`,columns:[`Trial`,`Population`,`Intervention`,`Reduction in diabetes`],rows:[[`Da Qing (China, 1997; 30-year 2019)`,`IGT`,`Diet and/or exercise for 6 years`,`About 40-50% at 6 years; CV events and death down about 26% at 30 years`],[`Finnish DPS (2001)`,`IGT, overweight`,`Intensive lifestyle`,`58%`],[`DPP (USA, 2002)`,`IGT + raised FPG, BMI 24 or more`,`Lifestyle (7% weight loss, 150 min) vs metformin 850 mg BD`,`Lifestyle 58%, metformin 31%`],[`IDPP-1 (India, 2006)`,`IGT, Indians`,`Lifestyle, metformin 250 mg BD, or both`,`28.5%, 26.4%, 28.2%`],[`IDPP-3 (India, 2013)`,`Men with IGT (IT workers)`,`Mobile phone SMS lifestyle prompts`,`About 36%`],[`D-CLIP (Chennai, 2016)`,`Prediabetes, overweight`,`Lifestyle plus stepwise metformin`,`About 32%`],[`SURMOUNT-1 3-year (2024)`,`Obesity with prediabetes`,`Tirzepatide weekly`,`About 94%`]]},{heading:`Drugs that reduce progression to diabetes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metformin (first choice if drug needed)`,`500 mg once or twice daily with meals; DPP used 850 mg twice daily`,`Start 500 mg, increase weekly; long term with yearly review`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Acarbose`,`25-50 mg three times daily with meals, up to 100 mg TDS`,`Titrate every 2-4 weeks`,`Flatulence, diarrhoea; avoid in IBD`],[`Pioglitazone`,`15-30 mg orally once daily`,`Long term`,`Weight gain, oedema, heart failure, fractures; avoid in HF`],[`Orlistat`,`120 mg orally three times daily with fatty meals`,`Up to 4 years in XENDOS`,`Oily stools, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency`],[`Semaglutide 2.4 mg (obesity)`,`0.25 mg SC weekly, increase monthly to 2.4 mg`,`16-20 weeks to full dose; long term`,`Nausea, gallstones, pancreatitis; avoid with MTC/MEN2 history`],[`Tirzepatide (obesity)`,`2.5 mg SC weekly, increase by 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 5-15 mg`,`Long term`,`Nausea, vomiting, gallstones; avoid with MTC/MEN2 history`]]},{heading:`IFG vs IGT vs raised HbA1c`,columns:[`Feature`,`IFG`,`IGT`,`HbA1c 5.7-6.4%`],rows:[[`Main defect`,`Hepatic insulin resistance, first-phase loss`,`Muscle insulin resistance, late-phase loss`,`Mixed; average glycaemia`],[`Test`,`Fasting glucose`,`75 g OGTT`,`HbA1c`],[`CV risk`,`Modest`,`Higher`,`Rises above 6.0%`],[`Common in Indians`,`Less`,`More - isolated IGT common`,`Affected by anaemia`],[`Best evidence for prevention`,`Limited`,`Strong (DPP, IDPP, Da Qing)`,`Moderate`]]}],redFlags:[`Glucose in the **diabetic range with ketonuria, weight loss or vomiting** - not prediabetes; manage as new diabetes, consider DKA.`,`**Rapid rise** in HbA1c or glucose over a year despite lifestyle change - re-classify and treat.`,`Lean young person with prediabetes and **abdominal pain or steatorrhoea** - exclude pancreatic disease.`,`Prediabetes with **chest pain on exertion** or claudication - assess for cardiovascular disease now.`,`New hyperglycaemia on **antipsychotics or steroids** with polyuria - may already be diabetes.`,`Woman with past GDM **planning pregnancy** - test and optimise before conception.`],pearls:[`Prediabetes: **100-125 fasting, 140-199 at 2 hours, 5.7-6.4% HbA1c** (ADA); WHO IFG starts at **110**.`,`**DPP: lifestyle 58%, metformin 31%**; **IDPP-1: 28.5%, 26.4%, 28.2%**.`,`**7% weight loss and 150 minutes a week** - the two numbers of prevention.`,`In Indians, **OGTT beats FPG** - isolated IGT is common.`,`**IDRS 60 or more** = high risk; four items, maximum 100.`,`**NP-NCD**: screen everyone 30 and over; CBAC plus random capillary glucose.`,`Metformin for prevention works best with **BMI 35 or more, age under 60, past GDM**.`,`**Da Qing 30 years**: lifestyle reduced CV events and mortality - prevention saves lives, not just glucose.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-q1`,stem:`In the Indian Diabetes Prevention Programme (IDPP-1) in people with impaired glucose tolerance, what was the approximate relative risk reduction in incident diabetes with lifestyle modification over 3 years?`,options:[`58%`,`28.5%`,`72%`,`10%`],answer:1,explanation:`IDPP-1 showed relative risk reductions of 28.5% with lifestyle, 26.4% with metformin 250 mg twice daily and 28.2% with both, against a 3-year control incidence of 55%. The 58% figure is from the American DPP and the Finnish DPS; 72% is pioglitazone in ACT NOW; 10% understates the Indian result.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-q2`,stem:`A 42-year-old man has fasting glucose 96 mg/dL and HbA1c 5.6%, but his waist is 98 cm and both parents have diabetes. Which test is most likely to reveal his dysglycaemia?`,options:[`Repeat fasting glucose next week`,`Random capillary glucose`,`75 g oral glucose tolerance test`,`Fasting insulin level`],answer:2,explanation:`Asian Indians frequently have isolated impaired glucose tolerance with normal fasting glucose and HbA1c, so the 75 g OGTT is the most sensitive test in a high-risk person. Repeating the fasting glucose will miss post-load hyperglycaemia, a random capillary value is only a screening tool, and fasting insulin is not a diagnostic criterion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-q3`,stem:`According to ADA 2026, in which person with prediabetes is metformin for prevention most strongly supported?`,options:[`A 72-year-old man with BMI 24 and HbA1c 5.8%`,`A 14-year-old with obesity and IGT`,`A 32-year-old woman with past gestational diabetes and BMI 36`,`A 50-year-old with isolated IFG of 102 mg/dL and BMI 22`],answer:2,explanation:`Metformin prevented diabetes best in DPP participants with BMI 35 or more, age under 60 and women with prior gestational diabetes (about 50% reduction in the GDM subgroup). Older, lean people with mild values gain little; metformin did not prevent progression in youth (RISE). Mild isolated IFG in a lean person is lower risk and lifestyle comes first.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-q4`,stem:`Which item is NOT part of the Indian Diabetes Risk Score (IDRS)?`,options:[`Age`,`Waist circumference`,`Body mass index`,`Family history of diabetes`],answer:2,explanation:`IDRS uses four simple items: age, abdominal obesity measured by waist circumference, physical activity and family history, scored to a maximum of 100 with 60 or more meaning high risk. BMI is deliberately left out because waist captures the visceral adiposity that matters in Asian Indians and needs no weighing scale.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-q5`,stem:`Which long-term outcome was demonstrated by the 30-year follow-up of the Da Qing Diabetes Prevention Study?`,options:[`Lifestyle intervention reduced cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality`,`Metformin reduced amputations by half`,`Vitamin D supplementation prevented diabetes`,`Lifestyle intervention had no lasting benefit once it stopped`],answer:0,explanation:`Six years of diet and exercise in Chinese adults with IGT delayed diabetes by about 4 years and, at 30 years, reduced cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality by about a quarter - a legacy effect. Da Qing did not test metformin; vitamin D failed to prevent diabetes in the D2d trial; the persistent benefit contradicts the claim of no lasting effect.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define prediabetes. Discuss the screening, evidence base and strategies for the prevention of type 2 diabetes in India.`,openingLines:[`Prediabetes is intermediate hyperglycaemia - IFG 100-125 mg/dL, IGT 140-199 mg/dL at 2 hours or HbA1c 5.7-6.4% (ADA 2026) - that progresses to diabetes at 5-10% a year and raises cardiovascular risk.`,`In India it affects 15.3% of adults (ICMR-INDIAB 2023), more than diabetes itself, making prevention a public-health priority.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`ADA vs WHO (IFG 110-125) criteria; IEC HbA1c 6.0-6.4%; IDF 1-hour 155.`,`15.3% Indian adults; faster progression in Asian Indians; thin-fat phenotype.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`IFG - hepatic insulin resistance; IGT - muscle insulin resistance; combined worst.`,`Beta-cell loss already about 50% at upper IGT.`]},{heading:`Screening`,points:[`NP-NCD: everyone 30 and over, CBAC plus random capillary glucose at Ayushman Arogya Mandir.`,`IDRS 60 or more - high risk; OGTT most sensitive in Indians.`,`ADA: from 35, earlier if BMI 23 or more with risk factors; post-GDM every 1-3 years.`]},{heading:`Evidence`,points:[`Da Qing, Finnish DPS, DPP (58%, 31%), IDPP-1 (28.5%, 26.4%), IDPP-3 SMS (36%), D-CLIP (32%).`,`Legacy: Da Qing 30-year CV and mortality benefit; DPPOS 27% and 18% at 15 years.`]},{heading:`Strategies`,points:[`Individual: 7% weight loss, 150 min/week activity, resistance training, ICMR-NIN 2024 diet, yoga, sleep, quit tobacco.`,`Drugs: metformin 500-850 mg BD for high risk; GLP-1 RA/tirzepatide for obesity; bariatric surgery.`,`Population: NP-NCD, 75/25 initiative, Fit India, Eat Right India, maternal nutrition.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Yearly glucose or HbA1c; BP, lipids, fatty liver; aim for prediabetes remission.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: screening (CBAC/IDRS) - confirm (OGTT/HbA1c) - lifestyle - metformin - yearly review`,`Table of landmark prevention trials`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, criteria and burden`,marks:2},{part:`Screening in India`,marks:2},{part:`Evidence from trials`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention strategies with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`prediabetes`,`IDRS`,`DPP`,`IDPP`,`NP-NCD`,`metformin`,`lifestyle`]},{id:`endocrine-prediabetes-prevention-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Indian Diabetes Risk Score (IDRS).`,openingLines:[`IDRS is a simple, validated four-item score developed by the Madras Diabetes Research Foundation (Mohan, 2005) to identify Indians at high risk of undiagnosed diabetes without any blood test.`,`A score of 60 or more out of 100 indicates high risk and warrants glucose testing.`],answer:[{heading:`Components and scoring`,points:[`Age: under 35 = 0, 35-49 = 20, 50 or more = 30.`,`Waist: women under 80/men under 90 = 0; 80-89/90-99 = 10; 90 or more/100 or more = 20.`,`Physical activity: vigorous 0, moderate 10, mild 20, sedentary 30.`,`Family history: none 0, one parent 10, both 20.`]},{heading:`Interpretation and use`,points:[`Under 30 low, 30-50 moderate, 60 or more high risk.`,`Cheap, no lab; used in camps and primary care; CBAC under NP-NCD is a similar tool.`,`Limitations: does not replace glucose testing; validated in urban South India first.`]}],mustDraw:[`IDRS scoring table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and origin`,marks:1},{part:`Components with scores`,marks:2},{part:`Interpretation and uses`,marks:2}],keywords:[`IDRS`,`MDRF`,`waist circumference`,`CBAC`,`risk score`]}],references:[`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Sections 2 and 3: Diagnosis and Prevention or Delay of Diabetes`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diabetes mellitus: diagnosis, classification and pathophysiology`,`Diabetes Prevention Program Research Group. Reduction in the incidence of type 2 diabetes with lifestyle intervention or metformin, N Engl J Med 2002`,`Ramachandran A et al. The Indian Diabetes Prevention Programme (IDPP-1), Diabetologia 2006; Ramachandran A et al. Mobile phone messaging (IDPP-3), Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2013`,`Mohan V et al. A simplified Indian Diabetes Risk Score for screening for undiagnosed diabetic subjects, J Assoc Physicians India 2005`,`Anjana RM et al. ICMR-INDIAB national cross-sectional study 17, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023`,`ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians, 2024; MoHFW Operational Guidelines, National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD), 2023`,`Gong Q et al. Morbidity and mortality after lifestyle intervention for people with IGT: 30-year results of the Da Qing study, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2019`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide`,title:`GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide in type 2 diabetes`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**GLP-1 receptor agonists** (semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide) and the **dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide** are incretin-based drugs that cause **glucose-dependent insulin release, glucagon suppression, slowed gastric emptying and central satiety**, lowering HbA1c by **1-2.3%** and weight by **5-15%**; long-acting GLP-1 RAs with proven benefit are preferred in **ASCVD, CKD and obesity**, titrated slowly to limit nausea, and avoided with **personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2**.`,keywords:[`GLP-1 RA`,`glucagon-like peptide-1`,`semaglutide`,`Ozempic`,`Rybelsus`,`oral semaglutide`,`liraglutide`,`Victoza`,`dulaglutide`,`Trulicity`,`exenatide`,`tirzepatide`,`Mounjaro`,`twincretin`,`GIP`,`incretin`,`SUSTAIN-6`,`LEADER`,`FLOW trial`,`SURPASS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology of use`,points:[`**GLP-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs)** are peptide analogues of the gut hormone glucagon-like peptide-1, resistant to **DPP-4** breakdown, given by subcutaneous injection (daily or weekly) or, for semaglutide, by mouth. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tirzepatide** is a single 39-amino-acid peptide that agonises both the **GIP receptor and the GLP-1 receptor** ('twincretin'), given once weekly; it is the most potent glucose- and weight-lowering non-insulin agent available. [ADA 2026]`,`India has about **101 million adults with diabetes (11.4%)** and 136 million with prediabetes; **generalised obesity 28.6%** and **abdominal obesity 39.5%** - the population in which weight-centric therapy matters most. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Indians develop type 2 diabetes about a **decade earlier and at a lower BMI**, with more visceral fat, so weight loss of even **5-10%** improves glycaemia, BP and liver fat substantially. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`Use in India was limited by cost until **tirzepatide launched (March 2025)** and the **semaglutide patent expired (March 2026)**, after which many domestic generic semaglutide brands entered the market at lower prices. [CDSCO 2026]`,`GLP-1 RAs are now recommended **independent of HbA1c and of metformin use** in people with established ASCVD, high CV risk, CKD, heart failure with obesity or obesity itself. [ADA 2026]`,`They are **not insulin substitutes in insulin-deficient states**: in type 1 diabetes, LADA, fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes or catabolic hyperglycaemia, insulin is required. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Indications and patient selection`,points:[`**Established ASCVD** (MI, stroke, PAD, revascularisation): a GLP-1 RA with proven CV benefit (**semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide**) reduces MACE regardless of HbA1c. [ADA 2026]`,`**High CV risk** (age 55 or more with two or more of obesity, hypertension, smoking, dyslipidaemia, albuminuria) is treated as an indication on the same footing. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD with T2D**: SGLT2 inhibitor first if eGFR 20 or more; add **semaglutide 1 mg weekly** for kidney and CV protection (FLOW) - it can be used down to eGFR 15 without dose change. [KDIGO 2022 / ADA 2026]`,`**Obesity or weight-related comorbidity** (BMI 23 or more in Asians with comorbidity, or 25 or more): choose **semaglutide or tirzepatide** as the agent with the largest weight effect. [ADA 2026]`,`**MASLD/MASH** with T2D: GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide (or pioglitazone) improves liver fat and steatohepatitis; semaglutide 2.4 mg showed MASH resolution in **ESSENCE (2025)**. [ADA 2026 / AASLD 2025]`,`**HFpEF with obesity**: semaglutide (STEP-HFpEF) and tirzepatide (SUMMIT) improve symptoms and function; SGLT2 inhibitors remain first for any heart failure. [ADA 2026]`,`**Before starting basal insulin**, or when basal insulin is uptitrated without reaching target, a GLP-1 RA is preferred over adding prandial insulin - less hypoglycaemia and weight gain. [ADA 2026]`,`**Poor candidates**: lean insulin-deficient patients, frail elderly with weight loss or sarcopenia, gastroparesis, past pancreatitis, active proliferative retinopathy not yet treated, and pregnancy planning. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`]},{heading:`Pharmacology and mechanism`,points:[`Native **GLP-1 (7-36 amide)** is secreted by intestinal **L cells** after meals and has a half-life of about **2 minutes** because **DPP-4** cleaves it - analogues block this by amino-acid substitution and albumin binding or Fc fusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Incretin effect**: oral glucose releases about 60% more insulin than an equal IV load; this effect is blunted in T2D, and pharmacological GLP-1 levels restore it. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`On the beta cell, GLP-1R signals through **Gs - cAMP - PKA/Epac2** to amplify insulin secretion **only when glucose is raised** - so monotherapy rarely causes hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Glucagon is suppressed** in a glucose-dependent way (lowers hepatic glucose output), while the counter-regulatory glucagon response to hypoglycaemia is preserved. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Gastric emptying slows** (strongest with short-acting agents; partial tachyphylaxis with weekly agents), flattening post-meal peaks and causing nausea and fullness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central satiety**: GLP-1 RAs act on the **arcuate nucleus (POMC/CART neurons)** and brainstem area postrema to reduce hunger and food reward - the main driver of weight loss. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**GIP** acts on beta cells and adipose tissue and centrally reduces nausea; tirzepatide binds GIPR with affinity like native GIP and GLP-1R about **5-fold less** strongly, explaining greater weight loss with tolerable GI effects. [ADA 2026]`,`Half-lives: **semaglutide about 7 days** (C-18 fatty diacid binds albumin), **dulaglutide about 5 days** (Fc fusion), **tirzepatide about 5 days**, **liraglutide about 13 hours** (daily), exenatide 2.4 hours (twice daily). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oral semaglutide** is co-formulated with the absorption enhancer **SNAC**, which buffers gastric pH and allows uptake across the stomach - only about **1% bioavailable**, so it must be taken fasting with little water. [ADA 2026]`,`Clearance is by proteolysis, not the kidney, for semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide and tirzepatide - **no renal dose adjustment**; **exenatide is renally cleared** and avoided below eGFR 30. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification of agents`,points:[`**Short-acting (prandial) GLP-1 RAs**: exenatide twice daily, lixisenatide once daily - mainly lower post-meal glucose through gastric slowing; little used in India now. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Long-acting daily**: **liraglutide** 0.6-1.8 mg SC once daily - lowers fasting and post-meal glucose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Long-acting weekly**: **semaglutide SC** (0.25-2 mg), **dulaglutide** (0.75-4.5 mg), exenatide extended-release 2 mg. [ADA 2026]`,`**Oral GLP-1 RA**: **oral semaglutide** 3, 7, 14 mg once daily (a newer formulation uses 1.5, 4 and 9 mg doses with equivalent exposure in some markets). [ADA 2026]`,`**Dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist**: **tirzepatide** 2.5-15 mg SC once weekly. [ADA 2026]`,`By structure: **human GLP-1 backbone** (liraglutide 97%, semaglutide 94% homology, dulaglutide) versus **exendin-4 based** (exenatide, lixisenatide - from Gila monster saliva, more antibody formation). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`By CV outcome evidence: **proven MACE benefit** - semaglutide SC (SUSTAIN-6), oral semaglutide (SOUL), liraglutide (LEADER), dulaglutide (REWIND); **neutral** - lixisenatide (ELIXA), exenatide weekly (EXSCEL). [ADA 2026]`,`**Fixed-ratio combinations with basal insulin** (iGlarLixi, IDegLira) are covered in the insulin intensification topic. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment before starting - history`,points:[`Confirm **type 2 diabetes with residual beta-cell function** - ask about rapid weight loss, ketosis or past DKA, which point to insulin deficiency where GLP-1 RAs fail. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask for **personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2** (neck lump, phaeochromocytoma, hyperparathyroidism in relatives) - a boxed contraindication. [FDA label / ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **past pancreatitis**, gallstones, alcohol use and hypertriglyceridaemia - GLP-1 RAs are avoided after pancreatitis. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **GI symptoms**: early satiety, vomiting, gastroparesis, chronic constipation, bowel obstruction history - GLP-1 RAs worsen them. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diabetic retinopathy status**: date of last dilated fundus; untreated proliferative or severe non-proliferative retinopathy needs ophthalmology review before rapid HbA1c lowering with semaglutide. [ADA 2026]`,`**Concomitant drugs**: sulfonylurea or insulin (hypoglycaemia once GLP-1 RA added), **oral contraceptives with tirzepatide** (reduced absorption), and narrow-window oral drugs such as levothyroxine or warfarin. [FDA label]`,`**Pregnancy plans and contraception** in women of reproductive age, including PCOS patients whose fertility may improve with weight loss. [ADA 2026]`,`Practicalities: **cost and supply**, refrigerator at home, needle fear, ability to self-inject, fasting practices, and willingness to eat smaller meals. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination before and during therapy`,points:[`**Weight, BMI and waist circumference** at baseline and each visit - Asian cut-offs: overweight BMI 23, obesity 25; waist 90 cm men, 80 cm women. [ICMR 2018 / RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Pulse rate**: GLP-1 RAs raise resting heart rate by about **2-4 beats/min**; a larger rise needs evaluation. [ADA 2026]`,`**Blood pressure**: falls by about 2-5 mmHg systolic with weight loss - antihypertensives may need reduction, especially with vomiting. [ADA 2026]`,`**Thyroid examination** for nodules (MTC signal) and **neck** for lymph nodes in those with suggestive history. [FDA label]`,`**Abdomen**: right upper quadrant tenderness (cholecystitis), epigastric tenderness radiating to back (pancreatitis), distension (ileus) in any patient with persistent GI symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Injection sites** (abdomen, thigh, upper arm) for nodules or reactions, and signs of **dehydration** (postural drop, dry mucosa) after vomiting or diarrhoea. [ADA 2026]`,`In elderly patients, assess **muscle mass and strength** (grip strength, chair-rise) - weight loss on GLP-1 RAs includes 25-40% lean mass. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Choosing between drug options`,points:[`**GLP-1 RA vs SGLT2 inhibitor**: SGLT2 inhibitor is first for **heart failure (any EF) and CKD**; GLP-1 RA is first for **atherosclerotic disease, stroke and obesity**; both together are additive. [ADA 2026]`,`**GLP-1 RA vs DPP-4 inhibitor**: GLP-1 RAs give pharmacological GLP-1 levels - HbA1c fall 1-1.8% vs 0.5-0.8%, weight loss vs neutral, and proven CV benefit; **never combine them** (no added effect). [ADA 2026]`,`**GLP-1 RA vs basal insulin** as first injectable: GLP-1 RA gives similar HbA1c lowering with weight loss and no hypoglycaemia - preferred unless catabolic or HbA1c very high with symptoms. [ADA 2026]`,`**Tirzepatide vs semaglutide 1 mg** (SURPASS-2): tirzepatide lowered HbA1c by **2.0-2.3% vs 1.86%** and weight by **7.6-11.2 kg vs 5.7 kg**. [SURPASS-2, NEJM 2021]`,`**Tirzepatide CV outcome (SURPASS-CVOT, 2025)**: tirzepatide was **non-inferior to dulaglutide** for MACE (HR about 0.92) with lower all-cause death - so it now has CV outcome data in ASCVD. [SURPASS-CVOT 2025]`,`**Oral vs injectable semaglutide**: oral suits needle-averse patients but needs strict fasting administration; injectable has larger weight effect at full dose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Daily liraglutide vs weekly agents**: weekly agents improve adherence; liraglutide generics are a lower-cost option in India and are licensed from **age 10** for T2D. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations - baseline and monitoring`,points:[`**HbA1c** at baseline and every 3 months until stable; expect most of the fall within 12-16 weeks of reaching maintenance dose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Creatinine with eGFR and urine ACR** at baseline - selects patients with CKD for kidney-protective therapy and gives a baseline for AKI from dehydration. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Lipid profile and LFT (ALT)** - hypertriglyceridaemia (pancreatitis risk) and MASLD; FIB-4 for liver fibrosis risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**Dilated fundus examination** before starting in anyone with known retinopathy or long duration with HbA1c above 9%, and on follow-up. [ADA 2026]`,`**Serum calcitonin is not routinely measured** - rodent C-cell tumours have not been reproduced in humans; check only if a thyroid nodule is found. [ADA 2026 / FDA label]`,`**Serum lipase/amylase is not routinely monitored** - asymptomatic rises are common; check only with suspected pancreatitis (severe epigastric pain), where amylase or lipase over **3 times ULN** supports diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen** for biliary colic or suspected cholecystitis - rapid weight loss raises gallstone risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SMBG or CGM** when combined with insulin or sulfonylurea, to guide dose reduction of those drugs. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Prescribing in OPD - initiation and titration`,points:[`**Semaglutide SC**: **0.25 mg weekly for 4 weeks** (titration dose), then **0.5 mg weekly**; increase to **1 mg** after at least 4 weeks and to **2 mg** if needed - same weekday, any time, with or without food. [ADA 2026]`,`**Oral semaglutide**: **3 mg daily for 30 days**, then **7 mg**, then **14 mg** after 30 days if needed; take on waking with **120 mL water or less**, wait **30 minutes** before food, drink or other tablets. [ADA 2026]`,`**Liraglutide**: **0.6 mg SC daily for 1 week**, then **1.2 mg**, and **1.8 mg** if needed. [ADA 2026]`,`**Dulaglutide**: **0.75 mg weekly** (1.5 mg start in many), increase to 1.5 mg, then **3 mg and 4.5 mg** at 4-week steps for extra effect. [ADA 2026]`,`**Tirzepatide**: **2.5 mg weekly for 4 weeks** (initiation only), then **5 mg**; increase by **2.5 mg every 4 weeks** to 7.5, 10, 12.5, maximum **15 mg** weekly as needed. [ADA 2026]`,`**Reduce sulfonylurea** (halve or stop) and **basal insulin by about 10-20%** when starting if HbA1c is near target, to avoid hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Missed weekly dose**: semaglutide - take within **5 days**; tirzepatide - within **4 days**; dulaglutide - within **3 days**; otherwise skip and resume on the usual day. [FDA labels]`,`**Restart after a gap**: if 2 or more weeks of semaglutide or tirzepatide are missed, restart at a lower dose to avoid severe GI upset. [ADA 2026]`,`**Storage**: unopened pens in a refrigerator at **2-8 degrees C**, never frozen; in-use semaglutide pen up to **56 days** at room temperature below 30 degrees C; tirzepatide pens or vials up to **21 days** unrefrigerated. [Product labels]`,`Stay slow if nausea: **delay the next step** by 4 weeks rather than abandoning the drug; maximum dose is not needed if target is achieved. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and inpatient issues`,points:[`**GI effects** - nausea (15-30%), vomiting, diarrhoea, constipation, dyspepsia - peak during titration and settle in weeks; advise small low-fat meals, stop eating when full, slow escalation. [ADA 2026]`,`**Acute kidney injury** from volume depletion after vomiting or diarrhoea - pause the drug, rehydrate, and hold ACE inhibitor, ARB, SGLT2 inhibitor and diuretic during the illness (sick-day rules). [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Acute pancreatitis**: severe epigastric pain radiating to the back - stop the drug, check lipase, and **do not restart** if confirmed; causal link is not proven in large trials. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gallbladder disease** (cholelithiasis, cholecystitis) increases with dose and weight loss - evaluate biliary pain with ultrasound. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diabetic retinopathy worsening**: SUSTAIN-6 showed more retinopathy complications with semaglutide (HR about 1.76), mainly in those with pre-existing retinopathy and rapid HbA1c fall - an early-worsening phenomenon. [SUSTAIN-6, NEJM 2016]`,`**NAION** (non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy) was listed in 2025 as a **very rare** adverse effect of semaglutide - sudden painless visual loss needs urgent ophthalmology and stopping the drug. [EMA PRAC 2025]`,`**Perioperative aspiration risk** from retained gastric contents: the multisociety guidance continues GLP-1 RAs in most patients, with a **liquid diet for 24 hours** before surgery in high-risk cases and holding them if GI symptoms are present. [ASA/AGA multisociety 2024]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** occurs mainly with sulfonylurea or insulin co-therapy - reduce those doses. [ADA 2026]`,`**Injection-site reactions** and anti-drug antibodies are more common with exenatide; **ileus** and severe gastroparesis are rare but reported - stop the drug. [FDA label]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: contraindicated - stop when pregnancy is planned; **semaglutide at least 2 months before conception** because of its long washout; switch to insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Breastfeeding**: not recommended because of lack of data; use insulin or metformin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Tirzepatide and oral contraceptives**: delayed gastric emptying reduces absorption - use a non-oral method or add a barrier method for **4 weeks after starting and after each dose increase**. [FDA label]`,`**CKD**: semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide and tirzepatide need no dose change down to eGFR 15 and in dialysis with caution; avoid exenatide below eGFR 30. [KDIGO 2022 / ADA 2026]`,`**Elderly and frail**: weight loss may worsen sarcopenia and frailty - favour modest doses, adequate protein **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day** and resistance exercise; avoid if underweight. [ADA 2026]`,`**Children and adolescents**: liraglutide, exenatide weekly and dulaglutide are approved from **age 10** for type 2 diabetes. [ADA 2026]`,`**Ramadan and fasting**: weekly GLP-1 RAs are low-risk for hypoglycaemia; do not start or uptitrate within 6 weeks before Ramadan because of nausea and dehydration. [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Type 1 diabetes**: not approved; GLP-1 RAs used off-label for weight in obese type 1 patients must never replace insulin (DKA risk). [ADA 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if cost is prohibitive, use metformin plus SGLT2 inhibitor plus low-cost sulfonylurea or pioglitazone and target weight loss by diet - GLP-1 RA benefit is drug-specific, not essential for control. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Outcomes evidence and prognosis`,points:[`**LEADER** (liraglutide): MACE reduced **13%** (HR 0.87) and CV death 22% in T2D with high CV risk. [LEADER, NEJM 2016]`,`**SUSTAIN-6** (semaglutide SC): MACE reduced **26%** (HR 0.74), driven by non-fatal stroke. [SUSTAIN-6, NEJM 2016]`,`**REWIND** (dulaglutide): MACE reduced **12%** (HR 0.88) in a population where only about 31% had prior CVD. [REWIND, Lancet 2019]`,`**SOUL** (oral semaglutide 14 mg): MACE reduced **14%** (HR 0.86) - the first oral GLP-1 RA with CV benefit. [SOUL, NEJM 2025]`,`**FLOW** (semaglutide 1 mg in T2D with CKD): major kidney events reduced **24%**, with slower eGFR decline and lower CV and all-cause death. [FLOW, NEJM 2024]`,`**SELECT** (semaglutide 2.4 mg in non-diabetic obesity with CVD): MACE reduced **20%** - benefit beyond glucose lowering. [SELECT, NEJM 2023]`,`**Weight**: semaglutide 1 mg about 5-6 kg in T2D; **tirzepatide 15 mg** about **12-15%** in T2D (SURMOUNT-2) and about 21% without diabetes (SURMOUNT-1). [SURMOUNT, NEJM 2022]`,`**Stopping leads to regain**: about two-thirds of lost weight returns within a year of stopping semaglutide (STEP-1 extension) - these are long-term drugs. [STEP-1 extension 2022]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review at **4 weeks** after each dose step for tolerability, glucose log and hypoglycaemia; then every **3 months** with HbA1c, weight and BP. [ADA 2026]`,`Judge response at **3-6 months on maintenance dose**: an HbA1c fall under 0.5% or weight loss under 5% means reassess adherence, technique and dose before switching. [ADA 2026]`,`Deprescribe as glucose falls: reduce or stop **sulfonylurea** first, then reduce insulin; reconsider antihypertensives if BP falls. [ADA 2026]`,`Yearly **dilated fundus**, eGFR and urine ACR, lipid profile and LFT as for all T2D; more often in pre-existing retinopathy. [ADA 2026]`,`Refer: suspected pancreatitis, persistent vomiting with AKI, sudden visual loss, thyroid nodule, pregnancy, and inadequate response despite maximal therapy. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`Avoid **switching between GLP-1 RAs** for small differences; when switching, start the new agent at a dose equivalent to tolerated exposure (for example semaglutide 1 mg to tirzepatide 5 mg). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Counselling, access and programmes in India`,points:[`Teach **injection technique**: pen priming (for flow check on first use), 90-degree injection into abdomen or thigh, hold 6-10 seconds, rotate sites, single-use needles. [FITTER 2016]`,`Counsel **eat slowly, stop when full, small low-fat meals, avoid lying down after meals and avoid alcohol**; maintain 1.0-1.2 g/kg protein to protect muscle. [ADA 2026]`,`Warn about **red flags**: severe persistent abdominal pain, vomiting preventing fluid intake, yellow eyes, neck lump, sudden blurring of vision. [ADA 2026]`,`Warn against **compounded or unregulated GLP-1 products** sold online - dosing errors and contamination have caused harm; use licensed brands only. [ADA 2025 statement]`,`**Availability 2026**: semaglutide (injectable and oral; many generic brands since March 2026), liraglutide (originator and generics), dulaglutide and **tirzepatide (pens and vials since 2025)**; GLP-1 RAs are not on the NLEM or in NP-NCD free-drug lists. [NLEM 2022 / CDSCO 2026]`,`Under **NP-NCD** at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir, free drugs are metformin, glimepiride and insulin - GLP-1 RAs are prescribed at secondary/tertiary level or privately. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Combine with **lifestyle therapy** - 150 minutes per week of activity plus resistance exercise twice weekly, and a structured meal plan - for weight maintenance. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2026**: GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide recommended for glycaemia and weight in most adults with T2D and obesity, with CV/kidney benefit as independent indications. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025** added that a GLP-1 RA with proven benefit should be used in **CKD with T2D** to reduce CV events and kidney progression (FLOW) and for **MASLD/MASH**. [ADA 2025]`,`**ADA 2025** recommended **continuing GLP-1 RA after reaching weight goals** and warned against compounded products. [ADA 2025]`,`**SURPASS-CVOT (2025)** gave tirzepatide CV outcome data (non-inferior to dulaglutide); **SOUL (2025)** gave oral semaglutide proven MACE benefit. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Perioperative guidance changed**: ASA 2023 advised holding weekly agents 1 week before surgery; the **2024 multisociety guidance** now advises continuing in most with a 24-hour liquid diet for higher-risk patients. [ASA 2023 vs multisociety 2024]`,`**India 2025-2026**: tirzepatide launched in March 2025 and semaglutide generics after patent expiry in March 2026 widened access; CDSCO approved semaglutide 2.4 mg for obesity. [CDSCO 2025-2026]`,`Pipeline to name: **oral small-molecule orforglipron** (phase 3 ACHIEVE), **CagriSema** (cagrilintide-semaglutide) and **retatrutide** (GIP/GLP-1/glucagon triple agonist). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Incretin-based therapy in T2D'** - define incretin effect, contrast GLP-1 RA and DPP-4 inhibitor, list CVOTs, adverse effects and contraindications. [DNB pattern]`,`**Contraindications to remember**: personal/family history of **MTC or MEN2**, history of **pancreatitis**, pregnancy, type 1 diabetes, severe gastroparesis. [FDA label]`,`**Common mistake**: combining a DPP-4 inhibitor with a GLP-1 RA - no extra benefit and a waste of money. [ADA 2026]`,`**Common mistake**: escalating dose every week - titrate at **4-week** intervals (liraglutide weekly) to limit nausea. [ADA 2026]`,`Viva: why is hypoglycaemia rare? - insulin release and glucagon suppression are **glucose-dependent**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: why does oral semaglutide need **30 minutes fasting with 120 mL water or less**? - SNAC-mediated gastric absorption is easily blocked by food and fluid. [ADA 2026]`,`Viva: which GLP-1 RA should be avoided below eGFR 30? - **exenatide** (renal clearance). [ADA 2026]`]}],tables:[{heading:`GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Semaglutide SC`,`0.25 mg SC weekly start; maintenance 0.5-1 mg, max 2 mg`,`Increase every 4 weeks; missed dose within 5 days`,`GI upset, gallstones, retinopathy early worsening, rare NAION; MTC/MEN2, pancreatitis`],[`Oral semaglutide`,`3 mg OD on waking, then 7 mg, then 14 mg`,`Each step after 30 days; 30 min fast, 120 mL water or less`,`GI upset; poor absorption if taken with food; same contraindications`],[`Liraglutide`,`0.6 mg SC daily, then 1.2 mg, max 1.8 mg`,`Weekly steps; generics in India`,`GI upset, pancreatitis, gallstones; licensed from age 10`],[`Dulaglutide`,`0.75-1.5 mg SC weekly; up to 3 mg and 4.5 mg`,`Increase every 4 weeks; missed dose within 3 days`,`GI upset, injection-site reactions; MTC/MEN2`],[`Exenatide`,`5-10 mcg SC twice daily before meals, or 2 mg weekly ER`,`Increase after 1 month`,`Antibodies, injection nodules; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Tirzepatide`,`2.5 mg SC weekly for 4 weeks, then 5 mg; max 15 mg`,`Increase by 2.5 mg every 4 weeks; missed dose within 4 days`,`GI upset, gallstones, reduced oral contraceptive efficacy; MTC/MEN2, pancreatitis`]]},{heading:`Classification and outcome evidence`,columns:[`Group`,`Agents`,`Frequency`,`CV/renal outcome trial result`],rows:[[`Short-acting, exendin-based`,`Exenatide BD, lixisenatide`,`Daily or BD`,`ELIXA, EXSCEL - neutral MACE`],[`Long-acting daily, human-based`,`Liraglutide`,`Once daily`,`LEADER - MACE down 13%`],[`Long-acting weekly, human-based`,`Semaglutide SC, dulaglutide`,`Once weekly`,`SUSTAIN-6 MACE down 26%; REWIND down 12%; FLOW kidney events down 24%`],[`Oral GLP-1 RA`,`Oral semaglutide`,`Once daily, fasting`,`SOUL - MACE down 14%`],[`Dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist`,`Tirzepatide`,`Once weekly`,`SURPASS-CVOT - non-inferior to dulaglutide`]]},{heading:`Choosing the injectable or add-on - comparison`,columns:[`Feature`,`GLP-1 RA / tirzepatide`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`DPP-4 inhibitor`,`Basal insulin`],rows:[[`HbA1c fall`,`1.0-2.3%`,`0.5-0.8%`,`0.5-0.8%`,`Unlimited, dose-dependent`],[`Weight`,`Loss 5-15%`,`Loss 2-3 kg`,`Neutral`,`Gain 2-4 kg`],[`Hypoglycaemia alone`,`Rare`,`Rare`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Best organ benefit`,`ASCVD, stroke, obesity, CKD (semaglutide)`,`Heart failure, CKD`,`None proven`,`None proven`],[`Main limitation`,`Nausea, cost, injection`,`Genital mycosis, euglycaemic DKA`,`Modest efficacy`,`Hypoglycaemia, weight gain`]]},{heading:`GI symptoms on a GLP-1 RA - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Test`,`Action`],rows:[[`Expected drug nausea`,`Mild, after dose step, improving over weeks`,`None`,`Small meals, delay next step`],[`Acute pancreatitis`,`Severe epigastric pain to back, vomiting`,`Lipase over 3 times ULN, CT if unclear`,`Stop permanently, admit`],[`Acute cholecystitis`,`RUQ pain, fever, Murphy sign`,`Ultrasound abdomen`,`Hold drug, surgical referral`],[`Dehydration with AKI`,`Vomiting or diarrhoea, postural drop`,`Creatinine, electrolytes`,`Pause drug, fluids, sick-day rules`],[`Gastroparesis or ileus`,`Early satiety, distension, no flatus`,`X-ray abdomen, gastric emptying study`,`Stop drug, surgical review if obstruction`],[`DKA in insulin-deficient patient`,`Vomiting, abdominal pain, breathlessness`,`Ketones, venous gas`,`Admit; DKA protocol`]]},{heading:`Baseline and monitoring tests`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`HbA1c`,`Baseline, every 3 months`,`Target under 7% for most; expect 1-2% fall`],[`Weight and waist`,`Every visit`,`5% loss at 3 months on maintenance dose = responder`],[`eGFR and urine ACR`,`Baseline, yearly`,`ACR 30 mg/g or more or eGFR under 60 = CKD indication`],[`Dilated fundus`,`Baseline if long duration or retinopathy; yearly`,`Untreated PDR - ophthalmology before rapid lowering`],[`Lipase/amylase`,`Only with abdominal pain`,`Over 3 times ULN with pain = pancreatitis`],[`Calcitonin`,`Only with thyroid nodule`,`Not a routine screening test`]]}],redFlags:[`Severe epigastric pain radiating to the back with vomiting - suspected acute pancreatitis; stop the drug and admit.`,`Persistent vomiting or diarrhoea with reduced urine output or rising creatinine - pre-renal AKI.`,`Right upper quadrant pain with fever or jaundice - acute cholecystitis or cholangitis.`,`Sudden painless loss of vision - NAION or vitreous haemorrhage from worsening retinopathy; same-day ophthalmology.`,`Vomiting, abdominal pain and ketones in a lean or insulin-deficient patient - DKA, especially if insulin was stopped.`,`Abdominal distension, absolute constipation and no flatus - ileus or obstruction.`,`A new thyroid nodule or neck lymphadenopathy - evaluate for medullary thyroid carcinoma.`],pearls:[`Incretin action is **glucose-dependent** - GLP-1 RA monotherapy rarely causes hypoglycaemia.`,`**Organ first, HbA1c second**: ASCVD or obesity - GLP-1 RA; heart failure or CKD - SGLT2 inhibitor; both if both.`,`**'Start low, go slow'**: 4-weekly dose steps for weekly agents; nausea is dose- and speed-dependent.`,`**Never combine a DPP-4 inhibitor with a GLP-1 RA**.`,`**Exenatide is the renal exception** - avoid below eGFR 30; others need no renal dose change.`,`Oral semaglutide: **'30-120' rule** - 30 minutes fasting, 120 mL water or less.`,`**MTC/MEN2 and pancreatitis** - the two contraindications every examiner asks.`,`Tirzepatide **reduces oral contraceptive absorption** during initiation and titration - add a barrier method for 4 weeks.`,`**FLOW** made semaglutide a kidney drug; **SELECT** made it a heart drug even without diabetes.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on metformin 1 g twice daily has HbA1c 8.4%, BMI 31 kg/m2 and had a stroke last year. His eGFR is 78 and he has no heart failure. Which addition best reduces his future cardiovascular risk while helping weight?`,options:[`Teneligliptin 20 mg once daily`,`Semaglutide 0.25 mg SC weekly, titrated to 1 mg`,`Glimepiride 1 mg once daily`,`Pioglitazone 15 mg once daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Semaglutide has proven MACE reduction (SUSTAIN-6, largely from fewer strokes) and gives substantial weight loss, so it is the organ-protective choice for prior stroke with obesity. Teneligliptin is weight-neutral without CV benefit, glimepiride causes weight gain and hypoglycaemia, and pioglitazone reduces stroke recurrence in insulin resistance but causes weight gain and oedema.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-q2`,stem:`Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to starting tirzepatide?`,options:[`eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m2`,`Stable non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy`,`A brother treated for medullary thyroid carcinoma with phaeochromocytoma`,`Current use of basal insulin glargine`],answer:2,explanation:`A family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma with phaeochromocytoma suggests MEN2, which is a boxed contraindication to all long-acting GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide. Tirzepatide needs no renal dose change at eGFR 25, mild stable retinopathy only needs monitoring, and combining with basal insulin is allowed with insulin dose reduction.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-q3`,stem:`A woman starts oral semaglutide 3 mg. Which administration instruction is correct?`,options:[`Take with breakfast to reduce nausea`,`Take at bedtime with a full glass of milk`,`Take on waking with up to 120 mL plain water, then wait 30 minutes before food or other tablets`,`Crush the tablet and mix it with food if swallowing is difficult`],answer:2,explanation:`Oral semaglutide relies on the absorption enhancer SNAC in the stomach; bioavailability is about 1% and is abolished by food or larger fluid volumes, so it is taken fasting on waking with 120 mL water or less and a 30-minute wait. Taking it with breakfast or milk, or crushing it into food, prevents absorption.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-q4`,stem:`In which patient is exenatide twice daily specifically inappropriate while semaglutide could be used without dose adjustment?`,options:[`A 60-year-old with eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m2`,`A 45-year-old with BMI 34 kg/m2`,`A 58-year-old with prior myocardial infarction`,`A 50-year-old on glimepiride 2 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`Exenatide is cleared by the kidney and should be avoided below eGFR 30, whereas semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide and tirzepatide are degraded by proteolysis and need no renal dose change. Obesity, prior MI and sulfonylurea co-therapy do not single out exenatide; with glimepiride both drugs need the sulfonylurea dose reduced.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-q5`,stem:`A patient with long-standing T2D, HbA1c 11.2% and untreated proliferative diabetic retinopathy is started on semaglutide 1 mg. Six weeks later he has a vitreous haemorrhage. What best explains this?`,options:[`Direct toxicity of semaglutide on the optic nerve`,`Early worsening of retinopathy due to rapid HbA1c reduction`,`Hypertensive retinopathy from GLP-1 induced rise in BP`,`Hypoglycaemia-induced retinal ischaemia`],answer:1,explanation:`SUSTAIN-6 showed more retinopathy complications with semaglutide, concentrated in patients with pre-existing retinopathy whose HbA1c fell rapidly - the early-worsening phenomenon also seen with intensive insulin. GLP-1 RAs lower rather than raise BP, monotherapy rarely causes hypoglycaemia, and optic-nerve NAION is a very rare, different event causing painless visual loss without haemorrhage.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the mechanism of action, agents, dosing, cardiovascular and renal benefits, and adverse effects of GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide in type 2 diabetes. Add a note on their place in the treatment algorithm.`,openingLines:[`GLP-1 receptor agonists are DPP-4 resistant analogues of the incretin GLP-1 that cause glucose-dependent insulin secretion, glucagon suppression, delayed gastric emptying and central satiety.`,`Tirzepatide is a once-weekly dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist; both classes lower HbA1c by 1-2.3% and weight by 5-15%, and selected agents reduce cardiovascular and kidney events.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Incretin effect is blunted in T2D; GLP-1 RAs restore it at pharmacological levels.`,`Beta cell: Gs-cAMP-PKA, glucose-dependent insulin release - low hypoglycaemia risk.`,`Alpha cell: glucagon suppression; stomach: slower emptying; brain: arcuate POMC satiety.`,`Tirzepatide adds GIP receptor agonism - greater weight and HbA1c effect.`]},{heading:`Agents and doses`,points:[`Semaglutide SC 0.25 mg weekly x 4 weeks, 0.5 mg, 1 mg, max 2 mg; oral 3-7-14 mg daily.`,`Liraglutide 0.6-1.2-1.8 mg daily; dulaglutide 0.75-4.5 mg weekly.`,`Tirzepatide 2.5 mg weekly x 4 weeks, increase 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to max 15 mg.`]},{heading:`Benefits`,points:[`HbA1c fall 1-2.3%; weight loss 5-15%; BP and triglycerides fall.`,`CVOTs: LEADER 13%, SUSTAIN-6 26%, REWIND 12%, SOUL 14% MACE reduction; SURPASS-CVOT non-inferior.`,`FLOW: kidney events down 24%; SELECT: MACE down 20% in obesity without diabetes.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and contraindications`,points:[`Nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea; AKI from dehydration; gallstones; pancreatitis.`,`Retinopathy early worsening (semaglutide); rare NAION; perioperative aspiration.`,`Contraindicated: MTC/MEN2, pancreatitis, pregnancy, type 1 diabetes.`]},{heading:`Place in the algorithm (ADA 2026)`,points:[`ASCVD/high CV risk, CKD, obesity, MASLD: add GLP-1 RA regardless of HbA1c or metformin.`,`Preferred first injectable before basal insulin; combine with basal insulin before prandial insulin.`,`Indian context: tirzepatide since 2025, semaglutide generics since 2026; not on NP-NCD list.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of incretin actions on beta cell, alpha cell, stomach and brain`,`Drug table with doses and titration`,`Treatment flow: organ-based choice of GLP-1 RA vs SGLT2 inhibitor`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Agents and dosing`,marks:2},{part:`CV and renal benefits with trials`,marks:2.5},{part:`Adverse effects and contraindications`,marks:2},{part:`Place in algorithm`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`incretin`,`semaglutide`,`tirzepatide`,`SUSTAIN-6`,`LEADER`,`FLOW`,`MEN2`,`pancreatitis`]},{id:`endocrine-injectables-glp1-tirzepatide-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate GLP-1 receptor agonists from DPP-4 inhibitors.`,openingLines:[`Both are incretin-based therapies, but GLP-1 RAs give pharmacological GLP-1 receptor stimulation while DPP-4 inhibitors only raise endogenous GLP-1 and GIP two- to three-fold.`,`This explains the larger HbA1c and weight effects and proven cardiovascular benefit of GLP-1 RAs.`],answer:[{heading:`Pharmacology`,points:[`GLP-1 RA: peptide, injection (or oral semaglutide); DPP-4 inhibitor: oral small molecule.`,`GLP-1 RA: supraphysiological receptor activation; DPP-4i: physiological levels only.`]},{heading:`Efficacy`,points:[`HbA1c fall 1-1.8% (tirzepatide up to 2.3%) vs 0.5-0.8%.`,`Weight loss 5-15% vs weight neutral.`]},{heading:`Outcomes and safety`,points:[`GLP-1 RA: MACE reduction (LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, REWIND, SOUL), kidney benefit (FLOW); DPP-4i: CV neutral (TECOS, CARMELINA), saxagliptin raised HF admissions.`,`GLP-1 RA: nausea, gallstones; DPP-4i: well tolerated, rare pancreatitis, bullous pemphigoid, arthralgia.`,`Never combine the two.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: GLP-1 RA vs DPP-4 inhibitor`],markSplit:[{part:`Pharmacology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Efficacy`,marks:1.5},{part:`Outcomes and adverse effects`,marks:2}],keywords:[`GLP-1 RA`,`DPP-4 inhibitor`,`incretin`,`TECOS`,`weight`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on diabetes mellitus management`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Sections 9 and 10`,`KDIGO 2022 Clinical Practice Guideline for Diabetes Management in CKD`,`RSSDI-ESI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2022`,`ICMR-INDIAB national cross-sectional study, Lancet Diabetes & Endocrinology 2023`,`Perkovic V et al. FLOW trial, NEJM 2024; McGuire DK et al. SOUL trial, NEJM 2025`,`Multisociety clinical guidance on perioperative GLP-1 receptor agonists (ASA, AGA, ASMBS, ISPCOP, SAGES), 2024`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2023`]}),t.push({id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification`,title:`Intensifying insulin: basal-plus, basal-bolus, premixed and pumps`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Insulin intensification** is the stepwise addition of prandial cover when **fasting glucose is at target on basal insulin but HbA1c is not**, or when basal dose exceeds about **0.5 U/kg/day (overbasalisation)** - by adding a **GLP-1 RA or fixed-ratio combination**, a **single prandial dose (basal-plus)**, switching to **twice-daily premixed or co-formulated insulin (IDegAsp)**, or moving to **full basal-bolus** or a **pump**, with correct **injection technique and site rotation** to avoid lipohypertrophy.`,keywords:[`basal-plus`,`basal-bolus`,`multiple daily injections`,`MDI`,`premixed insulin`,`biphasic insulin aspart`,`Mixtard 30/70`,`IDegAsp`,`Ryzodeg`,`iGlarLixi`,`IDegLira`,`fixed-ratio combination`,`overbasalisation`,`FITTER`,`lipohypertrophy`,`insulin pump`,`CSII`,`automated insulin delivery`,`insulin icodec`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Intensification** means moving beyond basal insulin alone to cover **post-meal (prandial) glucose**, because basal insulin controls fasting glucose but not the meal-related rise. [ADA 2026]`,`Type 2 diabetes is progressive: beta-cell function declines a few percent per year, so most people on basal insulin need **prandial cover or a GLP-1 RA within a few years**. [Harrison 22e]`,`India has about **101 million adults with diabetes** and only about a third reach HbA1c under 7%; insulin is often started late and intensified later still (**clinical inertia**). [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`On high-carbohydrate Indian diets (rice, roti) **post-prandial glucose** contributes a large share of HbA1c, especially as HbA1c approaches 7-8%. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Premixed insulin twice daily** remains the commonest intensified regimen in India because of cost, fewer injections and fixed meal patterns. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`Human insulin (soluble, NPH, 30/70) and **insulin glargine** are on the **NLEM 2022** and available through Jan Aushadhi; human insulin is supplied free under NP-NCD at facility level. [NLEM 2022 / MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Initiation and basal titration are covered in the insulin initiation topic; this topic starts once **basal insulin has been optimised**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`When basal is not enough - causes and triggers`,points:[`**Classic trigger**: fasting glucose at target (80-130 mg/dL) but **HbA1c above target after 3-6 months** - the gap is post-meal hyperglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Overbasalisation**: basal dose above about **0.5 U/kg/day**, a **bedtime-to-morning glucose fall of 50 mg/dL or more**, hypoglycaemia (especially nocturnal) or high glucose variability - stop raising basal and add prandial cover. [ADA 2026]`,`**Post-meal glucose above 180 mg/dL** on SMBG or CGM despite normal fasting values. [ADA 2026]`,`**Rising insulin needs** from weight gain, infection, steroids or progressive beta-cell loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`Before intensifying, exclude **correctable causes**: missed doses, wrong technique, injection into lipohypertrophy, heat-damaged insulin, and unrecognised steroid or antipsychotic use. [FITTER 2024]`,`Insulin deficiency phenotypes (LADA, fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes, type 1) need **basal-bolus early** rather than stepwise additions. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Severe insulin resistance**: needs above about **2 U/kg/day** or more than 200 U/day - consider concentrated insulin (U-500 regular, U-300 glargine) and look for causes. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Physiology and insulin pharmacokinetics`,points:[`Normal secretion has a **basal component** (about 50% of daily insulin, suppressing hepatic glucose output between meals and overnight) and **prandial bursts** after each meal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Human regular (soluble) insulin** forms hexamers: onset 30 minutes, peak 2-3 hours, duration 6-8 hours - inject **30 minutes before meals**; late peak causes pre-next-meal hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rapid-acting analogues** (lispro, aspart, glulisine) dissociate quickly: onset 10-15 minutes, peak 1-2 hours, duration 3-5 hours - inject **0-15 minutes before meals**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ultra-rapid** faster aspart (with niacinamide) and URLi (lispro-aabc) start in about 5 minutes and can be given **at meal start or up to 20 minutes after**. [ADA 2026]`,`**NPH** (protamine crystals) peaks at 4-8 hours and lasts 12-16 hours - the peak explains nocturnal hypoglycaemia and its usefulness for steroid hyperglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Basal analogues**: glargine U-100 about 24 hours, **glargine U-300 and degludec** (up to 42 hours) have flatter profiles and less nocturnal hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Premixed insulins** contain a fixed ratio of soluble/rapid to protamine-bound insulin, so the second peak covers the next meal - this is why meal timing and size must be regular. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IDegAsp** (degludec 70% + aspart 30%) is a true co-formulation (no protamine): the basal part is peakless, so it does not need resuspension and covers 24 hours of basal when given once daily. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification of insulins and intensification regimens`,points:[`**By action**: ultra-rapid, rapid, short (regular), intermediate (NPH), long (glargine U-100, detemir), ultra-long (degludec, glargine U-300) and **once-weekly (icodec)**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Premixed human**: 30/70 (30% regular, 70% NPH) and 50/50. [NLEM 2022]`,`**Premixed analogue**: biphasic aspart 30/70 (and 50/50), lispro mix 25/75 and 50/50. [ADA 2026]`,`**Co-formulation**: **IDegAsp 70/30** once or twice daily with main meal(s). [ADA 2026]`,`**Fixed-ratio combinations (FRC)**: **iGlarLixi** (glargine 100 U + lixisenatide 33 mcg per mL) and **IDegLira** (degludec 1 U + liraglutide 0.036 mg per dose step). [ADA 2026]`,`**Regimens in order of complexity**: basal + GLP-1 RA/FRC, basal-plus (one prandial), basal-plus-two, **basal-bolus (MDI, 4 injections)**, premixed twice or thrice daily, and **pump (CSII) or automated insulin delivery**. [ADA 2026]`,`**4-T trial**: starting with basal insulin caused less hypoglycaemia and weight gain than prandial or biphasic insulin, though most patients eventually needed both components. [4-T, NEJM 2009]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - history and examination`,points:[`**Glucose pattern**: review SMBG or CGM at fasting, pre-meal, 2-hour post-meal and bedtime to find **which meal** drives the excess. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia history**: timing, symptoms, awareness and severe episodes needing help - recurrent night-time lows suggest overbasalisation. [ADA 2026]`,`**Meal pattern**: number and timing of meals, largest meal (often dinner in India), fasting days, shift work - premixes need regular meals; basal-bolus suits irregular ones. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Injection practice** (FITTER): needle length, reuse, site choice and rotation, angle, hold time, resuspension of NPH/premix, where insulin is stored at home. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Capability**: vision, dexterity, cognition, literacy, carer support and cost - decides between premix and MDI. [ADA 2026]`,`**Examine injection sites** by inspection and palpation: **lipohypertrophy** feels rubbery, often palpable rather than visible; also bruising, lipoatrophy and infection. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Weight and BMI** at each visit - insulin intensification adds 2-4 kg on average; consider a GLP-1 RA to offset it. [ADA 2026]`,`Look for **complications** that change targets: retinopathy (early worsening), autonomic neuropathy (hypoglycaemia unawareness), CKD (reduced insulin clearance). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of high HbA1c on basal insulin`,points:[`**Post-prandial hyperglycaemia** (fasting normal, post-meal over 180) - needs prandial cover or GLP-1 RA, not more basal. [ADA 2026]`,`**Under-titrated basal** (fasting still above 130) - keep titrating basal before adding prandial insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Overbasalisation** (basal over 0.5 U/kg, night lows, morning highs from rebound or overeating) - reduce basal, add prandial. [ADA 2026]`,`**Lipohypertrophy** - erratic glucose and unexplained hypoglycaemia; moving to healthy skin often needs a **10-20% dose reduction**. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Non-adherence or cost** - missed doses, rationing of insulin or strips. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Degraded insulin** - stored above 30 degrees C, frozen, or used beyond the in-use period (cloudy clear insulin, clumps). [Product labels]`,`**HbA1c artefact** - anaemia, haemoglobinopathy or CKD alter HbA1c; check glucose readings or CGM before escalating. [ADA 2026]`,`**Intercurrent causes** - infection, steroids, antipsychotics, hyperthyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring tools`,points:[`**HbA1c every 3 months** until at target, then 6-monthly; usual target **under 7%**, relaxed to under 8% in the elderly or those with hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Structured SMBG**: 7-point profile (pre- and 2-hour post- each meal plus bedtime) on 2-3 days before a review, or paired pre/post-meal tests. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**CGM** is recommended for anyone on insulin; targets **time in range (70-180) over 70%**, time below 70 under 4%, below 54 under 1%. [ADA 2026]`,`**Bedtime-to-morning differential** of 50 mg/dL or more and **post-prandial minus pre-prandial** rise over 50-60 mg/dL guide where to add insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Creatinine-eGFR**: falling eGFR reduces insulin clearance - lower doses as CKD advances. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**C-peptide** (fasting under 0.2 nmol/L suggests severe insulin deficiency) and GAD antibodies when the phenotype suggests LADA. [ADA 2026]`,`Check **haemoglobin** and consider fructosamine when HbA1c and glucose readings disagree. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`OPD management - choosing the next step`,points:[`**First option: add a GLP-1 RA** (or switch to an FRC) - similar HbA1c lowering to prandial insulin with weight loss and less hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**iGlarLixi**: start **15 units** (dose steps) if on under 30 U basal, **30 units** if on 30-60 U; maximum 60 units once daily within an hour before the first meal. [Product label / ADA 2026]`,`**IDegLira**: start **16 dose steps** if switching from basal insulin (10 if insulin-naive); maximum **50 dose steps** once daily, any time of day. [Product label / ADA 2026]`,`**Basal-plus**: add **4 units or 10% of basal** as rapid or regular insulin before the **largest meal**; if HbA1c under 8%, reduce basal by the same amount. [ADA 2026]`,`**Basal-plus-two, then basal-bolus**: add prandial doses to the next largest meals one at a time if targets are still missed. [ADA 2026]`,`**Switch to premix twice daily**: divide the current basal dose into **two-thirds before breakfast and one-third before dinner**, or half and half, and give 30 minutes (human) or 0-15 minutes (analogue) before meals. [ADA 2026]`,`**IDegAsp**: once daily with the largest meal, converting unit-for-unit from basal or once-daily premix; split into two doses with breakfast and dinner if needed. [ADA 2026 / Product label]`,`**Basal-bolus de novo** (catabolic or insulin-deficient): total **0.4-0.5 U/kg/day**, half basal, half divided across three meals. [ADA 2026]`,`**Continue metformin** (and SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 RA for organ protection); **stop sulfonylurea** once prandial insulin starts; stop pioglitazone if oedema. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Titration algorithms`,points:[`**Prandial dose**: increase by **1-2 units or 10-15%** twice weekly until the 2-hour post-meal (or next pre-meal) glucose is at target. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia**: find the cause; if none, reduce the responsible dose by **10-20%**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Premix twice daily**: adjust the **morning dose by the pre-dinner glucose** and the **evening dose by the next fasting glucose** - 2 units (or 10%) every 3-4 days. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`If post-lunch glucose stays high on premix BD, move to **premix thrice daily** (analogue premix) or to basal-bolus. [ADA 2026]`,`**Basal on basal-bolus**: titrate to fasting **80-130 mg/dL** by 2 units every 3 days, as during initiation. [ADA 2026]`,`**Carbohydrate counting** (insulin-to-carbohydrate ratio, often 1 U per 10-15 g; '500 rule' divided by TDD) and correction factor ('1800 rule' divided by TDD, mg/dL per unit) allow flexible meals in motivated patients. [ADA 2026]`,`**Simple alternative**: fixed meal doses with a supplemental scale (for example add 2 units if pre-meal glucose over 250 mg/dL). [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`]},{heading:`Pens, injection technique (FITTER) and storage`,points:[`**Needle**: a **4 mm pen needle at 90 degrees without a skin fold** suits nearly all adults and children, whatever the BMI; longer needles risk intramuscular injection and hypoglycaemia. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Hold the needle in for 10 seconds** after pressing the plunger to deliver the full dose; **prime 2 units** before each injection. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Resuspend cloudy insulin** (NPH, premix) by rolling 10 times and inverting 10 times until uniformly milky; IDegAsp is clear and needs no mixing. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Sites**: abdomen (fastest absorption, preferred for prandial), thighs, buttocks, upper arms; **rotate within a site** in a systematic quadrant pattern, each injection at least 1 cm from the last. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Never reuse needles** - reuse blunts the tip and is linked to lipohypertrophy; dispose in a sharps container or rigid bottle. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Storage**: unopened at **2-8 degrees C** (never frozen); in-use pen at room temperature below 30 degrees C for **28 days** (glargine, human), **42 days** (detemir), **56 days** (degludec). [Product labels]`,`In hot climates without refrigeration, use an **earthen pot (matka) or clay cooler** and keep insulin away from sunlight and car dashboards. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Syringes** must match concentration (U-40 vs U-100) - a mismatch gives a 2.5-fold dosing error; U-40 vials remain in some Indian markets. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`]},{heading:`Pumps and automated insulin delivery`,points:[`**CSII** delivers rapid-acting insulin continuously (basal rates, usually **40-50% of total daily dose**) with patient-triggered boluses. [ADA 2026]`,`**Automated insulin delivery (AID, hybrid closed loop)** links CGM to pump through an algorithm that adjusts basal delivery - more time in range and less hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`AID is recommended for **all adults and children with type 1 diabetes** able to use it, and **should be considered in type 2 diabetes on multiple daily injections**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Patch pumps** (tubeless) and simpler devices that deliver basal plus fixed bolus clicks suit type 2 patients on MDI. [ADA 2026]`,`Pump failure gives **no depot of long-acting insulin**: DKA can develop within hours - carry pens and a written back-up MDI plan. [ADA 2026]`,`In India pumps and AID are mostly self-funded and used in type 1 diabetes; **smart pens** that record dose and timing are a lower-cost middle option. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: simplify - prefer basal plus GLP-1 RA or once-daily IDegAsp to complex MDI; target HbA1c under 7.5-8%; avoid hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD**: insulin clearance falls - reduce doses (about 25% at eGFR under 50 in many algorithms) and use analogues for less hypoglycaemia. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: basal-bolus with NPH or detemir/glargine plus aspart or lispro; premix BD acceptable; tight targets (fasting under 95 mg/dL). [ADA 2026]`,`**Manual labourers and irregular meals**: basal-bolus with meal-skipping rule ('no meal, no bolus') is safer than premix. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Ramadan**: premix - take the usual morning dose at **Iftar** and reduce the evening dose by **25-50%** at **Suhoor**. [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: human premix 30/70 twice daily with syringes, or NPH plus regular, remains effective and affordable. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Severe insulin resistance**: U-500 regular insulin needs dedicated syringes or pens and specialist supervision. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Hypoglycaemia** rises with intensification - highest with premix and basal-bolus; educate on the **rule of 15** (15 g glucose, recheck at 15 minutes). [ADA 2026]`,`**Weight gain** of 2-4 kg follows; combine with GLP-1 RA or SGLT2 inhibitor to limit it. [ADA 2026]`,`**Lipohypertrophy** affects roughly a third to half of insulin users and causes erratic absorption, higher doses and unexplained hypoglycaemia. [FITTER 2024]`,`**Lipoatrophy** is now rare (old animal insulins); **insulin oedema** occurs transiently after rapid control. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early worsening of retinopathy** and **treatment-induced neuropathy** can follow very rapid HbA1c reduction - lower more gradually when HbA1c is very high. [ADA 2026]`,`Intensive control lowers microvascular complications (**UKPDS**) with a legacy effect on MI and death at 10-year follow-up. [UKPDS 80, NEJM 2008]`,`**ACCORD** showed higher mortality with very aggressive targets (HbA1c under 6%) in high-risk patients - individualise. [ACCORD, NEJM 2008]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, cost and programme links`,points:[`Contact every **1-2 weeks** during titration (phone or tele-consultation is acceptable), clinic every 3 months with HbA1c. [ADA 2026]`,`Check **injection sites at every visit** and the patient's technique at least yearly. [FITTER 2024]`,`Refer to a specialist for **recurrent severe hypoglycaemia, hypoglycaemia unawareness, total dose over 1.5-2 U/kg, pump candidacy** and pregnancy. [RSSDI-ESI 2022]`,`**Cost**: human insulin (vial and syringe) is the cheapest; glargine biosimilars have narrowed the gap; analogue premix and FRCs cost more. [NLEM 2022]`,`**NP-NCD** supplies human insulin free at CHC and district hospital level; Jan Aushadhi Kendras sell generic human insulin and glargine. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Teach **sick-day rules**: never stop basal insulin, check glucose 4-hourly, keep fluids up, check ketones in insulin-deficient patients. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA 2026 Section 9** keeps GLP-1 RA (or FRC) as the preferred add-on to basal insulin before prandial insulin, and names overbasalisation signs. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2026 Section 7**: CGM for all on insulin and **AID considered for type 2 diabetes on multiple daily injections**. [ADA 2026]`,`**FITTER Forward (2024)** expert recommendations reaffirm 4 mm needles at 90 degrees, no re-use, systematic rotation and site checks. [FITTER Forward 2024]`,`**Once-weekly basal insulin icodec** (ONWARDS trials) is approved in several countries (EU 2024); more hypoglycaemia in type 1 diabetes limits its use there; weekly **efsitora** completed phase 3 (QWINT). [ADA 2026]`,`**NLEM 2022** added insulin glargine to the essential list in India; biosimilar glargine and aspart widened access. [NLEM 2022]`,`Different sources split premix conversion differently (two-thirds/one-third in ADA, 50/50 in many analogue-premix labels) - both are accepted; adjust by glucose pattern. [ADA 2026 / Product labels]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Intensification of insulin therapy in type 2 diabetes'** - triggers, options with doses, titration and injection technique. [DNB pattern]`,`Define **overbasalisation** with its four signs - frequently asked and rewarded. [ADA 2026]`,`Write **basal-plus**: 4 units or 10% of basal before the largest meal - the most quoted number. [ADA 2026]`,`**Common mistake**: continuing to raise basal insulin when fasting glucose is already at target. [ADA 2026]`,`**Common mistake**: continuing a sulfonylurea after starting prandial or premixed insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`Viva: why does lipohypertrophy cause hypoglycaemia? - patient switches to normal skin at the same dose, and absorption suddenly increases. [FITTER 2024]`,`Viva: **'FITTER'** stands for Forum for Injection Technique and Therapy Expert Recommendations. [FITTER 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Insulins and combinations used for intensification`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Regular (soluble) human insulin`,`SC 30 min before meals; start 4 U or 10% of basal`,`Onset 30 min, lasts 6-8 h; +1-2 U twice weekly`,`Late hypoglycaemia before next meal`],[`Rapid analogue (aspart, lispro, glulisine)`,`SC 0-15 min before meals; start 4 U`,`Lasts 3-5 h; +10-15% twice weekly`,`Hypoglycaemia if meal missed`],[`Premix human 30/70`,`SC BD 30 min before breakfast and dinner; basal split 2/3 and 1/3`,`Adjust 2 U every 3-4 days by pre-dinner and fasting glucose`,`Hypoglycaemia, weight gain; needs regular meals; resuspend`],[`Biphasic aspart 30/70 or lispro 25/75`,`SC BD or TDS just before meals`,`As for premix; TDS if post-lunch high`,`As premix; costlier`],[`IDegAsp 70/30`,`SC once daily with largest meal, or BD`,`Unit-for-unit switch; adjust weekly by fasting glucose`,`Hypoglycaemia; clear, no mixing needed`],[`iGlarLixi`,`SC once daily before first meal; start 15 or 30 units`,`Max 60 units`,`Nausea; GLP-1 RA contraindications (MTC/MEN2, pancreatitis)`],[`IDegLira`,`SC once daily any time; start 16 dose steps from basal`,`Max 50 dose steps`,`Nausea; GLP-1 RA contraindications`]]},{heading:`Intensification regimens compared`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Injections/day`,`Best for`,`Drawback`],rows:[[`Basal + GLP-1 RA or FRC`,`1 (+ weekly)`,`Obese, hypoglycaemia-prone, want simplicity`,`Nausea, cost`],[`Basal-plus`,`2`,`One dominant meal (often dinner)`,`Other meals uncovered`],[`Premix BD`,`2`,`Regular meals, limited literacy, cost-sensitive`,`Rigid meals, hypoglycaemia, post-lunch high`],[`IDegAsp OD/BD`,`1-2`,`Want fewer injections with basal cover`,`Cost`],[`Basal-bolus (MDI)`,`4`,`Insulin-deficient, irregular meals, motivated`,`Complex, most injections`],[`Pump / AID`,`Continuous`,`Type 1, selected MDI type 2`,`Cost, training, DKA if pump fails`]]},{heading:`High HbA1c on basal insulin - differential`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Action`],rows:[[`Under-titrated basal`,`Fasting above 130 mg/dL`,`Keep titrating basal`],[`Post-prandial hyperglycaemia`,`Fasting normal, post-meal over 180`,`GLP-1 RA, FRC or prandial insulin`],[`Overbasalisation`,`Basal over 0.5 U/kg, night lows, bedtime-morning fall 50+`,`Cut basal, add prandial`],[`Lipohypertrophy`,`Rubbery swelling at injection site, erratic glucose`,`Rotate, new site, reduce dose 10-20%`],[`Damaged insulin or technique`,`Heat exposure, needle reuse, short hold time`,`Re-educate, replace insulin`],[`Non-adherence or cost`,`Missing doses, strips not used`,`Simplify, cheaper insulin, NP-NCD supply`]]},{heading:`Insulin action profiles`,columns:[`Insulin`,`Onset`,`Peak`,`Duration`,`In-use life at room temperature`],rows:[[`Rapid analogue`,`10-15 min`,`1-2 h`,`3-5 h`,`28 days`],[`Regular human`,`30 min`,`2-3 h`,`6-8 h`,`28 days`],[`NPH`,`1-2 h`,`4-8 h`,`12-16 h`,`28 days`],[`Glargine U-100`,`1-2 h`,`Flat`,`About 24 h`,`28 days`],[`Degludec`,`1-2 h`,`Flat`,`Up to 42 h`,`56 days`],[`Detemir`,`1-2 h`,`Relatively flat`,`12-24 h`,`42 days`]]}],redFlags:[`Recurrent severe hypoglycaemia (needing another person's help) or hypoglycaemia unawareness.`,`Glucose persistently above 300 mg/dL with ketones, vomiting or drowsiness - DKA or HHS.`,`Pump user with unexplained hyperglycaemia above 250 mg/dL and ketones - pump failure; give SC insulin and assess.`,`Rapid weight loss with rising insulin needs in a lean patient - insulin deficiency or another illness (TB, malignancy).`,`Infected injection site with cellulitis or abscess.`,`Sudden visual loss after rapid HbA1c reduction - retinopathy worsening.`,`Total insulin need over 2 U/kg/day - severe insulin resistance needing specialist work-up.`],pearls:[`**Fasting right, HbA1c wrong - fix the meals, not the basal.**`,`Overbasalisation: **0.5 U/kg, 50 mg/dL drop, lows, variability**.`,`**Basal-plus = 4 U or 10%** before the biggest meal.`,`Premix: **morning dose by pre-dinner sugar, evening dose by fasting sugar**.`,`**No meal, no bolus** - prandial insulin follows food.`,`**4 mm, 90 degrees, 10 seconds, never reuse, rotate** - the FITTER five.`,`Moving out of a lipohypertrophy lump? **Cut the dose 10-20%**.`,`**U-40 vial with U-100 syringe = 2.5-fold error**.`,`Prandial insulin started? **Stop the sulfonylurea**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old woman (70 kg) on metformin and glargine 24 units has fasting glucose 110-125 mg/dL, 2-hour post-dinner glucose 260-290 mg/dL and HbA1c 8.6%. She cannot afford a GLP-1 RA. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Increase glargine by 2 units every 3 days`,`Add 4 units of rapid-acting insulin before dinner`,`Add glimepiride 2 mg before breakfast`,`Switch glargine to NPH at bedtime`],answer:1,explanation:`Fasting glucose is at target, so the excess HbA1c comes from post-meal glucose, and the largest excursion is after dinner; basal-plus with 4 units (or 10% of basal) before that meal is correct. Raising glargine would cause overbasalisation and nocturnal lows, glimepiride adds hypoglycaemia without targeting dinner, and NPH would not cover the meal.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-q2`,stem:`Which finding best indicates overbasalisation in a person on basal insulin?`,options:[`Fasting glucose of 150 mg/dL on glargine 0.2 U/kg`,`Bedtime glucose 260 mg/dL falling to 120 mg/dL by morning on glargine 0.7 U/kg`,`Post-lunch glucose of 190 mg/dL with fasting 100 mg/dL`,`HbA1c of 7.4% with no hypoglycaemia`],answer:1,explanation:`Overbasalisation is suggested by basal above about 0.5 U/kg/day plus a bedtime-to-morning fall of 50 mg/dL or more, hypoglycaemia or high variability - all present in the 0.7 U/kg patient. A fasting value of 150 on 0.2 U/kg means basal is under-titrated, a modest post-lunch rise is ordinary post-prandial hyperglycaemia, and HbA1c 7.4% alone says nothing about basal excess.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-q3`,stem:`A man on human premix 30/70 twice daily has fasting glucose 180 mg/dL and pre-dinner glucose 110 mg/dL. Which adjustment is correct?`,options:[`Increase the pre-dinner dose`,`Increase the pre-breakfast dose`,`Reduce the pre-dinner dose`,`Add a bedtime snack`],answer:0,explanation:`On twice-daily premix the evening dose (its NPH component) controls overnight and fasting glucose, while the morning dose controls the pre-dinner value. High fasting glucose with a normal pre-dinner value means the pre-dinner dose should go up by about 2 units every 3-4 days. Raising the morning dose would cause afternoon lows, and a bedtime snack would worsen fasting glucose; first exclude nocturnal hypoglycaemia with rebound.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-q4`,stem:`According to injection-technique (FITTER) recommendations, which practice is correct for an obese adult using an insulin pen?`,options:[`8 mm needle with a lifted skin fold at 45 degrees`,`Reuse the needle for one week to reduce cost`,`Inject repeatedly into the same soft area because it hurts less`,`4 mm needle at 90 degrees without a skin fold, holding for 10 seconds`],answer:3,explanation:`Skin thickness is about 2-2.5 mm at all BMIs, so a 4 mm pen needle at 90 degrees reaches subcutaneous fat without a skin fold, and holding 10 seconds ensures the full dose. Longer needles risk intramuscular injection; needle reuse and injecting into the same soft spot (usually lipohypertrophy) cause erratic absorption.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-q5`,stem:`A patient with HbA1c 9.0% who has injected insulin into a large abdominal lipohypertrophic lump for years is taught to rotate to healthy skin. His dose is 60 units/day. What should be done to the dose?`,options:[`Keep the same dose and review in 3 months`,`Increase the dose by 10% because healthy skin absorbs less`,`Reduce the dose by about 10-20% and monitor glucose closely`,`Stop insulin for a day to avoid stacking`],answer:2,explanation:`Absorption from lipohypertrophic tissue is reduced and erratic, so once injections move to healthy skin the same dose acts more strongly and can cause hypoglycaemia; FITTER advises reducing the dose by about 10-20% with close monitoring. Keeping or raising the dose risks hypoglycaemia, and stopping insulin risks severe hyperglycaemia or ketosis.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes on metformin and basal insulin has HbA1c 9%. Discuss the options for intensifying his treatment, with doses, titration and patient education.`,openingLines:[`Insulin intensification is the addition of prandial cover when fasting glucose is at target on optimised basal insulin but HbA1c remains above target, or when basal exceeds about 0.5 U/kg/day.`,`Options are adding a GLP-1 RA or fixed-ratio combination, basal-plus, premixed or co-formulated insulin, basal-bolus and pumps, chosen by glucose pattern, meals, capability and cost.`],answer:[{heading:`Assess first`,points:[`Fasting, pre- and post-meal SMBG or CGM; hypoglycaemia; basal dose per kg.`,`Exclude technique errors, lipohypertrophy, non-adherence, damaged insulin, steroids.`,`If fasting is high, titrate basal first; if overbasalised, stop increasing basal.`]},{heading:`Option 1 - GLP-1 RA or FRC`,points:[`Add semaglutide/dulaglutide/tirzepatide, or switch to iGlarLixi (15-30 U) or IDegLira (16 steps).`,`Weight loss and less hypoglycaemia than prandial insulin.`]},{heading:`Option 2 - basal-plus to basal-bolus`,points:[`Add 4 U or 10% of basal before largest meal; reduce basal by same if HbA1c under 8%.`,`Titrate 1-2 U or 10-15% twice weekly; add to other meals stepwise.`]},{heading:`Option 3 - premix or IDegAsp`,points:[`Split basal dose 2/3 before breakfast and 1/3 before dinner.`,`Morning dose by pre-dinner, evening dose by fasting glucose; 2 U every 3-4 days.`,`IDegAsp once daily with largest meal, unit-for-unit.`]},{heading:`Adjuncts and stopping drugs`,points:[`Continue metformin and SGLT2i/GLP-1 RA for organ protection; stop sulfonylurea.`,`Pump/AID for selected MDI users.`]},{heading:`Education`,points:[`FITTER technique: 4 mm, 90 degrees, 10 seconds, rotate, no reuse.`,`Storage 2-8 degrees C; in-use 28 days; hypoglycaemia rule of 15; sick-day rules.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: basal optimised - GLP-1 RA/FRC - basal-plus - basal-bolus, with premix as alternative arm`,`Insulin action profile graph (rapid, regular, NPH, basal analogue)`],markSplit:[{part:`Assessment and triggers`,marks:2},{part:`Options with doses`,marks:4},{part:`Titration`,marks:2},{part:`Education and technique`,marks:2}],keywords:[`basal-plus`,`premix`,`overbasalisation`,`FRC`,`FITTER`,`lipohypertrophy`]},{id:`endocrine-insulin-intensification-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on insulin injection technique and lipohypertrophy.`,openingLines:[`Correct injection technique ensures insulin reaches subcutaneous fat consistently; errors are a common hidden cause of poor control and hypoglycaemia.`,`Lipohypertrophy is a rubbery thickening of fat at repeatedly used, often needle-reused sites, affecting roughly a third to half of insulin users.`],answer:[{heading:`Technique (FITTER)`,points:[`4 mm pen needle at 90 degrees, no skin fold; prime 2 U; hold 10 seconds.`,`Resuspend cloudy insulin by rolling and inverting 10 times each.`,`Rotate in quadrants, 1 cm apart; never reuse needles; sharps disposal.`]},{heading:`Lipohypertrophy`,points:[`Cause: repeated injection at the same spot and needle reuse.`,`Detect by inspection and palpation at every visit.`,`Effect: erratic, reduced absorption; higher doses; unexplained hypoglycaemia.`,`Manage: avoid the area for months, rotate, reduce dose 10-20% on moving to healthy skin.`]}],mustDraw:[`Abdominal rotation quadrant diagram`,`90-degree injection with 4 mm needle in skin layers`],markSplit:[{part:`Technique`,marks:2.5},{part:`Lipohypertrophy`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`FITTER`,`4 mm needle`,`rotation`,`lipohypertrophy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - diabetes mellitus: management and therapy`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Sections 7 and 9`,`FITTER Forward Expert Recommendations for injecting and infusing in diabetes, 2024; Frid AH et al. New insulin delivery recommendations, Mayo Clinic Proceedings 2016`,`RSSDI-ESI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2022`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, 2022`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2023`,`IDF-DAR Diabetes and Ramadan Practical Guidelines, 2021`]}),t.push({id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia`,title:`Hyperglycaemia in hospital and perioperative diabetes management`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Inpatient hyperglycaemia** is any blood glucose **above 140 mg/dL** in a hospitalised patient; for most ICU and ward patients insulin is started when glucose is **persistently 180 mg/dL or more** and titrated to **140-180 mg/dL**, using **scheduled basal-bolus or basal-plus-correction insulin (not sliding scale alone)** on the ward and **IV insulin infusion** in the critically ill, with **SGLT2 inhibitors stopped 3 days before surgery**, structured NPO and feeding protocols, and an **HbA1c-guided discharge plan**.`,keywords:[`inpatient diabetes`,`hospital hyperglycaemia`,`stress hyperglycaemia`,`basal-bolus`,`correction insulin`,`sliding scale`,`RABBIT 2`,`NICE-SUGAR`,`VRIII`,`variable rate intravenous insulin infusion`,`perioperative diabetes`,`NPO`,`nil by mouth`,`enteral feeding insulin`,`parenteral nutrition insulin`,`euglycaemic DKA`,`JBDS`,`ADA Section 16`,`discharge planning`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hospital hyperglycaemia** is blood glucose **above 140 mg/dL**; values persistently above this need evaluation and, if 180 mg/dL or more, treatment. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia in hospital**: level 1 under 70 mg/dL, **level 2 under 54 mg/dL**, level 3 an event with altered mental or physical state needing assistance. [ADA 2026]`,`Hyperglycaemia is found in roughly **one-third of adult admissions**; about a third of these have **no prior diagnosis** of diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`India's diabetes burden (**11.4% of adults**, ICMR-INDIAB) means a large share of medical and surgical beds - and most cardiac and renal wards - hold people with diabetes. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Stress hyperglycaemia** is transient hyperglycaemia during acute illness in a person with normal HbA1c; it predicts worse outcomes in MI, stroke, sepsis and trauma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Uncontrolled hospital glucose increases **infection, wound dehiscence, length of stay and mortality**; hypoglycaemia is equally harmful - both are hospital quality markers. [ADA 2026]`,`Indian reality: many wards still rely on **'RBS-based sliding scale'** regular insulin - a practice the guidelines specifically discourage. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Counter-regulatory stress hormones** (cortisol, catecholamines, glucagon, growth hormone) and **cytokines** in illness, surgery and trauma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: glucocorticoids (commonest iatrogenic cause), vasopressors, tacrolimus, thiazides, atypical antipsychotics, octreotide. [ADA 2026]`,`**Nutrition**: dextrose infusions, enteral feeds, **parenteral nutrition** and calorie-dense supplements. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pre-existing diabetes** with omitted or mistimed home drugs and inactivity in bed. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Hypoglycaemia risk factors in hospital**: reduced intake or NPO, renal or hepatic failure, sepsis, sudden steroid taper, insulin not matched to meals, elderly and frail. [ADA 2026]`,`**Euglycaemic DKA risk**: SGLT2 inhibitor continued while fasting or acutely ill, low-carbohydrate intake, insulin dose reduction. [ADA 2026]`,`**Surgery** raises glucose through stress and fasting; major surgery, cardiac surgery and long procedures carry the highest risk. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Stress hormones increase **hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis** while causing **peripheral insulin resistance** in muscle and fat. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6** impair insulin signalling (IRS-1 serine phosphorylation) and beta-cell secretion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hyperglycaemia impairs **neutrophil chemotaxis, phagocytosis and oxidative burst** - the link to postoperative infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`High glucose causes **osmotic diuresis**, volume depletion and electrolyte loss, and increases **endothelial dysfunction, oxidative stress and thrombosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Insulin deficiency with fasting drives **lipolysis and ketogenesis**; SGLT2 inhibitors further lower insulin and raise glucagon - hence **euglycaemic DKA** with glucose under 250 mg/dL. [ADA 2026]`,`**Sliding-scale-only regimens** treat hyperglycaemia after it occurs, ignore basal needs and cause a **'roller-coaster'** of highs and lows. [RABBIT 2, Diabetes Care 2007]`,`In type 1 diabetes, **omitting basal insulin** even when NPO causes DKA within hours - basal insulin is a physiological requirement, not glucose treatment. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and targets that matter`,points:[`**Categories on admission**: known diabetes; newly diagnosed diabetes (HbA1c 6.5% or more); stress hyperglycaemia (HbA1c under 6.5%). [ADA 2026]`,`**Target for most ICU and non-ICU patients: 140-180 mg/dL**; **100-180 mg/dL** may suit selected patients (e.g. cardiac surgery) if achievable without hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Start insulin** when glucose is **persistently 180 mg/dL or more** (two readings). [ADA 2026]`,`**Perioperative target 100-180 mg/dL** within 4 hours before and during surgery. [ADA 2026]`,`**JBDS target** in surgery and on IV insulin is **6-10 mmol/L (108-180 mg/dL)**, acceptable 4-12 mmol/L (72-216 mg/dL). [JBDS 2023]`,`**NICE-SUGAR**: intensive control (81-108 mg/dL) in ICU **increased 90-day mortality** and severe hypoglycaemia compared with under 180 mg/dL. [NICE-SUGAR, NEJM 2009]`,`Regimens by intake: **eating well - basal-bolus**; **poor intake or NPO - basal plus correction**; **critically ill - IV insulin infusion**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - history and examination`,points:[`**Diabetes history**: type, duration, home drugs with doses and timing, last doses (especially SGLT2 inhibitor, weekly GLP-1 RA, basal insulin), hypoglycaemia history. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Type 1 or insulin-deficient?** - lean, young onset, pancreatitis, previous DKA: basal insulin must never be stopped. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Intake plan**: eating, NPO for surgery or procedure, enteral or parenteral feeding - determines the regimen. [ADA 2026]`,`**Steroid and other hyperglycaemic drugs** being started in hospital. [ADA 2026]`,`Examine **hydration**, Kussmaul breathing, ketotic breath and conscious level for DKA/HHS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for the **precipitant**: infection (chest, urine, skin, foot ulcer), MI, stroke, pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pre-operative assessment**: cardiac and renal status, autonomic neuropathy (postural BP drop, resting tachycardia - intraoperative instability, aspiration), feet and pressure areas. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Stress hyperglycaemia vs undiagnosed diabetes** - HbA1c 6.5% or more on admission means pre-existing diabetes; under 6.5% suggests stress hyperglycaemia (retest after recovery). [ADA 2026]`,`**DKA** - glucose usually over 250 mg/dL, ketones 3 mmol/L or more, pH under 7.3, bicarbonate under 18 - see the DKA and HHS topic. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**Euglycaemic DKA** - ketoacidosis with glucose under 250 mg/dL, usually on an SGLT2 inhibitor; check ketones in any unwell patient on this class. [ADA 2026]`,`**HHS** - glucose usually 600 mg/dL or more with effective osmolality over 300 mOsm/kg and minimal ketosis. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**Spurious readings**: capillary meters are inaccurate with **shock, hypoxia, severe anaemia, hyperbilirubinaemia, high-dose vitamin C** and some dialysis fluids (icodextrin with older GDH-PQQ strips falsely high). [ADA 2026]`,`**Steroid-induced pattern** - normal fasting, high afternoon and evening values on morning prednisolone (see the steroid-induced hyperglycaemia topic). [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**HbA1c on admission** in everyone with glucose above 140 mg/dL unless done in the last 3 months. [ADA 2026]`,`**Point-of-care capillary glucose**: **before meals and at bedtime** if eating; **every 4-6 hours** if NPO or on continuous feeds; **every 1-2 hours** on IV insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Blood or urine ketones** if glucose over 250 mg/dL, if type 1, if vomiting, or if on an SGLT2 inhibitor and unwell (blood beta-hydroxybutyrate over 3 mmol/L with acidosis = DKA). [JBDS 2023]`,`**Electrolytes and creatinine** - potassium falls with IV insulin; renal function guides insulin and drug dosing. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Venous blood gas** when DKA or HHS is suspected; **serum osmolality** for HHS. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**CGM in hospital**: may be used in selected patients with confirmation by point-of-care testing, and continued if the patient already uses one and can manage it. [ADA 2026]`,`**Laboratory venous glucose** to confirm extreme or discordant capillary values. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Ward (non-ICU) management`,points:[`**Basal-bolus** for patients eating: total daily dose **0.3-0.5 U/kg** (use **0.2-0.3 U/kg** if age over 70, eGFR under 45 or low weight); give **half as basal** once daily and half as **rapid-acting split before meals**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Basal plus correction** for poor intake or NPO: basal **0.1-0.25 U/kg/day** plus correction rapid insulin for glucose above target. [ADA 2026]`,`**Correction scale** (insulin-sensitive, usual, resistant) is added to scheduled doses; it is **never the only insulin** for persistent hyperglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**RABBIT 2**: basal-bolus achieved better glucose than sliding-scale regular insulin, and **RABBIT 2 Surgery** showed fewer postoperative complications. [Umpierrez, Diabetes Care 2007 and 2011]`,`**Already on insulin**: continue home basal at about **75-80%** of usual dose if intake is reduced, and adjust daily by 10-20%. [ADA 2026]`,`**Non-insulin drugs in hospital**: most are stopped in acutely ill patients; **DPP-4 inhibitors** (e.g. sitagliptin) with basal insulin are an option for mild hyperglycaemia in stable non-ICU patients. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia protocol**: under 70 mg/dL give **15-20 g fast glucose** (or **25 mL of 50% dextrose IV** if NPO or unable to swallow; glucagon 1 mg IM if no access); recheck in 15 minutes; review the regimen after any value under 70. [ADA 2026]`,`Adjust **daily**: if fasting high, increase basal 10-20%; if pre-meal values high, increase the preceding prandial dose; if under 70, reduce the responsible dose 10-20%. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`ICU and IV insulin infusion`,points:[`**IV insulin infusion** for critically ill patients, DKA/HHS, prolonged NPO in type 1 diabetes, major surgery, labour, and uncontrolled hyperglycaemia despite SC insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`Start when glucose is persistently **180 mg/dL or more**; target **140-180 mg/dL** using a **validated written protocol** with hourly checks. [ADA 2026]`,`**Preparation (JBDS VRIII)**: **50 units human soluble insulin in 49.5 mL 0.9% saline = 1 unit/mL** by syringe pump. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Substrate fluid** with VRIII: **0.45% saline with 5% glucose and 0.15% KCl (20 mmol/L)** or 0.3% KCl, usually at **83 mL/hour** to prevent hypoglycaemia and hyponatraemia. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Continue long-acting basal insulin** (about 80% of usual dose) during VRIII so that stopping the infusion does not cause rebound hyperglycaemia or DKA. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Check potassium** at least daily (more often in DKA) and keep it 4-5 mmol/L. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Transition to SC**: give basal SC insulin **2 hours before stopping the infusion** (or continue background basal); estimate total daily dose as **about 70-80% of 24-hour IV requirement** if stable. [ADA 2026]`,`Avoid **intensive glucose targets** (81-108 mg/dL) - they increase hypoglycaemia and mortality (NICE-SUGAR). [NICE-SUGAR 2009]`]},{heading:`Perioperative management`,points:[`**Pre-operative HbA1c**: elective surgery is usually postponed if HbA1c is **above 8.5% (69 mmol/mol)** where delay is safe, to optimise control. [JBDS 2023]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors**: stop **3 days before surgery** (**ertugliflozin 4 days**) and restart when eating and drinking normally and haemodynamically stable. [ADA 2026 / FDA 2022]`,`JBDS 2023 differs: omit SGLT2 inhibitors **the day before and the day of surgery**, and check ketones perioperatively - Indian practice follows the ADA/FDA 3-day rule. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Metformin**: hold on the **day of surgery** (ADA); JBDS allows continuing if only one meal is missed and eGFR is adequate; hold if contrast or AKI risk. [ADA 2026 / JBDS 2023]`,`**Sulfonylureas, DPP-4 inhibitors and other oral agents**: hold on the morning of surgery. [ADA 2026]`,`**Basal insulin**: give **75-80% of the usual long-acting dose** (or **50% of NPH**) the evening before or morning of surgery. [ADA 2026]`,`**Premixed insulin**: give about **half the usual morning dose** as basal-equivalent with a glucose-containing fluid, or switch to basal plus correction for the day. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Weekly GLP-1 RA**: continue in most; **24-hour liquid diet** before surgery for high-risk patients; delay surgery or use rapid-sequence induction if GI symptoms or full stomach on ultrasound. [Multisociety 2024]`,`**Glucose monitoring**: every **2 hours** during surgery on SC regimen and **hourly** on IV insulin; target **100-180 mg/dL**; schedule people with diabetes **first on the list**. [ADA 2026 / JBDS 2023]`,`**Minor procedures** (missing one meal) can be done with adjusted SC insulin; **major surgery or more than one missed meal** - VRIII. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Enteral and parenteral nutrition, steroids and special groups`,points:[`**Continuous enteral feeds**: basal insulin (once or twice daily) **30-50% of total daily dose** plus scheduled **regular insulin every 6 hours** or rapid-acting every 4 hours for the nutritional component (often 1 unit per 10-15 g carbohydrate). [ADA 2026]`,`**NPH every 8-12 hours** can cover continuous feeds; if feeds stop, give **IV 10% dextrose** at the feed rate to prevent hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Bolus feeds**: give rapid or regular insulin **before each bolus**, about 1 unit per 10-15 g carbohydrate, plus correction. [ADA 2026]`,`**Parenteral nutrition**: add **regular insulin to the TPN bag**, starting about **1 unit per 10 g dextrose**, adjusted daily, with correction insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Glucocorticoids**: morning prednisolone - **NPH with the steroid dose** (about 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone, max 0.4 U/kg); details in the steroid-induced hyperglycaemia topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**Elderly and CKD**: lower weight-based doses (**0.2-0.3 U/kg**) and avoid hypoglycaemia; glipizide or DPP-4 inhibitors are safer than glibenclamide. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy and labour**: VRIII with tighter targets (**72-126 mg/dL**) in labour; reduce insulin immediately after delivery (requirement falls by about half). [JBDS 2023]`,`**Type 1 diabetes**: never omit basal insulin, even NPO; insulin pump users may continue the pump for short procedures if protocol and staff allow. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Iatrogenic hypoglycaemia** - from missed meals after insulin, renal failure, steroid taper, interrupted feeds or insulin errors; **level 2 events** are linked to higher mortality. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hospital-acquired DKA** from omitted basal insulin in type 1 diabetes or from SGLT2 inhibitors. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Postoperative infection, poor wound healing and anastomotic leak** rise with glucose above 180-200 mg/dL. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hyperglycaemia in acute MI and stroke** predicts larger infarcts and higher mortality; treat glucose above 180 mg/dL. [AHA/ASA 2019]`,`**Insulin errors** (U vs 0 misreading, wrong insulin, IV dosing errors) are among the commonest serious medication errors - **write 'units' in full**. [ISMP]`,`**Stress hyperglycaemia** carries a high risk of later diabetes - repeat testing at 6-12 weeks. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Discharge planning and follow-up`,points:[`Start discharge planning **at admission**; use **admission HbA1c** to choose the discharge regimen. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c under 7%**: resume the pre-admission regimen (if no contraindication) or stop insulin if it was only for stress hyperglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c 7-9%**: resume pre-admission oral drugs and add basal insulin at about **50% of hospital basal dose**, or intensify oral therapy. [ADA 2026]`,`**HbA1c above 9-10%**: discharge on **basal-bolus at about 80% of hospital dose** or basal plus oral agents, with close follow-up. [ADA 2026]`,`Teach **insulin technique, hypoglycaemia recognition and sick-day rules**; provide glucometer, strips, pen needles and a written plan. [ADA 2026]`,`**Medication reconciliation**: restart SGLT2 inhibitors, ACE inhibitors and metformin when stable; stop correction insulin and hospital-only drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**Follow-up within 1 month** (within 1-2 weeks if insulin changed or glucose uncontrolled); communicate with the primary care team or NP-NCD clinic. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Prevention, systems and Indian programmes`,points:[`Every hospital should have **written insulin order sets**, a hypoglycaemia protocol and an inpatient diabetes team or nurse educator. [ADA 2026]`,`**Remove sliding-scale-only order sets** and replace them with basal-bolus/correction templates. [ADA 2026]`,`**Time insulin with meals**: rapid-acting insulin within 15 minutes of the tray; delay or omit if the patient cannot eat. [ADA 2026]`,`**Perioperative pathway**: pre-assessment clinic 4-6 weeks ahead, HbA1c, written drug-holding instructions and first-on-list scheduling. [JBDS 2023]`,`Link discharged patients with the **NP-NCD clinic** at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir for follow-up and free drugs; **PM-JAY** covers hospital admissions for complications. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Screen all admissions over 30 years in India with glucose or HbA1c - hospital stay is an opportunity to diagnose diabetes. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2026 Section 16** keeps 140-180 mg/dL for most, endorses CGM use in hospital in selected patients with POC confirmation, and supports DPP-4 inhibitor plus basal insulin for mild hyperglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2025-2026**: stop SGLT2 inhibitors **3-4 days** before surgery; continue GLP-1 RAs in most with a pre-operative liquid diet if high risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**JBDS-IP 2023 perioperative guideline** revised SGLT2 inhibitor advice and VRIII substrate fluids. [JBDS 2023]`,`**ADA/EASD/JBDS/AACE/DTS 2024 consensus on hyperglycaemic crises** changed DKA definition (glucose 200 mg/dL or more or known diabetes, ketones 3.0 mmol/L or more) - relevant to inpatient ketone monitoring. [Hyperglycaemic crises consensus 2024]`,`**Multisociety 2024 GLP-1 RA perioperative guidance** replaced the ASA 2023 1-week hold. [Multisociety 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Perioperative management of a diabetic patient undergoing elective surgery'** - pre-op assessment, drug holding, insulin adjustment, VRIII, post-op and discharge. [DNB pattern]`,`Quote **140-180 mg/dL** and the **NICE-SUGAR** reason for not going lower. [ADA 2026]`,`**Common mistake**: sliding-scale insulin alone - write basal-bolus or basal plus correction. [ADA 2026]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping basal insulin in a type 1 patient kept NPO. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting SGLT2 inhibitor holding and missing euglycaemic DKA. [ADA 2026]`,`Viva: **why continue basal insulin during VRIII?** - prevents rebound hyperglycaemia and DKA when the infusion stops. [JBDS 2023]`,`Viva: how to dose **basal-bolus on admission** - 0.3-0.5 U/kg/day, half basal, half prandial; 0.2-0.3 in elderly or CKD. [ADA 2026]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Insulin regimens and drug handling in hospital`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Basal-bolus SC (eating)`,`TDD 0.3-0.5 U/kg (0.2-0.3 elderly/CKD); 50% basal OD, 50% rapid split over meals`,`Adjust daily by 10-20%`,`Hypoglycaemia if meals missed`],[`Basal plus correction (poor intake/NPO)`,`Basal 0.1-0.25 U/kg/day SC + rapid correction scale`,`Adjust daily`,`Not for persistent high glucose on full diet`],[`IV insulin infusion (VRIII)`,`50 U soluble insulin in 49.5 mL saline (1 U/mL); rate by hourly glucose`,`Until eating and stable; overlap SC basal 2 h`,`Hypoglycaemia, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia`],[`Hypoglycaemia rescue`,`15-20 g oral glucose; or 25 mL 50% dextrose IV; or glucagon 1 mg IM`,`Recheck in 15 minutes`,`Rebound; review regimen`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Hold 3 days pre-op (ertugliflozin 4 days); hold when acutely ill`,`Restart when eating, stable`,`Euglycaemic DKA`],[`Metformin`,`Hold on day of surgery or contrast; hold in AKI, sepsis, hypoxia`,`Restart when eGFR stable and eating`,`Lactic acidosis (rare)`],[`Weekly GLP-1 RA`,`Continue in most; 24-h liquid diet if high risk`,`Hold if nausea, vomiting, ileus`,`Aspiration risk`]]},{heading:`Pre-operative drug instructions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Day before surgery`,`Day of surgery`,`Restart`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Usual`,`Hold (ADA); JBDS may continue if one meal missed`,`When eating, eGFR stable`],[`Sulfonylurea`,`Usual`,`Hold`,`When eating normally`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Stopped 3 days earlier (ADA/FDA)`,`Hold`,`When eating and stable`],[`DPP-4 inhibitor`,`Usual`,`Hold (may continue)`,`When eating`],[`Long-acting basal insulin`,`75-80% of usual dose`,`75-80% if morning dose`,`Full dose when eating`],[`NPH insulin`,`Usual evening dose (75-80%)`,`50% of morning dose`,`Full dose when eating`],[`Premixed insulin`,`Usual`,`Half the morning dose or switch to basal/VRIII`,`Usual when eating`]]},{heading:`Hyperglycaemia in an admitted patient - differential`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key feature`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Stress hyperglycaemia`,`Acute illness, HbA1c under 6.5%`,`HbA1c; repeat after recovery`],[`Undiagnosed diabetes`,`HbA1c 6.5% or more`,`HbA1c`],[`DKA`,`Ketosis, acidosis, usually glucose over 250`,`Ketones 3 mmol/L or more, pH under 7.3`],[`Euglycaemic DKA`,`On SGLT2 inhibitor, glucose under 250`,`Ketones, venous gas`],[`HHS`,`Glucose 600 or more, osmolality over 300, dehydration`,`Serum osmolality`],[`Steroid-induced`,`Normal fasting, high afternoon-evening`,`Pre-lunch and pre-dinner glucose`]]},{heading:`Monitoring and target cut-offs`,columns:[`Situation`,`Glucose target (mg/dL)`,`Check frequency`],rows:[[`Ward, eating`,`140-180 (pre-meal under 140 acceptable if safe)`,`Before meals and bedtime`],[`NPO or continuous feeds`,`140-180`,`Every 4-6 hours`],[`IV insulin / ICU`,`140-180`,`Every 1-2 hours`],[`Perioperative`,`100-180 (JBDS 108-180)`,`Every 1-2 hours during surgery`],[`Labour`,`72-126`,`Hourly`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Under 70 = treat; under 54 = clinically significant`,`Recheck in 15 minutes`]]},{heading:`Discharge regimen by admission HbA1c`,columns:[`Admission HbA1c`,`Discharge plan`,`Follow-up`],rows:[[`Under 7%`,`Resume pre-admission regimen`,`Within 1 month`],[`7-9%`,`Pre-admission orals plus basal insulin about 50% of hospital basal, or intensify orals`,`1-2 weeks`],[`Above 9-10%`,`Basal-bolus at about 80% of hospital dose, or basal plus orals`,`1-2 weeks, phone contact earlier`],[`Stress hyperglycaemia`,`Stop insulin; lifestyle advice`,`Repeat HbA1c or OGTT at 6-12 weeks`]]}],redFlags:[`Glucose above 250 mg/dL with ketones 1.5 mmol/L or more, vomiting or abdominal pain - DKA work-up.`,`Unwell patient on an SGLT2 inhibitor with ketones, even if glucose is normal - euglycaemic DKA.`,`Glucose under 54 mg/dL or any hypoglycaemia with altered consciousness - level 2/3 event; treat and review.`,`Type 1 diabetes patient NPO with basal insulin omitted.`,`Glucose over 600 mg/dL with altered sensorium - HHS.`,`Serum potassium under 3.5 mmol/L on IV insulin.`,`Persistent glucose above 300 mg/dL despite scheduled insulin - escalate to IV insulin.`],pearls:[`**140-180** for most inpatients; **never chase 80-110** (NICE-SUGAR).`,`**Sliding scale alone is a sin** - always basal plus correction at least (RABBIT 2).`,`**0.3-0.5 U/kg/day, half and half** - the admission insulin formula; 0.2-0.3 in elderly or CKD.`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor off 3 days before surgery** (ertugliflozin 4).`,`**Type 1 never goes without basal insulin**, even NPO.`,`**VRIII: 50 units in 50 mL, keep the basal running**.`,`**HbA1c on every admission** - it tells you diabetes vs stress and drives discharge.`,`**Write 'units', never 'U'** - a U read as 0 gives a 10-fold overdose.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes admitted with pneumonia is eating well. His glucose readings are 220-280 mg/dL. Weight 70 kg, eGFR 75. Which regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Regular insulin by sliding scale every 6 hours only`,`Basal-bolus insulin, total about 28 units/day, half as basal and half before meals, plus correction`,`Continue glimepiride and add metformin 1 g twice daily`,`IV insulin infusion with target 80-110 mg/dL`],answer:1,explanation:`For a non-ICU patient who is eating, scheduled basal-bolus insulin at 0.3-0.5 U/kg/day (about 0.4 U/kg here) with correction doses is recommended; RABBIT 2 showed it beats sliding scale. Sliding scale alone causes roller-coaster glucose, sulfonylureas and metformin are unsuitable in acute infection, and an 80-110 target raises hypoglycaemia and mortality (NICE-SUGAR).`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-q2`,stem:`A patient taking dapagliflozin is scheduled for elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy. According to ADA guidance, when should dapagliflozin be stopped?`,options:[`On the morning of surgery`,`One week before surgery`,`It should be continued throughout`,`3 days before surgery`],answer:3,explanation:`ADA and FDA advise stopping canagliflozin, dapagliflozin and empagliflozin 3 days before surgery (ertugliflozin 4 days) to reduce euglycaemic DKA, restarting when eating and stable. Stopping only that morning leaves drug effect (JBDS 2023 accepts omitting the day before and day of surgery, but the 3-day rule is the ADA standard), one week is unnecessarily long, and continuing increases ketoacidosis risk.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-q3`,stem:`A 24-year-old with type 1 diabetes on glargine 20 units at night and aspart with meals is kept nil by mouth for an emergency appendicectomy. Which instruction is correct?`,options:[`Omit all insulin until he can eat`,`Give only correction aspart for glucose above 250 mg/dL`,`Continue glargine (about 80% of dose) and start VRIII with glucose-containing fluid`,`Give his usual aspart doses at mealtimes even though he is fasting`],answer:2,explanation:`People with type 1 diabetes need basal insulin at all times to prevent ketogenesis; for major surgery with more than one missed meal, continue the long-acting insulin (about 80%) and run a variable-rate IV insulin infusion with 5% glucose substrate. Omitting insulin or using correction doses only risks DKA, and giving mealtime aspart while fasting causes hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-q4`,stem:`A man on continuous nasogastric feeds receives glargine plus regular insulin every 6 hours. The feed is stopped abruptly because of aspiration. What is the most appropriate immediate action?`,options:[`Start IV 10% dextrose at the previous feed rate and omit the nutritional regular insulin`,`Stop glargine immediately`,`Continue the regular insulin doses unchanged`,`Give 50% dextrose bolus every 4 hours regardless of glucose`],answer:0,explanation:`When continuous feeds are interrupted, the nutritional insulin component no longer has substrate, so ADA advises IV 10% dextrose at the feed rate and stopping the nutritional doses while keeping basal insulin. Stopping glargine risks rebound hyperglycaemia and ketosis, continuing the regular doses causes hypoglycaemia, and scheduled 50% dextrose boluses are unphysiological.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-q5`,stem:`In a patient with stress hyperglycaemia during sepsis, which admission HbA1c result supports the diagnosis of stress hyperglycaemia rather than pre-existing diabetes?`,options:[`HbA1c 6.9%`,`HbA1c 5.8%`,`HbA1c 8.2%`,`HbA1c 10.1%`],answer:1,explanation:`An admission HbA1c under 6.5% with hyperglycaemia during acute illness suggests stress hyperglycaemia, which still needs retesting after recovery because future diabetes risk is high. Values of 6.5% or more, such as 6.9%, 8.2% or 10.1%, indicate diabetes that predated the admission and should shape the discharge regimen.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the perioperative management of a patient with type 2 diabetes on metformin, dapagliflozin and basal insulin posted for elective major abdominal surgery.`,openingLines:[`Perioperative diabetes care aims to keep glucose 100-180 mg/dL, avoid hypoglycaemia and ketoacidosis, and return the patient safely to the usual regimen.`,`It rests on pre-operative optimisation, correct holding of drugs (SGLT2 inhibitor 3 days before), basal insulin adjustment, VRIII for major surgery and structured discharge.`],answer:[{heading:`Pre-operative assessment`,points:[`HbA1c: postpone elective surgery if above 8.5% where safe; cardiac, renal, autonomic, feet.`,`First on the list; written drug instructions given at pre-assessment.`]},{heading:`Drug adjustments`,points:[`Dapagliflozin: stop 3 days before (ADA/FDA); JBDS omits day before and day of surgery.`,`Metformin: hold on day of surgery; basal insulin 75-80% of usual dose.`,`Sulfonylurea and DPP-4 inhibitor held on morning of surgery.`]},{heading:`Intraoperative`,points:[`Major surgery: VRIII - 50 U soluble insulin in 49.5 mL saline (1 U/mL).`,`Substrate 0.45% saline + 5% glucose + 0.15% KCl; hourly glucose; target 100-180 (JBDS 108-180).`,`Continue basal insulin; check potassium.`]},{heading:`Postoperative`,points:[`When eating: basal-bolus, overlap SC basal 2 hours before stopping VRIII.`,`Restart metformin when eGFR stable, SGLT2 inhibitor when eating and stable; ketones if unwell.`,`Hypoglycaemia protocol: 15-20 g glucose or 25 mL 50% dextrose IV.`]},{heading:`Discharge`,points:[`HbA1c-guided regimen; education; follow-up 1-2 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pre-operative drug instruction table (drug, day before, day of surgery)`,`Flow chart: minor vs major surgery - SC adjustment vs VRIII`],markSplit:[{part:`Pre-operative assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Drug adjustments`,marks:3},{part:`Intraoperative VRIII`,marks:2.5},{part:`Postoperative and discharge`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`perioperative`,`VRIII`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`basal insulin`,`JBDS`,`140-180`]},{id:`endocrine-inpatient-hyperglycaemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Differentiate sliding-scale insulin from basal-bolus insulin in hospitalised patients.`,openingLines:[`Sliding-scale insulin is reactive short-acting insulin given only when glucose is high; basal-bolus is scheduled insulin that mirrors physiological basal and prandial needs plus correction.`,`Guidelines discourage sliding scale alone because it gives poorer control with more hyper- and hypoglycaemia.`],answer:[{heading:`Principle`,points:[`Sliding scale: treats after the event; no basal cover.`,`Basal-bolus: 0.3-0.5 U/kg/day, 50% basal, 50% prandial, plus correction.`]},{heading:`Outcomes`,points:[`RABBIT 2: basal-bolus reached target in more patients.`,`RABBIT 2 Surgery: fewer wound infections, pneumonia, AKI with basal-bolus.`]},{heading:`Risks and use`,points:[`Sliding scale: roller-coaster glucose; DKA risk in type 1.`,`Basal-bolus: hypoglycaemia if meals missed - hold prandial when not eating.`,`Sliding scale alone acceptable only briefly in mild hyperglycaemia without known diabetes.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of sliding scale vs basal-bolus`],markSplit:[{part:`Principle and doses`,marks:2},{part:`Evidence`,marks:1.5},{part:`Risks and use`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`sliding scale`,`basal-bolus`,`RABBIT 2`,`correction insulin`]}],references:[`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 16: Diabetes Care in the Hospital`,`Joint British Diabetes Societies for Inpatient Care (JBDS-IP). Perioperative care of people with diabetes undergoing surgery, 2023 revision; Variable rate intravenous insulin infusion guideline`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`NICE-SUGAR Study Investigators. Intensive versus conventional glucose control in critically ill patients, NEJM 2009`,`Umpierrez GE et al. RABBIT 2 trial, Diabetes Care 2007; RABBIT 2 Surgery, Diabetes Care 2011`,`Umpierrez GE et al. Hyperglycemic crises in adults with diabetes: a consensus report (ADA, EASD, JBDS, AACE, DTS), Diabetes Care 2024`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2023`]}),t.push({id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia`,title:`Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia and diabetes in special settings`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia** is a rise in glucose caused by glucocorticoids through **hepatic gluconeogenesis, peripheral insulin resistance and beta-cell suppression**; with **once-daily morning prednisolone** it produces **normal fasting but high afternoon-evening glucose**, so it is detected by **pre-lunch or pre-dinner testing** and treated with **morning NPH insulin matched to the steroid dose** (about 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone) or gliclazide, tapered with the steroid; the same drug-driven logic applies to **post-transplant diabetes, HIV-associated and antipsychotic-induced diabetes**.`,keywords:[`glucocorticoid-induced hyperglycaemia`,`steroid-induced diabetes`,`prednisolone`,`dexamethasone`,`methylprednisolone pulse`,`NPH insulin`,`afternoon hyperglycaemia`,`post-transplant diabetes mellitus`,`PTDM`,`NODAT`,`tacrolimus`,`calcineurin inhibitor`,`HIV diabetes`,`dolutegravir`,`protease inhibitor`,`antipsychotic-induced diabetes`,`olanzapine`,`clozapine`,`drug-induced diabetes`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Glucocorticoid-induced hyperglycaemia** is new or worsened hyperglycaemia during glucocorticoid therapy; **steroid-induced diabetes** is hyperglycaemia meeting diagnostic thresholds, which may resolve or persist after stopping. [JBDS 2023]`,`A meta-analysis of non-diabetic patients on systemic steroids found **hyperglycaemia in about 32%** and **diabetes in about 19%**. [Liu, Endocr Pract 2014]`,`In hospital, more than half of patients without known diabetes on high-dose steroids develop at least one glucose above 200 mg/dL. [ADA 2026]`,`India uses steroids widely - asthma/COPD exacerbations, autoimmune disease, TB meningitis and pericarditis, oncology, transplant, and over-the-counter misuse in 'quack' prescriptions. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`The **COVID-19 second wave (2021)** showed the danger: dexamethasone-driven hyperglycaemia contributed to the epidemic of **COVID-associated mucormycosis** in India (see the infections in diabetes topic). [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`**Post-transplant diabetes (PTDM)** affects about **10-40% of kidney transplant recipients**, mostly in the first year. [International PTDM consensus 2014/2024]`,`**Antipsychotics** (clozapine, olanzapine) roughly double diabetes risk; people with severe mental illness have 2-3 times the diabetes prevalence of the general population. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Dose and potency**: risk rises with prednisolone-equivalent dose (above about **20 mg/day**), pulses (methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg) and long-acting agents (dexamethasone). [JBDS 2023]`,`**Patient factors**: pre-existing diabetes or prediabetes, **HbA1c 6% or more**, older age, obesity, family history, past GDM, South Asian ethnicity. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Route**: systemic oral/IV highest; **intra-articular and epidural** injections cause transient hyperglycaemia for 2-3 days; high-dose inhaled steroids have a small effect; topical rarely. [ADA 2026]`,`**Transplant drugs**: **tacrolimus** (more than cyclosporine), glucocorticoids, sirolimus/everolimus; plus hepatitis C, CMV, obesity and older age. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**HIV**: older **protease inhibitors** (insulin resistance), **thymidine NRTIs** (stavudine, zidovudine - lipodystrophy), **integrase inhibitors** (dolutegravir - weight gain and rare early hyperglycaemia), plus hepatitis C coinfection. [ADA 2026 / NACO 2021]`,`**Antipsychotics**: highest risk **clozapine and olanzapine**, intermediate **quetiapine, risperidone, paliperidone**, lowest **aripiprazole, ziprasidone, lurasidone**. [ADA/APA consensus 2004 / ADA 2026]`,`**Other drugs**: thiazides, beta-blockers, statins (small), **immune checkpoint inhibitors** (insulin-deficient diabetes), alpelisib, L-asparaginase, pentamidine, somatostatin analogues, fluoroquinolones (dysglycaemia). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Glucocorticoids induce **PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase** in the liver, increasing **gluconeogenesis**, and supply substrate by **muscle proteolysis and lipolysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`They cause **peripheral insulin resistance** - reduced IRS-1/PI3K signalling and **GLUT4 translocation** in muscle - the dominant post-meal effect. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`They **suppress beta-cell insulin secretion** and increase glucagon - so people with limited beta-cell reserve decompensate. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Prednisolone pattern**: a single morning dose peaks in effect at **4-8 hours**, so glucose rises from **late morning, peaks in the afternoon-evening** and returns towards normal overnight - **fasting glucose may be normal**. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Dexamethasone** (half-life 36-54 hours) and **twice-daily or split doses** cause hyperglycaemia across 24 hours, including fasting. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Calcineurin inhibitors** (tacrolimus) inhibit the **calcineurin-NFAT pathway** in beta cells, reducing insulin gene transcription and beta-cell mass - partly reversible on dose reduction. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**Antipsychotics** cause weight gain through **H1 and 5-HT2C** antagonism and also have direct effects on insulin sensitivity and beta cells - DKA can occur even without weight gain. [ADA 2026]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitor (anti-PD-1/PD-L1) diabetes** is autoimmune beta-cell destruction - abrupt, often with DKA, low C-peptide and permanent insulin need. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Pre-existing diabetes worsened by steroids** vs **new steroid-induced hyperglycaemia** vs **unmasked pre-existing undiagnosed diabetes** (HbA1c 6.5% or more before or at start). [JBDS 2023]`,`**By steroid pattern**: once-daily morning (prednisolone, methylprednisolone) - afternoon peak; twice-daily or long-acting (dexamethasone) - all-day hyperglycaemia; pulses - 24-48-hour spike. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Glucocorticoid potency equivalence** (anti-inflammatory): hydrocortisone 20 mg = **prednisolone 5 mg** = methylprednisolone 4 mg = **dexamethasone 0.75 mg**. [BNF 2025]`,`**PTDM** (replaces the old term NODAT) is diagnosed once the patient is stable on maintenance immunosuppression without acute infection; hyperglycaemia in the early weeks is **transient post-transplant hyperglycaemia**. [International PTDM consensus 2014/2024]`,`**Antipsychotic metabolic risk tiers**: high (clozapine, olanzapine), moderate (quetiapine, risperidone), low (aripiprazole, lurasidone, ziprasidone). [ADA/APA consensus 2004]`,`**ADA classification**: all of these fall under **'specific types of diabetes - drug- or chemical-induced'**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Often **asymptomatic** - detected by monitoring; symptomatic patients have polyuria, thirst and fatigue in the afternoon and evening. [JBDS 2023]`,`Ask **steroid name, dose, timing, route and planned duration and taper**, and whether pulses or injections were given. [JBDS 2023]`,`Ask about **diabetes or prediabetes, family history and past GDM** - predicts who will need treatment. [JBDS 2023]`,`Ask about **over-the-counter or traditional medicines** - unlabelled steroids in some ayurvedic or 'joint pain' preparations cause Cushingoid features and hyperglycaemia. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`In **transplant recipients**: date of transplant, immunosuppressive regimen, tacrolimus levels, rejection episodes, CMV and hepatitis C status. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`In **HIV**: ART regimen and changes, duration, weight gain after switching to dolutegravir-based regimen, hepatitis B/C coinfection. [NACO 2021]`,`In **severe mental illness**: antipsychotic, duration, weight gain, smoking, diet and whether the patient can self-manage medicines. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hyperglycaemic crisis**: HHS can follow high-dose steroids in the elderly, and DKA can occur with clozapine, olanzapine or checkpoint inhibitors. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Cushingoid features** with long-term steroids: moon face, dorsocervical fat pad, purple striae, thin skin, proximal myopathy - these also raise insulin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weight, BMI, waist and BP** - steroids and antipsychotics raise both, and weight-based insulin dosing needs actual weight. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hydration and conscious level** in elderly patients on high-dose steroids - HHS presents with confusion and dehydration. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**HIV lipodystrophy**: facial and limb lipoatrophy, central fat gain, buffalo hump - markers of insulin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** suggests underlying insulin resistance that steroids or antipsychotics unmask. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infections**: oral candidiasis, skin infections, and in India **rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis** (facial pain, black eschar, proptosis) in steroid-treated diabetics. [ICMR advisory 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Unmasked type 2 diabetes** - HbA1c 6.5% or more before or soon after starting steroids; hyperglycaemia persists after the steroid is stopped. [ADA 2026]`,`**Stress hyperglycaemia** from the illness being treated (pneumonia, sepsis) - fasting and daytime values both raised; settles with recovery. [ADA 2026]`,`**Endogenous Cushing syndrome** - hyperglycaemia with Cushingoid features without exogenous steroid exposure (see the Cushing and adrenal topic). [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes** - abrupt onset, DKA, low C-peptide, often antibody negative, permanent - unlike steroid hyperglycaemia given for checkpoint toxicity. [ADA 2026]`,`**Antipsychotic-associated DKA** - can occur within weeks of starting clozapine/olanzapine, even in non-obese patients. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pancreatic diabetes** - pancreatitis (L-asparaginase, alcohol), pancreatectomy, fibrocalculous disease. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Before starting steroids** (expected over a few days): HbA1c and fasting glucose to identify unknown diabetes or prediabetes. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Monitoring on morning prednisolone**: capillary glucose **once daily before lunch or before the evening meal** (or 2 hours after lunch) - fasting tests alone miss it. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Treatment threshold (JBDS)**: capillary glucose **above 12 mmol/L (about 216 mg/dL) on two occasions** in 24 hours; ADA uses persistent values 180 mg/dL or more in hospital. [JBDS 2023 / ADA 2026]`,`**Target**: **6-10 mmol/L (108-180 mg/dL)**, acceptable 4-12 mmol/L; looser in end-of-life care. [JBDS 2023]`,`If glucose is above **20 mmol/L (360 mg/dL)**, check **ketones** (DKA) and osmolality (HHS). [JBDS 2023]`,`**PTDM diagnosis**: **OGTT is the gold standard**; HbA1c is unreliable in the first year (anaemia, transfusion, erythropoietin) - a normal HbA1c does not exclude PTDM. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**HIV**: screen with **fasting glucose before starting ART, when switching ART and 3-6 months after**, then yearly if normal; HbA1c may underestimate glycaemia in HIV. [ADA 2026]`,`**Antipsychotics**: baseline weight, waist, BP, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipids; weight at **4, 8 and 12 weeks**; glucose and lipids at **12 weeks**, then yearly. [ADA/APA consensus 2004]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - steroid-induced hyperglycaemia`,points:[`**Mild hyperglycaemia on once-daily prednisolone**: **gliclazide 40 mg in the morning**, increased by 40 mg every day or two to **240 mg/day** (up to 320 mg in two doses) - its daytime action fits the steroid curve. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Insulin of choice for morning prednisolone: NPH (isophane) once in the morning with the steroid** - its 4-8-hour peak matches the steroid effect. [JBDS 2023 / ADA 2026]`,`**Weight-based NPH dose**: about **0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone** (0.1 at 10 mg, 0.2 at 20 mg, 0.3 at 30 mg), maximum **0.4 U/kg at 40 mg or more**. [ADA 2026]`,`**JBDS alternative**: start NPH **10 units** (or 0.1 U/kg) in the morning in the insulin-naive and increase by **10-20% daily** until pre-evening glucose is in target. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Already on insulin**: increase the dose covering the afternoon (morning NPH or premix morning dose, or lunch/dinner bolus) by **10-20% daily**; basal-bolus users need larger prandial doses at lunch and dinner. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Dexamethasone or twice-daily steroids**: use **basal insulin (glargine) or twice-daily NPH**, as fasting glucose is also high. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Metformin, DPP-4 inhibitors and GLP-1 RAs** have modest effect on steroid hyperglycaemia; **SGLT2 inhibitors** raise DKA risk in the acutely ill - insulin or sulfonylurea is preferred in short courses. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Taper the glucose-lowering drug with the steroid**: reduce insulin or gliclazide in proportion to each steroid reduction, and stop when glucose normalises - **hypoglycaemia risk is highest during taper**. [JBDS 2023]`,`Avoid **evening insulin or long-acting sulfonylureas (glibenclamide)** for a morning steroid - they act overnight when steroid effect has worn off. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - high-dose and pulse steroids`,points:[`Inpatients on steroids: monitor glucose **before meals and at bedtime**; start insulin when persistently **180 mg/dL or more**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hospital NPH regimen** for morning prednisolone: NPH 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone in the morning, plus correction insulin; ADA notes that **prandial insulin needs often exceed basal needs** with steroids. [ADA 2026]`,`**Methylprednisolone pulses** (500-1000 mg IV): expect a 24-48-hour rise; use correction plus NPH on pulse days, or **IV insulin** if glucose is persistently above 300 mg/dL. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Dexamethasone 6 mg daily (as in COVID-19)**: basal glargine plus prandial insulin, as hyperglycaemia lasts through the day and night. [ADA 2026]`,`**HHS risk**: elderly on high-dose steroids with infection - check osmolality and hydration; treat as per HHS protocol. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**Type 1 diabetes on steroids**: increase basal and bolus doses (often 20-40% or more), monitor ketones, and never stop basal insulin. [JBDS 2023]`]},{heading:`Special settings - post-transplant, HIV and antipsychotics`,points:[`**PTDM early (first weeks)**: hyperglycaemia is common and managed with **basal insulin** - early insulin may protect beta cells; avoid labelling as PTDM until stable. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**PTDM later**: lifestyle, **metformin** if eGFR allows, **DPP-4 inhibitors** (no interaction with tacrolimus), GLP-1 RAs; **SGLT2 inhibitors** are increasingly used in stable kidney transplant recipients with care for UTI. [International PTDM consensus 2024 / KDIGO 2022]`,`**Immunosuppression changes** (tacrolimus dose reduction, steroid minimisation) are decided by the transplant team - never altered by the primary physician alone. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**HIV - metformin with dolutegravir**: dolutegravir raises metformin levels (AUC up by about **79%**), so **limit metformin to 1 g/day** and monitor; NACO first-line is **TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)**. [NACO 2021]`,`**HIV - other choices**: DPP-4 inhibitors, GLP-1 RAs, SGLT2 inhibitors and insulin can all be used; check interactions with **ritonavir- or cobicistat-boosted** regimens, which alter levels of several sulfonylureas and statins. [ADA 2026]`,`**Antipsychotic-induced diabetes**: switch to a lower-risk antipsychotic **only with the psychiatrist**; **metformin 500 mg BD to 1 g BD** limits antipsychotic weight gain; GLP-1 RAs are effective. [ADA 2026 / NICE 2014]`,`**Severe mental illness**: integrated care, simple regimens, supervised medication and carers; screen yearly for diabetes and CV risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes**: **insulin for life**; teach DKA warning signs to all patients on anti-PD-1 therapy. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Other special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: antenatal steroids for fetal lung maturity (betamethasone 12 mg IM x2, 24 hours apart) raise glucose for about 72 hours - increase insulin and monitor closely, using a VRIII if needed. [JBDS 2023 / NICE NG3]`,`**Elderly**: prefer gliclazide or NPH with conservative titration; hypoglycaemia during taper is the main hazard. [JBDS 2023]`,`**CKD**: lower insulin doses and avoid sulfonylureas in advanced CKD; prefer NPH or basal insulin. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**End of life** (palliative steroids): relax targets to about **108-270 mg/dL (6-15 mmol/L)** and avoid symptomatic hyperglycaemia; once-daily morning NPH or gliclazide. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Intra-articular injections**: warn people with diabetes of a rise for **2-3 days** and to check glucose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: human NPH in the morning with afternoon capillary checks, or gliclazide, is cheap, effective and available under NP-NCD and Jan Aushadhi. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**HHS and DKA** with high-dose steroids, especially in the elderly and in undiagnosed diabetes. [ADA/JBDS 2024 consensus]`,`**Infections**: mucormycosis, TB reactivation, candidiasis, poor wound healing - hyperglycaemia and steroids impair immunity together. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`**Hypoglycaemia during steroid taper** if insulin or sulfonylurea is not reduced. [JBDS 2023]`,`Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia usually **resolves within 24-48 hours** of stopping short courses, but many patients have underlying prediabetes and later develop diabetes. [JBDS 2023]`,`**PTDM** increases **graft loss, cardiovascular events and mortality** after kidney transplant. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**Severe mental illness with diabetes** carries 10-20 years lower life expectancy, mostly from cardiovascular disease. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`Give every patient starting steroids **written advice** on symptoms of hyperglycaemia and when to test; diabetics get a **steroid sick-day plan**. [JBDS 2023]`,`After steroids stop, recheck **HbA1c at 3 months** (or fasting glucose/OGTT) in anyone who developed hyperglycaemia. [JBDS 2023]`,`Use the **lowest steroid dose for the shortest time**; consider steroid-sparing agents and inhaled/topical routes. [ADA 2026]`,`Refer: glucose persistently above 360 mg/dL, ketones, HHS, type 1 on steroids, PTDM, checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes, pregnancy. [JBDS 2023]`,`Link to **NP-NCD** screening at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir; **NTEP** bidirectional TB-diabetes screening in steroid-treated diabetics. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023 / NTEP 2024]`,`Transplant and HIV patients: **shared care** with transplant team or ART centre; yearly diabetes screening. [NACO 2021 / KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**JBDS-IP 'Management of hyperglycaemia and steroid (glucocorticoid) therapy' (2023 update)** keeps gliclazide or morning NPH as first choices and the 12 mmol/L treatment threshold. [JBDS 2023]`,`**ADA Standards of Care 2026 Section 16** retains weight-based NPH dosing for steroids and notes prandial needs exceed basal. [ADA 2026]`,`**ADA 2026 Section 2** lists drug-induced diabetes (glucocorticoids, calcineurin inhibitors, antipsychotics, checkpoint inhibitors) and HIV screening with fasting glucose around ART changes. [ADA 2026]`,`**International PTDM consensus update (2024)** reaffirmed OGTT-based diagnosis after stabilisation and supports newer agents (SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 RAs) in stable recipients. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`**India**: ICMR COVID-19 advisories (2021) on judicious steroid use and glucose control to prevent mucormycosis remain the reference lesson. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`Sources differ on the treatment threshold - **JBDS 216 mg/dL x2** vs **ADA 180 mg/dL persistently** in hospital; both accept a 140-180 working target. [JBDS 2023 vs ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Steroid-induced diabetes - pathogenesis and management'** - draw the afternoon glucose curve against prednisolone timing. [DNB pattern]`,`Viva: why is fasting glucose normal? - **single morning prednisolone dose wears off overnight**. [JBDS 2023]`,`Viva: which insulin and when? - **NPH in the morning with the steroid**. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: adding bedtime insulin or glibenclamide - causes nocturnal hypoglycaemia. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: not reducing insulin as the steroid is tapered. [JBDS 2023]`,`Viva: why is HbA1c unreliable after transplant? - anaemia, transfusions and erythropoietin; use OGTT. [International PTDM consensus 2024]`,`Viva: metformin dose limit with dolutegravir? - **1 g/day**. [NACO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for steroid-induced and drug-induced hyperglycaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Gliclazide`,`40 mg oral in the morning with steroid`,`Increase 40 mg every 1-2 days to 240 mg (max 320 mg in two doses); taper with steroid`,`Hypoglycaemia during taper; avoid advanced CKD`],[`NPH (isophane) insulin`,`0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone SC in the morning (max 0.4 U/kg); or 10 U start`,`Increase 10-20% daily; reduce with steroid taper`,`Hypoglycaemia if steroid stopped or reduced`],[`Basal glargine or NPH BD`,`0.1-0.2 U/kg/day SC for dexamethasone or BD steroids`,`Titrate daily`,`Nocturnal hypoglycaemia once steroid stops`],[`Metformin (with dolutegravir)`,`500 mg BD, maximum 1 g/day`,`Long term`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency; lactic acidosis in AKI`],[`Metformin (antipsychotic weight gain)`,`500 mg BD up to 1 g BD`,`Long term`,`GI upset; hold in AKI`],[`DPP-4 inhibitor (PTDM)`,`Sitagliptin 100 mg OD (renal dose 25-50 mg)`,`Long term`,`Rare pancreatitis; no tacrolimus interaction`]]},{heading:`Steroid type and glucose pattern`,columns:[`Steroid regimen`,`Glucose pattern`,`Best monitoring time`,`Preferred treatment`],rows:[[`Prednisolone once in the morning`,`Normal fasting, peak afternoon-evening`,`Before lunch or before evening meal`,`Morning NPH or gliclazide`],[`Prednisolone twice daily`,`Raised through day, partly overnight`,`Pre-meal and fasting`,`NPH BD or basal-bolus`],[`Dexamethasone (any timing)`,`Raised all 24 hours`,`Fasting and pre-meal`,`Basal glargine plus prandial`],[`Methylprednisolone pulse`,`Spike for 24-48 hours`,`4-6 hourly`,`NPH plus correction or IV insulin`],[`Intra-articular injection`,`Rise for 2-3 days`,`Daily pre-dinner`,`Temporary dose increase`]]},{heading:`Drug-induced diabetes - special settings compared`,columns:[`Setting`,`Culprit drugs`,`Mechanism`,`Diagnosis tip`,`Management tip`],rows:[[`Glucocorticoids`,`Prednisolone, dexamethasone`,`Gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance, beta-cell suppression`,`Test afternoon, not fasting`,`Morning NPH, taper with steroid`],[`Post-transplant`,`Tacrolimus, steroids, mTOR inhibitors`,`Calcineurin-NFAT beta-cell toxicity`,`OGTT; HbA1c unreliable first year`,`Early insulin; DPP-4i, metformin later`],[`HIV`,`Old PIs, stavudine, zidovudine, dolutegravir`,`Insulin resistance, lipodystrophy, weight gain`,`FPG before and 3-6 months after ART change`,`Metformin max 1 g with dolutegravir`],[`Antipsychotics`,`Clozapine, olanzapine`,`Weight gain (H1, 5-HT2C), direct effects`,`Weight at 4, 8, 12 weeks; glucose at 12 weeks`,`Metformin; switch drug with psychiatrist`],[`Checkpoint inhibitors`,`Anti-PD-1, anti-PD-L1`,`Autoimmune beta-cell destruction`,`Abrupt DKA, low C-peptide`,`Lifelong insulin`]]},{heading:`Monitoring thresholds and targets`,columns:[`Item`,`Cut-off`,`Source`],rows:[[`Start treatment (outpatient)`,`Capillary glucose above 216 mg/dL (12 mmol/L) twice in 24 h`,`JBDS 2023`],[`Start insulin (inpatient)`,`Persistently 180 mg/dL or more`,`ADA 2026`],[`Target range`,`108-180 mg/dL (6-10 mmol/L), acceptable 72-216`,`JBDS 2023`],[`Check ketones`,`Glucose above 360 mg/dL (20 mmol/L)`,`JBDS 2023`],[`End-of-life target`,`108-270 mg/dL (6-15 mmol/L)`,`JBDS 2023`],[`Steroid equivalence`,`Prednisolone 5 mg = hydrocortisone 20 mg = dexamethasone 0.75 mg`,`BNF 2025`]]}],redFlags:[`Glucose above 360 mg/dL with ketones, vomiting or drowsiness - DKA.`,`Elderly patient on high-dose steroids with confusion, dehydration and glucose over 600 mg/dL - HHS.`,`Facial pain, nasal black eschar, proptosis or vision loss in a steroid-treated diabetic - mucormycosis; emergency referral.`,`Sudden DKA in a patient on anti-PD-1 therapy - checkpoint-inhibitor diabetes.`,`DKA within weeks of starting clozapine or olanzapine.`,`Hypoglycaemia after steroid stopped or tapered without reducing insulin or sulfonylurea.`,`Rising creatinine or rejection signs in a transplant recipient with new hyperglycaemia - transplant team review.`],pearls:[`**Morning steroid, afternoon sugar** - test before lunch or dinner, not fasting.`,`**NPH with the prednisolone, at the same time** - 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg, max 0.4 U/kg.`,`**Taper the insulin with the steroid** - or meet hypoglycaemia.`,`**Dexamethasone = all-day sugar** - needs basal insulin.`,`**Prednisolone 5 = hydrocortisone 20 = dexamethasone 0.75**.`,`**PTDM: OGTT, not HbA1c**, in the first year.`,`**Dolutegravir + metformin = cap at 1 g/day**.`,`**Clozapine and olanzapine** top the antipsychotic risk list; aripiprazole is the safest.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-q1`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman without known diabetes is started on prednisolone 40 mg each morning for polymyalgia-like illness. Which glucose test is most likely to detect steroid-induced hyperglycaemia?`,options:[`Fasting plasma glucose at 7 am`,`Capillary glucose at 3 am`,`Capillary glucose before the evening meal`,`HbA1c after one week`],answer:2,explanation:`A single morning prednisolone dose raises glucose from late morning, peaking in the afternoon and evening, and the effect wanes overnight, so pre-evening-meal (or pre-lunch) testing is recommended. Fasting and 3 am values are often normal, and HbA1c changes too slowly to reflect a week of steroid exposure.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-q2`,stem:`A 70 kg man on prednisolone 30 mg every morning has pre-dinner glucose of 280-320 mg/dL and fasting 105 mg/dL. Which insulin regimen fits best?`,options:[`Glargine 20 units at bedtime`,`NPH about 21 units in the morning with the prednisolone`,`Regular insulin sliding scale at bedtime`,`Premixed 30/70 insulin before dinner`],answer:1,explanation:`Weight-based dosing is about 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone, giving 0.3 U/kg (about 21 units) of NPH in the morning, whose 4-8-hour peak matches the afternoon steroid effect. Bedtime glargine or a dinner premix would act overnight when glucose is already normal and cause nocturnal hypoglycaemia, and bedtime sliding scale misses the daytime peak.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-q3`,stem:`A kidney transplant recipient 5 months after surgery on tacrolimus has HbA1c 6.1%, mild anaemia and fasting glucose 118 mg/dL. What is the best test to diagnose post-transplant diabetes?`,options:[`Repeat HbA1c in 3 months`,`Random capillary glucose`,`Serum fructosamine`,`75 g oral glucose tolerance test`],answer:3,explanation:`The international PTDM consensus regards the OGTT as the gold standard because HbA1c is unreliable in the first year after transplant due to anaemia, transfusions and erythropoietin, and HbA1c under 6.5% does not exclude PTDM. Random capillary glucose and fructosamine lack diagnostic validation here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-q4`,stem:`A man with HIV on tenofovir, lamivudine and dolutegravir is newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. What is the key prescribing point for metformin?`,options:[`Metformin is contraindicated with dolutegravir`,`Use metformin but limit the total dose to about 1 g/day`,`Metformin must be given at a minimum of 2 g/day`,`Switch dolutegravir to a protease inhibitor before starting metformin`],answer:1,explanation:`Dolutegravir inhibits the OCT2 and MATE transporters, raising metformin exposure by about 79%, so NACO advises limiting metformin to about 1 g/day with monitoring rather than avoiding it. Higher doses increase GI effects and lactic acidosis risk, and switching effective first-line ART to a protease inhibitor would worsen insulin resistance without need.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-q5`,stem:`Which antipsychotic carries the lowest risk of weight gain and diabetes?`,options:[`Olanzapine`,`Clozapine`,`Aripiprazole`,`Quetiapine`],answer:2,explanation:`Aripiprazole, a partial D2 agonist with little H1 and 5-HT2C blockade, is among the lowest metabolic-risk antipsychotics. Clozapine and olanzapine carry the highest risk of weight gain, diabetes and even DKA, and quetiapine is intermediate.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, glucose pattern, monitoring and management of steroid-induced hyperglycaemia. Add a note on post-transplant diabetes.`,openingLines:[`Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia is new or worsened hyperglycaemia during glucocorticoid therapy, caused by increased hepatic gluconeogenesis, peripheral insulin resistance and beta-cell suppression.`,`With morning prednisolone, fasting glucose is often normal but afternoon-evening glucose is high, so it is detected by pre-lunch or pre-dinner testing and treated with morning NPH or gliclazide tapered with the steroid.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`PEPCK induction - gluconeogenesis; proteolysis and lipolysis supply substrate.`,`Reduced GLUT4 translocation - insulin resistance; beta-cell suppression, raised glucagon.`,`Risk: dose above 20 mg prednisolone, prediabetes, age, obesity, family history.`]},{heading:`Glucose pattern`,points:[`Morning prednisolone: peak 4-8 hours, normal fasting.`,`Dexamethasone or BD steroids: all-day hyperglycaemia.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Baseline HbA1c; daily pre-lunch or pre-dinner capillary glucose.`,`Treat if above 216 mg/dL twice (JBDS) or persistently 180 or more in hospital (ADA); target 108-180.`,`Ketones if above 360 mg/dL.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Gliclazide 40 mg morning up to 240-320 mg/day.`,`NPH in the morning: 0.1 U/kg per 10 mg prednisolone, max 0.4 U/kg; titrate 10-20% daily.`,`Dexamethasone: basal glargine plus prandial.`,`Taper insulin with steroid; recheck HbA1c at 3 months after stopping.`]},{heading:`Post-transplant diabetes`,points:[`10-40% after kidney transplant; tacrolimus, steroids.`,`OGTT after stabilisation; HbA1c unreliable in year 1.`,`Early basal insulin; later metformin, DPP-4 inhibitors, GLP-1 RA, SGLT2 inhibitors with care.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph: glucose curve over 24 hours with morning prednisolone vs NPH action profile`,`Table: steroid regimen vs glucose pattern vs treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Glucose pattern and monitoring`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management`,marks:3.5},{part:`Post-transplant diabetes`,marks:2}],keywords:[`prednisolone`,`NPH`,`gliclazide`,`JBDS`,`PTDM`,`tacrolimus`]},{id:`endocrine-steroid-induced-hyperglycaemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on antipsychotic-induced diabetes and metabolic monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Second-generation antipsychotics, especially clozapine and olanzapine, cause weight gain, insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia and diabetes, occasionally presenting as DKA.`,`Structured metabolic monitoring from baseline and early intervention reduce the excess cardiovascular mortality of severe mental illness.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk tiers and mechanism`,points:[`High: clozapine, olanzapine; moderate: quetiapine, risperidone; low: aripiprazole, lurasidone, ziprasidone.`,`H1 and 5-HT2C antagonism - appetite and weight gain; direct insulin resistance.`]},{heading:`Monitoring (ADA/APA)`,points:[`Baseline weight, waist, BP, FPG or HbA1c, lipids, family history.`,`Weight at 4, 8, 12 weeks; glucose and lipids at 12 weeks; then yearly.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Lifestyle; metformin 500 mg BD to 1 g BD for weight gain or hyperglycaemia.`,`GLP-1 RA if needed; switch antipsychotic only with the psychiatrist.`,`Watch for DKA early in therapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Monitoring timeline table (baseline, 4, 8, 12 weeks, yearly)`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk tiers and mechanism`,marks:1.5},{part:`Monitoring`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`clozapine`,`olanzapine`,`aripiprazole`,`metabolic monitoring`,`metformin`]}],references:[`Joint British Diabetes Societies for Inpatient Care (JBDS-IP). Management of hyperglycaemia and steroid (glucocorticoid) therapy, 2023 update`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Sections 2 and 16`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Sharif A et al. International consensus on post-transplantation diabetes mellitus, 2014, with 2024 update`,`National AIDS Control Organisation (NACO). National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`,`ADA/APA/AACE/NAASO Consensus Development Conference on Antipsychotic Drugs and Obesity and Diabetes, Diabetes Care 2004`,`Liu XX et al. Hyperglycemia induced by glucocorticoids in nondiabetic patients: a meta-analysis, Endocrine Practice 2014`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2023`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease`,title:`Diabetic kidney disease`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Diabetic kidney disease (DKD)** is CKD attributed to diabetes - a **persistently raised urine albumin-creatinine ratio (UACR 30 mg/g or more)** and/or **eGFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m2** for more than 3 months - managed on four pillars: **maximum-tolerated ACE inhibitor or ARB, SGLT2 inhibitor (eGFR 20 or more), finerenone for residual albuminuria and a GLP-1 receptor agonist**, on a base of BP, glucose, lipid and lifestyle control.`,keywords:[`DKD`,`diabetic nephropathy`,`CKD in diabetes`,`UACR`,`microalbuminuria`,`moderately increased albuminuria`,`Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules`,`KDIGO 2022`,`KDIGO 2024`,`finerenone`,`FIDELIO-DKD`,`FLOW trial`,`semaglutide`,`dapagliflozin`,`empagliflozin`,`CREDENCE`,`KFRE`,`eGFR dip`,`renal dosing`,`hyperkalaemia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**DKD** is a clinical diagnosis: CKD (UACR **30 mg/g or more** and/or eGFR **under 60 mL/min/1.73 m2** for **more than 3 months**) in a person with diabetes, with no feature pointing to another cause - biopsy-proven lesions are called **diabetic nephropathy**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`About **30-40%** of people with diabetes develop CKD; diabetes is the **leading cause of kidney failure** worldwide, accounting for roughly 30-50% of new dialysis starts. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the **Indian CKD Registry**, diabetic nephropathy was the **commonest single cause of CKD (about 31%)**. [Indian CKD Registry 2012]`,`In urban Chennai (**CURES**), microalbuminuria was found in about **27%** and overt nephropathy in about **2%** of people with type 2 diabetes. [CURES 2007]`,`In **type 1 diabetes** albuminuria rarely appears before **5 years** of disease; in **type 2 diabetes** up to 10-20% already have albuminuria or low eGFR at diagnosis because onset is silent. [ADA 2026]`,`Most people with DKD **die of cardiovascular disease before reaching dialysis** - albuminuria and low eGFR each independently multiply cardiovascular risk. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Up to **half of type 2 patients with low eGFR have no albuminuria** (non-albuminuric DKD), so screening must use **both UACR and eGFR**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Duration and glycaemia**: risk rises with years of diabetes and HbA1c; DCCT/EDIC and UKPDS showed intensive control lowers incident albuminuria by about **30-40%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertension** both causes and results from DKD; uncontrolled BP accelerates eGFR loss more than any other modifiable factor. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Genetic susceptibility**: familial clustering, and higher risk in South Asians, African and Indigenous populations than in white Europeans at the same HbA1c. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obesity, smoking, dyslipidaemia** and **high dietary sodium and protein** add to progression. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Low nephron number** from fetal undernutrition (low birth weight) is proposed as a reason Indians develop CKD earlier. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent AKI** - dehydration, sepsis, NSAIDs, contrast and herbal or indigenous medicines - steps CKD down; ask specifically about painkiller and Ayurvedic powder use in India. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Diabetic retinopathy** is a marker of microvascular susceptibility - its presence makes DKD the likely cause of kidney disease, especially in type 1. [KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Hyperfiltration first**: high filtered glucose up-regulates proximal tubular **SGLT2**, so more sodium is reabsorbed with glucose; less NaCl reaches the **macula densa**, tubuloglomerular feedback dilates the afferent arteriole and glomerular pressure rises. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angiotensin II** constricts the **efferent arteriole**, further raising intraglomerular pressure - the reason **ACE inhibitors and ARBs** (efferent dilation) and **SGLT2 inhibitors** (restore TGF, afferent constriction) both protect the kidney and both cause an early, acceptable **eGFR dip**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Metabolic injury**: advanced glycation end-products, PKC activation, the polyol pathway and oxidative stress thicken the **glomerular basement membrane** and expand the **mesangium**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Podocyte loss and slit-diaphragm damage** let albumin through; filtered albumin is itself toxic to tubules, driving **tubulointerstitial inflammation and fibrosis** - which correlates best with eGFR loss. [Brenner and Rector 11e]`,`**Mineralocorticoid receptor overactivation** in kidney and heart promotes inflammation and fibrosis independent of BP - the target of the **non-steroidal MRA finerenone**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules)** - PAS-positive mesangial nodules - is the classic lesion; **arteriolar hyalinosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles** is characteristic of diabetes. [Robbins 10e]`,`Kidneys stay **normal-sized or enlarged** in DKD until late, unlike most CKD - so normal-sized kidneys on ultrasound do not exclude advanced disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Insulin is partly cleared by the kidney**: as eGFR falls, insulin and sulfonylureas accumulate and renal gluconeogenesis falls, so **hypoglycaemia becomes more common and HbA1c may fall without treatment change**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**KDIGO CGA staging** is the one to write: Cause, GFR category (**G1 90 or more, G2 60-89, G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 under 15**) and Albuminuria category (**A1 under 30, A2 30-300, A3 over 300 mg/g**). [KDIGO 2024]`,`The **heat map** combines G and A: green (low risk), yellow, orange and red (very high risk) - it sets monitoring frequency (**1, 1, 2, 3 and 4 or more times a year**) and referral. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Mogensen stages** (type 1, historical): 1 hyperfiltration/hypertrophy, 2 silent lesions, 3 incipient nephropathy (microalbuminuria), 4 overt nephropathy (macroalbuminuria, falling GFR), 5 ESKD. [Mogensen 1983]`,`Old terms: **microalbuminuria** = 30-300 mg/g (now **moderately increased, A2**); **macroalbuminuria** = over 300 mg/g (now **severely increased, A3**); KDIGO recommends the new terms. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Renal Pathology Society classification** (Tervaert 2010): class I GBM thickening, IIa mild and IIb severe mesangial expansion, **III nodular sclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson)**, IV advanced sclerosis (over 50% glomeruli). [RPS 2010]`,`Phenotypes: **albuminuric with falling eGFR** (classic), **non-albuminuric DKD** (older, vascular, type 2), and **rapid decliners** (eGFR loss over 5 mL/min/year). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Usually **asymptomatic** until G4 - picked up only on UACR and eGFR screening; this is the core message for OPD practice. [ADA 2026]`,`**Frothy urine** suggests heavy proteinuria; **swelling of feet and face** suggests nephrotic-range proteinuria or fluid retention. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uraemic symptoms** in G4-G5: anorexia, nausea, morning vomiting, pruritus, fatigue, hiccups, restless legs, reduced urine output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Falling insulin needs or new hypoglycaemia** in a long-standing diabetic is a clue to declining eGFR. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask for **co-existing microvascular disease** - visual loss (retinopathy), tingling feet (neuropathy) - and for **ischaemic heart disease, stroke, claudication**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Drug history**: NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, lithium, PPIs, herbal powders, contrast exposure, and current doses of metformin, sulfonylureas and gliptins. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Atypical clues to another disease**: haematuria, rash, joint pains, haemoptysis, sudden-onset nephrotic syndrome or rapidly rising creatinine. [KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Blood pressure** seated after 5 minutes, both arms at first visit, and **standing BP** (autonomic neuropathy causes orthostatic hypotension that limits BP drugs). [KDIGO 2021 BP]`,`**Pedal and periorbital oedema**, ascites or pleural effusion with heavy albuminuria (nephrotic picture). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor** - anaemia of CKD appears earlier in diabetes than in other CKD at the same eGFR. [KDIGO 2012 anaemia]`,`**Dilated fundoscopy**: retinopathy supports DKD; its absence in a type 1 patient with proteinuria prompts a search for another cause. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Feet**: neuropathy, pulses and ulcers - DKD patients have a higher amputation risk. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Uraemic signs** late: sallow skin, scratch marks, asterixis, pericardial rub, Kussmaul breathing of metabolic acidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal bruit** with resistant hypertension or a big eGFR fall on ACE inhibitor suggests **renal artery stenosis**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Non-diabetic kidney disease** coexists in up to a third of biopsied type 2 diabetics with atypical features. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect another cause if: **no retinopathy** (especially type 1), **diabetes under 5 years** (type 1), **active sediment** (dysmorphic RBC, RBC casts), **sudden nephrotic syndrome**, **rapid eGFR fall**, or systemic disease features. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Hypertensive nephrosclerosis**: long-standing hypertension, modest proteinuria, small kidneys, often no retinopathy beyond hypertensive changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renovascular disease**: eGFR fall over 30% after starting ACE inhibitor/ARB, flash pulmonary oedema, asymmetric kidneys. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Membranous nephropathy, IgA nephropathy, MCD and FSGS** are the commonest non-diabetic lesions on biopsy in Indian series; anti-PLA2R antibody helps with membranous. [KDIGO 2021 GN]`,`**Obstructive uropathy** (prostate, neurogenic bladder from autonomic neuropathy) and **chronic pyelonephritis/papillary necrosis** from recurrent UTI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transient albuminuria** - fever, exercise in last 24 h, UTI, heart failure, marked hyperglycaemia, menstruation - repeat UACR before labelling. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Spot morning UACR** is the screening test (not dipstick); confirm with **2 of 3 samples abnormal over 3-6 months**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Serum creatinine with eGFR by the race-free CKD-EPI 2021 equation**; add **cystatin C (eGFRcr-cys)** when creatinine is unreliable - extremes of muscle mass, amputees, vegetarians, cirrhosis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Urine microscopy** for RBCs and casts; a positive dipstick with no albumin suggests non-albumin protein (myeloma, tubular). [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Serum potassium, bicarbonate, haemoglobin, calcium, phosphate, PTH and 25-OH vitamin D** from G3a onwards. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Ultrasound kidneys**: size, echogenicity, obstruction, post-void residual; asymmetry over 1.5 cm suggests renovascular disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HbA1c is unreliable in G4-G5** (anaemia, iron, ESA, uraemia) - use CGM-derived **glucose management indicator** or SMBG. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Kidney Failure Risk Equation (KFRE)** - age, sex, eGFR, UACR - gives the 2- and 5-year risk of kidney failure and guides referral. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Kidney biopsy** only when non-diabetic disease is suspected (see differential) - not for routine DKD. [KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Lifestyle**: salt under **2 g sodium (5 g salt) a day**, protein **0.8 g/kg/day** (avoid over 1.3 g/kg), **150 minutes/week** moderate exercise, stop smoking, weight loss if obese. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**ACE inhibitor or ARB** (e.g. **ramipril 2.5 mg OD up to 10 mg**, telmisartan 40-80 mg, losartan 50-100 mg) for diabetes with hypertension **and** albuminuria, titrated to the **maximum tolerated dose**; never combine ACEi with ARB. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Check **creatinine and potassium 2-4 weeks** after starting or raising RAS blockade; **accept creatinine rise up to 30%**; hold or reduce if K over 5.5 mmol/L. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor** - **dapagliflozin 10 mg OD or empagliflozin 10 mg OD** - for all type 2 diabetes with CKD and **eGFR 20 or more**, irrespective of HbA1c; continue until dialysis or transplant. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Finerenone** for type 2 diabetes with **eGFR 25 or more, UACR 30 mg/g or more and normal potassium** despite maximum RAS blockade: **10 mg OD if eGFR 25-59, 20 mg OD if 60 or more**; increase to 20 mg at 4 weeks if K 4.8 or less. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**GLP-1 receptor agonist** with proven kidney benefit - **semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly SC, titrated to 1 mg** (FLOW) - when glycaemic targets are not met or for extra cardiorenal protection; usable down to eGFR 15 without dose change. [ADA 2026]`,`**Metformin** while eGFR 30 or more - full dose if 45 or more, **maximum 1000 mg/day at 30-44**, **stop below 30**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Statin** for all with diabetes and CKD (e.g. **atorvastatin 20-40 mg**, no renal adjustment); aspirin only for established ASCVD. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**HbA1c target individualised from under 6.5% to under 8.0%** - looser with advanced CKD, hypoglycaemia risk, short life expectancy. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**BP target**: SBP **under 120 mmHg** if tolerated with standardised measurement (KDIGO 2021) or **under 130/80** (ADA 2026) - give both and use clinical judgement in the frail. [KDIGO 2021 BP]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for AKI on CKD, K over 6.0 mmol/L or ECG changes, fluid overload/pulmonary oedema, uraemic encephalopathy or pericarditis, severe metabolic acidosis, or recurrent hypoglycaemia. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Hyperkalaemia**: ECG; **calcium gluconate 10% 10 mL IV over 5-10 min** if ECG changes; **regular insulin 10 units IV with 25 g glucose** (50 mL of 50% dextrose); salbutamol 10-20 mg nebulised; then a binder and stop culprits. [UK Kidney Association 2023]`,`Potassium binders: **sodium zirconium cyclosilicate** or **patiromer** enable continuation of RAS blockade/finerenone where available; **calcium polystyrene sulfonate 15 g 3-4 times daily** is the widely available Indian option. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**AKI**: stop metformin, SGLT2i, ACEi/ARB, finerenone, diuretics and NSAIDs while acutely ill (sick-day rules); restart once eating and creatinine is stable. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Insulin in hospital**: reduce doses by about **25%** when eGFR falls sharply; prefer basal-bolus over sliding scale; watch for prolonged hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas (needs dextrose infusion and observation). [ADA 2026]`,`**Emergency dialysis indications (AEIOU)**: refractory **A**cidosis, **E**lectrolytes (K), **I**ntoxication, refractory **O**verload, **U**raemic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contrast**: avoid unnecessary contrast; give isotonic saline pre-hydration if eGFR under 30 and hold metformin 48 h around iodinated contrast when eGFR is under 30 or AKI is present. [ACR Contrast Manual 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: stop ACEi/ARB, SGLT2i, finerenone and GLP-1RA before conception; use **labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa** and insulin; DKD raises **pre-eclampsia** risk - start **aspirin 150 mg at night from 12 weeks**. [FOGSI 2023]`,`**Type 1 diabetes**: ACEi/ARB for albuminuria; SGLT2i and finerenone are **not licensed** in type 1 (DKA risk; trials ongoing). [ADA 2026]`,`**Children and adolescents with type 1**: start UACR screening at **puberty or age 10, after 5 years of diabetes**, then yearly. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Elderly and frail**: relax HbA1c (under 8.0%) and SBP targets, watch orthostatic hypotension and hyperkalaemia, and stop drugs that only treat numbers. [ADA 2026]`,`**Dialysis**: stop SGLT2i, metformin, finerenone; insulin (reduced doses), linagliptin, GLP-1RA (with caution) and short-acting glipizide are options. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Kidney transplant**: post-transplant diabetes is common with tacrolimus and steroids; SGLT2i use after transplant is expanding but guidelines do not yet mandate it. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: telmisartan/losartan, generic dapagliflozin and atorvastatin are low-cost; **UACR by point-of-care strip** or, failing that, the **urine protein-creatinine ratio** is acceptable. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular events and heart failure** are the leading cause of death; each step up in albuminuria and down in eGFR increases risk. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Anaemia** (erythropoietin deficiency, iron deficiency), **CKD-mineral bone disorder** (hyperphosphataemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism) and **metabolic acidosis** from G3b. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Hyperkalaemia** - type 4 RTA (hyporeninaemic hypoaldosteronism) is common in DKD even at modest eGFR, and worsens with RAS blockade. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** from reduced insulin clearance and sulfonylurea accumulation. [ADA 2026]`,`Untreated macroalbuminuria loses eGFR at about **10-12 mL/min/year**; modern four-pillar therapy can slow this to near-physiological decline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Survival on dialysis** is worse in diabetics than non-diabetics, largely from cardiovascular disease and infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **fall in albuminuria by 30% or more** on treatment predicts slower progression and is a useful treatment response marker. [KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Screen** UACR and eGFR **at diagnosis of type 2** and **5 years after diagnosis of type 1**, then **at least yearly**; more often with established CKD per the heat map. [ADA 2026]`,`**Refer to nephrology** if eGFR **under 30**, persistent **UACR 300 mg/g or more**, sustained eGFR fall over **25%** or over **5 mL/min/year**, uncertain cause, refractory hyperkalaemia or resistant hypertension. [KDIGO 2024]`,`KDIGO 2024 also advises referral when the **5-year KFRE risk is 3-5% or more**, and planning for kidney replacement therapy when the **2-year risk exceeds 40%**. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Monitor** UACR and eGFR (frequency by heat map), potassium after every RAS/finerenone change, HbA1c 3-6 monthly, lipids yearly, Hb and bone profile from G3. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Vaccinate**: hepatitis B (before dialysis), influenza yearly, pneumococcal. [ADA 2026]`,`**Protect veins** in the non-dominant arm in G4 for future fistula - avoid cannulation and blood draws there. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Good glycaemic and BP control from diagnosis** prevents albuminuria - the legacy effect of early control persists for decades (UKPDS, DCCT/EDIC). [Harrison 22e]`,`**NP-NCD** screening at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** (over 30 years) identifies diabetes and hypertension; UACR and creatinine should follow at the CHC or district NCD clinic. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Pradhan Mantri National Dialysis Programme** provides free haemodialysis (and peritoneal dialysis) at district hospitals for BPL families under PPP mode. [MoHFW PMNDP 2016]`,`Counsel on **NSAID avoidance**, herbal/indigenous medicine risks, hydration in heat and illness, and **sick-day rules** (see Diabetes in special situations topic). [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Jan Aushadhi** stocks telmisartan, losartan, atorvastatin, metformin and SGLT2 inhibitors at low cost - cost is the major barrier to four-pillar therapy in India. [PMBJP 2024]`,`Teach the patient to know their **eGFR and UACR numbers** and to show their drug list to every prescriber. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**KDIGO 2022 Diabetes in CKD**: SGLT2i for eGFR **20 or more** (was 30), continue until dialysis; finerenone for residual albuminuria; GLP-1RA as preferred add-on; **HbA1c target under 6.5% to under 8.0%**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**KDIGO 2024 CKD guideline**: race-free CKD-EPI 2021, wider cystatin C use, KFRE-based referral, and **SGLT2i for CKD with eGFR 20 or more** in diabetes and many non-diabetic patients. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**FLOW trial (2024)**: **semaglutide 1 mg weekly** cut major kidney disease events by **24%** in type 2 diabetes with CKD - first GLP-1RA with a dedicated kidney-outcome indication. [NEJM 2024]`,`**ADA Standards 2025-2026**: recommend a GLP-1RA with demonstrated benefit (semaglutide) to reduce kidney and CV outcomes in T2D with CKD, alongside SGLT2i and finerenone. [ADA 2026]`,`**CONFIDENCE (2025)**: starting **finerenone plus empagliflozin together** lowered UACR more (about 52%) than either alone (about 30%) - supports simultaneous rather than sequential pillars. [NEJM 2025]`,`**FINEARTS-HF (2024)**: finerenone reduced heart failure events in HFmrEF/HFpEF - another reason it fits the cardiorenal patient. [NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: **'Discuss screening, staging and management of diabetic nephropathy'** - draw the KDIGO heat map and the four-pillar ladder. [Exam pattern]`,`Viva: **why does eGFR dip after SGLT2i or ACEi?** - lowered intraglomerular pressure; up to 30% is acceptable and predicts long-term benefit. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Common mistake: using **dipstick protein** for screening, or labelling albuminuria from a **single UACR**. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: **stopping SGLT2i because eGFR is under 45** - glycaemic effect falls, but kidney and heart protection continues down to 20. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Common mistake: **combining ACEi and ARB** (ONTARGET, VA NEPHRON-D - more hyperkalaemia and AKI, no benefit). [KDIGO 2022]`,`Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules = **nodular glomerulosclerosis, RPS class III**; also seen in light-chain deposition and amyloid (differential on biopsy). [Robbins 10e]`,`Know trial names: **RENAAL, IDNT** (ARBs), **CREDENCE, DAPA-CKD, EMPA-KIDNEY** (SGLT2i), **FIDELIO/FIGARO-DKD** (finerenone), **FLOW** (semaglutide). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`KDIGO CGA staging and monitoring frequency (times per year)`,columns:[`GFR category (mL/min/1.73 m2)`,`A1: UACR under 30 mg/g`,`A2: UACR 30-300 mg/g`,`A3: UACR over 300 mg/g`],rows:[[`G1: 90 or more`,`Low risk - 1 (if CKD)`,`Moderate - 1`,`High - 2`],[`G2: 60-89`,`Low risk - 1 (if CKD)`,`Moderate - 1`,`High - 2`],[`G3a: 45-59`,`Moderate - 1`,`High - 2`,`Very high - 3`],[`G3b: 30-44`,`High - 2`,`Very high - 3`,`Very high - 3`],[`G4: 15-29`,`Very high - 3`,`Very high - 3`,`Very high - 4 or more`],[`G5: under 15`,`Very high - 4 or more`,`Very high - 4 or more`,`Very high - 4 or more`]]},{heading:`Kidney-protective drugs in diabetic kidney disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ramipril (ACEi)`,`2.5 mg OD oral, up to 10 mg OD`,`Double every 2-4 weeks to maximum tolerated; lifelong`,`Cough, angioedema, hyperkalaemia, AKI; contraindicated in pregnancy and bilateral RAS`],[`Telmisartan / losartan (ARB)`,`Telmisartan 40-80 mg OD; losartan 50-100 mg OD oral`,`Titrate to maximum tolerated; lifelong`,`Hyperkalaemia, AKI, hypotension; teratogenic; never with ACEi`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin (SGLT2i)`,`10 mg OD oral, no titration`,`Start if eGFR 20 or more; continue until dialysis`,`Genital mycosis, volume depletion, euglycaemic DKA; hold 3 days before surgery and when acutely ill`],[`Finerenone (ns-MRA)`,`10 mg OD if eGFR 25-59; 20 mg OD if 60 or more`,`Up to 20 mg at 4 weeks if K 4.8 or less; hold if K over 5.5`,`Hyperkalaemia; avoid with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin); not in type 1`],[`Semaglutide (GLP-1RA)`,`0.25 mg SC weekly x 4 weeks, then 0.5 mg, then 1 mg`,`Step up every 4 weeks; long-term`,`Nausea, vomiting, gallstones, rapid retinopathy worsening; avoid with MTC/MEN2 history`],[`Atorvastatin`,`10-40 mg OD oral`,`Lifelong; no renal adjustment`,`Myalgia, raised transaminases; do not start de novo on dialysis`]]},{heading:`Glucose-lowering drug dosing by eGFR (mL/min/1.73 m2)`,columns:[`Drug`,`eGFR 45 or more`,`eGFR 30-44`,`eGFR under 30 / dialysis`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Full dose (up to 2 g/day)`,`Maximum 1000 mg/day; do not start new`,`Stop`],[`Gliclazide / glipizide`,`Usual dose`,`Start low, monitor glucose`,`Use with caution; glipizide preferred`],[`Glimepiride / glibenclamide`,`Glimepiride 1 mg start; avoid glibenclamide`,`Glimepiride with caution`,`Avoid both`],[`Sitagliptin`,`100 mg OD`,`50 mg OD`,`25 mg OD`],[`Linagliptin / teneligliptin`,`5 mg / 20 mg OD`,`No change`,`No change (linagliptin preferred)`],[`Vildagliptin`,`50 mg BD`,`50 mg OD`,`50 mg OD`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin`,`10 mg OD (glucose and organ benefit)`,`10 mg OD (organ benefit, less glucose effect)`,`Start/continue down to 20; stop on dialysis`],[`GLP-1RA (semaglutide, dulaglutide, liraglutide)`,`No change`,`No change`,`No change to eGFR 15; caution on dialysis`],[`Pioglitazone`,`No change`,`No change`,`No change but fluid retention and fracture risk`],[`Insulin`,`Usual`,`Reduce and titrate carefully`,`Often 25-50% lower dose; hypoglycaemia risk`]]},{heading:`DKD versus non-diabetic kidney disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Favours DKD`,`Favours non-diabetic disease (consider biopsy)`],rows:[[`Diabetes duration`,`Over 5-10 years (type 1)`,`Under 5 years in type 1`],[`Retinopathy`,`Present`,`Absent, especially in type 1`],[`Urine sediment`,`Bland`,`Dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts`],[`Onset of proteinuria`,`Gradual rise over years`,`Sudden nephrotic syndrome`],[`eGFR trajectory`,`Slow decline`,`Rapid fall or over 30% fall after ACEi/ARB`],[`Systemic features`,`None`,`Rash, arthritis, haemoptysis, low complement, positive ANA/ANCA/PLA2R`],[`Kidney size`,`Normal or large`,`Small (hypertensive) or asymmetric (renovascular)`]]},{heading:`Albuminuria measurement and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Normal (A1)`,`Moderately increased (A2)`,`Severely increased (A3)`],rows:[[`UACR (mg/g)`,`Under 30`,`30-300`,`Over 300`],[`UACR (mg/mmol)`,`Under 3`,`3-30`,`Over 30`],[`24-hour albumin excretion (mg/day)`,`Under 30`,`30-300`,`Over 300`],[`Urine PCR (mg/g)`,`Under 150`,`150-500`,`Over 500`],[`Dipstick protein`,`Negative to trace`,`Trace to +`,`+ or more`]]}],redFlags:[`**Serum potassium 6.0 mmol/L or more** or hyperkalaemic ECG changes (peaked T, wide QRS)`,`**eGFR fall over 30% within 2-3 months** of starting ACEi/ARB - think renal artery stenosis or volume depletion`,`**Active urine sediment** (RBC casts) or sudden nephrotic syndrome - suspect glomerulonephritis`,`**Uraemic features**: pericardial rub, encephalopathy, asterixis, bleeding`,`**Pulmonary oedema or anuria**`,`**Recurrent or prolonged hypoglycaemia** on sulfonylurea or insulin with falling eGFR`,`**eGFR under 30** or 2-year KFRE risk over 40% without a nephrology plan`],pearls:[`**Screen with UACR plus eGFR, never dipstick alone; confirm with 2 of 3 samples over 3-6 months.**`,`Four pillars: **RAS blocker + SGLT2i + finerenone + GLP-1RA** - start early, ideally together.`,`**eGFR dip up to 30%** after ACEi/ARB or SGLT2i = haemodynamic and protective; do not stop.`,`**SGLT2i: start if eGFR 20 or more, continue till dialysis** - glucose effect fades below 45, kidney benefit does not.`,`**Finerenone needs K 4.8 or less to start** and eGFR 25 or more; recheck K at 4 weeks.`,`**No retinopathy + proteinuria in type 1 = look for another disease.**`,`Kidneys stay **normal or large** in DKD - size does not rule it out.`,`**Metformin: full above 45, halve at 30-44, stop below 30.**`,`Falling insulin needs and new hypoglycaemia in old diabetic = **check eGFR**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 9 years has eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73 m2 and UACR 420 mg/g on telmisartan 80 mg daily and metformin. Serum potassium is 4.6 mmol/L. Which addition has the strongest evidence to slow kidney failure?`,options:[`Dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily`,`Adding ramipril 10 mg to telmisartan`,`Spironolactone 50 mg once daily`,`Glimepiride 2 mg once daily`],answer:0,explanation:`An SGLT2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin is recommended for type 2 diabetes with CKD and eGFR 20 or more regardless of HbA1c (DAPA-CKD, CREDENCE, EMPA-KIDNEY). Dual ACEi plus ARB increases hyperkalaemia and AKI without benefit (ONTARGET, VA NEPHRON-D). Spironolactone is a steroidal MRA without kidney-outcome evidence and with high hyperkalaemia risk; finerenone is the evidence-based MRA. Glimepiride lowers glucose but has no kidney-protective effect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-q2`,stem:`Two weeks after starting empagliflozin, a patient's eGFR falls from 52 to 44 mL/min/1.73 m2. He is well, euvolaemic and potassium is normal. What is the best action?`,options:[`Stop empagliflozin and refer to nephrology`,`Switch empagliflozin to a DPP-4 inhibitor`,`Order renal Doppler for renal artery stenosis`,`Continue empagliflozin and recheck renal function`],answer:3,explanation:`An early eGFR dip (here about 15%) after an SGLT2 inhibitor reflects restored tubuloglomerular feedback and lower intraglomerular pressure; dips up to 30% are expected, and the drug should be continued. Stopping or switching to a gliptin throws away kidney and heart protection. A renovascular work-up is considered only when the fall exceeds 30% or there are other clues, such as with ACEi/ARB in bilateral stenosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-q3`,stem:`Which finding in a patient with type 1 diabetes and nephrotic-range proteinuria most strongly suggests a non-diabetic cause and prompts kidney biopsy?`,options:[`Diabetes duration of 14 years`,`Absence of diabetic retinopathy on dilated fundoscopy`,`Enlarged kidneys on ultrasound`,`Hypertension of 150/90 mmHg`],answer:1,explanation:`In type 1 diabetes, DKD almost always accompanies retinopathy; proteinuria without retinopathy suggests another glomerular disease and warrants biopsy. A duration over 10 years is typical of DKD. Kidneys in DKD are normal-sized or enlarged, so enlargement fits DKD. Hypertension is a common accompaniment of DKD and does not point elsewhere.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-q4`,stem:`A woman with type 2 diabetes has eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73 m2, UACR 180 mg/g despite losartan 100 mg and dapagliflozin, and potassium 4.5 mmol/L. Finerenone is planned. What is the correct starting dose?`,options:[`20 mg once daily`,`5 mg once daily`,`10 mg once daily`,`25 mg twice daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Finerenone starts at 10 mg once daily when eGFR is 25-59 and 20 mg when eGFR is 60 or more, provided potassium is 4.8 mmol/L or less; it is increased to 20 mg after 4 weeks if potassium allows. Starting at 20 mg is for eGFR 60 or more. Five mg is not a finerenone dose, and 25 mg twice daily is an eplerenone-like regimen, not finerenone.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-q5`,stem:`According to KDIGO, a person with eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73 m2 and UACR 50 mg/g falls into which category, and how often should kidney function be monitored?`,options:[`G3a A2; once a year`,`G4 A1; twice a year`,`G3b A3; four times a year`,`G3b A2; three times a year`],answer:3,explanation:`eGFR 30-44 is G3b and UACR 30-300 mg/g is A2; on the KDIGO heat map G3b A2 is very high risk, monitored about three times a year. G3a is 45-59, so the first choice misplaces the eGFR. G4 begins below 30 and A1 is under 30 mg/g. A3 is over 300 mg/g, so the albuminuria is overstated in the remaining choice.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, screening, staging and current management of diabetic kidney disease in a family medicine setting.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic kidney disease is CKD attributed to diabetes - persistent UACR 30 mg/g or more and/or eGFR under 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for over 3 months - and is the commonest cause of CKD in India (about 31% in the Indian CKD Registry).`,`It is silent until late, so detection depends on yearly UACR and eGFR, and management now rests on four pillars: RAS blockade, SGLT2 inhibitor, finerenone and GLP-1 receptor agonist (KDIGO 2022, ADA 2026).`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`SGLT2 up-regulation - less NaCl at macula densa - afferent dilation - **hyperfiltration**.`,`Angiotensin II efferent constriction raises glomerular pressure.`,`AGEs, PKC, polyol pathway - GBM thickening, mesangial expansion, **Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules**.`,`Podocyte loss - albuminuria - tubulointerstitial fibrosis; MR overactivation adds inflammation.`]},{heading:`Screening`,points:[`UACR + eGFR at diagnosis of T2D and 5 years after T1D, then yearly.`,`Confirm with **2 of 3 UACR** over 3-6 months; exclude fever, UTI, exercise, menstruation.`,`eGFR by race-free CKD-EPI 2021; cystatin C if muscle mass abnormal.`]},{heading:`Staging`,points:[`KDIGO CGA: G1-G5 and A1-A3; heat map decides monitoring (1-4 times a year).`,`Mogensen stages 1-5 for type 1 (historical).`,`RPS pathology classes I-IV.`]},{heading:`Management - lifestyle and risk factors`,points:[`Salt under 5 g/day, protein 0.8 g/kg/day, exercise 150 min/week, stop smoking.`,`BP under 130/80 (ADA) or SBP under 120 if tolerated (KDIGO 2021).`,`HbA1c 6.5% to under 8% individualised; statin for all.`]},{heading:`Management - four pillars`,points:[`**ACEi/ARB** to maximum tolerated dose if albuminuria with hypertension; check K and creatinine at 2-4 weeks.`,`**SGLT2i** (dapagliflozin/empagliflozin 10 mg) if eGFR 20 or more, until dialysis.`,`**Finerenone** 10-20 mg if UACR 30 or more, eGFR 25 or more, K 4.8 or less.`,`**GLP-1RA** (semaglutide 1 mg weekly - FLOW) for glucose and cardiorenal benefit.`]},{heading:`Drug dosing and safety`,points:[`Metformin full above 45, max 1 g at 30-44, stop below 30; avoid glibenclamide; dose-adjust sitagliptin.`,`Sick-day rules: hold metformin, SGLT2i, ACEi/ARB, diuretics, NSAIDs when dehydrated.`]},{heading:`Referral and programme`,points:[`Refer eGFR under 30, UACR 300 or more, rapid decline, unclear cause, K problems, KFRE 5-year risk 3-5%.`,`NP-NCD screening; PM National Dialysis Programme for kidney failure.`]}],mustDraw:[`KDIGO GFR x albuminuria heat map with monitoring frequency`,`Four-pillar treatment ladder (RASi - SGLT2i - finerenone - GLP-1RA)`,`Diagram of afferent/efferent arteriole showing where ACEi and SGLT2i act`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Screening and staging`,marks:3},{part:`Management (lifestyle, four pillars, dosing)`,marks:4},{part:`Referral and programme`,marks:1}],keywords:[`UACR`,`KDIGO`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`finerenone`,`FLOW`,`eGFR dip`,`heat map`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-kidney-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the dosing of glucose-lowering drugs in chronic kidney disease.`,openingLines:[`As eGFR falls, renal clearance of metformin, sulfonylureas, some gliptins and insulin falls, raising the risk of lactic acidosis and hypoglycaemia.`,`KDIGO 2022 recommends metformin while eGFR is 30 or more and an SGLT2 inhibitor from eGFR 20 regardless of glucose.`],answer:[{heading:`Metformin and SGLT2 inhibitors`,points:[`Metformin full dose at eGFR 45 or more, maximum 1 g/day at 30-44, stop under 30 and around contrast/AKI.`,`SGLT2i start if eGFR 20 or more for organ protection; glucose effect small under 45; stop on dialysis.`]},{heading:`Sulfonylureas and gliptins`,points:[`Avoid glibenclamide; glipizide/gliclazide preferred; glimepiride start 1 mg.`,`Sitagliptin 50 mg at eGFR 30-44, 25 mg under 30; linagliptin and teneligliptin need no change.`]},{heading:`Injectables and others`,points:[`GLP-1RA no dose change to eGFR 15; nausea may cause volume depletion.`,`Insulin needs fall (often 25-50% in G4-5); pioglitazone no change but fluid retention.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`HbA1c unreliable in G4-G5; use CGM/GMI; target HbA1c 6.5-8% individualised.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: drug versus eGFR bands (45 or more, 30-44, under 30)`],markSplit:[{part:`Principles`,marks:1},{part:`Drug-by-drug table`,marks:3},{part:`Monitoring and targets`,marks:1}],keywords:[`metformin`,`eGFR`,`sitagliptin`,`linagliptin`,`hypoglycaemia`,`renal dosing`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`KDIGO 2022 Clinical Practice Guideline for Diabetes Management in Chronic Kidney Disease`,`KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease`,`KDIGO 2021 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Blood Pressure in CKD`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026 (Section 11: CKD and risk management)`,`Perkovic V et al. Effects of semaglutide on chronic kidney disease in type 2 diabetes (FLOW). NEJM 2024`,`Rajapurkar MM et al. What do we know about CKD in India: first report of the Indian CKD Registry. BMC Nephrology 2012`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Diabetes and CKD, 2023`]}),n.push({id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy`,title:`Diabetic neuropathy: peripheral, painful and autonomic`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Diabetic neuropathy** is the presence of symptoms and/or signs of peripheral or autonomic nerve dysfunction in a person with diabetes after **exclusion of other causes**; the commonest form is a length-dependent **distal symmetric polyneuropathy** detected by the **10 g monofilament plus 128 Hz tuning fork**, with painful neuropathy treated first-line by **pregabalin/gabapentin, duloxetine or amitriptyline** and autonomic neuropathy causing **orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis, erectile and bladder dysfunction**.`,keywords:[`DPN`,`DSPN`,`distal symmetric polyneuropathy`,`painful diabetic neuropathy`,`cardiovascular autonomic neuropathy`,`CAN`,`gastroparesis`,`10 g monofilament`,`128 Hz tuning fork`,`VPT biothesiometer`,`DN4`,`pregabalin`,`duloxetine`,`amitriptyline`,`tapentadol`,`capsaicin patch`,`OPTION-DM`,`diabetic amyotrophy`,`Charcot foot`,`erectile dysfunction`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Diabetic neuropathies** are a heterogeneous group; the commonest is **distal symmetric polyneuropathy (DSPN)**, about **75%** of all diabetic neuropathy. [ADA 2017 position statement]`,`DSPN affects about **50% of people with long-standing diabetes**; up to **half are asymptomatic**, so it must be screened for, not waited for. [ADA 2026]`,`In India, **CURES** found neuropathy in about **26%** of people with type 2 diabetes in urban Chennai; prevalence is higher in hospital cohorts and in rural patients with late diagnosis. [CURES 2008]`,`**Painful DSPN** occurs in about **15-25%** of all people with diabetes and markedly impairs sleep, mood and work. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Cardiovascular autonomic neuropathy (CAN)** is present in about 20% of unselected diabetics and up to 65% with long duration, and roughly doubles mortality. [ADA 2026]`,`Neuropathy is the main cause of **foot ulceration and non-traumatic amputation** - India has one of the highest numbers of diabetic foot amputations worldwide. [IWGDF 2023]`,`DSPN can be present **at diagnosis of type 2 diabetes** (long silent hyperglycaemia) and even in **prediabetes** (small-fibre neuropathy). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Duration of diabetes and HbA1c** are the strongest risk factors; intensive control in **type 1** cut neuropathy by about **60%** (DCCT), with a much smaller effect in type 2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic syndrome components** - obesity, hypertriglyceridaemia, hypertension, smoking - drive neuropathy in type 2 independent of glucose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Height** (longer nerves), age, alcohol, and **vitamin B12 deficiency from long-term metformin** add risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD, retinopathy and peripheral arterial disease** cluster with neuropathy - each is a marker of microvascular damage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rapid glycaemic improvement** (HbA1c fall over 2% in 3 months) can cause **treatment-induced neuropathy (insulin neuritis)** - acute painful small-fibre and autonomic neuropathy. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Neurotoxic co-factors** common in India: isoniazid without pyridoxine, alcohol, leprosy, and chemotherapy. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Hyperglycaemia floods Schwann cells and neurons (insulin-independent glucose uptake): the **polyol pathway** (aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol) depletes NADPH and myo-inositol. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Advanced glycation end-products**, PKC activation, hexosamine flux and **mitochondrial oxidative stress** damage axons and Schwann cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microangiopathy of the vasa nervorum** - endoneurial capillary basement membrane thickening and occlusion - causes nerve ischaemia; focal infarcts explain **mononeuropathies** such as pupil-sparing third nerve palsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Dyslipidaemia generates oxidised LDL and free fatty acids that injure dorsal root ganglia - why lipids matter in type 2. [ADA 2017 position statement]`,`The **longest axons fail first** (length-dependent dying-back axonopathy), so symptoms begin in the toes and rise to the knees before the fingertips - the **stocking-glove** pattern. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Small fibres (A-delta, C)** carry pain, temperature and autonomic function - their loss causes burning pain and later painless injury; **large fibres (A-beta)** carry vibration, proprioception and reflexes - their loss causes ataxia and falls. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Neuropathic pain** comes from ectopic firing of damaged nociceptors, sodium channel (Nav1.7, Nav1.8) up-regulation and **central sensitisation** with loss of descending inhibition - targets of gabapentinoids (alpha-2-delta), SNRIs/TCAs (descending inhibition) and sodium-channel blockers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autonomic denervation** begins in the vagus (longest autonomic nerve), so **resting tachycardia** (vagal loss) precedes sympathetic loss with orthostatic hypotension. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Diffuse**: DSPN (small-fibre predominant, large-fibre predominant, mixed - commonest) and **autonomic** (cardiovascular, GI, genitourinary, sudomotor, hypoglycaemia unawareness, pupillary). [ADA 2017 position statement]`,`**Mononeuropathy**: isolated cranial (**III - pupil sparing**, VI, VII) or peripheral (median at carpal tunnel, ulnar, peroneal); mononeuritis multiplex - exclude vasculitis. [ADA 2017 position statement]`,`**Radiculopathy or polyradiculopathy**: **diabetic amyotrophy (lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy)** - painful asymmetric proximal thigh weakness with weight loss; and **thoracic radiculopathy** (truncal band pain). [ADA 2017 position statement]`,`**Treatment-induced neuropathy** after rapid HbA1c fall - acute, painful, usually reversible over 12-18 months. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Toronto consensus** grading of DSPN: **possible** (symptoms or signs), **probable** (two or more of symptoms, reduced sensation, reduced ankle reflexes), **confirmed** (plus abnormal nerve conduction), **subclinical** (abnormal NCS only). [Toronto Consensus 2010]`,`**IWGDF foot risk grade** (0-3) links neuropathy to ulcer risk and follow-up frequency (see Diabetic foot topic). [IWGDF 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Positive symptoms**: burning, electric-shock or lancinating pain, pins and needles, **allodynia** (bedsheet pain), worse at **night** - typical of small-fibre disease. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Negative symptoms**: numbness, 'walking on cotton wool', feeling of wearing socks, unnoticed injuries or burns from hot water or walking barefoot on hot floors. [ADA 2026]`,`**Unsteadiness in the dark** and falls suggest large-fibre (proprioceptive) loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autonomic**: dizziness on standing, early satiety, vomiting of food eaten hours ago, **nocturnal diarrhoea**, constipation, erectile dysfunction, incomplete voiding, **gustatory sweating**, dry cracked feet, hypoglycaemia unawareness. [ADA 2026]`,`**Erratic glucose with unexplained post-meal hypoglycaemia** on insulin suggests gastroparesis (food delivery lags insulin). [ADA 2026]`,`Score pain with **DN4** (4 or more of 10 suggests neuropathic pain) and screen symptoms with the **MNSI questionnaire**; ask about sleep, mood and work. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`Ask for **alcohol, B12-relevant diet (strict vegetarian), metformin duration, isoniazid, chemotherapy** and family history of neuropathy. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**10 g Semmes-Weinstein monofilament** at plantar first, third and fifth metatarsal heads and hallux - inability to feel it = **loss of protective sensation (LOPS)** and high ulcer risk. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**128 Hz tuning fork** at the dorsum of the hallux distal phalanx - vibration lost before other modalities in large-fibre disease; **biothesiometer VPT over 25 V** indicates high ulcer risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pinprick and temperature** sensation test small fibres; **ankle reflexes** reduced or absent; **Romberg** positive with proprioceptive loss. [ADA 2026]`,`**Ipswich touch test** (light touch of the tips of toes 1, 3, 5 with the index finger) is a validated alternative where no monofilament is available. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Foot**: dry skin, fissures, callus at pressure points, **claw toes**, prominent metatarsal heads, ulcers, and a **warm swollen foot (Charcot)**. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Lying and standing BP** - fall of **20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes** = orthostatic hypotension; **resting HR over 100** suggests vagal neuropathy. [ADA 2026]`,`**Small-muscle wasting** of hands and feet, foot drop (peroneal), proximal thigh wasting (amyotrophy), and pupil-sparing ptosis with ophthalmoplegia (third nerve). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpate nerves** (ulnar, common peroneal, great auricular) - **thickened nerves** point to leprosy, the key Indian differential. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Vitamin B12 deficiency** - especially on metformin over 4 years or vegetarians; may coexist with DSPN; look for anaemia, glossitis, posterior column signs. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypothyroidism, CKD (uraemic neuropathy), alcohol** and **drugs** (isoniazid, vincristine, metronidazole, nitrofurantoin, amiodarone). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leprosy** - hypopigmented anaesthetic patches, thickened nerves, asymmetric mononeuritis multiplex - still common in parts of India. [NLEP 2023]`,`**CIDP** - proximal and distal weakness, global areflexia, very high CSF protein, demyelinating NCS - treatable with immunoglobulin or steroids; commoner in diabetics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paraproteinaemia, vasculitis, HIV, hereditary (Charcot-Marie-Tooth)** - suspect when asymmetric, motor-predominant, rapidly progressive or with pes cavus and family history. [ADA 2026]`,`**Lumbar canal stenosis, Morton neuroma, plantar fasciitis and peripheral arterial disease** mimic foot pain; **ischaemic rest pain** is relieved by hanging the foot down. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac causes of tachycardia and syncope**, volume depletion and drug-induced orthostasis (alpha blockers, diuretics, TCAs) mimic CAN. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Diagnosis of DSPN is **clinical**; electrodiagnosis is not needed for typical symmetric disease. [ADA 2026]`,`**Baseline tests**: HbA1c, **vitamin B12** (with methylmalonic acid if borderline), TSH, creatinine-eGFR, CBC; add **serum protein electrophoresis** if atypical. [ADA 2026]`,`**Nerve conduction studies** for atypical features - asymmetry, motor predominance, rapid progression, proximal weakness; normal NCS does not exclude small-fibre neuropathy. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Small-fibre tests** (tertiary): skin biopsy for **intra-epidermal nerve fibre density**, corneal confocal microscopy, quantitative sensory testing, sudomotor testing (Sudoscan). [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**CAN tests (Ewing battery)**: **heart-rate variation with deep breathing (E:I ratio)**, **Valsalva ratio**, **30:15 ratio on standing**, BP response to standing and sustained handgrip; ECG for **QTc prolongation**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gastroparesis**: first exclude obstruction with **upper GI endoscopy**; then **4-hour gastric emptying scintigraphy** (over 10% retention at 4 hours is delayed) or breath test; stop GLP-1RA and opioids before testing. [ACG 2022]`,`**Bladder**: ultrasound **post-void residual** (over 100 mL abnormal), urine culture, urodynamics if needed. [ADA 2026]`,`**Charcot foot**: X-ray may be normal early - **MRI** shows bone marrow oedema; skin **temperature difference over 2 degrees C** versus the other foot supports active Charcot. [IWGDF 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Glucose control** to prevent or slow DSPN (strong in type 1, modest in type 2); plus BP, lipids, weight, stopping smoking and alcohol. [ADA 2026]`,`**Foot protection** for all with LOPS: daily self-inspection, never barefoot, well-fitted footwear, moisturise but not between toes, nail care, check bath water with elbow. [IWGDF 2023]`,`Painful DPN first-line (any one): **pregabalin 75 mg BD, increase to 150 mg BD in 1 week (max 300 mg BD)**; **duloxetine 30 mg OD for 1 week then 60 mg OD (max 120 mg)**; **amitriptyline 10 mg at night up to 25-75 mg**; or **gabapentin 300 mg at night titrated to 900-3600 mg/day in three doses**. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**OPTION-DM (2022)**: amitriptyline, duloxetine and pregabalin gave similar relief; **combining duloxetine with pregabalin** helped patients with partial response to monotherapy. [Lancet 2022]`,`Give each drug an **adequate trial of 4-6 weeks at therapeutic dose**; aim for 30-50% pain reduction and better sleep, not zero pain. [NICE CG173]`,`**Topical**: capsaicin 0.075% cream 3-4 times daily or **capsaicin 8% patch** for 30 minutes on the feet every 3 months; lidocaine 5% plaster for localised pain. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Opioids** (including **tapentadol ER**, FDA-approved for painful DPN, and tramadol) are **not recommended first- or second-line** because of dependence, falls and constipation. [ADA 2026]`,`Check **B12 periodically on metformin** and replace if low: methylcobalamin or cyanocobalamin 1000 mcg IM on alternate days for 2 weeks then monthly, or 1000-2000 mcg orally daily. [ADA 2026]`,`Agents with weak evidence widely prescribed in India - **alpha-lipoic acid 600 mg/day, epalrestat 50 mg TDS, methylcobalamin combinations** - are not recommended by ADA as primary therapy for pain. [ADA 2022 compendium]`]},{heading:`Autonomic neuropathy management`,points:[`**Orthostatic hypotension**: stop alpha blockers, diuretics, TCAs where possible; fluids 2-2.5 L/day, salt liberalisation, **waist-high compression**, head-up bed tilt, slow rising, physical counter-manoeuvres. [ADA 2026]`,`Drugs for OH: **midodrine 2.5-10 mg TDS** in the daytime (not within 4 hours of bed - supine hypertension); **fludrocortisone 0.1-0.2 mg OD** (hypokalaemia, oedema); droxidopa where available. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gastroparesis**: small frequent **low-fat, low-fibre** meals, puree diet if severe; stop GLP-1RA, pramlintide, opioids; optimise glucose (hyperglycaemia slows emptying). [ACG 2022]`,`Prokinetics: **metoclopramide 5-10 mg TDS 30 minutes before meals for up to 12 weeks** (tardive dyskinesia); **domperidone 10 mg TDS** (QT prolongation, check ECG); **erythromycin** short courses (tachyphylaxis); gastric electrical stimulation if refractory. [ACG 2022]`,`Insulin timing in gastroparesis: give **rapid-acting insulin after the meal** or split the dose; CGM helps. [ADA 2026]`,`**Erectile dysfunction**: **sildenafil 50 mg (25-100 mg) 1 hour before sex** or **tadalafil 10-20 mg on demand or 5 mg daily**; contraindicated with nitrates; check testosterone if low libido. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diabetic cystopathy**: timed and double voiding, **clean intermittent self-catheterisation** if PVR high; treat UTIs; avoid anticholinergics that worsen retention. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diabetic diarrhoea**: exclude bacterial overgrowth (trial of rifaximin), bile acid malabsorption and coeliac disease; loperamide symptomatically. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for foot sepsis or ulcer with spreading cellulitis, acute Charcot needing offloading, severe gastroparesis with dehydration or ketosis, recurrent syncope, or painful neuropathy with suicidality. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Acute Charcot foot**: immediate **non-removable knee-high offloading (total contact cast)** until temperature difference settles; refer urgently to the diabetic foot service. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Gastroparesis flare**: IV fluids, correct potassium and magnesium, IV insulin infusion, nasogastric decompression, **IV erythromycin 3 mg/kg 8-hourly** or IV metoclopramide; enteral jejunal feeding if prolonged. [ACG 2022]`,`**CAN and anaesthesia**: higher risk of intraoperative hypotension, arrhythmia and **silent ischaemia** - inform the anaesthetist and monitor closely. [ADA 2026]`,`**Silent MI**: think of ischaemia in a diabetic with CAN who has unexplained breathlessness, fatigue, vomiting or new heart failure - do an ECG and troponin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia unawareness** in the ward - relax targets, CGM, avoid beta blocker-masked tachycardia confusion. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: start pregabalin at **25-50 mg at night**; avoid amitriptyline over 25 mg (anticholinergic, falls, Beers criteria); prefer duloxetine or topical agents. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**CKD**: reduce **pregabalin** (eGFR 30-60: 75-300 mg/day; 15-30: 25-150 mg/day) and **gabapentin**; avoid duloxetine if eGFR under 30. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Heart disease**: avoid TCAs after recent MI, with arrhythmia or QT prolongation; duloxetine can raise BP slightly. [NICE CG173]`,`**Pregnancy**: most neuropathic pain drugs lack safety data - use the lowest effective dose after specialist discussion; gabapentinoids carry possible malformation signals. [ADA 2026]`,`**Liver disease and alcohol**: avoid duloxetine (hepatotoxicity). [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Children and adolescents with type 1**: screen for neuropathy from **puberty or age 10, after 5 years of diabetes**. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: monofilament plus tuning fork (or Ipswich touch test) and generic **amitriptyline or pregabalin** from Jan Aushadhi are enough for screening and first-line care. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Foot ulcers** (lifetime risk up to 25-34%), infection, osteomyelitis and **amputation** - neuropathy is present in most diabetic foot ulcers. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Charcot neuro-osteoarthropathy** - midfoot collapse (rocker-bottom foot) if offloading is delayed. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Falls and fractures** from large-fibre loss, orthostatic hypotension and sedating pain drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**CAN** predicts silent ischaemia, arrhythmia, sudden death and perioperative instability; it roughly doubles mortality. [ADA 2026]`,`**Depression, anxiety and insomnia** accompany painful DPN - screen with PHQ-9. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`Established DSPN is **largely irreversible**; treatment slows progression and controls pain, while treatment-induced neuropathy and amyotrophy usually improve over 1-2 years. [ADA 2022 compendium]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Screen** for DSPN **at diagnosis of type 2** and **5 years after type 1**, then **at least yearly**; screen for CAN when microvascular complications or hypoglycaemia unawareness exist. [ADA 2026]`,`**Foot review frequency by IWGDF risk**: grade 0 yearly, 1 every 6-12 months, 2 every 3-6 months, 3 every 1-3 months. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Refer to neurology** for atypical neuropathy (asymmetric, motor, proximal, rapid), suspected CIDP or vasculitis; **pain clinic** for refractory pain (spinal cord stimulation). [ADA 2026]`,`**Refer to gastroenterology** for refractory gastroparesis or weight loss; **urology** for retention or failed PDE5 inhibitor. [ACG 2022]`,`**Same-day foot clinic referral** for ulcer, infection, ischaemia or a hot swollen foot. [IWGDF 2023]`,`Review pain treatment every **4-6 weeks** during titration; taper rather than stop gabapentinoids and SNRIs abruptly. [NICE CG173]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Early glucose, BP and lipid control** and stopping smoking prevent or delay neuropathy; exercise improves small-fibre function. [ADA 2026]`,`**NP-NCD** foot examination with monofilament at Ayushman Arogya Mandir and NCD clinics; footwear counselling is part of the package. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Teach **daily foot care**: inspect with a mirror, never walk barefoot (including temples and homes), test water temperature, avoid home remedies for corns. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Offloading footwear (MCR insoles, extra-depth shoes)** for LOPS with deformity; avoid tight chappals with toe posts. [IWGDF 2023]`,`Counsel about **gabapentinoid misuse** and driving when sedated; pregabalin is a controlled drug in several countries. [NICE CG173]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA 2022 compendium on painful DPN** and **ADA Standards 2026**: gabapentinoids, SNRIs, TCAs and **sodium-channel blockers** are first-line options; opioids including tapentadol are not first- or second-line. [ADA 2026]`,`**OPTION-DM (Lancet 2022)**: no difference between amitriptyline, duloxetine and pregabalin; combination therapy for partial responders. [Lancet 2022]`,`**IWGDF 2023**: updated risk stratification, Charcot guidance (temperature difference, MRI, total contact cast) and offloading recommendations. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Capsaicin 8% patch** is FDA-approved for painful DPN; **high-frequency (10 kHz) spinal cord stimulation** is an option for refractory pain. [ADA 2022 compendium]`,`**Vitamin B12 monitoring** on long-term metformin is now a routine standard, especially with neuropathy. [ADA 2026]`,`**ACG 2022 gastroparesis guideline**: metoclopramide is the only FDA-approved drug, limited to 12 weeks; 4-hour scintigraphy is the standard test. [ACG 2022]`,`New non-opioid **Nav1.8 blockers (suzetrigine)** are licensed for acute pain and under trial for DPN - not yet guideline therapy. [FDA 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic: **'Classify diabetic neuropathy and discuss management of painful neuropathy'** - draw the classification tree and a first-line drug table. [Exam pattern]`,`Viva: **how do you test with a monofilament?** - perpendicular, buckle for 1-2 seconds, 4 sites (hallux, 1st, 3rd, 5th MT heads), avoid callus, include a sham application. [IWGDF 2023]`,`Viva: **why is the third nerve palsy pupil-sparing?** - ischaemia hits the core; pupillomotor fibres run on the periphery and survive (compression by aneurysm hits them first). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Ewing's tests** for CAN - deep breathing, Valsalva, 30:15, standing BP, handgrip. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting B12 and leprosy** in the Indian differential. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: prescribing **metoclopramide indefinitely** or domperidone without an ECG in a patient on QT-prolonging drugs. [ACG 2022]`,`Common mistake: starting **amitriptyline 75 mg in an elderly patient** - start 10 mg at night. [AGS Beers 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pregabalin`,`75 mg BD oral (25-50 mg at night in elderly)`,`Increase to 150 mg BD in 1 week; max 300 mg BD; trial 4-6 weeks`,`Dizziness, sedation, oedema, weight gain, misuse; reduce in CKD`],[`Gabapentin`,`300 mg at night oral`,`Increase by 300 mg every 1-3 days to 900-3600 mg/day in 3 doses`,`Sedation, dizziness, ataxia; reduce in CKD`],[`Duloxetine`,`30 mg OD oral`,`60 mg OD after 1 week; max 120 mg/day`,`Nausea, sweating, raised BP; avoid in liver disease, eGFR under 30, with MAOIs`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night oral`,`Increase every 1-2 weeks to 25-75 mg`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, orthostasis, arrhythmia, falls; avoid recent MI, glaucoma, elderly high dose`],[`Capsaicin`,`0.075% cream 3-4 times daily, or 8% patch 30 min on feet`,`Patch repeated every 3 months`,`Burning, erythema; not on broken skin`],[`Tapentadol ER (reserve)`,`50 mg BD oral`,`Up to 100-250 mg BD; max 500 mg/day`,`Dependence, constipation, seizures, serotonin syndrome; not first or second line`]]},{heading:`Classification of diabetic neuropathies`,columns:[`Group`,`Type`,`Key clinical feature`],rows:[[`Diffuse`,`Distal symmetric polyneuropathy`,`Stocking-glove sensory loss, burning pain, absent ankle jerks`],[`Diffuse`,`Autonomic neuropathy`,`Resting tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis, ED, cystopathy`],[`Mononeuropathy`,`Cranial (III, VI, VII) or limb (median, ulnar, peroneal)`,`Pupil-sparing III palsy; carpal tunnel; foot drop`],[`Radiculo-plexopathy`,`Diabetic amyotrophy; thoracic radiculopathy`,`Painful proximal thigh weakness with weight loss; truncal band pain`],[`Treatment-induced`,`Insulin neuritis`,`Acute pain and autonomic symptoms after HbA1c falls over 2% in 3 months`]]},{heading:`Bedside screening tests and their meaning`,columns:[`Test`,`How`,`Abnormal`,`Fibre tested`],rows:[[`10 g monofilament`,`Hallux and 1st, 3rd, 5th metatarsal heads`,`Not felt at any site = loss of protective sensation`,`Large (pressure)`],[`128 Hz tuning fork`,`Dorsum of hallux distal phalanx`,`Lost or reduced vibration`,`Large (A-beta)`],[`Biothesiometer (VPT)`,`Pulp of great toe`,`Over 25 V = high ulcer risk`,`Large`],[`Pinprick / temperature`,`Dorsum of foot, ascending`,`Reduced, stocking distribution`,`Small (A-delta, C)`],[`Ankle reflex`,`With reinforcement`,`Absent`,`Large`],[`Lying-standing BP`,`Supine 5 min then standing 3 min`,`SBP fall 20 or DBP fall 10 mmHg`,`Autonomic (sympathetic)`],[`Deep-breathing HR variation`,`6 breaths/minute, ECG`,`E:I ratio reduced for age`,`Autonomic (vagal)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of peripheral neuropathy in a diabetic`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Test`],rows:[[`Vitamin B12 deficiency`,`Metformin use, vegetarian, anaemia, posterior column signs`,`Serum B12, methylmalonic acid`],[`Leprosy`,`Thickened nerves, hypopigmented anaesthetic patches, asymmetric`,`Slit-skin smear, nerve palpation`],[`CIDP`,`Proximal plus distal weakness, global areflexia`,`NCS (demyelinating), CSF protein`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Carpal tunnel, slow-relaxing reflexes`,`TSH`],[`Alcohol / drugs`,`History of alcohol, isoniazid, vincristine, metronidazole`,`History, thiamine`],[`Uraemic neuropathy`,`eGFR under 15, restless legs`,`Creatinine, eGFR`],[`Paraproteinaemia / vasculitis`,`Rapid, asymmetric, systemic features`,`SPEP, ANCA, ESR`]]},{heading:`Autonomic neuropathy - organ, symptom and treatment`,columns:[`System`,`Presentation`,`Test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Cardiovascular`,`Resting tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, silent MI`,`Ewing battery, lying-standing BP, QTc`,`Fluids, salt, stockings, midodrine, fludrocortisone`],[`Gastric`,`Early satiety, vomiting, erratic glucose`,`Endoscopy then 4-h scintigraphy`,`Small low-fat meals, metoclopramide up to 12 weeks, domperidone`],[`Intestinal`,`Nocturnal diarrhoea, constipation`,`Exclude SIBO, coeliac, bile acid diarrhoea`,`Loperamide, rifaximin, laxatives`],[`Genitourinary`,`ED, retrograde ejaculation, incomplete voiding`,`Post-void residual, testosterone`,`PDE5 inhibitor, timed voiding, CISC`],[`Sudomotor`,`Dry cracked feet, gustatory sweating`,`Sudoscan`,`Emollients, foot care`],[`Hypoglycaemia unawareness`,`Neuroglycopenia without warning`,`History, CGM`,`Avoid hypoglycaemia 2-3 weeks, CGM, relax targets`]]}],redFlags:[`**Hot, red, swollen foot** with intact skin in a neuropathic patient - acute Charcot until proven otherwise`,`**Foot ulcer with cellulitis, crepitus or ischaemia**`,`**Rapidly progressive, asymmetric or motor-predominant weakness** - think CIDP, vasculitis`,`**Recurrent syncope** from orthostatic hypotension`,`**Persistent vomiting with dehydration or ketosis** in gastroparesis`,`**Unexplained breathlessness or vomiting** with CAN - silent MI`,`**Acute urinary retention**`,`**Suicidal thoughts** with chronic neuropathic pain`],pearls:[`**Monofilament plus one other test** (vibration, pinprick, temperature or ankle reflex) - yearly.`,`**Small fibres hurt, large fibres fall**: pain and burns versus ataxia and absent reflexes.`,`**Pain drugs: Pregabalin, Duloxetine, Amitriptyline (PDA)** - all equal (OPTION-DM); combine if partial.`,`**Start low in the elderly**: amitriptyline 10 mg, pregabalin 25-50 mg.`,`**Resting tachycardia is the earliest sign of CAN** - the vagus is the longest autonomic nerve.`,`**Pupil-sparing third nerve palsy** = diabetic infarct; pupil involved = aneurysm until proved otherwise.`,`**Check B12** on metformin and **feel the nerves** for leprosy in every Indian neuropathy.`,`**Gastroparesis**: give rapid insulin after the meal, and stop GLP-1RA first.`,`**Charcot**: temperature difference over 2 degrees C and normal X-ray - get MRI and cast.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes for 12 years has burning feet worse at night, DN4 score 6/10, and eGFR 75 mL/min/1.73 m2. She has no heart or liver disease. Which is an appropriate first-line drug according to current ADA guidance?`,options:[`Tapentadol extended release 50 mg twice daily`,`Methylcobalamin 1500 mcg daily alone`,`Duloxetine 30 mg once daily, increasing to 60 mg`,`Carbamazepine 400 mg twice daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Duloxetine (an SNRI) is a first-line drug for painful DPN alongside pregabalin/gabapentin and amitriptyline, and OPTION-DM found them similarly effective. Tapentadol, though FDA-approved, is an opioid and is not recommended first or second line because of dependence and adverse effects. Methylcobalamin does not treat neuropathic pain unless B12 is deficient. Carbamazepine is effective in trigeminal neuralgia but has poor evidence and many interactions in DPN.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-q2`,stem:`Which bedside finding is the earliest sign of cardiovascular autonomic neuropathy in diabetes?`,options:[`Resting heart rate over 100 per minute`,`Fall in systolic BP of 30 mmHg on standing`,`Absent ankle reflexes`,`Gustatory sweating`],answer:0,explanation:`The vagus is the longest autonomic nerve, so parasympathetic denervation comes first, giving resting tachycardia and loss of heart-rate variability. Orthostatic hypotension reflects later sympathetic efferent loss. Absent ankle reflexes indicate large-fibre somatic neuropathy, not CAN. Gustatory sweating is sudomotor autonomic dysfunction and not a cardiovascular sign.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old diabetic presents with sudden diplopia and ptosis on the right; the right eye is down and out, and the right pupil is equal and reactive. There is no headache of thunderclap type. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Posterior communicating artery aneurysm`,`Uncal herniation`,`Ischaemic (microvascular) third nerve palsy`,`Myasthenia gravis`],answer:2,explanation:`Diabetic third nerve palsy is an ischaemic infarct of the central nerve core; the superficially placed pupillomotor fibres are spared, so the pupil reacts. A posterior communicating aneurysm compresses from outside and dilates the pupil early, as does uncal herniation, which also causes reduced consciousness. Myasthenia causes fatigable ptosis without a fixed down-and-out eye and spares pupils but would not fit a single nerve pattern so cleanly.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-q4`,stem:`A man with long-standing type 2 diabetes and peripheral neuropathy has a warm, swollen, red right midfoot without ulcer. Foot temperature is 3 degrees C higher than the left. X-ray is normal. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Oral antibiotics and review in 2 weeks`,`Non-removable offloading (total contact cast) and MRI`,`Diagnose gout and start colchicine`,`Reassure as X-ray is normal and advise elevation`],answer:1,explanation:`A hot swollen neuropathic foot with a temperature difference over 2 degrees C and intact skin is acute Charcot neuro-osteoarthropathy until proved otherwise; early X-rays are often normal and MRI shows marrow oedema. Immediate non-removable offloading prevents midfoot collapse. Antibiotics for presumed cellulitis or treating it as gout delays offloading, and reassurance based on a normal X-ray is the classic error leading to rocker-bottom deformity.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-q5`,stem:`In screening for loss of protective sensation with a 10 g monofilament, which is correct technique?`,options:[`Apply over callused areas where ulcers usually form`,`Drag the filament along the sole for 5 seconds`,`Test only the dorsum of the great toe`,`Press perpendicular until the filament buckles, for about 1-2 seconds`],answer:3,explanation:`The monofilament is applied perpendicular to the skin until it bends (delivering 10 g) for 1-2 seconds at the hallux and first, third and fifth metatarsal heads, avoiding callus, with a sham application included. Callus blunts sensation and gives false positives. Dragging is not standardised and tests a different modality. The dorsum of the hallux is where the tuning fork, not the monofilament, is applied.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify diabetic neuropathies. Describe the screening of diabetic peripheral neuropathy and the management of painful diabetic neuropathy.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic neuropathy is symptoms and/or signs of peripheral or autonomic nerve dysfunction in a person with diabetes after exclusion of other causes; distal symmetric polyneuropathy makes up about 75%.`,`About half of those with long-standing diabetes have DSPN and half of them are asymptomatic, so yearly screening with monofilament and tuning fork is mandatory (ADA 2026).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification (ADA 2017)`,points:[`Diffuse: DSPN (small, large, mixed fibre) and autonomic.`,`Mononeuropathy: cranial III/VI/VII, median, ulnar, peroneal; mononeuritis multiplex.`,`Radiculo-plexopathy: diabetic amyotrophy, thoracic radiculopathy.`,`Treatment-induced neuropathy.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis in brief`,points:[`Polyol pathway, AGEs, PKC, oxidative stress, vasa nervorum microangiopathy.`,`Length-dependent axonopathy - stocking-glove pattern.`]},{heading:`Screening`,points:[`At diagnosis of T2D and 5 years after T1D, then yearly.`,`**10 g monofilament** at 4 plantar sites plus one of vibration (128 Hz), pinprick, temperature, ankle reflex.`,`History with MNSI; DN4 for pain; lying-standing BP and resting HR for CAN.`,`Exclude B12 deficiency, hypothyroidism, CKD, alcohol, leprosy; NCS only if atypical.`]},{heading:`Management - general`,points:[`Glucose, BP, lipid control; stop smoking and alcohol.`,`Foot care education, footwear, IWGDF risk-based review.`]},{heading:`Painful neuropathy - drugs`,points:[`Pregabalin 75 mg BD to 150-300 mg BD; gabapentin 900-3600 mg/day.`,`Duloxetine 30 mg then 60 mg OD; amitriptyline 10-75 mg at night.`,`Trial 4-6 weeks; switch or combine (OPTION-DM).`,`Topical capsaicin; opioids such as tapentadol only as last resort; pain clinic for refractory pain.`]},{heading:`Referral`,points:[`Atypical neuropathy to neurology; ulcer or Charcot to foot clinic same day.`]}],mustDraw:[`Classification tree of diabetic neuropathies`,`Foot diagram showing monofilament sites`,`Painful DPN treatment ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:3},{part:`Screening`,marks:3},{part:`Painful DPN management`,marks:3},{part:`Referral and foot care`,marks:1}],keywords:[`DSPN`,`monofilament`,`pregabalin`,`duloxetine`,`amitriptyline`,`OPTION-DM`,`Charcot`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-neuropathy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on diabetic autonomic neuropathy.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic autonomic neuropathy is damage to sympathetic and parasympathetic fibres causing cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, genitourinary and sudomotor dysfunction.`,`Cardiovascular autonomic neuropathy is the most important form - it roughly doubles mortality and causes silent ischaemia.`],answer:[{heading:`Features`,points:[`CAN: resting tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, exercise intolerance, silent MI, QTc prolongation.`,`GI: gastroparesis, nocturnal diarrhoea, constipation; GU: ED, cystopathy; sudomotor: dry feet.`,`Hypoglycaemia unawareness.`]},{heading:`Tests`,points:[`Ewing battery: deep breathing, Valsalva, 30:15, standing BP, handgrip.`,`Gastric emptying scintigraphy after endoscopy; post-void residual.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`OH: stop culprit drugs, fluids, salt, stockings; midodrine 2.5-10 mg TDS, fludrocortisone 0.1-0.2 mg.`,`Gastroparesis: small low-fat meals, metoclopramide up to 12 weeks, domperidone; insulin after meals.`,`ED: sildenafil or tadalafil (not with nitrates); cystopathy: timed voiding, CISC.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: system - symptom - test - treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and CAN`,marks:2},{part:`Other systems and tests`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`CAN`,`Ewing tests`,`orthostatic hypotension`,`gastroparesis`,`midodrine`,`metoclopramide`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026 (Section 12: Retinopathy, Neuropathy and Foot Care)`,`Pop-Busui R et al. Diabetic Neuropathy: A Position Statement by the American Diabetes Association. Diabetes Care 2017`,`Pop-Busui R et al. Diagnosis and Treatment of Painful Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy. ADA Clinical Compendia 2022`,`Tesfaye S et al. OPTION-DM trial. Lancet 2022`,`IWGDF Guidelines on the Prevention and Management of Diabetes-related Foot Disease, 2023`,`Camilleri M et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Gastroparesis, 2022`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Diabetes, 2023`]}),n.push({id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye`,title:`Diabetic retinopathy and the diabetic eye`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Diabetic retinopathy (DR)** is a progressive **microangiopathy of the retina** - pericyte loss, microaneurysms and capillary closure leading to **ischaemia-driven VEGF release** - graded on the **International Clinical DR scale** from mild non-proliferative to **proliferative DR**, with **diabetic macular oedema** the commonest cause of visual loss; it is prevented by **yearly dilated fundus or retinal-photo screening (AI-assisted in India)** and treated with **intravitreal anti-VEGF, laser photocoagulation and control of glucose, BP and lipids**.`,keywords:[`DR`,`NPDR`,`PDR`,`DME`,`CSME`,`ETDRS`,`4-2-1 rule`,`anti-VEGF`,`ranibizumab`,`aflibercept`,`bevacizumab`,`faricimab`,`panretinal photocoagulation`,`OCT`,`fundus camera`,`AI screening`,`early worsening`,`semaglutide retinopathy`,`rubeosis iridis`,`snowflake cataract`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Diabetic retinopathy** is a chronic, progressive microvascular complication of diabetes affecting the retinal capillaries; **diabetic macular oedema (DME)** can occur at any stage. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`Globally about **one-third of people with diabetes have DR** and about **one in ten has vision-threatening DR**; DR is a leading cause of blindness in working-age adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, the **SMART India study** found DR in about **12.5%** and vision-threatening DR in about **4%** of people with diabetes; the **National Diabetes and DR Survey** (aged 50 or more) found DR in about **17%**. [SMART India 2021]`,`With over 100 million Indians with diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB 2023), even these rates mean **millions with sight-threatening disease**, most unscreened. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Almost all people with **type 1 diabetes** and over 60% with **type 2** have some DR after **20 years**. [Harrison 22e]`,`DR is **asymptomatic** until macular oedema or haemorrhage occurs - by then treatment saves remaining vision but rarely restores lost vision. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Duration of diabetes** is the strongest predictor; **HbA1c** is the strongest modifiable one - DCCT showed intensive control cut new DR by about **76%** in type 1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertension**: tight BP control in UKPDS reduced DR progression by about **34%**. [UKPDS 38]`,`**Dyslipidaemia** promotes hard exudates and DME; **fenofibrate** slowed progression in FIELD, ACCORD-Eye and LENS. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diabetic kidney disease** (albuminuria) travels with DR - screen the eye when you find albuminuria and vice versa. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy**, **puberty** and **rapid improvement of glucose** (insulin intensification, GLP-1RA, bariatric surgery) can accelerate DR. [ADA 2026]`,`**Anaemia, smoking, cataract surgery** and South Asian ethnicity are additional risk factors. [AAO PPP 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Retinal endothelium and pericytes take up glucose without insulin; the **polyol pathway, AGEs, PKC-beta** and oxidative stress cause **pericyte loss and basement membrane thickening**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Loss of pericyte support creates outpouchings - **microaneurysms**, the first visible sign; weak capillaries leak (**dot-blot haemorrhages, hard exudates** of lipoprotein). [Kanski 9e]`,`**Leukostasis and capillary closure** cause retinal ischaemia - **cotton wool spots** (nerve fibre layer infarcts), **venous beading** and **IRMA**. [Kanski 9e]`,`Ischaemic retina releases **VEGF**, which drives **neovascularisation** of the disc (NVD), elsewhere (NVE) and iris (**rubeosis**) - these fragile vessels bleed into the vitreous and pull the retina off (**tractional detachment**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**VEGF and inflammatory cytokines** also break the blood-retinal barrier, so fluid collects in the macula - **DME** - the reason **anti-VEGF injections** work for both DME and PDR. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`Neurodegeneration of the inner retina begins before visible vascular lesions - explaining early colour and contrast loss. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Early worsening** with rapid glucose reduction is thought to result from a sudden fall in retinal perfusion and IGF-1/VEGF up-regulation in already ischaemic retina. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**International Clinical DR scale**: no apparent DR; **mild NPDR** (microaneurysms only); **moderate NPDR** (more than microaneurysms, less than severe); **severe NPDR**; **PDR**. [AAO 2003 / ICO 2017]`,`**Severe NPDR (4-2-1 rule)**: over 20 intraretinal haemorrhages in **each of 4 quadrants**, definite **venous beading in 2 or more quadrants**, or prominent **IRMA in 1 or more quadrant** - with no neovascularisation. [ETDRS]`,`Severe NPDR carries about a **50% risk of PDR within 1 year** - the reason it needs specialist care. [ETDRS]`,`**PDR**: neovascularisation (NVD/NVE) and/or vitreous or preretinal haemorrhage; **high-risk PDR** (DRS): NVD of a third of disc area or more, any NVD with vitreous haemorrhage, or NVE half disc area or more with haemorrhage. [DRS]`,`**DME**: classified as **centre-involving (CI-DME)** or **non-centre-involving** on OCT; ETDRS **clinically significant macular oedema (CSME)** is thickening or hard exudates with thickening within **500 microns of the fovea**, or thickening of 1 disc area or more within 1 disc diameter. [ETDRS]`,`**Referable DR** for screening programmes = moderate NPDR or worse, and/or any DME. [ICO 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**No symptoms** in most - DR is found on screening; this is the central message. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gradual blurring of central vision** and difficulty reading suggest **DME**. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Sudden floaters, a shower of black dots or sudden painless loss** of vision suggest **vitreous haemorrhage** from PDR. [Kanski 9e]`,`**A curtain or shadow** across vision suggests **tractional retinal detachment**. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Painful red eye with haloes** and very poor vision in a long-standing diabetic suggests **neovascular glaucoma**. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Fluctuating refraction** - changing glasses power - with glucose swings, and slow glare with cataract. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`Ask about **pregnancy plans, recent intensification of treatment (insulin, semaglutide), BP, kidney disease and previous laser or injections**. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Visual acuity** with Snellen chart and **pinhole** (pinhole improvement suggests refractive error, not maculopathy). [ICO 2017]`,`**Dilated fundoscopy**: tropicamide 1% with phenylephrine 2.5% eye drops; check for a shallow anterior chamber first. [ICO 2017]`,`**NPDR**: microaneurysms, dot-blot and flame haemorrhages, **hard exudates** (yellow, circinate rings around leaking microaneurysms), **cotton wool spots**, venous beading and IRMA. [Kanski 9e]`,`**PDR**: fronds of new vessels on the disc or along arcades, preretinal (boat-shaped) haemorrhage, fibrovascular proliferation. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Maculopathy**: hard exudates near the fovea, a dull foveal reflex and reduced vision; OCT is needed to confirm thickening. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Anterior segment**: **rubeosis iridis** (check before dilating), raised IOP, cataract (cortical, posterior subcapsular or **snowflake** in young type 1). [Kanski 9e]`,`**RAPD** suggests extensive ischaemia, haemorrhage-related detachment or optic neuropathy. [Kanski 9e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hypertensive retinopathy**: arteriolar narrowing, AV nicking, flame haemorrhages, cotton wool spots, disc swelling - often coexists with DR. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Retinal vein occlusion**: unilateral, sectoral or whole-fundus haemorrhages along one vein ('blood and thunder'). [Kanski 9e]`,`**Eales disease**: young Indian men, peripheral periphlebitis, recurrent vitreous haemorrhage, associated with tuberculin hypersensitivity. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Ocular ischaemic syndrome** (carotid stenosis), **radiation retinopathy**, **sickle cell retinopathy** and **HIV microvasculopathy** mimic DR. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Anaemic and leukaemic retinopathy**: haemorrhages with white centres (Roth spots) and pale fundus. [Kanski 9e]`,`Non-DR causes of visual loss in diabetics: **cataract, glaucoma, age-related macular degeneration, NAION** - check before attributing all loss to DR. [AAO PPP 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Screening**: dilated fundus examination by an eye specialist, or **two-field 45-degree retinal photographs** (non-mydriatic or mydriatic) graded by a trained grader or **validated AI**. [ADA 2026]`,`**AI-based screening**: autonomous FDA-cleared systems (LumineticsCore/IDx-DR, EyeArt) and Indian **smartphone fundus cameras with offline AI** (e.g. Remidio Medios) show sensitivity over 90% for referable DR. [ADA 2026]`,`**OCT**: confirms and measures DME (central subfield thickness), guides anti-VEGF re-treatment. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Fundus fluorescein angiography**: shows capillary non-perfusion, leaking microaneurysms and new vessels; **OCT angiography** is a non-invasive alternative. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**B-scan ultrasound** when vitreous haemorrhage or dense cataract hides the retina - to exclude retinal detachment. [Kanski 9e]`,`**IOP measurement and gonioscopy** if rubeosis is present. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Systemic**: HbA1c, BP, lipids, UACR and eGFR - DR is a marker of systemic microvascular disease. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Glucose, BP and lipids** slow DR: HbA1c individualised around **under 7%**, BP under **130/80**, statin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fenofibrate 145-160 mg OD** can be added in type 2 with NPDR to slow progression (FIELD, ACCORD-Eye, LENS) - check eGFR. [ADA 2026]`,`**Aspirin is not contraindicated** in DR - it does not increase vitreous haemorrhage risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**Before starting semaglutide or intensive insulin** in someone with existing DR (especially moderate NPDR or worse), get a retinal exam and plan **closer eye follow-up**; lower HbA1c gradually where possible. [ADA 2026]`,`Note that **pioglitazone** can worsen macular oedema through fluid retention. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**No DR and good control**: rescreen every **1-2 years**; **any DR**: at least **yearly**, more often when progressing. [ADA 2026]`,`Counsel: **retinal photos or dilated exams are painless and save sight**; driving after dilation is unsafe for 4-6 hours. [ICO 2017]`]},{heading:`Ophthalmic treatment - specialist and IPD`,points:[`**Centre-involving DME with visual loss**: **intravitreal anti-VEGF** - **ranibizumab 0.5 mg**, **aflibercept 2 mg** (or 8 mg), **faricimab 6 mg**, or low-cost **bevacizumab 1.25 mg** (off-label, widely used in India) - monthly loading (usually 3-5) then treat-and-extend. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**DRCR Protocol T**: aflibercept gave better vision than bevacizumab when baseline vision was 6/15 or worse; outcomes were similar with better vision. [DRCR Protocol T]`,`**CI-DME with good vision (6/7.5 or better)**: close observation is acceptable, with anti-VEGF if vision drops (DRCR Protocol V). [DRCR Protocol V]`,`**Steroid implants**: **dexamethasone 0.7 mg intravitreal implant** (lasts 3-6 months) or fluocinolone implant for anti-VEGF non-responders, pseudophakic eyes, pregnancy or recent stroke/MI - risk of **cataract and raised IOP**. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Focal/grid laser** for non-centre-involving DME; micropulse laser is an option. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**PDR**: **panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)** halves severe visual loss (DRS); **anti-VEGF is a valid alternative** (Protocol S) but needs reliable follow-up - in India, where loss to follow-up is common, **PRP (or PRP plus anti-VEGF)** is often safer. [DRCR Protocol S]`,`**Vitrectomy** for non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage, tractional detachment threatening the macula, and combined tractional-rhegmatogenous detachment. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Neovascular glaucoma**: anti-VEGF, PRP, IOP-lowering drops, then glaucoma drainage device or cyclophotocoagulation - an emergency referral. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Severe NPDR**: anti-VEGF lowered progression to PDR (Protocol W) but gave **no visual benefit at 4 years**, so close observation or PRP remain standard. [DRCR Protocol W 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy** (pre-existing type 1 or 2): eye exam **before conception or in the first trimester**, then **each trimester and for 1 year postpartum**; laser is safe; **avoid anti-VEGF**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gestational diabetes** does not need retinal screening during pregnancy (no increased DR risk). [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 1 children and adolescents**: first exam **3-5 years after diagnosis once aged 11 or more (or puberty)**, then every 2 years if normal. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Cataract surgery** can worsen DME and DR: treat DME before surgery or at the time; watch for postoperative macular oedema. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Elderly**: cataract and AMD coexist; use OCT to decide the cause of poor vision. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Patients on anti-VEGF**: report a recent MI or stroke to the ophthalmologist; systemic risk is low but steroid implants may be preferred soon after events. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: non-mydriatic or smartphone fundus cameras at **PHC/Ayushman Arogya Mandir** with AI or tele-grading and referral to district eye units. [NPCBVI 2023]`]},{heading:`The rest of the diabetic eye`,points:[`**Cataract** occurs 2-5 times more often and earlier; **snowflake (metabolic) cataract** in young poorly controlled type 1 is reversible early. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Refractive shifts**: hyperglycaemia causes myopic shift (lens swelling); delay new glasses until glucose is stable for 4-6 weeks. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Glaucoma**: neovascular glaucoma with PDR; slightly higher risk of primary open-angle glaucoma. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Cranial nerve palsies**: pupil-sparing third, sixth and fourth nerve palsies - recover over 3 months (see Diabetic neuropathy topic). [Kanski 9e]`,`**Diabetic papillopathy**: transient disc swelling in young type 1 with mild visual loss; resolves in months. [Kanski 9e]`,`**Ocular surface**: dry eye, corneal epithelial defects and neurotrophic keratopathy from corneal nerve loss; **recurrent styes**; **rhino-orbital mucormycosis** (see Infections in diabetes topic). [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**NAION** (non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy): EMA (2025) listed it as a **very rare** side effect of semaglutide - ask about sudden painless visual loss. [EMA PRAC 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Vitreous haemorrhage**, **tractional retinal detachment**, **neovascular glaucoma** and **macular ischaemia** cause irreversible blindness. [Kanski 9e]`,`PRP reduces **severe visual loss by about 50%** in high-risk PDR (DRS); laser for CSME reduces moderate visual loss by about 50% (ETDRS). [DRS / ETDRS]`,`Anti-VEGF for DME improves vision by about **2 lines** on average, needing several injections in year 1 and fewer later. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**PRP side effects**: peripheral field loss, night vision loss, worsened DME - may affect **driving eligibility**. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**Intravitreal injection risks**: endophthalmitis (about 1 in 2000-3000), raised IOP, retinal detachment. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`DR presence predicts **cardiovascular events and nephropathy** - treat the whole patient. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and programme links`,points:[`**Screening start**: type 2 **at diagnosis**; type 1 **within 5 years of diagnosis**; pregestational diabetes before conception or first trimester. [ADA 2026]`,`**ICO follow-up**: no DR every 1-2 years; mild NPDR every 1-2 years; moderate NPDR every **6-12 months**; severe NPDR within **3 months**; PDR within **1 month**. [ICO 2017]`,`**Same-day referral**: sudden visual loss, vitreous haemorrhage, retinal detachment, **rubeosis or painful red eye with raised IOP**. [ICO 2017]`,`**Prompt referral (within weeks)**: severe NPDR, PDR, any centre-involving DME, unexplained drop in acuity. [ICO 2017]`,`**Before semaglutide/insulin intensification** with moderate NPDR or worse - coordinate with the ophthalmologist. [ADA 2026]`,`**National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCBVI)** includes DR screening and treatment through district eye units and medical colleges. [NPCBVI 2023]`,`**NP-NCD** links diabetes registration at Ayushman Arogya Mandir to eye screening; opportunistic fundus photography during NCD visits raises coverage. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`Tele-ophthalmology and AI programmes (e.g. Aravind, Sankara Nethralaya, state programmes) have shown that **AI-graded smartphone screening** works at primary care level. [SMART India 2021]`,`Counsel: **DR can be silent**; keep yearly eye appointments; control sugar, BP and lipids; stop smoking; report floaters or sudden blur the same day. [ADA 2026]`,`**Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY** covers vitrectomy, laser and cataract surgery for eligible families. [PM-JAY 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA Standards 2025-2026**: **AI-based and telemedicine retinal screening** endorsed as alternatives to traditional exams; retinal exam advised before and during **GLP-1RA (especially semaglutide)** in people with existing DR. [ADA 2026]`,`**AAO Diabetic Retinopathy Preferred Practice Pattern 2024**: anti-VEGF first-line for CI-DME with visual loss; PRP or anti-VEGF for PDR; observation for good-vision CI-DME. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**New longer-acting agents**: **faricimab** (anti-VEGF plus angiopoietin-2) and **aflibercept 8 mg** allow dosing every 12-16 weeks in many patients. [AAO PPP 2024]`,`**DRCR Protocol W 4-year results (2023)**: anti-VEGF in severe NPDR prevents PDR complications but does not improve vision. [DRCR Protocol W 2023]`,`**LENS trial (2024)**: fenofibrate reduced progression to referable DR or treatment by about 27% in Scotland. [NEJM Evidence 2024]`,`**EMA 2025**: NAION added as a very rare adverse effect of semaglutide. [EMA PRAC 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic: **'Classify diabetic retinopathy and outline screening and management'** - write the International scale with the 4-2-1 rule and the ICO follow-up table. [Exam pattern]`,`Viva: **first sign of DR?** - microaneurysms; **commonest cause of visual loss?** - DME; **cause of sudden loss?** - vitreous haemorrhage. [Kanski 9e]`,`Viva: **why does vision sometimes worsen when glucose improves fast?** - early worsening; seen with intensive insulin (DCCT) and semaglutide (SUSTAIN-6). [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: **stopping aspirin because of DR** - not needed. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: **screening GDM women for DR** - not required; pregestational diabetes needs trimester-wise exams. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: prescribing new glasses at diagnosis of diabetes while glucose is high. [AAO PPP 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`International Clinical Diabetic Retinopathy Severity Scale`,columns:[`Stage`,`Dilated fundus findings`,`Risk / follow-up (ICO)`],rows:[[`No apparent DR`,`No abnormalities`,`Rescreen 1-2 years`],[`Mild NPDR`,`Microaneurysms only`,`Rescreen 1-2 years`],[`Moderate NPDR`,`More than microaneurysms but less than severe NPDR`,`6-12 months; referable`],[`Severe NPDR`,`4-2-1: 20+ haemorrhages in 4 quadrants, venous beading in 2, IRMA in 1; no NV`,`About 50% to PDR in 1 year; specialist within 3 months`],[`PDR`,`Neovascularisation (NVD/NVE) and/or vitreous or preretinal haemorrhage`,`Treat urgently; review within 1 month`],[`DME (any stage)`,`Retinal thickening/exudates; centre-involving or not on OCT`,`CI-DME: anti-VEGF; refer promptly`]]},{heading:`Treatments for diabetic retinopathy and macular oedema`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ranibizumab`,`0.5 mg intravitreal (biosimilars available in India)`,`Monthly until stable, then treat-and-extend`,`Endophthalmitis, raised IOP; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Aflibercept`,`2 mg intravitreal (8 mg high-dose)`,`2 mg: 5 monthly then every 8 weeks; 8 mg: 3 monthly then every 12-16 weeks`,`Endophthalmitis, IOP rise; caution after recent stroke/MI`],[`Bevacizumab (off-label)`,`1.25 mg intravitreal`,`Monthly then as needed`,`Low cost; compounding contamination risk; less effective if vision 6/15 or worse`],[`Faricimab`,`6 mg intravitreal`,`4 monthly loading, then up to every 16 weeks`,`Intraocular inflammation, endophthalmitis`],[`Dexamethasone implant`,`0.7 mg intravitreal`,`Repeat every 3-6 months`,`Cataract, raised IOP (steroid responders)`],[`Panretinal photocoagulation`,`Laser in 1-3 sittings`,`Single course; top-up if active`,`Peripheral field and night vision loss, DME worsening`],[`Fenofibrate (systemic)`,`145-160 mg OD oral`,`Long-term in type 2 with NPDR`,`Raised creatinine, gallstones, myopathy with statin; avoid eGFR under 30`]]},{heading:`Screening schedule for diabetic retinopathy`,columns:[`Group`,`First exam`,`Repeat`],rows:[[`Type 2 diabetes`,`At diagnosis`,`Every 1-2 years if no DR; yearly or more if DR`],[`Type 1 diabetes`,`Within 5 years of diagnosis (age 11 or puberty onwards)`,`Every 1-2 years if no DR`],[`Pregestational diabetes, planning pregnancy`,`Before conception or first trimester`,`Every trimester and for 1 year postpartum`],[`Gestational diabetes`,`Not required`,`Not required`],[`Before semaglutide / insulin intensification with DR`,`Baseline exam`,`Closer follow-up per ophthalmologist`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of diabetic retinopathy`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Laterality`],rows:[[`Hypertensive retinopathy`,`AV nicking, arteriolar narrowing, disc swelling`,`Bilateral`],[`Retinal vein occlusion`,`Haemorrhages along one vein territory`,`Unilateral`],[`Eales disease`,`Young male, peripheral periphlebitis, vitreous haemorrhage`,`Often bilateral`],[`Ocular ischaemic syndrome`,`Mid-peripheral haemorrhages, carotid stenosis, ocular pain`,`Unilateral`],[`Radiation retinopathy`,`History of head/neck radiotherapy`,`Field-dependent`],[`Anaemic / leukaemic retinopathy`,`Roth spots, pale fundus, low Hb`,`Bilateral`]]}],redFlags:[`**Sudden painless loss of vision or a shower of floaters** - vitreous haemorrhage or detachment`,`**Curtain across the visual field** - retinal detachment`,`**Painful red eye with haloes and raised IOP** - neovascular glaucoma`,`**Rubeosis iridis** on torch examination`,`**New neovascularisation (PDR)** or severe NPDR on screening photographs`,`**Drop in central vision** with exudates near the fovea - centre-involving DME`,`**Pregnant woman with pre-existing diabetes and moderate NPDR or worse**`],pearls:[`**First sign: microaneurysm; commonest cause of visual loss: DME; sudden loss: vitreous haemorrhage.**`,`**4-2-1 rule** for severe NPDR: haemorrhages 4 quadrants, beading 2, IRMA 1.`,`**Severe NPDR = 50% PDR in a year.**`,`**Aspirin is safe in DR.**`,`**GDM needs no retinal screening; pregestational diabetes needs one every trimester.**`,`**Anti-VEGF for CI-DME; PRP (or anti-VEGF) for PDR** - choose PRP if follow-up is doubtful.`,`**Lower HbA1c gradually** in DR patients starting semaglutide or insulin - early worsening.`,`**Snowflake cataract** = young type 1 with poor control.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-q1`,stem:`Fundus photography of a 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes shows more than 20 intraretinal haemorrhages in each of four quadrants, without venous beading, IRMA or neovascularisation. How is this graded?`,options:[`Moderate non-proliferative DR`,`Severe non-proliferative DR`,`Proliferative DR`,`Mild non-proliferative DR`],answer:1,explanation:`Severe NPDR requires any one feature of the 4-2-1 rule - over 20 haemorrhages in each of 4 quadrants, venous beading in 2 quadrants or IRMA in 1 quadrant - so haemorrhages alone in 4 quadrants suffice. Moderate NPDR has more than microaneurysms but falls short of these criteria. Proliferative DR needs neovascularisation or vitreous/preretinal haemorrhage. Mild NPDR is microaneurysms only.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-q2`,stem:`A woman with gestational diabetes diagnosed at 26 weeks asks whether she needs a retinal examination. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Retinal examination every trimester and 1 year postpartum`,`Dilated exam monthly until delivery`,`Retinal screening is not needed for gestational diabetes`,`Fluorescein angiography at 28 weeks`],answer:2,explanation:`Gestational diabetes does not carry a risk of DR during pregnancy, so ADA does not recommend retinal screening for it. Trimester-wise examination and 1 year postpartum is for women with pre-existing type 1 or type 2 diabetes. Monthly dilated exams are not indicated, and fluorescein angiography is avoided in pregnancy unless essential.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old with HbA1c 11% and known moderate NPDR is about to start semaglutide. What is the most appropriate action regarding the eyes?`,options:[`Avoid all GLP-1 receptor agonists permanently`,`Start semaglutide and do the next eye check in 2 years`,`Start high-dose semaglutide to lower HbA1c as fast as possible`,`Arrange a retinal examination and closer ophthalmic follow-up while lowering HbA1c gradually`],answer:3,explanation:`Rapid HbA1c reduction can transiently worsen existing DR (seen in DCCT and in SUSTAIN-6 with semaglutide), so ADA advises a retinal exam before starting, closer monitoring and gradual glucose lowering, not avoidance. GLP-1RA benefits outweigh this risk, so permanent avoidance is wrong. A 2-year interval is too long for moderate NPDR, and rapid escalation increases early worsening.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-q4`,stem:`A patient with centre-involving diabetic macular oedema has visual acuity 6/24. Which is first-line treatment?`,options:[`Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections`,`Panretinal photocoagulation`,`Oral acetazolamide`,`Immediate vitrectomy`],answer:0,explanation:`Anti-VEGF agents (ranibizumab, aflibercept, faricimab or bevacizumab) are first-line for centre-involving DME with visual loss and improve acuity on average by about two lines; Protocol T showed aflibercept is best when vision is 6/15 or worse. PRP treats PDR and can worsen DME. Acetazolamide has no role. Vitrectomy is for non-clearing haemorrhage or traction, not routine DME.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-q5`,stem:`In a rural Indian patient with high-risk PDR who lives far from the eye hospital and is unlikely to return monthly, which approach is generally preferred?`,options:[`Anti-VEGF injection alone every month`,`Observation with 6-monthly review`,`Panretinal photocoagulation, with or without anti-VEGF`,`Oral fenofibrate alone`],answer:2,explanation:`Protocol S showed anti-VEGF is non-inferior to PRP for PDR, but only with reliable frequent follow-up; when patients are lost to follow-up, anti-VEGF-alone eyes can develop severe complications, so PRP (durable) is preferred, possibly combined with anti-VEGF. Monthly-injection-only plans fail without follow-up. Observation of high-risk PDR risks severe visual loss. Fenofibrate slows NPDR progression but does not treat PDR.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathogenesis and classification of diabetic retinopathy. Outline a screening programme for diabetic retinopathy suitable for primary care in India and the principles of management.`,openingLines:[`Diabetic retinopathy is a progressive retinal microangiopathy of diabetes in which capillary damage and ischaemia lead to VEGF-driven leakage (macular oedema) and neovascularisation (proliferative DR).`,`About 12.5% of Indians with diabetes have DR and 4% have vision-threatening DR (SMART India), mostly asymptomatic - so screening is the only way to prevent blindness.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Polyol, AGE, PKC pathways - pericyte loss, BM thickening - microaneurysms, leakage.`,`Capillary closure - ischaemia - VEGF - neovascularisation and DME.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`International scale: no DR, mild, moderate, severe NPDR (4-2-1), PDR.`,`DME: centre-involving versus non-centre-involving; ETDRS CSME.`,`High-risk PDR (DRS criteria).`]},{heading:`Screening programme`,points:[`Who: all diabetics registered under NP-NCD; T2D at diagnosis, T1D within 5 years.`,`How: non-mydriatic or smartphone fundus camera at PHC/Ayushman Arogya Mandir; AI or tele-grading.`,`Referable DR (moderate NPDR or worse, any DME) sent to district eye unit (NPCBVI).`,`Interval: 1-2 years no DR; yearly with DR; trimester-wise in pregnancy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Systemic: glucose, BP under 130/80, statin, fenofibrate; gradual HbA1c lowering.`,`CI-DME: anti-VEGF; non-CI-DME: focal laser or observation.`,`PDR: PRP or anti-VEGF; vitrectomy for non-clearing haemorrhage or traction.`]},{heading:`Referral red flags`,points:[`Sudden visual loss, floaters, curtain, painful red eye - same day.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of DR screening from PHC to district eye unit`,`Table of International DR scale with follow-up intervals`,`Pathogenesis flow: hyperglycaemia - pericyte loss - ischaemia - VEGF`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Screening programme`,marks:3},{part:`Management and referral`,marks:3}],keywords:[`NPDR`,`PDR`,`4-2-1 rule`,`DME`,`anti-VEGF`,`PRP`,`AI screening`,`NPCBVI`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetic-retinopathy-eye-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate non-proliferative from proliferative diabetic retinopathy.`,openingLines:[`NPDR is intraretinal microvascular disease without new vessels; PDR is defined by neovascularisation or vitreous/preretinal haemorrhage driven by retinal ischaemia and VEGF.`,`The distinction decides urgency: PDR needs treatment within weeks to prevent blindness.`],answer:[{heading:`Features`,points:[`NPDR: microaneurysms, dot-blot haemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton wool spots, venous beading, IRMA.`,`PDR: NVD, NVE, preretinal or vitreous haemorrhage, fibrovascular proliferation, tractional detachment.`]},{heading:`Symptoms and risk`,points:[`NPDR: usually asymptomatic unless DME; severe NPDR 50% to PDR in 1 year.`,`PDR: sudden floaters or loss of vision; neovascular glaucoma.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`NPDR: risk-factor control, fenofibrate, follow-up 6-12 months (moderate) or within 3 months (severe).`,`PDR: PRP or anti-VEGF; vitrectomy when needed.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: NPDR versus PDR`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Fundus features and symptoms`,marks:2},{part:`Risk and management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`NPDR`,`PDR`,`IRMA`,`neovascularisation`,`PRP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026 (Section 12: Retinopathy, Neuropathy and Foot Care)`,`American Academy of Ophthalmology. Diabetic Retinopathy Preferred Practice Pattern, 2024`,`International Council of Ophthalmology Guidelines for Diabetic Eye Care, updated 2017`,`Raman R et al. Prevalence of diabetic retinopathy in India: SMART India study. BMJ Open Ophthalmology 2021`,`Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology, 9th edition, 2020`,`National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCBVI), MoHFW, 2023`,`ICMR Guidelines for Diabetic Retinopathy, 2019`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests`,title:`Thyroid function tests: interpretation, patterns and pitfalls`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Thyroid function tests** read the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis: **TSH is the single best first-line test** because of its **log-linear inverse relation to free T4**, while **free T4 and free T3** confirm and grade the abnormality, **TPO and TSH-receptor antibodies** give the cause, and **radioiodine uptake** separates overproduction from destruction - always interpreted against age, pregnancy, illness, drugs and assay interference such as **biotin**.`,keywords:[`TFT`,`TSH`,`free T4`,`FT3`,`reverse T3`,`sick euthyroid syndrome`,`non-thyroidal illness`,`biotin interference`,`macro-TSH`,`heterophile antibodies`,`anti-TPO`,`TRAb`,`TSI`,`thyroglobulin`,`RAIU`,`technetium scan`,`central hypothyroidism`,`resistance to thyroid hormone`,`TSHoma`,`trimester-specific TSH`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, physiology and the Indian burden`,points:[`**Thyroid function tests (TFTs)** are the serum TSH, free T4 and free T3 measurements that classify thyroid status; antibodies, uptake and imaging then establish the **cause**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The thyroid secretes mainly **T4 (about 80%)**; about **80% of circulating T3** comes from peripheral **deiodination of T4** by deiodinases D1 and D2, and T3 is the active hormone at the nuclear receptor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Over 99.9% of T4** (about 99.97%) and **about 99.7% of T3** are protein-bound - mainly to **TBG**, then transthyretin and albumin - so total hormone levels change with binding proteins while free levels do not. [Williams 15e]`,`**India**: an eight-city survey found **hypothyroidism in about 11%** of adults (about one-third previously undiagnosed) and **anti-TPO positivity in about 22%**, far higher than Western series. [Unnikrishnan, IJEM 2013]`,`**Subclinical hypothyroidism** (raised TSH, normal FT4) was the commonest abnormality in that survey at about **8%**, which is why a single raised TSH is the commonest TFT question in the Indian OPD. [Unnikrishnan, IJEM 2013]`,`Women are affected about **three times more often** than men, and prevalence rises with age and with iodine excess as well as deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital hypothyroidism** is about **1 in 1000-1200** births in Indian newborn-screening studies - roughly twice Western rates - making heel-prick TSH screening a public-health priority. [ICMR newborn screening task force]`]},{heading:`The HPT axis and why TSH leads (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**TRH** from the hypothalamus stimulates **TSH** from the anterior pituitary; TSH drives iodide trapping, synthesis and release; **T3 generated locally by D2** in the pituitary feeds back to suppress TRH and TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`The relation is **log-linear**: a **2-fold change in free T4** produces about a **100-fold change in TSH**, so TSH is the most sensitive marker of primary thyroid dysfunction. [Williams 15e]`,`Each person has a narrow individual **set point** within the wide population range, so a value inside the reference range may still be abnormal for that patient. [Williams 15e]`,`TSH lags behind hormone changes by **6-8 weeks**: after starting levothyroxine or antithyroid drugs, TSH equilibrates slowly and can stay **suppressed for months** after Graves is controlled. [ATA 2016]`,`In **central (secondary) hypothyroidism** TSH can be low, normal or even mildly raised but **biologically inactive** (abnormal glycosylation) - TSH is useless here and **free T4** must be followed. [ATA 2014]`,`**Free hormone hypothesis**: only the unbound fraction enters cells, so free T4 reflects status better than total T4 when binding proteins change (pregnancy, oestrogen, liver disease, nephrotic syndrome). [Williams 15e]`,`**Reverse T3** is made from T4 by **D3** (inner-ring deiodination) and is inactive; it rises when D1 is inhibited and D3 is induced, as in illness, fasting and amiodarone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroglobulin** is the storage protein released with hormone; it is **high in thyroiditis and Graves** and **low in factitious thyrotoxicosis** - the key discriminator for exogenous hormone. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`The tests: what each measures`,points:[`**TSH (third-generation immunometric assay)** has a functional sensitivity of about **0.01 mIU/L**; the usual adult reference is about **0.4-4.0 mIU/L**, but each laboratory sets its own. [ATA 2014]`,`**Free T4** is usually measured by **analogue immunoassay**; typical adult range is about **0.8-1.8 ng/dL (10-23 pmol/L)**. Immunoassays are binding-protein dependent; **equilibrium dialysis or LC-MS/MS** is the reference method. [Williams 15e]`,`**Free or total T3** is needed only for suspected **thyrotoxicosis with normal FT4** (T3 toxicosis) and to grade severity; it is **not useful in diagnosing hypothyroidism** because T3 is maintained until late. [ATA 2016]`,`**Anti-TPO antibodies** mark autoimmune thyroiditis - positive in about **90% of Hashimoto** and about **75% of Graves**, and in **10-15% of euthyroid adults**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TSH-receptor antibodies (TRAb)**: third-generation TBII assays have about **97% sensitivity and 99% specificity** for Graves disease; **TSI** bioassays measure the stimulating fraction. [ATA 2016]`,`**Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies** add little for diagnosis but must be measured with **thyroglobulin in thyroid cancer follow-up**, because they cause falsely low thyroglobulin. [ATA 2025 DTC]`,`**Radioiodine uptake (RAIU)** measures how avidly the gland traps iodine; **24-hour uptake** in iodine-sufficient areas is roughly **10-30%**, and the range shifts with dietary iodine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scintigraphy** (Tc-99m pertechnetate or I-123) shows the **distribution** of uptake - diffuse, patchy or a single hot nodule; **Tc-99m** shows trapping only and is cheaper and widely available in India. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification: the TFT patterns examiners ask`,points:[`**Low TSH, high FT4 and/or FT3**: overt primary thyrotoxicosis - Graves, toxic nodule, thyroiditis, exogenous hormone, hCG-mediated. [ATA 2016]`,`**Low TSH, normal FT4, high FT3**: **T3 toxicosis** - early Graves, toxic nodule, iodine deficiency areas. [ATA 2016]`,`**Low TSH, normal FT4 and FT3**: **subclinical hyperthyroidism** - graded **TSH below 0.1** (grade 2) or **0.1-0.4 mIU/L** (grade 1); also first trimester, steroids, dopamine, non-thyroidal illness and recovery from thyrotoxicosis. [ETA 2015]`,`**High TSH, low FT4**: overt **primary hypothyroidism**; **High TSH, normal FT4**: **subclinical hypothyroidism** - grade it **TSH 4.5-9.9** or **10 or more mIU/L**. [ETA 2013]`,`**Low or inappropriately normal TSH with low FT4**: **central hypothyroidism** or severe non-thyroidal illness - check the rest of the pituitary before giving thyroxine. [ATA 2014]`,`**Normal or raised TSH with raised FT4**: **inappropriate TSH secretion** - TSHoma, **resistance to thyroid hormone (THR-beta mutation)**, or more commonly **assay interference**, intermittent levothyroxine adherence or amiodarone. [ETA 2014 RTH]`,`**Normal TSH, high FT4, low-normal FT3** in a sick or treated patient: **amiodarone**, high-dose propranolol, acute illness, or levothyroxine taken just before the sample. [Williams 15e]`,`**Rule**: a **discordant** pattern (TSH and FT4 moving in the same direction) means think **pituitary, illness, drugs or the assay** before treating. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`When to test: history in the OPD`,points:[`Test when symptoms suggest thyroid disease: **fatigue, weight change, cold or heat intolerance, palpitation, tremor, menstrual change, constipation or loose stools**, hoarseness or goitre. [ATA 2014]`,`Test in **high-risk groups**: past thyroid surgery or radioiodine, neck irradiation, type 1 diabetes, other autoimmune disease, Down or Turner syndrome, family history, and **amiodarone, lithium or checkpoint inhibitors**. [ATA 2014]`,`Test in **atrial fibrillation, unexplained heart failure, dyslipidaemia, hyponatraemia, raised CK, infertility, recurrent miscarriage and depression or cognitive decline**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **biotin** (hair, skin and nail supplements, multivitamins) and high-dose biotin for neurological disease - the commonest cause of a falsely 'Graves' pattern today. [FDA safety communication 2019]`,`Ask about **iodinated contrast** in the last 2 months, amiodarone, kelp or iodine supplements - they block uptake scans and can induce dysfunction. [ATA 2016]`,`Ask the **timing of the levothyroxine dose** and adherence; a dose taken just before testing raises FT4 for several hours while TSH stays unchanged. [ATA 2014]`,`Ask about **pregnancy, postpartum status (up to 1 year)** and oral contraceptives, which change binding proteins and reference ranges. [ATA 2017]`,`**Screening** of asymptomatic non-pregnant adults is **not recommended** by USPSTF (insufficient evidence); case-finding in symptomatic and high-risk people is the practical Indian approach. [USPSTF 2015]`]},{heading:`Examination that changes interpretation`,points:[`**Goitre**: diffuse with a **bruit** suggests Graves; **tender** goitre suggests subacute thyroiditis; nodular suggests toxic multinodular goitre; a **small or impalpable** gland with hypothyroidism suggests atrophic thyroiditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eye signs** - proptosis, chemosis, restricted gaze - are specific to **Graves disease** and make TRAb almost redundant; lid lag alone occurs with any thyrotoxicosis. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Pretibial myxoedema and acropachy** are pathognomonic of Graves. [Williams 15e]`,`**Features of hypopituitarism** - loss of axillary and pubic hair, pale skin, visual-field defect, galactorrhoea - point to **central hypothyroidism** when TSH is not raised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed relaxation of ankle jerks** and bradycardia support hypothyroidism; a normal pulse with a 'hypothyroid' TFT should prompt a check for interference or illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of acute illness** (sepsis, cardiac failure, starvation) mean the TFT may reflect **non-thyroidal illness** rather than thyroid disease. [Williams 15e]`,`**Absence of a goitre in a young patient with thyrotoxicosis** and low uptake suggests **factitious thyrotoxicosis** - confirm with a low thyroglobulin. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of discordant results`,points:[`**Raised FT4 with non-suppressed TSH**: first repeat on a different platform and exclude **biotin, heterophile antibodies and anti-T4 antibodies** before thinking of TSHoma or RTH. [ETA 2014 RTH]`,`**TSHoma**: raised **alpha-subunit**, alpha-subunit/TSH molar ratio above 1, pituitary adenoma on MRI, **no TSH rise to TRH**, and often co-secretion of GH or prolactin. [ETA 2013 TSHoma]`,`**Resistance to thyroid hormone beta**: family history, **normal alpha-subunit**, normal MRI, **THRB mutation**; patients are often goitrous with tachycardia but no true thyrotoxicosis elsewhere. [ETA 2014 RTH]`,`**Familial dysalbuminaemic hyperthyroxinaemia**: abnormal albumin binds T4 strongly, giving **high total and analogue-assay FT4 with normal TSH** in a euthyroid patient; equilibrium dialysis is normal. [Williams 15e]`,`**Raised TSH with normal FT4 and no symptoms**: recovery phase of illness, **macro-TSH**, heterophile antibodies, untreated adrenal insufficiency (TSH up to about 10 that falls with hydrocortisone), and obesity (mild TSH rise). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low TSH with normal hormones**: glucocorticoids, dopamine, **first trimester hCG**, elderly, recovery from thyrotoxicosis and genuine subclinical hyperthyroidism - repeat in **6-12 weeks** before labelling. [ETA 2015]`,`**Low FT4 with normal TSH**: central hypothyroidism, **phenytoin or carbamazepine** (lower measured FT4 in analogue assays), late pregnancy assays, and severe illness. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line to tertiary`,points:[`**First line in OPD or district hospital**: **TSH alone** for screening; add **FT4** if TSH is abnormal, if pituitary disease is suspected, or if the patient is unwell. [ATA 2014]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis work-up**: TSH, FT4, **FT3**; then **TRAb** (first choice in India where scans are limited) or **RAIU/scan**; ultrasound with Doppler if neither is available. [ATA 2016]`,`**T3:T4 ratio** (total T3 ng/dL to total T4 mcg/dL) **above 20** favours Graves or toxic nodular goitre; **below 20** favours destructive thyroiditis. [ATA 2016]`,`**Uptake patterns**: **diffuse high** - Graves; **patchy** - toxic multinodular goitre; **single hot nodule with suppressed gland** - toxic adenoma; **near-zero** - thyroiditis, exogenous hormone, iodine load, struma ovarii. [ATA 2016]`,`**Colour Doppler ultrasound**: increased vascularity and high peak systolic velocity in Graves and type 1 amiodarone thyrotoxicosis; **reduced flow** in destructive thyroiditis - a radiation-free alternative to uptake. [ETA 2018]`,`**Hypothyroidism work-up**: TSH and FT4; **anti-TPO** to confirm autoimmunity and predict progression in subclinical disease; no routine ultrasound unless a nodule is felt. [ATA 2014]`,`**Central hypothyroidism**: morning **cortisol**, prolactin, LH/FSH with sex steroids, IGF-1 and **pituitary MRI**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Interference work-up**: repeat after **stopping biotin for at least 2-3 days**, dilution studies, **PEG precipitation** for macro-TSH, heterophile-blocking tubes and testing on a **second platform**. [Endocr Rev 2018 interference review]`]},{heading:`OPD interpretation and action`,points:[`**Never treat a single abnormal TSH** in an asymptomatic person: repeat TSH with FT4 in **6-12 weeks**, because up to half of mildly abnormal values normalise. [ATA 2014]`,`**Subclinical hypothyroidism**: treat if **TSH 10 or more**, or TSH 4.5-9.9 with symptoms, positive anti-TPO, pregnancy or planning pregnancy; avoid treating mild rises in people **over 65-70** (TRUST trial). [ETA 2013; TRUST 2017]`,`**Subclinical hyperthyroidism with TSH below 0.1**: treat in people **over 65**, with heart disease, AF or osteoporosis; observe grade 1 in the young. [ATA 2016]`,`On **levothyroxine**, check TSH **6-8 weeks** after any dose change, then every **6-12 months** once stable; aim for the lab range (about 0.5-2.5 in pregnancy planning). [ATA 2014]`,`On **antithyroid drugs**, follow **FT4 and FT3**, not TSH, for the first **3 months**: TSH stays suppressed even when the patient becomes hypothyroid. [ATA 2016]`,`In **central hypothyroidism** titrate to **FT4 in the upper half** of the reference range and give **hydrocortisone before thyroxine** if ACTH deficiency is not excluded. [ATA 2014]`,`Take the **levothyroxine dose after the blood sample** on the morning of testing for a consistent FT4 reading. [ATA 2014]`]},{heading:`Hospital and ICU: non-thyroidal illness (sick euthyroid)`,points:[`**Non-thyroidal illness syndrome** is altered TFTs in systemic illness without intrinsic thyroid disease; **low T3** is seen in up to **70%** of hospitalised patients. [Williams 15e]`,`Mechanism: cytokines (**IL-6, TNF-alpha**) and cortisol **inhibit D1 and induce D3**, so T3 falls and **reverse T3 rises**; reduced TRH and binding-protein inhibitors lower T4 and TSH in severe illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stages**: mild - **low T3 only**; moderate to severe - **low T3 and low T4** with normal or low TSH; recovery - **TSH rises transiently (can reach about 20 mIU/L)** before normalising. [Williams 15e]`,`A **low total T4 in ICU** is a marker of **poor prognosis** (mortality rises sharply when T4 is below about 4 mcg/dL), not an indication for thyroid hormone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Do not treat** non-thyroidal illness with T4 or T3 - trials have shown no benefit; avoid testing acutely ill patients unless thyroid disease is strongly suspected. [ATA 2014]`,`**Dopamine and high-dose glucocorticoids** used in ICU suppress TSH and can unmask central-looking patterns. [Harrison 22e]`,`If thyroid disease is suspected in illness, a **TSH above 20** or **suppressed below 0.01 with high FT4** suggests true dysfunction; otherwise **repeat 4-6 weeks after recovery**. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Special situations: age, pregnancy, children, CKD`,points:[`**Elderly**: the TSH distribution shifts upward - the **97.5th centile is about 7.5 mIU/L over 80 years** - so mild TSH rises are often physiological. [NHANES III; ETA 2013]`,`**Pregnancy**: hCG stimulates the TSH receptor, so TSH falls in the **first trimester**; use **trimester-specific local ranges**; if unavailable, take about **4.0 mIU/L** as the upper limit in the first trimester. [ATA 2017]`,`In pregnancy **TBG doubles** under oestrogen, so total T4 rises about **1.5 times**; FT4 immunoassays read falsely low late in pregnancy - **total T4 with a 1.5x adjusted range** is an acceptable alternative. [ATA 2017]`,`**India**: MoHFW recommends **universal TSH screening at the first antenatal visit** because of the high prevalence; detail is in the Thyroid disease in pregnancy topic. [MoHFW 2014 national guideline]`,`**Newborns and children**: TSH surges to about **60-80 mIU/L** in the first hours after birth, so heel-prick screening is done **after 48-72 hours**; paediatric ranges are higher than adult ones. [ISPAE 2018]`,`**CKD and nephrotic syndrome**: low T3 is common; nephrotic urinary loss of TBG and thyroxine raises levothyroxine needs; TSH remains the guide. [Williams 15e]`,`**Obesity** is associated with a mildly raised TSH that falls with weight loss; do not start levothyroxine for weight loss. [ETA 2013]`]},{heading:`Assay interference and pitfalls`,points:[`**Biotin**: streptavidin-biotin immunoassays give **falsely low TSH** (sandwich assays) and **falsely high FT4, FT3 and TRAb** (competitive assays) - an exact mimic of **Graves disease**. [FDA safety communication 2019]`,`Biotin doses of **5-10 mg/day** (hair supplements) and especially **100-300 mg/day** (neurology) interfere; stop biotin for **at least 2 days**, longer with high doses, before sampling. [Endocr Rev 2018 interference review]`,`**Heterophile antibodies and HAMA** (vets, mouse exposure, monoclonal therapy) bridge the capture and detection antibodies, usually giving **falsely high TSH**. [Williams 15e]`,`**Macro-TSH** (TSH bound to IgG) gives a **persistently high TSH with normal FT4** and no symptoms; **PEG precipitation recovery below about 25%** suggests it. [Endocr Rev 2018 interference review]`,`**Heparin** (even a flush) activates lipoprotein lipase **in vitro**, releasing free fatty acids that displace T4 and give a **falsely high FT4**; take samples before heparin or at least 10 hours after. [Williams 15e]`,`**Anti-T4 or anti-T3 antibodies** (autoimmune thyroiditis) give spuriously high free hormone values in some assays. [Williams 15e]`,`**Clue to interference**: results that do not fit the clinical picture, or TSH and FT4 both abnormal in the 'wrong' direction - talk to the laboratory before referring for a pituitary MRI. [Endocr Rev 2018 interference review]`]},{heading:`Drug effects on thyroid tests`,points:[`**Lower TSH**: glucocorticoids, **dopamine and dobutamine**, octreotide, bexarotene, metformin (in hypothyroid patients) and opioids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raise TSH**: dopamine antagonists (**metoclopramide, domperidone**), and drugs causing hypothyroidism - **amiodarone, lithium, checkpoint inhibitors, tyrosine kinase inhibitors, interferon-alpha**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raise TBG** (total T4 up, FT4 normal): **oestrogens and pregnancy, tamoxifen, raloxifene**, methadone, heroin, mitotane, hepatitis. **Lower TBG**: androgens, glucocorticoids, nephrotic syndrome, liver failure. [Williams 15e]`,`**Displace T4 from TBG** (transient FT4 rise): **furosemide in high IV dose, salicylates, NSAIDs (mefenamic acid), heparin**. [Williams 15e]`,`**Increase hepatic clearance of T4** (raise levothyroxine needs): **phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, rifampicin**; phenytoin and carbamazepine also lower measured FT4 in euthyroid people. [ATA 2014]`,`**Reduce levothyroxine absorption**: **calcium, iron, PPIs, sucralfate, aluminium antacids, bile acid sequestrants, soy and coffee** - separate by **4 hours**. [ATA 2014]`,`**Amiodarone** inhibits D1: **FT4 rises 20-40%, T3 falls, reverse T3 rises and TSH rises transiently** in the first 3 months - a normal 'amiodarone pattern' that is not thyrotoxicosis. [ETA 2018]`,`**Propranolol in high doses** (over 160 mg/day) modestly lowers T3 conversion; **iodinated contrast** abolishes uptake for **4-8 weeks**. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer to an endocrinologist for **discordant TFTs** not explained by drugs or illness, **suspected central hypothyroidism**, TSHoma or RTH. [ATA 2014]`,`Refer **thyrotoxicosis** for cause-specific therapy if TRAb or uptake is unavailable locally; start a beta-blocker meanwhile. [ATA 2016]`,`**Euthyroid with positive anti-TPO**: check TSH **every 1-2 years**, and before and during pregnancy - progression to hypothyroidism is about **4% per year** when TSH is also raised. [Whickham survey]`,`**Amiodarone**: TFT at baseline, then **every 3-6 months** during therapy and for up to a year after stopping. [ETA 2018]`,`**Lithium**: TSH at baseline and **every 6 months**; **checkpoint inhibitors**: TSH and FT4 **every 4-6 weeks** during treatment. [NICE CG185; ASCO 2021]`,`**Untreated subclinical hypothyroidism**: repeat TSH **every 6-12 months**; subclinical hyperthyroidism: every **3-6 months** initially. [ETA 2013; ETA 2015]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Assay interference**: current practice is to ask about **biotin**, use a second platform and PEG precipitation before labelling rare disorders such as TSHoma or RTH; no new formal guideline changed this in 2023-2026. [Endocr Rev 2018 interference review]`,`**FDA biotin warning** remains active - laboratories and clinicians must screen for biotin before thyroid, troponin and hormone immunoassays; many manufacturers have redesigned assays to tolerate higher biotin. [FDA safety communication 2019]`,`**Age-appropriate TSH ranges**: growing evidence (TRUST and IEMO pooled analyses) supports **not treating subclinical hypothyroidism with TSH below 10 in older adults**. [BMJ Rapid Recommendation 2019; TRUST 2017]`,`**Thyroid cancer follow-up** uses thyroglobulin with **anti-Tg antibodies** measured on every sample, with dynamic risk stratification. [ATA 2025 DTC]`,`**Graves diagnosis**: TRAb is now preferred over uptake scans as the first discriminating test in most settings, including India, because it is faster and avoids radiation. [ETA 2018 Graves; ATA 2016]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitor era**: oncology guidelines mandate baseline and **4-6 weekly TSH and FT4**, and cortisol testing when TSH is low with low FT4 (hypophysitis). [ESMO 2022; ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: 'Why is TSH the first-line test?' - answer the **log-linear relation** and its failure in **central hypothyroidism**. [Williams 15e]`,`Classic pitfall: a young woman on hair supplements with **low TSH, high FT4 and positive TRAb** but no symptoms - **biotin interference**, not Graves. [FDA safety communication 2019]`,`Classic pitfall: giving levothyroxine to a patient with **low FT4 and low TSH** before excluding **ACTH deficiency** - can precipitate adrenal crisis. [ATA 2014]`,`Classic pitfall: titrating carbimazole against a **suppressed TSH** - follow FT4 and FT3 for the first months. [ATA 2016]`,`Know the **sick euthyroid stages** and that T3 falls first; know that **reverse T3** rises. [Harrison 22e]`,`Know uptake: **high - Graves, TMNG, toxic adenoma; low - thyroiditis, exogenous, iodine, struma ovarii**. [ATA 2016]`,`Know trimester TSH: first-trimester upper limit about **4.0 mIU/L** if local ranges are lacking. [ATA 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`TFT patterns and their causes`,columns:[`TSH`,`Free T4`,`Free T3`,`Interpretation`,`Common causes`],rows:[[`Low (below 0.1)`,`High`,`High`,`Overt thyrotoxicosis`,`Graves, toxic nodule, thyroiditis, exogenous T4, hCG`],[`Low`,`Normal`,`High`,`T3 toxicosis`,`Early Graves, toxic adenoma, iodine deficiency`],[`Low`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Subclinical hyperthyroidism`,`Nodular goitre, over-replacement, steroids, dopamine, first trimester, NTI`],[`High`,`Low`,`Low or normal`,`Overt primary hypothyroidism`,`Hashimoto, post-RAI or surgery, iodine deficiency, drugs`],[`High (4.5-10+)`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Subclinical hypothyroidism`,`Early Hashimoto, recovery from NTI, macro-TSH, adrenal insufficiency`],[`Low or normal`,`Low`,`Low`,`Central hypothyroidism or NTI`,`Pituitary tumour, Sheehan, apoplexy, severe illness`],[`Normal or high`,`High`,`High`,`Inappropriate TSH`,`Assay interference, TSHoma, resistance to thyroid hormone`],[`Normal or transiently high`,`High-normal or high`,`Low`,`Amiodarone pattern`,`Amiodarone (D1 inhibition), acute illness`]]},{heading:`Tests, reference values and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Typical reference / cut-off`,`Use`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`TSH (3rd generation)`,`0.4-4.0 mIU/L; about 7.5 upper limit over 80 years`,`First-line screen and LT4 monitoring`,`Useless in central disease; lags 6-8 weeks`],[`Free T4`,`0.8-1.8 ng/dL (10-23 pmol/L)`,`Confirms and grades; central disease`,`Binding-protein, heparin and biotin artefacts`],[`Free T3`,`2.3-4.2 pg/mL (lab-specific)`,`T3 toxicosis; severity`,`Falls in any illness; not for hypothyroidism`],[`Anti-TPO`,`Positive above lab cut-off`,`Autoimmune thyroiditis; risk of progression`,`Positive in 10-15% of euthyroid adults`],[`TRAb (TBII)`,`Above about 1.75 IU/L (assay-specific)`,`Graves diagnosis, pregnancy risk, relapse prediction`,`Biotin gives false positives`],[`Thyroglobulin`,`Low in exogenous T4`,`Factitious thyrotoxicosis; DTC follow-up`,`Anti-Tg antibodies cause false lows`],[`24-h RAIU`,`About 10-30% (iodine-dependent)`,`High versus low uptake thyrotoxicosis`,`Blocked 4-8 weeks after contrast; not in pregnancy`],[`T3:T4 ratio`,`Above 20 (ng/dL : mcg/dL)`,`Favours Graves over thyroiditis`,`Unreliable on antithyroid drugs`]]},{heading:`Drug effects on thyroid tests`,columns:[`Drug`,`Mechanism`,`Effect on tests`,`Practical action`],rows:[[`Biotin (5 mg/day or more)`,`Interferes with streptavidin-biotin assays`,`Falsely low TSH, high FT4, FT3, TRAb`,`Stop at least 2-3 days, retest`],[`Glucocorticoids, dopamine`,`Suppress TSH secretion`,`Low TSH`,`Interpret with FT4; retest off drug`],[`Amiodarone`,`Inhibits D1, iodine load, direct toxicity`,`High FT4, low T3, high rT3, transient high TSH`,`Baseline then 3-6 monthly TFT`],[`Oestrogen, pregnancy, tamoxifen`,`Raise TBG`,`High total T4, normal FT4; higher LT4 need`,`Use free hormone or adjusted total T4`],[`Phenytoin, carbamazepine, rifampicin`,`Induce T4 metabolism; displace binding`,`Low measured FT4, normal TSH`,`Rely on TSH; raise LT4 dose`],[`Heparin`,`In vitro lipolysis, FFA displacement`,`Falsely high FT4`,`Sample before heparin`],[`Calcium, iron, PPI, sucralfate`,`Reduce LT4 absorption`,`Rising TSH on stable dose`,`Separate by 4 hours`],[`Lithium`,`Blocks hormone release`,`High TSH, goitre`,`TSH 6-monthly; add LT4, continue lithium`]]},{heading:`Discordant TFTs: differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`TSH and FT4`,`Discriminating test`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Assay interference (biotin, heterophile)`,`Any pattern that does not fit`,`Repeat off biotin; second platform; dilution`,`Patient clinically well`],[`TSHoma`,`Normal or high TSH, high FT4`,`High alpha-subunit; MRI adenoma; no TRH response`,`Visual-field loss, GH or PRL co-secretion`],[`Resistance to thyroid hormone beta`,`Normal or high TSH, high FT4`,`Normal alpha-subunit; THRB mutation; family testing`,`Family affected; MRI normal`],[`Central hypothyroidism`,`Low or normal TSH, low FT4`,`Cortisol, prolactin, gonadotrophins, MRI`,`Other pituitary deficits`],[`Non-thyroidal illness`,`Low T3 first; low T4 and TSH if severe`,`Repeat after recovery`,`Sick ICU patient`],[`Macro-TSH`,`High TSH, normal FT4`,`PEG precipitation recovery low`,`No symptoms, no antibodies`]]},{heading:`Uptake and scan patterns in thyrotoxicosis`,columns:[`Cause`,`RAIU`,`Scan pattern`,`Other clue`],rows:[[`Graves disease`,`High`,`Diffuse uniform uptake`,`TRAb positive, eye signs, bruit`],[`Toxic multinodular goitre`,`High or normal`,`Patchy hot and cold areas`,`Elderly, nodular goitre`],[`Toxic adenoma`,`High or normal`,`Single hot nodule, rest suppressed`,`Palpable nodule, TRAb negative`],[`Subacute or painless thyroiditis`,`Very low (below 5%)`,`Little or no uptake`,`Tender gland or postpartum; ESR high in subacute`],[`Factitious thyrotoxicosis`,`Very low`,`No uptake`,`Low thyroglobulin, no goitre`],[`Iodine-induced and AIT type 2`,`Low`,`Poor uptake`,`Recent contrast or amiodarone`]]}],redFlags:[`**Low FT4 with low or normal TSH** and headache, visual-field loss or hypotension - suspect pituitary disease and give hydrocortisone before thyroxine.`,`**Thyrotoxicosis with fever, delirium, heart failure or AF with fast rate** - possible thyroid storm; admit.`,`**Very high TSH with drowsiness, hypothermia or hyponatraemia** - myxoedema coma; admit to ICU.`,`**Fever or sore throat on carbimazole** - check neutrophil count the same day for agranulocytosis.`,`**Abnormal TFT in pregnancy** - same-week obstetric-endocrine plan; uncontrolled disease harms the fetus.`,`**Painful tender goitre with dysphagia or stridor**, or a rapidly growing hard goitre - urgent evaluation for haemorrhage, lymphoma or anaplastic cancer.`,`**Neonatal heel-prick TSH above the recall cut-off** - recall and start levothyroxine within 2 weeks of birth.`],pearls:[`**TSH: the 2-fold FT4 change gives a 100-fold TSH change** - the log-linear rule.`,`**'TSH lies in three places'**: pituitary disease, the first months of treatment, and acute illness.`,`**Biotin mimics Graves**: low TSH, high FT4, positive TRAb - ask about hair supplements.`,`**Sick euthyroid: T3 falls first, rT3 rises, TSH climbs on recovery** - do not treat.`,`**Low uptake causes - 'TIES'**: Thyroiditis, Iodine load, Exogenous hormone, Struma ovarii.`,`**Thyroglobulin low with thyrotoxicosis = factitious** until proved otherwise.`,`**T3:T4 ratio above 20 = synthesis (Graves or nodular)**; below 20 = destruction.`,`**Before thyroxine in central hypothyroidism, cortisol first** - steroids before thyroid.`,`**Elderly TSH up to about 7.5 can be normal**; first-trimester upper limit about 4.0.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman taking a hair-and-nail supplement has TSH 0.02 mIU/L, FT4 3.1 ng/dL and positive TRAb, but she has no symptoms, no goitre and a pulse of 72/min. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start carbimazole 20 mg daily`,`Arrange a radioiodine uptake scan`,`Stop the supplement and repeat the tests after a few days`,`Start propranolol and review in 3 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Hair supplements contain high-dose biotin, which interferes with streptavidin-biotin immunoassays: it falsely lowers sandwich-assay TSH and falsely raises competitive-assay FT4 and TRAb - an exact biochemical mimic of Graves. The discordance with a clinically euthyroid patient is the clue. Starting carbimazole or propranolol treats a laboratory artefact, and an uptake scan exposes her to radiation before the artefact is excluded.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with headache and bitemporal field loss has TSH 1.2 mIU/L and FT4 0.5 ng/dL (low). Which is the most appropriate immediate action before starting levothyroxine?`,options:[`Measure anti-TPO antibodies`,`Check morning cortisol and give hydrocortisone if deficient`,`Repeat TSH in 6 weeks`,`Order a thyroid ultrasound`],answer:1,explanation:`A low FT4 with an inappropriately normal TSH and a pituitary mass is central hypothyroidism. Thyroxine accelerates cortisol clearance and can precipitate adrenal crisis if coexisting ACTH deficiency is untreated, so cortisol must be assessed and replaced first. Anti-TPO and thyroid ultrasound assess the gland, which is not the problem, and waiting 6 weeks ignores a pituitary mass.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-q3`,stem:`In a patient in ICU with severe sepsis, which pattern is most typical of non-thyroidal illness syndrome?`,options:[`High TSH, low FT4, high T3`,`Suppressed TSH, high FT4, high T3`,`High TSH, high FT4, low reverse T3`,`Low T3, raised reverse T3, normal or low TSH`],answer:3,explanation:`Cytokines inhibit type 1 deiodinase and induce type 3 deiodinase, so T3 falls and reverse T3 rises; in severe illness T4 and TSH also fall. The thyrotoxic pattern of suppressed TSH with high FT4 and T3 is primary hyperthyroidism, and a high TSH with low FT4 is primary hypothyroidism - although TSH can rise transiently during recovery, the hallmark of NTI is low T3 with high rT3.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-q4`,stem:`A woman with thyrotoxicosis and a non-tender gland has a 24-hour radioiodine uptake of 2% and a very low serum thyroglobulin. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Exogenous (factitious) thyroid hormone intake`,`Painless lymphocytic thyroiditis`,`Graves disease`,`Toxic multinodular goitre`],answer:0,explanation:`Low uptake thyrotoxicosis means either destruction (thyroiditis) or an extrathyroidal source. Thyroglobulin is released with destruction and is high in thyroiditis, but it is suppressed when thyroid hormone is taken by mouth, so a low thyroglobulin points to factitious intake. Graves disease and toxic multinodular goitre are high-uptake causes, and painless thyroiditis would have a raised thyroglobulin.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-q5`,stem:`According to ATA 2017, if a laboratory has no trimester-specific reference range, what upper limit of TSH should be used in the first trimester?`,options:[`2.5 mIU/L`,`4.0 mIU/L`,`5.5 mIU/L`,`10 mIU/L`],answer:1,explanation:`ATA 2017 replaced the old fixed cut-off of 2.5 mIU/L with population- and trimester-specific ranges; where these are unavailable, an upper limit of about 4.0 mIU/L is used in the first trimester (roughly 0.5 below the non-pregnant limit). The 2.5 value was the outdated 2011 cut-off, while 5.5 and 10 would miss clinically relevant hypothyroidism in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the physiological basis of thyroid function tests. Discuss the interpretation of TSH, free T4 and free T3 patterns, and the common pitfalls in their interpretation in clinical practice.`,openingLines:[`Thyroid function tests assess the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis; TSH is the most sensitive first-line test because its relation to free T4 is inverse and log-linear - a 2-fold change in FT4 gives about a 100-fold change in TSH.`,`Interpretation depends on the pattern of TSH with free hormones, and on age, pregnancy, illness, drugs and assay interference, all of which can mimic thyroid disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Physiology that underlies the tests`,points:[`TRH stimulates TSH, which drives T4 (80%) and T3 (20%) secretion; most T3 comes from peripheral deiodination by D1 and D2.`,`Over 99.9% of T4 is protein-bound (TBG, transthyretin, albumin); only free hormone is active.`,`Negative feedback via pituitary D2-generated T3 sets an individual TSH set point.`]},{heading:`The tests`,points:[`TSH third-generation assay (0.4-4.0 mIU/L); FT4 (0.8-1.8 ng/dL); FT3 for T3 toxicosis.`,`Antibodies: anti-TPO (Hashimoto), TRAb (Graves), anti-Tg (with thyroglobulin in cancer follow-up).`,`RAIU and scan: high versus low uptake; thyroglobulin for factitious disease.`]},{heading:`Patterns and interpretation`,points:[`Low TSH with high FT4/FT3 - overt thyrotoxicosis; low TSH with normal hormones - subclinical.`,`High TSH with low FT4 - primary hypothyroidism; high TSH with normal FT4 - subclinical.`,`Low or normal TSH with low FT4 - central hypothyroidism or NTI.`,`Normal or high TSH with high FT4 - interference, TSHoma or RTH.`]},{heading:`Non-thyroidal illness`,points:[`Low T3 with high rT3 from D1 inhibition and D3 induction; low T4 in severe illness predicts mortality.`,`TSH rises transiently in recovery; do not treat; retest after recovery.`]},{heading:`Pitfalls`,points:[`Biotin - falsely low TSH and high FT4 and TRAb; heterophile antibodies and macro-TSH - falsely high TSH.`,`Drugs: steroids and dopamine lower TSH; oestrogen raises TBG; amiodarone raises FT4 and rT3; heparin raises FT4 in vitro.`,`TSH lag after treatment; TSH useless in central disease; age and pregnancy ranges.`]},{heading:`Practical approach`,points:[`TSH first; add FT4 if abnormal or pituitary disease suspected; repeat mild abnormalities in 6-12 weeks.`,`Use TRAb or uptake to find the cause of thyrotoxicosis; check cortisol before thyroxine in central hypothyroidism.`]}],mustDraw:[`HPT axis diagram with feedback loops and the deiodinase steps`,`TFT pattern table: TSH, FT4, FT3 and interpretation`,`Log-linear TSH versus FT4 curve`],markSplit:[{part:`Physiology and tests`,marks:2},{part:`Pattern table and interpretation`,marks:4},{part:`Non-thyroidal illness`,marks:1},{part:`Pitfalls (drugs, assay, age, pregnancy)`,marks:3}],keywords:[`TSH`,`free T4`,`log-linear`,`sick euthyroid`,`biotin`,`central hypothyroidism`,`RAIU`,`TRAb`]},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-function-tests-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on sick euthyroid syndrome (non-thyroidal illness syndrome).`,openingLines:[`Non-thyroidal illness syndrome is an alteration of thyroid function tests during systemic illness, starvation or surgery in the absence of intrinsic thyroid disease.`,`The hallmark is a low T3 with a raised reverse T3; in severe illness T4 and TSH also fall.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha), cortisol and fasting inhibit D1 and induce D3.`,`Reduced TRH and TSH secretion and binding-protein inhibitors lower T4 in severe illness.`]},{heading:`Stages`,points:[`Low T3 syndrome; low T3 and low T4 syndrome; recovery with transient TSH rise up to about 20 mIU/L.`]},{heading:`Significance and management`,points:[`Low T4 in ICU predicts mortality.`,`No benefit from T4 or T3 treatment; avoid testing in acute illness; repeat 4-6 weeks after recovery.`,`Distinguish from central hypothyroidism (pituitary signs, cortisol) and primary hypothyroidism (TSH above 20).`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of T3, T4, rT3 and TSH against severity and recovery`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Stages and pattern`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`low T3`,`reverse T3`,`deiodinase`,`ICU`,`recovery TSH rise`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the thyroid gland`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`American Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Treatment of Hypothyroidism, 2014`,`2016 ATA Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis`,`2017 ATA Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Thyroid Disease During Pregnancy and the Postpartum`,`ETA Guideline: Management of Subclinical Hypothyroidism, 2013; ETA Guideline on Subclinical Hyperthyroidism, 2015`,`FDA Safety Communication: Biotin interference with laboratory tests, 2017 (updated 2019)`,`Unnikrishnan AG et al. Thyroid disorders in India: an epidemiological perspective. Indian J Endocrinol Metab 2013; MoHFW National Guidelines for Screening of Hypothyroidism during Pregnancy, 2014`]}),r.push({id:`endocrine-graves-disease`,title:`Graves disease and thyroid eye disease`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Graves disease** is an organ-specific autoimmune disorder in which **stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies (TRAb/TSI)** cause **diffuse goitre and hyperthyroidism**, often with **Graves orbitopathy** and occasionally pretibial myxoedema; it is treated with **thionamides (carbimazole/methimazole) for 12-18 months, radioiodine or total thyroidectomy**, and active moderate-to-severe orbitopathy with **IV methylprednisolone plus mycophenolate (EUGOGO 2021)** or **teprotumumab**.`,keywords:[`Graves disease`,`Basedow disease`,`TRAb`,`TSI`,`carbimazole`,`methimazole`,`propylthiouracil`,`agranulocytosis`,`block and replace`,`radioiodine`,`total thyroidectomy`,`Graves orbitopathy`,`thyroid eye disease`,`clinical activity score`,`EUGOGO`,`teprotumumab`,`selenium`,`IV methylprednisolone`,`pretibial myxoedema`,`GREAT score`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Graves disease** is autoimmune hyperthyroidism caused by **IgG antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor**; it is the commonest cause of hyperthyroidism in iodine-sufficient populations (**60-80%**). [Harrison 22e]`,`Annual incidence is about **20-50 per 100,000**; lifetime risk is about **3% in women and 0.5% in men** (female to male about **5-10:1**), peaking at **30-50 years**. [Williams 15e]`,`**India**: Graves is the leading cause of thyrotoxicosis in hospital series; since universal salt iodisation, autoimmune thyroid disease has become more common, and toxic multinodular goitre persists in previously iodine-deficient hill regions. [Unnikrishnan, IJEM 2013]`,`**Graves orbitopathy (thyroid eye disease, TED)** is clinically evident in about **25-40%** of patients at diagnosis, but **moderate-to-severe** disease in only about **5-6%** and sight-threatening disease in about **3-5%**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Pretibial myxoedema (thyroid dermopathy)** occurs in about **1-4%** and almost always with orbitopathy; **acropachy** in under 1%. [Williams 15e]`,`About **10%** of TED occurs in **euthyroid or hypothyroid** patients (euthyroid Graves, Hashimoto), so the eye can precede or follow the thyroid by years. [ATA/ETA TED consensus 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Genetic susceptibility** accounts for about **70-80%** of risk (twin studies): HLA-DR3, **CTLA-4, PTPN22**, CD40, TSHR and thyroglobulin gene polymorphisms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Female sex**, family history and other autoimmunity (type 1 diabetes, vitiligo, pernicious anaemia, coeliac disease, myasthenia) increase risk. [Williams 15e]`,`**Smoking** raises the risk of Graves about 2-fold and of orbitopathy about **5-fold**, and makes orbitopathy more severe and less responsive. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Stress, high iodine intake, postpartum period** (immune rebound) and **infection** are recognised triggers. [Williams 15e]`,`**Drug triggers**: **alemtuzumab** (Graves in up to about a third of treated MS patients), immune checkpoint inhibitors (rarely), interferon-alpha and **highly active antiretroviral therapy (immune reconstitution)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Radioiodine** can provoke new or worse orbitopathy in about **15%**, particularly in smokers with high TRAb and active eye disease. [EUGOGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why the signs and the drugs`,points:[`**TRAb (TSI)** bind the TSH receptor and mimic TSH, stimulating cAMP-driven hormone synthesis, iodide uptake and follicular hyperplasia - giving a **diffuse vascular goitre with a bruit** and suppressed pituitary TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Excess T3** raises Na-K-ATPase activity and thermogenesis: **heat intolerance, sweating, weight loss with hyperphagia**; increases beta-1 receptors and lowers SVR: **tachycardia, wide pulse pressure, AF, high-output failure**. [Williams 15e]`,`Heightened **adrenergic sensitivity** explains tremor, anxiety, **lid retraction and lid lag** (Muller muscle) - which is why **beta-blockers** relieve symptoms without altering hormone levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orbitopathy**: TRAb and **IGF-1 receptor** signalling activate **orbital fibroblasts**, which secrete **glycosaminoglycans (hyaluronan)** and differentiate into adipocytes, swelling muscles and fat inside a fixed bony orbit - proptosis, restriction, diplopia and **optic nerve compression at the apex**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`The **inferior and medial recti** are enlarged first, so **upgaze and abduction** are restricted earliest; muscle enlargement **spares the tendons** on CT, unlike orbital myositis. [Williams 15e]`,`**Thionamides** inhibit **thyroid peroxidase**, blocking iodination and coupling; **PTU also blocks peripheral D1** conversion of T4 to T3; thionamides also have a modest immunomodulatory effect that lowers TRAb. [ATA 2016]`,`**Teprotumumab** is a monoclonal antibody against **IGF-1R**, blocking the TSHR/IGF-1R crosstalk on orbital fibroblasts - hence its effect on proptosis. [ATA/ETA TED consensus 2022]`,`**Natural history**: TED follows **Rundle's curve** - an active inflammatory phase of **6-18 months**, a plateau, then an inactive fibrotic phase; immunosuppression works only in the active phase. [EUGOGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Thyrotoxicosis severity**: overt (low TSH, high FT4/FT3) or subclinical; grade FT4 as multiples of the upper limit to choose the starting dose. [ATA 2016]`,`**Goitre grading (WHO)**: grade 0 none; grade 1 palpable not visible; grade 2 visible with the neck in normal position. [WHO 2007]`,`**Clinical Activity Score (CAS)**, 7 items at first visit, one point each: spontaneous retrobulbar pain, pain on eye movement, eyelid redness, conjunctival redness, eyelid swelling, **chemosis**, caruncle swelling; **CAS 3 or more of 7 = active**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`On follow-up CAS becomes **10 points** by adding: proptosis increase of 2 mm or more, eye movement decrease of 8 degrees or more, and visual-acuity loss in 1-3 months. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**EUGOGO severity**: **mild** - minor impact on life, lid retraction under 2 mm, exophthalmos under 3 mm above normal, transient or no diplopia; **moderate-to-severe** - lid retraction 2 mm or more, exophthalmos 3 mm or more, inconstant or constant diplopia, moderate soft-tissue involvement. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Sight-threatening TED**: **dysthyroid optic neuropathy (DON)** and/or **corneal breakdown**; rarely globe subluxation. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**NOSPECS** (Werner) is the older mnemonic grading - No signs, Only signs, Soft tissue, Proptosis, Extraocular muscles, Corneal, Sight loss - still asked in Indian vivas. [Werner 1977]`,`**Exophthalmometry (Hertel)**: upper normal about **20 mm** in Asians (lower than in Africans); a difference of over 2 mm between eyes is abnormal. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history`,points:[`**Weight loss despite increased appetite**, heat intolerance, sweating, palpitation, tremor, anxiety, insomnia, **frequent loose stools**, fatigue and **proximal weakness**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Women: **oligomenorrhoea**, subfertility; men: reduced libido, gynaecomastia (raised SHBG and aromatisation). [Williams 15e]`,`**Eye symptoms**: grittiness, watering, photophobia, retro-orbital ache, **diplopia** (worse on waking or on upgaze), and **blurred or dim colour vision** - the last suggests optic neuropathy. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Elderly** may have **apathetic thyrotoxicosis** - weight loss, AF, heart failure and depression with few adrenergic signs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxic periodic paralysis** - sudden flaccid weakness with **hypokalaemia** after a carbohydrate meal or exercise - occurs mostly in **Asian men** and may be the first presentation. [ATA 2016]`,`Ask about **smoking, pregnancy plans, breastfeeding, biotin**, previous drug reactions and occupation (radioiodine and children at home). [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Hands**: warm moist palms, **fine tremor**, palmar erythema, **onycholysis (Plummer nails)**, **acropachy** (clubbing with periosteal new bone). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Pulse**: sinus tachycardia that persists in sleep, **AF**, collapsing pulse, wide pulse pressure; **systolic flow murmur** (Means-Lerman scratch). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Goitre**: diffuse, smooth, firm, moves with swallowing, with a **bruit or thrill** - the bruit is specific to Graves. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Lid signs**: **Dalrymple** (lid retraction, sclera visible above the cornea), **von Graefe** (lid lag on downgaze), **Stellwag** (infrequent blinking), **Joffroy** (absent forehead wrinkling on upgaze), **Moebius** (poor convergence). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Orbitopathy**: proptosis (look from above), **chemosis**, caruncle swelling, periorbital oedema, **restricted upgaze and abduction**, exposure keratitis, and **relative afferent pupillary defect** and colour-vision loss in DON. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Pretibial myxoedema**: raised, non-pitting, orange-peel plaques over the shins; **proximal myopathy** (difficulty rising from a squat), brisk reflexes. [Williams 15e]`,`**Bedside DON screen** in every visit: visual acuity, **colour vision (Ishihara red desaturation)**, pupils for RAPD, and fundus for disc swelling. [EUGOGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Toxic multinodular goitre** - older, nodular gland, patchy uptake, TRAb negative, no orbitopathy. [ATA 2016]`,`**Toxic adenoma** - single nodule, hot on scan with suppressed rest, TRAb negative. [ATA 2016]`,`**Painless or postpartum thyroiditis** - small non-tender goitre, **low uptake**, T3:T4 ratio below 20, self-limiting; **subacute thyroiditis** - painful tender gland, high ESR. [ATA 2016]`,`**Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis** - first trimester, hyperemesis, **TRAb negative**, no goitre or eye signs, settles by 18-20 weeks. [ATA 2017]`,`**Anxiety disorder** - normal TFT; weight usually stable; palms cold and clammy rather than warm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Unilateral proptosis**: TED is still the commonest cause in adults, but exclude **orbital tumour, lymphoma, carotid-cavernous fistula, orbital cellulitis and IgG4 disease** with imaging. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Diplopia with ptosis** in a Graves patient - consider coexisting **myasthenia gravis** (ptosis is not a TED sign; TED gives lid retraction). [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**TSH suppressed** (below 0.01 mIU/L) with **raised FT4 and/or FT3**; about 5-10% have T3 toxicosis. [ATA 2016]`,`**TRAb** confirms Graves (sensitivity about 97%, specificity about 99%); it also predicts relapse and **fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis** in pregnancy. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**RAIU or Tc-99m scan**: diffuse high uptake; needed mainly when TRAb is negative or a nodule is felt, and to plan radioiodine dose. [ATA 2016]`,`**Baseline before thionamides**: **complete blood count with differential** and **liver function tests** - to separate drug toxicity from disease effects later (thyrotoxicosis itself can cause mild neutropenia and raised ALP). [ATA 2016]`,`**Other baseline**: ECG (AF), glucose, calcium, potassium (if periodic paralysis), lipids; echo if heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ultrasound with Doppler**: diffuse hypoechoic, hypervascular gland ('thyroid inferno'); ultrasound any palpable nodule before radioiodine. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Orbit imaging (MRI or non-contrast CT)**: for **asymmetric or atypical** disease, suspected DON (apical crowding) and before decompression; STIR MRI shows muscle activity. [EUGOGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: antithyroid drugs`,points:[`**Beta-blocker** for symptoms: **propranolol 10-40 mg three to four times daily** (also reduces T4 to T3 conversion) or **atenolol 25-100 mg once daily**; avoid in asthma (use diltiazem). [ATA 2016]`,`**Carbimazole/methimazole is the drug of choice** (once daily, less hepatotoxic); **carbimazole 10 mg is about methimazole 6 mg**. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Starting dose by FT4**: methimazole **5-10 mg** if FT4 is 1-1.5 times the upper limit, **10-20 mg** if 1.5-2 times, **30-40 mg** if 2-3 times; in India carbimazole is commonly started at **20-40 mg/day**. [ATA 2016]`,`**Titration regimen**: check FT4/FT3 at **4-6 weeks**, reduce the dose by 30-50% as levels normalise, maintain on **5-10 mg/day** for **12-18 months**, then stop if TRAb is negative. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Block-and-replace** (carbimazole 30-40 mg plus levothyroxine) for about 6 months has **more side effects and no lower relapse**; reserve for unstable disease; **never in pregnancy**. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Remission and relapse**: about **50% relapse** after a course, mostly within the first year; higher with large goitre, high TRAb, young age, smoking and high FT4 - the **GREAT score** estimates the risk. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Long-term low-dose methimazole** (for **5 years or more**) is an accepted alternative to ablation, with lower relapse rates in randomised trials. [ETA 2018 Graves; Azizi RCTs]`,`**PTU** (100-150 mg three times daily) is reserved for the **first trimester of pregnancy, thyroid storm**, and minor reactions to methimazole when RAI or surgery is not an option, because of **severe hepatotoxicity**. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects of thionamides`,points:[`**Minor reactions** (5%): rash, urticaria, **arthralgia**, GI upset; treat rash with antihistamine; **arthralgia** may herald the 'antithyroid arthritis syndrome' - stop the drug. [ATA 2016]`,`**Agranulocytosis** (neutrophils below 500/microlitre) in about **0.2-0.5%**, usually in the **first 3 months**, dose-related for methimazole; presents with **fever and sore throat**. [ATA 2016]`,`Give every patient **written advice** to stop the drug and get a **same-day CBC** if fever, sore throat or mouth ulcers occur; routine CBC monitoring is optional. [ATA 2016]`,`**Agranulocytosis management**: stop the drug, admit, broad-spectrum antibiotics if febrile, **G-CSF** may help; **never switch to the other thionamide** (cross-reactivity) - move to RAI or surgery. [ATA 2016]`,`**Hepatotoxicity**: **PTU - fulminant hepatocellular necrosis** (FDA boxed warning 2010, about 1 in 10,000 adults, higher in children); **methimazole - cholestatic jaundice**. [ATA 2016]`,`**PTU** also causes **ANCA-positive vasculitis**; methimazole rarely causes insulin autoimmune syndrome (hypoglycaemia) and acute pancreatitis. [ETA 2018 Graves]`,`**Teratogenicity**: methimazole/carbimazole **embryopathy** (aplasia cutis, choanal and oesophageal atresia, omphalocele) with exposure in **weeks 6-10**; PTU causes milder face, neck and urinary malformations. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Definitive therapy: radioiodine and surgery`,points:[`**Radioiodine (I-131)**: fixed activity of about **10-15 mCi (370-555 MBq)** aims at **hypothyroidism**, usually within **2-6 months**; stop carbimazole **3-5 days** before. [ATA 2016]`,`**RAI contraindications**: **pregnancy and breastfeeding**, planned pregnancy within **6 months**, suspicion of cancer, and **active moderate-to-severe or sight-threatening orbitopathy**. [ATA 2016]`,`**Steroid cover with RAI**: mild active TED or smokers at risk - **oral prednisolone about 0.3-0.5 mg/kg/day** started 1-3 days after RAI and tapered over **about 3 months**; a lower dose (about **0.2 mg/kg for 6 weeks**) suffices in milder cases. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**After RAI**: check FT4 and T3 at **4-6 weeks**, then every 4-6 weeks; start levothyroxine promptly when hypothyroid, since **hypothyroidism worsens TED**. [ATA 2016]`,`**Total thyroidectomy** for **large goitre, compressive symptoms, suspicious nodule, moderate-to-severe TED, pregnancy needing definitive therapy (second trimester)** or patient preference; relapse is essentially nil. [ATA 2016]`,`**Preparation for surgery**: make euthyroid with thionamide; **potassium iodide** (SSKI 1-2 drops or Lugol 5-7 drops three times daily) for **10 days** pre-op reduces gland vascularity; correct vitamin D and calcium. [ATA 2016]`,`**Surgical risks** (high-volume surgeon): permanent **hypoparathyroidism under 2%**, permanent **recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy under 1%**; lifelong levothyroxine **1.6 mcg/kg/day**. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Management of Graves orbitopathy (EUGOGO 2021)`,points:[`**For all**: restore and keep **euthyroidism**, **stop smoking**, lubricant drops, sunglasses, head-up sleeping and prisms for diplopia; refer moderate-to-severe disease to a combined thyroid-eye clinic. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Mild active TED of short duration**: **selenium 100 mcg twice daily (selenium selenite) for 6 months** - benefit shown in selenium-deficient populations; local measures otherwise. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Moderate-to-severe active TED - first line**: **IV methylprednisolone 0.5 g weekly for 6 weeks then 0.25 g weekly for 6 weeks (cumulative 4.5 g) plus mycophenolate sodium 0.72 g/day for 24 weeks**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Severe or constant-diplopia active disease**: higher-dose IVMP up to **7.5 g cumulative**; never exceed **8 g per course**; check LFT, glucose, BP and hepatitis serology because of **acute liver injury** risk. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Second line**: a second IVMP course, **oral prednisolone plus ciclosporin or azathioprine**, **orbital radiotherapy** (with steroids; avoid in diabetic retinopathy), **teprotumumab, rituximab or tocilizumab**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Teprotumumab**: **10 mg/kg IV, then 20 mg/kg every 3 weeks for 7 more infusions (8 in total)**; reduces proptosis by about **3 mm** and diplopia; side effects - **hyperglycaemia, hearing impairment, IBD flare, muscle cramps**; contraindicated in pregnancy; costly and not widely available in India. [ATA/ETA TED consensus 2022]`,`**Sight-threatening DON**: **IVMP 0.5-1 g daily for 3 consecutive days (or alternate days), repeated the next week**; if response is poor within **2 weeks**, **urgent orbital decompression**. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Inactive (burnt-out) disease**: rehabilitative surgery in order - **orbital decompression, then squint surgery, then eyelid surgery** - after 6 months of inactivity. [EUGOGO 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **PTU in the first trimester**, then methimazole may be used; lowest dose to keep FT4 at or just above the upper limit; measure **TRAb early and at 18-22 weeks** (fetal monitoring if over 3 times the upper limit); details in the Thyroid disease in pregnancy topic. [ATA 2017]`,`**Women planning pregnancy**: discuss definitive therapy before conception, or switch to PTU before conception or as soon as pregnancy is confirmed; delay conception **6 months after RAI**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Breastfeeding**: methimazole up to **20 mg/day** or PTU up to **450 mg/day** is safe. [ATA 2017]`,`**Children**: methimazole only (**PTU is avoided**), about **0.2-0.5 mg/kg/day**; remission is lower, so long courses or definitive therapy are often needed. [ETA 2022 paediatric Graves]`,`**Elderly and cardiac disease**: beta-blocker plus anticoagulation by CHA2DS2-VA score for AF; **radioiodine** is often preferred as definitive therapy after pre-treatment with thionamide. [ATA 2016]`,`**Diabetes**: thyrotoxicosis worsens glycaemia; **IVMP raises glucose** - monitor; orbital radiotherapy is avoided with retinopathy; teprotumumab worsens hyperglycaemia. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: carbimazole, propranolol and Lugol iodine are on the **NLEM** and in Jan Aushadhi; TRAb, RAI and IVMP are mainly at district or tertiary centres. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications, follow-up and prognosis`,points:[`**Complications of untreated disease**: **AF and stroke**, high-output heart failure, **osteoporosis**, thyroid storm, periodic paralysis, and pregnancy loss or preterm birth. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow-up on thionamides**: FT4 and FT3 every **4-6 weeks** until stable, then every **2-3 months**; TSH becomes useful after about 3 months; TRAb before stopping. [ATA 2016]`,`**After stopping**: TFT **monthly for 6 months**, then every 3 months for a year, then yearly - because relapse is commonest in the first year. [ATA 2016]`,`**Late hypothyroidism**: about **20%** of patients in remission become hypothyroid over 10-20 years (coexisting Hashimoto); check TSH yearly lifelong. [Williams 15e]`,`**TED prognosis**: most mild disease improves or stabilises; **smoking and uncontrolled thyroid status** predict progression; quality-of-life loss is substantial - use the **GO-QOL** questionnaire. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Refer urgently** to ophthalmology: reduced acuity, colour desaturation, RAPD, corneal ulcer, globe subluxation or rapidly progressive disease. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**Counselling**: smoking cessation (including passive smoking), avoid iodine supplements and kelp, contraception with carbimazole, and a written agranulocytosis warning. [ETA 2018 Graves]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**EUGOGO 2021** made **IVMP plus mycophenolate** the first-line therapy for active moderate-to-severe TED, replacing IVMP alone. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`**ATA/ETA 2022 TED consensus** places **teprotumumab** as a preferred option where available, especially for proptosis and diplopia, and IVMP where cost or access limits use. [ATA/ETA TED consensus 2022]`,`**FDA label update 2023**: teprotumumab indication extended to TED **regardless of activity or duration**, and a warning added for **hearing impairment** (baseline and follow-up audiometry advised). [FDA teprotumumab label 2023]`,`**Newer agents in trials (2024-2026)**: other IGF-1R antibodies and oral small molecules, and **FcRn inhibitors** that lower TRAb, are in phase 3 trials; none is yet standard in India. [ATA/ETA TED consensus 2022]`,`**Long-term methimazole** is now widely accepted as a valid alternative to ablation, backed by randomised data showing relapse rates around 15% versus about 50% after a standard course. [ETA 2018 Graves; Azizi RCTs]`,`**Relapse prediction**: the **GREAT score** (age, FT4, TRAb, goitre size) is used to counsel patients at diagnosis about the chance of remission. [ETA 2018 Graves]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: aetiopathogenesis, features, investigations and management of Graves disease - draw the TRAb-TSHR mechanism and a treatment flow chart. [Harrison 22e]`,`Know the **three treatment options** with pros and cons - drugs (relapse), RAI (hypothyroidism, TED worsening), surgery (hypoparathyroidism, nerve palsy). [ATA 2016]`,`Know the **agranulocytosis advice** by heart: stop, same-day CBC, no switching thionamide. [ATA 2016]`,`Know **CAS 7 items** and the cut-off of **3**; examiners ask what 'active' means and why it matters (immunosuppression works only then). [EUGOGO 2021]`,`Common mistake: giving **RAI to a smoker with active TED** without steroid cover. [EUGOGO 2021]`,`Common mistake: using **PTU as routine first-line** - methimazole/carbimazole is first-line except in first trimester and storm. [ATA 2016]`,`Know eponymous **lid signs** (Dalrymple, von Graefe, Stellwag, Joffroy, Moebius) and that **lid lag occurs in any thyrotoxicosis**, while proptosis is specific to Graves. [Hutchison 25e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in Graves disease and orbitopathy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Carbimazole`,`20-40 mg/day oral (once daily); maintenance 5-10 mg`,`Titrate every 4-6 weeks; total 12-18 months, or long-term low dose`,`Rash, arthralgia, agranulocytosis 0.2-0.5%, cholestasis, embryopathy (weeks 6-10)`],[`Methimazole`,`5-40 mg/day oral by FT4 multiple`,`As carbimazole`,`As carbimazole`],[`Propylthiouracil`,`100-150 mg three times daily oral`,`First trimester; storm; short-term`,`Fulminant hepatic necrosis, ANCA vasculitis, agranulocytosis`],[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg three to four times daily oral`,`Until euthyroid`,`Bronchospasm, bradycardia; avoid in asthma (use diltiazem)`],[`Potassium iodide (Lugol/SSKI)`,`Lugol 5-7 drops or SSKI 1-2 drops three times daily`,`10 days before thyroidectomy`,`Rash, sialadenitis; escape after 2 weeks`],[`Radioiodine I-131`,`10-15 mCi oral single dose`,`Hypothyroid in 2-6 months`,`Pregnancy, breastfeeding, active moderate-severe TED`],[`IV methylprednisolone`,`0.5 g weekly x 6, then 0.25 g weekly x 6 (4.5 g)`,`12 weeks; maximum 8 g per course`,`Acute liver injury, hyperglycaemia, hypertension`],[`Mycophenolate sodium`,`0.72 g/day oral (MMF 1 g/day)`,`24 weeks with IVMP`,`Cytopenia, infection, teratogenic`],[`Selenium (selenite)`,`100 mcg twice daily oral`,`6 months`,`Mild TED only; avoid excess`],[`Teprotumumab`,`10 mg/kg IV, then 20 mg/kg every 3 weeks`,`8 infusions (21 weeks)`,`Hyperglycaemia, hearing loss, IBD flare, pregnancy`]]},{heading:`EUGOGO severity and activity of Graves orbitopathy`,columns:[`Grade`,`Features`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Lid retraction under 2 mm, proptosis under 3 mm above normal, mild soft tissue, transient or no diplopia`,`Local measures; selenium 6 months if active and recent`],[`Moderate-to-severe, active (CAS 3 or more)`,`Lid retraction 2 mm or more, proptosis 3 mm or more, inconstant or constant diplopia`,`IVMP 4.5 g plus mycophenolate; teprotumumab alternative`],[`Moderate-to-severe, inactive`,`Same signs, CAS under 3 for 6 months`,`Rehabilitative surgery: decompression, squint, lids`],[`Sight-threatening`,`Optic neuropathy or corneal breakdown`,`IVMP 0.5-1 g for 3 days; decompression within 2 weeks if poor response`]]},{heading:`Choice of definitive treatment`,columns:[`Feature`,`Antithyroid drugs`,`Radioiodine`,`Total thyroidectomy`],rows:[[`Best for`,`Mild disease, small goitre, low TRAb, pregnancy, children`,`Relapse, elderly, cardiac disease, no TED`,`Large goitre, nodule, moderate-severe TED, pregnancy needing cure`],[`Remission or cure`,`About 50% remission`,`Cure in 80-90% after one dose`,`Near 100%`],[`Main risks`,`Agranulocytosis, hepatotoxicity, relapse`,`Hypothyroidism, TED worsening`,`Hypoparathyroidism, RLN palsy`],[`Contraindications`,`Previous severe reaction`,`Pregnancy, breastfeeding, active TED`,`High surgical risk`],[`Levothyroxine after`,`No (unless block-replace)`,`Usually lifelong`,`Always lifelong`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of thyrotoxicosis with goitre`,columns:[`Condition`,`Gland`,`Uptake/TRAb`,`Distinguishing feature`],rows:[[`Graves disease`,`Diffuse, bruit`,`High diffuse; TRAb positive`,`Orbitopathy, dermopathy`],[`Toxic multinodular goitre`,`Multinodular`,`Patchy; TRAb negative`,`Elderly, iodine-deficient area`],[`Toxic adenoma`,`Single nodule`,`Hot nodule; TRAb negative`,`Rest of gland suppressed`],[`Subacute thyroiditis`,`Tender`,`Very low`,`Fever, ESR high, post-viral`],[`Painless or postpartum thyroiditis`,`Small, non-tender`,`Low; TRAb negative`,`Self-limiting triphasic course`],[`Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis`,`No goitre`,`TRAb negative`,`First trimester, hyperemesis`]]},{heading:`Monitoring schedule`,columns:[`Stage`,`Tests`,`Interval`,`Action`],rows:[[`Before starting thionamide`,`CBC with differential, LFT, TFT, TRAb`,`Baseline`,`Document baseline neutrophils and enzymes`],[`Titration`,`FT4 and FT3 (not TSH)`,`Every 4-6 weeks`,`Reduce dose 30-50% as levels fall`],[`Maintenance`,`TSH, FT4`,`Every 2-3 months`,`Keep euthyroid; TRAb before stopping`],[`After stopping`,`TSH, FT4`,`Monthly x 6, then 3-monthly x 1 year, then yearly`,`Detect relapse early`],[`After RAI`,`FT4, T3`,`4-6 weeks then every 4-6 weeks`,`Start LT4 when hypothyroid`],[`IVMP course`,`LFT, glucose, BP`,`Before each infusion`,`Stop if ALT rises over 3-5 times`]]}],redFlags:[`**Fever, sore throat or mouth ulcers on carbimazole or PTU** - stop the drug, same-day CBC; admit if neutrophils below 500.`,`**Reduced visual acuity, colour desaturation or RAPD** - dysthyroid optic neuropathy; same-day ophthalmology and IVMP.`,`**Corneal ulceration or inability to close the eyes** - urgent ophthalmology referral.`,`**Fever, delirium, AF with fast rate or heart failure** in a thyrotoxic patient - thyroid storm; admit to HDU or ICU.`,`**Jaundice, dark urine or abdominal pain on PTU** - stop and check LFT urgently.`,`**Sudden flaccid weakness** with low potassium - thyrotoxic periodic paralysis; admit for cautious potassium and propranolol.`,`**Thyrotoxicosis in pregnancy** with high TRAb - joint obstetric-endocrine care and fetal monitoring.`],pearls:[`**Lid lag = any thyrotoxicosis; proptosis = Graves**.`,`**Carbimazole first, PTU for the 3 Ps**: first trimester of Pregnancy, storm (Peri-crisis), and Prior minor reaction with no alternative.`,`**Fever + sore throat on a thionamide = CBC today** - never switch to the other thionamide after agranulocytosis.`,`**CAS 3 of 7 = active** - immunosuppress only active disease.`,`**EUGOGO 2021: IVMP 4.5 g + mycophenolate** for moderate-to-severe active TED; never exceed 8 g.`,`**Smoker + active TED + RAI = steroid cover**; better still, choose another option.`,`**Order of eye surgery: decompression, squint, lids** - 'out, straight, shut'.`,`**TRAb high at stopping = relapse likely**; use the GREAT score to counsel.`,`**Bruit over the thyroid is specific for Graves**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old woman on carbimazole 30 mg daily for 5 weeks presents with fever of 39 C and a sore throat. Her absolute neutrophil count is 300/microlitre. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Continue carbimazole and start oral antibiotics`,`Stop carbimazole, admit, start broad-spectrum antibiotics and plan definitive therapy`,`Switch to propylthiouracil 100 mg three times daily`,`Halve the carbimazole dose and repeat the count in a week`],answer:1,explanation:`This is thionamide-induced agranulocytosis (neutrophils below 500), which occurs mostly in the first 3 months. The drug must be stopped permanently, the febrile neutropenic patient admitted for broad-spectrum antibiotics (G-CSF may help), and radioiodine or surgery planned. Switching to PTU is wrong because cross-reactivity is common, and continuing or halving the dose risks fatal sepsis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-q2`,stem:`According to EUGOGO 2021, what is the first-line treatment for active moderate-to-severe Graves orbitopathy?`,options:[`Selenium 100 mcg twice daily for 6 months`,`Urgent orbital decompression`,`Oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg for 3 months`,`IV methylprednisolone 4.5 g over 12 weeks with mycophenolate`],answer:3,explanation:`EUGOGO 2021 recommends weekly IV methylprednisolone (0.5 g x 6, then 0.25 g x 6, total 4.5 g) combined with mycophenolate sodium for 24 weeks. IV steroids are more effective and better tolerated than oral prednisolone. Selenium is only for mild disease, and decompression is for sight-threatening optic neuropathy failing IV steroids or for inactive rehabilitative surgery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-q3`,stem:`Which of these is a contraindication to radioiodine therapy in Graves disease?`,options:[`Active moderate-to-severe orbitopathy`,`Age over 60 years`,`Atrial fibrillation`,`Relapse after a course of carbimazole`],answer:0,explanation:`Radioiodine can worsen active orbitopathy in about 15% of patients, so active moderate-to-severe or sight-threatening eye disease is a contraindication; surgery or drugs are preferred. Older age, AF and relapse after antithyroid drugs are, by contrast, indications that favour radioiodine as definitive treatment. Pregnancy and breastfeeding are the other absolute contraindications.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-q4`,stem:`A patient with Graves orbitopathy has spontaneous retrobulbar ache, eyelid redness, conjunctival redness and chemosis but no pain on eye movement, eyelid swelling or caruncle swelling. What is the Clinical Activity Score and its meaning?`,options:[`2 of 7 - inactive`,`3 of 7 - borderline, observe`,`4 of 7 - active disease`,`7 of 10 - sight-threatening`],answer:2,explanation:`Each of the seven initial CAS items scores one point: retrobulbar pain, pain on eye movement, lid redness, conjunctival redness, lid swelling, chemosis and caruncle swelling. Four items are present, giving 4 of 7, and a score of 3 or more indicates active disease that may respond to immunosuppression. The 10-point version is for follow-up only, and CAS measures activity, not sight-threat, which depends on optic neuropathy or corneal breakdown.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-q5`,stem:`Which adverse effect is specifically associated with teprotumumab therapy for thyroid eye disease?`,options:[`Fulminant hepatic necrosis`,`Hearing impairment`,`Agranulocytosis`,`Hypokalaemic paralysis`],answer:1,explanation:`Teprotumumab, an IGF-1 receptor antibody, causes hyperglycaemia, muscle cramps, inflammatory bowel disease flares and hearing impairment, which led to an FDA label warning in 2023 and advice for audiometry. Fulminant hepatic necrosis is the PTU boxed warning, agranulocytosis is a thionamide reaction, and hypokalaemic paralysis is a feature of thyrotoxicosis itself.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, investigations and management of Graves disease. Add a note on the adverse effects of antithyroid drugs.`,openingLines:[`Graves disease is an autoimmune disorder in which stimulating IgG antibodies against the TSH receptor (TRAb/TSI) cause diffuse goitre and hyperthyroidism, often with orbitopathy and dermopathy.`,`It causes 60-80% of hyperthyroidism, affects women 5-10 times more than men, and is treated with thionamides, radioiodine or total thyroidectomy.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis`,points:[`Genetic (HLA-DR3, CTLA-4, PTPN22) plus environment (smoking, stress, iodine, postpartum, alemtuzumab).`,`TRAb stimulate TSHR - hormone excess and diffuse goitre; orbital fibroblasts (TSHR/IGF-1R) - GAG and fat expansion.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Weight loss with hyperphagia, heat intolerance, palpitation, tremor, diarrhoea, menstrual change, proximal myopathy.`,`Diffuse goitre with bruit, AF, lid signs, proptosis, chemosis, pretibial myxoedema, acropachy.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Suppressed TSH, high FT4/FT3; TRAb positive; diffuse high uptake when needed.`,`Baseline CBC and LFT; ECG; ultrasound if nodule; orbit MRI/CT if atypical or DON.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Propranolol for symptoms.`,`Carbimazole 20-40 mg/day titrated, 12-18 months; block-replace rarely; long-term low dose an option.`,`RAI 10-15 mCi (not in pregnancy or active TED; steroid cover in smokers); total thyroidectomy after Lugol iodine.`,`Pregnancy: PTU first trimester; TRAb monitoring.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects of thionamides`,points:[`Minor: rash, arthralgia; major: agranulocytosis 0.2-0.5% (stop, CBC, never switch), PTU hepatic necrosis, methimazole cholestasis, PTU ANCA vasculitis, embryopathy.`]},{heading:`Orbitopathy in brief`,points:[`Stop smoking, euthyroidism, selenium for mild; IVMP plus mycophenolate for active moderate-severe; decompression for DON.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of TRAb stimulating the TSH receptor and orbital fibroblast`,`Management flow chart: drugs vs RAI vs surgery`,`Table of thionamide adverse effects`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiopathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Antithyroid drug adverse effects`,marks:2}],keywords:[`TRAb`,`carbimazole`,`agranulocytosis`,`radioiodine`,`thyroidectomy`,`orbitopathy`,`PTU`]},{id:`endocrine-graves-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the assessment and management of Graves orbitopathy.`,openingLines:[`Graves orbitopathy is autoimmune inflammation of orbital fat and extraocular muscles driven by TSHR and IGF-1R signalling on orbital fibroblasts, clinically evident in 25-40% of Graves patients.`,`Management depends on two assessments: activity (Clinical Activity Score) and severity (EUGOGO grading).`],answer:[{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`CAS 7 items; 3 or more = active.`,`Severity: mild, moderate-to-severe, sight-threatening (DON, corneal breakdown).`,`Acuity, colour vision, pupils, Hertel exophthalmometry, eye movements; MRI/CT if atypical.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`All: stop smoking, euthyroidism, lubricants, prisms.`,`Mild: selenium 100 mcg twice daily for 6 months.`,`Moderate-severe active: IVMP 4.5 g over 12 weeks plus mycophenolate; teprotumumab, radiotherapy, rituximab as alternatives.`,`DON: IVMP 0.5-1 g for 3 days; decompression if poor response in 2 weeks.`,`Inactive: decompression, squint, lid surgery in that order.`]}],mustDraw:[`Rundle curve of activity over time`,`EUGOGO management ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis and assessment (CAS, severity)`,marks:2},{part:`Management by grade`,marks:3}],keywords:[`CAS`,`EUGOGO 2021`,`IVMP`,`mycophenolate`,`teprotumumab`,`selenium`,`DON`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Hyperthyroidism`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis`,`2018 European Thyroid Association Guideline for the Management of Graves' Hyperthyroidism`,`The 2021 European Group on Graves' Orbitopathy (EUGOGO) Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Medical Management of Graves' Orbitopathy`,`Management of Thyroid Eye Disease: A Consensus Statement by the American Thyroid Association and the European Thyroid Association, 2022`,`2017 ATA Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Thyroid Disease During Pregnancy and the Postpartum`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), India, 2022`]}),r.push({id:`endocrine-thyroiditis`,title:`Thyroiditis: subacute, painless, postpartum, Hashimoto and drug-induced`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Thyroiditis** is inflammation of the thyroid that either **destroys follicles** - releasing stored hormone to give **low-uptake thyrotoxicosis** followed by a hypothyroid phase and usually recovery (**subacute, painless, postpartum, drug-induced**) - or causes **chronic autoimmune hypothyroidism (Hashimoto)**; destructive thyrotoxicosis is treated with **beta-blockers (and NSAIDs or prednisolone if painful), never antithyroid drugs**.`,keywords:[`de Quervain thyroiditis`,`subacute granulomatous thyroiditis`,`painless thyroiditis`,`silent thyroiditis`,`postpartum thyroiditis`,`Hashimoto thyroiditis`,`chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis`,`Hashitoxicosis`,`Riedel thyroiditis`,`IgG4 thyroiditis`,`acute suppurative thyroiditis`,`amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis`,`AIT type 1`,`AIT type 2`,`lithium`,`immune checkpoint inhibitor thyroiditis`,`triphasic course`,`prednisolone`,`ESR`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Thyroiditis** is a group of inflammatory thyroid disorders classified by **pain, tempo and cause**; destructive forms give a **triphasic course** - thyrotoxic (weeks), hypothyroid (weeks to months), recovery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hashimoto (chronic autoimmune) thyroiditis** is the commonest cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas, including post-iodisation India, where **anti-TPO positivity is about 22%** in adults. [Unnikrishnan, IJEM 2013]`,`**Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis** has an incidence of about **5 per 100,000 per year**, peaks in women aged **40-50**, and is the commonest cause of a **painful thyroid**. [Williams 15e]`,`**Postpartum thyroiditis** affects about **5-8%** of women in the year after delivery (higher in anti-TPO-positive women and type 1 diabetes); Indian hospital studies report similar rates. [ATA 2017]`,`**Painless (silent) sporadic thyroiditis** causes about **1-5%** of thyrotoxicosis; it is the non-pregnant counterpart of postpartum thyroiditis. [ATA 2016]`,`**Drug-induced thyroiditis** is rising with **immune checkpoint inhibitors** (thyroid dysfunction in about **10-20%** on anti-PD-1 and higher with combination therapy), amiodarone and lithium. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Subacute granulomatous**: post-viral (mumps, coxsackie, adenovirus, influenza, **SARS-CoV-2** and rarely after COVID-19 vaccines), with **HLA-B35** susceptibility; often 2-8 weeks after an upper respiratory infection. [Williams 15e]`,`**Painless and postpartum**: autoimmune lymphocytic infiltration; risk factors are **positive anti-TPO** (30-50% of TPO-positive pregnant women develop PPT), type 1 diabetes, previous PPT and family history. [ATA 2017]`,`**Hashimoto**: genetic (HLA-DR3, DR5, CTLA-4), female sex, iodine excess, selenium deficiency, smoking cessation, other autoimmunity and **Down and Turner syndromes**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone** contains **37% iodine by weight** (a 200 mg tablet carries about 75 mg iodine), and is also directly cytotoxic to follicular cells. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Lithium** is concentrated in the thyroid and inhibits hormone release; it causes goitre and hypothyroidism and, rarely, painless thyroiditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitors** (pembrolizumab, nivolumab, atezolizumab, ipilimumab) break tolerance; **anti-PD-1/PD-L1** mainly cause thyroiditis, **anti-CTLA-4** mainly hypophysitis. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Others**: interferon-alpha, IL-2, **alemtuzumab** (Graves or thyroiditis), tyrosine kinase inhibitors (sunitinib), radiation, palpation or trauma, and **acute suppurative** infection via a **left pyriform sinus fistula**. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: the triphasic course`,points:[`Inflammation **disrupts follicles**, releasing preformed T4, T3 and **thyroglobulin** into the blood; synthesis is not increased, so **radioiodine uptake is very low (below 5%)**. [ATA 2016]`,`Because only the stored pool is released, the thyrotoxic phase lasts **about 3-6 weeks** and is self-limiting; **antithyroid drugs do nothing** since new synthesis is already shut off. [ATA 2016]`,`Released hormone suppresses TSH; once stores are exhausted and follicles are damaged, a **hypothyroid phase** follows for weeks to months, then most patients **recover** as follicles regenerate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subacute thyroiditis** shows **granulomas with multinucleated giant cells** and is driven by a cytotoxic T-cell response to viral antigen - hence pain, fever and a **high ESR and CRP**. [Williams 15e]`,`**Hashimoto**: T-cell mediated destruction with **lymphoid follicles, germinal centres and Hurthle (oncocytic) cells**, with antibodies to TPO and thyroglobulin; apoptosis via Fas and cytotoxic T cells causes gradual failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hashitoxicosis**: an early transient thyrotoxic phase from destruction in Hashimoto, sometimes with coexisting TRAb. [Williams 15e]`,`**Amiodarone**: type 1 AIT is **iodine-induced excess synthesis** in an abnormal gland (Jod-Basedow); type 2 AIT is **drug-induced destructive thyroiditis** in a normal gland; hypothyroidism (AIH) results from a failure to escape the **Wolff-Chaikoff effect**. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Riedel thyroiditis** is part of **IgG4-related disease** in many cases: dense fibrosis extending beyond the capsule into neck structures. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Painful**: subacute granulomatous (de Quervain), **acute suppurative**, radiation-induced, traumatic or palpation thyroiditis. [Williams 15e]`,`**Painless destructive**: silent sporadic, **postpartum**, drug-induced (amiodarone type 2, lithium, checkpoint inhibitors, interferon, TKIs). [ATA 2016]`,`**Chronic**: **Hashimoto** (goitrous) and atrophic thyroiditis, fibrous variant, **IgG4-related thyroiditis**, **Riedel** (invasive fibrous). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis**: **type 1** (synthesis, abnormal gland), **type 2** (destructive, normal gland), **mixed**. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor thyroid irAE grading** (CTCAE): grade 1 asymptomatic, grade 2 symptomatic but able to do daily activities, grade 3-4 severe or life-threatening. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history`,points:[`**Subacute thyroiditis**: anterior neck pain radiating to the **jaw or ear**, worse on swallowing, with fever, malaise, myalgia and a preceding upper respiratory infection; then thyrotoxic symptoms. [Williams 15e]`,`**Painless and postpartum**: mild thyrotoxic symptoms (palpitation, irritability, fatigue) often **dismissed as postpartum stress**; the hypothyroid phase brings fatigue, low mood and impaired concentration. [ATA 2017]`,`**Postpartum timing**: thyrotoxic phase about **1-4 months** after delivery, hypothyroid phase about **3-8 months**; it may follow miscarriage too. [ATA 2017]`,`**Hashimoto**: slow onset of hypothyroid symptoms, a painless firm goitre, or detection on routine TSH; rapid painful enlargement suggests **lymphoma or haemorrhage**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone thyrotoxicosis**: may present only as **recurrence of AF or VT, unexplained weight loss or worsening heart failure**, sometimes years after starting or months after stopping. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor thyroiditis**: typically **4-12 weeks** after starting therapy - transient thyrotoxicosis then hypothyroidism, often asymptomatic and found on routine testing. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Acute suppurative**: high fever, very tender unilateral swelling, dysphagia, often a child with a recurrent **left-sided** neck abscess. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Subacute**: exquisitely **tender, firm, nodular goitre**, often starting on one side and 'creeping' to the other; fever and tachycardia. [Williams 15e]`,`**Painless and postpartum**: small, firm, **non-tender** diffuse goitre or a normal gland; no eye signs and no bruit. [ATA 2016]`,`**Hashimoto**: firm, rubbery, **bosselated** symmetrical goitre with a palpable pyramidal lobe, or an impalpable gland in atrophic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Riedel**: **stony-hard, fixed** goitre with compressive symptoms, sometimes with retroperitoneal fibrosis or sclerosing cholangitis; mimics anaplastic carcinoma. [Williams 15e]`,`**Acute suppurative**: hot, red, fluctuant, unilateral swelling with cervical nodes. [Williams 15e]`,`**Absence of orbitopathy and bruit** helps separate destructive thyroiditis from Graves at the bedside. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Graves disease**: bruit, eye signs, **TRAb positive, high uptake**, T3:T4 ratio above 20, does not resolve by itself. [ATA 2016]`,`**Haemorrhage into a nodule**: sudden painful swelling, normal ESR, cystic nodule on ultrasound. [Williams 15e]`,`**Anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma**: older patient, rapidly enlarging **hard** mass with stridor; lymphoma typically on a background of Hashimoto. [ATA 2025 DTC]`,`**Pharyngitis, dental or ear disease**: pain referred to the jaw and ear can mislead - palpate the thyroid in every 'sore throat with fever and tachycardia'. [Williams 15e]`,`**Postpartum Graves**: onset often later (after 6 months), larger goitre, eye signs, **TRAb positive**, persistent thyrotoxicosis. [ATA 2017]`,`**Postpartum depression**: may coexist with the hypothyroid phase - check TSH in every woman with postpartum depression. [ATA 2017]`,`**Factitious thyrotoxicosis**: low uptake but **low thyroglobulin** and no goitre. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**TFT**: low TSH with high FT4 in the thyrotoxic phase; **T3:T4 ratio below 20** (relatively more T4 released than in Graves). [ATA 2016]`,`**ESR**: markedly raised in subacute thyroiditis (**often above 50 mm/h**, frequently above 100) with high CRP and mild leucocytosis; **normal** in painless and postpartum thyroiditis. [Williams 15e]`,`**RAIU**: very low (**below 5%**) in all destructive forms; the scan is contraindicated in pregnancy and requires interruption of breastfeeding. [ATA 2017]`,`**TRAb**: negative in thyroiditis - the easiest way to exclude Graves in lactating women; **anti-TPO** is usually positive in painless, postpartum and Hashimoto thyroiditis. [ATA 2017]`,`**Ultrasound**: subacute - poorly defined **hypoechoic areas** with reduced vascularity; Hashimoto - **heterogeneous, pseudonodular hypoechoic** gland; Doppler low flow separates AIT2 from AIT1. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Thyroglobulin** is high in destructive thyroiditis and low in factitious intake. [ATA 2016]`,`**FNAC**: only for a discrete nodule, suspected lymphoma or Riedel (core biopsy and IgG4 staining); giant cells confirm subacute thyroiditis but are rarely needed. [Williams 15e]`,`**Acute suppurative**: ultrasound or CT, aspiration for culture, and **barium swallow or CT** after recovery to find a pyriform sinus fistula. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**Beta-blocker** for the thyrotoxic phase: **propranolol 10-40 mg three to four times daily** or atenolol 25-50 mg once daily; continue until FT4 normalises. [ATA 2016]`,`**Antithyroid drugs are not used** in destructive thyroiditis - there is no excess synthesis. [ATA 2016]`,`**Subacute, mild pain**: **NSAIDs** (ibuprofen 400-600 mg three times daily or naproxen 250-500 mg twice daily) for 1-2 weeks. [ATA 2016]`,`**Subacute, severe pain or NSAID failure**: **prednisolone 40 mg daily for 1-2 weeks, then taper over 2-4 weeks** or longer; pain resolves within 24-48 hours - failure to respond should prompt rethinking the diagnosis. [ATA 2016]`,`**Relapse** after steroid taper occurs in about **10-20%**; restart the previous dose and taper more slowly. [Williams 15e]`,`**Hypothyroid phase**: levothyroxine only if symptomatic, **TSH above 10**, or in women breastfeeding or planning pregnancy; try withdrawal after **6-12 months**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Hashimoto with hypothyroidism**: levothyroxine **1.6 mcg/kg/day** (lower in elderly and cardiac disease); details in the Hypothyroidism topic. [ATA 2014]`,`**Selenium** for Hashimoto lowers anti-TPO titres but has **no proven clinical benefit** outside pregnancy research - not routinely recommended. [ETA 2013]`]},{heading:`Drug-induced thyroiditis: amiodarone, lithium and checkpoint inhibitors`,points:[`**AIT type 1**: underlying nodular goitre or latent Graves, **normal or high uptake** (may be low in iodine-sufficient areas), **increased Doppler flow**; treat with **high-dose methimazole 40-60 mg/day** (potassium perchlorate where available). [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**AIT type 2**: normal gland, **very low uptake, absent vascularity**; treat with **prednisolone about 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day (typically 30-40 mg) for about 2 weeks, tapered over about 3 months**. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Mixed or unclear AIT**: combine methimazole and prednisolone from the start; **thyroidectomy** for refractory disease or rapidly worsening cardiac function. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`Whether to **stop amiodarone** is a joint decision with the cardiologist; in AIT2 it may be continued if essential; its long half-life (about **50-100 days**) means effects persist for months. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Amiodarone-induced hypothyroidism**: continue amiodarone and **add levothyroxine**; more common in iodine-sufficient areas and anti-TPO-positive patients. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Lithium**: hypothyroidism in up to about **20%** and goitre in up to 40%; **continue lithium and add levothyroxine**; monitor TSH every 6 months. [NICE CG185]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitors**: grade 1-2 - **continue immunotherapy**, beta-blocker for thyrotoxicosis, levothyroxine when hypothyroid (about two-thirds become permanently hypothyroid); grade 3-4 - hold until recovery; **steroids are not needed** for thyroiditis. [ASCO 2021]`,`In checkpoint-inhibitor patients with **low TSH and low FT4**, suspect **hypophysitis** - give hydrocortisone before levothyroxine. Full detail is in the Drug-induced thyroid dysfunction topic. [ESMO 2022]`]},{heading:`IPD and emergency care`,points:[`Admit **acute suppurative thyroiditis**: IV antibiotics against Staphylococcus, Streptococcus and anaerobes (for example **amoxicillin-clavulanate or clindamycin**), drainage of abscess, later fistula excision. [Williams 15e]`,`Admit **AIT with arrhythmia or heart failure** - high mortality in elderly patients with poor LV function; early thyroidectomy is considered when medical therapy fails. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Compressive Riedel or IgG4 thyroiditis**: glucocorticoids, tamoxifen or rituximab; surgery (isthmusectomy) to relieve tracheal compression. [Williams 15e]`,`**Severe subacute thyroiditis** with high fever and tachycardia rarely mimics storm; treat with prednisolone and beta-blockers, not thionamides. [ATA 2016]`,`**Rapidly enlarging painful goitre in Hashimoto** with stridor - urgent ultrasound and core biopsy for **primary thyroid lymphoma**. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Postpartum**: **propranolol** is preferred in breastfeeding (lowest effective dose); RAIU contraindicated while breastfeeding; use **TRAb** to exclude Graves. [ATA 2017]`,`**Postpartum thyroiditis follow-up**: TSH every **4-8 weeks** in the hypothyroid phase, then **yearly TSH** because up to about **50% develop permanent hypothyroidism** within 5-10 years. [ATA 2017]`,`**Recurrence** of PPT in a subsequent pregnancy is about **70%** - check TSH postpartum in every later pregnancy; full detail is in the Thyroid disease in pregnancy topic. [ATA 2017]`,`**Elderly and cardiac**: even short thyrotoxic phases can trigger AF; keep beta-blockade and consider anticoagulation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: prednisolone for subacute thyroiditis worsens glycaemia - monitor glucose and adjust; type 1 diabetes triples the risk of PPT. [ATA 2017]`,`**Children**: recurrent left neck abscess suggests **pyriform sinus fistula**; Hashimoto is the commonest cause of goitre and acquired hypothyroidism in older children. [Williams 15e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Subacute**: permanent hypothyroidism in about **5-15%**; recurrence after full recovery in about **2-4%**. [Williams 15e]`,`**Painless and postpartum**: recovery is usual, but permanent hypothyroidism develops in up to **50%** over 5-10 years, especially with high anti-TPO. [ATA 2017]`,`**Hashimoto**: progression from subclinical to overt hypothyroidism about **4% per year** with positive antibodies; lifetime risk of **primary thyroid lymphoma** (MALT or diffuse large B-cell) is increased many-fold though absolute risk is small. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hashimoto encephalopathy (SREAT)**: rare steroid-responsive encephalopathy with high anti-TPO, seizures and confusion. [Williams 15e]`,`**AIT**: mortality is higher than other thyrotoxicosis because of underlying heart disease. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Checkpoint-inhibitor thyroiditis** may predict better tumour response in some studies; it rarely requires stopping cancer therapy. [ESMO 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Refer**: uncertain cause of thyrotoxicosis, AIT, suspected lymphoma or Riedel, acute suppurative thyroiditis, and steroid-dependent subacute thyroiditis. [ATA 2016]`,`**Monitor TFT every 2-4 weeks** in the thyrotoxic phase, then every 4-8 weeks until recovery. [ATA 2016]`,`**Yearly TSH lifelong** after subacute, painless or postpartum thyroiditis, and in anti-TPO-positive euthyroid people every 1-2 years. [ATA 2017]`,`**Counsel postpartum women**: symptoms may be mistaken for new-baby fatigue; check TSH before the next pregnancy; antibodies mean higher risk. [ATA 2017]`,`**Amiodarone and lithium users**: baseline TFT, then TSH every 3-6 months (amiodarone) or 6 months (lithium), and up to a year after stopping amiodarone. [ETA 2018 amiodarone; NICE CG185]`,`**Iodised salt** under the National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme is still recommended; avoid **excess iodine supplements** in people with autoimmune thyroiditis. [NIDDCP MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Checkpoint inhibitor irAE guidance** (ASCO 2021, ESMO 2022, and NCCN updates through 2025) - continue immunotherapy for grade 1-2 thyroiditis, test TSH and FT4 every 4-6 weeks, and screen cortisol when FT4 is low with low TSH. [ESMO 2022; ASCO 2021]`,`**SARS-CoV-2**: post-COVID and post-vaccine subacute thyroiditis are recognised entities (reports 2020-2023); management is unchanged - NSAIDs or prednisolone. [Williams 15e]`,`**ATA 2017 pregnancy guideline** remains the main reference for postpartum thyroiditis - beta-blockers, selective levothyroxine, yearly TSH. [ATA 2017]`,`**ETA 2018 amiodarone guideline** still guides AIT subtyping; recent series favour **early thyroidectomy** in AIT with severe LV dysfunction. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**IgG4-related disease** classification (ACR/EULAR 2019) now underpins the work-up of Riedel and fibrosing thyroiditis. [ACR/EULAR 2019 IgG4-RD]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva**: 'Why don't antithyroid drugs work in thyroiditis?' - destruction, not synthesis; uptake is low. [ATA 2016]`,`**Viva**: compare **subacute vs painless vs Graves** - pain, ESR, uptake, TRAb, course. [ATA 2016]`,`**Viva**: AIT type 1 vs type 2 - gland, uptake, Doppler, treatment. [ETA 2018 amiodarone]`,`**Common mistake**: starting carbimazole for postpartum thyrotoxicosis without TRAb or uptake. [ATA 2017]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping lithium for hypothyroidism - add levothyroxine instead. [NICE CG185]`,`**Classic sign**: pain radiating to the **ear** with a tender goitre and ESR above 50 - de Quervain. [Williams 15e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of thyroiditis compared`,columns:[`Type`,`Pain`,`ESR`,`Uptake`,`Antibodies`,`Course`],rows:[[`Subacute (de Quervain)`,`Severe, radiates to ear`,`High (often over 50)`,`Very low`,`Usually negative`,`Triphasic; 5-15% permanent hypo`],[`Painless (silent)`,`None`,`Normal`,`Very low`,`Anti-TPO often positive`,`Triphasic; recurrences possible`],[`Postpartum`,`None`,`Normal`,`Very low`,`Anti-TPO positive`,`1-4 months toxic, 3-8 months hypo; up to 50% permanent`],[`Hashimoto`,`None`,`Normal`,`Variable`,`Anti-TPO 90%`,`Progressive hypothyroidism`],[`Acute suppurative`,`Severe, unilateral`,`High, leucocytosis`,`Normal`,`Negative`,`Abscess; fistula`],[`Riedel / IgG4`,`None or mild`,`May be high`,`Low or normal`,`Variable; raised IgG4`,`Fibrosis, compression`],[`Checkpoint-inhibitor`,`None`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Anti-TPO in some`,`Toxic 4-12 weeks, then permanent hypo in about two-thirds`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in thyroiditis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg oral three to four times daily`,`Until FT4 normal (2-8 weeks)`,`Asthma, bradycardia, heart failure`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400-600 mg oral three times daily with food`,`1-2 weeks for mild subacute pain`,`Gastritis, renal impairment`],[`Prednisolone (subacute)`,`40 mg oral daily`,`1-2 weeks, taper over 2-4 weeks or longer`,`Hyperglycaemia, relapse on taper`],[`Prednisolone (AIT type 2)`,`0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day oral (about 30-40 mg)`,`About 2 weeks then taper over 3 months`,`Infection, hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis`],[`Methimazole (AIT type 1)`,`40-60 mg/day oral`,`Until euthyroid, then taper`,`Agranulocytosis, hepatotoxicity`],[`Levothyroxine`,`25-100 mcg/day oral (full 1.6 mcg/kg in Hashimoto)`,`Trial off at 6-12 months in transient thyroiditis`,`Over-replacement, AF, bone loss`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`1.2 g IV every 8 hours`,`Until abscess resolves, then oral`,`Allergy; drain abscess`]]},{heading:`Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis: type 1 vs type 2`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1`,`Type 2`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Iodine-induced excess synthesis`,`Destructive thyroiditis`],[`Underlying gland`,`Nodular goitre or latent Graves`,`Normal`],[`Uptake`,`Normal or high (may be low)`,`Very low or absent`],[`Doppler flow`,`Increased`,`Absent or reduced`],[`Onset`,`Earlier, often within the first year`,`Later, often years into therapy`],[`Treatment`,`Methimazole 40-60 mg (plus perchlorate)`,`Prednisolone 30-40 mg tapered over 3 months`],[`Hypothyroidism later`,`Rare`,`Possible`]]},{heading:`Destructive thyroiditis vs Graves disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Destructive thyroiditis`,`Graves disease`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Release of stored hormone`,`TRAb-driven synthesis`],[`Goitre`,`Small, tender (subacute) or non-tender`,`Diffuse with bruit`],[`Eye signs`,`Absent`,`Present in 25-40%`],[`T3:T4 ratio`,`Below 20`,`Above 20`],[`Uptake`,`Below 5%`,`High`],[`TRAb`,`Negative`,`Positive`],[`Course`,`Self-limiting, weeks`,`Persistent`],[`Antithyroid drugs`,`Not useful`,`First line`]]},{heading:`Monitoring after thyroiditis`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`Interval`],rows:[[`Thyrotoxic phase`,`TSH, FT4`,`Every 2-4 weeks`],[`Hypothyroid phase`,`TSH, FT4`,`Every 4-8 weeks until recovery`],[`After recovery (subacute, painless, postpartum)`,`TSH`,`Yearly lifelong`],[`Amiodarone`,`TSH, FT4`,`Baseline, then 3-6 monthly, and 1 year after stopping`],[`Lithium`,`TSH`,`Baseline, then 6-monthly`],[`Checkpoint inhibitors`,`TSH, FT4`,`Every 4-6 weeks during therapy`]]}],redFlags:[`**Rapidly enlarging hard goitre with stridor or dysphagia** - suspect anaplastic carcinoma, lymphoma or Riedel; urgent referral.`,`**Hot, fluctuant, unilateral neck swelling with high fever** - acute suppurative thyroiditis; admit for IV antibiotics and drainage.`,`**Thyrotoxicosis on amiodarone with worsening heart failure or VT** - admit; AIT carries high mortality.`,`**Low TSH with low FT4 on a checkpoint inhibitor** - possible hypophysitis; check cortisol, give hydrocortisone first.`,`**Fever, delirium and AF** in a thyroiditis patient - consider storm or sepsis; admit.`,`**Postpartum woman with severe depression or suicidal thoughts** and abnormal TFT - same-day psychiatric and medical review.`],pearls:[`**Thyroiditis = destruction, not production** - low uptake, no role for carbimazole.`,`**De Quervain: pain to the ear, ESR above 50, uptake below 5%, dramatic steroid response**.`,`**Failure of pain to settle within 48-72 hours of prednisolone - rethink the diagnosis**.`,`**Postpartum thyroiditis: toxic at 1-4 months, hypo at 3-8 months**; yearly TSH after.`,`**Use TRAb, not uptake, to exclude Graves in a breastfeeding mother**.`,`**AIT 1 = iodine + abnormal gland + flow; AIT 2 = destruction + normal gland + no flow**.`,`**Lithium hypothyroidism - add levothyroxine, keep lithium**.`,`**Checkpoint thyroiditis - keep the cancer drug; low TSH and low FT4 = think pituitary**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-q1`,stem:`A 42-year-old woman has fever, painful neck swelling radiating to the right ear, a tender goitre, TSH below 0.01 mIU/L, FT4 3.0 ng/dL and ESR 88 mm/h. What is the most appropriate treatment if NSAIDs have failed?`,options:[`Carbimazole 30 mg daily`,`Radioiodine ablation`,`Prednisolone 40 mg daily with a gradual taper`,`Urgent total thyroidectomy`],answer:2,explanation:`Painful tender goitre, high ESR and thyrotoxicosis after a viral illness is subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis. When NSAIDs fail, prednisolone 40 mg daily for 1-2 weeks tapered over 2-4 weeks relieves pain within days. Carbimazole is useless because the thyrotoxicosis comes from destruction, not synthesis; radioiodine would not be taken up, and surgery is unnecessary for a self-limiting condition.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-q2`,stem:`A woman 3 months after delivery, who is breastfeeding, has palpitations, a small non-tender goitre, low TSH and raised FT4. What is the most useful test to separate postpartum thyroiditis from Graves disease?`,options:[`Radioiodine uptake scan`,`TSH-receptor antibody (TRAb)`,`ESR`,`Anti-TPO antibody`],answer:1,explanation:`TRAb is positive in Graves and negative in postpartum thyroiditis, and it is safe during breastfeeding. An uptake scan would also discriminate but needs interruption of breastfeeding. Anti-TPO is positive in both postpartum thyroiditis and many Graves patients, so it does not separate them, and ESR is normal in both painless postpartum thyroiditis and Graves.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old man on amiodarone for 3 years develops thyrotoxicosis. He has no goitre, Doppler shows absent vascularity and 24-hour uptake is 1%. What is the treatment of choice?`,options:[`Oral prednisolone tapered over about 3 months`,`High-dose methimazole alone`,`Radioiodine therapy`,`Levothyroxine`],answer:0,explanation:`A normal gland with absent Doppler flow and very low uptake after years of amiodarone is type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis, a destructive thyroiditis that responds to glucocorticoids (prednisolone 30-40 mg tapered over about 3 months). Methimazole alone is the treatment of type 1 (excess synthesis); radioiodine is not taken up in either type, and levothyroxine would worsen thyrotoxicosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-q4`,stem:`A patient with bipolar disorder well controlled on lithium is found to have TSH 12 mIU/L and low FT4. What is the best management?`,options:[`Stop lithium and switch to valproate`,`Start carbimazole`,`Observe and repeat TSH in a year`,`Continue lithium and start levothyroxine`],answer:3,explanation:`Lithium-induced hypothyroidism is common and is treated by adding levothyroxine while continuing lithium, which is often the most effective mood stabiliser for the patient. Stopping lithium risks relapse of bipolar disorder, carbimazole would worsen hypothyroidism, and an overt hypothyroid state with low FT4 should not simply be observed.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-q5`,stem:`A patient on pembrolizumab has TSH 0.05 mIU/L and FT4 2.4 ng/dL six weeks after starting, with mild palpitations. What is the recommended management?`,options:[`Stop pembrolizumab permanently and start prednisolone 1 mg/kg`,`Start carbimazole 30 mg daily`,`Continue pembrolizumab, give a beta-blocker and monitor for the hypothyroid phase`,`Give radioiodine`],answer:2,explanation:`Checkpoint-inhibitor thyroiditis is a painless destructive thyroiditis; grade 1-2 disease is managed by continuing immunotherapy, a beta-blocker for symptoms and TSH/FT4 every 4-6 weeks, starting levothyroxine when hypothyroidism follows (permanent in about two-thirds). Steroids are not needed and stopping the cancer drug is unwarranted; carbimazole and radioiodine treat overproduction, which is absent here.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify thyroiditis. Discuss the clinical features, investigations and management of subacute thyroiditis and postpartum thyroiditis.`,openingLines:[`Thyroiditis is inflammation of the thyroid gland; destructive forms release stored hormone, producing low-uptake thyrotoxicosis followed by a hypothyroid phase and usually recovery.`,`It is classified as painful (subacute, suppurative, radiation), painless (silent, postpartum, drug-induced) and chronic (Hashimoto, Riedel, IgG4).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Painful: subacute granulomatous, acute suppurative, radiation, trauma.`,`Painless: silent, postpartum, amiodarone type 2, lithium, checkpoint inhibitors, interferon.`,`Chronic: Hashimoto, atrophic, Riedel/IgG4.`]},{heading:`Subacute thyroiditis - features`,points:[`Post-viral, HLA-B35, women 40-50; neck pain to the ear, fever, tender goitre.`,`Triphasic course; giant-cell granulomas.`]},{heading:`Subacute thyroiditis - investigations and treatment`,points:[`Low TSH, high FT4, ESR above 50, uptake below 5%, TRAb negative, hypoechoic areas on ultrasound.`,`Propranolol; NSAIDs; prednisolone 40 mg 1-2 weeks then taper; no antithyroid drugs.`]},{heading:`Postpartum thyroiditis - features`,points:[`Within 1 year of delivery, 5-8%; anti-TPO positive, type 1 diabetes.`,`Thyrotoxic at 1-4 months, hypothyroid at 3-8 months; overlap with postpartum depression.`]},{heading:`Postpartum thyroiditis - investigations and treatment`,points:[`Low TSH, TRAb negative, anti-TPO positive; uptake low (avoid while breastfeeding).`,`Propranolol for symptoms; levothyroxine if symptomatic, TSH above 10, breastfeeding or planning pregnancy; withdraw at 6-12 months.`,`Yearly TSH: up to 50% permanent hypothyroidism; 70% recurrence in later pregnancy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of the triphasic course (FT4 and TSH against months)`,`Comparison table: subacute vs painless vs Graves`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Subacute thyroiditis`,marks:4},{part:`Postpartum thyroiditis`,marks:4}],keywords:[`triphasic`,`ESR`,`uptake`,`prednisolone`,`TRAb`,`anti-TPO`,`postpartum`]},{id:`endocrine-thyroiditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate type 1 and type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis.`,openingLines:[`Amiodarone contains 37% iodine by weight and causes thyrotoxicosis by two mechanisms: iodine-induced excess synthesis in an abnormal gland (type 1) and destructive thyroiditis in a normal gland (type 2).`,`The distinction decides treatment: thionamides for type 1, glucocorticoids for type 2.`],answer:[{heading:`Differences`,points:[`Gland: nodular or latent Graves (type 1) vs normal (type 2).`,`Uptake: normal or high vs very low; Doppler: increased vs absent flow.`,`Onset: early vs late; treatment: methimazole 40-60 mg (perchlorate) vs prednisolone 30-40 mg tapered over 3 months.`]},{heading:`Common points`,points:[`Mixed forms need both drugs; thyroidectomy for refractory disease or cardiac decompensation.`,`Amiodarone decision with cardiologist; long half-life.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table of type 1 vs type 2 AIT`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and gland`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations (uptake, Doppler)`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`amiodarone`,`Jod-Basedow`,`Doppler`,`perchlorate`,`prednisolone`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Thyroiditis`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis`,`2017 ATA Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Thyroid Disease During Pregnancy and the Postpartum`,`2018 European Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Management of Amiodarone-Associated Thyroid Dysfunction`,`ASCO Guideline: Management of Immune-Related Adverse Events in Patients Treated With Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy, 2021`,`ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of toxicities from immunotherapy, 2022`,`NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (lithium monitoring), updated 2023`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer`,title:`Thyroid cancer: differentiated, medullary and anaplastic`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`Thyroid cancer is a malignancy of the **follicular cells** (differentiated **papillary** and **follicular** carcinoma, poorly differentiated and **anaplastic** carcinoma) or of the **parafollicular C cells** (**medullary carcinoma**); it is diagnosed by **ultrasound-guided FNAC reported in the Bethesda system**, treated by **surgery tailored to risk**, with **radioiodine and TSH suppression** reserved for follicular-cell cancers at higher risk of recurrence and followed up with **thyroglobulin (DTC) or calcitonin/CEA (MTC)**.`,keywords:[`papillary thyroid carcinoma`,`PTC`,`follicular thyroid carcinoma`,`FTC`,`medullary thyroid carcinoma`,`MTC`,`anaplastic thyroid carcinoma`,`ATC`,`Bethesda 2023`,`radioactive iodine I-131`,`thyroglobulin`,`calcitonin`,`RET proto-oncogene`,`BRAF V600E`,`TSH suppression`,`lenvatinib`,`selpercatinib`,`NIFTP`,`oncocytic (Hurthle cell) carcinoma`,`papillary microcarcinoma active surveillance`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Thyroid cancer** is the commonest endocrine malignancy: about **8.2 lakh new cases and 47,000 deaths worldwide in 2022**, ranking about 7th among all cancers, with women affected about **3 times** as often as men. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`Histological share: **papillary 80-85%**, follicular about 10%, oncocytic 3-5%, **medullary 1-2%**, and **anaplastic under 2%** - yet anaplastic causes a large share of thyroid cancer deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`Incidence has risen several-fold over three decades largely through **overdiagnosis of small papillary cancers** by ultrasound; in South Korea screening raised incidence about 15-fold with **no change in mortality**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, the **National Cancer Registry Programme** shows thyroid cancer among the **leading cancers in women**, with the highest rates in **Thiruvananthapuram and other Kerala registries** and in parts of the North-East. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`Peak age: papillary cancer in **20-50 years**, follicular a decade later, anaplastic in the **seventh decade**; medullary is sporadic in about 75% and hereditary (MEN2 or familial MTC) in about 25%. [Harrison 22e]`,`Outlook is excellent for most: **10-year survival above 95% for papillary**, about 85-90% for follicular, about 75% for medullary overall, while median survival for anaplastic was historically **about 5-6 months**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Ionising radiation to the neck in childhood** is the strongest environmental cause (head-neck radiotherapy, Chernobyl and Fukushima-type exposure); risk is highest when exposed before 5 years and persists for decades. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iodine status** shapes histology: iodine-sufficient populations have more **papillary**, iodine-deficient populations more **follicular and anaplastic** cancer - so salt iodisation shifted the Indian mix toward papillary. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Family history**: a first-degree relative with non-medullary thyroid cancer raises risk 3-8 fold; syndromes include **FAP** (cribriform-morular variant), **Cowden/PTEN** (follicular), **Carney complex** and **DICER1**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Germline RET mutation** causes hereditary MTC: **MEN2A** (MTC, phaeochromocytoma, hyperparathyroidism) and **MEN2B** (MTC, phaeo, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus) - see the MEN syndromes topic. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`**Long-standing goitre** is the usual soil for anaplastic transformation; **Hashimoto thyroiditis** is the main risk factor for **primary thyroid lymphoma** (about 60-fold risk). [Harrison 22e]`,`Obesity and female sex hormones are weak associations; a nodule in a **man, a child or a patient over 70** carries a higher chance of malignancy than one in a middle-aged woman. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and molecular basis`,points:[`Follicular-cell cancers are driven by the **MAPK pathway**: **BRAF V600E** in about 60% of papillary (classic and tall-cell, more aggressive), **RAS** in follicular and follicular-variant tumours, and **RET/PTC and NTRK fusions** in radiation-induced and childhood papillary cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TERT promoter mutation** added to BRAF or RAS marks aggressive behaviour, RAI resistance and higher mortality; **TP53 loss** characterises anaplastic and poorly differentiated cancer. [WHO 2022]`,`**Papillary cancer** spreads by **lymphatics** to central (level VI) and lateral neck nodes; the diagnosis is nuclear - **Orphan Annie (ground-glass) nuclei, nuclear grooves, pseudo-inclusions** and **psammoma bodies** - so FNAC can confirm it. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Follicular cancer** spreads by **blood** to **bone and lung**; it is defined by **capsular or vascular invasion**, which cytology cannot see - hence FNAC reports only 'follicular neoplasm' and **lobectomy** is needed for the diagnosis. [Robbins 10e]`,`Differentiated cells keep the **sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)**, **TSH receptor** and **thyroglobulin** synthesis - the basis of **radioiodine therapy, TSH suppression and thyroglobulin as a tumour marker**; dedifferentiation loses NIS and causes RAI-refractory disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Medullary cancer** arises from neural-crest **C cells** that secrete **calcitonin and CEA**; they lack NIS and TSH receptors, so **RAI and TSH suppression are useless** in MTC. Stromal amyloid is derived from calcitonin. [Harrison 22e]`,`High calcitonin and other peptides (serotonin, prostaglandins, VIP) cause **secretory diarrhoea and flushing** in advanced MTC; ectopic **ACTH** can cause Cushing syndrome. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**WHO 2022 (5th edition)** groups follicular-cell tumours as benign, **low-risk neoplasms** (NIFTP, uncertain malignant potential tumours, hyalinising trabecular tumour) and malignant: papillary, follicular, invasive encapsulated follicular-variant papillary, **oncocytic carcinoma** (replacing 'Hurthle cell'), high-grade (poorly differentiated and differentiated high-grade) and anaplastic. [WHO 2022]`,`**NIFTP** (non-invasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features), reclassified from cancer in 2016, needs only **lobectomy without RAI or TSH suppression**. [WHO 2022]`,`**AJCC 8th edition** T stage: **T1 2 cm or less** (T1a 1 cm or less), **T2 over 2-4 cm**, **T3a over 4 cm** confined, **T3b gross extension into strap muscles**, **T4a** into subcutaneous tissue, larynx, trachea, oesophagus or recurrent laryngeal nerve, **T4b** prevertebral fascia or encasing carotid/mediastinal vessels. [AJCC 8e]`,`N stage: **N1a** level VI/VII (central, upper mediastinal) and **N1b** lateral (levels I-V) or retropharyngeal nodes. [AJCC 8e]`,`DTC stage uses an **age cut-off of 55 years**: under 55, any T any N M0 is **stage I** and M1 is **stage II**; at 55 or more stage rises with size, nodes and gross extension (stage IVB if M1). [AJCC 8e]`,`**Anaplastic carcinoma is always stage IV**: IVA intrathyroidal, IVB gross extrathyroidal extension or nodes, IVC distant metastases. [AJCC 8e]`,`**ATA risk of recurrence** (used to decide RAI and TSH target): **low** (intrathyroidal, no vascular invasion, 5 or fewer micrometastatic nodes under 0.2 cm) under 5%; **intermediate** (microscopic extension, aggressive histology, vascular invasion, over 5 nodes 0.2-3 cm) 5-20%; **high** (gross extension, incomplete resection, distant metastases, node over 3 cm, FTC with more than 4 foci of vascular invasion) over 20%. [ATA 2015, retained ATA 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most present as an **asymptomatic thyroid nodule** found by the patient, at a check-up or as an **incidentaloma** on carotid Doppler, CT or PET. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspicious history: **rapid growth**, **new hoarseness** (recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion), **dysphagia, stridor**, neck node swelling, childhood neck irradiation, and family history of thyroid cancer or MEN2. [ATA 2025]`,`**Follicular cancer** may first present with a **pathological fracture or a pulsatile bony metastasis** (skull, spine, pelvis), or lung nodules. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**MTC** presents as a nodule with lateral nodes, or with **diarrhoea and flushing** in advanced disease; ask about **headache-palpitation-sweating** (phaeo) and **renal stones** (hyperparathyroidism) in the patient and family. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`**Anaplastic carcinoma**: an elderly person with a long-standing goitre that **enlarges over weeks**, with pain, hoarseness, dysphagia and breathlessness - an emergency. [ATA anaplastic 2021]`,`Most patients are **euthyroid**; hyperthyroid symptoms make a nodule more likely benign (toxic adenoma), though cancer can coexist with Graves disease. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`A **hard, irregular, fixed nodule** that does not move with deglutition (fixity to trachea or strap muscles) suggests extrathyroidal extension. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Cervical lymphadenopathy**, especially ipsilateral jugular chain nodes, may be the only sign of a small papillary primary (old term 'lateral aberrant thyroid'). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Vocal-cord palsy** on indirect or fibre-optic laryngoscopy before surgery is mandatory to document - a palsied cord with a nodule strongly suggests invasion. [ATA 2025]`,`**Berry sign** - absent carotid pulsation on the side of the mass - indicates malignant encasement of the carotid sheath. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Stridor, Pemberton sign** (facial plethora on raising arms) and tracheal deviation mark airway compromise or retrosternal extension. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`MEN2B stigmata: **mucosal neuromas** of lips and tongue, **marfanoid habitus**, thickened corneal nerves and intestinal ganglioneuromatosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Benign colloid nodule or multinodular goitre** - soft, mobile, spongiform or purely cystic on ultrasound, Bethesda II on FNAC (see the Goitre and iodine deficiency topic). [ATA 2025]`,`**Follicular adenoma** - cytology identical to follicular carcinoma; only the excised capsule tells them apart. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Hashimoto nodule (pseudonodule)** - heterogeneous gland, high anti-TPO; FNAC shows lymphocytes and oncocytes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Primary thyroid lymphoma** - rapidly enlarging mass on Hashimoto background in an elderly woman, like anaplastic cancer; needs **core biopsy with flow cytometry** because it is treated by chemotherapy, not surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis** - a painful tender nodule with high ESR and low uptake, settling in weeks (see the Thyroiditis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastasis to the thyroid** (kidney, lung, breast, melanoma) and **tuberculous cervical nodes**, the commonest neck node disease in India, can mimic a nodule with nodes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**TSH first**: a low TSH suggests a hyperfunctioning nodule - do a **radionuclide scan**; a 'hot' nodule rarely needs FNAC. Normal or high TSH goes to ultrasound and FNAC. [ATA 2025]`,`**Neck ultrasound** grades the nodule (ACR TI-RADS or ATA patterns) and maps the lateral nodes; suspicious features are **solid hypoechoic, taller-than-wide, irregular margins, microcalcification and extrathyroidal extension** (details in the Hyperthyroidism and nodule topic). [ACR TI-RADS 2017]`,`**US-guided FNAC** reported by **Bethesda 2023 (3rd edition)**: I non-diagnostic (risk of malignancy about 13%), II benign (about 4%), III **AUS** (about 22%), IV **follicular neoplasm** (about 30%), V suspicious for malignancy (about 74%), VI malignant (about 97%). [Bethesda 2023]`,`**Molecular testing** on Bethesda III-IV aspirates (gene panels such as ThyroSeq or Afirma; BRAF, RAS, RET/PTC, TERT) can avoid diagnostic surgery where available - largely in tertiary centres in India. [Bethesda 2023]`,`**Pre-operative work-up for cancer**: lateral neck ultrasound, **laryngoscopy**, and **contrast CT or MRI** if the mass is fixed, retrosternal or causes hoarseness or stridor; avoid unnecessary iodinated contrast if early RAI is planned. [ATA 2025]`,`**Serum calcitonin** (normal roughly under 10 pg/mL in men, under 5 pg/mL in women, assay-dependent) and **CEA** for suspected MTC; **calcitonin over 500 pg/mL** suggests distant spread and needs chest, liver and bone imaging. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`Every MTC needs **germline RET testing** and, before surgery, **plasma metanephrines** (to exclude phaeochromocytoma) and **serum calcium/PTH**. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`**Pre-operative serum thyroglobulin is not useful** for diagnosis because benign goitres also raise it; Tg becomes a marker only after total thyroidectomy. [ATA 2025]`,`Suspected anaplastic carcinoma: **core or open biopsy** with **BRAF V600E immunohistochemistry** and rapid staging (CT neck-chest, PET-CT) within days. [ATA anaplastic 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`The family physician's role: **TSH, ultrasound and FNAC** for any suspicious nodule, prompt referral of Bethesda IV-VI to a thyroid or ENT/endocrine surgeon, and lifelong follow-up after treatment. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Bethesda II**: repeat ultrasound at 12-24 months by TI-RADS; **Bethesda I**: repeat US-guided FNAC after about 3 months; **Bethesda III**: repeat FNAC or molecular test, or diagnostic lobectomy. [Bethesda 2023]`,`**Papillary microcarcinoma (1 cm or less)** without nodes, extrathyroidal extension or high-risk location (near trachea or nerve) may be offered **active surveillance** with ultrasound every 6-12 months instead of surgery. [ATA 2025]`,`**Levothyroxine after total thyroidectomy** about **1.6-2 microgram/kg/day** orally on an empty stomach, adjusted to the TSH target; after lobectomy many need no thyroxine if TSH stays within target. [ATA 2025]`,`Check **TSH 6-8 weeks** after any dose change; ask about palpitations and bone health when TSH is suppressed (AF and osteoporosis risk), especially in postmenopausal women. [ATA 2025]`,`Counsel that differentiated thyroid cancer is **highly curable** and that the follow-up marker is **thyroglobulin with anti-Tg antibody** measured in the same laboratory each time. [ATA 2025]`]},{heading:`Definitive treatment and inpatient care`,points:[`**Surgery for DTC**: **total thyroidectomy** for tumours **over 4 cm**, gross extrathyroidal extension, clinical node metastases or distant metastases; **lobectomy is sufficient** for low-risk unifocal intrathyroidal tumours up to 4 cm without nodes. [ATA 2025]`,`**Therapeutic central (level VI) neck dissection** for clinically involved central nodes and **lateral (levels II-V) dissection** for biopsy-proven lateral nodes; prophylactic central dissection is considered for T3-T4 or lateral N1 disease. [ATA 2025]`,`Post-thyroidectomy ward care: **check calcium and PTH** (PTH under about 15 pg/mL predicts hypocalcaemia; give calcium plus calcitriol), watch for **neck haematoma** (open the wound at the bedside if airway is compromised) and document voice. [BTA 2014]`,`**Radioiodine (I-131)** after total thyroidectomy is **selective**: not routine for ATA low-risk, considered for intermediate, and recommended for high-risk; doses about **30 mCi (1.1 GBq) for remnant ablation**, 30-150 mCi as adjuvant, and **100-200 mCi for known disease**. [ATA 2025]`,`RAI preparation: **TSH over 30 mIU/L** by **levothyroxine withdrawal for 3-4 weeks** or **recombinant TSH 0.9 mg IM on 2 consecutive days**; **low-iodine diet for 1-2 weeks**; negative pregnancy test; stop breastfeeding at least 3 months before. [ATA 2025]`,`**MTC**: **total thyroidectomy plus central neck dissection**, lateral dissection guided by imaging and calcitonin; operate on any **phaeochromocytoma first**. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`**Anaplastic carcinoma**: secure the airway, rapid BRAF testing, and for **BRAF V600E** tumours **dabrafenib 150 mg twice daily plus trametinib 2 mg once daily**, often neoadjuvant before surgery; otherwise resection if feasible plus chemoradiation, with early palliative care. [ATA anaplastic 2021]`,`**RAI-refractory DTC** with progressive disease: **lenvatinib 24 mg once daily** or **sorafenib 400 mg twice daily**; cabozantinib second-line; selpercatinib for RET fusions, larotrectinib for NTRK fusions. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Advanced MTC**: **selpercatinib 160 mg twice daily** (120 mg if under 50 kg) for RET-mutant disease, or vandetanib 300 mg daily or cabozantinib; start only for **progressive or symptomatic** disease, not for a rising calcitonin alone. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: a DTC found in early pregnancy can be operated in the **second trimester** if it grows substantially; otherwise defer surgery to after delivery - outcome is unchanged. **RAI is absolutely contraindicated** in pregnancy and lactation. [ATA 2017 pregnancy]`,`Avoid pregnancy for **6-12 months after RAI**; men should wait about 3-4 months before conception. [ATA 2025]`,`**Children** present with more nodal and lung metastases yet have excellent survival; nodules are more often malignant (about 20-25%) and are managed by paediatric thyroid teams. [ATA paediatric 2015]`,`**Elderly**: TSH suppression raises AF and fracture risk - targets are relaxed; anaplastic and poorly differentiated cancers cluster here. [ATA 2025]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: RAI clearance is slow, so the activity is reduced and dialysis timed with the nuclear medicine team. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: I-131 therapy is available only at nuclear medicine centres (government medical colleges, regional cancer centres, AIIMS); **PM-JAY packages** cover thyroidectomy and RAI. [PM-JAY HBP 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Surgical: **permanent hypoparathyroidism** about 1-3% and **recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy** about 1-2% in high-volume hands; bilateral nerve palsy causes stridor needing tracheostomy. [BTA 2014]`,`**Superior laryngeal nerve (external branch)** injury causes loss of high pitch and voice fatigue - important in singers and teachers. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`RAI: **sialadenitis and dry mouth**, taste change, nasolacrimal duct obstruction, transient gonadal effects, and small risk of **second cancers (leukaemia)** with high cumulative activity. [ATA 2025]`,`Long-term **TSH suppression**: atrial fibrillation, bone loss in postmenopausal women and anxiety - the reason targets were de-escalated. [ATA 2025]`,`**Dynamic risk stratification** re-classifies response at 6-24 months: **excellent** (recurrence 1-4%), **indeterminate**, **biochemical incomplete** (about 20% develop structural disease) and **structural incomplete** (disease-specific death 11% with loco-regional, up to 50% with distant disease). [ATA 2015]`,`MTC: post-operative **calcitonin undetectable** predicts cure; a **calcitonin doubling time under 6 months** predicts poor survival. Anaplastic: 1-year survival about 20% historically, improving with BRAF-targeted therapy. [ATA MTC 2015]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Thyroglobulin plus anti-Tg antibody** every 6-12 months for the first years: anti-Tg antibodies (present in about 25%) cause **falsely low Tg**, so a rising antibody titre itself suggests disease. [ATA 2025]`,`Response after total thyroidectomy and RAI: **excellent** = suppressed Tg under 0.2 ng/mL or stimulated Tg under 1 ng/mL with negative imaging; **biochemical incomplete** = suppressed Tg 1 ng/mL or more, stimulated Tg 10 ng/mL or more, or rising anti-Tg. [ATA 2015]`,`**Neck ultrasound at 6-12 months** after surgery, then at intervals by risk; diagnostic whole-body RAI scan only for high-risk or unexplained Tg rise. [ATA 2025]`,`**FDG PET-CT** when Tg is high (above about 10 ng/mL) and the RAI scan is negative - FDG-avid disease is usually RAI-refractory and more aggressive. [ATA 2025]`,`**TSH targets (ATA 2015 framework)**: structural incomplete **under 0.1 mIU/L**; biochemical incomplete **0.1-0.5**; high-risk with excellent response 0.1-0.5 for up to 5 years then 0.5-2; low-risk or lobectomy with excellent response **0.5-2 mIU/L**. [ATA 2015, de-escalated in ATA 2025]`,`**MTC follow-up**: calcitonin and CEA **3 months after surgery**, then every 6-12 months; compute doubling times; screen **RET-positive relatives** and offer prophylactic thyroidectomy. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`**Prophylactic thyroidectomy** in RET carriers: **highest risk (M918T, MEN2B) in the first year of life**; **high risk (codon 634, A883F) at or before age 5** guided by calcitonin; moderate risk when calcitonin starts to rise. [ATA MTC 2015]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Do not screen** asymptomatic adults for thyroid cancer by ultrasound or palpation - it drives overdiagnosis without lowering mortality. [USPSTF 2017]`,`**Cascade genetic testing** of first-degree relatives of every MTC patient with a RET mutation prevents death from hereditary MTC. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`Shield the thyroid in children during radiotherapy and avoid unnecessary CT of the neck; **potassium iodide** blocks thyroid uptake after nuclear accidents. [WHO 2017]`,`Counsel on **radiation safety after RAI**: sleep separately and keep distance from children and pregnant women for several days, double-flush the toilet, and use separate utensils - per the nuclear medicine discharge advice. [AERB]`,`Explain **lifelong levothyroxine** after total thyroidectomy: take daily on an empty stomach, separate from calcium and iron by 4 hours, and never stop before RAI unless told. [ATA 2025]`,`Link to the **NP-NCD** cancer care pathway and **PM-JAY** for cost of surgery, RAI and targeted therapy. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ATA 2025 adult DTC guideline** replaces ATA 2015: adopts **WHO 2022 terms**, endorses **active surveillance** for low-risk microcarcinoma, accepts **lobectomy for low-risk tumours up to 4 cm**, makes RAI **selective** and de-escalates TSH suppression. [ATA 2025]`,`ATA 2025 recommends **tumour genomic profiling** (BRAF, RET, NTRK, ALK and others) in advanced, RAI-refractory or high-grade disease to choose targeted therapy. [ATA 2025]`,`**Bethesda 2023 (3rd edition)** uses single names for categories III (**AUS**, dropping FLUS) and IV (**follicular neoplasm**, dropping 'suspicious for'), and publishes average risk of malignancy for each category. [Bethesda 2023]`,`**WHO 2022** renames Hurthle cell carcinoma **oncocytic carcinoma**, introduces **differentiated high-grade thyroid carcinoma**, and stops calling the tall-cell, hobnail and columnar forms 'variants' (now subtypes). [WHO 2022]`,`**LIBRETTO-531 (2023)**: selpercatinib gave longer progression-free survival than cabozantinib or vandetanib in RET-mutant MTC, making it the preferred first systemic drug. [NEJM 2023]`,`**ATA 2021 anaplastic guideline**: urgent BRAF testing and dabrafenib-trametinib (including neoadjuvant use) have changed ATC from uniformly fatal to occasionally resectable. [ATA anaplastic 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: 'Why can FNAC not diagnose follicular carcinoma?' - because it needs **capsular or vascular invasion** seen only on histology. [Robbins 10e]`,`'Why is RAI useless in MTC?' - C cells lack the **sodium-iodide symporter**; 'Why is Tg the marker after total thyroidectomy?' - only thyroid tissue makes it. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **ordering serum thyroglobulin to diagnose a nodule** - it has no diagnostic value. [ATA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **operating on MTC without excluding phaeochromocytoma** - hypertensive crisis under anaesthesia. [ATA MTC 2015]`,`Remember the **age cut-off 55** in AJCC 8 and that a patient under 55 with distant metastases is only **stage II**. [AJCC 8e]`,`Lymph node metastasis is common in papillary cancer but **affects recurrence more than survival** in the young. [ATA 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Bethesda System for Reporting Thyroid Cytopathology, 2023`,columns:[`Category`,`Name`,`Average risk of malignancy`,`Usual management`],rows:[[`I`,`Non-diagnostic`,`About 13%`,`Repeat US-guided FNAC after about 3 months`],[`II`,`Benign`,`About 4%`,`Clinical and ultrasound follow-up`],[`III`,`Atypia of undetermined significance (AUS)`,`About 22%`,`Repeat FNAC, molecular testing, diagnostic lobectomy or surveillance`],[`IV`,`Follicular neoplasm`,`About 30%`,`Molecular testing or diagnostic lobectomy`],[`V`,`Suspicious for malignancy`,`About 74%`,`Molecular testing, lobectomy or near-total thyroidectomy`],[`VI`,`Malignant`,`About 97%`,`Lobectomy or total thyroidectomy by risk`]]},{heading:`Types of thyroid cancer compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Papillary`,`Follicular`,`Medullary`,`Anaplastic`],rows:[[`Cell of origin`,`Follicular cell`,`Follicular cell`,`Parafollicular C cell`,`Follicular cell (dedifferentiated)`],[`Share`,`80-85%`,`About 10%`,`1-2%`,`Under 2%`],[`Typical age`,`20-50 years`,`40-60 years`,`Any; children in MEN2`,`Over 60 years`],[`Spread`,`Lymphatic (neck nodes)`,`Blood (bone, lung)`,`Lymphatic and blood`,`Local invasion, all routes`],[`Key genetics`,`BRAF V600E, RET/PTC, NTRK`,`RAS, PAX8-PPARG`,`RET (germline in 25%)`,`TP53, TERT, BRAF`],[`Hallmark pathology`,`Orphan Annie nuclei, psammoma bodies`,`Capsular or vascular invasion`,`Amyloid stroma, calcitonin staining`,`Spindle and giant cells`],[`Tumour marker`,`Thyroglobulin`,`Thyroglobulin`,`Calcitonin and CEA`,`None useful`],[`RAI useful`,`Yes, selectively`,`Yes, selectively`,`No`,`No`],[`10-year survival`,`Over 95%`,`85-90%`,`About 75%`,`Median survival months`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in thyroid cancer`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6-2 microgram/kg/day orally, empty stomach`,`Lifelong; titrate every 6-8 weeks to the risk-based TSH target`,`Over-suppression causes AF and bone loss; separate from calcium, iron, PPIs`],[`Radioiodine (I-131)`,`30 mCi ablation; 30-150 mCi adjuvant; 100-200 mCi for known disease, orally`,`Single dose; repeat only for RAI-avid persistent disease`,`Sialadenitis, dry mouth, taste loss; contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation`],[`Recombinant TSH (thyrotropin alfa)`,`0.9 mg IM daily for 2 days`,`Before RAI or stimulated Tg`,`Nausea, headache; avoids hypothyroid withdrawal; costly`],[`Calcium carbonate plus calcitriol`,`Elemental calcium 1-3 g/day plus calcitriol 0.25-1 microgram/day orally`,`Post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia; taper by calcium and PTH`,`Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, stones`],[`Lenvatinib`,`24 mg orally once daily`,`Until progression or toxicity (RAI-refractory DTC)`,`Hypertension, proteinuria, diarrhoea, fatigue, fistula, bleeding`],[`Sorafenib`,`400 mg orally twice daily`,`Until progression (RAI-refractory DTC)`,`Hand-foot skin reaction, diarrhoea, hypertension, raised TSH`],[`Selpercatinib`,`160 mg orally twice daily (120 mg if under 50 kg)`,`Until progression (RET-mutant MTC, RET-fusion DTC)`,`Hypertension, QT prolongation, raised transaminases, dry mouth`],[`Dabrafenib plus trametinib`,`150 mg twice daily plus 2 mg once daily orally`,`BRAF V600E anaplastic; neoadjuvant or until progression`,`Pyrexia, rash, reduced ejection fraction, uveitis`],[`Vandetanib / cabozantinib`,`300 mg once daily / 140 mg once daily (capsule) orally`,`Progressive MTC when selpercatinib not available`,`Vandetanib QT prolongation; cabozantinib bleeding, fistula, hand-foot syndrome`]]},{heading:`ATA response to therapy after total thyroidectomy and RAI`,columns:[`Response`,`Suppressed Tg`,`Stimulated Tg`,`Imaging`,`TSH target`],rows:[[`Excellent`,`Under 0.2 ng/mL`,`Under 1 ng/mL`,`Negative`,`0.5-2 mIU/L (0.1-0.5 for up to 5 years if high risk)`],[`Indeterminate`,`0.2-1 ng/mL`,`1-10 ng/mL`,`Non-specific findings`,`0.5-2 mIU/L if low risk; 0.1-0.5 if intermediate or high risk`],[`Biochemical incomplete`,`1 ng/mL or more`,`10 ng/mL or more`,`Negative`,`0.1-0.5 mIU/L`],[`Structural incomplete`,`Any`,`Any`,`Structural or functional disease`,`Under 0.1 mIU/L`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a rapidly enlarging thyroid mass`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Confirming test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Anaplastic carcinoma`,`Elderly, old goitre, hard fixed, hoarse in weeks`,`Core biopsy, BRAF IHC`,`Airway, BRAF-MEK inhibitors, surgery, chemoradiation`],[`Primary thyroid lymphoma`,`Elderly woman with Hashimoto, rubbery`,`Core biopsy with flow cytometry`,`R-CHOP chemotherapy plus radiotherapy`],[`Haemorrhage into a nodule`,`Sudden painful swelling over hours`,`Ultrasound: cystic with debris`,`Aspiration, observation`],[`Subacute thyroiditis`,`Pain to jaw or ear, fever, tender`,`High ESR, low uptake`,`NSAID or prednisolone`],[`Poorly differentiated carcinoma`,`Rapid growth, nodes, older adult`,`Histology (high mitoses, necrosis)`,`Total thyroidectomy, RAI if avid`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor, breathlessness or a rapidly enlarging neck mass - suspect anaplastic carcinoma or lymphoma and secure the airway today`,`New hoarseness with a thyroid nodule - recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion`,`Hard, fixed nodule with ipsilateral cervical lymph nodes`,`Neck swelling or breathlessness within hours of thyroidectomy - expanding haematoma; open the wound`,`Perioral tingling, carpopedal spasm or Chvostek sign after thyroidectomy - acute hypocalcaemia`,`Thyroid nodule with diarrhoea and flushing or a family history of MTC or phaeochromocytoma`,`Thyroid nodule in a child or after childhood neck irradiation`],pearls:[`**Papillary is 'P' for Popular, Palpable nodes, Psammoma bodies, Positive prognosis.**`,`Follicular spreads by **blood to bone and lung**; papillary by **lymph to neck nodes**.`,`**FNAC cannot diagnose follicular carcinoma** - invasion is a histological diagnosis.`,`**Thyroglobulin is a follow-up marker, never a diagnostic test** for a nodule.`,`**Always measure anti-Tg antibody with Tg** - antibodies falsely lower Tg.`,`Before operating on MTC, **exclude phaeochromocytoma** with plasma metanephrines.`,`**RAI and TSH suppression do nothing in MTC** - C cells lack NIS and TSH receptors.`,`AJCC 8: **age 55** is the cut-off; under 55 the worst DTC stage is **II**.`,`**Anaplastic carcinoma is always stage IV** - test BRAF V600E within days.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman has a 2.5 cm solid thyroid nodule. US-guided FNAC is reported as Bethesda category IV. Which statement about this report is correct?`,options:[`It confirms follicular carcinoma and needs total thyroidectomy plus radioiodine`,`It cannot distinguish follicular adenoma from carcinoma, so molecular testing or diagnostic lobectomy is advised`,`It is a non-diagnostic sample and should simply be repeated after 3 months`,`It carries a risk of malignancy above 90% and is treated as papillary carcinoma`],answer:1,explanation:`Bethesda IV (follicular neoplasm) carries about a 30% risk of malignancy; cytology cannot see capsular or vascular invasion, so adenoma and carcinoma look the same and the next step is molecular testing or diagnostic lobectomy. It is not proof of carcinoma, it is not non-diagnostic (that is category I), and a risk above 90% belongs to category VI.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has medullary thyroid carcinoma on FNAC with a raised calcitonin. Before thyroidectomy, which test is mandatory?`,options:[`Serum thyroglobulin`,`Radioiodine uptake scan`,`Plasma free metanephrines`,`Anti-TPO antibody`],answer:2,explanation:`About a quarter of MTC is hereditary (MEN2), and an undiagnosed phaeochromocytoma causes a hypertensive crisis under anaesthesia, so plasma metanephrines are mandatory and a phaeo is removed first. Thyroglobulin is a marker of follicular cell tumours, C cells do not take up iodine so an uptake scan adds nothing, and anti-TPO has no role in MTC staging.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman had total thyroidectomy and radioiodine for papillary carcinoma one year ago. Suppressed thyroglobulin is undetectable but anti-thyroglobulin antibody titre has doubled. Neck ultrasound is normal. What is the best interpretation?`,options:[`Excellent response; reduce follow-up to every 5 years`,`Laboratory error, since Tg is undetectable`,`Expected rise after RAI that needs no action`,`Possible persistent disease, because rising anti-Tg antibody can mask thyroglobulin`],answer:3,explanation:`Anti-Tg antibodies interfere with immunometric Tg assays and give falsely low results; a rising titre is classed as biochemical incomplete response and warrants closer imaging. Calling it excellent ignores the antibody trend, and a doubling titre a year after treatment is neither a typical lab error nor an expected post-RAI change - antibodies should fall when tissue is gone.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-q4`,stem:`Under AJCC 8th edition staging, a 40-year-old woman with papillary carcinoma, lateral neck nodes and lung metastases is in which stage?`,options:[`Stage II`,`Stage I`,`Stage III`,`Stage IVB`],answer:0,explanation:`AJCC 8 uses an age cut-off of 55 for differentiated thyroid cancer: under 55, any T and any N without metastasis is stage I and any distant metastasis is stage II. Stage III and IVB apply only to patients aged 55 or more, and stage I would be correct only if there were no distant metastases.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-q5`,stem:`A 70-year-old man with a long-standing goitre has rapid enlargement over 3 weeks, hoarseness and stridor. Core biopsy shows anaplastic carcinoma positive for BRAF V600E. Which systemic therapy has the best evidence?`,options:[`High-dose radioiodine 200 mCi`,`Dabrafenib plus trametinib`,`Levothyroxine TSH suppression`,`Vandetanib`],answer:1,explanation:`BRAF V600E-mutant anaplastic carcinoma responds to combined BRAF and MEK inhibition with dabrafenib and trametinib, which can also be used neoadjuvantly to make the tumour resectable. Anaplastic cells lose the sodium-iodide symporter so radioiodine is useless, TSH suppression has no effect on a dedifferentiated tumour, and vandetanib is a RET-directed drug for medullary carcinoma.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify thyroid malignancies. Describe the evaluation of a thyroid nodule and the management and follow-up of differentiated thyroid cancer.`,openingLines:[`Thyroid cancer is the commonest endocrine malignancy; 80-85% are papillary, and most have a 10-year survival above 95%.`,`Management is risk-adapted: surgery for all, radioiodine and TSH suppression selectively, and lifelong follow-up with thyroglobulin.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification (WHO 2022)`,points:[`Follicular cell: **papillary**, follicular, oncocytic, high-grade (poorly differentiated, differentiated high-grade), **anaplastic**.`,`C cell: **medullary** (sporadic 75%, hereditary MEN2 25%).`,`Others: primary lymphoma, metastases; NIFTP is a low-risk neoplasm, not cancer.`]},{heading:`Evaluation of a nodule`,points:[`History of rapid growth, hoarseness, radiation, family history; examine for hardness, fixity, nodes, cord palsy.`,`**TSH** first: low TSH means scintigraphy; normal or high goes to **ultrasound** with TI-RADS.`,`**US-guided FNAC** reported by **Bethesda 2023** I-VI with risk of malignancy 13, 4, 22, 30, 74, 97%.`,`Calcitonin if MTC suspected; laryngoscopy and CT for fixed or retrosternal masses.`]},{heading:`Surgery`,points:[`**Lobectomy** for low-risk unifocal tumours up to 4 cm without nodes; active surveillance an option for low-risk microcarcinoma.`,`**Total thyroidectomy** for over 4 cm, gross extrathyroidal extension, nodes or metastases.`,`Therapeutic central and lateral neck dissection for involved nodes.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification and radioiodine`,points:[`ATA risk of recurrence: low (under 5%), intermediate (5-20%), high (over 20%).`,`RAI not routine for low risk; considered for intermediate; recommended for high risk.`,`Prepare with TSH over 30 (withdrawal or rhTSH), low-iodine diet, pregnancy test.`]},{heading:`TSH suppression and follow-up`,points:[`Levothyroxine 1.6-2 microgram/kg; TSH under 0.1 for structural disease, 0.5-2 for excellent response.`,`Tg with anti-Tg antibody and neck ultrasound at 6-12 months; classify response (excellent, indeterminate, biochemical, structural).`,`FDG PET-CT if Tg high and RAI scan negative; lenvatinib or sorafenib for progressive RAI-refractory disease.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Papillary over 95%, follicular 85-90% at 10 years; worse with age over 55, size, extension, distant metastases.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: nodule to TSH to ultrasound to FNAC (Bethesda) to surgery`,`Table: Bethesda categories with risk of malignancy`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Nodule evaluation and Bethesda`,marks:3},{part:`Surgery, RAI and TSH suppression`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up and prognosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Bethesda`,`ATA risk`,`radioiodine`,`thyroglobulin`,`TSH suppression`,`lobectomy`]},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-cancer-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.`,openingLines:[`Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from the calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells and forms 1-2% of thyroid cancers.`,`About 25% are hereditary due to germline RET mutations (MEN2A, MEN2B, familial MTC).`],answer:[{heading:`Features`,points:[`Nodule with nodes; diarrhoea and flushing in advanced disease; amyloid stroma on histology.`,`MEN2A: MTC, phaeo, hyperparathyroidism; MEN2B: MTC, phaeo, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Calcitonin and CEA**; calcitonin over 500 pg/mL needs imaging for distant metastases.`,`**Germline RET** in all; **plasma metanephrines** and calcium before surgery.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`**Total thyroidectomy plus central neck dissection**; remove phaeo first.`,`No role for RAI or TSH suppression; selpercatinib for progressive RET-mutant disease.`,`Prophylactic thyroidectomy in carriers: M918T in first year, codon 634 by age 5.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Calcitonin and CEA at 3 months, then 6-12 monthly; doubling time under 6 months is ominous.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: MEN2A versus MEN2B components`],markSplit:[{part:`Origin and genetics`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`C cells`,`calcitonin`,`RET`,`MEN2`,`phaeochromocytoma`,`selpercatinib`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Thyroid cancer chapter`,`Ringel MD, Sosa JA, et al. 2025 American Thyroid Association Management Guidelines for Adult Patients with Differentiated Thyroid Cancer. Thyroid, 2025`,`Ali SZ, VanderLaan PA, eds. The Bethesda System for Reporting Thyroid Cytopathology, 3rd edition, 2023`,`Wells SA Jr, et al. Revised American Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Management of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma. Thyroid, 2015`,`Bible KC, et al. 2021 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Management of Patients with Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer. Thyroid, 2021`,`WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`,`AJCC Cancer Staging Manual, 8th edition, 2017`,`ICMR-NCDIR Report of the National Cancer Registry Programme, 2020`]}),i.push({id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy`,title:`Thyroid disease in pregnancy and postpartum`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`Pregnancy changes thyroid physiology (**hCG stimulates the TSH receptor**, **TBG doubles**, iodine need rises to **250 microgram/day**), so thyroid status is judged against **trimester-specific TSH ranges**; **overt hypothyroidism and Graves disease must be treated** because both harm mother and fetus - **levothyroxine dose is raised by 25-30% as soon as pregnancy is confirmed**, **PTU is used in the first trimester and methimazole/carbimazole thereafter**, and **postpartum thyroiditis** is screened for in the year after delivery.`,keywords:[`gestational hypothyroidism`,`subclinical hypothyroidism in pregnancy`,`trimester-specific TSH`,`levothyroxine dose increase`,`propylthiouracil PTU`,`carbimazole embryopathy`,`methimazole`,`gestational transient thyrotoxicosis`,`hyperemesis gravidarum`,`TRAb`,`TSH receptor antibody`,`fetal thyrotoxicosis`,`neonatal Graves`,`postpartum thyroiditis`,`TPO antibody`,`isolated hypothyroxinaemia`,`universal thyroid screening pregnancy`,`MoHFW 2014 hypothyroidism pregnancy guideline`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Overt hypothyroidism** in pregnancy is a TSH above the trimester-specific range with **low free T4**, or **TSH over 10 mIU/L** whatever the T4; **subclinical hypothyroidism** is a raised TSH with normal free T4. [ATA 2017]`,`Globally overt hypothyroidism affects about **0.3-0.5%** and subclinical hypothyroidism **2-3%** of pregnancies; **TPO antibodies** are present in **5-15%** of women of reproductive age. [ATA 2017]`,`India has a much higher burden: a multicentre study from **11 cities in 9 states** found **hypothyroidism in about 13%** of first-trimester women, mostly subclinical. [Dhanwal, IJEM 2013]`,`**Hyperthyroidism** complicates about **0.1-0.4%** (Graves disease 85% of true hyperthyroidism), while **gestational transient thyrotoxicosis** affects **1-3%** of pregnancies, more in Asian women and with hyperemesis. [ATA 2017]`,`**Postpartum thyroiditis** occurs in about **5%** of women (range 1-17%) within a year of delivery, in **30-50% of TPO-positive** women and three times more often in type 1 diabetes. [ATA 2017]`,`**Iodine requirement** in pregnancy and lactation is **250 microgram/day** (WHO); India's iodised salt programme has reduced but not abolished deficiency in pregnant women. [WHO 2007]`]},{heading:`Physiology and pathophysiology`,points:[`**hCG shares its alpha subunit with TSH** and weakly stimulates the TSH receptor; at the hCG peak (**8-12 weeks**) free T4 rises slightly and **TSH falls**, so the first-trimester TSH range is lower. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Oestrogen raises TBG** (reduced hepatic clearance from sialylation), so **total T4 and T3 rise about 1.5-fold by 16 weeks** - total T4 times 1.5 is a valid alternative when free T4 assays are unreliable. [ATA 2017]`,`**Type 3 deiodinase in the placenta** inactivates T4, and the expanded plasma volume and renal iodide clearance increase demand - so a woman with **no thyroid reserve** (post-thyroidectomy, RAI, Hashimoto) cannot raise output and **needs 25-50% more levothyroxine**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **fetus depends entirely on maternal T4 until 10-12 weeks**, when its own thyroid starts concentrating iodine; fetal TSH-driven secretion matures by **18-20 weeks** - maternal hypothyroxinaemia in the first half of pregnancy impairs neuronal migration and myelination. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**IgG crosses the placenta**: maternal **TRAb** stimulates the fetal thyroid from about 20 weeks (fetal and neonatal thyrotoxicosis), while **antithyroid drugs** also cross and can cause fetal goitre and hypothyroidism. [ATA 2017]`,`Pregnancy is a state of **immune tolerance**; the **postpartum immune rebound** unmasks autoimmune thyroiditis, causing destructive release of stored hormone (thyrotoxic phase) followed by hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Very high hCG** (hyperemesis, twins, **molar pregnancy, choriocarcinoma**) produces hCG-mediated thyrotoxicosis without autoimmunity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Hypothyroidism: **Hashimoto (autoimmune) thyroiditis** is the commonest cause where iodine is sufficient; **iodine deficiency** remains a cause in endemic districts; others are post-thyroidectomy, post-RAI and drugs. [ATA 2017]`,`Hyperthyroidism: **gestational transient thyrotoxicosis** and **Graves disease** account for most; toxic nodules, molar pregnancy and thyroiditis are rare. [ATA 2017]`,`High-risk features for targeted testing: **past thyroid disease or surgery**, symptoms or goitre, **TPO positivity**, type 1 diabetes or other autoimmune disease, **age over 30**, BMI 40 or more, **recurrent miscarriage or preterm birth**, infertility, head-neck radiation, family history, amiodarone, lithium or recent iodinated contrast, and residence in an iodine-deficient area. [ATA 2017]`,`**TPO antibody positivity** doubles the risk of miscarriage and preterm birth and predicts progression of TSH during pregnancy and postpartum thyroiditis. [ATA 2017]`,`Infertility and **IVF (controlled ovarian stimulation)** raise oestrogen and TBG abruptly and can precipitate hypothyroidism in TPO-positive women. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Reference ranges and classification`,points:[`Use **population- and trimester-specific TSH ranges** from the local laboratory; where unavailable, take the **upper limit of TSH as about 4.0 mIU/L** in the first trimester, returning gradually towards the non-pregnant range in the second and third. [ATA 2017]`,`The older fixed limits of **2.5 (first), 3.0 (second and third trimester)** from ATA 2011 are **no longer recommended as the upper limit** because they over-diagnose; 2.5 survives only as the **treatment target** for women already on levothyroxine. [ATA 2017]`,`Indian guidance: **MoHFW 2014** and Indian studies show higher first-trimester TSH limits than western data; many Indian laboratories still report 2.5/3.0, which labels too many women hypothyroid. [MoHFW 2014]`,`**Free T4 immunoassays are unreliable** in late pregnancy (high TBG, low albumin); interpret with method-specific trimester ranges, or use **total T4 with a 1.5-fold upward adjustment** after 16 weeks. [ATA 2017]`,`Categories: overt hypothyroidism, **subclinical hypothyroidism** (TPO positive or negative), **isolated hypothyroxinaemia** (normal TSH, free T4 below the 2.5th-5th centile), euthyroid TPO-positive, gestational transient thyrotoxicosis, Graves disease. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Hypothyroid symptoms (tiredness, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance) **overlap with normal pregnancy** - most cases are found by testing, not symptoms. [ATA 2017]`,`**Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis**: first-trimester nausea, **hyperemesis**, weight loss and palpitations with **no previous thyroid history, no goitre and no eye signs**, settling by **14-18 weeks**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Graves disease**: symptoms before pregnancy, **goitre with bruit, eye signs**, pretibial myxoedema, and a failure to gain weight despite good appetite. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask every woman on levothyroxine about pregnancy plans - she must **increase her dose as soon as the pregnancy test is positive**, not at the first antenatal visit. [ATA 2017]`,`Postpartum: **palpitations and anxiety at 1-6 months** (thyrotoxic phase) or **fatigue, low mood and poor concentration at 4-8 months** (hypothyroid phase) - often mistaken for postpartum depression or 'new-mother tiredness'. [ATA 2017]`,`Past history of **Graves treated with RAI or surgery** matters: TRAb may persist and affect the fetus even though the mother is now hypothyroid on levothyroxine. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Thyroid examination: **diffuse goitre with bruit** suggests Graves; a firm bosselated goitre suggests Hashimoto; no goitre fits gestational transient thyrotoxicosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eye signs** (proptosis, lid retraction with lid lag) indicate Graves and exclude gestational transient thyrotoxicosis. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Resting **pulse over 100/min**, wide pulse pressure, tremor and **failure to gain weight** suggest true hyperthyroidism; normal pregnancy raises pulse by only 10-15/min. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`Check **blood pressure and proteinuria** - both overt hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism increase pre-eclampsia risk. [ATA 2017]`,`Fetal assessment in TRAb-positive women: **fetal heart rate over 160/min**, fetal goitre, growth restriction, hydrops or advanced bone age on ultrasound suggest fetal thyrotoxicosis. [ATA 2017]`,`Postpartum: a **small painless goitre** in a woman with thyrotoxic symptoms favours postpartum thyroiditis; tenderness suggests subacute thyroiditis. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis versus Graves**: GTT has no prior history, no goitre or eye signs, **TRAb negative**, and a high **free T4 with a normal or near-normal T3** that settles by 18 weeks; Graves is TRAb positive and persists. [ATA 2017]`,`**Low TSH in the first trimester is normal** in about 15% of healthy women; treat only when free T4 is clearly raised and the clinical picture fits. [ATA 2017]`,`**Molar pregnancy or choriocarcinoma**: thyrotoxicosis with very high hCG, uterus larger than dates and snowstorm ultrasound. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postpartum thyroiditis versus postpartum Graves**: thyroiditis is TRAb negative, has a **T3:T4 ratio under 20** (ng/microgram), no bruit or eye signs and **low uptake**; Graves is TRAb positive with high uptake and a higher T3:T4 ratio. [ATA 2017]`,`**Postpartum depression**: every woman with postpartum depression should have a **TSH** checked. [ATA 2017]`,`**Sheehan syndrome** after postpartum haemorrhage: failure of lactation, amenorrhoea and **low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH** (secondary hypothyroidism) - see the Hypopituitarism topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**TSH at the first antenatal visit** (ideally before 9 weeks or at pregnancy confirmation); add **free T4** if TSH is abnormal. [MoHFW 2014]`,`**TPO antibody** when TSH is above 2.5 mIU/L - it decides treatment of subclinical hypothyroidism. [ATA 2017]`,`**TRAb** at diagnosis of thyrotoxicosis to separate Graves from GTT; in Graves (current or treated by RAI/surgery) repeat at **18-22 weeks**, and again at **30-34 weeks** if still raised or the mother is on antithyroid drugs. [ATA 2017]`,`A **TRAb over 3 times the upper limit** predicts fetal or neonatal thyrotoxicosis and needs serial fetal ultrasound and neonatal thyroid testing. [ATA 2017]`,`**Radioiodine uptake and scans are contraindicated** in pregnancy; in lactation a **technetium scan** is preferred with milk pumped and discarded for 24 hours. [ATA 2017]`,`Women on levothyroxine: **TSH every 4 weeks until 16-20 weeks**, then at least once at **26-32 weeks**. [ATA 2017]`,`Graves on antithyroid drugs: **free T4 (or total T4) and TSH every 2-4 weeks** at first, then every 4-6 weeks; **aim for free T4 at or just above the upper limit**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Newborn screening** (cord blood or heel-prick TSH at 24-72 hours) is essential, especially after maternal TRAb positivity or ATD treatment. [MoHFW 2014]`]},{heading:`Management of hypothyroidism in OPD`,points:[`**Known hypothyroid woman**: increase levothyroxine by **25-30% as soon as pregnancy is confirmed** - simplest is **two extra tablets a week** (9 doses a week instead of 7); after thyroidectomy or RAI the rise may need to be 40-50%. [ATA 2017]`,`**Treatment target**: TSH in the **lower half of the trimester range, or under 2.5 mIU/L** where no local range exists. [ATA 2017]`,`**Overt hypothyroidism diagnosed in pregnancy**: start levothyroxine **1.6-2 microgram/kg/day** (full dose, not titrated up slowly) and recheck in 4 weeks. [ATA 2017]`,`**Subclinical hypothyroidism - treat**: TPO positive with TSH above the range, and TPO negative with **TSH over 10**; **consider** for TPO positive with TSH 2.5 to upper limit and TPO negative with TSH above the range but under 10; starting dose about **25-50 microgram/day**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Do not treat** TPO-negative women with a normal TSH, or **isolated hypothyroxinaemia** routinely - the CATS and TSH trials showed no IQ benefit from late treatment. [ATA 2017]`,`Levothyroxine on an **empty stomach**, separated by **4 hours from iron and calcium** supplements given in antenatal care - the commonest reason for a rising TSH in Indian practice. [ATA 2017]`,`**Euthyroid TPO-positive** women: check TSH every 4 weeks until mid-pregnancy; levothyroxine for recurrent miscarriage did not improve live births in **TABLET (2019)** and **T4LIFE (2024)** trials. [ESHRE 2022]`,`After delivery, **return to the pre-pregnancy dose** and check TSH at **6 weeks**; stop levothyroxine started for subclinical hypothyroidism (TSH under 5, low dose) and recheck TSH in 6 weeks. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Management of hyperthyroidism`,points:[`**Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis**: supportive - antiemetics, IV fluids and thiamine for hyperemesis, **propranolol 10-20 mg every 6-8 hours** briefly for symptoms; **antithyroid drugs are not indicated**. [ATA 2017]`,`**Graves in the first trimester (to 16 weeks)**: **propylthiouracil 50-150 mg three times daily** because methimazole/carbimazole cause **embryopathy** in weeks 6-10 (aplasia cutis, choanal and oesophageal atresia, omphalocele). [ATA 2017]`,`**After 16 weeks**: PTU can be continued or switched to **methimazole 5-15 mg/day** (or **carbimazole 10-20 mg/day**) - PTU carries a small risk of **fulminant hepatitis**; dose ratio **methimazole to PTU about 1:20**. [ATA 2017]`,`Use the **lowest dose that keeps free T4 at or just above the upper limit**; over-treatment causes **fetal goitre and hypothyroidism**; **block-and-replace is contraindicated** (levothyroxine crosses poorly, ATD crosses well). [ATA 2017]`,`A woman on methimazole with a positive test who is **euthyroid on a low dose** may stop the drug with **TSH and free T4 every 1-2 weeks**; otherwise switch to PTU straight away. [ATA 2017]`,`**Thyroidectomy in the second trimester** if drugs fail, cause major adverse effects or are refused; **radioiodine is absolutely contraindicated** in pregnancy. [ATA 2017]`,`Women planning pregnancy after Graves: reach stable euthyroidism first; after **RAI, wait at least 6 months** before conceiving; consider definitive therapy before pregnancy if TRAb stays high. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency care`,points:[`Admit for **hyperemesis with dehydration or ketosis**, **thyroid storm**, heart failure or uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis near term. [ATA 2017]`,`**Thyroid storm in pregnancy** is treated as outside pregnancy: PTU loading 500-1000 mg then 250 mg every 4 hours, potassium iodide an hour after PTU, hydrocortisone, beta-blocker and cooling - with fetal monitoring (see the Thyroid storm topic). [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Labour, caesarean section, infection and pre-eclampsia can **precipitate storm** in poorly controlled Graves - aim for control before term. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Myxoedema coma** is rare in pregnancy; give IV levothyroxine plus hydrocortisone in ICU. [Harrison 22e]`,`Neonate of a TRAb-positive mother: assess for **neonatal thyrotoxicosis** (tachycardia, irritability, goitre, poor weight gain), which may appear after **5-10 days** when maternal ATD clears; treat with methimazole and propranolol. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Postpartum thyroiditis and lactation`,points:[`Classic triphasic course: **thyrotoxic phase at 1-6 months** (about 1 in 3), **hypothyroid phase at 4-8 months** (about 4 in 10) or both (about 1 in 4); most recover by 12 months. [ATA 2017]`,`Thyrotoxic phase: **propranolol 10-40 mg every 6-8 hours** for symptoms; **no antithyroid drugs** (destructive, not overproduction). Recheck TSH every 2 months to catch the hypothyroid phase. [ATA 2017]`,`Hypothyroid phase: levothyroxine if symptomatic, **TSH over 10**, breastfeeding or planning another pregnancy; try tapering after **6-12 months**. [ATA 2017]`,`About **20-50% develop permanent hypothyroidism** within 5-10 years - check **TSH yearly**; recurrence in the next pregnancy is common. [ATA 2017]`,`Breastfeeding: **methimazole up to 20 mg/day** or **PTU up to 450 mg/day** is safe; levothyroxine is safe at any dose. [ATA 2017]`,`Lactating women need **250 microgram iodine daily**; avoid **high-dose iodine and povidone-iodine** exposure that can cause neonatal hypothyroidism. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated overt hypothyroidism: **miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, placental abruption, preterm birth, low birth weight, stillbirth** and lower child IQ (about 7 points in Haddow's cohort). [ATA 2017]`,`Subclinical hypothyroidism, especially with TPO positivity, is linked to miscarriage and preterm birth; benefit of treatment is clearest for **TSH over 4 with TPO positivity**. [ATA 2017]`,`Uncontrolled hyperthyroidism: **miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, maternal heart failure, thyroid storm, preterm birth, growth restriction and stillbirth**. [ATA 2017]`,`Fetal and neonatal: **fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis in 1-5%** of Graves pregnancies (TRAb), **fetal hypothyroidism and goitre** from excessive ATD, and **methimazole embryopathy** or PTU hepatotoxicity. [ATA 2017]`,`With good control, maternal and fetal outcomes are **similar to the general population**. [ATA 2017]`]},{heading:`Screening, prevention and the Indian debate`,points:[`**ATA 2017** found insufficient evidence for or against **universal screening** and recommends **targeted case finding** in high-risk women (list above) - this misses 30-80% of affected women. [ATA 2017]`,`**ACOG 2020** does not recommend universal screening and does not recommend treating subclinical hypothyroidism in pregnancy. [ACOG PB 223, 2020]`,`**MoHFW 2014 national guideline** recommends **universal TSH screening of all pregnant women at the first antenatal visit** in India because of the high prevalence; the **Indian Thyroid Society** and FOGSI support this. [MoHFW 2014]`,`Pre-conception: check TSH in women on levothyroxine and aim for **TSH under 2.5** before conceiving; counsel the **two-extra-tablets** rule. [ATA 2017]`,`**Iodine**: use iodised salt and, where intake is inadequate, **150 microgram iodide daily** in prenatal supplements from pre-conception through lactation; avoid excess over 500 microgram/day. [ATA 2017]`,`Programme link: TSH testing within **PMSMA antenatal check-ups** and the **Free Diagnostics Service Initiative**; newborn TSH screening under **RBSK**. [MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ATA 2017** is still the main pregnancy thyroid guideline: it dropped the fixed 2.5/3.0 upper limits for a **local range or about 4.0 mIU/L**, and graded subclinical treatment by **TPO status**. [ATA 2017]`,`**ESHRE 2022 recurrent pregnancy loss guideline** does not recommend levothyroxine for **euthyroid TPO-positive** women; **T4LIFE (2024)** confirmed no benefit on live birth. [ESHRE 2022]`,`**ACOG Practice Bulletin 223 (2020)** stays more conservative than ATA and MoHFW on screening and subclinical treatment - name the conflict in the exam. [ACOG PB 223, 2020]`,`**ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism guideline**: PTU to 16 weeks, then PTU or methimazole; switching is no longer mandatory. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Indian practice continues to follow **MoHFW 2014 universal screening**, with the **Indian Thyroid Society** emphasising Indian trimester-specific ranges. [MoHFW 2014]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: 'Why does TSH fall in the first trimester?' - **hCG cross-stimulates the TSH receptor**. 'Why does the levothyroxine dose rise?' - TBG, placental deiodinase, volume and fetal transfer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **starting carbimazole for gestational transient thyrotoxicosis** - it is TRAb negative and self-limiting. [ATA 2017]`,`Common mistake: using **carbimazole in the first trimester** (embryopathy) or **block-and-replace** in pregnancy. [ATA 2017]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **TRAb in a woman previously treated with RAI or surgery** for Graves - the fetus can still be thyrotoxic. [ATA 2017]`,`Remember the **'9 tablets a week' rule** and the **4-weekly TSH** until mid-pregnancy. [ATA 2017]`,`Know the **conflict**: ATA targeted case finding, ACOG no screening, **MoHFW universal screening** in India. [MoHFW 2014]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for thyroid disease in pregnancy and lactation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Levothyroxine (known hypothyroid)`,`Pre-pregnancy dose plus 25-30% (two extra tablets a week) orally`,`TSH every 4 weeks to 20 weeks; back to pre-pregnancy dose after delivery`,`Separate from iron and calcium by 4 hours; over-replacement causes thyrotoxicosis`],[`Levothyroxine (new overt)`,`1.6-2 microgram/kg/day orally`,`Recheck TSH and free T4 in 4 weeks`,`As above`],[`Levothyroxine (subclinical)`,`25-50 microgram/day orally`,`Target TSH under 2.5; reassess postpartum`,`Safe; may stop after delivery if low dose`],[`Propylthiouracil`,`50-150 mg orally three times daily`,`First trimester (to 16 weeks); lowest dose for free T4 at upper limit`,`Fulminant hepatitis, agranulocytosis, ANCA vasculitis; up to 450 mg/day in lactation`],[`Methimazole / carbimazole`,`Methimazole 5-15 mg/day or carbimazole 10-20 mg/day orally`,`Second and third trimesters; lactation up to 20 mg methimazole`,`Embryopathy in weeks 6-10; agranulocytosis, rash, cholestasis`],[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg orally every 6-8 hours`,`Short term (2-6 weeks) for symptoms`,`Fetal growth restriction, neonatal bradycardia and hypoglycaemia with long use`],[`Iodide supplement`,`150 microgram/day orally`,`Pre-conception through lactation`,`Avoid total over 500 microgram/day`]]},{heading:`Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis versus Graves disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis`,`Graves disease`],rows:[[`Onset`,`First trimester, with hCG peak`,`Any time; often before pregnancy`],[`Hyperemesis`,`Common`,`Uncommon`],[`Goitre and bruit`,`Absent`,`Diffuse goitre with bruit`],[`Eye signs`,`Absent`,`Often present`],[`TRAb`,`Negative`,`Positive`],[`T3 pattern`,`Free T4 high, T3 modest`,`T3 often disproportionately high`],[`Course`,`Resolves by 14-18 weeks`,`Persists; may improve in late pregnancy, flare postpartum`],[`Treatment`,`Supportive, short beta-blocker`,`PTU first trimester, then PTU or methimazole`]]},{heading:`Who to treat: subclinical hypothyroidism in pregnancy (ATA 2017)`,columns:[`TSH`,`TPO antibody positive`,`TPO antibody negative`],rows:[[`Over 10 mIU/L`,`Treat`,`Treat`],[`Above trimester range, under 10`,`Treat`,`Consider treatment`],[`2.5 to upper limit of range`,`Consider treatment`,`Do not treat`],[`Normal, under 2.5`,`Do not treat; monitor TSH every 4 weeks`,`Do not treat`]]},{heading:`Postpartum thyroiditis versus postpartum Graves`,columns:[`Feature`,`Postpartum thyroiditis`,`Postpartum Graves`],rows:[[`Timing`,`1-6 months after delivery`,`Usually after 3-6 months`],[`Goitre`,`Small, painless`,`Diffuse with bruit`],[`Eye signs`,`Absent`,`May be present`],[`TRAb`,`Negative`,`Positive`],[`T3:T4 ratio (ng/microgram)`,`Under 20`,`Over 20`],[`Uptake scan`,`Low`,`High`],[`Treatment`,`Propranolol; watch for hypothyroid phase`,`Antithyroid drugs`]]},{heading:`Monitoring schedule`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`When`],rows:[[`All pregnant women (India)`,`TSH`,`First antenatal visit (MoHFW 2014)`],[`On levothyroxine`,`TSH`,`Every 4 weeks to 16-20 weeks, once at 26-32 weeks, 6 weeks postpartum`],[`Graves on drugs`,`Free or total T4 and TSH`,`Every 2-4 weeks initially, then 4-6 weeks`],[`Graves, current or past`,`TRAb`,`First trimester, 18-22 weeks, 30-34 weeks if raised`],[`TPO-positive euthyroid`,`TSH`,`Every 4 weeks to mid-pregnancy; 3 and 6 months postpartum`],[`After postpartum thyroiditis`,`TSH`,`Yearly lifelong`]]}],redFlags:[`Hyperemesis with dehydration, ketosis or weight loss over 5% - admit`,`Thyrotoxic woman with fever, delirium, heart rate over 140/min or heart failure - suspect thyroid storm`,`Fetal heart rate persistently over 160/min, fetal goitre or hydrops in a TRAb-positive mother`,`Sore throat or fever on PTU or carbimazole - stop the drug and check neutrophil count`,`Jaundice, dark urine or right upper quadrant pain on PTU - stop and check liver function`,`Thyrotoxicosis with uterus larger than dates or very high hCG - exclude molar pregnancy`,`Irritable, tachycardic, poorly feeding newborn of a mother with Graves disease`],pearls:[`**hCG is a weak TSH** - low first-trimester TSH is often normal.`,`**Nine tablets a week**: raise levothyroxine 25-30% the day the test is positive.`,`**PTU in the first trimester, methimazole or carbimazole after** - 'P for Pregnancy's first part'.`,`**No block-and-replace in pregnancy** - ATD crosses the placenta, levothyroxine barely does.`,`**Gestational transient thyrotoxicosis**: no goitre, no eyes, no TRAb - no antithyroid drug.`,`**TRAb crosses the placenta** - measure it even if Graves was treated by RAI or surgery.`,`Keep free T4 at the **upper limit of normal** on ATD - the fetus is more sensitive than the mother.`,`**Every postpartum depression needs a TSH.**`,`India: **MoHFW 2014 universal TSH screening** versus ATA targeted case finding.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman on levothyroxine 100 microgram daily for Hashimoto thyroiditis has a positive home pregnancy test at 5 weeks. TSH last month was 1.8 mIU/L. What is the best immediate advice?`,options:[`Increase the dose to 9 tablets a week now and check TSH in 4 weeks`,`Continue the same dose and check TSH at the first antenatal visit at 12 weeks`,`Stop levothyroxine until the first trimester is over because of teratogenicity`,`Switch to liothyronine, which crosses the placenta better`],answer:0,explanation:`Requirement rises 25-50% from the first weeks of pregnancy because TBG and placental deiodinase rise and a Hashimoto thyroid cannot compensate; taking two extra tablets a week (about 29% more) at once protects fetal neurodevelopment. Waiting until 12 weeks leaves the critical early period undertreated, levothyroxine is not teratogenic, and liothyronine does not supply the T4 the fetal brain needs.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-q2`,stem:`At 9 weeks, a primigravida with severe vomiting has TSH under 0.01 mIU/L and free T4 slightly above the upper limit. There is no goitre or eye sign and TRAb is negative. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Carbimazole 20 mg daily`,`Propylthiouracil 100 mg three times daily`,`Radioiodine uptake scan to confirm the cause`,`Supportive care with fluids and antiemetics, and recheck thyroid tests in 3-4 weeks`],answer:3,explanation:`This is gestational transient thyrotoxicosis driven by high hCG: TRAb negative, no goitre or ophthalmopathy, linked to hyperemesis and resolving by 14-18 weeks, so only supportive care is needed. PTU would treat Graves, not hCG-mediated thyrotoxicosis; carbimazole is also embryopathic in the first trimester; and radioiodine is contraindicated in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-q3`,stem:`A woman with Graves disease on carbimazole 10 mg daily becomes pregnant and needs continued antithyroid treatment at 6 weeks. Which change is recommended?`,options:[`Continue carbimazole and add levothyroxine as block-and-replace`,`Switch to propylthiouracil until about 16 weeks`,`Stop all treatment and give radioiodine after delivery`,`Double the carbimazole dose to keep TSH within the normal range`],answer:1,explanation:`Methimazole and carbimazole cause embryopathy (aplasia cutis, choanal and oesophageal atresia) mainly in weeks 6-10, so PTU is used through the first trimester. Block-and-replace needs high ATD doses that cross the placenta while levothyroxine does not, causing fetal hypothyroidism; uncontrolled hyperthyroidism is itself harmful; and aiming for a normal TSH by doubling the dose over-treats the fetus.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-q4`,stem:`At 8 weeks, a woman has TSH 6.2 mIU/L (laboratory first-trimester upper limit 4.0), normal free T4 and negative TPO antibody. According to ATA 2017, what is the best approach?`,options:[`No treatment is ever indicated when TPO antibody is negative`,`Treatment is mandatory, with levothyroxine 100 microgram daily`,`Levothyroxine may be considered, for example 25-50 microgram daily`,`Give iodine 500 microgram daily instead of levothyroxine`],answer:2,explanation:`ATA 2017 grades treatment by TSH and TPO status: TPO-negative women with TSH above the trimester range but under 10 'may be considered' for low-dose levothyroxine, while treatment is recommended only if TPO-positive or TSH is over 10. It is therefore not mandatory at a full dose, but neither is treatment forbidden, and high-dose iodine does not correct hypothyroidism and may worsen autoimmune thyroid disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-q5`,stem:`A woman treated with radioiodine for Graves disease 3 years ago is now on levothyroxine and is 20 weeks pregnant. Fetal ultrasound shows heart rate 175/min and a fetal goitre. Which test best explains this?`,options:[`Maternal TSH receptor antibody (TRAb)`,`Maternal anti-TPO antibody`,`Maternal serum thyroglobulin`,`Maternal serum calcitonin`],answer:0,explanation:`TRAb can persist for years after RAI ablation; this IgG crosses the placenta from about 20 weeks and stimulates the fetal thyroid, causing fetal tachycardia and goitre even though the mother is hypothyroid on levothyroxine. Anti-TPO antibody does not stimulate the TSH receptor, thyroglobulin reflects thyroid tissue mass rather than fetal stimulation, and calcitonin is a marker for medullary carcinoma.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the physiological changes in thyroid function during pregnancy. Discuss the diagnosis and management of hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism in pregnancy. Add a note on screening in India.`,openingLines:[`Pregnancy raises TBG, lowers TSH in the first trimester through hCG and increases iodine and hormone needs by about 50%, so thyroid status is judged against trimester-specific ranges.`,`In India about 13% of first-trimester women are hypothyroid, which is why MoHFW 2014 recommends universal TSH screening.`],answer:[{heading:`Physiological changes`,points:[`**hCG stimulates TSH receptor**: TSH falls at 8-12 weeks.`,`**TBG doubles**: total T4 and T3 rise 1.5-fold; free T4 assays unreliable.`,`Placental deiodinase, volume expansion and renal iodide loss: iodine need **250 microgram/day**.`,`Fetus depends on maternal T4 until 10-12 weeks; fetal thyroid functional by 18-20 weeks.`]},{heading:`Hypothyroidism - diagnosis`,points:[`Trimester-specific TSH; if unavailable upper limit about **4.0** in first trimester.`,`Overt: TSH raised with low free T4, or TSH over 10; subclinical: raised TSH, normal free T4.`,`TPO antibody when TSH over 2.5.`]},{heading:`Hypothyroidism - management`,points:[`Known hypothyroid: **increase dose 25-30% (9 tablets a week)** at positive test.`,`New overt: 1.6-2 microgram/kg/day; subclinical by TPO status table (25-50 microgram).`,`Target TSH under 2.5 or lower half of range; TSH every 4 weeks to 20 weeks.`,`Postpartum: pre-pregnancy dose, TSH at 6 weeks.`]},{heading:`Hyperthyroidism`,points:[`GTT versus Graves: TRAb, goitre, eye signs; GTT supportive only.`,`Graves: **PTU to 16 weeks**, then PTU or methimazole; free T4 at upper limit; no block-replace.`,`TRAb at 18-22 weeks; fetal monitoring if over 3 times upper limit.`,`Surgery in second trimester if needed; RAI contraindicated.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, abruption, preterm, low IQ; storm and heart failure; fetal and neonatal thyrotoxicosis.`]},{heading:`Screening in India`,points:[`ATA 2017: targeted case finding; ACOG 2020: no universal screening.`,`**MoHFW 2014: universal TSH at first antenatal visit**, supported by Indian Thyroid Society.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph or table: TSH and hCG across trimesters`,`Flow chart: TSH at first visit to TPO to treatment decision`,`Table: gestational transient thyrotoxicosis versus Graves`],markSplit:[{part:`Physiology`,marks:2},{part:`Hypothyroidism`,marks:3},{part:`Hyperthyroidism`,marks:3},{part:`Screening debate`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hCG`,`TBG`,`trimester-specific TSH`,`levothyroxine increase`,`PTU`,`TRAb`,`MoHFW 2014`]},{id:`endocrine-thyroid-pregnancy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on postpartum thyroiditis.`,openingLines:[`Postpartum thyroiditis is autoimmune destructive thyroiditis within 12 months of delivery or abortion in a woman who was euthyroid before pregnancy.`,`It affects about 5% of women and 30-50% of those who are TPO antibody positive.`],answer:[{heading:`Course`,points:[`Thyrotoxic phase 1-6 months, hypothyroid phase 4-8 months, recovery by 12 months.`,`20-50% develop permanent hypothyroidism.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Low TSH with TRAb negative, T3:T4 under 20, low uptake - distinguishes from Graves.`,`Check TSH in every postpartum depression.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Thyrotoxic: propranolol only; no antithyroid drugs.`,`Hypothyroid: levothyroxine if symptomatic, TSH over 10 or planning pregnancy; taper at 6-12 months.`,`TSH yearly lifelong.`]}],mustDraw:[`Triphasic curve: free T4 against months after delivery`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Course and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`postpartum thyroiditis`,`TPO`,`triphasic`,`propranolol`,`permanent hypothyroidism`]}],references:[`Alexander EK, et al. 2017 Guidelines of the American Thyroid Association for the Diagnosis and Management of Thyroid Disease During Pregnancy and the Postpartum. Thyroid, 2017`,`Ross DS, et al. 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis. Thyroid, 2016`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India. National Guidelines for Screening of Hypothyroidism during Pregnancy, 2014`,`ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 223: Thyroid Disease in Pregnancy, 2020`,`ESHRE Guideline: Recurrent Pregnancy Loss, 2022 update`,`Dhanwal DK, et al. Prevalence of hypothyroidism in pregnancy: an epidemiological study from 11 cities in 9 states of India. Indian J Endocrinol Metab, 2013`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Obstetrics, 26th edition, 2022`]}),i.push({id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine`,title:`Goitre and iodine deficiency disorders`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`A **goitre** is any enlargement of the thyroid (clinically, each lobe larger than the terminal phalanx of the patient's thumb); worldwide its commonest cause is **iodine deficiency**, the world's leading **preventable cause of brain damage**, which India tackles through the **National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP)** by **universal salt iodisation - 30 ppm at production and at least 15 ppm at consumer level** - while **multinodular goitre** is managed by ruling out cancer, treating toxicity and relieving compression.`,keywords:[`goitre`,`goiter`,`iodine deficiency disorders`,`IDD`,`NIDDCP`,`National Goitre Control Programme`,`iodised salt`,`15 ppm`,`urinary iodine concentration`,`endemic cretinism`,`multinodular goitre`,`toxic multinodular goitre`,`Plummer disease`,`retrosternal goitre`,`Pemberton sign`,`Jod-Basedow`,`Wolff-Chaikoff effect`,`Pendred syndrome`,`double fortified salt`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Goitre** is enlargement of the thyroid gland; WHO defines a palpable goitre as **each lobe larger than the terminal phalanx of the subject's thumb**, and on ultrasound adult volume over about **18 mL in women and 25 mL in men**. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`**Iodine deficiency disorders (IDD)** - a term coined by **Hetzel (1983)** - are all the effects of iodine deficiency on growth and development, of which goitre is only the visible tip. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`Globally about **1.9 billion people** had insufficient iodine intake in the 2011 estimate; iodine deficiency is the **commonest preventable cause of mental retardation**, lowering population IQ by about **13 points** in severe deficiency. [Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2015]`,`A **total goitre rate of 5% or more in school children (6-12 years)** marks IDD as a public health problem; 5-19.9% mild, 20-29.9% moderate and **30% or more severe**. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`In India **no state is free of IDD**; NIDDCP district surveys found goitre endemic (rate over 10%) in the large majority of districts surveyed, historically in the **sub-Himalayan goitre belt** but also in the plains and coastal areas. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`,`Household use of **iodised salt** has risen to about **94% (NFHS-5, 2019-21)**, but the **National Iodine and Salt Intake Survey (2018-19)** found only about **76% of households using adequately iodised salt (15 ppm or more)**. [NFHS-5 2021; NISI 2019]`,`Where iodine is sufficient, the commonest causes of goitre are **multinodular goitre and Hashimoto thyroiditis**; palpable nodules occur in about 5% of adults, ultrasound nodules in up to 50-60%. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Iodine deficiency**: soil leached by glaciation, heavy rain and floods (Himalayan foothills, Gangetic flood plains); diet without sea fish or iodised salt. [Park 27e]`,`**Goitrogens** worsen deficiency: **thiocyanates** from cassava, **brassica vegetables** (cabbage, cauliflower), pearl millet and soy; perchlorate and nitrate in water; **selenium and iron deficiency** impair deiodinases and thyroid peroxidase. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Physiological goitre** at **puberty and pregnancy** from increased hormone demand and hCG stimulation. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Autoimmune**: Hashimoto thyroiditis (firm, lobulated) and Graves disease (diffuse with bruit). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dyshormonogenesis** (autosomal recessive defects in peroxidase, thyroglobulin, iodide transport) - **Pendred syndrome** (pendrin, SLC26A4) combines goitre with **sensorineural deafness**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: lithium, amiodarone, antithyroid drugs and excess iodine; **infiltration** (amyloid, Riedel thyroiditis), and **neoplasm** (see the Thyroid cancer topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk of **multinodular goitre** rises with age, female sex, smoking (thiocyanate), and family history; twin studies show strong heritability. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Iodine is needed for **T4 and T3 synthesis**: intake below about 100 microgram/day lowers hormone output, **TSH rises**, and the gland hypertrophies and increases iodide trapping (adaptive goitre). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low intrathyroidal iodine itself** increases sensitivity to TSH and local growth factors (IGF-1, EGF, FGF), so goitre can enlarge even with normal serum TSH. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`The deficient gland **preferentially secretes T3** (more hormone per iodine atom), so patients are often clinically euthyroid with low-normal T4. [Harrison 22e]`,`Over years, **follicles grow heterogeneously**; some clones acquire **activating TSH-receptor or Gs-alpha mutations** and become **autonomous**, producing nodules that later make the patient thyrotoxic (**toxic MNG, Plummer disease**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jod-Basedow effect**: giving iodine (salt programme, contrast, amiodarone) to an elderly person with **autonomous nodules** removes the substrate limit and causes iodine-induced hyperthyroidism - a recognised early cost of iodisation. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Wolff-Chaikoff effect**: a large iodine load acutely blocks organification; normal glands escape within days, but glands with **Hashimoto, prior RAI or fetal immaturity** fail to escape and become hypothyroid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fetal brain damage**: maternal and fetal hypothyroxinaemia in the first half of pregnancy disturbs neuronal migration, myelination and cochlear development, causing cretinism. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and the IDD spectrum`,points:[`**WHO goitre grading (2007)**: **grade 0** no palpable or visible goitre; **grade 1** palpable but not visible with the neck in normal position; **grade 2** visible with the neck in normal position. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`Clinical classification: **diffuse** (simple, Graves, Hashimoto), **nodular** (solitary or multinodular; toxic or non-toxic), with retrosternal extension where relevant. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**IDD in the fetus**: abortion, stillbirth, congenital anomalies, raised perinatal mortality and **endemic cretinism**. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`**IDD in the neonate and child**: neonatal goitre and hypothyroidism, juvenile hypothyroidism, **impaired mental and physical development**, poor school performance. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`**IDD in adults**: goitre and its complications, hypothyroidism, impaired mental function, reduced work output and **iodine-induced hyperthyroidism**. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`**Endemic cretinism**: **neurological type** (deaf-mutism, spastic diplegia, squint, severe intellectual disability, usually euthyroid) from early fetal deficiency; **myxoedematous type** (severe hypothyroidism, dwarfism, less deafness) from continuing postnatal hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Population iodine status by median urinary iodine** (school children/non-pregnant adults): under 20 microgram/L severe, 20-49 moderate, 50-99 mild deficiency, **100-199 adequate**, 200-299 above requirement, 300 or more excessive; in pregnancy **150-249 adequate**. [WHO 2013]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most goitres are **painless neck swellings** noticed by the patient or family, often for years; ask about **duration and recent change in size**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Compressive symptoms**: dysphagia, a **choking sensation lying flat**, dyspnoea or noisy breathing, cough, and **hoarseness** (which suggests malignancy more than compression). [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`**Toxic MNG** in an older patient: weight loss, **new atrial fibrillation**, heart failure or **apathetic thyrotoxicosis** with few adrenergic symptoms. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Ask about **recent iodine exposure** - contrast CT or angiography, amiodarone, povidone-iodine, kelp supplements - a classic trigger of thyrotoxicosis in MNG. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Ask about **salt use** (loose crystal or rock salt, salt stored open or added during cooking), place of residence, family members with goitre, and **children with deafness or developmental delay**. [Park 27e]`,`Red-flag history: **rapid growth, pain, hoarseness, childhood irradiation** - evaluate for cancer, lymphoma or haemorrhage. [ATA 2015 nodules]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Inspect from the front: the gland **moves up on swallowing** (attached to the larynx by the pretracheal fascia); a thyroglossal cyst moves on protruding the tongue. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Palpate from behind: size, surface (diffuse or nodular), consistency, tenderness, **lower border** (if not palpable, suspect retrosternal extension), and cervical nodes. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Tracheal deviation**, **Kocher test** (stridor on gentle lateral lobe pressure) and percussion dullness over the manubrium suggest airway compression or retrosternal goitre. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Pemberton sign**: raising both arms above the head for about a minute causes **facial plethora, distended neck veins** and stridor in retrosternal goitre (thoracic inlet obstruction). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **thyrotoxic signs** (pulse irregular in AF, tremor, warm skin) - toxic MNG has **no eye signs** of Graves except lid retraction. [Harrison 22e]`,`In endemic areas examine children for **deaf-mutism, squint, spasticity and short stature**, and use WHO grades for surveys. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Graves disease**: diffuse soft goitre with bruit, eye signs, TRAb positive, diffuse high uptake on scan (see the Graves topic). [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`**Hashimoto thyroiditis**: firm, rubbery, lobulated goitre with **high anti-TPO**, often hypothyroid; ultrasound shows heterogeneous hypoechoic gland. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid cancer**: hard, fixed, single dominant nodule with nodes or hoarseness - FNAC of suspicious nodules even inside an MNG (the cancer risk per nodule is similar to a solitary nodule). [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`**Subacute thyroiditis**: painful tender goitre after a viral illness, high ESR, low uptake. [Harrison 22e]`,`Neck swellings that are not thyroid: **thyroglossal cyst** (moves with tongue protrusion), **cervical lymph nodes** (TB is common in India), branchial cyst, lipoma. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Retrosternal goitre versus mediastinal masses** (thymoma, lymphoma, germ cell tumour) - continuity with the cervical thyroid on CT settles it. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**TSH** in every goitre; free T4 and T3 if TSH is low (T3 toxicosis is common in toxic MNG); **anti-TPO** if TSH is raised or the gland is firm. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`**Neck ultrasound**: volume, nodularity, and **TI-RADS grading of each nodule**; select nodules for FNAC by sonographic pattern, not by size alone. [ACR TI-RADS 2017]`,`**FNAC** of suspicious or dominant nodules, reported by **Bethesda 2023**; not needed for nodules that are 'hot' on scan or purely cystic or spongiform. [Bethesda 2023]`,`**Radionuclide scan (technetium-99m or I-123)** when TSH is low: **toxic MNG shows patchy uptake with hot and cold areas**, toxic adenoma a single hot nodule with suppression of the rest. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Large or retrosternal goitre: **CT neck and chest** (non-contrast if possible, or contrast with awareness of iodine load) for tracheal narrowing and substernal extent; **flow-volume loop** shows fixed upper airway obstruction; laryngoscopy before surgery. [BTA 2014]`,`**Urinary iodine is a population measure**: a single spot UIC in one person varies too much from day to day to judge that person's iodine status. [WHO 2013]`,`Programme indicators: **median UIC** in school children and pregnant women, **goitre rate in 6-12 year olds**, **neonatal TSH over 5 mIU/L in more than 3%** of newborns, and **salt iodine at household level** (spot test kit or titration). [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`**Salt testing kit**: a drop of test solution turns iodised salt blue-violet; the colour chart reads **0, under 15 and 15 ppm or more**; iodometric titration is the laboratory standard. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Small, euthyroid, non-compressive goitre with benign or no suspicious nodules**: reassure and **observe** with clinical review and ultrasound every 1-2 years. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`Ensure **adequate iodine**: iodised salt for the household, and **iodine 150 microgram/day** in pregnancy and lactation supplements where intake is uncertain. [ATA 2017 pregnancy]`,`**Levothyroxine suppression to shrink a euthyroid goitre is not recommended** - modest benefit, risk of AF and bone loss, and regrowth on stopping. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`Hypothyroid goitre (Hashimoto or iodine deficiency): **levothyroxine 1.6 microgram/kg/day** (25-50 microgram in the elderly or cardiac patients), titrated to TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxic MNG**: control with **carbimazole 10-30 mg/day** (or methimazole 10-20 mg) plus **propranolol 20-40 mg three times daily**; remission does not occur, so plan definitive treatment or **long-term low-dose antithyroid drug** in the elderly or unfit. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Avoid **iodinated contrast and amiodarone** where possible in MNG; if contrast is unavoidable in a high-risk patient, check thyroid function after 2-4 weeks. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Refer to a surgeon for **compression, retrosternal extension, suspicious cytology, rapid growth, toxicity needing definitive treatment or cosmetic concern**. [BTA 2014]`]},{heading:`Definitive and inpatient management`,points:[`**Surgery** for large symptomatic MNG: **total thyroidectomy** is preferred over subtotal resection to avoid recurrence (recurrence after subtotal 15-40% over decades). [BTA 2014]`,`**Retrosternal goitre** is removed through the **neck in over 95%**; sternotomy is needed only for deep posterior mediastinal or recurrent goitres. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Make the toxic patient **euthyroid before surgery** with antithyroid drugs and beta-blocker; **Lugol iodine is not used** for toxic MNG (risk of worsening thyrotoxicosis). [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`**Radioiodine** for toxic MNG or for volume reduction in the elderly unfit for surgery: higher activities (often **10-30 mCi**) are needed; volume falls about **40-50%** over 1-2 years; recombinant TSH can augment uptake. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Airway emergency from a large goitre (haemorrhage into a nodule, malignant change): **sit upright, humidified oxygen, early anaesthetic and ENT help**; intubation may need fibre-optic or awake technique. [BTA 2014]`,`Post-thyroidectomy: monitor **calcium and PTH**, voice and wound; **tracheomalacia** after long-standing compression is rare but may need prolonged intubation. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: iodine need **250 microgram/day**; goitre often enlarges; maternal deficiency causes fetal hypothyroidism and cretinism - ensure iodised salt and supplements (see Thyroid disease in pregnancy). [WHO 2007]`,`**Neonates**: excess iodine (povidone-iodine on the cord or mother) can cause transient hypothyroidism through the Wolff-Chaikoff effect; congenital hypothyroidism is detected by **newborn TSH screening**. [ATA 2017 pregnancy]`,`**Children** with goitre need TSH and anti-TPO; adolescent **colloid goitre** usually regresses; Pendred syndrome if deafness coexists. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: toxic MNG presents as AF or heart failure; prefer RAI or long-term low-dose antithyroid drug over surgery if frail. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`**Hill and tribal areas** with poor access to packaged iodised salt: **iodised oil (Lipiodol)** 400 mg iodine orally once a year for women of reproductive age is the WHO option where salt iodisation cannot reach. [WHO 2007]`,`**Salt reduction and iodisation are compatible**: WHO advises adjusting iodine concentration to the falling salt intake rather than abandoning iodisation. [WHO 2014]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Tracheal compression** and stridor, **oesophageal compression**, **SVC obstruction** and **recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy** in large or retrosternal goitres. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Haemorrhage into a nodule** causes sudden painful enlargement and may threaten the airway. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis** (toxic MNG) with AF, embolic stroke, heart failure and osteoporosis in the elderly. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`**Malignancy** in a nodule of an MNG (risk per nodule similar to a solitary nodule) - never assume an MNG is benign. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`Population consequences of IDD: **cretinism, lower IQ, poorer school performance and economic productivity**, and higher stillbirth and infant mortality - all preventable. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`Prognosis of non-toxic MNG is good; many grow slowly and about **10% become toxic over 10 years**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Same-day referral for **stridor, rapid growth, hoarseness, or haemorrhage into a nodule**. [BTA 2014]`,`Non-toxic MNG under observation: **TSH yearly** (to catch toxic change) and **ultrasound every 1-2 years**; re-biopsy nodules that grow over 50% in volume or develop suspicious features. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`After RAI: **TSH and free T4 at 6 weeks**, then every 1-3 months for 6 months; hypothyroidism develops in a minority after RAI for MNG. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`After total thyroidectomy: lifelong levothyroxine with **TSH 6-8 weeks after starting**, then yearly. [BTA 2014]`,`Community follow-up: **ASHA salt testing** at household level, and monitoring of iodine content at production, wholesale and retail through the **State IDD Cell and IDD monitoring laboratory**. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme (NIDDCP)`,points:[`**National Goitre Control Programme (1962)** was renamed the **National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP) in 1992**, shifting from goitre to the whole IDD spectrum; it now runs under the **National Health Mission (NCD flexipool)**. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`,`Core strategy is **universal salt iodisation**: iodine content of **at least 30 ppm at production** and **at least 15 ppm at consumer level** (as **potassium iodate**, more stable in hot humid climates). [FSSAI 2011]`,`**Sale of non-iodised salt for direct human consumption is banned** under the Food Safety and Standards (Prohibition and Restrictions on Sales) Regulations 2011. [FSSAI 2011]`,`Programme components: **surveys** to assess IDD magnitude, supply of iodised salt, **resurveys every 5 years** in districts, **laboratory monitoring** of salt and urinary iodine, and **health education**. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`,`**Double fortified salt** (iron plus iodine, developed at NIN Hyderabad) is supplied through ICDS, mid-day meals and PDS in several states to tackle anaemia and IDD together. [FSSAI 2018]`,`**Global Iodine Deficiency Disorders Prevention Day - 21 October** - is used for awareness campaigns. [MoHFW NIDDCP]`,`Counsel households: buy **packaged iodised salt**, store it in a **closed container away from heat and moisture**, and add salt **after cooking** where possible to limit iodine loss. [Park 27e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**NFHS-5 (2019-21)** reports about **94% of households using iodised salt**, while **NISI 2018-19** found about 76% using adequately iodised salt - coverage is high but quality gaps remain. [NFHS-5 2021; NISI 2019]`,`**WHO 2014 salt iodisation guideline** recommends iodising **all food-grade salt** and setting the level by national salt intake, in harmony with salt-reduction targets. [WHO 2014]`,`**Bethesda 2023** and **ACR TI-RADS** now guide nodule biopsy inside multinodular goitres. [Bethesda 2023]`,`**ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism guideline** accepts **long-term low-dose methimazole** as an alternative to RAI or surgery for toxic MNG in selected patients. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Expert reviews stress **monitoring for iodine excess** as well as deficiency (median UIC 300 microgram/L or more) as iodisation matures. [Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'NIDDCP'** - history, 30 ppm/15 ppm, ban on non-iodised salt, monitoring indicators, salt testing kit. [Park 27e]`,`Classic viva: **'Why is urinary iodine not used for individual diagnosis?'** - large day-to-day variation; it is a population median. [WHO 2013]`,`Know the **WHO indicators** for IDD elimination: median UIC 100-199 microgram/L, over 90% households using adequately iodised salt, goitre rate under 5%. [WHO/UNICEF/ICCIDD 2007]`,`Common mistake: **giving levothyroxine to 'shrink' a euthyroid MNG**. [ATA 2015 nodules]`,`Common mistake: **using Lugol iodine before surgery in toxic MNG** - it is for Graves only. [ATA 2016 hyperthyroidism]`,`Distinguish **Jod-Basedow (iodine-induced hyperthyroidism)** from **Wolff-Chaikoff (iodine-induced hypothyroidism)**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Indicators of iodine status (WHO)`,columns:[`Indicator`,`Group`,`Cut-off or target`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Median urinary iodine`,`School children, non-pregnant adults`,`100-199 microgram/L`,`Adequate; under 20 severe, 20-49 moderate, 50-99 mild deficiency`],[`Median urinary iodine`,`Pregnant women`,`150-249 microgram/L`,`Adequate; under 150 insufficient; 500 or more excessive`],[`Total goitre rate`,`Children 6-12 years`,`Under 5%`,`5-19.9% mild, 20-29.9% moderate, 30% or more severe IDD`],[`Households using adequately iodised salt`,`Households`,`Over 90%`,`Programme goal for elimination`],[`Neonatal TSH over 5 mIU/L`,`Newborns (heel prick)`,`Under 3% of newborns`,`Higher proportion signals iodine deficiency`],[`Salt iodine content (India)`,`Production / consumer`,`30 ppm / 15 ppm or more`,`FSSAI standard; spot kit reads 0, under 15, 15 or more`]]},{heading:`Daily iodine requirement`,columns:[`Group`,`Requirement (microgram/day)`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Infants and children 0-5 years`,`90`,`From breast milk or formula`],[`Children 6-12 years`,`120`,`Goitre surveys use this age group`],[`Adolescents and adults`,`150`,`Met by about 10 g of salt at 15 ppm`],[`Pregnancy`,`250`,`Fetal brain needs maternal T4`],[`Lactation`,`250`,`Iodine passes into breast milk`]]},{heading:`Causes of goitre and their discriminating features`,columns:[`Cause`,`Goitre`,`Thyroid status`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Iodine deficiency (endemic)`,`Diffuse, later nodular`,`Euthyroid or hypothyroid`,`Community median UIC, salt testing`],[`Multinodular goitre`,`Nodular, may be retrosternal`,`Euthyroid or toxic`,`Ultrasound, TSH, scan if TSH low`],[`Graves disease`,`Diffuse, soft, bruit`,`Hyperthyroid`,`TRAb, diffuse high uptake`],[`Hashimoto thyroiditis`,`Firm, lobulated`,`Euthyroid or hypothyroid`,`Anti-TPO`],[`Subacute thyroiditis`,`Tender`,`Transient thyrotoxicosis`,`High ESR, low uptake`],[`Dyshormonogenesis (Pendred)`,`Diffuse from childhood`,`Euthyroid or hypothyroid`,`Deafness, genetic testing`],[`Malignancy or lymphoma`,`Hard, fixed, rapid growth`,`Usually euthyroid`,`FNAC or core biopsy`]]},{heading:`Drugs and agents in goitre and IDD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Iodised salt (potassium iodate)`,`30 ppm at production, 15 ppm at consumer, in food`,`Lifelong population measure`,`Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism in elderly with autonomous nodules (early phase)`],[`Iodised oil (Lipiodol)`,`400 mg iodine orally once a year (women of reproductive age); 200 mg for young children`,`Annual, where salt iodisation cannot reach`,`Iodine-induced thyroid dysfunction; not routine where salt is adequate`],[`Potassium iodide supplement`,`150 microgram/day orally`,`Pre-conception, pregnancy and lactation`,`Excess over 500 microgram/day may cause dysfunction`],[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day orally; 25-50 microgram in elderly`,`Only for hypothyroid goitre; titrate to TSH every 6-8 weeks`,`Not for shrinking euthyroid goitre; AF and bone loss if overdosed`],[`Carbimazole / methimazole`,`Carbimazole 10-30 mg/day or methimazole 10-20 mg/day orally`,`Until definitive therapy, or long-term low dose in toxic MNG`,`Agranulocytosis, rash, hepatitis; first-trimester embryopathy`],[`Propranolol`,`20-40 mg orally three times daily`,`Until euthyroid`,`Asthma, heart block, decompensated heart failure`],[`Radioiodine (I-131)`,`Often 10-30 mCi orally for toxic or large MNG`,`Single dose; repeat after 6 months if needed`,`Contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation; transient swelling, hypothyroidism`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor, orthopnoea or a choking sensation lying flat - airway compression`,`Sudden painful enlargement of a goitre - haemorrhage into a nodule`,`Hoarseness or a hard fixed nodule with cervical nodes - suspect cancer`,`Pemberton sign or facial swelling with distended neck veins - thoracic inlet or SVC obstruction`,`New atrial fibrillation or heart failure in an elderly person with MNG - toxic MNG`,`Thyrotoxicosis after contrast CT or amiodarone in a patient with goitre - Jod-Basedow`,`Rapidly enlarging goitre in an elderly patient with Hashimoto - lymphoma or anaplastic carcinoma`],pearls:[`**30 at the factory, 15 at the family table** - ppm of iodine in salt.`,`**Requirements 90-120-150-250**: under 5, school age, adults, pregnancy and lactation.`,`**Iodine deficiency is the commonest preventable cause of brain damage.**`,`**Urinary iodine is a population median, not a personal test.**`,`**Goitre survey age group is 6-12 years**; 5% or more means a public health problem.`,`**Jod-Basedow = iodine causes hyperthyroidism; Wolff-Chaikoff = iodine blocks hormone synthesis.**`,`**Never use levothyroxine to shrink a euthyroid goitre.**`,`**Toxic MNG does not remit** - plan RAI, surgery or long-term low-dose antithyroid drug.`,`**NGCP 1962 became NIDDCP 1992.**`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-q1`,stem:`Under India's salt iodisation standard, what is the minimum iodine content of salt at the consumer (household) level?`,options:[`5 ppm`,`30 ppm`,`50 ppm`,`15 ppm`],answer:3,explanation:`Indian food standards require at least 30 ppm iodine at the point of production to allow for losses in storage and transport, so that at least 15 ppm reaches the household. Thirty ppm is the production figure, not the consumer figure, while 5 ppm and 50 ppm are not programme standards.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-q2`,stem:`A district health officer wants to know whether iodine deficiency is still a public health problem in her district. Which is the best indicator of current iodine intake?`,options:[`Median urinary iodine concentration in school children`,`Spot urinary iodine of each goitrous adult`,`Serum free T4 in pregnant women`,`Number of thyroidectomies done at the district hospital`],answer:0,explanation:`The median urinary iodine of a representative sample of school children (and pregnant women) reflects recent population intake; 100-199 microgram/L is adequate. A single spot sample in an individual varies too much from day to day, free T4 is insensitive and confounded by pregnancy, and surgical numbers reflect access to care rather than iodine intake.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-q3`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman with a long-standing multinodular goitre develops palpitations and weight loss 3 weeks after a contrast-enhanced CT scan. TSH is suppressed. What is the most likely mechanism?`,options:[`Wolff-Chaikoff effect`,`Jod-Basedow phenomenon`,`Postpartum-type destructive thyroiditis`,`TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma`],answer:1,explanation:`The Jod-Basedow phenomenon is iodine-induced hyperthyroidism: autonomous nodules in an MNG had been limited by iodine supply, and the contrast iodine load lets them overproduce hormone. The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is the opposite, iodine blocking organification and causing hypothyroidism; destructive thyroiditis is not linked to contrast; and a TSH-secreting adenoma would give a normal or high TSH, not a suppressed one.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman has a large euthyroid multinodular goitre with no compressive symptoms. Ultrasound shows spongiform nodules only. Which management is NOT recommended?`,options:[`Observation with yearly TSH and periodic ultrasound`,`Ensuring adequate dietary iodine with iodised salt`,`Levothyroxine in TSH-suppressive doses to shrink the goitre`,`Surgical referral if the goitre becomes compressive or cosmetically troubling`],answer:2,explanation:`Current guidelines advise against levothyroxine suppression for euthyroid goitre: shrinkage is modest, regrowth follows stopping, and iatrogenic subclinical hyperthyroidism raises AF and fracture risk. Observation with TSH and ultrasound, adequate iodine, and surgery for compression or cosmesis are all appropriate parts of care.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-q5`,stem:`In an iodine-deficient hill district, a 10-year-old child has deaf-mutism, squint, spastic diplegia and severe intellectual disability but is clinically euthyroid. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`This is myxoedematous cretinism and responds fully to levothyroxine`,`This is Pendred syndrome caused by a pendrin mutation`,`It results from iodine excess after salt iodisation`,`This is neurological cretinism from maternal iodine deficiency early in pregnancy`],answer:3,explanation:`Neurological endemic cretinism follows maternal and fetal hypothyroxinaemia in the first half of pregnancy, damaging the developing brain and cochlea, so the child has deaf-mutism, squint and spasticity while often being euthyroid; the damage is not reversible. Myxoedematous cretinism shows severe hypothyroidism and dwarfism, Pendred syndrome gives goitre with deafness but not spastic diplegia and cretinism, and iodine excess does not cause cretinism.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the spectrum of iodine deficiency disorders. Discuss the National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme and the indicators used to monitor it.`,openingLines:[`Iodine deficiency disorders are all the consequences of inadequate iodine intake on growth and development, of which goitre is only the visible part; iodine deficiency is the commonest preventable cause of brain damage.`,`India addresses it through NIDDCP by universal salt iodisation - 30 ppm at production and at least 15 ppm at consumer level.`],answer:[{heading:`Iodine physiology and requirement`,points:[`Needed for T4 and T3; requirement 90 (under 5), 120 (6-12 years), 150 (adults), **250 microgram/day in pregnancy and lactation**.`,`Deficiency: low T4, high TSH, goitre; fetal brain depends on maternal T4.`]},{heading:`Spectrum of IDD`,points:[`Fetus: abortion, stillbirth, anomalies, **endemic cretinism** (neurological and myxoedematous).`,`Neonate and child: goitre, hypothyroidism, impaired mental and physical development.`,`Adult: goitre and complications, hypothyroidism, impaired work output, iodine-induced hyperthyroidism.`]},{heading:`NIDDCP`,points:[`NGCP 1962, renamed NIDDCP 1992; under NHM.`,`Surveys and resurveys every 5 years; supply of iodised salt (potassium iodate).`,`**30 ppm production, 15 ppm consumer**; ban on non-iodised salt (FSSAI 2011).`,`Laboratory monitoring of salt and urine; health education; double fortified salt.`]},{heading:`Monitoring indicators (WHO)`,points:[`Median UIC 100-199 microgram/L in children; 150-249 in pregnancy.`,`Goitre rate under 5% in children 6-12 years.`,`Over 90% households using adequately iodised salt.`,`Neonatal TSH over 5 mIU/L in under 3%.`]},{heading:`Current status and challenges`,points:[`NFHS-5: about 94% households use iodised salt; NISI 2018-19: about 76% adequately iodised.`,`Gaps: loose crystal salt, storage losses, hill and tribal areas; watch for iodine excess.`]},{heading:`Role of the family physician`,points:[`Promote packaged iodised salt, store closed, add after cooking; supplements in pregnancy; ASHA salt testing.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: IDD spectrum by life stage`,`Table: WHO indicators with cut-offs`,`Flow chart: salt from factory (30 ppm) to household (15 ppm) with monitoring points`],markSplit:[{part:`Physiology and requirements`,marks:1},{part:`IDD spectrum`,marks:3},{part:`NIDDCP components`,marks:3},{part:`Indicators and current status`,marks:3}],keywords:[`IDD`,`NIDDCP`,`15 ppm`,`urinary iodine`,`cretinism`,`goitre rate`,`salt testing kit`]},{id:`endocrine-goitre-iodine-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on retrosternal goitre.`,openingLines:[`A retrosternal goitre is a thyroid enlargement with a substantial part (commonly over 50%) lying below the thoracic inlet, usually a multinodular goitre growing downward.`,`Its importance lies in silent airway, oesophageal and venous compression.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Dyspnoea, orthopnoea, stridor, dysphagia; lower border not palpable; tracheal deviation.`,`**Pemberton sign**: facial plethora and venous distension on raising the arms.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`TSH; CT neck and chest for extent and tracheal narrowing; flow-volume loop; laryngoscopy.`,`FNAC of suspicious accessible nodules.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Surgery (total thyroidectomy) is the treatment of choice; over 95% via the neck, sternotomy rarely.`,`RAI for the elderly unfit; avoid iodinated contrast surprises in toxic glands.`,`Complications: airway emergency, nerve palsy, hypocalcaemia, tracheomalacia.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram: Pemberton sign and thoracic inlet obstruction`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`retrosternal goitre`,`Pemberton sign`,`thoracic inlet`,`CT`,`cervical approach`]}],references:[`WHO, UNICEF, ICCIDD. Assessment of Iodine Deficiency Disorders and Monitoring their Elimination: A Guide for Programme Managers, 3rd edition, 2007`,`WHO. Guideline: Fortification of Food-Grade Salt with Iodine for the Prevention and Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders, 2014`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India. National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme (NIDDCP) operational guidelines`,`International Institute for Population Sciences. National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5) 2019-21, India Report, 2021`,`Ross DS, et al. 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis. Thyroid, 2016`,`Zimmermann MB, Boelaert K. Iodine deficiency and thyroid disorders. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol, 2015`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine, 27th edition, 2023`]}),i.push({id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid`,title:`Drug-induced thyroid dysfunction: amiodarone, lithium, immune checkpoint inhibitors and others`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`Many drugs alter thyroid function by **iodine load (amiodarone, contrast)**, **blocking hormone release (lithium)**, **triggering autoimmunity (interferon-alpha, immune checkpoint inhibitors, alemtuzumab)**, **destroying follicles (TKIs, amiodarone type 2)**, **suppressing TSH (glucocorticoids, dopamine, bexarotene)** or **impairing levothyroxine absorption and clearance**; the rule is **baseline TSH, scheduled monitoring, and treating the thyroid while usually continuing the essential drug** - with **amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis type 1 treated by thionamides and type 2 by glucocorticoids**.`,keywords:[`amiodarone-induced hypothyroidism`,`AIH`,`amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis`,`AIT type 1`,`AIT type 2`,`lithium goitre`,`interferon-alpha thyroiditis`,`immune checkpoint inhibitor thyroiditis`,`pembrolizumab`,`nivolumab`,`ipilimumab hypophysitis`,`sunitinib hypothyroidism`,`alemtuzumab Graves`,`iodinated contrast thyrotoxicosis`,`levothyroxine malabsorption`,`biotin interference`,`potassium perchlorate`,`bexarotene central hypothyroidism`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Drug-induced thyroid dysfunction** is hypothyroidism, thyrotoxicosis or an abnormal thyroid test caused by a medicine, contrast agent or supplement - common enough that a **drug history is part of every abnormal TSH**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone** causes thyroid dysfunction in about **15-20%** of treated patients: **hypothyroidism (AIH) predominates in iodine-sufficient areas** and **thyrotoxicosis (AIT) in iodine-deficient areas**. [ETA 2018]`,`**Lithium** causes **goitre in up to 40-50%** and **hypothyroidism in about 20%** of long-term users, mostly women and those with TPO antibodies; thyrotoxicosis is uncommon. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Immune checkpoint inhibitors** cause thyroid dysfunction - the commonest endocrine immune-related adverse event - in roughly **10-20%** on anti-PD-1/PD-L1 and more with **combined anti-CTLA-4 plus anti-PD-1**. [ASCO 2021]`,`**VEGF-targeted tyrosine kinase inhibitors** (sunitinib, sorafenib, pazopanib, axitinib, lenvatinib) cause raised TSH in about **20-50%** of patients. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Alemtuzumab** for multiple sclerosis causes autoimmune thyroid disease, mainly **Graves disease, in about a third** of patients, often 1-3 years after treatment. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In India, amiodarone, lithium and increasing use of **ICIs and TKIs** in oncology, plus iodinated contrast for CT and angiography, make this a growing OPD problem. [API Textbook 12e]`]},{heading:`Drugs and mechanisms (aetiology)`,points:[`**Iodine excess**: amiodarone, **iodinated contrast**, povidone-iodine, kelp - causes Wolff-Chaikoff hypothyroidism in autoimmune glands or **Jod-Basedow thyrotoxicosis** in nodular glands. [ETA 2021 contrast]`,`**Reduced release or synthesis**: lithium (blocks hormone release), thionamides, perchlorate; **aminoglutethimide** and sulfonamides are older examples. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoimmunity**: interferon-alpha, interleukin-2, **ICIs**, alemtuzumab, and **immune reconstitution** after antiretroviral therapy or stem cell transplant (Graves disease). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Destructive thyroiditis**: amiodarone (type 2 AIT), ICIs, TKIs (capillary regression from VEGF inhibition), lithium and interferon (painless thyroiditis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**TSH suppression (central)**: high-dose **glucocorticoids, dopamine and dobutamine, somatostatin analogues (octreotide)**, **bexarotene** (RXR agonist - central hypothyroidism in most users), and ICIs via **hypophysitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Binding-protein changes**: oestrogen, tamoxifen, methadone raise TBG (higher levothyroxine need); androgens and glucocorticoids lower TBG; **heparin** releases free fatty acids that falsely raise free T4 in vitro. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Increased clearance**: rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital and **imatinib** speed T4 metabolism (type 3 deiodinase, glucuronidation) - only matters in patients on levothyroxine or with limited reserve. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reduced levothyroxine absorption**: calcium, iron, **proton pump inhibitors**, sucralfate, aluminium antacids, bile acid sequestrants, sevelamer, orlistat, soy and coffee. [ATA 2014 hypothyroidism]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - amiodarone in detail`,points:[`Amiodarone is **37% iodine by weight**: a 200 mg tablet contains about **75 mg iodine** and releases about **6-7 mg of free iodide** a day - roughly **40 times the daily requirement**. [ETA 2018]`,`It is **highly lipophilic** with a half-life of about **50-100 days**, stored in fat, liver and thyroid - so dysfunction can appear **months to years after stopping**. [ETA 2018]`,`It **inhibits type 1 and 2 deiodinase**, so in the **euthyroid patient** T4 and reverse T3 rise, T3 falls slightly and **TSH rises transiently** in the first 3 months - these changes alone are **not disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AIH**: iodine-induced **failure to escape the Wolff-Chaikoff effect**, most often in women with **pre-existing Hashimoto (TPO positive)**. [ETA 2018]`,`**AIT type 1**: iodine-induced **excess hormone synthesis** in an abnormal gland (**nodular goitre or latent Graves**) - a Jod-Basedow mechanism. [ETA 2018]`,`**AIT type 2**: **direct cytotoxic destructive thyroiditis** in a previously normal gland - release of stored hormone, no increased synthesis; now the commonest form of AIT. [ETA 2018]`,`Amiodarone and its metabolite **desethylamiodarone** also block T3 binding to its nuclear receptor, contributing to a hypothyroid-like effect in tissues. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - lithium, ICIs and TKIs`,points:[`**Lithium** is concentrated by the thyroid and **inhibits hormone release** (and iodine coupling); TSH rises and drives **goitre** - the same release block is why lithium is sometimes used with radioiodine. [Harrison 22e]`,`Lithium also **accelerates thyroid autoimmunity** in TPO-positive patients and occasionally causes painless thyroiditis; its other endocrine effects are **hyperparathyroidism (hypercalcaemia)** and **nephrogenic diabetes insipidus**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Anti-PD-1 / PD-L1** (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, atezolizumab, durvalumab) remove peripheral tolerance, causing **T-cell destructive thyroiditis**: a short **painless thyrotoxic phase at 4-8 weeks**, then **permanent hypothyroidism** in most. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Anti-CTLA-4** (ipilimumab) chiefly causes **hypophysitis** (central hypothyroidism, central adrenal insufficiency, hypogonadism); thyroid effects are less common than with anti-PD-1. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Graves disease** after ICIs is rare; most thyrotoxicosis is destructive, so **thionamides are not used** unless TRAb is positive with high uptake. [ASCO 2021]`,`**VEGF TKIs** cause **capillary regression** in the highly vascular thyroid, destructive thyroiditis and reduced iodine uptake, and increase **type 3 deiodinase** - raising levothyroxine needs in thyroidectomised patients (imatinib, sorafenib). [ESMO 2022]`,`**Interferon-alpha** induces both **autoimmune (Hashimoto, Graves)** and **destructive** thyroiditis in about 10-15%; baseline **TPO positivity** is the strongest predictor. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`By effect: **hypothyroidism** (amiodarone, lithium, ICIs, TKIs, interferon, iodine, bexarotene-central), **thyrotoxicosis** (amiodarone, iodine, ICI or interferon thyroiditis, alemtuzumab Graves, excess levothyroxine) and **test abnormalities without disease** (heparin, biotin, TBG changes). [Harrison 22e]`,`**AIT classification**: **type 1** (synthesis, abnormal gland), **type 2** (destruction, normal gland) and **mixed or indeterminate** forms. [ETA 2018]`,`ICI thyroid toxicity is graded by **CTCAE**: **grade 1** asymptomatic, **grade 2** symptomatic but able to do daily activities, **grade 3** severe symptoms limiting self-care, **grade 4** life-threatening. [ASCO 2021]`,`By level of the axis: **primary (thyroid)** - raised TSH with low free T4; **central (pituitary)** - low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH (ipilimumab hypophysitis, bexarotene, glucocorticoids). [ESMO 2022]`,`**Factitious thyrotoxicosis** from levothyroxine or 'weight-loss' preparations: high T4, suppressed TSH, **low thyroglobulin** and low uptake. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Take a **complete drug history**: antiarrhythmics, psychiatric drugs, cancer immunotherapy and targeted therapy, MS drugs, hepatitis treatment, recent **contrast CT or angiography**, biotin and 'hair-skin-nail' supplements. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AIT** often presents as **worsening of the arrhythmia** (recurrent AF or VT), unexplained weight loss or new heart failure rather than classic thyrotoxicosis, because amiodarone's beta-blocking effect masks adrenergic symptoms. [ETA 2018]`,`**AIH** presents with fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain and, in the elderly, worsening heart failure; it usually appears in the **first 6-12 months** of therapy. [ETA 2018]`,`**ICI thyroiditis**: palpitations, tremor and anxiety at **4-8 weeks** after starting, often attributed to cancer or anxiety, followed by fatigue and weight gain. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Hypophysitis on ipilimumab**: **headache, fatigue, nausea, hypotension** and hyponatraemia at **2-3 months** - an adrenal emergency hidden behind 'fatigue of cancer'. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Lithium**: a painless goitre, mild hypothyroid symptoms that may be mistaken for depression, and polyuria from nephrogenic DI. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Pulse rate and rhythm: **new AF** on amiodarone suggests AIT; **bradycardia** may reflect AIH or amiodarone itself. [ETA 2018]`,`**Goitre**: nodular in AIT type 1, small or normal in AIT type 2; diffuse in lithium-induced goitre. [ETA 2018]`,`**Postural hypotension**, hyponatraemia and loss of axillary or pubic hair suggest ICI **hypophysitis** with adrenal insufficiency. [ESMO 2022]`,`Look for **thyroid eye disease** after alemtuzumab or immune reconstitution - it points to true Graves. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Amiodarone signs: **blue-grey skin pigmentation**, photosensitivity, corneal microdeposits and pulmonary crackles (lung toxicity). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed relaxation of ankle reflexes**, dry skin and periorbital puffiness in drug-induced hypothyroidism are identical to primary hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Euthyroid amiodarone effect** (raised T4, normal T3, normal TSH) versus AIT (suppressed TSH with **raised free T3** as well as free T4). [ETA 2018]`,`**AIT type 1 versus type 2**: nodular or Graves gland, increased vascularity on colour Doppler and measurable uptake favour type 1; normal gland, **absent vascularity** and **very low uptake** favour type 2. [ETA 2018]`,`**ICI destructive thyroiditis versus Graves**: thyroiditis is TRAb negative with low uptake and self-limiting thyrotoxicosis; Graves is TRAb positive with high uptake. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Primary versus central hypothyroidism** on ICIs: TSH high in thyroiditis; TSH low or normal with low free T4 in hypophysitis - **check morning cortisol** before giving levothyroxine. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Biotin interference** (streptavidin-biotin immunoassays) gives **falsely low TSH and falsely high free T4 and TRAb** - mimicking Graves; stop biotin for 2-3 days and repeat. [FDA 2019]`,`**Non-thyroidal illness** in very sick patients on dopamine or steroids: low T3, low or normal TSH - do not treat; repeat after recovery. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Baseline TSH (and free T4, anti-TPO, thyroid ultrasound if goitre)** before amiodarone, lithium, ICIs, TKIs, interferon and alemtuzumab. [ETA 2018]`,`Amiodarone: **TSH, free T4 and free T3** at baseline, **at 3 months, then every 3-6 months**, and for at least **1-2 years after stopping**. [ETA 2018]`,`**Colour-flow Doppler** of the thyroid: increased vascularity in type 1 AIT, absent in type 2; **radioiodine uptake** is usually low in both in iodine-sufficient areas, so Doppler is more useful. [ETA 2018]`,`**TRAb** to detect Graves (AIT type 1, ICI, alemtuzumab); **anti-TPO** to predict AIH, lithium hypothyroidism and interferon thyroiditis. [ETA 2018]`,`ICIs: **TSH and free T4 before each cycle** (every 4-6 weeks) for the first 6 months, then every 12 weeks; **morning cortisol and ACTH** if TSH is low or normal with low free T4, plus pituitary MRI for headache. [ASCO 2021]`,`TKIs: **TSH at baseline and monthly** for the first 4-6 months, then every 2-3 months. [ESMO 2022]`,`Lithium: **TSH (and calcium, creatinine) at baseline, at 6 months, then every 6-12 months**. [NICE CG185]`,`After iodinated contrast in **high-risk patients** (MNG, latent Graves, elderly with heart disease) check TSH at **3-4 weeks**; infants 3 years or younger exposed to contrast need TFTs within **3 weeks**. [ETA 2021 contrast]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**General rule**: treat the thyroid and **continue the essential drug** (lithium, ICI, TKI, often amiodarone) - decide with the prescribing specialist. [ETA 2018]`,`**AIH**: start **levothyroxine** (25-50 microgram in cardiac patients, titrate to TSH in the normal range) and **continue amiodarone**. [ETA 2018]`,`**Lithium hypothyroidism or goitre**: **levothyroxine**; **do not stop lithium** for thyroid reasons. [NICE CG185]`,`**ICI thyroiditis, thyrotoxic phase**: **propranolol 10-40 mg every 6-8 hours** or atenolol for symptoms, **no thionamides**; recheck TFTs every 2-3 weeks to catch the hypothyroid phase. [ASCO 2021]`,`**ICI hypothyroidism**: levothyroxine when **TSH over 10** or symptomatic (about **1.6 microgram/kg/day**, lower start in the elderly or cardiac patients); ICI usually continues. [ASCO 2021]`,`**ICI hypophysitis**: **hydrocortisone first** (15-25 mg/day in divided doses for adrenal insufficiency), then **levothyroxine after 2-3 days** - giving thyroxine first can precipitate adrenal crisis. [ESMO 2022]`,`**TKI hypothyroidism**: levothyroxine to normal TSH; **continue the TKI**; raise levothyroxine in thyroidectomised patients starting imatinib or sorafenib. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Levothyroxine malabsorption**: take it **30-60 minutes before breakfast** with water (or at bedtime), **4 hours apart from calcium, iron, antacids and bile acid sequestrants**; check adherence, coeliac disease and H. pylori before escalating the dose. [ATA 2014 hypothyroidism]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and specialist management of AIT`,points:[`**AIT type 1**: **methimazole 40-60 mg/day** (or carbimazole equivalent) - high doses because the iodine-laden gland responds slowly; **potassium perchlorate 1 g/day for up to 4-6 weeks** may be added to block iodide uptake where available. [ETA 2018]`,`**AIT type 2**: **prednisolone 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day (about 30-40 mg)** for 2-4 weeks, then tapered over about **3 months**; response within 1-2 weeks supports the diagnosis. [ETA 2018]`,`**Mixed or uncertain AIT**: combine **methimazole and prednisolone** from the start, or start methimazole and add steroids if no response. [ETA 2018]`,`**Whether to stop amiodarone** is a cardiology decision: stopping helps type 1 but makes little difference in type 2, and the drug may be life-saving - ETA 2018 allows continuation in type 2 when needed. [ETA 2018]`,`**Urgent total thyroidectomy** for AIT refractory to medical therapy or with **deteriorating cardiac function** - mortality rises with prolonged thyrotoxicosis in poor ventricles. [ETA 2018]`,`**Radioiodine is usually not possible** after amiodarone because the iodine load keeps uptake low for months. [ETA 2018]`,`**Grade 3-4 ICI thyroid or pituitary toxicity**: admit, hold the ICI until stable, give stress-dose hydrocortisone if adrenal insufficiency is possible, and involve endocrinology. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: amiodarone is avoided (fetal hypothyroidism and goitre); lithium in pregnancy needs TSH each trimester; avoid povidone-iodine and contrast where possible. [ATA 2017 pregnancy]`,`**Neonates and infants** exposed to iodinated contrast or maternal iodine can develop transient hypothyroidism - check TFTs within 3 weeks of exposure in infants 3 years or younger. [FDA 2022]`,`**Elderly with heart disease**: start levothyroxine at **12.5-25 microgram/day** and titrate slowly; AIT carries high cardiac mortality in poor left ventricular function. [ETA 2018]`,`**Women on oral contraceptives or HRT** who are on levothyroxine need a TSH check 6-8 weeks after starting oestrogen (TBG rise). [ATA 2014 hypothyroidism]`,`**TB treatment**: rifampicin increases levothyroxine needs, and ethionamide and **PAS** (for drug-resistant TB) cause hypothyroidism - check TSH monthly to 3-monthly on DR-TB regimens. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Dronedarone** contains no iodine and has far fewer thyroid effects, but is less effective and contraindicated in advanced heart failure. [ESC AF 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**AIT** worsens arrhythmias and heart failure and carries **higher mortality in patients with low ejection fraction**; prompt control and early surgery in refractory cases improve outcome. [ETA 2018]`,`AIH is usually **permanent if TPO positive** but may remit after stopping amiodarone in antibody-negative patients. [ETA 2018]`,`**ICI thyroiditis** usually leads to **permanent hypothyroidism** but rarely forces stopping cancer therapy; several studies link it with **better tumour response**. [ASCO 2021]`,`**ICI hypophysitis** leaves **permanent ACTH deficiency** in most patients - lifelong hydrocortisone and a steroid card. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Missed central adrenal insufficiency** treated with levothyroxine alone can precipitate **adrenal crisis**. [ESMO 2022]`,`Unrecognised **levothyroxine malabsorption** leads to repeated dose escalation and then **iatrogenic thyrotoxicosis** when the interacting drug is stopped. [ATA 2014 hypothyroidism]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to endocrinology** for AIT, ICI-related central hypothyroidism or adrenal insufficiency, grade 3-4 toxicity, and thyrotoxicosis in pregnancy. [ASCO 2021]`,`Monitoring calendar: **amiodarone every 3-6 months**, **lithium every 6-12 months**, **ICI before each cycle**, **TKI monthly at first**, **alemtuzumab every 3 months for 4 years** after the last course. [ETA 2018]`,`**Prevent** contrast-induced thyrotoxicosis in high-risk patients (untreated Graves, autonomous nodules): avoid unnecessary contrast, check TSH first, and consider **prophylactic methimazole** in selected cases. [ETA 2021 contrast]`,`Before RAI scans or therapy, **delay 1-2 months after iodinated contrast** (longer after amiodarone) because iodine blocks uptake. [ETA 2021 contrast]`,`Counsel patients on **steroid sick-day rules and a steroid card** after hypophysitis, and on **timing levothyroxine** away from calcium, iron and PPIs. [Society for Endocrinology 2018]`,`Ask about **biotin supplements** before any thyroid test, and stop them 2-3 days before. [FDA 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ETA 2018 amiodarone guideline**: Doppler-based typing, **prednisolone for type 2**, methimazole (plus perchlorate) for type 1, combined therapy for mixed forms, and amiodarone may continue in type 2. [ETA 2018]`,`**ETA 2021 iodinated contrast guideline**: no routine screening or prophylaxis for all, but **TSH before contrast** and selective prophylaxis in high-risk patients, and follow-up TFTs. [ETA 2021 contrast]`,`**ASCO 2021** and **ESMO 2022** immune-related adverse event guidelines: continue ICIs for most thyroid toxicity, **beta-blockers not thionamides** for thyroiditis, **hydrocortisone before levothyroxine** in hypophysitis. [ESMO 2022]`,`**FDA 2022** added a recommendation to monitor thyroid function in children **3 years or younger** within 3 weeks of iodinated contrast. [FDA 2022]`,`**FDA 2019** safety communication on **biotin interference** in immunoassays, including thyroid tests and troponin. [FDA 2019]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Differentiate type 1 and type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis'** - mechanism, gland, Doppler, uptake, treatment. [ETA 2018]`,`Viva: **'How much iodine is in amiodarone?'** - 37% by weight; 75 mg per 200 mg tablet. [ETA 2018]`,`Common mistake: **treating the euthyroid amiodarone pattern** (high T4, normal TSH) as thyrotoxicosis. [ETA 2018]`,`Common mistake: **stopping lithium** because TSH is raised - treat with levothyroxine instead. [NICE CG185]`,`Common mistake: **giving levothyroxine before hydrocortisone** in ICI hypophysitis. [ESMO 2022]`,`Remember **'PD-1 hits the thyroid, CTLA-4 hits the pituitary'**. [ESMO 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Type 1 versus type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`AIT type 1`,`AIT type 2`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Iodine-induced excess synthesis (Jod-Basedow)`,`Destructive thyroiditis (direct toxicity)`],[`Underlying gland`,`Nodular goitre or latent Graves`,`Previously normal`],[`Onset`,`Often early`,`Often later, after 2-3 years`],[`Colour Doppler`,`Normal or increased vascularity`,`Absent vascularity`],[`Radioiodine uptake`,`Low, normal or raised`,`Very low (under 1-3%)`],[`TRAb`,`May be positive`,`Negative`],[`Treatment`,`Methimazole 40-60 mg/day plus perchlorate`,`Prednisolone 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day tapered over 3 months`],[`Later hypothyroidism`,`Uncommon`,`Possible`]]},{heading:`Drugs causing thyroid dysfunction`,columns:[`Drug`,`Main effect`,`Mechanism`,`Monitoring`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`Hypothyroidism or thyrotoxicosis`,`Iodine load, deiodinase block, destructive thyroiditis`,`TSH, FT4, FT3 baseline, 3 months, then 3-6 monthly`],[`Lithium`,`Goitre, hypothyroidism`,`Blocks hormone release`,`TSH baseline, 6 months, then 6-12 monthly`],[`Anti-PD-1 / PD-L1`,`Thyroiditis then hypothyroidism`,`T-cell autoimmunity`,`TSH, FT4 before each cycle`],[`Anti-CTLA-4 (ipilimumab)`,`Hypophysitis (central)`,`Pituitary autoimmunity`,`TSH, FT4, morning cortisol`],[`VEGF TKIs (sunitinib)`,`Hypothyroidism`,`Capillary regression, thyroiditis`,`TSH monthly for 4-6 months`],[`Interferon-alpha`,`Hypo or hyperthyroidism`,`Autoimmune and destructive`,`TSH every 3 months on therapy`],[`Alemtuzumab`,`Graves disease`,`Immune reconstitution autoimmunity`,`TSH every 3 months for 4 years`],[`Iodinated contrast`,`Thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism`,`Iodine excess`,`TSH at 3-4 weeks in high-risk; infants within 3 weeks`],[`Bexarotene`,`Central hypothyroidism`,`RXR-mediated TSH suppression`,`Free T4 (TSH unreliable)`]]},{heading:`Drugs used to treat drug-induced thyroid dysfunction`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day orally; 12.5-25 microgram in elderly cardiac`,`Titrate every 6-8 weeks to TSH`,`Angina and AF if over-replaced; give after hydrocortisone in hypophysitis`],[`Methimazole / carbimazole`,`Methimazole 40-60 mg/day (carbimazole about 60 mg/day) orally`,`Until euthyroid; often months in AIT type 1`,`Agranulocytosis, hepatitis, rash`],[`Potassium perchlorate`,`1 g/day orally in divided doses`,`Maximum 4-6 weeks`,`Aplastic anaemia, nephrotic syndrome; limited availability`],[`Prednisolone`,`0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day (about 30-40 mg) orally`,`2-4 weeks then taper over about 3 months`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection, osteoporosis; recurrence on rapid taper`],[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg orally every 6-8 hours`,`During thyrotoxic phase`,`Asthma, heart block; caution in decompensated heart failure`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`15-25 mg/day orally in 2-3 doses; stress doses when ill`,`Lifelong in persistent ACTH deficiency`,`Must precede levothyroxine; sick-day rules`]]},{heading:`Common causes of reduced levothyroxine effect`,columns:[`Cause`,`Mechanism`,`Action`],rows:[[`Calcium, iron, antacids, sucralfate`,`Bind levothyroxine in gut`,`Separate by 4 hours`],[`Proton pump inhibitors, H. pylori, atrophic gastritis`,`Less gastric acid, poor dissolution`,`Recheck TSH; treat H. pylori; consider liquid or softgel form`],[`Bile acid sequestrants, sevelamer, orlistat`,`Binding or fat malabsorption`,`Separate by 4 hours or more`],[`Rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, imatinib`,`Faster hepatic metabolism`,`Increase dose; TSH 6-8 weeks after start and stop`],[`Oestrogen (OCP, HRT, pregnancy)`,`Higher TBG`,`Increase dose; check TSH`],[`Coffee, soy, high-fibre breakfast`,`Reduced absorption`,`Take on empty stomach 30-60 minutes before food`],[`Coeliac disease`,`Small bowel malabsorption`,`tTG-IgA; gluten-free diet`]]}],redFlags:[`Recurrent ventricular tachycardia, new AF or worsening heart failure on amiodarone with suppressed TSH - AIT`,`Hypotension, vomiting, hyponatraemia or severe headache on an immune checkpoint inhibitor - hypophysitis with adrenal insufficiency`,`Low free T4 with low or normal TSH on ipilimumab - check cortisol before giving levothyroxine`,`Fever or sore throat on high-dose methimazole or perchlorate - agranulocytosis`,`Thyrotoxicosis in a patient with poor ejection fraction - consider urgent thyroidectomy`,`Severe hypothyroidism with drowsiness or hypothermia on lithium or amiodarone - myxoedema coma`,`Thyrotoxicosis after contrast CT in an elderly patient with multinodular goitre and heart disease`],pearls:[`**Amiodarone is 37% iodine** - one 200 mg tablet carries about 75 mg iodine.`,`**AIT type 1 = Iodine (synthesis), type 2 = Inflammation (destruction).** Type 1 gets thionamide, type 2 gets steroid.`,`**Doppler decides**: vascular gland type 1, silent gland type 2.`,`**High T4, normal TSH on amiodarone is not thyrotoxicosis.**`,`**Do not stop lithium for hypothyroidism** - add levothyroxine.`,`**PD-1 hits the thyroid, CTLA-4 hits the pituitary.**`,`**Hydrocortisone before thyroxine** in any central hypothyroidism.`,`**Biotin fakes Graves**: low TSH, high T4 and TRAb on streptavidin assays.`,`Levothyroxine: **empty stomach, 4 hours from calcium and iron**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old man has taken amiodarone 200 mg daily for 2 years. He now has recurrent AF and weight loss. TSH is suppressed, free T4 and free T3 are high, the thyroid is not enlarged, colour Doppler shows absent vascularity and TRAb is negative. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Radioiodine ablation`,`Prednisolone 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/day tapered over about 3 months`,`Levothyroxine`,`Potassium iodide (Lugol solution)`],answer:1,explanation:`A normal gland with absent Doppler vascularity and negative TRAb indicates type 2 AIT, a destructive thyroiditis that responds to glucocorticoids. Radioiodine is not feasible because the iodine load keeps uptake very low, levothyroxine would worsen thyrotoxicosis, and more iodine adds to the problem rather than blocking a destructive process.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman on lithium for bipolar disorder for 5 years has TSH 12 mIU/L with low free T4 and a small diffuse goitre. Her mood is stable. What is the best management?`,options:[`Start levothyroxine and continue lithium`,`Stop lithium and switch to another mood stabiliser`,`Start carbimazole`,`Observe without treatment, as this is a laboratory artefact`],answer:0,explanation:`Lithium blocks thyroid hormone release, causing goitre and hypothyroidism; the standard approach is levothyroxine while continuing an effective mood stabiliser. Stopping lithium risks relapse and is not needed for thyroid reasons, carbimazole would worsen hypothyroidism, and a raised TSH with low free T4 is overt hypothyroidism, not an artefact.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old man with melanoma on ipilimumab plus nivolumab has severe fatigue, headache and BP 90/60 mmHg. Sodium is 128 mmol/L, free T4 is low and TSH is 0.4 mIU/L. What should be done first?`,options:[`Start levothyroxine 100 microgram daily`,`Start methimazole for thyroiditis`,`Check cortisol and give hydrocortisone before any levothyroxine`,`Permanently stop all immunotherapy and observe`],answer:2,explanation:`Low free T4 with inappropriately low TSH, hypotension and hyponatraemia point to ICI hypophysitis with central adrenal insufficiency, which needs hydrocortisone first; levothyroxine alone speeds cortisol clearance and can precipitate adrenal crisis. Methimazole has no role in central hypothyroidism, and hypophysitis is managed with hormone replacement rather than routinely stopping immunotherapy for good.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-q4`,stem:`A woman on levothyroxine 100 microgram daily with previously normal TSH now has TSH 9 mIU/L. Which recent change is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Starting amlodipine for hypertension`,`Starting paracetamol for knee pain`,`Stopping a combined oral contraceptive pill`,`Starting calcium carbonate and ferrous sulphate taken with her morning tablet`],answer:3,explanation:`Calcium and iron bind levothyroxine in the gut and reduce absorption, so taking them together raises TSH; they should be separated by about 4 hours. Amlodipine and paracetamol do not interfere, and stopping an oral contraceptive lowers TBG, which reduces rather than increases levothyroxine requirement.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-q5`,stem:`A patient on amiodarone for 2 months has TSH 4.2 mIU/L (normal), free T4 slightly above the upper limit and free T3 low-normal. He feels well. What is the correct interpretation?`,options:[`Type 1 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis needing methimazole`,`Expected euthyroid effect of amiodarone from deiodinase inhibition; continue monitoring`,`Type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis needing prednisolone`,`Central hypothyroidism needing levothyroxine`],answer:1,explanation:`Amiodarone inhibits type 1 deiodinase, so T4 and reverse T3 rise and T3 falls, with a normal or transiently raised TSH; this is the euthyroid amiodarone pattern and needs only monitoring. Either type of thyrotoxicosis would suppress TSH and raise T3, and central hypothyroidism would show a low free T4, not a high one.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Enumerate drugs that cause thyroid dysfunction with their mechanisms. Discuss the effects of amiodarone on the thyroid and the management of amiodarone-induced thyroid dysfunction.`,openingLines:[`Drugs affect the thyroid through iodine load, blocked hormone release, induced autoimmunity, destructive thyroiditis, TSH suppression and altered levothyroxine absorption or clearance.`,`Amiodarone, 37% iodine by weight, causes dysfunction in about 15-20% of users - hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient and thyrotoxicosis in iodine-deficient areas.`],answer:[{heading:`Drugs and mechanisms`,points:[`Iodine: amiodarone, contrast, povidone-iodine.`,`Release block: lithium. Autoimmunity: interferon, ICIs, alemtuzumab.`,`Destruction: amiodarone type 2, ICIs, TKIs. TSH suppression: steroids, dopamine, octreotide, bexarotene.`,`Absorption and clearance: calcium, iron, PPIs; rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine.`]},{heading:`Amiodarone pharmacology`,points:[`75 mg iodine per 200 mg tablet; half-life 50-100 days.`,`Deiodinase block: T4 up, T3 down, TSH transiently up - euthyroid pattern.`]},{heading:`Amiodarone-induced hypothyroidism`,points:[`Failure to escape Wolff-Chaikoff, usually TPO positive.`,`Levothyroxine; continue amiodarone.`]},{heading:`Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis`,points:[`Type 1: abnormal gland, increased Doppler flow; methimazole 40-60 mg plus perchlorate.`,`Type 2: normal gland, absent flow; prednisolone 0.5-0.7 mg/kg tapered over 3 months.`,`Mixed: both; refractory or failing heart: total thyroidectomy.`,`Amiodarone continuation decided with cardiology.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and prevention`,points:[`TSH, FT4, FT3 baseline, 3 months, then 3-6 monthly and 1-2 years after stopping.`,`Baseline ultrasound and TPO; consider dronedarone where suitable.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: AIT type 1 versus type 2`,`Flow chart: abnormal TSH on amiodarone to AIH or AIT typing to treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Drugs and mechanisms`,marks:3},{part:`Amiodarone effects and AIH`,marks:2},{part:`AIT typing and treatment`,marks:4},{part:`Monitoring`,marks:1}],keywords:[`amiodarone`,`AIT type 1`,`AIT type 2`,`Wolff-Chaikoff`,`lithium`,`ICI`,`deiodinase`]},{id:`endocrine-drug-induced-thyroid-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on endocrine adverse effects of immune checkpoint inhibitors affecting the thyroid and pituitary.`,openingLines:[`Immune checkpoint inhibitors release T-cell brakes and cause immune-related adverse events; thyroid dysfunction is the commonest endocrine one.`,`Anti-PD-1/PD-L1 mainly cause thyroiditis; anti-CTLA-4 mainly causes hypophysitis.`],answer:[{heading:`Thyroiditis`,points:[`Painless thyrotoxic phase at 4-8 weeks, then hypothyroidism in most.`,`Beta-blocker only for thyrotoxic phase; levothyroxine if TSH over 10 or symptomatic.`,`ICI usually continued.`]},{heading:`Hypophysitis`,points:[`Headache, fatigue, hypotension, hyponatraemia; low FT4 with low or normal TSH.`,`Morning cortisol, ACTH, MRI; **hydrocortisone before levothyroxine**; steroid card.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`TSH and FT4 before each cycle for 6 months, then 3-monthly; cortisol if central pattern.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: abnormal TFT on ICI - primary versus central pattern`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and incidence`,marks:1},{part:`Thyroiditis`,marks:2},{part:`Hypophysitis and monitoring`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pembrolizumab`,`nivolumab`,`ipilimumab`,`thyroiditis`,`hypophysitis`,`hydrocortisone`]}],references:[`Bartalena L, et al. 2018 European Thyroid Association (ETA) Guidelines for the Management of Amiodarone-Associated Thyroid Dysfunction. Eur Thyroid J, 2018`,`Bednarczuk T, et al. 2021 European Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Management of Iodine-Based Contrast Media-Induced Thyroid Dysfunction. Eur Thyroid J, 2021`,`Schneider BJ, et al. Management of Immune-Related Adverse Events in Patients Treated With Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy: ASCO Guideline Update. J Clin Oncol, 2021`,`Haanen J, et al. Management of toxicities from immunotherapy: ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline. Ann Oncol, 2022`,`Jonklaas J, et al. Guidelines for the Treatment of Hypothyroidism: American Thyroid Association, 2014`,`NICE Clinical Guideline CG185: Bipolar disorder - assessment and management, 2014 (updated 2023)`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach`,title:`Pituitary tumours and incidentalomas: mass effects and hormonal work-up`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`A **pituitary adenoma (now pituitary neuroendocrine tumour, PitNET, WHO 2022)** is a benign tumour of the anterior pituitary that presents by **hormone excess** (prolactin, GH, ACTH, rarely TSH), **hypopituitarism** or **mass effect** (headache, **bitemporal hemianopia**, cranial nerve palsy); every lesion - including an **incidentaloma** - needs **prolactin and IGF-1**, a **pituitary function screen if 10 mm or more**, **visual fields if it touches the chiasm**, and **transsphenoidal surgery** for visual loss or any functioning tumour except a prolactinoma.`,keywords:[`pituitary adenoma`,`PitNET`,`pituitary neuroendocrine tumour`,`pituitary incidentaloma`,`non-functioning pituitary adenoma`,`NFPA`,`macroadenoma`,`microadenoma`,`bitemporal hemianopia`,`optic chiasm`,`stalk effect`,`Knosp grade`,`transsphenoidal surgery`,`endoscopic endonasal`,`craniopharyngioma`,`Rathke cleft cyst`,`sellar mass`,`cavernous sinus`,`visual field perimetry`,`pituitary MRI`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`A **pituitary adenoma** is a benign monoclonal tumour of anterior pituitary cells; the **WHO 2022 (5th edition)** classification renames it **pituitary neuroendocrine tumour (PitNET)** and types it by **transcription-factor lineage** (PIT1, TPIT, SF1, or none). [WHO 2022]`,`**Microadenoma** is under 10 mm, **macroadenoma** 10 mm or more and **giant adenoma** 40 mm or more; size drives the work-up because mass effects and hypopituitarism are almost confined to macroadenomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clinically relevant pituitary adenomas** have a prevalence of about **1 in 1000** (roughly 80-115 per 100,000) in population studies, far higher than once believed. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **pituitary incidentaloma** is a lesion found on imaging done for an unrelated reason; incidental lesions appear in about **10% of normal MRI scans** and in a similar proportion at autopsy, nearly all microadenomas. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`Relative frequency among clinically apparent tumours: **prolactinoma about 40-50%**, **non-functioning (mostly gonadotroph) about 30-35%**, **somatotroph 10-15%**, **corticotroph 5-10%**, **thyrotroph under 1%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Sellar masses are the **third commonest intracranial tumour** after meningioma and glioma; Indian neurosurgical series show patients present late, with **macroadenomas and visual loss in the majority**, because headache and menstrual change are ignored. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Pituitary carcinoma** - renamed **metastatic PitNET** in WHO 2022 - is defined only by craniospinal or distant metastasis and is very rare (well under 1%). [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Most adenomas are **sporadic**; somatic **GNAS** mutations (gsp oncogene) occur in up to 40% of somatotroph tumours and **USP8** mutations in about half of corticotroph tumours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MEN1** (menin, chromosome 11q13): pituitary adenoma (commonly prolactinoma) with **primary hyperparathyroidism and pancreatic NETs** - ask about kidney stones, peptic ulcer and hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AIP mutation (familial isolated pituitary adenoma)**: young patients, aggressive somatotroph or lacto-somatotroph macroadenomas - test anyone with a pituitary adenoma before age 30 or with gigantism. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Other syndromes: **Carney complex** (PRKAR1A, spotty pigmentation, myxomas), **McCune-Albright** (GNAS mosaic, cafe-au-lait, fibrous dysplasia), **MEN4** (CDKN1B), **X-LAG** (GPR101 duplication, infant gigantism) and SDH-related paraganglioma syndromes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pituitary hyperplasia** (not tumour) follows **long-standing primary hypothyroidism** (TRH drive), **pregnancy** (lactotroph hyperplasia, gland up to 10-12 mm) and puberty - a symmetric enlarged gland that shrinks with treatment. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Radiation exposure in childhood and prior cranial surgery are weak risk factors; unlike many tumours, there is **no proven lifestyle cause**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: how a sellar mass causes symptoms`,points:[`**Mass effect upward**: the optic chiasm lies about 10 mm above the diaphragma sellae; a tumour growing up compresses the **crossing inferonasal fibres first**, giving **superior bitemporal quadrantanopia**, then full **bitemporal hemianopia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **pre-fixed or post-fixed chiasm** changes the pattern - optic nerve compression gives a **central scotoma or junctional scotoma**, and optic tract compression gives **homonymous hemianopia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lateral extension into the cavernous sinus** can involve **CN III (commonest), IV, VI, V1 and V2**; slow growth rarely causes palsy, so a sudden palsy suggests **apoplexy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypopituitarism by compression**: the portal vessels and normal gland are squeezed, and hormones fail in the order **GH, then LH/FSH, then TSH, then ACTH** - prolactin rises rather than falls. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stalk effect**: compression of the stalk interrupts **dopamine** carriage from the hypothalamus, releasing lactotrophs from tonic inhibition - prolactin rises modestly, usually **under 100 ng/mL** (often quoted up to 150). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Headache** comes from stretching of the dura and diaphragma sellae and does not correlate with tumour size; **CSF rhinorrhoea** follows downward erosion of the sellar floor, often after dopamine-agonist shrinkage. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypothalamic invasion** (craniopharyngioma, giant tumours) disturbs appetite, temperature, sleep and thirst; **posterior pituitary failure (AVP deficiency) is rare in adenomas** and points instead to craniopharyngioma, hypophysitis, metastasis or granuloma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**By size**: micro (under 10 mm), macro (10 mm or more), giant (40 mm or more). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By function**: functioning (prolactinoma, somatotroph - acromegaly, corticotroph - Cushing disease, thyrotroph - central hyperthyroidism, rare gonadotroph) and **non-functioning** (clinically silent; most are SF1 gonadotroph). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By lineage (WHO 2022)**: **PIT1** (somatotroph, lactotroph, thyrotroph, mammosomatotroph), **TPIT** (corticotroph), **SF1** (gonadotroph), and **null cell** (no lineage marker). [WHO 2022]`,`**Knosp grade (0-4)** describes cavernous sinus invasion on coronal MRI relative to the intercavernous carotid lines; grade 3-4 means invasion and **lower surgical cure**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hardy classification** grades sellar floor erosion (I-IV) and suprasellar extension (A-E) - an older radiological scheme still asked in viva. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**High-risk subtypes** (WHO 2022): sparsely granulated somatotroph, silent corticotroph, Crooke cell, **immature PIT1-lineage** and male lactotroph tumours - more aggressive and recurrent. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Headache** (frontal, retro-orbital, chronic) is the commonest mass symptom; a **sudden thunderclap headache with vomiting and visual loss** is **pituitary apoplexy** until proved otherwise - see the Hypopituitarism and Sheehan topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visual symptoms**: bumping into objects or people on both sides, trouble driving, missing words at the ends of lines - patients often go first to an optician, so ask about recent spectacle changes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypogonadism** is the earliest endocrine clue: **oligo/amenorrhoea, infertility, galactorrhoea** in women; **low libido and erectile dysfunction** in men (who therefore present later with bigger tumours). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hormone-excess clues**: change in ring or shoe size and sweating (acromegaly), weight gain with bruising and proximal weakness (Cushing disease), palpitations with a goitre and non-suppressed TSH (TSHoma). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypopituitary symptoms**: fatigue, cold intolerance, pallor, loss of body hair, postural dizziness, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia - often vague and attributed to anaemia or depression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diplopia or ptosis** (CN III), facial numbness (V1/V2) and **clear nasal drip** (CSF leak - test for beta-2 transferrin) are less common but important. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Ask for **MEN1 symptoms** (kidney stones, peptic ulcers, hypoglycaemia), **family history** of pituitary tumours, and **drugs** that raise prolactin (levosulpiride, risperidone, metoclopramide). [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Visual fields by confrontation with a red pin** (red desaturation is the earliest sign); test each eye separately and each quadrant - a **superior temporal defect** is the classic early finding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visual acuity and fundoscopy**: **optic atrophy (pale disc)** means long-standing compression and predicts poorer recovery after surgery; papilloedema is rare. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Eye movements and pupils** for CN III palsy (ptosis, eye down and out, dilated pupil) and **corneal reflex/facial sensation** for V1-V2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of excess**: acromegalic facies, broad spade hands, prognathism; Cushingoid striae, proximal myopathy; **galactorrhoea on gentle expression**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of deficiency**: fine wrinkled pale skin (**alabaster skin**), sparse axillary and pubic hair, small soft testes, postural BP drop. [Harrison 22e]`,`Measure **BP, BMI, testicular volume (orchidometer)** and look for a **goitre** - a goitre with a pituitary mass raises TSHoma or acromegaly. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a sellar mass`,points:[`**Craniopharyngioma**: bimodal age (children 5-14, adults 50-75), **suprasellar calcification and cyst** with 'machinery-oil' fluid, AVP deficiency and growth failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rathke cleft cyst**: non-enhancing intrasellar cyst between anterior and posterior lobes, often with an intracystic nodule; usually asymptomatic and followed with MRI. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypophysitis** (lymphocytic in late pregnancy or postpartum, IgG4-related, **immune checkpoint inhibitor - ipilimumab**): thick stalk, symmetric enhancement, **ACTH deficiency and AVP deficiency** out of proportion to size. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Meningioma** (dural tail, hyperostosis, normal pituitary seen separately) and **internal carotid aneurysm** - an aneurysm must be **excluded with MR or CT angiography** before any transsphenoidal approach. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Granulomas** (tuberculosis - important in India, sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis) and **metastasis** (breast, lung; thick stalk with AVP deficiency in an older patient). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Physiological enlargement and hyperplasia**: pregnancy, puberty, and **primary hypothyroidism** - always check TSH before calling an enlarged pituitary a tumour. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Germinoma** in a child or young adult with AVP deficiency and a thick stalk; check serum and CSF beta-hCG and AFP. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**MRI pituitary with gadolinium (dedicated thin coronal and sagittal cuts)** is the imaging test; a microadenoma is hypointense on the early dynamic T1 post-contrast images. A routine brain MRI may miss it. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Hypersecretion screen in every pituitary lesion**: **serum prolactin** and **age- and sex-matched IGF-1**; test for Cushing (1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression, late-night salivary cortisol or 24-h urine free cortisol) **only when clinically suspected** per the 2011 guideline, though many centres screen all. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Hypopituitarism screen** in macroadenomas (and microadenomas 6-9 mm if symptomatic): **8-9 am cortisol, free T4 with TSH, LH/FSH with testosterone or oestradiol, IGF-1, serum sodium**. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Read central hypothyroidism correctly**: **low free T4 with a low or 'normal' TSH** - TSH alone misses it. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Prolactin pitfalls**: with a macroadenoma and only modest prolactin, **re-assay at 1:100 dilution** to exclude the **hook effect**; a level **over 250 ng/mL** almost always means prolactinoma, while **under 100 ng/mL** with a macroadenoma suggests stalk effect. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Formal visual fields** (Humphrey 24-2 automated or Goldmann perimetry) for any lesion **abutting or compressing the chiasm**; **OCT of the retinal nerve fibre layer** predicts visual recovery after decompression. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**CT** shows calcification (craniopharyngioma) and sellar bone erosion and is used when MRI is contraindicated; **CT or MR angiography** if aneurysm is possible. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Advanced: **inferior petrosal sinus sampling** for ACTH-dependent Cushing with an equivocal MRI, **PET with 68Ga-DOTATATE or 11C-methionine** for residual or occult tumours, and **genetic testing** (MEN1, AIP) in young or familial cases. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Prolactinoma** (even macro with visual loss) is treated **medically first with cabergoline** - see the Hyperprolactinaemia and prolactinoma topic. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Microincidentaloma, non-functioning**: no treatment; **repeat MRI at 1 year**, then every 1-2 years for 3 years, then less often; no routine hormone testing unless symptoms. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Macroincidentaloma, non-functioning, not touching the chiasm**: **MRI at 6 months**, then yearly for 3 years, then less often; **hormone tests at 6 months, then yearly**; visual fields if it grows towards the chiasm. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Replace deficient hormones before surgery**: glucocorticoid first (hydrocortisone 15-20 mg/day in divided doses), then thyroxine - never thyroxine first in suspected ACTH deficiency. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Teach steroid sick-day rules** and give a **steroid alert card** to anyone with ACTH deficiency. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Counsel on driving**: a patient with a bitemporal field defect may not meet the visual field standard for driving until fields are reassessed. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Refer all macroadenomas** and every functioning or growing lesion to an endocrinologist working with a **pituitary neurosurgeon** (Pituitary Tumour Centre of Excellence model). [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Surgery, radiotherapy and inpatient care`,points:[`**Indications for surgery** (Endocrine Society 2011): **visual field defect**, ophthalmoplegia or other visual loss from the tumour, **lesion abutting or compressing the chiasm**, **apoplexy with visual disturbance**, and **hypersecreting tumours other than prolactinoma**. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Relative indications**: significant growth, loss of pituitary function, a lesion close to the chiasm in a woman **planning pregnancy**, and unremitting headache. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Endoscopic endonasal transsphenoidal surgery** is the standard approach; **transcranial** surgery is kept for large suprasellar or lateral extension not reachable from below. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Perioperative steroids**: give hydrocortisone cover (e.g. **50-100 mg IV at induction**) if ACTH deficiency is known or suspected; centres that use an intact-axis protocol withhold it and check **morning cortisol on post-op day 1-3**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Watch sodium daily for 10 days**: transient **AVP deficiency** (polyuria in the first 1-2 days) and **delayed SIADH (day 5-10)** - the **triphasic response**; advise **fluid restriction to about 1-1.5 L/day** in the first week after discharge in many centres. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Other complications: **CSF leak and meningitis**, new hypopituitarism, epistaxis, sinusitis, and rare **carotid artery injury**; mortality in expert centres is under 0.5%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Radiotherapy** (stereotactic radiosurgery or fractionated stereotactic RT) for residual or recurrent growing tumour; tumour control exceeds 90% but **hypopituitarism develops in 30-50% over 10 years**, so lifelong yearly testing is needed. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Aggressive PitNET**: after surgery and RT, **temozolomide** is first-line chemotherapy (ESE 2018); **dopamine agonists do not shrink most non-functioning tumours**. [ESE 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: the normal pituitary enlarges up to 10-12 mm; known macroadenomas may grow - **visual fields each trimester**, MRI without gadolinium only if symptoms appear. [ESE 2021]`,`**Children and adolescents**: think **craniopharyngioma** and germinoma; a pituitary adenoma under 30 years warrants **AIP and MEN1 genetic testing**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Elderly**: many non-functioning macroadenomas grow slowly - observation is reasonable if fields are normal and comorbidity makes surgery risky. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Primary hypothyroidism with a 'pituitary mass'**: treat with levothyroxine and repeat MRI in 3-6 months - hyperplasia regresses, avoiding needless surgery. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Patients on immune checkpoint inhibitors** with headache and fatigue: check morning cortisol and sodium - **ipilimumab hypophysitis** affects up to about 10%, anti-PD-1 drugs cause isolated ACTH deficiency. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if MRI is not available, a **contrast CT of the sella** plus **confrontation fields and prolactin** allows triage; refer visual loss the same week. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Visual recovery** after decompression occurs in about **two-thirds to three-quarters**; poor predictors are **long duration, optic atrophy and thin RNFL on OCT**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Non-functioning macroadenoma** after surgery recurs or regrows in about **15-40% at 10 years** without RT, depending on residual tumour - lifelong MRI follow-up is needed. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Natural history of incidentalomas**: about **10% of microincidentalomas** and **about 20-25% of macroincidentalomas** enlarge over years; apoplexy risk is about **0.2-0.6 per 100 patient-years**, higher in macroadenomas. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Untreated hypopituitarism** raises mortality, mainly through **unrecognised adrenal crisis**, cardiovascular disease and possibly GH deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Apoplexy** may be the first presentation in up to 80% of affected patients; it can cause blindness, coma and adrenal crisis. [SfE 2011]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Same-day referral**: sudden headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia, falling visual acuity, hypotension or hyponatraemia with a pituitary mass. [SfE 2011]`,`**Post-operative**: endocrine reassessment at **6-12 weeks**, **MRI at 3-4 months**, then yearly MRI for at least 5 years and less often after. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Visual fields** at 3 months after surgery and with any MRI growth or symptoms. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Yearly pituitary function testing** after radiotherapy, lifelong. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Primary-care role**: check adherence to hydrocortisone and thyroxine, sick-day rules, BMD and cardiovascular risk, and that a steroid card is carried. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Stop surveillance** of a non-functioning microincidentaloma that is stable for 3-5 years, if no new symptoms. [Endocrine Society 2011]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`There is **no primary prevention**; the aim is **early detection** - think of the pituitary in amenorrhoea-galactorrhoea, male low libido, unexplained bitemporal field loss and hyponatraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Screen relatives** of MEN1 or AIP carriers (prolactin, IGF-1, calcium, and MRI by protocol). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Explain the word 'tumour'**: most are benign, not cancer, and many incidentalomas need only scans. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`Under **PM-JAY**, transsphenoidal surgery is covered at empanelled neurosurgery centres; refer through the district hospital. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2022 (5th edition)**: adenoma renamed **PitNET**; classification by **PIT1/TPIT/SF1 lineage**; **pituitary carcinoma renamed metastatic PitNET**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Pituitary Society 2023 prolactinoma consensus**: surgery accepted as an **alternative first-line option** for selected well-circumscribed prolactinomas in expert centres. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**ESE/Endocrine Society 2024 adrenal insufficiency (glucocorticoid-induced) guideline**: lower cortisol cut-offs with newer **monoclonal immunoassays and LC-MS/MS** - peak cortisol about **14-15 microgram/dL** after Synacthen rather than the old 18. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`The **Endocrine Society 2011 incidentaloma guideline** remains the reference: prolactin and IGF-1 for all, function screen for macro, fields if near the chiasm. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Pituitary Tumour Centres of Excellence** (Pituitary Society): concentration of surgery in high-volume units with a dedicated surgeon improves cure and lowers complications. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: 'A woman with amenorrhoea, headache and bitemporal hemianopia - prolactin 80 ng/mL, MRI 25 mm mass' - answer: **stalk effect from a non-functioning macroadenoma** (or hook effect - dilute), not a prolactinoma. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Upper quadrants first** for pituitary (inferior chiasmal compression); **lower quadrants first** for craniopharyngioma (pressing from above). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Order of hormone loss**: 'Go Look For The Adrenal' - **GH, LH, FSH, TSH, ACTH**; prolactin goes up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving thyroxine before hydrocortisone in central hypothyroidism - it can precipitate **adrenal crisis**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Common mistake**: using TSH to monitor central hypothyroidism - use **free T4 (upper half of normal)**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**AVP deficiency at presentation** argues against an adenoma - think craniopharyngioma, hypophysitis, metastasis, TB. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Classification of pituitary adenomas (PitNETs)`,columns:[`Type (lineage)`,`Share of tumours`,`Hormone / syndrome`,`Screening test`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Lactotroph (PIT1)`,`40-50%`,`Prolactin - amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea, hypogonadism`,`Serum prolactin (dilute if macro)`,`Cabergoline`],[`Non-functioning / gonadotroph (SF1) or null cell`,`30-35%`,`None - mass effect, hypopituitarism`,`Normal hormones; mild prolactin rise`,`Surgery if near chiasm, else watch`],[`Somatotroph (PIT1)`,`10-15%`,`GH - acromegaly, gigantism`,`IGF-1, GH on 75 g OGTT`,`Transsphenoidal surgery`],[`Corticotroph (TPIT)`,`5-10%`,`ACTH - Cushing disease`,`1 mg DST, late-night salivary cortisol, UFC`,`Transsphenoidal surgery`],[`Thyrotroph (PIT1)`,`Under 1%`,`TSH - central hyperthyroidism, goitre`,`High FT4 with non-suppressed TSH`,`Surgery; somatostatin analogue`]]},{heading:`Work-up of a pituitary incidentaloma (Endocrine Society 2011)`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Hormone tests`,`Visual fields`,`MRI follow-up`,`Surgery if`],rows:[[`Microincidentaloma (under 10 mm)`,`Prolactin, IGF-1; cortisol tests if Cushingoid`,`Not needed`,`1 year, then every 1-2 years for 3 years`,`Hypersecretion (not prolactin) or growth`],[`Macroincidentaloma, away from chiasm`,`Prolactin, IGF-1, cortisol, FT4/TSH, LH/FSH, sex steroid`,`Baseline if near chiasm`,`6 months, then yearly for 3 years`,`Growth, new deficiency, pregnancy plans`],[`Macroincidentaloma touching chiasm`,`Full panel; dilute prolactin`,`Formal perimetry now`,`Per surgical plan`,`Field defect or chiasmal compression`],[`Cystic lesion (Rathke cleft)`,`Prolactin, IGF-1; function if large`,`If suprasellar`,`1 year, then spaced out`,`Visual loss or rapid growth`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a sellar mass`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Clue in history`,`MRI or CT clue`,`Hormone clue`],rows:[[`Pituitary macroadenoma`,`Adult, slow headache, hypogonadism`,`Snowman shape, displaces gland`,`AVP deficiency rare; prolactin under 100 (stalk)`],[`Craniopharyngioma`,`Child or older adult, growth failure`,`Suprasellar cyst with calcification on CT`,`AVP deficiency common`],[`Rathke cleft cyst`,`Usually incidental`,`Non-enhancing intrasellar cyst`,`Usually normal`],[`Lymphocytic hypophysitis`,`Late pregnancy or postpartum, severe headache`,`Thick stalk, symmetric enhancement`,`Early ACTH and AVP deficiency`],[`Meningioma`,`Older woman, visual loss`,`Dural tail, separate pituitary`,`Usually normal`],[`Metastasis or TB`,`Known cancer; TB contact, fever`,`Thick stalk, ring enhancement (TB)`,`AVP deficiency`],[`Pituitary hyperplasia`,`Untreated hypothyroidism or pregnancy`,`Symmetric diffuse enlargement`,`High TSH (hypothyroidism)`]]},{heading:`Drugs used around pituitary tumours`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cabergoline (prolactinoma)`,`0.25-0.5 mg orally once or twice a week`,`Increase monthly by prolactin; most need 2 mg/week or less`,`Nausea, postural hypotension, impulse control disorder; valvulopathy at high dose`],[`Hydrocortisone (ACTH deficiency)`,`15-20 mg/day orally in 2-3 doses (e.g. 10-5-5 mg)`,`Lifelong; double or triple on sick days`,`Over-replacement: weight gain, osteoporosis, diabetes`],[`Hydrocortisone (perioperative/apoplexy)`,`100 mg IV bolus, then 50 mg IV 6-hourly or 200 mg/24 h infusion`,`Taper to oral over days once stable`,`Hyperglycaemia; do not delay for tests`],[`Levothyroxine (central hypothyroidism)`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day orally (start 25-50 microgram in elderly)`,`Titrate to free T4 in upper half of normal`,`Start only after glucocorticoid cover`],[`Desmopressin (post-op AVP deficiency)`,`1-2 microgram SC/IV or 50-100 microgram oral or 60-120 microgram melt`,`Give only for proven polyuria with rising sodium`,`Hyponatraemia - avoid fixed regular doses early post-op`],[`Temozolomide (aggressive PitNET)`,`150-200 mg/m2 orally days 1-5 every 28 days`,`At least 3 cycles, assess response`,`Myelosuppression, nausea; specialist only`]]},{heading:`Visual field patterns and site of lesion`,columns:[`Field defect`,`Site`,`Typical cause`],rows:[[`Superior bitemporal quadrantanopia`,`Chiasm from below`,`Pituitary macroadenoma (early)`],[`Inferior bitemporal quadrantanopia`,`Chiasm from above`,`Craniopharyngioma, suprasellar meningioma`],[`Complete bitemporal hemianopia`,`Body of chiasm`,`Large pituitary tumour`],[`Junctional scotoma (central scotoma one eye + superotemporal defect other)`,`Optic nerve-chiasm junction`,`Tumour with post-fixed chiasm`],[`Homonymous hemianopia`,`Optic tract`,`Tumour with pre-fixed chiasm`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden severe (thunderclap) headache with vomiting, visual loss or ophthalmoplegia - pituitary apoplexy`,`Falling visual acuity or rapidly spreading field defect`,`Hypotension, vomiting, hyponatraemia or hypoglycaemia in a patient with a pituitary mass - adrenal crisis`,`Clear watery nasal discharge after starting cabergoline or after surgery - CSF leak, risk of meningitis`,`Polyuria with rising sodium after transsphenoidal surgery, or sodium under 130 mmol/L in the second week`,`Fever, neck stiffness and drowsiness after pituitary surgery - meningitis`,`New third nerve palsy with a dilated pupil`],pearls:[`Every pituitary lesion: **prolactin plus IGF-1**; every macro: **full function screen**; every chiasm contact: **perimetry**.`,`**Prolactin under 100 with a macroadenoma = stalk effect** (or hook effect - ask for 1:100 dilution).`,`**Steroids before thyroxine** - always.`,`Monitor central hypothyroidism with **free T4**, not TSH.`,`**AVP deficiency at diagnosis is not an adenoma** until proved - craniopharyngioma, hypophysitis, metastasis, TB.`,`**Exclude an aneurysm** before anyone goes through the sphenoid.`,`Prolactinoma is the **only pituitary tumour treated with tablets first**, even with visual loss.`,`Triphasic response after surgery: **polyuria (day 1-2), SIADH (day 5-10), then possible permanent AVP deficiency**.`,`Optic atrophy and thin **RNFL on OCT** predict poor visual recovery.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has an MRI for headache that shows a 6 mm non-enhancing pituitary lesion. He has no symptoms of hormone excess or deficiency and normal visual fields by confrontation. According to the Endocrine Society incidentaloma guideline, which initial hormone tests are recommended?`,options:[`Serum prolactin and IGF-1`,`Full anterior pituitary panel including Synacthen test`,`No hormone tests, repeat MRI in 1 year only`,`Serum prolactin alone`],answer:0,explanation:`The 2011 Endocrine Society guideline advises prolactin and IGF-1 in every incidentaloma, because a prolactinoma or a subtle somatotroph tumour changes management; Cushing testing is added only if clinically suggested. A full pituitary panel with dynamic testing is reserved for macroincidentalomas (or symptomatic 6-9 mm lesions), so it is excessive here. Doing no tests misses silent hypersecretion, and prolactin alone misses early acromegaly.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-q2`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman has amenorrhoea, headache and bitemporal hemianopia. MRI shows a 28 mm sellar mass with suprasellar extension. Serum prolactin is 72 ng/mL and is unchanged after a 1:100 dilution. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Macroprolactinoma`,`Prolactinoma with a hook effect`,`Non-functioning macroadenoma with stalk effect`,`Drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia`],answer:2,explanation:`A macroprolactinoma of 28 mm almost always gives prolactin well over 250 ng/mL; a modest level under 100 ng/mL with a large tumour reflects stalk compression cutting off dopamine (stalk effect), typical of a non-functioning adenoma. The hook effect is excluded because dilution did not raise the value. Drugs do not explain a 28 mm mass. This matters because a non-functioning tumour compressing the chiasm needs surgery, not cabergoline.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-q3`,stem:`In a pituitary macroadenoma compressing the optic chiasm from below, the earliest visual field defect is usually:`,options:[`Inferior bitemporal quadrantanopia`,`Right homonymous hemianopia`,`Bilateral central scotomas`,`Superior bitemporal quadrantanopia`],answer:3,explanation:`A pituitary tumour rises from below and first presses the inferonasal crossing fibres, which serve the superior temporal fields, so superior bitemporal quadrantanopia comes first. Inferior quadrantanopia suggests compression from above (craniopharyngioma, meningioma). A homonymous hemianopia indicates optic tract involvement, as with a pre-fixed chiasm, and central scotomas suggest optic nerve compression.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-q4`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman with a 22 mm non-functioning macroadenoma has fatigue. Free T4 is 0.6 ng/dL (low), TSH 1.8 mIU/L, and 8 am cortisol 4 microgram/dL. What is the correct first step in replacement?`,options:[`Start levothyroxine 50 microgram daily and recheck cortisol in 6 weeks`,`Start hydrocortisone, then add levothyroxine a few days later`,`Wait for TSH to rise above the reference range before treating`,`Start levothyroxine and hydrocortisone on the same day at full dose`],answer:1,explanation:`Low free T4 with an inappropriately normal TSH is central hypothyroidism, and a morning cortisol of 4 microgram/dL suggests ACTH deficiency. Thyroxine speeds cortisol clearance and can precipitate adrenal crisis, so glucocorticoid must start first and thyroxine follow a few days later. Waiting for TSH to rise is wrong because in central disease TSH never reliably rises.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-q5`,stem:`A sellar mass at presentation is accompanied by polyuria, thirst and a thickened pituitary stalk. Which lesion is least likely?`,options:[`Craniopharyngioma`,`Pituitary metastasis`,`Lymphocytic hypophysitis`,`Non-functioning pituitary adenoma`],answer:3,explanation:`Pituitary adenomas arise in the anterior lobe and very rarely cause AVP deficiency (diabetes insipidus) before surgery, because the posterior lobe and hypothalamic neurons are spared by slow growth. Craniopharyngioma, metastasis (often breast or lung), hypophysitis and granulomas such as TB involve the stalk or hypothalamus and commonly cause AVP deficiency, so their presence makes an adenoma unlikely.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the clinical presentation, evaluation and management of a pituitary macroadenoma. Add a note on the approach to a pituitary incidentaloma.`,openingLines:[`A pituitary adenoma (PitNET, WHO 2022) is a benign anterior pituitary tumour; a macroadenoma is 10 mm or more and presents with hormone excess, hypopituitarism or mass effect.`,`Prolactinomas are treated medically; all other macroadenomas compressing the chiasm or secreting hormone need transsphenoidal surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Size: micro under 10 mm, macro 10 mm or more, giant 40 mm or more.`,`Function: prolactinoma, somatotroph, corticotroph, thyrotroph, non-functioning (gonadotroph or null cell).`,`Lineage (WHO 2022): PIT1, TPIT, SF1, null cell; Knosp grade for cavernous sinus invasion.`]},{heading:`Clinical presentation`,points:[`Mass effect: headache, superior bitemporal quadrantanopia then hemianopia, CN III palsy, CSF rhinorrhoea.`,`Hypopituitarism in order GH, LH/FSH, TSH, ACTH; stalk effect raises prolactin under 100 ng/mL.`,`Hormone excess: amenorrhoea-galactorrhoea, acromegaly, Cushing disease, central hyperthyroidism.`,`Apoplexy: thunderclap headache, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia, shock.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`MRI pituitary with gadolinium; CT for calcification; angiography if aneurysm possible.`,`Hypersecretion: prolactin (diluted 1:100), IGF-1, overnight dexamethasone suppression if Cushingoid.`,`Hypopituitarism: 8-9 am cortisol, free T4 with TSH, LH/FSH and sex steroid, IGF-1, sodium.`,`Formal perimetry and OCT; fundus for optic atrophy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Prolactinoma: cabergoline first even with visual loss.`,`Others: endoscopic transsphenoidal surgery if chiasm compressed, visual loss or hypersecretion.`,`Replace hydrocortisone before thyroxine; sick-day rules and steroid card.`,`Post-op: sodium daily 10 days (triphasic response), function tests at 6-12 weeks, MRI at 3-4 months.`,`Residual or recurrent: radiotherapy or radiosurgery; temozolomide for aggressive tumours.`]},{heading:`Pituitary incidentaloma`,points:[`All: history, examination, prolactin and IGF-1.`,`Macro: full function screen; fields if near chiasm; MRI at 6 months then yearly for 3 years.`,`Micro: MRI at 1 year then every 1-2 years for 3 years; no surgery unless hypersecreting or growing.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Lifelong MRI and hormone review; yearly function tests after radiotherapy.`,`Refer same day for apoplexy, falling acuity or adrenal crisis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the optic chiasm showing compression from below and the resulting superior bitemporal field defect`,`Flow chart of incidentaloma work-up (micro vs macro)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features (mass, deficiency, excess)`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with interpretation`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management including surgery and post-op care`,marks:3},{part:`Incidentaloma approach`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`PitNET`,`macroadenoma`,`bitemporal hemianopia`,`stalk effect`,`transsphenoidal surgery`,`incidentaloma`]},{id:`endocrine-pituitary-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between pituitary macroadenoma and craniopharyngioma.`,openingLines:[`Both are sellar-suprasellar masses that cause visual loss and hypopituitarism, but they differ in origin, age, imaging and hormonal pattern.`,`A macroadenoma arises from anterior pituitary cells; a craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants.`],answer:[{heading:`Origin and age`,points:[`Adenoma: anterior pituitary cells (PitNET), adults 30-60 years.`,`Craniopharyngioma: squamous epithelial remnants of Rathke pouch, bimodal - children 5-14 and adults 50-75.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Adenoma: superior bitemporal defect, hypogonadism, may secrete hormone; AVP deficiency rare.`,`Craniopharyngioma: inferior bitemporal defect, raised ICP, growth failure, obesity, AVP deficiency common.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`Adenoma: solid enhancing 'snowman' mass enlarging the sella; calcification rare.`,`Craniopharyngioma: suprasellar cystic-solid mass with calcification on CT (adamantinomatous type).`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Adenoma: transsphenoidal surgery (cabergoline for prolactinoma).`,`Craniopharyngioma: surgery plus radiotherapy; BRAF V600E inhibitors for papillary type; long-term hypothalamic obesity.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: origin, age, field defect, AVP deficiency, calcification, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Origin and age`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical and hormonal differences`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`craniopharyngioma`,`Rathke pouch`,`calcification`,`AVP deficiency`,`macroadenoma`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pituitary tumour syndromes`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Freda PU et al. Pituitary incidentaloma: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2011`,`WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`,`Petersenn S et al. Diagnosis and management of prolactin-secreting pituitary adenomas: a Pituitary Society international consensus statement. Nat Rev Endocrinol 2023`,`Fleseriu M et al. Hormonal replacement in hypopituitarism in adults: Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline, 2016`,`Raverot G et al. European Society of Endocrinology clinical practice guidelines for aggressive pituitary tumours and carcinomas, 2018`,`Rajasekaran S et al. UK guidelines for the management of pituitary apoplexy (Society for Endocrinology), 2011`]}),a.push({id:`endocrine-prolactinoma`,title:`Hyperprolactinaemia and prolactinoma`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Hyperprolactinaemia** is a serum prolactin above the sex-specific upper limit (about **25 ng/mL in women, 20 ng/mL in men**) and causes **hypogonadotropic hypogonadism** (amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea, infertility, low libido); first exclude **pregnancy, drugs (levosulpiride, risperidone, metoclopramide), primary hypothyroidism, CKD and macroprolactin**, and treat a symptomatic **prolactinoma** with **cabergoline 0.25-0.5 mg once or twice weekly** - the only pituitary tumour where tablets come before surgery (Pituitary Society 2023).`,keywords:[`prolactin`,`hyperprolactinaemia`,`prolactinoma`,`lactotroph adenoma`,`galactorrhoea`,`amenorrhoea-galactorrhoea syndrome`,`macroprolactin`,`macroprolactinaemia`,`hook effect`,`stalk effect`,`cabergoline`,`bromocriptine`,`dopamine agonist resistance`,`cardiac valvulopathy`,`impulse control disorder`,`levosulpiride`,`risperidone`,`PEG precipitation`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hyperprolactinaemia** is a prolactin above the assay's sex-specific upper limit - roughly **25 ng/mL (about 530 mIU/L) in women and 20 ng/mL (about 425 mIU/L) in men**; 1 ng/mL equals about **21 mIU/L**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`It is the **commonest pituitary hormone excess** and the commonest endocrine cause of **secondary amenorrhoea after PCOS and pregnancy**; it is found in about **10-25% of women with secondary amenorrhoea** and a similar share with galactorrhoea and amenorrhoea together. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prolactinoma** (lactotroph PitNET) is the commonest functioning pituitary tumour, about **40-50%** of clinically apparent adenomas; prevalence is roughly **35-50 per 100,000**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sex pattern**: in women under 50 the ratio is about **10:1 female to male** and over 90% are **microprolactinomas**; men present later with **macroprolactinomas** because low libido is ignored. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`In Indian practice **drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia** - especially **levosulpiride** (prescribed widely for dyspepsia) and antipsychotics - is a leading cause of galactorrhoea seen in OPD; always check the prescription first. [API Textbook 2022]`,`**Macroprolactinaemia** (big-big prolactin) accounts for about **10-25%** of hyperprolactinaemia reports in laboratories without screening. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Physiological**: **pregnancy** (prolactin up to about 10 times normal at term), lactation, nipple stimulation, sleep, exercise, stress of venepuncture, coitus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs - dopamine antagonists**: antipsychotics (**risperidone, paliperidone, amisulpride, sulpiride, haloperidol**), antiemetics and prokinetics (**metoclopramide, domperidone, levosulpiride**) - can raise prolactin to 100-200 ng/mL or more. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Other drugs**: tricyclics and SSRIs (mild), **methyldopa, reserpine, verapamil**, opioids, high-dose oestrogen, H2 blockers (cimetidine IV), and cocaine; **aripiprazole lowers prolactin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pituitary and hypothalamic**: prolactinoma, **stalk effect** from any sellar mass, acromegaly co-secretion, hypophysitis, granulomas (TB, sarcoidosis), and cranial radiotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic**: **primary hypothyroidism** (TRH stimulates lactotrophs), **CKD** (reduced clearance, up to 30% of patients on dialysis), cirrhosis, PCOS (usually mild), and **chest-wall neural stimulation** (herpes zoster, burns, thoracotomy, nipple piercing). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Seizures** cause a transient rise in prolactin 10-20 minutes after a generalised seizure - used to separate epileptic seizures from psychogenic attacks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Idiopathic** hyperprolactinaemia is diagnosed only after imaging and exclusion; some are tiny adenomas below MRI resolution. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Prolactin is the only pituitary hormone under **predominant tonic inhibition** - **dopamine** from tuberoinfundibular neurons acts on lactotroph **D2 receptors**, so anything cutting dopamine (drugs, stalk compression) raises prolactin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TRH and oestrogen stimulate** prolactin - explaining hyperprolactinaemia in hypothyroidism and in pregnancy, where oestrogen-driven lactotroph hyperplasia enlarges the gland. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypogonadism mechanism**: prolactin suppresses hypothalamic **kisspeptin** neurons, reducing **GnRH pulsatility**, so LH and FSH fall - anovulation, amenorrhoea, low testosterone and **low bone density**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Galactorrhoea** needs an oestrogen-primed breast; it is common in premenopausal women and **rare in men and postmenopausal women**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cabergoline** is a long-acting D2 agonist (half-life about 65 hours) that suppresses prolactin synthesis and **shrinks lactotroph cells**, reducing tumour volume within weeks - hence medical therapy even for visual loss. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Valvulopathy** relates to **5-HT2B receptor agonism** by cabergoline and pergolide, causing fibroblast proliferation on valve leaflets - seen at Parkinson doses (3 mg/day or more), rarely at prolactinoma doses. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Macroprolactin** is prolactin bound to IgG; it is detected by immunoassays but is **biologically inactive** and cleared slowly, so it causes a high reading with no symptoms. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**By cause**: physiological, pharmacological, pathological (pituitary, hypothalamic, systemic) and idiopathic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prolactinoma by size**: **microprolactinoma under 10 mm**, **macroprolactinoma 10 mm or more**, **giant prolactinoma 40 mm or more** (often in young men, prolactin usually over 1000 ng/mL). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**By prolactin level (rule of thumb)**: under 100 ng/mL - drugs, stalk effect, microadenoma, systemic causes; **over 250 ng/mL** - almost always prolactinoma; over 500 ng/mL - macroprolactinoma. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**High-risk lactotroph tumours** (WHO 2022): **sparsely granulated in men** and those with high Ki-67 - more often resistant to dopamine agonists. [WHO 2022]`,`**Dopamine agonist resistance**: failure to normalise prolactin and to shrink tumour by at least **30%** on the maximum tolerated standard dose; seen in about **10% of microprolactinomas and 20% of macroprolactinomas**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Women**: **oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea**, **galactorrhoea**, infertility, reduced libido, dyspareunia (vaginal dryness from low oestrogen). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Men**: **low libido and erectile dysfunction**, infertility, reduced shaving, **gynaecomastia** (galactorrhoea rare); then headache and visual loss from macroadenoma. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Adolescents**: primary amenorrhoea, delayed or arrested puberty. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postmenopausal women**: no menstrual clue, so they present with **mass effects** of a large tumour. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Long-standing hypogonadism** causes **osteopenia and fractures**; ask about back pain and fractures. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Specific history: **drug list** (prokinetics, antipsychotics, antidepressants), **pregnancy**, thyroid symptoms, kidney or liver disease, chest wall injury, zoster, nipple stimulation. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Galactorrhoea** on gentle expression of each breast from periphery to nipple - milky, bilateral, multiductal; a **bloody or single-duct** discharge suggests breast pathology, not prolactin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visual fields** with red pin and **eye movements** in macroadenomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of hypogonadism**: small soft testes, reduced body hair, **gynaecomastia** in men; atrophic vaginal mucosa in women. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of hypothyroidism** (bradycardia, delayed reflexes, goitre) and of **acromegaly** (prolactin co-secretion in about a quarter of GH tumours). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest wall** for scars, zoster, piercings; signs of **CKD or cirrhosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hirsutism and acanthosis** point towards PCOS as the alternative cause of anovulation with mild hyperprolactinaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pregnancy** - exclude with urine or serum hCG before anything else in a woman with amenorrhoea. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Drug-induced**: symptom onset after starting the drug; prolactin usually **25-100 ng/mL** (risperidone and amisulpride can exceed 200); stopping for 3-4 days (if safe) normalises it. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Stalk effect** from a non-functioning macroadenoma: big tumour, prolactin **under 100 ng/mL**; cabergoline lowers prolactin but **does not shrink the tumour** - a dangerous trap. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Primary hypothyroidism**: high TSH, pituitary hyperplasia; resolves with levothyroxine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PCOS**: mild prolactin rise, hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovaries; a prolactin over 50 ng/mL warrants imaging rather than labelling PCOS. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Macroprolactinaemia**: high prolactin without symptoms, recovery **under 40% after PEG precipitation**; no treatment or imaging needed. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Galactorrhoea with normal prolactin** (idiopathic, recent lactation) - reassure; **nipple discharge** that is bloody needs breast imaging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**A single prolactin above the upper limit** drawn without undue venepuncture stress is enough to confirm hyperprolactinaemia; repeat a borderline value (or take samples 15-20 minutes apart via cannula). Dynamic tests are not used. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**First-line OPD panel**: **urine pregnancy test, TSH with free T4, creatinine, liver function**, and a careful drug history. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Macroprolactin screen** by **PEG precipitation** in mildly raised prolactin with few or no symptoms; recovery **under 40%** indicates predominant macroprolactin. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary MRI with gadolinium** when no physiological, drug or systemic cause is found, or the level is clearly high. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Hook effect**: very high prolactin saturates both antibodies of a two-site immunoassay, giving a falsely low result - with any **macroadenoma of 30 mm or more and prolactin under 200-250 ng/mL**, re-assay at **1:100 dilution**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`With a macroadenoma: **pituitary function screen** (cortisol, free T4, LH/FSH, testosterone or oestradiol, IGF-1) and **formal visual fields**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Bone density (DXA)** in long-standing hypogonadism; **baseline echocardiogram** before cabergoline is advised in UK practice (SfE/BSE 2019). [SfE/BSE 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Treat the cause**: stop or switch the offending drug (levosulpiride to a PPI; antipsychotic switch to **aripiprazole**, or add aripiprazole 5 mg - only with the psychiatrist). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Who needs treatment**: symptomatic hypogonadism (amenorrhoea, infertility, low libido, bone loss), troublesome galactorrhoea, and **all macroprolactinomas**; **asymptomatic microprolactinoma needs no treatment**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Cabergoline** is first line: start **0.25-0.5 mg orally once or twice weekly** at bedtime with food; titrate monthly by prolactin; most patients need **2 mg/week or less**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Bromocriptine** is second line or used when planning pregnancy: start **1.25 mg at bedtime with food**, increase to **2.5 mg two or three times daily** (up to 7.5-15 mg/day); more nausea and postural hypotension. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Microprolactinoma in a woman not wanting pregnancy**: a **combined oral contraceptive** (or HRT after menopause) to protect bone is acceptable instead of cabergoline. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Men**: cabergoline restores testosterone in most; add **testosterone** if hypogonadism persists despite normal prolactin (monitor prolactin, as aromatised oestrogen may stimulate the tumour). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Screen every patient on dopamine agonists for impulse control disorders** - pathological gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive shopping - and warn the family. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Cabergoline 0.5 mg tablets are available in India; **bromocriptine is on the NLEM**, useful where cost limits cabergoline. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Macroprolactinoma, resistance and inpatient care`,points:[`**Macroprolactinoma with visual loss**: start **cabergoline** urgently - prolactin falls within days and fields improve within weeks in most; **surgery is not first line** even with chiasmal compression. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Monitor**: prolactin at 1 month, visual fields at **2-4 weeks** if impaired, **MRI at 3-6 months** for macroadenomas. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Resistance**: increase cabergoline stepwise to **3.5 mg/week** (up to 7 mg/week in selected cases); switch from bromocriptine to cabergoline; then **transsphenoidal surgery**; then radiotherapy; **temozolomide** for aggressive tumours. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Surgery indications**: dopamine agonist intolerance or resistance, **apoplexy with visual loss**, **CSF leak**, cystic tumour, patient choice; and per the **2023 consensus, as a first-line alternative** for a well-circumscribed micro or non-invasive macroprolactinoma in an expert centre. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**CSF rhinorrhoea** after tumour shrinkage (clear salty drip, beta-2 transferrin positive) needs neurosurgical repair - risk of **meningitis**. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Apoplexy** in a prolactinoma: hydrocortisone first, then surgery or cabergoline depending on vision - see the Hypopituitarism and Sheehan topic. [SfE 2011]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy - microprolactinoma**: stop cabergoline **once pregnancy is confirmed**; risk of symptomatic enlargement is about **2-3%**; **do not measure prolactin in pregnancy** (it rises physiologically). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Pregnancy - macroprolactinoma**: symptomatic growth in about **20-30% if not previously operated**; check **visual fields each trimester**; continuing cabergoline is an option for large tumours near the chiasm. [ESE 2021]`,`**Tumour growth in pregnancy** (headache, field loss): MRI **without gadolinium**, restart **bromocriptine or cabergoline**; surgery in the second trimester only if drugs fail. [ESE 2021]`,`**Breastfeeding** is allowed after a prolactinoma; dopamine agonists suppress lactation, so restart them after weaning. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Psychiatric patients**: never stop an antipsychotic without the psychiatrist; options are switching to aripiprazole, adding aripiprazole, or oestrogen/testosterone replacement; **avoid dopamine agonists** that may worsen psychosis. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Postmenopausal women with microprolactinoma**: no treatment needed if asymptomatic - menopause removes the bone and fertility indication. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**CKD**: high prolactin from reduced clearance; cabergoline can help hypogonadal symptoms, but correct anaemia and dialysis adequacy first. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated hyperprolactinaemia causes **infertility, osteoporosis** and, with macroadenomas, **visual loss and hypopituitarism**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microprolactinomas rarely grow** - only about **5-10%** enlarge over years; many involute after menopause. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Cabergoline normalises prolactin in about **85% of microprolactinomas and 80% of macroprolactinomas** and shrinks most macroadenomas. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Cabergoline valvulopathy** at prolactinoma doses is rare; most series show only trivial regurgitation without clinical disease. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Impulse control disorders** affect roughly **10-25%** of treated patients (higher in men) and are often hidden. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Surgical cure** of microprolactinomas in expert hands is about **80-90%**, macroadenomas much lower (about 40-50%). [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and stopping therapy`,points:[`Refer all **macroprolactinomas**, men, children, pregnant women with a prolactinoma, and patients with unexplained prolactin over 100 ng/mL. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`On cabergoline: **prolactin every 1-3 months** while titrating, then every 6-12 months; MRI as needed for micro, at 3-6 months then yearly for macro. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Echocardiography**: UK SfE/BSE 2019 advises **baseline echo**, then **every 5 years on 2 mg/week or less** and **yearly above 2 mg/week**; the 2023 consensus advises echo mainly for higher cumulative doses. [SfE/BSE 2019]`,`**Stopping therapy**: consider after **at least 2 years** with **normal prolactin** and **no visible tumour** (or marked shrinkage away from the chiasm), ideally after tapering to the lowest dose. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`After withdrawal, **recurrence is about 50-65%**, mostly in the first year - check **prolactin every 3 months for a year**, then yearly. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Also consider withdrawing at **menopause** in women with microprolactinomas. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Rational prescribing**: avoid long-term levosulpiride or domperidone for dyspepsia and **check prolactin-related symptoms** in patients on antipsychotics. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Tell women that **fertility returns quickly** on cabergoline - advise contraception if pregnancy is not wanted. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`Warn about **nausea and postural dizziness**; take doses at bedtime with food. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`Warn patient and family about **impulse control changes** and to report them. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Bone health**: calcium, vitamin D, weight-bearing exercise; DXA if hypogonadal for over a year. [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Pituitary Society international consensus (Petersenn et al., Nat Rev Endocrinol 2023)** replaces the Endocrine Society 2011 guideline as the main reference. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Surgery upgraded**: an **alternative first-line option** for selected microprolactinomas and well-circumscribed macroprolactinomas by an expert surgeon, given high cure rates and avoidance of lifelong drugs. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Impulse control disorders** emphasised: screen all patients at start and during treatment. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Resistance** defined by both biochemical and tumour response; dose escalation up to about 3.5 mg/week before declaring failure. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Echocardiography** targeted to patients on higher cumulative doses, rather than routine for all (contrast with SfE/BSE 2019, which advises baseline echo for all). [Pituitary Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic MCQ**: high prolactin, no symptoms - **macroprolactin** (PEG precipitation). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Classic MCQ**: huge tumour, modest prolactin - **hook effect** (dilute) or **stalk effect** (does not change on dilution). [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Viva**: 'Why is prolactinoma treated medically even with visual loss?' - cabergoline shrinks tumour rapidly in most, avoids surgical hypopituitarism. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: measuring prolactin in pregnancy, or starting MRI before excluding pregnancy, hypothyroidism and drugs. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: treating an asymptomatic microprolactinoma, or treating stalk effect with cabergoline while the chiasm stays compressed. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**Mnemonic for causes - 'PRL HIGH'**: Pregnancy, Renal failure, Liver failure, Hypothyroidism, Idiopathic, Growth (tumour or stalk), Heavy drug use (antipsychotics, prokinetics). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for hyperprolactinaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cabergoline`,`0.25-0.5 mg orally once or twice weekly at bedtime`,`Raise by 0.25-0.5 mg/week monthly; most need 2 mg/week or less; up to 3.5 mg/week for resistance`,`Nausea, postural hypotension, headache, impulse control disorder, valvulopathy at high dose, CSF leak`],[`Bromocriptine`,`1.25 mg orally at bedtime, then 2.5 mg 2-3 times daily`,`Up to 7.5-15 mg/day; preferred by some before conception`,`More nausea and dizziness, nasal stuffiness, Raynaud, psychosis`],[`Combined oral contraceptive`,`Standard low-dose pill daily`,`Long-term in microprolactinoma not wanting pregnancy`,`VTE risk; check prolactin yearly`],[`Testosterone (men)`,`Testosterone undecanoate 1000 mg IM every 10-14 weeks or gel`,`If hypogonadism persists with normal prolactin`,`Polycythaemia; aromatisation may raise prolactin`],[`Aripiprazole (drug-induced, with psychiatrist)`,`5 mg orally daily added or as switch`,`Long-term`,`Akathisia; psychiatric review required`],[`Temozolomide (aggressive tumour)`,`150-200 mg/m2 orally days 1-5 every 28 days`,`At least 3 cycles`,`Myelosuppression; specialist only`]]},{heading:`Causes of hyperprolactinaemia`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Usual prolactin (ng/mL)`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Physiological`,`Pregnancy, lactation, stress, sleep, nipple stimulation`,`Variable; up to 200+ at term`,`Positive hCG; history`],[`Drugs`,`Levosulpiride, metoclopramide, domperidone, risperidone, amisulpride, haloperidol, TCA, verapamil, methyldopa`,`25-100 (risperidone over 200)`,`Temporal link, normalises off drug`],[`Systemic`,`Primary hypothyroidism, CKD, cirrhosis, chest wall lesions, seizures`,`Usually under 100`,`High TSH, raised creatinine`],[`Stalk effect`,`Non-functioning adenoma, craniopharyngioma, hypophysitis`,`Usually under 100`,`Large mass, prolactin unchanged on dilution`],[`Prolactinoma`,`Micro- or macro-lactotroph tumour`,`Over 250 suggests prolactinoma`,`Level parallels size`],[`Assay artefact`,`Macroprolactin`,`Any`,`No symptoms; PEG recovery under 40%`]]},{heading:`Lab pitfalls: macroprolactin, hook effect and stalk effect`,columns:[`Feature`,`Macroprolactinaemia`,`Hook effect`,`Stalk effect`],rows:[[`What it is`,`IgG-bound inactive prolactin`,`Antibody saturation by huge prolactin`,`Loss of dopamine inhibition`],[`Symptoms`,`Usually none`,`Mass effect, hypogonadism`,`Mass effect, hypogonadism`],[`Measured prolactin`,`High`,`Falsely normal or mildly high`,`Mildly high (under 100)`],[`Test`,`PEG precipitation, recovery under 40%`,`1:100 dilution - value rises sharply`,`Dilution unchanged`],[`Treatment`,`None`,`Cabergoline`,`Surgery if chiasm compressed`]]},{heading:`Prolactinoma in pregnancy (Pituitary Society 2023, ESE 2021)`,columns:[`Tumour`,`Risk of symptomatic growth`,`Dopamine agonist`,`Monitoring`],rows:[[`Microprolactinoma`,`About 2-3%`,`Stop when pregnancy confirmed`,`Clinical review each trimester; no prolactin`],[`Macroprolactinoma, previously operated or irradiated`,`About 4-5%`,`Stop, or continue if close to chiasm`,`Visual fields each trimester`],[`Macroprolactinoma, not operated`,`About 20-30%`,`Consider continuing; surgery before pregnancy is an option`,`Visual fields each trimester; MRI without gadolinium if symptoms`]]}],redFlags:[`Visual field loss or falling acuity with a macroprolactinoma`,`Sudden severe headache, vomiting, ophthalmoplegia - apoplexy`,`Clear watery nasal discharge after cabergoline starts - CSF leak`,`Headache or visual symptoms in a pregnant woman with a known prolactinoma`,`Prolactin rising despite maximal cabergoline, or tumour growth on MRI`,`New gambling, hypersexuality or compulsive spending on a dopamine agonist`,`Bloody or single-duct nipple discharge - breast pathology, not prolactin`],pearls:[`**Before any MRI**: pregnancy test, TSH, creatinine, drug history - 'PTCD'.`,`**Levosulpiride** is the commonest Indian OPD cause of drug-induced galactorrhoea.`,`**Over 250 ng/mL = prolactinoma**; **under 100 with a big mass = stalk effect**.`,`**Hook effect**: big tumour, low prolactin - ask for 1:100 dilution.`,`**Macroprolactin**: high number, no symptoms - PEG precipitation, recovery under 40%.`,`Prolactinoma is the **only pituitary tumour where tablets come before the knife**, even with visual loss.`,`**Do not measure prolactin in pregnancy**; follow symptoms and fields.`,`**Stop cabergoline** after 2 years of normal prolactin and no visible tumour; half recur.`,`Cabergoline needs an **impulse control** question at every visit.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman is referred with a prolactin of 68 ng/mL found during an infertility work-up for her husband's low sperm count. She has regular menses and no galactorrhoea. TSH and creatinine are normal and she takes no medicines. What is the most appropriate next test?`,options:[`Pituitary MRI with gadolinium`,`Prolactin after polyethylene glycol precipitation`,`Repeat prolactin at 1:100 dilution`,`Thyrotropin-releasing hormone stimulation test`],answer:1,explanation:`A raised prolactin in a woman with regular cycles and no galactorrhoea suggests macroprolactin, an IgG-bound inactive form; PEG precipitation (recovery under 40%) confirms it and avoids needless imaging and treatment. MRI comes only after macroprolactin and secondary causes are excluded. Dilution addresses the hook effect, which applies to large tumours with falsely low prolactin, not to a mild rise. TRH testing has no place in current practice.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-q2`,stem:`A 32-year-old man has headache and bitemporal hemianopia. MRI shows a 36 mm sellar mass invading the cavernous sinus. Prolactin is 3200 ng/mL. What is the best initial treatment?`,options:[`Urgent transsphenoidal surgery`,`Stereotactic radiosurgery`,`Cabergoline`,`Observation with repeat MRI in 3 months`],answer:2,explanation:`A prolactin of 3200 ng/mL with a large tumour confirms a macroprolactinoma. Cabergoline is first-line even with visual loss because it normalises prolactin and shrinks the tumour within weeks in most patients, often restoring vision without surgical risk. Surgery is reserved for resistance, intolerance, apoplexy or CSF leak; an invasive tumour is unlikely to be cured surgically anyway. Radiotherapy acts slowly and observation risks blindness.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old man on risperidone 4 mg daily for schizophrenia, well controlled, has gynaecomastia and erectile dysfunction. Prolactin is 145 ng/mL and pituitary MRI is normal. What is the best management?`,options:[`Liaise with the psychiatrist to switch to, or add, aripiprazole`,`Start cabergoline 0.5 mg twice weekly and continue risperidone`,`Stop risperidone immediately`,`Refer for transsphenoidal exploration`],answer:0,explanation:`This is drug-induced hyperprolactinaemia from D2 blockade by risperidone. The preferred approach, with the psychiatrist, is switching to or adding aripiprazole, a partial agonist that lowers prolactin. Dopamine agonists such as cabergoline may destabilise psychosis and are second-line. Abruptly stopping an antipsychotic risks relapse. Surgery has no role with a normal MRI.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-q4`,stem:`A 29-year-old woman with a 6 mm microprolactinoma controlled on cabergoline has a positive pregnancy test at 5 weeks. What is the recommended plan?`,options:[`Continue cabergoline and check prolactin every month`,`Switch to bromocriptine for the rest of pregnancy`,`Arrange MRI with gadolinium in each trimester`,`Stop cabergoline and review symptoms clinically each trimester`],answer:3,explanation:`The risk of symptomatic growth of a microprolactinoma in pregnancy is only about 2-3%, so the dopamine agonist is stopped once pregnancy is confirmed and the woman is reviewed for headache and visual symptoms. Prolactin rises physiologically in pregnancy, so measuring it is useless. Routine MRI is not done, and gadolinium is avoided. Bromocriptine is used only if symptomatic growth occurs.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-q5`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman has taken cabergoline 0.5 mg twice weekly for 3 years for a microprolactinoma. Prolactin has been normal for 2.5 years and MRI shows no visible tumour. She asks about stopping. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Stopping is contraindicated because recurrence is nearly universal`,`Tapering and stopping is reasonable, with prolactin checks every 3 months for the first year`,`She must first have transsphenoidal surgery to allow withdrawal`,`Withdrawal is allowed only after menopause`],answer:1,explanation:`After at least 2 years of normal prolactin and no visible tumour, the Pituitary Society 2023 consensus supports tapering and stopping, with prolactin every 3 months for a year and then yearly, because recurrence is about 50-65% and mostly early. Recurrence is common but not universal, so stopping is not contraindicated. Surgery is not a prerequisite. Menopause is another time to try withdrawal but not the only one.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Enumerate the causes of hyperprolactinaemia. Describe the evaluation of a woman with amenorrhoea and galactorrhoea, and the management of prolactinoma including pregnancy.`,openingLines:[`Hyperprolactinaemia is a prolactin above the sex-specific upper limit (about 25 ng/mL in women) and causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism through suppression of kisspeptin and GnRH pulsatility.`,`Prolactinoma is the commonest functioning pituitary tumour and the only one treated with drugs (cabergoline) first.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Physiological: pregnancy, lactation, stress, sleep, nipple stimulation.`,`Drugs: levosulpiride, metoclopramide, domperidone, risperidone, amisulpride, haloperidol, TCA, verapamil, methyldopa, opioids.`,`Pathological: prolactinoma, stalk effect, hypophysitis, granuloma; primary hypothyroidism, CKD, cirrhosis, chest wall lesions, seizures.`,`Assay: macroprolactin; idiopathic.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Dopamine tonic inhibition via D2; loss raises prolactin.`,`Prolactin suppresses kisspeptin and GnRH, so LH/FSH fall - anovulation and bone loss.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History: menses, galactorrhoea, drugs, pregnancy, thyroid, renal, visual symptoms.`,`Examination: galactorrhoea, fields, signs of hypothyroidism, PCOS, acromegaly.`,`Tests: hCG, prolactin (single value), TSH, creatinine, LFT; PEG for macroprolactin.`,`MRI pituitary; dilute prolactin with a macroadenoma; function screen and perimetry for macro; DXA.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stop or change causative drug; treat hypothyroidism.`,`Cabergoline 0.25-0.5 mg once or twice weekly, titrated monthly; bromocriptine 1.25 mg to 2.5 mg TDS alternative.`,`Asymptomatic micro: no treatment; micro not wanting pregnancy: OCP an option.`,`Resistance: escalate to 3.5 mg/week, then surgery, radiotherapy, temozolomide.`,`Surgery also first-line option in expert centres (Pituitary Society 2023).`,`Screen for impulse control disorders; echo for higher doses.`]},{heading:`Pregnancy`,points:[`Micro: stop agonist on confirmation (growth risk 2-3%); no prolactin measurement.`,`Macro: growth risk 20-30% if unoperated; fields each trimester; consider continuing agonist.`,`Symptomatic growth: MRI without gadolinium, restart agonist; breastfeeding allowed.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and withdrawal`,points:[`Prolactin 1-3 monthly during titration; MRI 3-6 months for macro.`,`Stop after 2 years of normal prolactin and no visible tumour; recurrence 50-65%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Hypothalamic-pituitary diagram showing dopamine inhibition of lactotrophs and prolactin suppression of GnRH`,`Flow chart: high prolactin - exclude pregnancy, drugs, hypothyroidism, CKD, macroprolactin - MRI`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and pitfalls`,marks:3},{part:`Management and resistance`,marks:3},{part:`Pregnancy and withdrawal`,marks:2}],keywords:[`prolactinoma`,`cabergoline`,`macroprolactin`,`hook effect`,`pregnancy`,`levosulpiride`]},{id:`endocrine-prolactinoma-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on macroprolactinaemia and the hook effect.`,openingLines:[`Both are laboratory pitfalls in measuring prolactin: macroprolactin gives a falsely high, clinically irrelevant result, and the hook effect gives a falsely low result in giant prolactinomas.`,`Missing either leads to wrong imaging, wrong treatment or wrong surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Macroprolactinaemia`,points:[`Macroprolactin is prolactin-IgG complex (over 150 kDa), biologically inactive and slowly cleared.`,`About 10-25% of unscreened hyperprolactinaemia; patient often asymptomatic.`,`Test: PEG precipitation; recovery under 40% means predominant macroprolactin.`,`No treatment or MRI needed if monomeric prolactin is normal.`]},{heading:`Hook effect`,points:[`In two-site immunoassays, very high prolactin saturates capture and detection antibodies, so no sandwich forms and the reading is falsely low.`,`Suspect with a macroadenoma (especially 30 mm or more) and prolactin under 200-250 ng/mL.`,`Re-assay at 1:100 dilution: true prolactinoma shows a much higher value.`,`Missing it leads to unnecessary surgery for a drug-responsive tumour.`]},{heading:`Contrast with stalk effect`,points:[`Stalk effect: modest prolactin from loss of dopamine, unchanged on dilution; tumour does not shrink on cabergoline.`]}],mustDraw:[`Sandwich immunoassay diagram showing antibody saturation in the hook effect`],markSplit:[{part:`Macroprolactinaemia`,marks:2},{part:`Hook effect`,marks:2},{part:`Stalk effect contrast`,marks:1}],keywords:[`macroprolactin`,`PEG precipitation`,`hook effect`,`dilution`,`stalk effect`]}],references:[`Petersenn S et al. Diagnosis and management of prolactin-secreting pituitary adenomas: a Pituitary Society international consensus statement. Nat Rev Endocrinol 2023`,`Melmed S et al. Diagnosis and treatment of hyperprolactinemia: Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline, 2011`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Luger A et al. ESE clinical practice guideline on functioning and nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas in pregnancy. Eur J Endocrinol 2021`,`Steeds RP et al. Echocardiography and monitoring patients receiving dopamine agonist therapy for hyperprolactinaemia: joint position statement of the British Society of Echocardiography, British Heart Valve Society and Society for Endocrinology, 2019`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), MoHFW, 2022`]}),a.push({id:`endocrine-acromegaly`,title:`Acromegaly and gigantism`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Acromegaly** is chronic **growth hormone excess after epiphyseal fusion**, almost always from a **somatotroph pituitary adenoma**, causing acral and soft-tissue overgrowth, **diabetes, hypertension, cardiomyopathy and sleep apnoea**; **gigantism** is the same excess before fusion. Diagnose with a raised **age-matched IGF-1** and failure of GH to fall **below 1 ng/mL (0.4 with sensitive assays) after a 75 g OGTT**; treat with **transsphenoidal surgery**, then **somatostatin receptor ligands, pegvisomant, cabergoline or radiotherapy** until IGF-1 is normal.`,keywords:[`acromegaly`,`gigantism`,`growth hormone`,`GH`,`IGF-1`,`somatomedin C`,`somatotroph adenoma`,`OGTT GH suppression`,`octreotide LAR`,`lanreotide`,`pasireotide`,`pegvisomant`,`somatostatin receptor ligand`,`SRL`,`AIP mutation`,`McCune-Albright`,`X-LAG`,`carpal tunnel`,`prognathism`,`acromegalic cardiomyopathy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Acromegaly** is a chronic disorder of **GH and IGF-1 excess** after the growth plates have fused; **gigantism** is GH excess before fusion, causing tall stature, usually with features of acromegaly later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prevalence about 3-14 per 100,000** and **incidence about 0.2-1.1 per 100,000 per year**; probably under-diagnosed, as screening of diabetic or OSA populations finds more cases. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Mean age at diagnosis is the **fourth to fifth decade**, men and women equally; the **diagnostic delay is about 5-10 years** because change is slow and noticed by old photographs or a visiting relative. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian tertiary series (AIIMS, PGIMER, CMC Vellore) report **macroadenomas in the large majority** (often over 80%) at diagnosis, with high rates of diabetes and hypertension - late presentation is the rule. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Untreated acromegaly **shortens life by about 10 years** with mortality about **2-fold**, mainly **cardiovascular and respiratory**; achieving normal IGF-1 and GH restores near-normal survival. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Over 95% are GH-secreting pituitary adenomas** (PIT1 lineage): densely granulated, **sparsely granulated** (larger, more invasive, SRL-resistant) and **mammosomatotroph** (co-secrete prolactin). [WHO 2022]`,`Somatic **GNAS (gsp) activating mutations** in about **40%** of somatotroph tumours - these respond well to somatostatin analogues. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial and syndromic**: **MEN1**, **AIP mutation (FIPA)** - young, aggressive, gigantism; **Carney complex**; **McCune-Albright** (polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, cafe-au-lait spots, precocious puberty); **X-LAG** (GPR101) in infants; **MEN4**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ectopic GHRH** (under 1%) from **bronchial or pancreatic NETs** causes pituitary hyperplasia - raised serum GHRH; ectopic GH secretion is extremely rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic** GH excess from unsupervised GH injections (body-building) mimics acromegaly with suppressed endogenous markers. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`GH is released in pulses under **GHRH stimulation and somatostatin inhibition**; tumour secretion is **non-pulsatile and fails to suppress with glucose**, the basis of the OGTT test. [Harrison 22e]`,`GH acts on the liver (via JAK2-STAT5) to produce **IGF-1**, which mediates **growth of bone, cartilage and soft tissue** - periosteal bone growth widens hands, jaw and skull because long bones cannot lengthen after fusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Direct GH actions are anti-insulin**: lipolysis, hepatic gluconeogenesis and peripheral insulin resistance - hence **impaired glucose tolerance or diabetes in about half**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Sodium retention** via renal epithelial sodium channel activation and **expanded plasma volume** cause **hypertension** and soft-tissue swelling. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Acromegalic cardiomyopathy**: concentric biventricular hypertrophy, diastolic dysfunction, later systolic failure, **arrhythmias and valve regurgitation** - a direct GH/IGF-1 effect on myocytes plus hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper airway soft-tissue and macroglossia** cause **obstructive sleep apnoea** in about 60-70%; central apnoea also occurs. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`IGF-1 is mitogenic: **colonic polyps**, **thyroid nodules (multinodular goitre)**, organomegaly (liver, kidney, heart) and possibly higher colorectal cancer risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cartilage overgrowth then degeneration** produces an early hypertrophic and later **degenerative arthropathy**, and **median nerve compression** at the wrist from soft-tissue swelling. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and disease activity`,points:[`**By age at onset**: **gigantism** (before epiphyseal fusion) vs **acromegaly** (after fusion). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By source**: pituitary (over 95%), ectopic GHRH, ectopic GH, iatrogenic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**By tumour size**: microadenoma (under 10 mm, about 20-30%) vs macroadenoma (most); **Knosp grade 3-4** predicts surgical failure. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Histological subtype (WHO 2022)**: densely granulated (SRL-sensitive, **T2-hypointense** on MRI) vs **sparsely granulated** (T2-hyperintense, SRL-resistant, consider pasireotide or pegvisomant). [WHO 2022]`,`**Activity status after treatment**: **controlled** - normal age-adjusted IGF-1 (with random GH under 1 ng/mL); **active** - raised IGF-1; **discordant** - one marker raised, follow IGF-1. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Changes in appearance**: enlarging **hands and feet** (ring and shoe size increase), coarsening face, spacing of teeth; old photographs are the best evidence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Excessive sweating (hyperhidrosis)** and oily skin are among the commonest and earliest symptoms - an important clue in a diabetic or hypertensive. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Headache** (tumour and GH effect), **visual symptoms**, **snoring and daytime sleepiness** (OSA), **joint pain**, and **tingling in the hands** (carpal tunnel). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypogonadism**: menstrual disturbance, galactorrhoea (co-secretion or stalk effect), low libido and erectile dysfunction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic and cardiac**: new diabetes, hypertension, breathlessness from heart failure, palpitations. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Deepening voice**, fatigue, and **dental malocclusion** (dentist may be first to notice). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Children**: accelerated linear growth above mid-parental target; think AIP or McCune-Albright. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Face**: frontal bossing, **prominent supraorbital ridges**, broad nose, thick lips, **prognathism (mandibular protrusion)**, **interdental separation**, macroglossia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hands and feet**: **spade-like hands**, thick fingers, thick heel pad (over 22 mm on X-ray), doughy sweaty palms; Tinel and Phalen signs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: thick oily skin, **skin tags (acrochordons)** - marker of colonic polyps - and acanthosis nigricans. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visual fields** (bitemporal defect), **goitre** (diffuse or multinodular), hepatomegaly and BP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: hypertension, displaced apex, signs of heart failure; **proximal myopathy** and kyphosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mnemonic 'ABCDEF'**: Arthralgia/Arthritis, BP high, Carpal tunnel, Diabetes, Enlarged organs, Field defects. [OHCM 11e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Familial coarse features or constitutional tall stature**: stable photographs, normal IGF-1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pachydermoperiostosis** (primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy): clubbing, thick skin folds, periostosis - normal IGF-1. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: coarse face and macroglossia, but cold intolerance, bradycardia and high TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudoacromegaly** from severe insulin resistance (acromegaloid features, acanthosis) or **minoxidil** - normal GH and IGF-1. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Gigantism mimics**: **Marfan** and **Klinefelter** (eunuchoid proportions, normal IGF-1), **Sotos syndrome** (cerebral gigantism, macrocephaly), **homocystinuria**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Physiologically high IGF-1**: puberty and pregnancy - use age-specific reference ranges. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Serum IGF-1** (age- and sex-adjusted) is the **screening and monitoring test** - integrated GH exposure, no diurnal swing; report as **multiples of the upper limit of normal (ULN)**. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**IGF-1 clearly raised (about 1.3 x ULN or more) with typical features confirms acromegaly**; an **OGTT is needed only when IGF-1 is equivocal**. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**75 g OGTT with GH every 30 minutes for 2 hours**: failure of GH to fall **below 1 ng/mL** confirms acromegaly; with modern sensitive assays a nadir **under 0.4 ng/mL** is normal. Do not do it in uncontrolled diabetes. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Random GH is useless** for diagnosis because normal GH is pulsatile and rises with stress, fasting and sleep. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**False IGF-1 values**: falsely **low** in **uncontrolled diabetes, liver disease, malnutrition, hypothyroidism, oral oestrogen**; falsely high in puberty, pregnancy. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**MRI pituitary with gadolinium**; **T2 signal** helps predict SRL response; if no pituitary tumour, measure **serum GHRH** and image chest and abdomen for a NET. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Complete work-up**: prolactin, pituitary function (cortisol, free T4, LH/FSH, sex steroids), **visual fields**, HbA1c, lipids, **ECG and echocardiogram**, **sleep study**, **colonoscopy at diagnosis**, thyroid ultrasound if goitre, spine X-ray for vertebral fractures. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`]},{heading:`Management - principles and surgery`,points:[`**Goals**: normal age-adjusted **IGF-1** (primary target), **random GH under 1 ng/mL**, control of tumour mass, preservation of pituitary function and treatment of comorbidities. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Endoscopic transsphenoidal surgery by an experienced pituitary surgeon** is first-line for all **microadenomas** and resectable macroadenomas, and for invasive tumours with visual compromise. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Surgical remission**: about **80-90% for microadenomas** and about **40-60% for macroadenomas**, falling with cavernous sinus invasion (Knosp 3-4) and higher GH. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Debulking** of an unresectable macroadenoma improves the response to later medical therapy. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Pre-operative SRL** is not routine; it may be used to reduce anaesthetic risk in severe OSA or cardiomyopathy (shrinks soft tissue). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Assess remission at 12 weeks or later** after surgery with IGF-1 (it falls slowly) and random GH; OGTT if needed. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Comorbidity care in parallel**: treat diabetes, hypertension, OSA (CPAP), heart failure and carpal tunnel with standard therapy - see the Hypertension and diabetes topics. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`]},{heading:`Medical therapy and radiotherapy`,points:[`**First-generation SRLs** (somatostatin receptor ligands, SSTR2): **octreotide LAR 20 mg IM every 4 weeks** (10-40 mg) or **lanreotide autogel 120 mg deep SC every 4 weeks** (60-120 mg; 120 mg can be stretched to every 6-8 weeks if controlled). Control IGF-1 in about **25-40%** in unselected patients, more in selected. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Oral octreotide capsules** (40-80 mg/day in divided doses on an empty stomach) are licensed abroad for patients already controlled on injections. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Pasireotide LAR** (multireceptor SRL, **40-60 mg IM every 4 weeks**) is more effective in SRL-resistant and sparsely granulated tumours but causes **hyperglycaemia in over half** - check glucose before and during. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Pegvisomant** (GH receptor antagonist) **10-30 mg SC daily** (after a 40-80 mg loading dose) normalises IGF-1 in about **60-90%**; GH rises, so **monitor IGF-1 only**; check **liver enzymes** and tumour size (MRI). [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Cabergoline 1-3.5 mg/week** for **mild disease (IGF-1 up to about 2.5 x ULN)** or added to an SRL; cheap and oral - useful in India. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Radiotherapy** (stereotactic radiosurgery or fractionated) for residual disease uncontrolled by drugs or where drugs are unaffordable; GH falls slowly over **5-10 years**; **hypopituitarism in up to 50% at 10 years**; medical bridging meanwhile. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Cost in India**: SRLs and pasireotide cost tens of thousands of rupees a month and **pegvisomant is not marketed in India**, so many centres use **surgery, then cabergoline and radiotherapy**, with SRLs where affordable or covered by schemes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and perioperative care`,points:[`**Airway**: macroglossia and soft-tissue swelling make intubation difficult - anaesthetic assessment, awake fibreoptic intubation may be needed; post-op CPAP for OSA. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Cardiac assessment** (ECG, echo) before surgery; optimise heart failure and arrhythmias. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Glucose falls rapidly** after successful surgery - reduce insulin and sulfonylurea doses to avoid hypoglycaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Standard post-pituitary-surgery care: **sodium daily for 10 days**, morning cortisol, steroid cover if ACTH deficient - see the Pituitary tumours topic. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Apoplexy** can occur in somatotroph adenomas; manage as in the Hypopituitarism and Sheehan topic. [SfE 2011]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: fertility is reduced; tumours rarely grow; **stop SRLs and pegvisomant** about 2 months before planned conception (or on confirmation); IGF-1 is unreliable in pregnancy; visual fields if macroadenoma. [ESE 2021]`,`**Children (gigantism)**: test for **AIP, MEN1, GPR101** and McCune-Albright; surgery first; target age-specific IGF-1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: IGF-1 may be falsely low when glucose is poorly controlled; pasireotide worsens and **pegvisomant improves glucose**. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Elderly**: milder disease; IGF-1 reference falls with age, so age-adjusted ranges are essential. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**McCune-Albright**: tumours are diffuse hyperplasia, surgery is difficult because of fibrous dysplasia of skull base - medical therapy preferred. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular**: hypertension (about one-third), **cardiomyopathy**, arrhythmias, valve disease - the leading cause of death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic**: **diabetes in about 30%** and impaired glucose tolerance in more. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Respiratory**: **obstructive sleep apnoea in up to 70%**; persists after cure in a proportion. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Skeletal**: arthropathy (often irreversible), **vertebral fractures** despite normal BMD, kyphosis. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Neoplastic**: **colonic polyps** and a modestly increased colorectal cancer risk; **thyroid nodules** and possibly thyroid cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Soft-tissue** changes regress partly with control; bone changes do not. Mortality returns to near normal when **IGF-1 is normalised**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer every suspected case** to an endocrinologist; treatment at a **Pituitary Tumour Centre of Excellence** gives better surgical cure. [Pituitary Society 2023]`,`**IGF-1 every 3-6 months** while titrating drugs, then 6-12 monthly; GH with IGF-1 except on pegvisomant. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**MRI** at 3-4 months after surgery, then yearly while residual tumour; 6-12 months after starting pegvisomant then yearly. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Colonoscopy** at diagnosis; repeat at **5 years if a polyp is found or IGF-1 stays high**, otherwise as for the general population. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Yearly**: BP, HbA1c, lipids, pituitary function (especially after RT), **echo and sleep study as indicated**, thyroid examination. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**On pegvisomant**: **liver enzymes monthly for 6 months**, then 6-monthly. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Early detection** is the only prevention: think of acromegaly in **diabetes with hypertension and sweating**, OSA with large hands, or carpal tunnel with coarse features - check IGF-1. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Genetic counselling** for AIP or MEN1 families; screen relatives with IGF-1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**2024 Acromegaly Consensus Conference (Giustina et al., Pituitary 2024)**: **IGF-1 clearly elevated with typical features is enough for diagnosis**; OGTT only for equivocal IGF-1; **GH nadir under 1 ng/mL** (lower with sensitive assays) retained. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Remission criteria (2024)**: **normal IGF-1** is the primary target; random GH under 1 ng/mL supports it; OGTT not needed in routine follow-up. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Personalised therapy**: choose second-line drugs by **T2 MRI signal, granulation pattern, glucose status and tumour residue** (pasireotide for SRL-resistant tumours with normal glucose; pegvisomant for diabetics). [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`,`**Comorbidity consensus**: structured screening and management of cardiac, metabolic, respiratory, skeletal and neoplastic complications. [Acromegaly Consensus 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva**: 'Best screening test?' - **IGF-1**; 'Confirmatory?' - **OGTT GH suppression**; 'Why not random GH?' - pulsatile. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Commonest cause of death**: cardiovascular disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**On pegvisomant monitor IGF-1, not GH** - GH rises because the receptor is blocked. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Skin tags** in acromegaly predict **colonic polyps**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: trusting a normal IGF-1 in a patient with uncontrolled diabetes or on oral oestrogen. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Spot diagnosis**: 'ring no longer fits, new shoes, sweaty, diabetic and hypertensive' - check IGF-1. [OHCM 11e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for acromegaly`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Octreotide LAR`,`20 mg IM every 4 weeks`,`Adjust to 10-40 mg by IGF-1 after 3 months`,`Gallstones, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, bradycardia, mild hyperglycaemia`],[`Lanreotide autogel`,`90-120 mg deep SC every 4 weeks`,`Extend to 6-8 weeks if controlled on 120 mg`,`As octreotide; injection-site nodules`],[`Pasireotide LAR`,`40 mg IM every 4 weeks, up to 60 mg`,`Second line for SRL resistance`,`Hyperglycaemia in over 50%, gallstones, QT prolongation`],[`Pegvisomant`,`40-80 mg SC loading, then 10 mg SC daily`,`Raise by 5 mg every 4-6 weeks to max 30 mg/day by IGF-1`,`Raised liver enzymes, lipohypertrophy; monitor tumour size; not in India`],[`Cabergoline`,`0.5 mg orally twice weekly`,`Up to 3.5 mg/week; mild disease or add-on`,`Nausea, postural hypotension, impulse control disorder`],[`Oral octreotide`,`40 mg/day in 2 doses, up to 80 mg`,`Only for patients controlled on injections`,`GI upset; take fasting`]]},{heading:`Diagnosis and remission cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Diagnosis`,`Remission/control`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`IGF-1 (age, sex adjusted)`,`Raised, often over 1.3 x ULN`,`Within age-adjusted normal range`,`Low in uncontrolled diabetes, liver disease, oral oestrogen`],[`GH nadir on 75 g OGTT`,`1 ng/mL or more (0.4 with sensitive assay)`,`Under 1 ng/mL (if needed)`,`Avoid in uncontrolled diabetes`],[`Random GH`,`Not used`,`Under 1 ng/mL supports control`,`Pulsatile; stress raises it`],[`MRI pituitary`,`Adenoma in over 95%`,`No residual growth`,`No tumour - check GHRH for ectopic source`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acromegalic or gigantic features`,columns:[`Condition`,`Distinguishing feature`,`IGF-1`],rows:[[`Acromegaly`,`Progressive change, sweating, diabetes, hypertension`,`High`],[`Pachydermoperiostosis`,`Clubbing, forehead skin folds, periostosis`,`Normal`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Cold intolerance, bradycardia, high TSH`,`Low or normal`],[`Insulin-resistance pseudoacromegaly`,`Severe acanthosis, very high insulin`,`Normal`],[`Marfan or Klinefelter (tall stature)`,`Arm span over height, small testes (Klinefelter)`,`Normal`],[`Sotos syndrome`,`Macrocephaly, early overgrowth, learning difficulty`,`Normal`]]},{heading:`Complications of acromegaly and their screening`,columns:[`System`,`Complication`,`Screening test`,`Frequency`],rows:[[`Cardiovascular`,`Hypertension, cardiomyopathy, arrhythmia, valve disease`,`BP, ECG, echo`,`At diagnosis, then yearly or as indicated`],[`Metabolic`,`Diabetes, IGT`,`HbA1c, fasting glucose`,`At diagnosis, then 6-12 monthly`],[`Respiratory`,`Obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Sleep study`,`At diagnosis, repeat after control`],[`Bowel`,`Colonic polyps, cancer`,`Colonoscopy`,`At diagnosis; 5-yearly if polyp or active disease`],[`Thyroid`,`Nodular goitre`,`Examination, ultrasound`,`At diagnosis`],[`Skeleton and nerves`,`Arthropathy, vertebral fractures, carpal tunnel`,`Spine X-ray, clinical`,`At diagnosis and as symptoms arise`]]}],redFlags:[`Visual field loss or falling acuity`,`Sudden severe headache with vomiting or ophthalmoplegia - apoplexy`,`Breathlessness, orthopnoea or arrhythmia - acromegalic cardiomyopathy`,`Severe sleep apnoea with daytime somnolence or hypercapnia`,`Rapid linear growth in a child or adolescent - gigantism`,`Hyperglycaemic crisis or rapidly worsening glucose, especially on pasireotide`,`Jaundice or ALT over 3 times normal on pegvisomant`],pearls:[`**IGF-1 to screen, OGTT to confirm (only if equivocal), IGF-1 to follow.**`,`OGTT: GH nadir **under 1 ng/mL** is normal (**0.4** with sensitive assays).`,`**Random GH is worthless** for diagnosis - it is pulsatile.`,`**Sweating** is an early, often-missed symptom; **skin tags** mark colonic polyps.`,`On **pegvisomant**, follow **IGF-1 only** - GH rises.`,`**Pasireotide raises glucose; pegvisomant lowers it.**`,`**Death is cardiovascular** - normalising IGF-1 restores near-normal survival.`,`Mnemonic **ABCDEF**: Arthropathy, BP, Carpal tunnel, Diabetes, Enlarged organs, Field defect.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-q1`,stem:`A 46-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension notices his wedding ring no longer fits and he sweats excessively. Which is the best initial screening test?`,options:[`Random serum growth hormone`,`Serum IGF-1 adjusted for age and sex`,`Overnight dexamethasone suppression test`,`Pituitary MRI with gadolinium`],answer:1,explanation:`IGF-1 reflects integrated GH secretion over 24 hours, has no diurnal swing and is the recommended screening test for acromegaly. Random GH is pulsatile and rises with stress, so a single value can mislead in either direction. The dexamethasone test screens for Cushing syndrome. MRI comes after biochemical confirmation, because incidental pituitary lesions are common and imaging alone does not prove GH excess.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-q2`,stem:`In the oral glucose tolerance test for acromegaly, which result confirms the diagnosis?`,options:[`Plasma glucose over 200 mg/dL at 2 hours`,`GH rising above 10 ng/mL after glucose`,`IGF-1 falling by 50% after glucose`,`Failure of GH to fall below 1 ng/mL after 75 g glucose`],answer:3,explanation:`Glucose normally suppresses GH secretion; autonomous tumour GH fails to fall below 1 ng/mL (about 0.4 ng/mL with sensitive assays) after 75 g glucose. A paradoxical rise may occur but is not the criterion. A 2-hour glucose over 200 mg/dL diagnoses diabetes, not acromegaly, and IGF-1 does not change acutely after a glucose load.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-q3`,stem:`A woman with acromegaly is controlled on pegvisomant. Which marker should be used to adjust the dose?`,options:[`Serum IGF-1`,`Random serum GH`,`GH nadir on OGTT`,`Tumour volume on MRI`],answer:0,explanation:`Pegvisomant blocks the GH receptor, so IGF-1 production falls while circulating GH rises; GH (random or on OGTT) therefore no longer reflects disease activity and IGF-1 is the only valid marker for titration. MRI is still done to watch the tumour, because pegvisomant does not act on the adenoma, but it is not used to set the dose.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-q4`,stem:`A 52-year-old with acromegaly has persistent raised IGF-1 after surgery and on maximal octreotide LAR. He has poorly controlled diabetes (HbA1c 9.4%). Which second-line drug is most appropriate?`,options:[`Pasireotide LAR`,`Bromocriptine`,`Pegvisomant`,`Higher-dose octreotide LAR`],answer:2,explanation:`Pegvisomant normalises IGF-1 in most patients resistant to somatostatin analogues and also improves insulin sensitivity, making it the best choice with poor glucose control. Pasireotide is effective in SRL resistance but causes hyperglycaemia in over half of patients, which would worsen his diabetes. Bromocriptine is weaker than cabergoline and helps only mild disease. He is already on the maximum octreotide dose.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-q5`,stem:`What is the most common cause of death in untreated acromegaly?`,options:[`Colorectal cancer`,`Cardiovascular disease`,`Pituitary apoplexy`,`Respiratory failure from sleep apnoea`],answer:1,explanation:`Cardiovascular disease - hypertension, acromegalic cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias and heart failure - is the leading cause of death in untreated acromegaly, followed by respiratory disease. Colorectal cancer risk is increased but accounts for fewer deaths. Apoplexy is uncommon. Normalising IGF-1 brings mortality back near that of the general population.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of acromegaly.`,openingLines:[`Acromegaly is chronic GH and IGF-1 excess after epiphyseal fusion, due to a somatotroph pituitary adenoma in over 95%; before fusion it causes gigantism.`,`It is diagnosed by a raised age-adjusted IGF-1 and failure of GH suppression on OGTT, and treated by surgery, then drugs or radiotherapy, aiming for a normal IGF-1.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Somatotroph adenoma (GNAS mutation in 40%); MEN1, AIP, Carney, McCune-Albright, X-LAG; ectopic GHRH (NET) under 1%.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`GH stimulates hepatic IGF-1 - periosteal bone and soft-tissue growth.`,`Direct anti-insulin effect - diabetes; sodium retention - hypertension; cardiomyopathy, OSA.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Coarse face, frontal bossing, prognathism, interdental spacing, macroglossia, spade hands, large feet.`,`Sweating, oily skin, skin tags, headache, carpal tunnel, arthropathy, OSA, voice change.`,`Diabetes, hypertension, cardiomyopathy, goitre, visual field defect, hypogonadism.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`IGF-1 age-adjusted raised; OGTT GH nadir 1 ng/mL or more if IGF-1 equivocal.`,`MRI pituitary; prolactin and pituitary function; visual fields.`,`Comorbidity screen: HbA1c, ECG, echo, sleep study, colonoscopy, thyroid.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Transsphenoidal surgery first-line (cure micro 80-90%, macro 40-60%).`,`Octreotide LAR 20 mg IM 4-weekly or lanreotide 120 mg deep SC 4-weekly.`,`Pasireotide LAR 40-60 mg (hyperglycaemia), pegvisomant 10-30 mg SC daily (monitor IGF-1, LFT), cabergoline for mild disease.`,`Radiotherapy for residual disease; hypopituitarism follow-up.`,`Treat diabetes, hypertension, OSA, heart failure.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Target normal IGF-1 and random GH under 1 ng/mL; IGF-1 3-6 monthly while titrating.`,`MRI, colonoscopy, echo, yearly pituitary function after RT.`]}],mustDraw:[`GH-IGF-1 axis diagram showing GHRH, somatostatin, GH, IGF-1 and sites of action of SRLs and pegvisomant`,`Treatment flow chart: surgery - SRL - pasireotide or pegvisomant - radiotherapy`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:4}],keywords:[`IGF-1`,`OGTT`,`somatostatin analogue`,`pegvisomant`,`transsphenoidal surgery`,`cardiomyopathy`]},{id:`endocrine-acromegaly-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of acromegaly not controlled after surgery.`,openingLines:[`About half of macroadenomas are not cured by surgery; the aim of second-line therapy is a normal age-adjusted IGF-1.`,`Choice depends on IGF-1 level, residual tumour, glucose status, MRI T2 signal and cost.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1 - assess at 12 weeks`,points:[`IGF-1 and random GH; MRI for residual tumour; pituitary function.`,`Consider repeat surgery if a resectable residue remains.`]},{heading:`Step 2 - first medical therapy`,points:[`Mild (IGF-1 up to 2.5 x ULN): cabergoline 0.5 mg twice weekly up to 3.5 mg/week.`,`Moderate-severe: octreotide LAR 20 mg IM or lanreotide 120 mg SC every 4 weeks.`]},{heading:`Step 3 - partial or no response`,points:[`Pasireotide LAR if glucose normal and T2-hyperintense tumour.`,`Pegvisomant alone or with SRL if diabetic or tumour stable.`,`Add cabergoline to SRL for modest residual IGF-1 elevation.`]},{heading:`Step 4 - radiotherapy`,points:[`Radiosurgery or fractionated RT for growing residue or drug failure/cost; bridge with drugs; yearly pituitary tests.`]}],mustDraw:[`Stepwise flow chart from post-operative assessment to medical therapy to radiotherapy`],markSplit:[{part:`Post-op assessment`,marks:1},{part:`First-line medical therapy`,marks:1.5},{part:`Second-line choices`,marks:1.5},{part:`Radiotherapy`,marks:1}],keywords:[`octreotide LAR`,`lanreotide`,`pasireotide`,`pegvisomant`,`cabergoline`,`radiotherapy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Katznelson L et al. Acromegaly: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2014`,`Giustina A et al. Consensus on criteria for acromegaly diagnosis and remission. Pituitary 2024 (Acromegaly Consensus Conference)`,`Giustina A et al. Multidisciplinary management of acromegaly: a consensus. Rev Endocr Metab Disord 2020`,`Luger A et al. ESE clinical practice guideline on functioning and nonfunctioning pituitary adenomas in pregnancy. Eur J Endocrinol 2021`,`WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`]}),a.push({id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism`,title:`Hypopituitarism and Sheehan syndrome`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Hypopituitarism** is deficiency of one or more pituitary hormones - most often from a **pituitary adenoma, its surgery or radiotherapy**, and in India from **Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis after haemorrhage)**, TBI, apoplexy, hypophysitis or snakebite; diagnose with **paired target-gland and pituitary hormones** (low free T4 with normal TSH, low cortisol with non-raised ACTH, low sex steroid with non-raised LH/FSH), replace **hydrocortisone before levothyroxine**, then sex steroids and GH, and teach **steroid sick-day rules**.`,keywords:[`hypopituitarism`,`panhypopituitarism`,`Sheehan syndrome`,`postpartum pituitary necrosis`,`Simmonds disease`,`pituitary apoplexy`,`secondary adrenal insufficiency`,`central hypothyroidism`,`hypogonadotropic hypogonadism`,`adult GH deficiency`,`insulin tolerance test`,`glucagon stimulation test`,`Synacthen test`,`lymphocytic hypophysitis`,`traumatic brain injury`,`Russell's viper`,`sick-day rules`,`hydrocortisone`,`empty sella`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hypopituitarism** is reduced secretion of one or more anterior (and sometimes posterior) pituitary hormones; **panhypopituitarism** means loss of all; isolated deficiencies (ACTH, GH, gonadotrophins) also occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`Prevalence is about **45 per 100,000** with incidence about **4 per 100,000 per year** in European studies; probably higher once TBI and radiotherapy are counted. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In the West, **pituitary adenomas and their treatment cause over half** of cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, **Sheehan syndrome** remains a leading cause: a community study in **Kashmir found it in 3.1% of parous women over 20** (Zargar et al., 2005), most with **home deliveries complicated by haemorrhage**. [Zargar 2005]`,`Indian series show a **diagnostic delay of many years (often 7-20)** after the index delivery; women present with **hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia, anaemia or adrenal crisis** during an intercurrent illness. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Hypopituitarism carries **increased mortality (about 1.2-2-fold)**, from cardiovascular disease, respiratory illness and **unrecognised adrenal crisis**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Tumours**: pituitary macroadenoma (commonest), craniopharyngioma, meningioma, Rathke cyst, germinoma, metastasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic**: **pituitary surgery**, **cranial radiotherapy** (dose-dependent, GH lost first, onset over years), and **immune checkpoint inhibitors** (ipilimumab hypophysitis, anti-PD-1 isolated ACTH deficiency). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Vascular**: **Sheehan syndrome** (postpartum haemorrhage), **pituitary apoplexy**, subarachnoid haemorrhage, and **Russell's viper (and other viper) envenomation** with DIC - a recognised cause in South Asia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Traumatic brain injury**: some degree of hypopituitarism in roughly **25-30% after moderate-severe TBI** (meta-analyses), GH and gonadotrophins most often; **blast injury and sports concussion** also implicated. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Inflammatory and infiltrative**: **lymphocytic hypophysitis** (late pregnancy, postpartum), IgG4 disease, **tuberculosis**, sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, **haemochromatosis** (gonadotrophs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infections**: TB meningitis, bacterial meningitis, fungal; **empty sella** (primary or secondary). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic** (children): **PROP1, POU1F1 (PIT1), HESX1** (septo-optic dysplasia), LHX3/4 - combined pituitary hormone deficiency; **Kallmann syndrome** for isolated gonadotrophin deficiency with anosmia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Sheehan mechanism**: in pregnancy the pituitary enlarges up to about **2-fold from lactotroph hyperplasia** but its portal blood supply does not; **hypovolaemic shock** from PPH causes vasospasm and **ischaemic necrosis**; small sella, DIC and diabetes add to risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Over 70-75% of the gland must be destroyed** before clinical deficiency appears; necrosis is followed by fibrosis, giving an **empty sella** on MRI years later. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Order of loss with compression or radiotherapy**: **GH, then LH/FSH, then TSH, then ACTH**; prolactin rises (stalk effect) except in Sheehan, where it **falls - hence failure of lactation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary adrenal insufficiency spares aldosterone** (controlled by renin-angiotensin), so there is **no hyperkalaemia and less salt craving**, and no hyperpigmentation because ACTH is low - patients are **pale**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyponatraemia** in hypopituitarism is dilutional: cortisol deficiency removes inhibition of **AVP** and impairs free-water excretion (SIADH-like); hypothyroidism contributes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** follows loss of the two counter-regulatory hormones **cortisol and GH**, especially in children and during fasting or illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Masked AVP deficiency**: cortisol is needed to excrete free water, so diabetes insipidus may only appear **after glucocorticoid replacement starts**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Why steroids before thyroxine**: thyroxine raises metabolic rate and **cortisol clearance**, and may tip a borderline adrenal into **crisis**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**By site**: **primary pituitary** (tumour, Sheehan, apoplexy, surgery) vs **hypothalamic** (craniopharyngioma, radiotherapy, TBI, infiltration) - hypothalamic disease more often causes **AVP deficiency and high prolactin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**By extent**: isolated (single axis), partial (multiple), **panhypopituitarism** (all axes, often with AVP deficiency). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By onset**: **acute** (apoplexy, Sheehan with shock, TBI, surgery - life-threatening adrenal crisis) vs **chronic insidious** (tumour, radiotherapy, late Sheehan). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sheehan presentation types**: **acute** (shock persisting after PPH, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, failure of lactation within days) vs **chronic** (presenting years later with amenorrhoea and fatigue). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Pituitary Apoplexy Score (UK 2011)**: level of consciousness, visual acuity, visual fields, ocular palsy - higher scores favour surgery. [SfE 2011]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**ACTH deficiency**: fatigue, weakness, anorexia, nausea, weight loss, **postural dizziness**, hypoglycaemia; **adrenal crisis** during infection, surgery or vomiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TSH deficiency**: cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, weight gain, slowness - milder than primary hypothyroidism, no goitre. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gonadotrophin deficiency**: amenorrhoea, infertility, hot flushes are often absent, **loss of libido**, erectile dysfunction, loss of body hair, **osteoporosis**; delayed puberty in adolescents. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GH deficiency in adults**: reduced energy and quality of life, **central obesity, reduced muscle mass**, dyslipidaemia, low bone density; **short stature** in children. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Sheehan history**: severe **PPH with transfusion**, often home delivery; **failure of lactation**, **no return of menses** after delivery, then loss of axillary and pubic hair, fatigue, hypothyroid symptoms. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Apoplexy history**: **sudden severe headache**, vomiting, visual loss, diplopia, drowsiness - triggers include **anticoagulants, surgery (especially cardiac), dopamine agonists, dynamic tests, pregnancy, head trauma**. [SfE 2011]`,`**AVP deficiency**: polyuria over 3 L/day, nocturia, thirst preference for cold water - see the Polyuria and AVP deficiency topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pale 'alabaster' skin**, fine facial wrinkling and absent tanning - loss of ACTH/MSH (contrast with **pigmentation in Addison disease**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sparse axillary and pubic hair**, breast atrophy, small soft testes, reduced shaving. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postural hypotension**, bradycardia, delayed ankle reflex relaxation, dry skin, puffy face. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Visual fields and eye movements** if a mass or apoplexy is suspected. [SfE 2011]`,`**Central obesity with reduced lean mass** in GH deficiency; **short stature** with plotted growth chart in children. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Anaemia** (normocytic) is common from loss of androgens, thyroid hormone and cortisol. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison)**: **hyperpigmentation, hyperkalaemia, salt craving**, high ACTH and renin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary hypothyroidism**: **high TSH**, goitre in Hashimoto; in hypopituitarism TSH is low or normal with low free T4. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Primary ovarian insufficiency or menopause**: **high FSH**, hot flushes; low or normal FSH with low oestradiol points to the pituitary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anorexia nervosa, severe illness and malnutrition**: functional hypogonadotropism and low T3 (sick euthyroid) - reverses with recovery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exogenous steroid withdrawal**: suppressed ACTH-cortisol from prednisolone, inhaled or topical steroids, or **unlabelled steroid-containing Ayurvedic or quack preparations** - common in Indian practice. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Opioid use**: suppresses gonadotrophins and can cause ACTH deficiency. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hyponatraemia from SIADH**: euvolaemic, but cortisol and thyroid function are normal - always exclude cortisol deficiency before calling SIADH. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Basal paired tests (8-9 am)**: **cortisol**, ACTH, **free T4 with TSH**, **LH, FSH with testosterone (men) or oestradiol (women)**, prolactin, IGF-1, sodium, glucose, haemoglobin. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Morning cortisol**: **under 3 microgram/dL (about 83 nmol/L) confirms** adrenal insufficiency; **over 15 microgram/dL (about 415 nmol/L) makes it unlikely**; values in between need a dynamic test. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Synacthen (cosyntropin) test, 250 microgram IV/IM**: peak cortisol at 30 or 60 minutes **at least 18 microgram/dL (500 nmol/L)** with older polyclonal assays, or **about 14-15 microgram/dL (400-420 nmol/L)** with newer monoclonal assays and LC-MS/MS; **falsely normal within 2-6 weeks of acute ACTH loss** (adrenals not yet atrophied). [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Insulin tolerance test** (gold standard for ACTH and GH): insulin 0.1-0.15 U/kg IV to glucose under 40 mg/dL; peak cortisol **18 microgram/dL or more** and GH **over 5 ng/mL** are normal; **contraindicated** in ischaemic heart disease, seizures and the elderly. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**GH stimulation (adult)**: **glucagon stimulation test** peak GH cut-off **3 ng/mL if BMI under 25**, and **1 ng/mL if BMI over 30** or low pre-test probability (AACE 2019); **macimorelin oral test** cut-off 2.8 ng/mL; with **3 or more other axes deficient and low IGF-1**, GH deficiency is assumed without testing. [AACE 2019]`,`**Central hypothyroidism**: **low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH** - TSH cannot be used for diagnosis or monitoring. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**MRI pituitary**: tumour, apoplexy (haemorrhage), thick stalk (hypophysitis, TB), or **empty sella** in late Sheehan; **CT** in the emergency for apoplexy if MRI unavailable. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Water deprivation test or copeptin** (hypertonic saline or arginine stimulated) if polyuria; do it **after cortisol is replaced**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - hormone replacement`,points:[`**Order matters**: 1. **glucocorticoid**, 2. **levothyroxine** a few days later, 3. **sex steroids**, 4. **GH**, and **desmopressin** if AVP deficient. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hydrocortisone 15-20 mg/day** orally in **2-3 divided doses** - largest on waking (e.g. **10 mg on waking, 5 mg at noon, 2.5-5 mg at 4 pm**); alternative **prednisolone 3-5 mg once daily** (widely used in India for cost and availability). **Fludrocortisone is not needed**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Levothyroxine about 1.6 microgram/kg/day** (start 25-50 microgram in elderly or cardiac patients); titrate to **free T4 in the upper half of the reference range**; ignore TSH. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Women (premenopausal)**: **oestrogen plus progestogen** until the age of natural menopause (about 50) - **transdermal oestradiol** preferred, or combined pill; no progestogen needed after hysterectomy. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Men**: **testosterone** - undecanoate 1000 mg IM every 10-14 weeks, enanthate or cypionate 200-250 mg IM every 2-3 weeks, or 1% gel 50 mg daily; check haematocrit and PSA (over 40). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Fertility**: men - **hCG 1500-2000 IU SC 2-3 times a week, plus FSH** if needed; women - gonadotrophin ovulation induction (or pulsatile GnRH in hypothalamic disease). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Adult GH replacement** for proven deficiency with symptoms: start **0.2-0.4 mg/day SC under 60 years** (0.1-0.2 mg over 60), titrate every 1-2 months to **IGF-1 in the normal range**; weekly long-acting GH (somapacitan) is an option where available; contraindicated in active malignancy. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Interactions**: starting **GH lowers free T4 and cortisol** (unmasking deficiency); **oral oestrogen** raises CBG and GH dose needs - recheck after changes. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Sick-day rules and steroid safety`,points:[`**Rule 1**: **double the daily hydrocortisone** for fever over 38 degrees C or illness needing bed rest or antibiotics, until recovered (usually 2-3 days); **triple** for high fever. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Rule 2**: if **vomiting, diarrhoea or unable to take tablets**, give **hydrocortisone 100 mg IM** and go to hospital. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Procedures**: minor (dental, endoscopy) - double the morning dose; **major surgery** - 100 mg IV at induction then **200 mg/24 h by infusion** (or 50 mg 6-hourly), halving daily as recovery allows. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`Give every patient a **steroid emergency card**, a medical alert bracelet, and an **IM hydrocortisone kit** with family training. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Enzyme-inducing drugs** (rifampicin - common in Indian TB treatment, phenytoin, carbamazepine) **increase hydrocortisone needs** - double the dose during rifampicin. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Avoid over-replacement**: weight gain, hypertension, diabetes, osteoporosis - adjust by symptoms, not by cortisol levels. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency: adrenal crisis and pituitary apoplexy`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis** (hypotension, vomiting, confusion, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia): **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM immediately**, then **200 mg/24 h by infusion or 50 mg IV 6-hourly**; **1 L 0.9% saline in the first hour**; dextrose if hypoglycaemic; treat the trigger. **Do not wait for test results**. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy** is haemorrhage or infarction of a (usually unknown) adenoma: **thunderclap headache**, vomiting, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia (CN III commonest), meningism and reduced consciousness - differentiate from SAH and meningitis. [SfE 2011]`,`**Apoplexy first steps**: ABC, **hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV** then 2-4 mg/h infusion (or 50-100 mg IM 6-hourly) after drawing blood for cortisol, sodium and glucose; correct fluids; **MRI (or CT)** to confirm. [SfE 2011]`,`**Apoplexy - surgery vs conservative (UK 2011)**: **urgent transsphenoidal decompression** for **severely reduced visual acuity, persistent or worsening field defects, or falling consciousness**; **ocular palsies alone** usually recover without surgery. [SfE 2011]`,`**Conservative care** (steroids, fluids, daily vision and electrolytes) gives similar outcomes in selected patients without severe visual loss; a **prolactinoma** with apoplexy may be treated with cabergoline. [SfE 2011]`,`**Acute Sheehan** (persistent shock, hypoglycaemia, failure of lactation after PPH): give **IV hydrocortisone** with resuscitation; test later once stable. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hyponatraemia** in hypopituitarism corrects with hydrocortisone; **watch for over-rapid correction** (limit about **8-10 mmol/L per 24 h**) because water diuresis starts once cortisol is given. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: hydrocortisone may need a **20-40% increase in the third trimester**; **stress dose (100 mg IV) at labour**; thyroxine needs rise - follow free T4 every 4-6 weeks. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Lymphocytic hypophysitis** in late pregnancy or postpartum: headache with ACTH deficiency out of proportion to size - glucocorticoids, avoid surgery unless vision threatened. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Children**: GH deficiency causes **growth failure and hypoglycaemia**; GH therapy (0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day) by paediatric endocrinologist; consider genetic causes - see the Short stature topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: lower hydrocortisone (about 15 mg/day) and thyroxine doses; GH testing with glucagon rather than ITT. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**After TBI or SAH**: test pituitary function **3-6 months and 12 months after injury** in moderate-severe cases or with symptoms; check cortisol acutely if hypotensive or hyponatraemic. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**After snakebite (viper)**: ask about past envenomation in unexplained hypopituitarism; follow up survivors of severe envenomation with DIC or acute kidney injury. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**On immune checkpoint inhibitors**: ACTH deficiency is usually **permanent**; thyroid and gonadal axes may recover. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis** occurs in about **6-8 per 100 patient-years** in adrenal insufficiency and is the main preventable cause of death. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Cardiovascular and metabolic**: GH deficiency and glucocorticoid over-replacement raise visceral fat, dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular risk. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Osteoporosis** from untreated hypogonadism and GH deficiency, and from over-replacement of steroids or thyroxine. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Infertility** and impaired quality of life (fatigue, depression). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sheehan syndrome** is usually **permanent and progressive**; recovery does not occur, but life expectancy approaches normal with adequate replacement and education. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Apoplexy outcomes**: visual acuity and fields improve in most after decompression or conservative care; **ocular palsies recover in most**; long-term hypopituitarism in about **60-80%**. [SfE 2011]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** all suspected hypopituitarism to an endocrinologist; same day for **adrenal crisis, apoplexy, visual loss or sodium under 125 mmol/L**. [SfE 2011]`,`**Review 6-12 monthly**: symptoms, weight, BP, signs of over- or under-replacement of hydrocortisone; **free T4**; testosterone or oestradiol adequacy; **IGF-1 on GH**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**DXA** every 2-3 years in long-standing hypogonadism or on GH; lipids and HbA1c yearly. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**MRI** as for the underlying tumour; **yearly pituitary function tests after radiotherapy**, lifelong. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Re-check sick-day knowledge and emergency kit** at every visit; update the steroid card. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Primary care** checks adherence, drug interactions (rifampicin), immunisations, and anaemia. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Prevention, national programme link and latest updates`,points:[`**Preventing Sheehan = preventing PPH**: institutional delivery, **active management of the third stage with oxytocin 10 IU IM**, early transfusion and shock care under **JSY, LaQshya and the MoHFW PPH guidelines**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Screen women with past severe PPH** who had **failure of lactation or no return of menses** - morning cortisol, free T4, prolactin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**ESE/Endocrine Society 2024 adrenal insufficiency guideline** (glucocorticoid-induced): **lower cortisol cut-offs with modern assays**, structured **stress-dose and sick-day education**, and emergency cards for all at risk. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Endocrine Society 2016 hypopituitarism guideline** remains current: free T4 upper half, hydrocortisone 15-20 mg/day, GH titrated to IGF-1. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**AACE 2019 GH guideline**: BMI-adjusted **glucagon stimulation test cut-offs**; **macimorelin** oral test as an alternative. [AACE 2019]`,`**Nomenclature 2022**: diabetes insipidus renamed **arginine vasopressin deficiency (AVP-D)** by an international working group. [AVP Nomenclature 2022]`,`**Long-acting weekly GH** (somapacitan and others) is now licensed for adults in several countries, improving adherence. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva**: 'Why no hyperkalaemia or pigmentation in Sheehan?' - aldosterone is controlled by renin-angiotensin, and ACTH/MSH is low. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'First sign of Sheehan?' - **failure of lactation** after PPH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: starting levothyroxine before hydrocortisone, precipitating adrenal crisis. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Common mistake**: monitoring central hypothyroidism with TSH, or doing a Synacthen test within weeks of acute ACTH loss (falsely normal). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Mnemonic for causes - 'the 9 Is'**: Invasive, Infarction, Infiltrative, Injury, Immunological, Iatrogenic, Infectious, Idiopathic, Isolated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyponatraemia in a woman with remote PPH** is Sheehan until proved otherwise - check cortisol before fluid restriction. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Hormone replacement in hypopituitarism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Hydrocortisone`,`15-20 mg/day orally in 2-3 doses (10 on waking, 5 noon, 2.5-5 at 4 pm)`,`Lifelong; double for fever, 100 mg IM if vomiting`,`Over-replacement: weight gain, diabetes, osteoporosis; double with rifampicin`],[`Prednisolone (alternative)`,`3-5 mg orally once daily in the morning`,`Lifelong; same sick-day rules`,`Longer action - more over-replacement risk`],[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day orally (25-50 microgram start if elderly or cardiac)`,`Titrate to free T4 upper half of normal every 6-8 weeks`,`Start only after glucocorticoid; AF and bone loss if excessive`],[`Transdermal oestradiol plus progestogen`,`Oestradiol patch 50-100 microgram/24 h or gel; micronised progesterone 200 mg days 1-12`,`Until about age 50`,`VTE (less with transdermal), breast tenderness`],[`Testosterone undecanoate`,`1000 mg IM every 10-14 weeks (or enanthate 250 mg every 2-3 weeks; gel 50 mg/day)`,`Lifelong; check haematocrit, PSA`,`Polycythaemia, acne; avoid in prostate or breast cancer`],[`Growth hormone (somatropin)`,`0.2-0.4 mg SC daily (0.1-0.2 mg over 60)`,`Titrate monthly to IGF-1 within normal`,`Oedema, arthralgia, carpal tunnel; not with active cancer`],[`Desmopressin`,`100-200 microgram orally 1-3 times daily, or 60-120 microgram melt, or 10 microgram nasal`,`Lifelong if AVP deficient; allow a daily breakthrough diuresis`,`Hyponatraemia`],[`Hydrocortisone (crisis/apoplexy)`,`100 mg IV/IM stat, then 200 mg/24 h infusion or 50 mg IV 6-hourly`,`Taper to oral over 1-3 days when stable`,`Hyperglycaemia; never delay for tests`]]},{heading:`Testing each pituitary axis`,columns:[`Axis`,`Basal test`,`Diagnostic cut-off`,`Dynamic test if equivocal`],rows:[[`ACTH-cortisol`,`8-9 am cortisol`,`Under 3 microgram/dL deficient; over 15 sufficient`,`Synacthen 250 microgram (peak 14-18 by assay) or ITT`],[`TSH-thyroid`,`Free T4 with TSH`,`Low free T4 with low or normal TSH`,`None needed`],[`LH/FSH-gonad`,`Testosterone (men), oestradiol and menses (women), LH/FSH`,`Low sex steroid with non-raised LH/FSH; amenorrhoea`,`None needed; postmenopausal - low FSH is diagnostic`],[`GH-IGF-1`,`IGF-1`,`Low IGF-1 with 3+ other deficits = GHD`,`Glucagon test (peak under 3 or 1 by BMI), ITT (under 5), macimorelin (under 2.8)`],[`Prolactin`,`Serum prolactin`,`Low in Sheehan; high with stalk lesion`,`None`],[`AVP`,`Urine volume, serum and urine osmolality, sodium`,`Polyuria over 3 L/day with dilute urine`,`Water deprivation or copeptin stimulation`]]},{heading:`Secondary (pituitary) vs primary (adrenal) adrenal insufficiency`,columns:[`Feature`,`Secondary - hypopituitarism`,`Primary - Addison disease`],rows:[[`ACTH`,`Low or normal`,`High`],[`Skin`,`Pale, alabaster`,`Hyperpigmented (buccal, palmar creases, scars)`],[`Aldosterone and potassium`,`Preserved; potassium normal`,`Deficient; hyperkalaemia, salt craving`],[`Sodium`,`Low (dilutional, AVP-mediated)`,`Low (salt loss)`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`Not needed`,`Needed, 50-200 microgram/day`],[`Other axes`,`Often low T4, LH/FSH, GH`,`Usually intact (autoimmune polyglandular may coexist)`]]},{heading:`Causes of hypopituitarism`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Tumours`,`Macroadenoma, craniopharyngioma, metastasis, germinoma`,`Mass on MRI, field defect`],[`Iatrogenic`,`Pituitary surgery, cranial radiotherapy, checkpoint inhibitors`,`Timeline after treatment`],[`Vascular`,`Sheehan, apoplexy, SAH, viper envenomation`,`PPH, thunderclap headache, snakebite`],[`Trauma`,`Moderate-severe TBI, blast injury`,`GH and gonadotrophins first`],[`Inflammatory and infiltrative`,`Lymphocytic or IgG4 hypophysitis, TB, sarcoid, LCH, haemochromatosis`,`Thick stalk, AVP deficiency`],[`Genetic`,`PROP1, POU1F1, HESX1, Kallmann`,`Childhood onset, anosmia`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension, vomiting, confusion or hypoglycaemia in a patient on or needing steroids - adrenal crisis`,`Thunderclap headache with visual loss, ophthalmoplegia or drowsiness - pituitary apoplexy`,`Persistent shock, hypoglycaemia or failed lactation after postpartum haemorrhage - acute Sheehan`,`Serum sodium under 125 mmol/L or symptomatic hyponatraemia`,`Polyuria with rising sodium after steroids start or after pituitary surgery`,`Falling visual acuity or spreading field defect`,`Unexplained hypoglycaemia in a woman with remote PPH`],pearls:[`**Steroids before thyroxine** - always.`,`**Free T4, not TSH**, to diagnose and monitor central hypothyroidism.`,`**Failure of lactation** is the first clue to Sheehan; **pale skin, normal potassium** separates it from Addison.`,`**Morning cortisol under 3 confirms, over 15 excludes** adrenal insufficiency.`,`**Synacthen is falsely normal** in the first 2-6 weeks after acute ACTH loss.`,`**Sick-day rules**: fever - double; vomiting - 100 mg IM and come in.`,`**Rifampicin doubles** hydrocortisone needs - common in Indian TB patients.`,`**DI can appear after** hydrocortisone is started (masked AVP deficiency).`,`Hormone loss order: **Go Look For The Adrenal** (GH, LH, FSH, TSH, ACTH).`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman had a massive postpartum haemorrhage needing 5 units of blood at home delivery 6 years ago. She could not breastfeed and has had no periods since. She now has fatigue, sodium 124 mmol/L and potassium 4.2 mmol/L. Which skin finding is expected?`,options:[`Hyperpigmentation of palmar creases and buccal mucosa`,`Vitiligo with patchy pigmentation`,`Pale skin with fine wrinkling and sparse body hair`,`Purple abdominal striae`],answer:2,explanation:`This is Sheehan syndrome. ACTH and related melanocortin peptides are low, so the skin is pale with fine wrinkles, and loss of gonadotrophins and ACTH-driven adrenal androgens causes sparse axillary and pubic hair. Hyperpigmentation indicates high ACTH in primary adrenal insufficiency, which would also give hyperkalaemia. Vitiligo is an autoimmune association of Addison disease, and purple striae suggest Cushing syndrome.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-q2`,stem:`A man with a non-functioning macroadenoma has free T4 0.5 ng/dL (low), TSH 1.2 mIU/L, and 8 am cortisol 5 microgram/dL. Which is the correct sequence of treatment?`,options:[`Hydrocortisone first, then levothyroxine a few days later`,`Levothyroxine first, then hydrocortisone after 6 weeks`,`Levothyroxine alone, since cortisol is above 3 microgram/dL`,`Fludrocortisone plus levothyroxine`],answer:0,explanation:`Low free T4 with non-raised TSH means central hypothyroidism, and a morning cortisol of 5 microgram/dL with a macroadenoma suggests ACTH deficiency (confirm dynamically, but cover meanwhile). Thyroxine accelerates cortisol metabolism and can precipitate adrenal crisis, so hydrocortisone comes first. A cortisol above 3 does not exclude deficiency - only a value above about 15 does. Fludrocortisone is unnecessary because aldosterone is preserved in pituitary disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-q3`,stem:`A patient underwent transsphenoidal surgery 10 days ago with new ACTH deficiency suspected. Why is a 250 microgram Synacthen test unreliable now?`,options:[`Synacthen is inactivated by post-operative stress`,`The adrenal cortex has not yet atrophied, so it still responds to exogenous ACTH`,`Post-operative AVP deficiency interferes with the cortisol assay`,`Cortisol-binding globulin is absent after surgery`],answer:1,explanation:`The Synacthen test relies on adrenal atrophy after chronic ACTH lack; in the first 2-6 weeks after acute ACTH loss the adrenal still responds normally to a pharmacological ACTH dose, giving a falsely reassuring result. Morning cortisol or an insulin tolerance test is used early instead. Synacthen is not inactivated by stress, AVP deficiency does not affect the cortisol assay, and CBG is not lost after surgery.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-q4`,stem:`A man on hydrocortisone 20 mg/day for hypopituitarism develops vomiting and diarrhoea and cannot keep tablets down. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Double the oral dose until symptoms settle`,`Stop hydrocortisone until he can eat`,`Switch to prednisolone 5 mg orally`,`Inject hydrocortisone 100 mg IM and attend hospital`],answer:3,explanation:`Vomiting means oral hydrocortisone will not be absorbed, so sick-day rule 2 applies: hydrocortisone 100 mg IM from the emergency kit and urgent hospital attendance for IV hydrocortisone and fluids. Doubling the oral dose is rule 1, for fever when tablets can be kept down. Stopping hydrocortisone risks adrenal crisis, and changing to another oral steroid does not solve the absorption problem.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-q5`,stem:`A 58-year-old man has sudden severe headache, vomiting and left ptosis with a dilated pupil. Visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes with full fields. MRI shows haemorrhage into a 2.4 cm pituitary mass. After IV hydrocortisone, what is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Emergency transsphenoidal decompression within 24 hours`,`Conservative management with close monitoring of vision, consciousness and electrolytes`,`Start cabergoline immediately`,`Lumbar puncture to exclude subarachnoid haemorrhage`],answer:1,explanation:`This is pituitary apoplexy with an isolated third nerve palsy but normal acuity and fields. UK guidelines advise surgery for severely reduced acuity, persistent or worsening field defects, or falling consciousness; ocular palsies alone usually recover with conservative care, so early surgery is not mandatory. Cabergoline is used only if the tumour is a prolactinoma. MRI has already shown the cause, so lumbar puncture adds risk without benefit.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the causes, clinical features, investigation and management of hypopituitarism. Add a note on Sheehan syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Hypopituitarism is deficiency of one or more pituitary hormones, caused mostly by pituitary tumours and their treatment, and in India often by Sheehan syndrome after postpartum haemorrhage.`,`Diagnosis rests on paired pituitary and target-gland hormones, and treatment on replacing glucocorticoid first, then thyroxine, sex steroids and GH.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes (9 Is)`,points:[`Invasive (adenoma, craniopharyngioma), Infarction (Sheehan, apoplexy, viper bite), Infiltrative (TB, sarcoid, haemochromatosis).`,`Injury (TBI, SAH), Immunological (hypophysitis, checkpoint inhibitors), Iatrogenic (surgery, radiotherapy).`,`Infectious, Idiopathic, Isolated/genetic (PROP1, Kallmann).`]},{heading:`Clinical features by axis`,points:[`ACTH: fatigue, postural hypotension, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, pale skin; no hyperkalaemia.`,`TSH: hypothyroid symptoms without goitre; LH/FSH: amenorrhoea, low libido, loss of hair, osteoporosis.`,`GH: reduced energy, central obesity, low muscle mass; children short stature; AVP: polyuria.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`8-9 am cortisol (under 3 deficient, over 15 normal), Synacthen or ITT if intermediate.`,`Free T4 with TSH; LH/FSH with testosterone or oestradiol; prolactin; IGF-1; GH stimulation (glucagon, ITT).`,`Sodium, glucose, Hb; MRI pituitary; water deprivation or copeptin if polyuria.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Hydrocortisone 15-20 mg/day in 2-3 doses first; then levothyroxine 1.6 microgram/kg to free T4 upper half.`,`Sex steroids (transdermal oestradiol plus progestogen; testosterone); hCG/FSH for fertility.`,`GH 0.2-0.4 mg/day SC titrated to IGF-1; desmopressin for AVP deficiency.`,`Sick-day rules, steroid card, IM hydrocortisone kit; double with rifampicin.`]},{heading:`Adrenal crisis and apoplexy`,points:[`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM then 200 mg/24 h; saline; glucose.`,`Apoplexy: steroids, MRI, surgery for severe acuity loss, field defect or falling consciousness.`]},{heading:`Sheehan syndrome`,points:[`Ischaemic necrosis of the enlarged pituitary after PPH with shock; 3.1% of parous women in Kashmir.`,`Failure of lactation, amenorrhoea, loss of hair, pale skin, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia; empty sella on MRI.`,`Lifelong replacement; prevention by institutional delivery and AMTSL with oxytocin.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the hypothalamic-pituitary-target gland axes with the order of hormone loss`,`Table comparing secondary and primary adrenal insufficiency`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Replacement and sick-day rules`,marks:2.5},{part:`Sheehan syndrome`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hypopituitarism`,`Sheehan`,`hydrocortisone`,`free T4`,`sick-day rules`,`apoplexy`]},{id:`endocrine-hypopituitarism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on pituitary apoplexy.`,openingLines:[`Pituitary apoplexy is an acute clinical syndrome from haemorrhage or infarction of a pituitary tumour, usually previously unknown.`,`It is an endocrine and neurosurgical emergency because of adrenal crisis and threat to vision.`],answer:[{heading:`Precipitants`,points:[`Anticoagulants, major surgery (cardiac), dynamic pituitary tests, dopamine agonists, pregnancy, head injury, hypertension.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Thunderclap headache, vomiting, visual loss, field defect, ophthalmoplegia (CN III), meningism, reduced consciousness, shock.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Cortisol, sodium, glucose, full pituitary panel; MRI (CT if unavailable); fields and acuity.`,`Pituitary Apoplexy Score: consciousness, acuity, fields, ocular palsy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV, then 2-4 mg/h or 50-100 mg IM 6-hourly; fluids.`,`Surgery for severe acuity loss, persistent field defect or falling consciousness.`,`Conservative care for isolated ocular palsies; cabergoline if prolactinoma.`,`Long-term: hypopituitarism in 60-80%; MRI follow-up.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected apoplexy - hydrocortisone - MRI - surgery vs conservative by visual and conscious status`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and precipitants`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`apoplexy`,`thunderclap headache`,`hydrocortisone`,`decompression`,`ophthalmoplegia`]}],references:[`Fleseriu M et al. Hormonal replacement in hypopituitarism in adults: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2016`,`Beuschlein F et al. European Society of Endocrinology and Endocrine Society joint clinical guideline: diagnosis and therapy of glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency, 2024`,`Rajasekaran S et al. UK guidelines for the management of pituitary apoplexy. Clin Endocrinol 2011`,`Yuen KCJ et al. AACE 2019 clinical practice guidelines for management of growth hormone deficiency in adults and transition patients`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Zargar AH et al. Epidemiologic aspects of postpartum pituitary hypofunction (Sheehan's syndrome). Fertil Steril 2005`,`MoHFW Guidelines for prevention and management of postpartum haemorrhage (LaQshya / Maternal Health Division), 2023`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus`,title:`Polyuria and arginine vasopressin deficiency/resistance (diabetes insipidus)`,oneLiner:`Diabetes insipidus, renamed in 2022 as **arginine vasopressin deficiency (AVP-D, central)** and **arginine vasopressin resistance (AVP-R, nephrogenic)**, is **hypotonic polyuria (urine over 50 mL/kg/24 h with urine osmolality below 300 mOsm/kg)** from failure to secrete or respond to AVP; it is separated from **primary polydipsia** by the **hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin test** or water deprivation, and AVP-D is treated with **desmopressin**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`diabetes insipidus`,`AVP deficiency`,`AVP-D`,`AVP resistance`,`AVP-R`,`central diabetes insipidus`,`cranial diabetes insipidus`,`nephrogenic diabetes insipidus`,`primary polydipsia`,`psychogenic polydipsia`,`polyuria`,`copeptin`,`water deprivation test`,`desmopressin`,`DDAVP`,`lithium`,`Wolfram syndrome DIDMOAD`,`triple-phase response`,`posterior pituitary bright spot`,`thiazide paradox`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Polyuria** in adults is a urine output **over 50 mL/kg/24 h (about 3 L/day)**; in children it is **over 2 L/m2/day**; a patient complaining of 'frequency' must first have a **24-hour urine volume** measured to separate true polyuria from frequency with normal volume. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypotonic polyuria** (urine osmolality **below 300 mOsm/kg**) has three causes - **AVP deficiency, AVP resistance and primary polydipsia**; polyuria with urine osmolality **above 300 mOsm/kg** is a **solute diuresis** (glucose, urea, mannitol, saline). [Harrison 22e]`,`In **2022** an international working group of the major endocrine societies renamed **central diabetes insipidus** as **arginine vasopressin deficiency (AVP-D)** and **nephrogenic diabetes insipidus** as **arginine vasopressin resistance (AVP-R)**, to stop confusion with diabetes mellitus (including missed desmopressin doses in hospital). [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`,`AVP-D is rare, with a prevalence of about **1 in 25,000**; fewer than 10% of cases are hereditary; acquired AVP-R from **lithium** is now more common than congenital AVP-R. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Primary polydipsia** is the commonest cause of hypotonic polyuria in outpatients: compulsive water drinking occurs in up to **10-20% of chronic psychiatric inpatients**, and 'healthy-lifestyle' drinking of 3-5 L/day is increasingly seen in urban clinics. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In Indian tertiary-centre series of AVP-D, **idiopathic (presumed autoimmune), post-neurosurgical and tumour-related** cases predominate, with **tuberculosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis and germinoma** prominent among infiltrative causes in young people. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab series]`,`**Gestational AVP-D** (transient) affects about **2-4 per 100,000 pregnancies**, caused by placental **vasopressinase (cystine aminopeptidase)** that degrades AVP but not desmopressin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**AVP-D causes - acquired**: idiopathic or autoimmune infundibuloneurohypophysitis (about 30-50%), **pituitary or hypothalamic surgery**, **head injury**, tumours (**craniopharyngioma, germinoma**, metastases from breast and lung), and **infiltration** (Langerhans cell histiocytosis, sarcoidosis, TB, IgG4 disease). [Harrison 22e]`,`A **pituitary adenoma** rarely causes AVP-D before surgery - diabetes insipidus at presentation in a sellar mass suggests **craniopharyngioma, germinoma, metastasis or hypophysitis** instead. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Immune checkpoint inhibitors** (anti-CTLA-4 more than anti-PD-1) cause hypophysitis that occasionally includes AVP-D; **post-COVID and post-vaccine hypophysitis** have been reported. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Hereditary AVP-D**: **autosomal dominant AVP gene mutations** (onset in childhood, misfolded prohormone kills magnocellular neurons) and **Wolfram syndrome (DIDMOAD - diabetes insipidus, diabetes mellitus, optic atrophy, deafness; WFS1 gene)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AVP-R causes**: congenital **X-linked AVPR2 mutations (about 90% of congenital cases)** and **aquaporin-2 (AQP2)** mutations; acquired from **lithium**, **hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia**, relief of urinary obstruction, sickle cell disease, amyloidosis, Sjogren syndrome and demeclocycline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lithium** causes AVP-R in about **20-40%** of long-term users; risk rises with duration and it may persist after the drug is stopped. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Primary polydipsia** has three forms: **psychogenic** (schizophrenia, anticholinergic dry mouth), **dipsogenic** (lowered thirst threshold - hypothalamic lesions, often idiopathic) and **iatrogenic or habitual** (advice to 'drink plenty'). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`AVP is made in the **supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei**, carried with its carrier protein **neurophysin II and copeptin** down the stalk and stored in the **posterior pituitary**; **copeptin** is released in equimolar amounts and is stable in the laboratory, so it is the surrogate marker for AVP. [Harrison 22e]`,`AVP release starts at a plasma osmolality of about **280-285 mOsm/kg**; thirst starts slightly higher, at about **290-295 mOsm/kg** - so normally AVP, not thirst, defends osmolality. [Harrison 22e]`,`AVP binds **V2 receptors** on the basolateral collecting-duct membrane, raises **cAMP**, and inserts **aquaporin-2** channels into the luminal membrane; water then moves into the hypertonic medulla and urine concentrates up to **1200 mOsm/kg**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Symptoms appear only when about **80-85% of magnocellular neurons** are lost; because thirst is intact, most patients keep **plasma sodium normal or high-normal** - hypernatraemia means thirst or water access has failed. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In **primary polydipsia** the excess water suppresses AVP and **washes out the medullary concentration gradient**, so even a normal kidney concentrates poorly on testing - the reason the water deprivation test is often indeterminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lithium** enters principal cells through the **epithelial sodium channel (ENaC)**, inhibits GSK-3 beta and down-regulates **aquaporin-2** - which is why **amiloride**, an ENaC blocker, reduces lithium AVP-R. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Thiazide paradox**: thiazides cause mild volume contraction, increase proximal sodium and water reabsorption and deliver less water to the collecting duct, cutting urine volume by **30-50%** in AVP-R. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Glucocorticoids** are needed to excrete free water, so coexisting **ACTH deficiency masks AVP-D**; starting hydrocortisone can **unmask** polyuria days later. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**AVP deficiency (AVP-D)**: complete or partial; central lesion; responds to desmopressin. [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`,`**AVP resistance (AVP-R)**: congenital or acquired; renal lesion; high AVP and copeptin; little or no response to desmopressin. [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`,`**Primary polydipsia**: excess intake; AVP and copeptin appropriately suppressed; must not be treated with desmopressin because of severe hyponatraemia risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gestational AVP-D**: placental vasopressinase in the third trimester; treat with desmopressin, which resists vasopressinase; resolves within 2-3 weeks after delivery. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Adipsic AVP-D** (osmoreceptor dysfunction): no thirst plus AVP deficiency, often after anterior communicating artery aneurysm clipping or craniopharyngioma surgery - causes dangerous recurrent hypernatraemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Post-surgical triple-phase response**: phase 1 AVP-D (days 1-5, axon shock), phase 2 **SIADH-like antidiuresis** (days 5-10, release of stored AVP from degenerating terminals), phase 3 **permanent AVP-D** if over 80-90% of neurons are lost. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history and examination`,points:[`**Abrupt onset** of polyuria, nocturia and thirst, with a **craving for ice-cold water**, points to **AVP-D**; gradual onset with fluctuating intake and drinking during the day only suggests **primary polydipsia**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Nocturia** waking the patient 3-4 times a night is typical of AVP-D and AVP-R; primary polydipsia patients often sleep through. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **headache, visual field loss, galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, growth failure** (sellar mass), **head injury or recent pituitary surgery**, and **lithium** exposure. [Harrison 22e]`,`In a child, **bed-wetting, irritability, failure to thrive and fever from dehydration** may be the only clues; check for **skin rash, bone lesions and ear discharge** (Langerhans cell histiocytosis). [Nelson 22e]`,`Ask for **psychiatric history**, anticholinergic drugs and **diet fads** ('drink 5 litres a day') before labelling AVP-D. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Elderly patients, unconscious or post-operative patients and infants **cannot drink to thirst** - they present with **hypernatraemia, confusion and seizures** rather than polyuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`Examine **visual fields by confrontation** (bitemporal hemianopia from a sellar or suprasellar mass) and **fundi** (optic atrophy in Wolfram syndrome or chronic compression). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for features of **hypopituitarism**: pale smooth skin, loss of body hair, small testes, short stature in children. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Look for **Langerhans cell histiocytosis** clues: seborrhoeic scalp rash, gum disease with loose teeth, lytic skull lesions, proptosis. [Nelson 22e]`,`Palpate the **bladder** (a large distended bladder and hydronephrosis occur with long-standing high-volume polyuria) and check for lymph nodes, hepatosplenomegaly and erythema nodosum (sarcoidosis, TB). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus** is the commonest cause of polyuria in Indian OPDs - urine sugar and capillary glucose come first. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia and hypokalaemia** cause acquired AVP-R and are both reversible - check calcium and potassium in every polyuric patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic kidney disease, post-obstructive diuresis and recovery phase of acute tubular necrosis** cause isosthenuric or solute polyuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary polydipsia** gives low-normal sodium (often below 140 mmol/L) and low plasma osmolality; AVP-D and AVP-R give high-normal sodium (often above 142 mmol/L). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Urinary frequency without polyuria** - UTI, overactive bladder, benign prostatic hyperplasia - is excluded by a normal 24-hour volume. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diuretic or laxative abuse** and **cerebral salt wasting** after neurosurgery cause polyuria with high urine sodium and volume depletion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line (OPD and district hospital)`,points:[`Confirm polyuria with a **24-hour urine volume**; then measure **urine osmolality** (or specific gravity below 1.005 as a crude marker) on the same collection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Send **plasma sodium, plasma osmolality, glucose, potassium, calcium, creatinine and urea**, with urine glucose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypotonic polyuria** = urine osmolality **below 300 mOsm/kg** with volume above 50 mL/kg/day; if serum sodium is **above 147 mmol/L** with dilute urine, the diagnosis is AVP-D or AVP-R and no deprivation test is needed. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`A **random baseline copeptin above 21.4 pmol/L**, without prior fluid restriction, diagnoses **AVP-R** with near 100% accuracy. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Water deprivation test**: fluids withheld (usually 8 hours) with hourly weight, urine volume and osmolality; stop if **weight falls by more than 3%** or **sodium exceeds 147-150 mmol/L**; then give **desmopressin 2 mcg IM/SC** (or 10-20 mcg intranasal) and measure urine osmolality over the next 2-4 hours. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Water deprivation interpretation: urine osmolality **above 800 mOsm/kg** after deprivation = normal or primary polydipsia; **below 300** with a **rise of more than 50%** after desmopressin = complete AVP-D; **below 300** with **little or no rise** = AVP-R; 300-800 = indeterminate (partial AVP-D vs polydipsia). [Harrison 22e]`,`The indirect water deprivation test has a diagnostic accuracy of only **about 70%** overall and under 50% for primary polydipsia - the reason copeptin-based tests have replaced it where available. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**Hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin**: **3% saline** bolus 250 mL then 0.15 mL/kg/min, with sodium every 30 min until **sodium reaches 150 mmol/L or more**; copeptin **4.9 pmol/L or less** = AVP-D, above 4.9 = primary polydipsia (accuracy about **96%**). [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Arginine-stimulated copeptin**: **L-arginine 0.5 g/kg (maximum 40 g) IV over 30 min**; copeptin at 60 min **3.8 pmol/L or less** = AVP-D; it is safer and simpler but less accurate. [Winzeler Lancet 2019]`,`In the head-to-head trial, hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin was correct in **about 96%** vs **about 74%** for arginine, so **hypertonic saline is the preferred test** where nursing supervision is possible. [Refardt NEJM 2023]`,`**Glucagon-stimulated copeptin** (1 mg SC) and **plasma AVP** assays exist; direct AVP measurement is unreliable (unstable, platelet-bound) and is no longer recommended. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**MRI pituitary with gadolinium**: loss of the **posterior pituitary bright spot on T1** supports AVP-D; **stalk thickening above 3 mm** suggests germinoma, LCH, hypophysitis or TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`In 'idiopathic' AVP-D with stalk thickening, **repeat MRI every 6 months for 2-3 years** (germinoma can declare itself late) and measure **serum and CSF beta-hCG and AFP**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Test **anterior pituitary function** (morning cortisol, TSH with FT4, LH, FSH, testosterone or oestradiol, IGF-1, prolactin) in every AVP-D patient - see the Hypopituitarism topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic testing** (AVP, AVPR2, AQP2, WFS1) in childhood-onset or familial disease. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - AVP-D`,points:[`**Desmopressin (DDAVP)**, a selective **V2 agonist** without pressor (V1) effect, is the drug of choice; start low at bedtime to control nocturia, then add daytime doses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oral desmopressin tablets**: **0.1 mg at bedtime**, titrated to **0.1-0.4 mg two or three times daily** (usual 0.1-1.2 mg/day). [BNF 2025]`,`**Sublingual melts**: **60-120 mcg** two or three times daily (usual 120-720 mcg/day); **intranasal spray** 10 mcg per puff, **10-40 mcg/day** in 1-3 doses; **injection 1-4 mcg SC or IV** once or twice daily. [BNF 2025]`,`The commonest harm is **dilutional hyponatraemia**: advise drinking **only to thirst**, check sodium at **1 week** after starting or changing a dose, and allow a **weekly 'breakthrough' period** of polyuria by delaying one dose. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`Partial AVP-D with urine volume under 4 L/day may need **no drug** if the patient has access to water and is comfortable. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Primary polydipsia**: **reduce intake gradually**, stop anticholinergics, use sugar-free gum or ice chips; there is no proven drug, and **desmopressin is contraindicated**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Treat the cause: stop drugs, correct calcium and potassium, refer sellar masses for neurosurgery; **replace cortisol first** if ACTH deficiency coexists. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management of AVP resistance and lithium`,points:[`**Lithium-induced AVP-R**: discuss with the psychiatrist about **switching mood stabiliser** if feasible; if lithium must continue, add **amiloride 5-10 mg once or twice daily**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg once or twice daily** with a **low-sodium (under 2 g/day) and low-protein diet** reduces urine volume by up to 50% in AVP-R. [Harrison 22e]`,`Thiazides **raise lithium levels by about 25-40%** - halve the lithium dose or monitor levels within 5-7 days when combining. [BNF 2025]`,`**NSAIDs** (indomethacin 25-50 mg two or three times daily) reduce prostaglandin-mediated antagonism of AVP and add 25-50% further reduction, but their renal toxicity limits long-term use. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**High-dose desmopressin** may help partial AVP-R; congenital X-linked AVP-R in infants needs **free access to water, hypotonic feeds and thiazide plus amiloride**. [Nelson 22e]`,`Monitor lithium patients with **sodium, creatinine, calcium and TSH every 6 months** - lithium also causes hyperparathyroidism and hypothyroidism. [NICE CG185 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** with sodium above 150 mmol/L, altered sensorium, inability to drink (post-op, stroke, sedation), or any planned water deprivation or hypertonic saline test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Desmopressin must never be omitted** in a hospitalised AVP-D patient who is nil by mouth - give it **parenterally (1-2 mcg SC/IV)**; omission has caused deaths and triggered a national patient safety alert in the UK. [NHS England Patient Safety Alert 2016]`,`**Hypernatraemia**: calculate free-water deficit = **0.6 x body weight (kg) x (Na/140 - 1)** (0.5 for women and the elderly); replace with oral water or **5% dextrose** and give desmopressin for AVP-D. [Harrison 22e]`,`Lower chronic hypernatraemia by **no more than 10-12 mmol/L in 24 hours** to avoid cerebral oedema; acute hypernatraemia (under 48 hours) may be corrected faster. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-pituitary surgery**: daily weight, strict intake-output, sodium **twice daily for the first 7-10 days**; give desmopressin only when urine is above **250-300 mL/h for 2-3 hours** with rising sodium, and **avoid fixed doses** because the SIADH phase can follow. [Society for Endocrinology inpatient guidance 2018]`,`In **adipsic AVP-D**, prescribe a **fixed daily water intake** with daily weighing and fixed desmopressin, and check sodium weekly. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: desmopressin is **safe** (about 5% oxytocic activity) and resists placental vasopressinase; requirements may **rise** in the third trimester; gestational AVP-D resolves after delivery. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Children**: use bedtime oral desmopressin at small doses, and always look for **germinoma, craniopharyngioma and LCH**; idiopathic AVP-D in a child needs serial MRI. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Elderly**: higher risk of **desmopressin-induced hyponatraemia**, especially at doses used for nocturia - check sodium at 3-7 days and 1 month. [BNF 2025]`,`**CKD**: desmopressin is contraindicated in nocturia formulations when eGFR is below 50 mL/min; in AVP-D use the lowest dose with sodium monitoring. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if osmolality is unavailable, use **urine specific gravity** (1.005 or less suggests hypotonic urine) and paired sodium, and refer for copeptin testing; never run a water deprivation test without hourly weighing. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`Patients with AVP-D need a **steroid and desmopressin alert card**; travel and gastroenteritis days need extra fluid and sometimes extra doses. [Endocrine Society 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Hypernatraemia** (when thirst or water access fails) causes brain shrinkage, **intracranial haemorrhage**, seizures and death; mortality of sodium above 160 mmol/L in hospital is about 40-60%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Desmopressin overdose** causes water intoxication with **hyponatraemic seizures** - the main iatrogenic risk. [BNF 2025]`,`Long-standing high-volume polyuria causes **megacystis, hydroureter and hydronephrosis**, especially in congenital AVP-R. [Nelson 22e]`,`Congenital AVP-R in infancy causes **repeated hypernatraemic dehydration and intellectual disability** if missed. [Nelson 22e]`,`Idiopathic AVP-D is usually permanent; post-traumatic and post-surgical AVP-D recovers in up to **50-60%** within weeks to a year. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Lithium AVP-R improves in a minority after stopping lithium; after **more than 10-15 years** it is often irreversible. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Refer** to an endocrinologist every patient with confirmed hypotonic polyuria; refer urgently with visual field loss, headache with a sellar mass, or sodium above 150 mmol/L. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`Follow-up: **sodium at 1 week** after starting desmopressin, then every 3-6 months once stable, and with every dose change or intercurrent illness. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`Monitor **growth and puberty** in children, and **anterior pituitary function yearly** in infiltrative and tumour-related disease. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Teach the patient: **drink only when thirsty on desmopressin**, recognise **headache, nausea and confusion** as hyponatraemia, and carry an alert card. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`Patients on **lithium** under the mental health programme (**District Mental Health Programme, NMHP**) should have renal function, sodium and polyuria screened every 6 months. [NICE CG185 2023]`,`Use the new terms **'AVP deficiency' and 'AVP resistance'** in prescriptions and discharge summaries so desmopressin is not confused with diabetes mellitus treatment. [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**2022**: international renaming to **AVP deficiency** and **AVP resistance**, adopted by the Endocrine Society, ESE, Pituitary Society, Society for Endocrinology and Japan Endocrine Society. [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`,`**2023**: the head-to-head trial established **hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin** (cut-off 4.9 pmol/L) as the reference test, superior to arginine stimulation. [Refardt NEJM 2023]`,`**Baseline copeptin above 21.4 pmol/L** for AVP-R removes the need for any stimulation test in suspected nephrogenic disease. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`Copeptin assays are available only in a few Indian laboratories; where unavailable, a **carefully supervised water deprivation test** remains acceptable but has about 70% accuracy. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Inpatient guidance stresses **desmopressin as a critical medicine** - never omit, convert to parenteral when nil by mouth. [Society for Endocrinology inpatient guidance 2018]`,`Immune checkpoint inhibitor hypophysitis and **post-COVID-19 hypophysitis** are newer recognised causes of AVP-D. [Endocrine Society 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to a patient with polyuria'** - start with 24-h volume and urine osmolality, separate solute from water diuresis, then AVP-D vs AVP-R vs primary polydipsia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Know the **triple-phase response** after pituitary surgery and the dates of each phase. [Harrison 22e]`,`Know why **thiazides paradoxically reduce polyuria** in AVP-R, and why **amiloride** is specific for lithium. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **giving desmopressin to a primary polydipsia patient** - causes severe hyponatraemia because the patient keeps drinking. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Common mistake: calling it **'central DI' only** in 2026 exams without the new AVP-D/AVP-R terms - name both. [AVP-D/AVP-R nomenclature statement 2022]`,`Remember the copeptin cut-offs: **21.4 (baseline, AVP-R), 4.9 (hypertonic saline, AVP-D), 3.8 (arginine, AVP-D)**. [Refardt NEJM 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for polyuria disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Desmopressin oral tablet`,`0.1 mg at bedtime; usual 0.1-0.4 mg two or three times daily`,`Lifelong in permanent AVP-D; titrate to nocturia and thirst; sodium at 1 week`,`Hyponatraemia, headache; contraindicated in primary polydipsia, SIADH, heart failure`],[`Desmopressin sublingual melt`,`60-120 mcg two or three times daily`,`As above`,`Hyponatraemia; useful with poor tablet absorption`],[`Desmopressin nasal spray`,`10 mcg per puff; 10-40 mcg/day in 1-3 doses`,`Absorption falls with rhinitis or nasal surgery`,`Nasal irritation, hyponatraemia`],[`Desmopressin injection`,`1-4 mcg SC or IV once or twice daily (post-op often 0.5-1 mcg)`,`Nil-by-mouth, post-op, hypernatraemia`,`Hyponatraemia; never give fixed post-op doses in the triple-phase window`],[`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`25 mg once or twice daily with salt restriction`,`Long term in AVP-R`,`Hypokalaemia, raises lithium levels, hyperuricaemia`],[`Amiloride`,`5-10 mg once or twice daily`,`Long term with lithium`,`Hyperkalaemia (avoid with ACE inhibitor in CKD)`],[`Indomethacin`,`25-50 mg two or three times daily`,`Short courses or children with AVP-R`,`Renal impairment, GI bleeding, hypertension`]]},{heading:`Classification of hypotonic polyuria`,columns:[`Type`,`Site and cause`,`Plasma sodium`,`Copeptin`,`Response to desmopressin`],rows:[[`AVP deficiency (central)`,`Hypothalamus or posterior pituitary: idiopathic, surgery, trauma, tumour, LCH, TB`,`High-normal or high`,`Low (4.9 pmol/L or less after hypertonic saline)`,`Urine osmolality rises more than 50%`],[`AVP resistance (nephrogenic)`,`Collecting duct: AVPR2, AQP2, lithium, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia`,`High-normal or high`,`High (baseline above 21.4 pmol/L)`,`Little or no rise`],[`Primary polydipsia`,`Excess intake: psychogenic, dipsogenic, habitual`,`Low-normal`,`Above 4.9 pmol/L after hypertonic saline`,`Partial rise; risk of hyponatraemia`],[`Gestational AVP-D`,`Placental vasopressinase`,`High-normal`,`Variable`,`Responds (desmopressin resists vasopressinase)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of polyuria`,columns:[`Condition`,`Urine osmolality`,`Discriminating feature`,`First test`],rows:[[`Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus`,`Above 300 (solute)`,`Glycosuria, high glucose`,`Capillary glucose, urine sugar`],[`Hypercalcaemia`,`Low (acquired AVP-R)`,`Constipation, stones, high calcium`,`Serum calcium, PTH`],[`Hypokalaemia`,`Low (acquired AVP-R)`,`Weakness, low potassium`,`Serum potassium`],[`CKD or post-obstructive diuresis`,`Around 300 (isosthenuria)`,`Raised creatinine, recent catheterisation`,`Creatinine, ultrasound`],[`Primary polydipsia`,`Low`,`Low-normal sodium, psychiatric history`,`Hypertonic saline copeptin`],[`AVP-D or AVP-R`,`Low`,`High-normal sodium, abrupt onset, nocturia`,`Baseline copeptin, then stimulated copeptin`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`How it is done`,`Cut-off`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`24-h urine volume`,`Timed collection`,`Above 50 mL/kg/day (about 3 L)`,`Confirms polyuria`],[`Urine osmolality`,`Same collection`,`Below 300 mOsm/kg`,`Hypotonic (water) polyuria`],[`Baseline copeptin`,`No prior fluid restriction`,`Above 21.4 pmol/L`,`AVP resistance`],[`Hypertonic saline copeptin`,`3% saline until sodium 150 mmol/L or more`,`4.9 pmol/L or less`,`AVP deficiency (above = primary polydipsia)`],[`Arginine copeptin`,`L-arginine 0.5 g/kg (max 40 g) IV over 30 min; sample at 60 min`,`3.8 pmol/L or less`,`AVP deficiency`],[`Water deprivation then desmopressin`,`Deprive to 3% weight loss or sodium 147-150, then desmopressin 2 mcg`,`Above 800 normal; below 300 with more than 50% rise AVP-D; no rise AVP-R`,`Indeterminate in 300-800 range`],[`MRI pituitary`,`T1 with gadolinium`,`Absent bright spot; stalk above 3 mm`,`AVP-D; infiltrative or neoplastic cause`]]}],redFlags:[`Serum sodium above 150 mmol/L or altered sensorium in a polyuric patient`,`Polyuria with headache, vomiting or visual field loss (sellar or suprasellar mass)`,`Post-pituitary surgery or head injury with urine above 250-300 mL/h`,`Infant or child with polyuria, fever and failure to thrive (congenital AVP-R, LCH, germinoma)`,`Patient on desmopressin with headache, vomiting, confusion or seizure (hyponatraemia)`,`AVP-D patient who is nil by mouth or unable to drink`,`Polyuria with sodium below 130 mmol/L in a psychiatric patient (water intoxication)`],pearls:[`New names (2022): **AVP deficiency** = central DI; **AVP resistance** = nephrogenic DI.`,`Copeptin numbers: **21.4 baseline = AVP-R; 4.9 after hypertonic saline = AVP-D; 3.8 after arginine = AVP-D**.`,`**Sodium high-normal** points to AVP-D or AVP-R; **sodium low-normal** points to primary polydipsia.`,`**Craving ice-cold water** and abrupt onset are classic of AVP-D.`,`Thiazides **reduce** urine volume in AVP-R (thiazide paradox); amiloride is the lithium antidote.`,`**ACTH deficiency masks AVP-D** - polyuria appears after hydrocortisone is started.`,`Triple-phase response: **DI (days 1-5), SIADH (days 5-10), permanent DI**.`,`Wolfram syndrome = **DIDMOAD** (WFS1).`,`**Never omit desmopressin** in a nil-by-mouth patient - give it SC or IV.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman has polyuria of 6 L/day. Plasma sodium is 138 mmol/L and urine osmolality 110 mOsm/kg. Baseline copeptin is 3 pmol/L. During 3% saline infusion her sodium reaches 151 mmol/L and copeptin rises to 9.2 pmol/L. What is the diagnosis?`,options:[`AVP deficiency (central diabetes insipidus)`,`Primary polydipsia`,`AVP resistance (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus)`,`Solute diuresis`],answer:1,explanation:`A hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin above 4.9 pmol/L at a sodium of 150 mmol/L or more means the posterior pituitary secretes AVP normally, so the hypotonic polyuria is from primary polydipsia. AVP deficiency would give copeptin 4.9 or less; AVP resistance shows a baseline copeptin above 21.4 pmol/L; a solute diuresis has urine osmolality above 300.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man on lithium for bipolar disorder for 12 years has polyuria. Baseline copeptin is 38 pmol/L. Lithium cannot be stopped. Which drug specifically reduces lithium entry into collecting-duct cells?`,options:[`Desmopressin`,`Indomethacin`,`Furosemide`,`Amiloride`],answer:3,explanation:`Lithium enters principal cells through the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC); amiloride blocks ENaC and so reduces lithium-induced AVP resistance. Desmopressin is ineffective because AVP levels (copeptin 38 pmol/L) are already high; indomethacin helps through prostaglandin inhibition but is nephrotoxic; furosemide worsens polyuria.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-q3`,stem:`Six days after transsphenoidal surgery for a craniopharyngioma, a patient who had polyuria on days 1-4 and was discharged on fixed-dose desmopressin is readmitted with confusion and a sodium of 118 mmol/L. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Cerebral salt wasting`,`Secondary adrenal insufficiency alone`,`Second (antidiuretic) phase of the triple-phase response`,`Recovery of AVP secretion with permanent cure`],answer:2,explanation:`The triple-phase response gives AVP deficiency in days 1-5, then an SIADH-like phase around days 5-10 as stored AVP leaks from degenerating terminals; fixed desmopressin in this window causes severe hyponatraemia. Cerebral salt wasting causes hypovolaemia and natriuresis; adrenal insufficiency can contribute but does not explain the timing after polyuria; permanent AVP-D often follows the second phase rather than cure.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-q4`,stem:`In a water deprivation test, urine osmolality stays at 180 mOsm/kg after 3% weight loss and rises to 420 mOsm/kg after desmopressin 2 mcg. This indicates:`,options:[`Complete AVP deficiency`,`AVP resistance`,`Primary polydipsia`,`Normal response`],answer:0,explanation:`Failure to concentrate above 300 mOsm/kg with dehydration, followed by a rise of more than 50% (here over 130%) after desmopressin, shows the kidney responds but no endogenous AVP is released - complete AVP deficiency. AVP resistance shows little or no rise after desmopressin; primary polydipsia and normal subjects concentrate during deprivation itself.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-q5`,stem:`A 9-year-old boy presents with polyuria, nocturnal enuresis and growth slowing. MRI shows absent posterior pituitary bright spot and a pituitary stalk 5 mm thick. Which next step is most important?`,options:[`Water deprivation test only and discharge on desmopressin`,`Serum and CSF beta-hCG and AFP with serial MRI`,`Start lithium-protective amiloride`,`Hypertonic saline test to exclude primary polydipsia`],answer:1,explanation:`AVP deficiency with stalk thickening in a child may be the first sign of germinoma or Langerhans cell histiocytosis; tumour markers (beta-hCG, AFP) and serial MRI every 6 months are needed. Desmopressin alone would miss the tumour; amiloride is for lithium; the MRI already makes primary polydipsia unlikely.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the approach to a patient with polyuria. Describe the causes, diagnosis and management of arginine vasopressin deficiency (central diabetes insipidus).`,openingLines:[`Polyuria is a urine output above 50 mL/kg/24 h (about 3 L/day); hypotonic polyuria (urine osmolality below 300 mOsm/kg) is due to AVP deficiency, AVP resistance or primary polydipsia.`,`In 2022 central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus were renamed arginine vasopressin deficiency (AVP-D) and arginine vasopressin resistance (AVP-R).`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1: confirm and classify polyuria`,points:[`24-h urine volume to separate polyuria from frequency.`,`Urine osmolality above 300 = solute diuresis (diabetes mellitus, urea, mannitol, saline).`,`Below 300 = water diuresis: AVP-D, AVP-R or primary polydipsia.`,`Baseline: sodium, glucose, potassium, calcium, creatinine.`]},{heading:`Physiology in brief`,points:[`AVP from supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei; released above 280-285 mOsm/kg.`,`V2 receptor, cAMP, aquaporin-2 insertion in collecting duct.`,`Copeptin co-secreted in equimolar amounts - stable surrogate.`]},{heading:`Causes of AVP deficiency`,points:[`Idiopathic/autoimmune, neurosurgery, head injury.`,`Tumours: craniopharyngioma, germinoma, metastasis.`,`Infiltrative: LCH, sarcoidosis, TB, IgG4; hypophysitis including checkpoint inhibitors.`,`Genetic: AVP gene (autosomal dominant), Wolfram (DIDMOAD).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Sodium above 147 with dilute urine: diagnosis made, no deprivation needed.`,`Baseline copeptin above 21.4 pmol/L: AVP-R.`,`Hypertonic saline copeptin: 4.9 pmol/L or less = AVP-D (accuracy about 96%).`,`Arginine copeptin 3.8 or less at 60 min = AVP-D (less accurate).`,`Water deprivation test: below 300 with more than 50% rise after desmopressin = AVP-D.`,`MRI pituitary: absent bright spot, stalk thickening; anterior pituitary function tests.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Desmopressin oral 0.1-0.4 mg two or three times daily, start at bedtime; melt 60-120 mcg; nasal 10-40 mcg/day; injection 1-4 mcg.`,`Drink only to thirst; sodium at 1 week; weekly breakthrough dose delay.`,`Treat cause; replace cortisol first if ACTH deficient.`,`Inpatient: never omit desmopressin; free-water deficit replacement with 5% dextrose; lower sodium by no more than 10-12 mmol/L/day.`]},{heading:`Special points and complications`,points:[`Post-surgery triple-phase response - avoid fixed doses.`,`Pregnancy: desmopressin safe, resists vasopressinase.`,`Complications: hypernatraemia, desmopressin hyponatraemia, hydronephrosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: polyuria to urine osmolality to copeptin-based separation of AVP-D, AVP-R and primary polydipsia`,`Table of water deprivation test results`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and approach to polyuria`,marks:2},{part:`Causes of AVP-D`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic tests with cut-offs`,marks:3},{part:`Management including inpatient safety`,marks:3}],keywords:[`polyuria`,`AVP deficiency`,`copeptin`,`water deprivation test`,`desmopressin`,`triple-phase response`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-insipidus-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate AVP deficiency, AVP resistance and primary polydipsia.`,openingLines:[`All three cause hypotonic polyuria with urine osmolality below 300 mOsm/kg.`,`They differ in the site of the defect, plasma sodium, copeptin level and response to desmopressin.`],answer:[{heading:`Defect and causes`,points:[`AVP-D: no AVP secretion - idiopathic, surgery, tumour, LCH, TB.`,`AVP-R: kidney cannot respond - AVPR2/AQP2, lithium, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia.`,`Primary polydipsia: excess intake - psychiatric, dipsogenic, habitual.`]},{heading:`Clinical and biochemical clues`,points:[`Sodium high-normal in AVP-D and AVP-R; low-normal in primary polydipsia.`,`Abrupt onset, nocturia and cold-water craving in AVP-D.`]},{heading:`Tests`,points:[`Baseline copeptin above 21.4 pmol/L: AVP-R.`,`Hypertonic saline copeptin 4.9 or less: AVP-D; above 4.9: primary polydipsia.`,`Desmopressin: big rise in AVP-D, little in AVP-R.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`AVP-D: desmopressin.`,`AVP-R: thiazide, low-salt diet, amiloride for lithium, NSAIDs.`,`Primary polydipsia: reduce intake; desmopressin dangerous.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Defect and causes`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical and biochemical clues`,marks:1},{part:`Tests with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`AVP-D`,`AVP-R`,`primary polydipsia`,`copeptin`,`thiazide`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the neurohypophysis`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Arima H, et al. Changing the name of diabetes insipidus: a position statement of the Working Group for Renaming Diabetes Insipidus, 2022`,`Fenske W, et al. A copeptin-based approach in the diagnosis of diabetes insipidus. N Engl J Med 2018`,`Refardt J, et al. Arginine or hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin to diagnose AVP deficiency. N Engl J Med 2023`,`Society for Endocrinology clinical guidance: inpatient management of cranial diabetes insipidus, 2018`,`Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd edition, 2024`]}),o.push({id:`endocrine-siadh`,title:`SIADH and hyponatraemia of endocrine origin`,oneLiner:`**SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis)** is **euvolaemic hypotonic hyponatraemia** (serum osmolality below 275 mOsm/kg) with **inappropriately concentrated urine (above 100 mOsm/kg)** and **urine sodium above 30 mmol/L**, diagnosed only after **adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, diuretics and renal failure are excluded**; it is treated with **fluid restriction, oral urea or tolvaptan**, and severe symptoms with **3% saline boluses**, never raising sodium by more than **10 mmol/L in 24 hours** (8 in high-risk patients) to prevent **osmotic demyelination**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`SIADH`,`SIAD`,`syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone`,`hyponatraemia`,`hyponatremia`,`euvolaemic hyponatraemia`,`tolvaptan`,`vaptan`,`urea`,`fluid restriction`,`3% saline`,`hypertonic saline`,`osmotic demyelination syndrome`,`central pontine myelinolysis`,`Furst formula`,`reset osmostat`,`cerebral salt wasting`,`Schwartz-Bartter criteria`,`secondary adrenal insufficiency`,`carbamazepine hyponatraemia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hyponatraemia** is serum sodium **below 135 mmol/L**; it is **mild 130-135, moderate 125-129 and profound below 125 mmol/L** by biochemistry, and **acute (under 48 hours) or chronic (48 hours or more)** by time. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Hyponatraemia is the commonest electrolyte disorder in hospital: about **15-30% of inpatients** have sodium below 135 mmol/L and **1-7%** below 125 mmol/L. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIADH** (now often called **SIAD - syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis**, as some cases have no measurable AVP) is the **commonest cause of euvolaemic hyponatraemia**, accounting for about **one-third of all hospital hyponatraemia**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In Indian hospital series of hyponatraemia, **SIADH, diuretics, and vomiting or diarrhoea** are the leading causes, with **tuberculous meningitis, pulmonary TB and pneumonia** prominent among SIADH triggers. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab series]`,`Even 'mild' chronic hyponatraemia matters: it causes **gait instability, attention deficits, falls and fractures** in the elderly and is an independent marker of **higher mortality**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Hyponatraemia of endocrine origin** = SIADH, **glucocorticoid deficiency** (primary or secondary adrenal insufficiency) and severe **hypothyroidism** - the last two must be excluded before SIADH is diagnosed. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Malignancy**: **small-cell lung cancer** (ectopic AVP in about 10-15%), head and neck, pancreatic, duodenal, lymphoma and olfactory neuroblastoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary**: pneumonia (bacterial, viral, Legionella), **tuberculosis**, lung abscess, asthma, positive-pressure ventilation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CNS**: meningitis and encephalitis (**TB meningitis is a classic Indian cause**), subarachnoid haemorrhage, stroke, head injury, Guillain-Barre syndrome, acute intermittent porphyria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: **SSRIs** (especially in the elderly), **carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine**, sodium valproate, tricyclics, antipsychotics, **cyclophosphamide, vincristine, cisplatin**, chlorpropamide, **opioids**, PPIs, **MDMA (ecstasy)** and desmopressin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Non-osmotic stimuli**: **nausea, pain and post-operative stress** are among the most potent AVP stimuli - the reason hypotonic fluids after surgery cause hyponatraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other**: HIV, **exercise-associated hyponatraemia** in marathon runners, and **nephrogenic syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis** (gain-of-function AVPR2 mutation, AVP undetectable). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Idiopathic SIADH** occurs mainly in the elderly; an occult malignancy should be sought before accepting it. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Inappropriate AVP acts on **V2 receptors**, inserts aquaporin-2 and causes **water retention**; total body water rises by only about **2-3 L**, not enough for clinical oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`The small volume expansion triggers **natriuresis (ANP, suppressed renin-aldosterone)**, so urine sodium stays **above 30 mmol/L** and the patient settles in a near-euvolaemic steady state. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low urea and uric acid** (urea below 21.6 mg/dL, uric acid below 4 mg/dL) result from expansion-driven increase in their clearance - useful supporting clues. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Brain adaptation**: within 48 hours, brain cells lose potassium, sodium and **organic osmolytes (glutamate, taurine, myo-inositol)** to limit swelling - so chronic hyponatraemia is better tolerated but the brain is **vulnerable to rapid correction**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS)**: too-fast correction shrinks the adapted cells before osmolytes are re-accumulated, injuring oligodendrocytes in the **pons** and extrapontine sites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cortisol deficiency** causes hyponatraemia because CRH is an AVP secretagogue and cortisol normally restrains AVP; **secondary adrenal insufficiency** therefore mimics SIADH exactly (no hyperkalaemia because aldosterone is intact). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypothyroidism** causes hyponatraemia mainly through reduced cardiac output and GFR; it is clinically relevant only in **severe hypothyroidism or myxoedema coma**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In SIADH, urine sodium follows intake; giving **0.9% saline (154 mmol/L)** to a patient with urine osmolality above about 530 mOsm/kg can **lower sodium further** because the salt is excreted in less water. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Essential criteria**: effective serum osmolality **below 275 mOsm/kg**; urine osmolality **above 100 mOsm/kg**; **clinical euvolaemia**; urine sodium **above 30 mmol/L** with normal salt and water intake. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Essential criteria (continued): **no adrenal, thyroid, pituitary or renal insufficiency** and **no recent diuretic use**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Supplementary criteria**: serum uric acid below 4 mg/dL, urea below 21.6 mg/dL, **fractional sodium excretion above 0.5%**, **fractional urea excretion above 55%**, failure to correct after 0.9% saline, and correction with fluid restriction. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Patterns of AVP secretion**: type A erratic (about 40%), type B **reset osmostat** (about 30%, sodium stable around 125-130 mmol/L, can dilute urine after water load), type C constant leak, type D no measurable AVP (nephrogenic SIAD). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Hyponatraemia work-up starts with **serum osmolality**: high (hyperglycaemia, mannitol), normal (**pseudohyponatraemia** - hyperlipidaemia, paraproteins), or low (true hypotonic). [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Corrected sodium in hyperglycaemia**: add **1.6 mmol/L (some use 2.4) for every 100 mg/dL glucose above 100 mg/dL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hypotonic hyponatraemia is then split by **urine osmolality** (100 or less = excess water intake or **beer potomania/tea-and-toast**) and by **urine sodium and volume status**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Mild chronic** hyponatraemia: often 'asymptomatic' but causes **fatigue, poor concentration, unsteady gait and falls**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Moderately severe symptoms**: nausea without vomiting, confusion, headache. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Severe symptoms**: vomiting, **seizures, somnolence, coma (GCS 8 or less)** and cardiorespiratory distress - these reflect cerebral oedema and need hypertonic saline regardless of the sodium number. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Take a **drug history** first: thiazides, SSRIs, carbamazepine/oxcarbazepine, chemotherapy, PPIs, opioids, recent desmopressin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Ask for **cough, haemoptysis, weight loss and smoking** (lung cancer, TB), headache and fever (meningitis), and recent surgery or pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask for **adrenal and pituitary clues**: weakness, anorexia, weight loss, pigmentation, salt craving, postpartum haemorrhage with failed lactation (**Sheehan syndrome** - common in India), long-term steroid intake and sudden withdrawal. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Ask for **hypothyroid symptoms**, fluid intake, alcohol and beer intake, and diet (tea-and-toast). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Volume assessment** is the key step but is only about 50% accurate clinically: look at JVP, postural BP and pulse, skin turgor, mucosa, capillary refill, oedema and ascites. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**SIADH**: no oedema, no postural drop, normal JVP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypovolaemia** (vomiting, diuretics, primary adrenal insufficiency, cerebral salt wasting): postural hypotension, tachycardia, dry mucosa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypervolaemia** (heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, advanced CKD): raised JVP, oedema, ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **pigmentation of palmar creases and buccal mucosa** (primary adrenal insufficiency), and **loss of axillary and pubic hair with pallor** (hypopituitarism). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Look for **clubbing and chest signs** (lung cancer, TB, abscess), **neck stiffness** (meningitis), and **myxoedema features** (slow-relaxing reflexes, puffy face, bradycardia). [Harrison 22e]`,`Document **GCS and focal deficits**; asterixis or extensor plantar suggests another cause or ODS. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Thiazide-induced hyponatraemia** mimics SIADH (urine sodium high, often euvolaemic) - stop the thiazide and reassess after 1-2 weeks. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Secondary adrenal insufficiency** gives identical biochemistry - check **8 am cortisol** in every patient labelled SIADH. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Primary adrenal insufficiency**: hypovolaemia, **hyperkalaemia**, raised urea and pigmentation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral salt wasting** (SAH, neurosurgery): **true hypovolaemia** with high urine sodium; treated with **saline and fludrocortisone**, the opposite of fluid restriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary polydipsia and beer potomania**: urine osmolality **100 mOsm/kg or less**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Pseudohyponatraemia** (lipaemic sample, myeloma) and **hyperglycaemic hyponatraemia** have normal or high serum osmolality. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Advanced CKD** cannot dilute urine and gives high urine sodium - check creatinine. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Paired serum and urine** (spot) samples: serum sodium, potassium, glucose, urea, creatinine, uric acid, **serum osmolality**; urine osmolality and urine sodium **before any treatment**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Calculated osmolality** = 2 x Na + glucose/18 + urea/6 (mg/dL); use measured osmolality when available. [Harrison 22e]`,`**8 am serum cortisol**: below 3-5 mcg/dL (83-140 nmol/L) strongly suggests adrenal insufficiency; above about **15 mcg/dL (about 415 nmol/L)** makes it unlikely; in between do a **short Synacthen test** (250 mcg; peak above about 14-15 mcg/dL with modern assays is normal). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**TSH and FT4**: hyponatraemia should be attributed to hypothyroidism only if hypothyroidism is **severe** (very high TSH, low FT4). [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Search for the cause: **chest X-ray** (and CT chest in smokers or with weight loss), sputum for TB, CT or MRI brain and **lumbar puncture** when CNS symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fractional excretion of urate above 12%** and **of urea above 55%** help in patients on diuretics. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Furst ratio** = (urine Na + urine K) / serum Na: **above 1** means fluid restriction will fail; **0.5-1** restrict to 500 mL/day; **below 0.5** restrict to 1 L/day. [Furst 2000; ESE 2014]`,`**Copeptin** is not routinely needed in SIADH but is low in the rare type D (nephrogenic) form. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - chronic SIADH`,points:[`**Treat the cause**: stop the offending drug (SSRI, carbamazepine, thiazide), treat pneumonia or TB, control pain and nausea. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**First line - fluid restriction**: total intake **500 mL below the 24-h urine output**, usually **800-1000 mL/day**, including tea, milk, dal and fruit; sodium rises slowly (1-2 mmol/L/day). [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Fluid restriction is likely to fail if **urine osmolality is above 500 mOsm/kg**, the **Furst ratio is above 1**, urine volume is under 1500 mL/day, or sodium rises less than 2 mmol/L/day in 24-48 hours. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Second line - oral urea**: **0.25-0.50 g/kg/day** (usually **15-30 g once or twice daily**) dissolved in juice; causes osmotic water diuresis; side effects are poor taste and, rarely, azotaemia; it may need compounding in India. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Tolvaptan** (oral V2 antagonist): start **15 mg once daily** (many now start **7.5 mg** to reduce overcorrection), titrate to **30-60 mg** at 24-hour intervals; **start in hospital** with sodium at **6-8 hours** and daily; **do not fluid restrict** in the first 24 hours. [US Expert Panel 2013; FDA label]`,`Tolvaptan limits: **avoid in liver disease** (hepatotoxicity), limit use to **30 days** per FDA label in hyponatraemia, avoid with **strong CYP3A4 inhibitors** (clarithromycin, ketoconazole), and it is costly; it is available in India. [FDA label 2013]`,`**Loop diuretic plus salt tablets** (furosemide 20-40 mg with NaCl 3 g/day) gave **no added benefit** over fluid restriction alone in a randomised trial and is no longer preferred. [Krisanapan RCT 2020]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** (empagliflozin 25 mg/day) raise sodium in SIADH by glycosuric osmotic diuresis in small randomised trials; they are an emerging option, not yet guideline first line. [Refardt RCT 2020]`,`**Demeclocycline** (300-600 mg twice daily) is now rarely used - slow onset, nephrotoxic, photosensitivity. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Severe symptoms** (vomiting, seizure, GCS 8 or less, cardiorespiratory arrest) of any duration: **150 mL 3% saline IV over 20 minutes**, check sodium, repeat **up to two more times** until sodium rises by **5 mmol/L**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`US alternative: **100 mL 3% saline over 10 minutes**, up to three times. [US Expert Panel 2013]`,`Rapid intermittent boluses (**SALSA trial**) caused **less undercorrection and no more overcorrection** than slow continuous infusion - boluses are preferred. [SALSA RCT 2021]`,`**Correction limits**: rise of **no more than 10 mmol/L in the first 24 hours and 8 mmol/L in each 24 hours thereafter** (ESE); in **high-ODS-risk** patients aim **4-6 and do not exceed 8 mmol/L/24 h**. [ESE 2014; US Expert Panel 2013]`,`**ODS high-risk**: sodium **105 mmol/L or less**, **alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, advanced liver disease**, and thiazide-induced hyponatraemia. [US Expert Panel 2013]`,`**Overcorrection rescue**: stop the hypertonic fluid; give **5% dextrose 10 mL/kg over 1 hour** with **desmopressin 2 mcg IV or SC** (repeat 8-hourly as needed) to bring sodium back below the limit. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`A **sudden water diuresis** (urine above 100 mL/h) during treatment means the cause has gone (cortisol replaced, nausea settled, drug stopped) - danger of overcorrection; consider **desmopressin clamp** (1-2 mcg 6-8 hourly with controlled hypertonic saline) in high-risk patients. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Monitor **sodium every 2-4 hours** in the first 24 hours of active correction, with strict intake-output and urine output hourly. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`If adrenal insufficiency is suspected, give **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV** after drawing cortisol - but expect a brisk water diuresis and overcorrection. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: SSRIs, thiazides and low solute intake (tea-and-toast) commonly combine; check sodium **2-4 weeks after starting an SSRI**. [NICE CKS 2024]`,`**Neurosurgical and SAH patients**: do **not** fluid restrict (cerebral vasospasm risk); differentiate cerebral salt wasting and use saline or fludrocortisone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: physiological fall in sodium of about **4-5 mmol/L** (reset osmostat); oxytocin infusions in large volumes of dextrose cause maternal and neonatal hyponatraemia. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Children**: use **isotonic maintenance fluid** (0.9% saline or balanced solution with dextrose) - hypotonic maintenance fluid causes hospital-acquired hyponatraemia. [AAP 2018]`,`**Cancer patients**: small-cell lung cancer SIADH improves with chemotherapy; tolvaptan or urea bridge the gap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure and cirrhosis** (hypervolaemic hyponatraemia) are covered in the cardiovascular and gastroenterology topics - tolvaptan carries liver risk in cirrhosis. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if osmolality is unavailable, use glucose, urea and spot urine sodium; refer before giving 3% saline without hourly sodium monitoring, but give a **bolus for seizures** before transfer. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cerebral oedema** in acute hyponatraemia (post-op, marathon, MDMA, psychogenic polydipsia) causes seizures, **non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema** and death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osmotic demyelination syndrome**: biphasic course - improvement then, **2-6 days** after correction, **dysarthria, dysphagia, spastic quadriparesis, pseudobulbar palsy and 'locked-in' state**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MRI in ODS** may be normal for up to **2-4 weeks**; recovery occurs in a substantial minority, so supportive care should not be withdrawn early. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Chronic mild hyponatraemia** increases falls (about 4-fold in one study), fractures and osteoporosis. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Hospital mortality is higher with hyponatraemia, mostly reflecting the **underlying disease** (cancer, heart failure, cirrhosis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Admit** with sodium below 125 mmol/L, any neurological symptoms, acute fall, or suspected adrenal crisis. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`**Refer** to an endocrinologist for SIADH without obvious cause, suspected adrenal or pituitary insufficiency, or need for tolvaptan. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`On fluid restriction or urea: sodium **weekly initially**, then monthly once stable. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`On tolvaptan: sodium at **6-8 hours, then daily** for the first days; **liver enzymes monthly**; stop if transaminases rise. [FDA label 2013]`,`In idiopathic SIADH in smokers, **repeat chest imaging** at intervals for occult lung cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel: **written fluid allowance**, avoid binge water drinking and beer, avoid the offending drug in future, report headache, vomiting or confusion. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`The **ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014** guideline and the **US Expert Panel 2013** remain the reference documents; they differ - ESE **advises against vaptans** for moderate or profound SIADH and prefers urea, while the US panel endorses tolvaptan. [ESE 2014; US Expert Panel 2013]`,`The term **'SIAD'** is increasingly used because type D cases have no detectable AVP. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Rapid intermittent bolus** hypertonic saline is now preferred over slow continuous infusion following the SALSA trial. [SALSA RCT 2021]`,`**Low-dose tolvaptan (7.5 mg)** start is increasingly used to cut overcorrection risk. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** and oral **urea** have growing randomised evidence as alternatives to vaptans for chronic SIADH. [Refardt RCT 2020]`,`Newer analyses suggest ODS is rare when correction stays under **8-10 mmol/L/day**, and overly cautious correction prolongs hospital stay - but correction limits are unchanged. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Diagnostic criteria and management of SIADH'** - always write the exclusion of **adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, diuretics and renal failure**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Know the **150 mL 3% saline over 20 minutes, repeat up to 3 times, aim 5 mmol/L rise** protocol. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Know the **10/8 rule** and the overcorrection rescue with **dextrose and desmopressin**. [ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA 2014]`,`Common mistake: **normal saline in SIADH** - may worsen sodium when urine osmolality is high. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **fluid restriction in SAH or cerebral salt wasting** - causes vasospasm and infarction. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mnemonic for causes: **'SIADH - Surgery, Intracranial, Alveolar (lung), Drugs, Hormones (hypothyroid, cortisol deficiency excluded) and malignancy'**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for SIADH`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`3% sodium chloride`,`150 mL IV over 20 min (US: 100 mL over 10 min), repeat up to 3 times`,`Until 5 mmol/L rise or symptoms settle`,`Overcorrection, ODS; needs 2-4 hourly sodium`],[`Oral urea`,`0.25-0.50 g/kg/day; usually 15-30 g once or twice daily`,`Long term in chronic SIADH`,`Bitter taste, GI upset; caution in severe CKD and liver failure`],[`Tolvaptan`,`7.5-15 mg once daily, titrate to 30-60 mg`,`Start in hospital; FDA limit 30 days for hyponatraemia`,`Overcorrection, thirst, hepatotoxicity; avoid in liver disease, hypovolaemia, CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Empagliflozin (emerging)`,`25 mg once daily orally`,`Trial use`,`Genital infection, volume depletion, euglycaemic ketoacidosis`],[`Demeclocycline`,`300-600 mg twice daily`,`Takes 3-5 days to act`,`Nephrotoxicity, photosensitivity; rarely used`],[`Desmopressin (rescue)`,`2 mcg IV or SC, repeat 8-hourly as needed`,`During overcorrection or as a clamp`,`Hyponatraemia if fluids not controlled`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`100 mg IV, then 50 mg 6-hourly in adrenal crisis`,`Until cause defined`,`Brisk water diuresis and overcorrection`]]},{heading:`SIADH diagnostic criteria`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Value`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Serum osmolality`,`Below 275 mOsm/kg`,`Excludes pseudo- and hyperglycaemic hyponatraemia`],[`Urine osmolality`,`Above 100 mOsm/kg`,`Inappropriately concentrated`],[`Volume status`,`Clinical euvolaemia`,`No oedema, no postural drop`],[`Urine sodium`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`With normal salt and water intake`],[`Exclusions`,`Normal cortisol, thyroid, renal function; no recent diuretics`,`Secondary adrenal insufficiency mimics SIADH`],[`Supportive`,`Uric acid below 4 mg/dL, urea below 21.6 mg/dL, FEurea above 55%`,`Correction with fluid restriction, not with saline`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of hypotonic hyponatraemia`,columns:[`Cause`,`Volume status`,`Urine sodium`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Vomiting, diarrhoea`,`Low`,`Below 30 mmol/L`,`History, raised urea`],[`Thiazide diuretic`,`Low or normal`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`Drug history, hypokalaemia`],[`Primary adrenal insufficiency`,`Low`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`Hyperkalaemia, pigmentation`],[`Cerebral salt wasting`,`Low`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`SAH or neurosurgery; true hypovolaemia`],[`SIADH`,`Normal`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`Low urea and uric acid`],[`Secondary adrenal insufficiency`,`Normal`,`Above 30 mmol/L`,`Low 8 am cortisol; pituitary history`],[`Severe hypothyroidism`,`Normal`,`Variable`,`Very high TSH, low FT4`],[`Heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic`,`High`,`Below 30 mmol/L (unless on diuretics)`,`Oedema, ascites, raised JVP`],[`Primary polydipsia, beer potomania`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Urine osmolality 100 or less`]]},{heading:`Severity and correction targets`,columns:[`Situation`,`Target or limit`,`Action`],rows:[[`Severe symptoms, any duration`,`Raise sodium 5 mmol/L in first hours`,`3% saline boluses`],[`First 24 hours (ESE)`,`10 mmol/L maximum`,`Stop hypertonic fluid when reached`],[`Each subsequent 24 hours`,`8 mmol/L maximum`,`Recheck 2-4 hourly during correction`],[`High ODS risk`,`4-6 mmol/L aim; 8 mmol/L maximum`,`Alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, liver disease, sodium 105 or less`],[`Overcorrection`,`Re-lower below limit`,`5% dextrose 10 mL/kg plus desmopressin 2 mcg`],[`Fluid restriction`,`Furst ratio above 1 predicts failure`,`Move to urea or tolvaptan`]]}],redFlags:[`Hyponatraemia with seizure, vomiting, drowsiness or GCS 8 or less`,`Serum sodium below 125 mmol/L, or a fall of 10 mmol/L or more within 48 hours`,`Hyponatraemia with hypotension, hyperkalaemia or pigmentation (adrenal crisis)`,`Sudden large urine output during correction (overcorrection risk)`,`New dysarthria, dysphagia or quadriparesis 2-6 days after correction (ODS)`,`Hyponatraemia with fever and neck stiffness (TB or bacterial meningitis)`,`Hyponatraemia with haemoptysis or weight loss in a smoker (small-cell lung cancer)`],pearls:[`SIADH is a **diagnosis of exclusion** - always check **8 am cortisol and TSH** first.`,`**Secondary adrenal insufficiency** looks exactly like SIADH; primary gives hyperkalaemia.`,`Correction rule: **10 then 8** mmol/L per 24 hours; **8** in high-risk.`,`Severe symptoms: **150 mL of 3% saline over 20 min, up to three times**, until sodium rises 5.`,`**Furst ratio above 1** = fluid restriction will fail.`,`Normal saline can **worsen** SIADH when urine osmolality exceeds about 530 mOsm/kg.`,`**Do not fluid restrict** in subarachnoid haemorrhage.`,`**Tolvaptan**: start in hospital, no fluid restriction in first 24 h, avoid in liver disease.`,`Overcorrection rescue: **D5W plus desmopressin**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-siadh-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman started on sertraline 3 weeks ago has sodium 124 mmol/L, serum osmolality 258 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, urine sodium 52 mmol/L and is clinically euvolaemic. She is alert. Before labelling this SIADH, which test is essential?`,options:[`Plasma copeptin`,`8 am serum cortisol`,`CT abdomen`,`Serum ADH level`],answer:1,explanation:`SIADH can be diagnosed only after excluding adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, diuretic use and renal failure; secondary adrenal insufficiency gives identical biochemistry, so a morning cortisol (with TSH) is essential. Copeptin and direct ADH levels are not required for diagnosis; CT abdomen does not address the main mimic.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-siadh-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with TB meningitis has a generalised seizure; sodium is 116 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?`,options:[`Fluid restriction to 800 mL/day`,`Tolvaptan 15 mg orally`,`0.9% saline 1 L over 4 hours`,`150 mL of 3% saline IV over 20 minutes, repeated as needed`],answer:3,explanation:`A seizure is a severe symptom of cerebral oedema and needs 3% saline boluses (150 mL over 20 minutes, up to three times) to raise sodium by about 5 mmol/L quickly. Fluid restriction and tolvaptan act over hours to days; isotonic saline is too dilute and may worsen sodium in SIADH with concentrated urine.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-siadh-q3`,stem:`A malnourished alcoholic man with sodium 104 mmol/L and potassium 2.8 mmol/L is being corrected. What is the maximum safe rise in sodium in the first 24 hours?`,options:[`8 mmol/L`,`12 mmol/L`,`15 mmol/L`,`20 mmol/L`],answer:0,explanation:`Alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia and sodium of 105 mmol/L or less put him at high risk of osmotic demyelination; the target is 4-6 mmol/L with a ceiling of 8 mmol/L in 24 hours. 10-12 mmol/L is the limit for average-risk patients; 15 and 20 mmol/L are dangerous for anyone with chronic hyponatraemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-siadh-q4`,stem:`A patient with chronic SIADH has urine osmolality 640 mOsm/kg, urine sodium 110 mmol/L, urine potassium 40 mmol/L and serum sodium 126 mmol/L. What does this predict?`,options:[`Fluid restriction to 1 L/day will be effective`,`Normal saline will correct the sodium`,`Fluid restriction alone is likely to fail`,`The diagnosis is cerebral salt wasting`],answer:2,explanation:`The Furst ratio (urine Na + urine K)/serum Na is 150/126, above 1, and urine osmolality is above 500 mOsm/kg - both predict failure of fluid restriction, so oral urea or tolvaptan is needed. Normal saline would be excreted in concentrated urine and may lower sodium; cerebral salt wasting requires hypovolaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-siadh-q5`,stem:`Twelve hours after starting 3% saline for sodium 112 mmol/L, a patient's sodium is 123 mmol/L and urine output has risen to 300 mL/h. What should be done?`,options:[`Continue 3% saline to reach 130 mmol/L`,`Start tolvaptan 15 mg`,`Give furosemide 40 mg IV`,`Stop hypertonic saline and give 5% dextrose with desmopressin 2 mcg`],answer:3,explanation:`An 11 mmol/L rise in 12 hours already exceeds the 10 mmol/L 24-hour limit, and a water diuresis predicts further overcorrection; the rescue is to stop hypertonic fluid and re-lower sodium with 5% dextrose plus desmopressin. Continuing saline, adding tolvaptan or giving furosemide would all raise sodium further and increase osmotic demyelination risk.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-siadh-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define SIADH. Discuss its causes, diagnostic criteria and management, including the prevention of osmotic demyelination.`,openingLines:[`SIADH is euvolaemic hypotonic hyponatraemia caused by AVP secretion or action that is inappropriate to plasma osmolality, with concentrated urine and urine sodium above 30 mmol/L.`,`It is a diagnosis of exclusion: adrenal insufficiency, hypothyroidism, diuretics and renal failure must be ruled out first.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Malignancy: small-cell lung cancer, head and neck, pancreas.`,`Lung: pneumonia, TB, abscess, ventilation.`,`CNS: TB meningitis, SAH, stroke, head injury, GBS.`,`Drugs: SSRIs, carbamazepine, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, opioids, PPIs, MDMA.`,`Others: post-operative pain and nausea, HIV, exercise, idiopathic in elderly.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`AVP via V2 receptor and aquaporin-2 retains water; natriuresis keeps patient euvolaemic.`,`Brain adapts in 48 hours by losing osmolytes - basis of ODS on rapid correction.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria (ESE 2014)`,points:[`Serum osmolality below 275; urine osmolality above 100; urine sodium above 30.`,`Clinical euvolaemia; normal cortisol, thyroid and renal function; no diuretics.`,`Supportive: uric acid below 4, urea below 21.6 mg/dL, FEurea above 55%.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Paired serum and urine osmolality and sodium before treatment.`,`8 am cortisol, TSH, FT4, glucose, creatinine.`,`Chest X-ray or CT, neuroimaging and CSF for cause.`]},{heading:`Acute management`,points:[`Severe symptoms: 150 mL 3% saline over 20 minutes, repeat up to 3 times, aim 5 mmol/L.`,`Limit: 10 mmol/L first 24 hours, 8 thereafter; 8 in high-risk.`,`Monitor sodium 2-4 hourly; rescue overcorrection with D5W and desmopressin.`]},{heading:`Chronic management`,points:[`Treat cause; stop drug.`,`Fluid restriction 800-1000 mL/day; Furst ratio predicts failure.`,`Oral urea 15-30 g/day; tolvaptan 7.5-15 mg started in hospital.`]},{heading:`ODS`,points:[`Risk factors: sodium 105 or less, alcoholism, malnutrition, hypokalaemia, liver disease.`,`Presents 2-6 days later with dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, locked-in state.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm of hyponatraemia: serum osmolality, urine osmolality, urine sodium and volume status`,`Table of correction limits`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Acute management with limits`,marks:3},{part:`Chronic management and ODS`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SIADH`,`hyponatraemia`,`3% saline`,`tolvaptan`,`urea`,`osmotic demyelination`]},{id:`endocrine-siadh-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate SIADH from cerebral salt wasting.`,openingLines:[`Both occur after CNS insults and give hyponatraemia with urine sodium above 30 mmol/L.`,`The key difference is volume status: euvolaemia in SIADH and true hypovolaemia in cerebral salt wasting.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`SIADH: inappropriate AVP, water retention.`,`Cerebral salt wasting: natriuretic peptides cause renal sodium loss, then volume depletion.`]},{heading:`Clinical and lab differences`,points:[`Volume: euvolaemic vs hypovolaemic (postural drop, raised haematocrit and urea).`,`Urine output: normal or low vs high.`,`Uric acid low in both; stays low after correction in CSW.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`SIADH: fluid restriction, urea, tolvaptan.`,`CSW: 0.9% or hypertonic saline and fludrocortisone; never fluid restrict.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical and lab differences`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SIADH`,`cerebral salt wasting`,`volume status`,`fludrocortisone`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Fluid and electrolyte disturbances`,`Spasovski G, et al. Clinical practice guideline on diagnosis and treatment of hyponatraemia (ESE/ESICM/ERA-EDTA), 2014`,`Verbalis JG, et al. Diagnosis, evaluation and treatment of hyponatremia: expert panel recommendations, Am J Med 2013`,`Baek SH, et al. Risk of overcorrection in rapid intermittent bolus vs slow continuous infusion therapies of hypertonic saline (SALSA trial), JAMA Intern Med 2021`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Bornstein SR, et al. Diagnosis and treatment of primary adrenal insufficiency: Endocrine Society guideline, 2016`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, 2023`]}),o.push({id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism`,title:`Primary hyperparathyroidism`,oneLiner:`**Primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT)** is autonomous PTH secretion, usually from a **single parathyroid adenoma (80-85%)**, causing **hypercalcaemia with a high or inappropriately normal PTH**; Indian patients are mostly **symptomatic (stones, bone disease, pancreatitis)**, and **parathyroidectomy** is the only cure, indicated in all symptomatic patients and in asymptomatic ones meeting the **5th International Workshop (2022)** criteria.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`primary hyperparathyroidism`,`PHPT`,`parathyroid adenoma`,`hypercalcaemia`,`PTH`,`normocalcaemic hyperparathyroidism`,`parathyroidectomy`,`minimally invasive parathyroidectomy`,`sestamibi scan`,`fluorocholine PET`,`4D-CT`,`cinacalcet`,`hungry bone syndrome`,`osteitis fibrosa cystica`,`brown tumour`,`familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia`,`MEN1`,`parathyroid carcinoma`,`stones bones groans moans`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**PHPT** is hypercalcaemia caused by **inappropriate, autonomous PTH secretion** from one or more parathyroid glands; PTH is **raised or inappropriately normal** for the calcium. [Harrison 22e]`,`PHPT is the **commonest cause of hypercalcaemia in outpatients**, and **malignancy** the commonest in inpatients; together they cause about **90%** of hypercalcaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Western prevalence is about **1-7 per 1000 adults**, highest in **postmenopausal women** (female:male about **3:1**); about **80% are asymptomatic**, found on routine calcium testing. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`The **Indian PHPT Registry** (PGIMER Chandigarh-led, multicentre since 2005) shows that most Indian patients are **symptomatic** at diagnosis, younger (mean age in the 40s), with **higher calcium and PTH, larger adenomas**, frequent **renal stones, bone disease and pancreatitis**, and very common **vitamin D deficiency**. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`The Indian phenotype is slowly shifting towards **milder disease** as routine calcium testing spreads in urban centres. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`**Normocalcaemic PHPT** - high PTH with consistently normal total and ionised calcium after excluding secondary causes - is recognised as a separate, usually milder phenotype. [5th International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Pathology**: single **adenoma about 80-85%**, **multigland hyperplasia 10-15%**, double adenoma 2-5%, and **parathyroid carcinoma under 1%** in the West (higher in some Indian series). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sporadic adenoma** genetics: **cyclin D1/PRAD1** overexpression (chromosome 11 inversion) and somatic **MEN1** mutations. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hereditary PHPT** (about 5-10%): **MEN1** (menin, multigland, about 95% have PHPT), **MEN2A** (RET), **hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumour syndrome (CDC73/parafibromin, carcinoma risk)**, familial isolated hyperparathyroidism, and **CDC73, CASR, GCM2** variants. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Risk factors**: **neck irradiation** in childhood, long-term **lithium** (raises the calcium set-point; about 10-15% of users have hypercalcaemia), age over 50, female sex. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Thiazides** unmask mild PHPT by reducing urinary calcium - hypercalcaemia persisting 3 months after stopping a thiazide suggests underlying PHPT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tertiary hyperparathyroidism** (autonomous glands after long-standing secondary HPT in CKD or after renal transplant) is a different entity and not covered here - see the Diabetic kidney disease and CKD topics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The adenoma has a **right-shifted calcium set-point** - the **calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR)** needs a higher calcium to suppress PTH, so calcium settles at a new higher level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTH on bone**: stimulates **RANKL** on osteoblasts, activating osteoclasts - continuous excess causes **cortical bone loss** (distal one-third radius) more than trabecular loss (spine). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTH on kidney**: increases distal **calcium reabsorption**, inhibits proximal **phosphate reabsorption** (low phosphate, phosphaturia), inhibits bicarbonate reabsorption (**mild hyperchloraemic acidosis**) and stimulates **1-alpha-hydroxylase** (high calcitriol). [Harrison 22e]`,`Despite increased tubular reabsorption, the high filtered load causes **hypercalciuria** in about 30-40%, with **nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia** causes **nephrogenic AVP resistance** (polyuria, polydipsia), shortened QT, constipation, peptic ulcer (gastrin) and **pancreatitis** (calcium activates trypsinogen). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin D deficiency** - very common in India - amplifies PTH secretion, increases gland size and bone disease, and **masks hypercalcaemia**. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`**Osteitis fibrosa cystica**: severe long-standing excess causes osteoclastic resorption, fibrous marrow replacement and **brown tumours** (haemosiderin-laden giant-cell lesions). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and phenotypes`,points:[`**Symptomatic (classic) PHPT**: kidney stones, nephrocalcinosis, osteitis fibrosa cystica, fragility fractures, pancreatitis, or symptomatic hypercalcaemia. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Asymptomatic PHPT**: detected biochemically without overt target-organ disease - but silent stones, low BMD and vertebral fractures must be looked for, and if present the patient is not truly asymptomatic. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Normocalcaemic PHPT**: PTH raised on at least two occasions over 3-6 months with normal albumin-adjusted and **ionised calcium**, after excluding **vitamin D insufficiency (25OHD below 30 ng/mL)**, **eGFR below 60**, thiazides, lithium, malabsorption and hypercalciuria. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Sporadic vs hereditary**: suspect hereditary disease if **age under 30**, **multigland disease**, family history, **atypical adenoma or carcinoma**, or associated endocrine tumours. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Parathyroid carcinoma** clues: calcium above **14 mg/dL**, PTH more than **3-5 times** the upper limit, **palpable neck mass**, both bone and kidney disease together, and recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Classic mnemonic: **'stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans and thrones'** (polyuria). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal**: recurrent **calcium oxalate or phosphate stones**, renal colic, haematuria, polyuria and nocturia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skeletal**: bone pain, **fragility fractures**, loss of height, proximal myopathy, and **jaw or long-bone swelling** (brown tumours) - a frequent Indian presentation. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`**GI**: constipation, anorexia, nausea, **peptic ulcer**, and **recurrent acute pancreatitis** - check calcium in every patient with pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuropsychiatric**: fatigue, depression, poor concentration, memory loss, and in severe cases confusion and coma. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Others**: hypertension, gout and pseudogout (chondrocalcinosis), and **band keratopathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **lithium, thiazides, calcium and vitamin D supplements**, neck irradiation, family history of stones, hypercalcaemia or endocrine tumours (MEN1). [5th International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`A **palpable neck mass** is unusual with adenomas and should raise suspicion of **parathyroid carcinoma** or a coexisting thyroid nodule. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Bony swellings** (maxilla, mandible, long bones, ribs) suggest **brown tumours** and may be mistaken for giant-cell tumour or metastasis. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`**Band keratopathy** - calcium deposition at the medial and lateral limbus of the cornea - is seen on slit lamp. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **proximal muscle weakness**, kyphosis and loss of height (vertebral fractures). [Harrison 22e]`,`Measure **BP** - hypertension is common and may not improve after surgery. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Look for **MEN clues**: prolactinoma features (galactorrhoea, visual field loss), acromegaly, gastrinoma (recurrent ulcers), and for MEN2A **thyroid nodule and phaeochromocytoma** symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia** (PTHrP, lytic metastases, myeloma): **PTH suppressed** (below about 20 pg/mL), rapid rise, sick patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (FHH)**: inactivating CaSR mutation, mild lifelong hypercalcaemia, normal or mildly raised PTH, **calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01**; surgery does **not** help. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Secondary hyperparathyroidism** (vitamin D deficiency, CKD, malabsorption): **high PTH with low or normal calcium** - the commonest cause of high PTH in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin D toxicity** and **granulomatous disease (TB, sarcoidosis)**: high calcium with **suppressed PTH**; high 25OHD in toxicity, high calcitriol in granulomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lithium and thiazide** effects: stop the drug and recheck after 3 months if safe. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Tertiary hyperparathyroidism**: high calcium and PTH in long-standing CKD or after transplant. [Harrison 22e]`,`For the full work-up of high calcium see the **Approach to hypercalcaemia** topic; hypercalcaemic crisis is covered in **Endocrine emergencies**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - biochemistry and target organs`,points:[`**Albumin-corrected calcium** = measured calcium + 0.8 x (4.0 - albumin g/dL); measure **ionised calcium** when albumin is abnormal or results are borderline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intact PTH** (second- or third-generation assay) **raised or inappropriately normal** (above about 25 pg/mL) with hypercalcaemia confirms PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Serum phosphate** low or low-normal, **chloride/phosphate ratio above 33** (in mg/dL) supports PHPT; **ALP** raised in bone disease and predicts hungry bone syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**25-hydroxyvitamin D**, creatinine with eGFR, and **24-hour urine calcium and creatinine**; calculate **calcium/creatinine clearance ratio** - below 0.01 suggests FHH, above 0.02 PHPT. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**DXA at three sites** - lumbar spine, hip and **distal one-third radius** (cortical site most affected); vertebral fracture assessment by VFA or lateral spine X-ray. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Renal imaging** (ultrasound or low-dose CT) for silent stones and nephrocalcinosis, even when asymptomatic. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**X-ray findings** in severe disease: **subperiosteal resorption on the radial side of the middle phalanges**, tapering of distal clavicles, **'salt-and-pepper' skull**, brown tumours and loss of lamina dura. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic testing** (MEN1, CDC73, CASR, RET) if **age under 30**, multigland disease, family history or carcinoma. [5th International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Localisation - for the surgeon, not for diagnosis`,points:[`**Localisation studies are not diagnostic** - they are done only after biochemical diagnosis and once surgery is planned; a negative scan does not exclude PHPT. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Neck ultrasound** (cheap, also assesses thyroid nodules) plus **Tc-99m sestamibi SPECT/CT** is the standard first-line pair in India; concordant results allow **focused surgery**. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**4D-CT** (multiphase contrast CT) has higher sensitivity than sestamibi, at a higher radiation dose. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**18F-fluorocholine PET/CT** is the most sensitive technique (about 90% or more), is recommended as a first- or second-line option by EANM, and is increasingly available in Indian tertiary centres. [EANM 2021]`,`**Selective venous sampling** for PTH is reserved for reoperations with negative imaging. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management - surgery`,points:[`**Parathyroidectomy** is the only cure and is indicated in **all symptomatic patients**; it is also appropriate in asymptomatic patients **without contraindications** even if no guideline criterion is met. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Asymptomatic surgical criteria (2022)**: serum calcium **above 1 mg/dL (0.25 mmol/L) above the upper limit of normal**; **T-score -2.5 or less** at lumbar spine, hip, femoral neck or distal one-third radius, or **vertebral fracture**; **eGFR below 60 mL/min**; **nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis** on imaging; **24-h urine calcium above 250 mg/day (women) or 300 mg/day (men)**; and **age under 50**. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Focused (minimally invasive) parathyroidectomy** with concordant imaging and **intra-operative PTH** (Miami criterion: fall of **more than 50% at 10 minutes** from the highest pre-excision value) cures about **95-98%**. [AAES 2016]`,`**Bilateral neck exploration** is needed for discordant or negative imaging, MEN1 and multigland disease (subtotal or total with autotransplantation). [AAES 2016]`,`**Parathyroid carcinoma** needs **en bloc resection** with the ipsilateral thyroid lobe; avoid capsular rupture. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Replete vitamin D before surgery** (target 25OHD at least 20-30 ng/mL) with cholecalciferol - it does not worsen hypercalcaemia and reduces hungry bone syndrome. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Surgery by a **high-volume parathyroid surgeon** reduces failure and recurrent laryngeal nerve injury. [AAES 2016]`]},{heading:`Management - medical and non-surgical`,points:[`For patients **not having surgery**: maintain **normal dietary calcium (1000-1200 mg/day)** - restriction worsens PTH and bone loss; drink **2-3 L of fluid daily**; avoid thiazides, lithium and dehydration. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Vitamin D**: keep 25OHD **at least 30 ng/mL** with cholecalciferol **800-2000 IU/day**, monitoring calcium. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Cinacalcet** (calcimimetic, sensitises CaSR): start **30 mg twice daily**, titrate every 2-4 weeks (up to **90 mg three or four times daily** in carcinoma); normalises calcium in about **70-80%** but **does not improve BMD**. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Bone protection**: **alendronate 70 mg weekly** or **denosumab 60 mg SC every 6 months** improves BMD (not calcium) in patients with osteoporosis who cannot have surgery. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Combine **cinacalcet plus a bisphosphonate** when both calcium and BMD need treatment. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Normocalcaemic PHPT**: no routine surgery; monitor yearly, treat osteoporosis medically, and consider surgery if hypercalcaemia or target-organ disease develops. [5th International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and post-operative care - hungry bone syndrome`,points:[`**Admit** with corrected calcium **above 14 mg/dL**, or calcium **above 12 mg/dL with symptoms** (vomiting, confusion, AKI, pancreatitis) - see Endocrine emergencies: **IV saline**, then **zoledronic acid 4 mg IV**, calcitonin for speed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-op calcium**: measure **12-24 hours** after surgery and daily; a transient fall is normal as remaining suppressed glands recover. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hungry bone syndrome**: profound and prolonged hypocalcaemia (corrected calcium **below 8.4 mg/dL for more than 4 days**) with **hypophosphataemia and hypomagnesaemia** after surgery, as demineralised bone avidly takes up calcium. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Hungry bone risk factors: **high pre-op ALP**, large adenoma, **osteitis fibrosa cystica or brown tumours**, very high PTH, older age and **vitamin D deficiency** - all common in Indian PHPT, making the syndrome much more frequent than in Western series. [Indian PHPT Registry]`,`Treat hungry bone with **IV calcium gluconate infusion**, then high-dose oral **elemental calcium 2-4 g/day (sometimes more)** with **calcitriol 0.5-2 mcg/day**, and **magnesium** replacement; it may last weeks to months. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Pre-operative **vitamin D repletion** and, in high-risk patients, pre-operative **bisphosphonate** may reduce hungry bone severity. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Check vocal cords if **hoarseness** after surgery (recurrent laryngeal nerve injury). [AAES 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: maternal hypercalcaemia raises risk of **miscarriage, pre-eclampsia, maternal pancreatitis** and **neonatal hypocalcaemic tetany** (suppressed fetal glands); surgery, if needed, is best in the **second trimester**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Elderly**: surgery is well tolerated under experienced hands; for frail patients, cinacalcet and bisphosphonates are options. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**CKD**: tertiary vs primary hyperparathyroidism must be separated; cinacalcet doses are lower in dialysis use. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MEN1**: multigland disease, high recurrence - **subtotal (3.5-gland) parathyroidectomy** or total with autotransplant, plus **thymectomy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lithium-associated PHPT**: often single adenoma; may continue lithium after surgery; cinacalcet if surgery not possible. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if sestamibi is unavailable, a good **neck ultrasound by an experienced operator** plus bilateral neck exploration by a trained surgeon still cures most patients. [AAES 2016]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Untreated: **recurrent stones, CKD, fractures, pancreatitis**, neurocognitive decline and possibly increased cardiovascular risk. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`After successful surgery, **BMD rises 5-12%** over 1-4 years (spine and hip most), **stone recurrence falls**, and fracture risk drops. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Persistent PHPT** (hypercalcaemia within 6 months of surgery) occurs in about **2-5%** and **recurrence** (after 6 months of normocalcaemia) in about 1-10%; higher in MEN1 and multigland disease. [AAES 2016]`,`About **25-40%** of asymptomatic patients followed without surgery show **progression** (calcium rise, BMD loss or stones) over 10-15 years. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Parathyroid carcinoma** has a **5-year survival of about 80-85%**; death usually comes from uncontrolled hypercalcaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Refer** to an endocrinologist every patient with PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia, and to an **experienced endocrine surgeon** once surgery is indicated. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Non-operated patients**: serum calcium and creatinine **every year**; **DXA every 1-2 years** (three sites); renal imaging if stones are suspected; 24-h urine calcium if clinically indicated. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**Post-op**: calcium at 1-2 weeks and **6 months** (cure defined as normocalcaemia at 6 months), then yearly; DXA at 1-2 years. [AAES 2016]`,`Counsel: **adequate fluid**, normal dietary calcium, avoid thiazides and dehydration, report vomiting or confusion. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Consider PHPT when a primary care physician sees **recurrent stones, low BMD at a young age or pancreatitis** - a single serum calcium in the NP-NCD OPD can pick it up. [Indian PHPT Registry]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**5th International Workshop on asymptomatic PHPT (2022)**: urinary calcium threshold lowered from **400 to 250 mg/day (women) and 300 mg/day (men)**, and **'kidney stone risk profile'** removed as a separate criterion. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`2022 workshop: surgery is **appropriate in all patients** who meet no criteria provided there are no contraindications - criteria define who **should**, not who **may**, have surgery. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`2022 workshop: **normocalcaemic PHPT** definition tightened (25OHD at least 30 ng/mL, eGFR at least 60, ionised calcium normal). [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`2022 workshop: **genetic testing** recommended at age under 30 years, multigland disease, family history or atypical/malignant histology. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`**18F-fluorocholine PET/CT** now a first- or second-line localisation tool. [EANM 2021]`,`Indian data continue to show **predominantly symptomatic disease** with frequent hungry bone syndrome, but a rising share of asymptomatic cases. [Indian PHPT Registry]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Clinical features, diagnosis and management of primary hyperparathyroidism'** - write the **2022 surgical criteria** exactly. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Know how to separate **PHPT from FHH** (calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01) and from **secondary HPT** (calcium low or normal). [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Know the **X-ray signs**: subperiosteal resorption of middle phalanges (radial side), salt-and-pepper skull, brown tumour, clavicle tapering. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: ordering a **sestamibi scan to 'diagnose' PHPT** - imaging only localises. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Common mistake: **restricting dietary calcium** or **withholding vitamin D** - both worsen PTH and bone disease. [5th International Workshop 2022]`,`Remember: **cinacalcet lowers calcium but not BMD**; bisphosphonates raise BMD but do not lower PTH. [5th International Workshop 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in primary hyperparathyroidism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cinacalcet`,`30 mg twice daily orally; up to 90 mg three or four times daily in carcinoma`,`Titrate every 2-4 weeks to calcium`,`Nausea, vomiting, hypocalcaemia; no BMD gain; CYP2D6 interactions`],[`Alendronate`,`70 mg once weekly orally`,`3-5 years with review`,`Oesophagitis; avoid if eGFR below 35`],[`Denosumab`,`60 mg SC every 6 months`,`Long term; never stop abruptly`,`Hypocalcaemia, rebound fractures on stopping`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV over 15 min (hypercalcaemia) or 5 mg yearly (osteoporosis)`,`Single dose in crisis`,`Acute-phase reaction, hypocalcaemia, renal toxicity`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`800-2000 IU/day orally (or 60,000 IU weekly loading for deficiency)`,`Target 25OHD at least 30 ng/mL`,`Monitor calcium`],[`Calcium gluconate 10% (post-op)`,`1-2 g IV bolus then infusion 0.5-1.5 mg/kg/h elemental`,`Until oral therapy controls calcium`,`Extravasation necrosis; ECG monitoring`],[`Calcitriol (post-op hungry bone)`,`0.5-2 mcg/day orally in divided doses`,`Weeks to months`,`Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria`]]},{heading:`Indications for parathyroidectomy (5th International Workshop 2022)`,columns:[`Domain`,`Criterion`],rows:[[`Symptoms`,`Any symptomatic PHPT (stones, fractures, osteitis fibrosa, pancreatitis, symptomatic hypercalcaemia)`],[`Serum calcium`,`More than 1 mg/dL (0.25 mmol/L) above upper limit of normal`],[`Skeleton`,`T-score -2.5 or less at spine, hip, femoral neck or distal 1/3 radius; or vertebral fracture`],[`Kidney function`,`eGFR below 60 mL/min/1.73 m2`],[`Kidney imaging`,`Nephrolithiasis or nephrocalcinosis`],[`Urine calcium`,`Above 250 mg/day (women) or 300 mg/day (men)`],[`Age`,`Under 50 years`],[`Other`,`Patient preference or inability to follow up, with no contraindication`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of high calcium or high PTH`,columns:[`Condition`,`Calcium`,`PTH`,`Phosphate`,`Key discriminator`],rows:[[`Primary hyperparathyroidism`,`High`,`High or inappropriately normal`,`Low-normal`,`Ca/Cr clearance ratio above 0.02`],[`Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia`,`Mildly high`,`Normal or mildly high`,`Normal`,`Ca/Cr clearance ratio below 0.01; family history`],[`Secondary HPT (vitamin D deficiency, CKD)`,`Low or normal`,`High`,`Low (vit D) or high (CKD)`,`Low 25OHD or raised creatinine`],[`Tertiary HPT`,`High`,`Very high`,`High`,`Long-standing CKD or transplant`],[`Malignancy (PTHrP, metastases)`,`High, rising fast`,`Suppressed`,`Low or normal`,`Raised PTHrP, known cancer`],[`Vitamin D toxicity or granuloma`,`High`,`Suppressed`,`High-normal`,`High 25OHD or calcitriol`],[`Normocalcaemic PHPT`,`Normal (ionised normal)`,`High`,`Normal`,`Secondary causes excluded`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their purpose`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding in PHPT`,`Purpose`],rows:[[`Corrected and ionised calcium`,`High`,`Confirms hypercalcaemia`],[`Intact PTH`,`High or inappropriately normal`,`Proves PTH-dependent cause`],[`25OHD`,`Often low in India`,`Replete before surgery; exclude secondary HPT`],[`24-h urine calcium and Ca/Cr clearance ratio`,`Above 0.02; often hypercalciuric`,`Excludes FHH; surgical criterion`],[`DXA (spine, hip, distal radius)`,`Low BMD, worst at radius`,`Surgical criterion; baseline`],[`Renal ultrasound or CT`,`Stones, nephrocalcinosis`,`Surgical criterion`],[`Neck USG plus sestamibi SPECT/CT`,`Single adenoma localised`,`Plan focused surgery`],[`Fluorocholine PET/CT or 4D-CT`,`Small or ectopic gland`,`Discordant or negative first-line imaging`]]}],redFlags:[`Corrected calcium above 14 mg/dL, or above 12 mg/dL with vomiting, confusion or AKI`,`Hypercalcaemia with acute pancreatitis`,`Palpable neck mass, calcium above 14 mg/dL or PTH over 3-5 times normal (parathyroid carcinoma)`,`Pathological fracture or expanding jaw or bone swelling (brown tumour)`,`Post-parathyroidectomy tingling, carpopedal spasm or seizure (hungry bone syndrome)`,`Hypercalcaemia in pregnancy`,`Hypercalcaemia with suppressed PTH (look for malignancy)`],pearls:[`**Stones, bones, groans, moans and thrones** - and in India add **brown tumours and pancreatitis**.`,`PTH **high or inappropriately normal** with high calcium = PHPT until proven otherwise.`,`Imaging **localises, never diagnoses**.`,`2022 urine calcium criterion: **above 250 (women) / 300 (men) mg/day**.`,`Cortical bone suffers most: measure the **distal one-third radius**.`,`**Ca/Cr clearance ratio below 0.01 = FHH** - do not operate.`,`**Cinacalcet lowers calcium, not BMD**.`,`Do **not** restrict dietary calcium; **do** replete vitamin D.`,`High pre-op **ALP** predicts **hungry bone syndrome**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old woman has corrected calcium 10.9 mg/dL (ULN 10.2), PTH 98 pg/mL, eGFR 82, 24-h urine calcium 280 mg/day, and DXA T-score -2.1 at spine and -2.0 at distal radius. No stones on ultrasound. By the 2022 International Workshop criteria, why is surgery indicated?`,options:[`Serum calcium more than 0.5 mg/dL above normal`,`T-score below -2.0`,`Urine calcium above 250 mg/day`,`PTH more than twice normal`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2022 workshop lowered the urinary calcium threshold to above 250 mg/day in women (300 in men), so 280 mg/day qualifies. The calcium criterion is more than 1 mg/dL above the upper limit (she is 0.7 above); the skeletal criterion is a T-score of -2.5 or less; PTH level itself is not a surgical criterion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-q2`,stem:`A 32-year-old man has calcium 10.8 mg/dL, PTH 62 pg/mL, 24-h urine calcium 60 mg, and calcium/creatinine clearance ratio 0.006. His mother had a 'failed' neck operation. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia`,`Parathyroid adenoma`,`MEN1 multigland hyperplasia`,`Vitamin D toxicity`],answer:0,explanation:`A calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01 with mild hypercalcaemia, normal-high PTH and a family member whose neck exploration failed is typical of FHH from an inactivating CaSR mutation; surgery does not help. PHPT (adenoma or MEN1) usually gives a ratio above 0.02; vitamin D toxicity suppresses PTH.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-q3`,stem:`Which radiological finding is most characteristic of hyperparathyroid bone disease?`,options:[`Codfish vertebrae`,`Looser zones in the pubic rami`,`Ivory vertebra`,`Subperiosteal resorption on the radial side of the middle phalanges`],answer:3,explanation:`Subperiosteal bone resorption on the radial aspect of the middle phalanges of the index and middle fingers is pathognomonic of hyperparathyroidism. Looser zones are typical of osteomalacia; codfish vertebrae occur in osteoporosis and osteomalacia; ivory vertebra suggests Paget disease, lymphoma or osteoblastic metastases.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-q4`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman with a 4 cm parathyroid adenoma, ALP 1100 IU/L and multiple brown tumours undergoes successful parathyroidectomy. On day 3 she has perioral tingling; calcium 7.1 mg/dL, phosphate 1.8 mg/dL, magnesium 1.4 mg/dL, PTH 48 pg/mL. What is the diagnosis?`,options:[`Permanent post-surgical hypoparathyroidism`,`Hungry bone syndrome`,`Pseudohypoparathyroidism`,`Persistent hyperparathyroidism`],answer:1,explanation:`Low calcium with low phosphate and magnesium and a measurable PTH after removing a large adenoma in a patient with high ALP and osteitis fibrosa is hungry bone syndrome - remineralising bone takes up calcium and phosphate. Post-surgical hypoparathyroidism gives low PTH with high phosphate; persistent PHPT keeps calcium high; pseudohypoparathyroidism is a congenital PTH-resistance disorder.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-q5`,stem:`A frail 81-year-old woman with PHPT (calcium 11.6 mg/dL) and osteoporosis declines surgery. Which combination best controls both calcium and bone density?`,options:[`Calcium restriction to 400 mg/day plus thiazide`,`Cinacalcet alone`,`Cinacalcet plus alendronate`,`Furosemide plus calcitonin`],answer:2,explanation:`Cinacalcet normalises calcium in most patients but does not improve BMD, while bisphosphonates improve BMD without lowering calcium - so the combination treats both. Calcium restriction worsens PTH and bone loss and thiazides raise calcium; furosemide and calcitonin are short-term crisis measures only.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the aetiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management of primary hyperparathyroidism. Add a note on the indications for surgery in asymptomatic disease.`,openingLines:[`Primary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous PTH secretion, usually from a single parathyroid adenoma, causing hypercalcaemia with a high or inappropriately normal PTH.`,`It is the commonest cause of hypercalcaemia in outpatients; in India it is mostly symptomatic, with stones, bone disease and pancreatitis.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Single adenoma 80-85%, hyperplasia 10-15%, double adenoma 2-5%, carcinoma under 1%.`,`Hereditary: MEN1, MEN2A, HPT-jaw tumour (CDC73).`,`Risk factors: neck irradiation, lithium.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Right-shifted CaSR set-point.`,`PTH: bone resorption via RANKL, distal calcium reabsorption, phosphaturia, calcitriol synthesis.`,`Vitamin D deficiency worsens disease in India.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Stones, bones, groans, moans, thrones.`,`Brown tumours, fractures, pancreatitis, peptic ulcer, band keratopathy.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Corrected or ionised calcium high with PTH high or inappropriately normal.`,`Phosphate low-normal; 25OHD; 24-h urine calcium; Ca/Cr clearance ratio above 0.02 (below 0.01 FHH).`,`DXA at spine, hip and distal radius; renal imaging.`,`X-rays: subperiosteal resorption, salt-and-pepper skull.`,`Localisation after diagnosis: neck USG, sestamibi SPECT/CT, 4D-CT, fluorocholine PET.`]},{heading:`Surgical indications (2022)`,points:[`All symptomatic patients.`,`Calcium more than 1 mg/dL above ULN; T-score -2.5 or less or vertebral fracture.`,`eGFR below 60; stones or nephrocalcinosis; urine calcium above 250/300 mg/day; age under 50.`]},{heading:`Surgery and post-op care`,points:[`Focused parathyroidectomy with intra-op PTH (more than 50% fall at 10 min).`,`Bilateral exploration for MEN1 or discordant imaging.`,`Hungry bone syndrome: IV calcium, calcitriol, magnesium.`]},{heading:`Medical management`,points:[`Normal calcium intake, hydration, vitamin D repletion.`,`Cinacalcet 30 mg twice daily for calcium; bisphosphonate or denosumab for BMD.`,`Annual calcium and creatinine; DXA every 1-2 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of PTH actions on bone, kidney and gut`,`Table of 2022 surgical criteria`,`Diagnostic flow chart for hypercalcaemia with high PTH`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and localisation`,marks:2},{part:`Surgical criteria and surgery`,marks:2},{part:`Medical management and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PHPT`,`parathyroid adenoma`,`PTH`,`parathyroidectomy`,`5th International Workshop`,`hungry bone`]},{id:`endocrine-primary-hyperparathyroidism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on hungry bone syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Hungry bone syndrome is severe, prolonged hypocalcaemia with hypophosphataemia and hypomagnesaemia after parathyroidectomy, caused by rapid remineralisation of demineralised bone.`,`It is defined as calcium below 8.4 mg/dL for more than 4 days after surgery, and is common in Indian patients with severe bone disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`High pre-op ALP, large adenoma, very high PTH.`,`Osteitis fibrosa cystica, brown tumours, vitamin D deficiency, older age.`]},{heading:`Features`,points:[`Perioral tingling, carpopedal spasm, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, seizures.`,`Low calcium, low phosphate, low magnesium, PTH normal or high (vs hypoparathyroidism: low PTH, high phosphate).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`IV 10% calcium gluconate bolus and infusion.`,`Oral elemental calcium 2-4 g/day or more, calcitriol 0.5-2 mcg/day, magnesium.`,`Prevention: vitamin D repletion and pre-op bisphosphonate in high-risk patients.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing hungry bone syndrome with post-surgical hypoparathyroidism`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Features and differentiation`,marks:1},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hungry bone syndrome`,`parathyroidectomy`,`hypocalcaemia`,`calcitriol`,`ALP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the parathyroid gland and calcium homeostasis`,`Bilezikian JP, et al. Evaluation and management of primary hyperparathyroidism: summary statement and guidelines from the Fifth International Workshop. J Bone Miner Res 2022`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Wilhelm SM, et al. American Association of Endocrine Surgeons guidelines for definitive management of primary hyperparathyroidism, JAMA Surg 2016`,`Petranovic Ovcaricek P, et al. EANM practice guidelines for parathyroid imaging, 2021`,`Bhadada SK, et al. Indian Primary Hyperparathyroidism Registry: clinical profile and outcomes (PGIMER Chandigarh)`]}),o.push({id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism`,title:`Hypoparathyroidism and hypocalcaemia`,oneLiner:`**Hypoparathyroidism** is **hypocalcaemia with high phosphate and a low or inappropriately normal PTH**, caused in about **75% by neck surgery (post-thyroidectomy)**; acute symptomatic hypocalcaemia is treated with **IV 10% calcium gluconate**, and chronic disease with **oral calcium plus active vitamin D (calcitriol or alfacalcidol)**, aiming for **low-normal calcium without hypercalciuria**, with **PTH replacement (palopegteriparatide)** for those not controlled - while **pseudohypoparathyroidism** is end-organ **PTH resistance with high PTH**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`hypoparathyroidism`,`hypocalcaemia`,`hypocalcemia`,`post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia`,`tetany`,`Chvostek sign`,`Trousseau sign`,`calcium gluconate`,`calcitriol`,`alfacalcidol`,`palopegteriparatide`,`TransCon PTH`,`teriparatide`,`pseudohypoparathyroidism`,`Albright hereditary osteodystrophy`,`GNAS`,`autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1`,`DiGeorge syndrome`,`basal ganglia calcification`,`hypomagnesaemia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hypocalcaemia** is albumin-corrected calcium **below 8.5 mg/dL (2.12 mmol/L)** or ionised calcium **below 4.6 mg/dL (1.15 mmol/L)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoparathyroidism** is hypocalcaemia with **low or inappropriately normal PTH** on two occasions at least 2 weeks apart; **chronic post-surgical** hypoparathyroidism is defined when it persists **beyond 12 months** after surgery. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Prevalence is about **25-40 per 100,000** in Western populations; about **75%** is **post-surgical**, mostly after total thyroidectomy. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Transient** hypocalcaemia occurs in about **25-30%** after total thyroidectomy and **permanent** hypoparathyroidism in about **1-3%** in high-volume centres - higher after central neck dissection, Graves surgery, re-operation and in low-volume hands. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`In India, **vitamin D deficiency** with secondary hyperparathyroidism is by far the **commonest cause of hypocalcaemia**, and Indian series of **idiopathic (non-surgical) hypoparathyroidism** (AIIMS New Delhi) show a high frequency of **basal ganglia calcification and cataract** at diagnosis because of long diagnostic delay. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab series]`,`**Transfusional iron overload in thalassaemia major** is an important Indian cause of acquired hypoparathyroidism in adolescents. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Post-surgical** (commonest): devascularisation, removal or trauma of glands at **thyroidectomy, parathyroidectomy, laryngeal or oesophageal cancer surgery**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Autoimmune**: isolated, or as part of **APS-1 (APECED; AIRE gene)** - the triad of **chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis, hypoparathyroidism and Addison disease**, usually in childhood; activating **CaSR antibodies** also occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic**: **22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)** with thymic aplasia, cardiac defects and cleft palate; **autosomal dominant hypocalcaemia (ADH1, activating CASR; ADH2, GNA11)** with low-normal PTH and **hypercalciuria**; **HDR syndrome (GATA3)**; PTH gene, GCM2, mitochondrial disorders. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Infiltrative and destructive**: haemochromatosis and **thalassaemia iron overload**, Wilson disease, granulomas, metastases, and neck **irradiation** or radioiodine (rare). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional**: **severe hypomagnesaemia** (below 1 mg/dL) impairs PTH secretion and action - common with **PPIs, alcohol, diuretics, cisplatin, diarrhoea**; severe hypermagnesaemia (IV MgSO4 in eclampsia) also suppresses PTH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-parathyroid hypocalcaemia** (high PTH): **vitamin D deficiency**, CKD, malabsorption, **acute pancreatitis**, rhabdomyolysis, **tumour lysis**, massive transfusion (citrate), **hungry bone syndrome**, osteoblastic metastases, and drugs - bisphosphonates, **denosumab**, foscarnet, phenytoin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PHP)**: resistance to PTH from **GNAS** defects - high PTH, low calcium, high phosphate. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Without PTH, the kidney loses its **distal calcium reabsorption** (so calcium is lost in urine at a lower serum level - **hypercalciuria on treatment**), cannot excrete **phosphate** (hyperphosphataemia), and makes little **calcitriol** (low gut calcium absorption). [Harrison 22e]`,`Low ionised calcium **lowers the threshold for depolarisation** of nerve and muscle membranes, causing spontaneous firing - **tetany, paraesthesia, laryngospasm and seizures**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alkalosis** (hyperventilation, vomiting) increases calcium binding to albumin and lowers ionised calcium - the reason anxious overbreathing can precipitate tetany. [Harrison 22e]`,`A high **calcium-phosphate product** drives **ectopic calcification** - basal ganglia, lens (cataract), kidney (nephrocalcinosis) - over years. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Conventional therapy is unphysiological**: calcitriol raises gut absorption of both calcium and phosphate, but without PTH's renal action the extra calcium is excreted - so raising serum calcium to mid-normal causes **hypercalciuria, stones and CKD**; hence the **low-normal target**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Magnesium** is needed for PTH secretion and PTH receptor signalling - hypocalcaemia will **not correct until magnesium is replaced**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PHP type 1a**: maternally inherited inactivating **GNAS** mutation reduces Gs-alpha in imprinted tissues (renal proximal tubule, thyroid, gonads, pituitary), causing PTH, TSH, GHRH and gonadotropin resistance plus the **Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (AHO)** phenotype. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**By PTH**: low or inappropriately normal PTH = **hypoparathyroidism**; high PTH = **secondary hyperparathyroidism** (vitamin D deficiency, CKD) or **PTH resistance** (pseudohypoparathyroidism). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By duration after surgery**: transient (resolves within weeks to 6 months) vs **chronic (persisting more than 12 months)**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**PHP 1a**: AHO plus multihormone resistance (maternal GNAS); **pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism (PPHP)**: AHO **without** hormone resistance (paternal GNAS); **PHP 1b**: renal PTH resistance (sometimes mild TSH resistance) **without AHO** (GNAS methylation defect); **PHP 2**: cAMP rises but no phosphaturia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`The **2018 international consensus** proposed the umbrella term **inactivating PTH/PTHrP signalling disorder (iPPSD)** for PHP and related conditions. [International PHP Consensus 2018]`,`**Ellsworth-Howard test** (historic): after PTH injection, urinary **cAMP and phosphate** fail to rise in PHP 1; cAMP rises but phosphate does not in PHP 2. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Acute**: perioral and fingertip **tingling (paraesthesia)**, **carpopedal spasm**, muscle cramps, **laryngospasm (stridor)**, bronchospasm and **seizures**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-thyroidectomy**: symptoms typically start **24-72 hours** after surgery - ask on the first post-operative day about tingling. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Chronic**: fatigue, **'brain fog'**, depression, anxiety, poor quality of life - persists even when calcium is in range on conventional therapy. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Neuropsychiatric and movement**: seizures misdiagnosed as **epilepsy** (common in Indian idiopathic hypoparathyroidism), **parkinsonism and chorea** from basal ganglia calcification, and **papilloedema** (benign intracranial hypertension). [Indian J Endocrinol Metab series]`,`**Eyes**: **cataract** (posterior subcapsular) at a young age; **teeth**: enamel hypoplasia and delayed eruption when onset is in childhood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac**: palpitations, syncope (prolonged QT), and **hypocalcaemic cardiomyopathy** with reversible heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **neck surgery, recurrent candidiasis, adrenal symptoms, family history**, PPIs, alcohol, diarrhoea, recent denosumab or zoledronic acid, and transfusion history. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Chvostek sign**: tapping the **facial nerve 2 cm anterior to the tragus** causes twitching of the upper lip or face; it is **positive in about 10% of normal people** and absent in about 30% of hypocalcaemic patients - so it is non-specific. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trousseau sign**: inflate a BP cuff **20 mmHg above systolic for 3 minutes**; **carpal spasm** (flexion at wrist and MCP joints, extension of IP joints, adduction of thumb - 'main d'accoucheur') is more **specific** (positive in about 1-4% of normal people). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-thyroidectomy scar** - the commonest clue; examine for **stridor and voice change** (recurrent laryngeal nerve). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Albright hereditary osteodystrophy**: **short stature, round face, obesity, short 4th and 5th metacarpals** (knuckle-knuckle-dimple-dimple sign), subcutaneous ossification and intellectual impairment. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**APS-1 clues**: oral and nail **candidiasis**, pigmentation (Addison), alopecia, vitiligo, dental enamel defects. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other signs**: dry coarse skin, brittle nails, **cataract** on slit lamp, papilloedema, and features of **DiGeorge** (facial dysmorphism, cardiac murmur). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Vitamin D deficiency** (commonest in India): low or low-normal calcium, **low phosphate**, **high PTH**, high ALP, low 25OHD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: low calcium, **high phosphate**, **high PTH**, raised creatinine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypomagnesaemia**: low calcium, low or normal PTH that recovers when magnesium is replaced. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudohypoparathyroidism**: low calcium, high phosphate, **high PTH**, normal creatinine. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hungry bone syndrome**: after parathyroidectomy, low calcium **with low phosphate and magnesium** and normal or high PTH. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Pseudohypocalcaemia**: low total calcium from **low albumin** (cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome) with normal ionised calcium; gadolinium contrast can also falsely lower some assays. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperventilation tetany** (panic attack): normal total calcium, low ionised calcium from respiratory alkalosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Albumin-corrected calcium** = measured calcium + 0.8 x (4.0 - albumin g/dL); measure **ionised calcium** in sick patients and when albumin is abnormal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intact PTH**, **phosphate, magnesium, creatinine, 25OHD**, ALP; in hypoparathyroidism PTH is **low or inappropriately normal** with **high phosphate**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**ECG**: **prolonged QTc**, which can lead to torsades de pointes; heart block in severe cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**24-hour urine calcium and creatinine** at baseline and on treatment - hypercalciuria (above 250 mg/day women, 300 mg/day men, or above 4 mg/kg/day) drives renal complications. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Renal ultrasound** (nephrocalcinosis, stones), **slit-lamp examination** (cataract) and **CT head** if neurological features (basal ganglia calcification). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Post-thyroidectomy prediction**: a **PTH below about 10-15 pg/mL** measured 4-24 hours after surgery predicts hypocalcaemia and justifies early calcium plus calcitriol. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Non-surgical cases: **genetic testing** (CASR, GNA11, GATA3, AIRE, 22q11.2), **autoantibodies** and adrenal function in APS-1; in suspected PHP, **TSH, FT4, GNAS testing**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency - acute hypocalcaemia`,points:[`Give **IV calcium** for **symptomatic** hypocalcaemia (tetany, seizure, laryngospasm, prolonged QT, arrhythmia) or corrected calcium **below 7.5 mg/dL (1.9 mmol/L)** even if asymptomatic. [Society for Endocrinology Emergency Guidance 2016]`,`**Bolus**: **10-20 mL of 10% calcium gluconate** (93-186 mg elemental calcium) diluted in **50-100 mL of 5% dextrose or saline over 10-20 minutes** with **ECG monitoring**; repeat if symptoms persist. [Society for Endocrinology Emergency Guidance 2016]`,`**Infusion**: **100 mL of 10% calcium gluconate in 1 L of 5% dextrose or 0.9% saline** at about **50-100 mL/h** (about **0.5-1.5 mg/kg/h elemental calcium**), adjusted to calcium checked **every 4-6 hours**. [Society for Endocrinology Emergency Guidance 2016]`,`**Calcium chloride 10%** contains about **three times more elemental calcium** (273 mg per 10 mL) and is irritant - give only via a **central line** or in cardiac arrest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Correct magnesium first or together**: **magnesium sulphate 2 g (8 mmol) IV over 10-20 minutes**, then infusion as needed - hypocalcaemia is refractory otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`Cautions: do **not mix calcium with bicarbonate or phosphate** (precipitation); calcium potentiates **digoxin toxicity**; extravasation causes **tissue necrosis**. [BNF 2025]`,`**Start oral calcium and calcitriol at the same time** in hypoparathyroidism so the infusion can be weaned in 24-72 hours. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Post-thyroidectomy**: if PTH is low on the first post-op day, start **calcium carbonate 1-2 g elemental/day plus calcitriol 0.25-0.5 mcg twice daily** prophylactically; check calcium daily until stable. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - chronic hypoparathyroidism`,points:[`**Treatment targets**: **albumin-corrected calcium in the lower half of or slightly below the normal range** (about 8.0-8.5 mg/dL), **free of symptoms**; 24-h urine calcium **within the normal range for sex**; **normal phosphate and magnesium**; and **25OHD at least 30 ng/mL**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Calcium carbonate** (40% elemental) taken **with meals**, or **calcium citrate** (21% elemental; better with PPIs or achlorhydria); typical dose **500-1000 mg elemental two or three times daily** - calcium also binds dietary phosphate. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Calcitriol** **0.25-0.5 mcg twice daily** (range 0.25-2 mcg/day) or **alfacalcidol 0.5-4 mcg once daily** (widely used in India; needs hepatic hydroxylation, about twice the calcitriol dose); onset in 1-3 days, so dose changes show quickly. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Cholecalciferol** **1000-2000 IU/day** (or 60,000 IU monthly in Indian practice) to keep 25OHD at least 30 ng/mL - plain vitamin D cannot replace active vitamin D because PTH is needed for 1-alpha-hydroxylation. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Hypercalciuria**: lower the calcium target, use a **low-salt diet** and add **hydrochlorothiazide 12.5-50 mg/day** (contraindicated in ADH1 with care, and watch potassium and magnesium). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Hyperphosphataemia**: **low-phosphate diet** (limit dairy, colas, processed food) and, if needed, more calcium with meals or a non-calcium binder. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Magnesium**: replace if low with oral magnesium salts; avoid PPIs where possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ADH1 (activating CASR)**: treat **only if symptomatic**, at the lowest effective dose, because conventional therapy worsens hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`PTH replacement therapy`,points:[`**Indications** for PTH replacement (2022 guidelines): **inadequate control** of calcium; **pill burden** too high; **hypercalciuria, stones, nephrocalcinosis or reduced eGFR**; **hyperphosphataemia** or high Ca x P product; malabsorption; and **poor quality of life**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Palopegteriparatide (TransCon PTH 1-34)** is a prodrug releasing PTH(1-34) steadily for 24 hours; given **once daily SC**, starting **18 mcg/day**, titrated in **3 mcg steps** (range **6-60 mcg/day**) while active vitamin D and calcium are withdrawn. [FDA approval 2024; PaTHway trial]`,`In the **PaTHway** phase 3 trial, about **79%** on palopegteriparatide achieved normal calcium **independent of conventional therapy** vs about 5% on placebo, with **normalised urinary calcium** and better quality-of-life scores. [PaTHway trial 2023]`,`Palopegteriparatide was approved by the **EMA (2023)** and the **US FDA (August 2024)**; **rhPTH(1-84) (Natpara)** was **discontinued worldwide at the end of 2024**. [FDA approval 2024]`,`**Teriparatide (PTH 1-34) 20 mcg twice or three times daily SC** is used **off-label** where palopegteriparatide is unavailable, including in India; its short half-life causes fluctuating calcium and hypercalciuria. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Emerging agents**: **eneboparatide** (long-acting PTH1 receptor agonist) and the oral calcilytic **encaleret** for ADH1 are in phase 3 trials. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Monitor calcium closely when starting PTH therapy - **hypercalcaemia** occurs if active vitamin D is not reduced, and **hypocalcaemia** if a dose is missed. [FDA approval 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: calcitriol requirements often **fall** (placental calcitriol and PTHrP) and may rise in some; check calcium **every 3-4 weeks**, and again after delivery and during **lactation** (PTHrP from the breast can cause hypercalcaemia). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Maternal hypocalcaemia** causes **fetal secondary hyperparathyroidism** with skeletal demineralisation; **maternal hypercalcaemia** suppresses fetal glands and causes **neonatal hypocalcaemia** - keep mother in the low-normal range. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Children**: DiGeorge, APS-1 and genetic causes predominate; monitor growth, teeth and kidneys; PTH therapy is under study in children. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Thalassaemia major**: screen calcium yearly from about age 10 years; optimise **iron chelation**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Neonatal hypocalcaemia**: early (first 72 hours: prematurity, asphyxia, infant of diabetic mother) and late (cow's milk phosphate load, maternal vitamin D deficiency, DiGeorge). [Nelson 22e]`,`**Denosumab** in CKD or vitamin D deficiency can cause severe hypocalcaemia - **replete vitamin D and check calcium** before every dose. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if PTH cannot be measured, **hypocalcaemia with high phosphate and normal creatinine** after neck surgery is hypoparathyroidism until proven otherwise; calcium carbonate, calcium gluconate injection and cholecalciferol are on the **NLEM 2022**. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Renal**: hypercalciuria, **nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis and CKD** - risk of CKD is several-fold higher than in the general population. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Neurological**: **basal ganglia calcification** (correlates with duration of hypocalcaemia and high Ca x P product), seizures, parkinsonism and cognitive impairment. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Eye**: **posterior subcapsular cataract**; **cardiac**: prolonged QT, arrhythmia, reversible cardiomyopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infections, depression and reduced quality of life** are more common despite 'adequate' calcium on conventional therapy. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Bone**: high BMD with low turnover (adynamic), but fracture risk at some sites may not be reduced. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Most **post-surgical** hypocalcaemia recovers in **weeks to months**; recovery after 12 months is uncommon. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** to an endocrinologist all chronic hypoparathyroidism, non-surgical cases, hypercalciuria, children and pregnancy. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**During dose adjustment**: serum calcium **weekly to fortnightly**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**When stable**: calcium, phosphate, magnesium and creatinine **every 3-6 months**; **24-h urine calcium yearly** (or sooner after dose changes). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Renal imaging** at baseline and if stones or falling eGFR are suspected; **eye examination** for cataract; **CT head** if neurological symptoms. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Counsel: recognise **tingling and cramps**, never stop calcitriol suddenly, take calcium **with meals**, avoid long gaps, report vomiting or diarrhoea (malabsorption of doses), and carry an alert card. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Prevention**: total thyroidectomy by **high-volume surgeons**, identification and preservation of parathyroids (autofluorescence imaging is emerging), and **autotransplantation** of devascularised glands. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**First international hypoparathyroidism guidelines (2022)**, GRADE-based: chronic post-surgical disease defined as lasting **more than 12 months**; targets set at **low-normal calcium with normal urine calcium**. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`2022 guidelines list **explicit indications for PTH replacement** (poor control, hypercalciuria, renal disease, hyperphosphataemia, pill burden, poor quality of life). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`**Palopegteriparatide** approved by the **EMA in 2023** and **FDA in August 2024**, after the PaTHway trial. [FDA approval 2024]`,`**rhPTH(1-84) (Natpara)** was **discontinued globally at the end of 2024**. [FDA approval 2024]`,`**Eneboparatide** and **encaleret** (for ADH1) are in phase 3 trials; **post-surgical PTH measurement** is now standard for predicting hypocalcaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`The **iPPSD** classification (2018 consensus) is replacing older PHP subtypes in genetic reports. [International PHP Consensus 2018]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **demonstrate Chvostek and Trousseau signs** and say which is more specific (Trousseau). [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic question: **'Management of post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia'** - IV calcium gluconate, magnesium, oral calcium and calcitriol. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Know **why plain vitamin D does not work** in hypoparathyroidism (no PTH-driven 1-alpha-hydroxylation) and why **thiazides help** (reduce urinary calcium). [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Common mistake: aiming for **mid-normal calcium** on conventional therapy - causes hypercalciuria and stones. [International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022]`,`Common mistake: failing to correct **magnesium**, and giving calcium **through the same line as bicarbonate**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mnemonic for PHP vs PPHP: **'Pseudo-pseudo = looks but no labs'** - AHO appearance with normal calcium. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Mnemonic for hypocalcaemia signs - **CATs go numb**: Convulsions, Arrhythmias, Tetany, and numbness around the mouth and fingers. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in hypocalcaemia and hypoparathyroidism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10-20 mL IV over 10-20 min; infusion 100 mL in 1 L at 50-100 mL/h`,`Until oral therapy takes over (24-72 h)`,`Extravasation necrosis; arrhythmia with digoxin; never with bicarbonate`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`2 g (8 mmol) IV over 10-20 min, then infusion`,`Until magnesium normal`,`Flushing, hypotension; caution in renal failure`],[`Calcium carbonate`,`500-1000 mg elemental two or three times daily with meals`,`Long term`,`Constipation, bloating; poor absorption with PPIs`],[`Calcium citrate`,`500-1000 mg elemental two or three times daily`,`Long term`,`More tablets (21% elemental); preferred with achlorhydria`],[`Calcitriol`,`0.25-0.5 mcg twice daily (0.25-2 mcg/day)`,`Adjust every 1-2 weeks`,`Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria`],[`Alfacalcidol`,`0.5-4 mcg once daily`,`Adjust every 1-2 weeks`,`Hypercalcaemia; needs hepatic activation`],[`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`12.5-50 mg daily with low-salt diet`,`Long term for hypercalciuria`,`Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia`],[`Palopegteriparatide`,`18 mcg SC daily, titrate by 3 mcg (6-60 mcg/day)`,`Long term`,`Hyper- or hypocalcaemia during titration, injection-site reactions`],[`Teriparatide (off-label)`,`20 mcg SC twice or three times daily`,`Long term`,`Fluctuating calcium, hypercalciuria`]]},{heading:`Causes of hypocalcaemia by PTH and phosphate`,columns:[`Cause`,`PTH`,`Phosphate`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Post-surgical hypoparathyroidism`,`Low`,`High`,`Neck scar, recent surgery`],[`Autoimmune or genetic hypoparathyroidism`,`Low`,`High`,`Candidiasis, Addison, DiGeorge features`],[`ADH1 (activating CASR)`,`Low-normal`,`High`,`Hypercalciuria, family history`],[`Hypomagnesaemia`,`Low or normal`,`Variable`,`PPI, alcohol, diarrhoea; Mg below 1 mg/dL`],[`Vitamin D deficiency`,`High`,`Low`,`Low 25OHD, high ALP`],[`CKD`,`High`,`High`,`Raised creatinine`],[`Pseudohypoparathyroidism`,`High`,`High`,`AHO features, normal creatinine`],[`Hungry bone syndrome`,`Normal or high`,`Low`,`After parathyroidectomy, high ALP`]]},{heading:`Pseudohypoparathyroidism and related disorders`,columns:[`Type`,`Inheritance`,`AHO`,`Hormone resistance`,`Calcium and PTH`],rows:[[`PHP 1a`,`Maternal GNAS mutation`,`Present`,`PTH, TSH, GHRH, gonadotropins`,`Low calcium, high PTH`],[`PPHP`,`Paternal GNAS mutation`,`Present`,`None`,`Normal`],[`PHP 1b`,`GNAS methylation (imprinting) defect`,`Usually absent`,`PTH (renal), sometimes mild TSH`,`Low calcium, high PTH`],[`PHP 2`,`Unclear`,`Absent`,`Post-cAMP renal defect`,`Low calcium, high PTH`]]},{heading:`Signs, targets and monitoring`,columns:[`Item`,`Detail`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Chvostek sign`,`Tap facial nerve 2 cm anterior to tragus`,`Non-specific (10% of normals positive)`],[`Trousseau sign`,`Cuff 20 mmHg above systolic for 3 min`,`Carpal spasm; more specific`],[`ECG`,`Prolonged QTc`,`Risk of torsades`],[`Serum calcium target`,`Low-normal or slightly below normal, symptom-free`,`Avoids hypercalciuria`],[`Urine calcium target`,`Normal for sex (below 250 women, 300 men mg/day)`,`Protects kidneys`],[`25OHD target`,`At least 30 ng/mL`,`Replete with cholecalciferol`],[`Stable monitoring`,`Calcium, phosphate, Mg, creatinine every 3-6 months; urine calcium yearly`,`International Hypoparathyroidism Guidelines 2022`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor or laryngospasm after thyroidectomy`,`Seizure, tetany or carpopedal spasm with low calcium`,`Corrected calcium below 7.5 mg/dL even without symptoms`,`Prolonged QTc, arrhythmia or heart failure with hypocalcaemia`,`Hypocalcaemia not responding to IV calcium (check magnesium)`,`Hypoparathyroid patient with vomiting or diarrhoea unable to take doses`,`Hypocalcaemia in pregnancy or a newborn`],pearls:[`Hypoparathyroidism = **low calcium, high phosphate, low PTH**.`,`**Trousseau is more specific than Chvostek**.`,`**Correct magnesium** or calcium will not correct.`,`Target **low-normal calcium** - mid-normal causes hypercalciuria.`,`**Plain vitamin D cannot replace calcitriol** without PTH.`,`**Thiazides** reduce urinary calcium in hypoparathyroidism.`,`Post-op **PTH below 10-15 pg/mL** predicts hypocalcaemia.`,`**Palopegteriparatide** (FDA 2024) is the PTH replacement of choice; Natpara discontinued 2024.`,`PHP 1a: **maternal GNAS**, AHO plus resistance; PPHP: **paternal**, AHO only.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-q1`,stem:`On the second day after total thyroidectomy for multinodular goitre, a 40-year-old woman has perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. Corrected calcium is 6.9 mg/dL and QTc 500 ms. What is the immediate treatment?`,options:[`Oral calcium carbonate 1 g`,`Calcium chloride 10% via peripheral cannula as a rapid push`,`10-20 mL of 10% calcium gluconate in 50-100 mL diluent IV over 10-20 minutes with ECG monitoring`,`Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU orally`],answer:2,explanation:`Symptomatic hypocalcaemia with prolonged QTc needs IV calcium gluconate given slowly with ECG monitoring, followed by an infusion and oral calcium plus calcitriol. Oral calcium and cholecalciferol are too slow; calcium chloride is irritant and should be given only via a central line or in cardiac arrest.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old alcoholic man on a PPI has tetany. Calcium is 7.0 mg/dL, phosphate 3.2 mg/dL, PTH 12 pg/mL and magnesium 0.7 mg/dL. Calcium does not rise despite two IV calcium boluses. What should be done next?`,options:[`Give magnesium sulphate 2 g IV`,`Start calcitriol 2 mcg/day`,`Start palopegteriparatide`,`Order genetic testing for CASR`],answer:0,explanation:`Severe hypomagnesaemia impairs both PTH secretion and PTH action, causing functional hypoparathyroidism with refractory hypocalcaemia; magnesium replacement corrects it. Calcitriol and PTH replacement are for true hypoparathyroidism, and genetic testing is irrelevant when a reversible cause is present.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-q3`,stem:`A 13-year-old girl with short stature, round face, obesity and short 4th and 5th metacarpals has calcium 7.4 mg/dL, phosphate 6.8 mg/dL, PTH 210 pg/mL and TSH 8.5 mIU/L. Her mother has the same appearance but normal biochemistry. What is the diagnosis?`,options:[`Pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism`,`Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1a`,`Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1`,`Vitamin D-dependent rickets`],answer:1,explanation:`Albright hereditary osteodystrophy with PTH resistance (low calcium, high phosphate, high PTH) and TSH resistance is PHP 1a, caused by a GNAS mutation inherited from the mother. The mother's own phenotype (AHO with normal labs) fits pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism, but the daughter has hormone resistance; APS-1 causes low PTH; rickets gives low phosphate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-q4`,stem:`A woman with chronic post-surgical hypoparathyroidism on calcium carbonate and calcitriol has serum calcium 9.4 mg/dL and 24-hour urine calcium 390 mg. What is the best adjustment?`,options:[`Increase calcitriol to raise calcium to 10 mg/dL`,`Stop all calcium supplements`,`Add furosemide 40 mg daily`,`Lower the calcium target to low-normal, add a thiazide and low-salt diet`],answer:3,explanation:`Without PTH, raising serum calcium increases urinary calcium; the 2022 guidelines target low-normal calcium with normal urine calcium, and thiazides with salt restriction reduce calciuria. Increasing calcitriol worsens hypercalciuria; stopping calcium abruptly risks tetany; furosemide increases urinary calcium loss.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-q5`,stem:`Which drug was approved by the US FDA in 2024 as a once-daily PTH replacement for chronic hypoparathyroidism in adults?`,options:[`Abaloparatide`,`Teriparatide`,`Palopegteriparatide`,`Romosozumab`],answer:2,explanation:`Palopegteriparatide (TransCon PTH) is a prodrug releasing PTH(1-34) steadily over 24 hours and was approved by the FDA in August 2024 after the PaTHway trial. Teriparatide is used off-label with multiple daily doses; abaloparatide and romosozumab are osteoporosis drugs without this indication.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Enumerate the causes of hypocalcaemia. Discuss the clinical features, diagnosis and management of post-thyroidectomy hypoparathyroidism.`,openingLines:[`Hypocalcaemia is albumin-corrected calcium below 8.5 mg/dL; hypoparathyroidism is hypocalcaemia with high phosphate and a low or inappropriately normal PTH.`,`About 75% of hypoparathyroidism follows neck surgery; it is chronic if it persists beyond 12 months.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes of hypocalcaemia`,points:[`Low PTH: post-surgical, autoimmune (APS-1), DiGeorge, ADH1, iron overload, hypomagnesaemia.`,`High PTH: vitamin D deficiency, CKD, pseudohypoparathyroidism, hungry bone.`,`Others: pancreatitis, rhabdomyolysis, tumour lysis, citrate, denosumab, bisphosphonates.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Paraesthesia, carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, seizures, 24-72 hours after surgery.`,`Chvostek (non-specific) and Trousseau (specific) signs.`,`Prolonged QTc, cardiomyopathy; chronic: cataract, basal ganglia calcification.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Corrected or ionised calcium, PTH, phosphate, magnesium, creatinine, 25OHD.`,`ECG; 24-h urine calcium; renal ultrasound, slit lamp.`,`Post-op PTH below 10-15 pg/mL predicts hypocalcaemia.`]},{heading:`Acute management`,points:[`10-20 mL 10% calcium gluconate over 10-20 min with ECG monitoring.`,`Infusion 100 mL 10% gluconate in 1 L at 50-100 mL/h; calcium every 4-6 hours.`,`Magnesium sulphate 2 g IV if low; avoid bicarbonate in the same line.`]},{heading:`Chronic management`,points:[`Calcium carbonate 500-1000 mg elemental two or three times daily with meals.`,`Calcitriol 0.25-0.5 mcg twice daily or alfacalcidol 0.5-4 mcg/day; cholecalciferol for 25OHD at least 30.`,`Targets: low-normal calcium, normal urine calcium, phosphate and magnesium.`,`Thiazide plus low-salt diet for hypercalciuria.`]},{heading:`PTH replacement and follow-up`,points:[`Palopegteriparatide 18 mcg SC daily, titrated (FDA 2024) when conventional therapy fails.`,`Calcium, phosphate, magnesium, creatinine every 3-6 months; urine calcium yearly.`,`Prevention: high-volume surgeon, preserve and autotransplant glands.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of hypocalcaemia by PTH level`,`Diagram of Trousseau sign (main d'accoucheur)`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and signs`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Acute and chronic management`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up and newer therapy`,marks:1}],keywords:[`hypoparathyroidism`,`post-thyroidectomy`,`calcium gluconate`,`calcitriol`,`Trousseau`,`palopegteriparatide`]},{id:`endocrine-hypoparathyroidism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on pseudohypoparathyroidism.`,openingLines:[`Pseudohypoparathyroidism is end-organ resistance to PTH caused by GNAS defects, giving low calcium, high phosphate and high PTH.`,`It is classified as PHP 1a, 1b, 2 and pseudo-pseudohypoparathyroidism; the 2018 consensus groups them as iPPSD.`],answer:[{heading:`Types`,points:[`PHP 1a: maternal GNAS; AHO plus PTH, TSH, GHRH, gonadotropin resistance.`,`PPHP: paternal GNAS; AHO without hormone resistance.`,`PHP 1b: GNAS methylation defect; renal PTH resistance, no AHO.`,`PHP 2: cAMP rises but no phosphaturia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`AHO: short stature, round face, obesity, short 4th/5th metacarpals, subcutaneous ossification.`,`Hypocalcaemic symptoms, hypothyroidism, hypogonadism.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Low calcium, high phosphate, high PTH, normal creatinine and 25OHD.`,`Ellsworth-Howard test (urinary cAMP and phosphate); GNAS testing.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Calcitriol and calcium; aim normal PTH (kidney calcium reabsorption is preserved, so less hypercalciuria).`,`Treat hypothyroidism and other deficiencies.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing PHP 1a, PPHP, PHP 1b and PHP 2`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and types`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`pseudohypoparathyroidism`,`GNAS`,`Albright hereditary osteodystrophy`,`PPHP`,`iPPSD`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the parathyroid gland and calcium homeostasis`,`Khan AA, et al. Management of hypoparathyroidism: international guidelines (Second International Workshop). J Bone Miner Res 2022`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Society for Endocrinology Endocrine Emergency Guidance: emergency management of acute hypocalcaemia in adult patients, 2016`,`Khan AA, et al. Efficacy and safety of palopegteriparatide in adults with hypoparathyroidism (PaTHway trial), JCEM 2023; US FDA approval 2024`,`Mantovani G, et al. Diagnosis and management of pseudohypoparathyroidism and related disorders: first international consensus statement, Nat Rev Endocrinol 2018`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), Government of India, 2022`]});var s=[];s.push({id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism`,title:`Primary aldosteronism`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Primary aldosteronism** is **renin-independent, non-suppressible aldosterone secretion** from one or both adrenals (aldosterone-producing adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia), the **commonest curable cause of secondary hypertension**; screen with the **aldosterone-renin ratio (suppressed renin with inappropriately normal or high aldosterone)**, confirm, lateralise by **adrenal venous sampling**, and treat unilateral disease with **laparoscopic adrenalectomy** and bilateral disease with a **mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist**.`,keywords:[`PA`,`Conn syndrome`,`hyperaldosteronism`,`aldosterone-renin ratio`,`ARR`,`aldosterone-producing adenoma`,`APA`,`bilateral adrenal hyperplasia`,`idiopathic hyperaldosteronism`,`adrenal venous sampling`,`AVS`,`saline infusion test`,`spironolactone`,`eplerenone`,`glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism`,`KCNJ5`,`hypokalaemic hypertension`,`resistant hypertension`,`baxdrostat`,`aldosterone synthase inhibitor`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Primary aldosteronism (PA)** is autonomous aldosterone production that is **independent of the renin-angiotensin system** and **not suppressed by sodium loading**, causing hypertension, sodium retention, renin suppression and (in a minority) potassium loss. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`PA is found in about **5-10% of all hypertensives** and **about 20% of resistant hypertension**, rising with BP grade - it is the **commonest endocrine (and curable) cause of hypertension**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Community studies with sensitive assays show a **continuum of renin-independent aldosterone excess** even in normotensive people, so overt PA is the tip of a much larger iceberg. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Only **9-37% of patients have hypokalaemia** - the classic 'hypokalaemic hypertension' picture misses most cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`Fewer than **2-3% of eligible hypertensives worldwide are ever screened**; PA remains one of the most under-diagnosed conditions in medicine. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**India**: with about **22-30% adult hypertension prevalence** (NFHS-5, ICMR-INDIAB) there are likely several million people with PA, but screening is rare outside tertiary endocrine units and most present only after **stroke, AF or CKD** have occurred. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Indian single-centre series report **aldosterone-producing adenomas presenting younger, with severe hypokalaemia and larger tumours**, a sign of late diagnosis rather than a different disease. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab]`,`**Subtype split**: about **two-thirds bilateral (idiopathic) adrenal hyperplasia** and **one-third unilateral** disease (mostly aldosterone-producing adenoma, rarely unilateral hyperplasia or carcinoma). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Aldosterone-producing adenoma (APA, Conn adenoma)**: usually under 2 cm, golden-yellow, unilateral - the surgically curable form. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral idiopathic hyperaldosteronism (IHA)**: micronodular or diffuse zona glomerulosa hyperplasia - treated medically. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Somatic driver mutations** in APAs: **KCNJ5** (potassium channel - about 40% in Western series and **60-80% in East Asian series**), ATP1A1, ATP2B3, CACNA1D and CTNNB1. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Aldosterone-producing micronodules (cell clusters)** with CACNA1D mutations increase with age - explaining the rising prevalence of renin-independent aldosteronism in older adults. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Familial hyperaldosteronism (FH)**: type I - **glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism** (CYP11B1/CYP11B2 chimeric gene, dexamethasone-suppressible); type II - CLCN2; type III - germline KCNJ5; type IV - CACNA1H. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Rare**: aldosterone-secreting adrenocortical carcinoma (large mass, often co-secretes cortisol or androgens) and ectopic aldosterone secretion (ovary, kidney). [Harrison 22e]`,`**High-risk groups to screen**: resistant hypertension, BP 150/100 or more, hypokalaemia (spontaneous or diuretic-induced), adrenal incidentaloma with hypertension, **obstructive sleep apnoea**, **AF without structural cause**, hypertension under 40 years, and family history of early hypertension or stroke under 40. [AHA/ACC 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Aldosterone binds the **mineralocorticoid receptor (MR)** in the principal cells of the **collecting duct**, increasing **ENaC** (apical sodium channel) and basolateral **Na-K-ATPase** - sodium is reabsorbed and potassium and hydrogen ions are secreted. [Harrison 22e]`,`Sodium retention expands volume and raises BP; volume expansion then **suppresses renin** - the biochemical fingerprint (high aldosterone, low renin) that the ARR exploits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aldosterone escape**: after 3-5 days of retention, pressure natriuresis and ANP limit further sodium gain, so **oedema is absent** and serum sodium is only high-normal (142-146 mmol/L). [Harrison 22e]`,`Potassium wasting causes **hypokalaemia**, which in turn **reduces aldosterone secretion** - correcting potassium before testing avoids a false-negative ARR. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Hydrogen-ion secretion causes **metabolic alkalosis**; hypokalaemia causes **nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (polyuria, nocturia)**, muscle weakness and impaired insulin secretion (glucose intolerance). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-epithelial MR effects** - inflammation, fibrosis and oxidative stress in heart, vessels and kidney - explain why PA carries **more cardiovascular damage than essential hypertension at the same BP**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Glomerular hyperfiltration in PA **masks underlying CKD**; eGFR often falls after adrenalectomy or MRA, unmasking true kidney function. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Rationale for drugs**: MR antagonists block the receptor (spironolactone, eplerenone); amiloride blocks ENaC downstream; aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2) inhibitors stop production at source. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and subtypes`,points:[`**By laterality** (the one that decides treatment): **unilateral** (APA, unilateral adrenal hyperplasia, carcinoma) - surgical; **bilateral** (IHA, familial forms) - medical. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Sporadic vs familial**: test for familial forms when PA starts **before 20 years** or there is a family history of PA or stroke under 40. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**FH type I (GRA)**: autosomal dominant, early severe hypertension, early haemorrhagic stroke from intracranial aneurysm; treat with low-dose glucocorticoid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity spectrum**: from mild renin-independent aldosteronism with normokalaemia to florid hypokalaemic PA - the 2025 guideline treats it as a continuum, not a yes/no state. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**PASO outcome criteria** (Primary Aldosteronism Surgical Outcome, 2017) grade surgical success as **complete, partial or absent** for both clinical (BP) and biochemical (K, ARR) cure. [PASO 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most patients have **only hypertension** - often moderate to severe, **resistant to three drugs** including a diuretic, or detected young. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hypokalaemia symptoms: **muscle weakness, cramps, fatigue, palpitations**, rarely **hypokalaemic periodic paralysis** (especially in Asian men). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polyuria and nocturia** from hypokalaemic nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diuretic-induced hypokalaemia** that is severe (potassium under 3.0 mmol/L) or slow to recover is a classic clue. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Cardiovascular events at young age**: stroke, AF, heart failure, MI - PA carries about **2-fold stroke, 3-4-fold AF and 2-fold heart failure risk** vs essential hypertension. [Monticone, Lancet Diab Endocrinol 2018]`,`Ask about **snoring and daytime sleepiness** (OSA is both a cause and a consequence of aldosterone excess). [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`Ask about **liquorice**, carbenoxolone and topical/oral steroids - these cause pseudohyperaldosteronism with low aldosterone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Family history** of early hypertension or haemorrhagic stroke (FH-I). [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Hypertension without oedema** - absence of oedema despite sodium retention reflects aldosterone escape. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertensive target-organ damage** disproportionate to BP duration: LVH (heaving apex), grade II-III retinopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Proximal muscle weakness, reduced reflexes** with hypokalaemia; **Trousseau and Chvostek signs** can appear with alkalosis-related low ionised calcium and hypomagnesaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Irregular pulse** (AF) - a structural-heart-disease-free AF in a hypertensive is a screening trigger. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**No Cushingoid features** expected - their presence with PA suggests a co-secreting adenoma or carcinoma. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Look for **features of other secondary causes**: renal bruit (renovascular), radio-femoral delay (coarctation), paroxysms (phaeochromocytoma), large neck (OSA). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Secondary hyperaldosteronism** (renovascular disease, diuretics, malignant hypertension, renin-secreting tumour): aldosterone high but **renin also high** - ARR normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liquorice ingestion / apparent mineralocorticoid excess (11-beta-HSD2 deficiency)**: hypertension, hypokalaemia, **low renin and low aldosterone** - cortisol acts on the MR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liddle syndrome** (gain-of-function ENaC): low renin, **low aldosterone**, responds to **amiloride, not spironolactone**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cushing syndrome / ectopic ACTH**: cortisol overwhelms 11-beta-HSD2; hypokalaemia with low aldosterone and Cushingoid or catabolic features. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CAH (11-beta-hydroxylase or 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency)**: DOC excess, low renin and low aldosterone, virilisation or sexual infantilism - see the CAH topic. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Bartter and Gitelman syndromes**: hypokalaemic alkalosis but **normal or low BP and high renin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low-renin essential hypertension** (common in older and African-origin patients): renin low but aldosterone also low - no autonomous excess. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - screening in OPD and district hospital`,points:[`**Screening test**: morning (after 2 hours upright, seated 5-15 minutes) **plasma aldosterone and plasma renin activity (PRA) or direct renin concentration (DRC)** - the **aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR)**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Positive screen**: **suppressed renin** (PRA under about 1 ng/mL/h or DRC under about 8 mU/L) with **aldosterone not suppressed** (commonly 10 ng/dL or more by immunoassay; LC-MS/MS thresholds are lower) and an ARR above the laboratory cut-off (often **20-30 ng/dL per ng/mL/h**). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Correct potassium first** (target 4.0 mmol/L or more) and allow liberal salt - hypokalaemia lowers aldosterone and causes false negatives. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**2025 change**: screening can be done **on most antihypertensives without stopping them**; interpret with drug effects in mind and repeat after switching only if results are equivocal. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**False negatives** (renin raised): diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, dihydropyridine CCBs, MRAs, hypokalaemia, low-salt diet, pregnancy. **False positives** (renin lowered): **beta-blockers, clonidine, NSAIDs**, CKD, older age, oestrogen pills (lower DRC). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Drugs with minimal interference** if a washout is needed: **verapamil SR, hydralazine, prazosin or doxazosin**; MRAs need 4-6 weeks off only if renin is not suppressed on them. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Supportive bedside tests**: serum potassium, sodium (high-normal), **venous bicarbonate (alkalosis)**, 24-hour urinary potassium over 30 mmol/day with hypokalaemia (renal loss), ECG (U waves, LVH), echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renin suppression is the key variable** - a high ARR from very low renin with low aldosterone is not PA; always read the two numbers, not just the ratio. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations - confirmation, subtype and lateralisation (tertiary)`,points:[`**Saline infusion test** (2 L normal saline over 4 hours, seated version preferred): post-infusion aldosterone **over about 6-10 ng/dL confirms**, under 5 ng/dL makes PA unlikely. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Oral salt loading**: sodium intake over 200 mmol/day for 3 days, then **24-hour urine aldosterone over 12 mcg/day** with urine sodium over 200 mmol/day confirms. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Captopril challenge** (25-50 mg orally): aldosterone **suppressed by under 30%** with renin still suppressed supports PA - simplest outpatient option. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Fludrocortisone suppression test** (0.1 mg 6-hourly for 4 days with salt and potassium): most accurate but needs admission; rarely used in India. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Skip confirmation** when the screen is unequivocal: **spontaneous hypokalaemia, undetectable renin and aldosterone over 20 ng/dL**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Adrenal CT (thin-slice, adrenal protocol)** in all confirmed cases who would consider surgery - to exclude carcinoma (large, heterogeneous, over 4 cm) and map anatomy; **CT alone mislateralises in about 40%** because non-functioning incidentalomas are common. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Adrenal venous sampling (AVS)**: gold standard for lateralisation in surgical candidates; **selectivity index** (adrenal/IVC cortisol) **2 or more unstimulated or 3-5 or more with ACTH**, and **lateralisation index** (cortisol-corrected aldosterone ratio) **4 or more indicates unilateral** disease, under 3 bilateral. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`AVS may be omitted in a **patient under 35 with spontaneous hypokalaemia, marked aldosterone excess and a clear unilateral adenoma on CT**; functional PET (**11C-metomidate, MATCH trial**) is an emerging non-invasive alternative. [MATCH, Nat Med 2023]`,`**Genetic testing** for FH-I (CYP11B1/CYP11B2 chimera) and KCNJ5 when onset is before 20 years or there is a family history. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Unilateral PA - laparoscopic unilateral adrenalectomy** is first choice: **hypokalaemia corrects in nearly 100%**, BP improves in almost all and is **cured in about 35-60%** (lower with older age, long duration and obesity). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Bilateral PA or patient unfit / unwilling for surgery - MRA**: **spironolactone 12.5-25 mg once daily**, titrate every 4-8 weeks to **50-100 mg/day** (higher doses rarely needed). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Eplerenone 25-50 mg twice daily** if spironolactone causes **gynaecomastia, breast pain, erectile dysfunction or menstrual upset** (about 10% at 50 mg/day, much more above 100 mg) - more selective but shorter-acting and costlier. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Target of MRA therapy**: titrate until **renin is un-suppressed** (PRA 1 ng/mL/h or more) - patients who keep suppressed renin on MRA retain excess cardiovascular and AF risk. [Hundemer, Lancet Diab Endocrinol 2018]`,`**Amiloride 5-20 mg/day** (ENaC blocker) is an alternative or add-on when MRAs are not tolerated; it corrects potassium but has no anti-fibrotic MR effect. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Salt restriction to under 5 g/day** (under 2 g sodium) enhances MRA response; add CCB or ACE inhibitor/ARB if BP stays above target. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**FH-I (GRA)**: smallest dose of glucocorticoid that normalises BP - **dexamethasone 0.125-0.25 mg or prednisolone 2.5-5 mg at bedtime**; in children prefer MRA. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Monitor potassium and creatinine** at 1 week, 4 weeks, then 3-monthly after starting or changing an MRA, especially with CKD, diabetes or ACE inhibitor/ARB use. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: when AVS or surgery is not accessible, the 2025 guideline accepts a **targeted MRA-first approach** after a positive screen (and a confirmation where available). [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** with **potassium under 2.5-3.0 mmol/L**, hypokalaemic paralysis, ECG changes (U waves, arrhythmia), or hypertensive emergency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IV potassium chloride**: up to **10 mmol/h peripherally** (20 mmol/h via central line with cardiac monitoring), with oral KCl 40-80 mmol/day; correct **magnesium** too. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Hypertensive emergency** is treated as usual (labetalol, nicardipine, nitroglycerine) - see the Hypertension topic; PA work-up follows once stable. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**Pre-adrenalectomy**: control BP and **normalise potassium with an MRA** for 2-4 weeks; this also reduces post-operative hyperkalaemia. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Post-adrenalectomy**: **stop MRA and potassium supplements on day 1**, reduce antihypertensives, measure **potassium weekly for 4 weeks** - contralateral zona glomerulosa suppression causes **hyperkalaemia in about 5-15%** (more with CKD), occasionally needing fludrocortisone. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Check **plasma aldosterone and renin the morning after surgery** to document biochemical cure. [PASO 2017]`,`If the 1 mg DST shows **concomitant cortisol co-secretion** pre-operatively, give **perioperative hydrocortisone cover** to prevent post-op adrenal insufficiency. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: hypertension and hypokalaemia may worsen or paradoxically improve (progesterone is an MR antagonist); **spironolactone is avoided** (anti-androgen, feminises male fetus); use **methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine**, amiloride or eplerenone if needed; surgery if required in the **second trimester**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**CKD**: renin is lower and potassium higher, so ARR may be falsely positive and MRAs risk hyperkalaemia - start low (spironolactone 12.5 mg) and monitor; post-adrenalectomy eGFR may fall as hyperfiltration resolves. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: PA worsens insulin secretion via hypokalaemia; MRA or surgery improves glycaemia modestly; avoid confusion with SGLT2 inhibitor-related volume changes. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Elderly over 65**: higher false-positive ARR (low renin with age), more micronodular disease and lower surgical cure - weigh AVS and surgery against comorbidity. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Children and young adults**: suspect **familial forms** and test genetically; GRA presents in childhood with early stroke. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**OSA**: 10-20% of patients with OSA and resistant hypertension have PA - screen them. [AHA/ACC 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular**: higher rates of **stroke (about 2.6-fold), CAD, AF (3.5-fold), heart failure and LVH** than matched essential hypertension. [Monticone, Lancet Diab Endocrinol 2018]`,`**Renal**: hyperfiltration, albuminuria and progressive CKD; hypokalaemic nephropathy with cysts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic**: impaired glucose tolerance, metabolic syndrome, osteoporosis (calciuria). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypokalaemic complications**: periodic paralysis, rhabdomyolysis, **arrhythmia and sudden death**, ileus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prognosis after targeted treatment**: adrenalectomy and adequately dosed MRA both reduce cardiovascular events; **AF and mortality risk falls toward that of essential hypertension** when renin is un-suppressed. [Hundemer, Lancet Diab Endocrinol 2018]`,`Residual hypertension after surgery is predicted by **age over 50, long duration, obesity and family history of hypertension** (the Aldosteronoma Resolution Score). [Zarnegar, Ann Surg 2008]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and programme link`,points:[`**Refer to endocrinology** after a positive ARR, for confirmation, CT and AVS; refer urgently if potassium is under 3.0 mmol/L or there is an adrenal mass over 4 cm. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**On MRA**: potassium and creatinine at 1 and 4 weeks then **3-6 monthly**; BP each visit; **renin yearly** to confirm un-suppression; ask about gynaecomastia. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**After adrenalectomy**: potassium weekly for 4 weeks, BP and **ARR at 3 months and 6-12 months** (PASO outcome), then yearly BP and potassium - recurrence in the other gland can occur. [PASO 2017]`,`**Echo and ECG** at baseline and as indicated to track LVH regression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**First-degree relatives** of familial PA should be screened for hypertension and tested genetically. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Under **NP-NCD**, hypertensives are screened over 30 years at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** - flag those with **hypokalaemia, resistant BP or onset under 40** for referral for ARR. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`The **India Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI)** protocol escalates to referral when BP stays uncontrolled on three drugs - this is the moment to think of PA. [IHCI 2023]`,`Counsel that PA is **potentially curable** and that treating it specifically protects the heart and kidney beyond BP lowering. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Salt under 5 g/day**, weight control, OSA treatment and avoidance of liquorice support therapy. [WHO 2023]`,`**Spironolactone** is on the **NLEM** and available through **Jan Aushadhi** at low cost - cost is not a barrier to medical treatment. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Endocrine Society 2025 PA guideline**: recommends **screening all patients with hypertension** (not only high-risk groups) because PA is common and under-diagnosed. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Endocrine Society 2025**: screening can be done **without stopping most antihypertensives**, using **renin suppression** as the central criterion and assay-specific thresholds (immunoassay vs LC-MS/MS). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Endocrine Society 2025**: confirmatory tests can be **bypassed** when renin is suppressed with high aldosterone and spontaneous hypokalaemia; a **targeted MRA-first** strategy is acceptable when AVS or surgery is not wanted or available. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**AHA/ACC 2025 hypertension guideline**: screen for PA in **resistant hypertension, hypokalaemia, adrenal incidentaloma, OSA** and family history of early hypertension or stroke, regardless of current antihypertensives. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**ESH 2023 hypertension guideline**: lists PA as the most common secondary cause and supports ARR screening in resistant and young-onset hypertension. [ESH 2023]`,`**Aldosterone synthase inhibitors** (baxdrostat - BaxHTN, NEJM 2025; lorundrostat - Launch-HTN 2025) lower BP substantially in uncontrolled and resistant hypertension; not yet licensed for PA in India. [NEJM 2025]`,`**Functional PET** (11C-metomidate, 68Ga-pentixafor) is being validated as an alternative to AVS for lateralisation. [MATCH, Nat Med 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic triad asked: **hypertension, hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis** - but say that **most PA is normokalaemic**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Why no oedema in PA? **Aldosterone escape** via pressure natriuresis and ANP. [Harrison 22e]`,`Which drugs give a **false-positive ARR**? Beta-blockers, clonidine, NSAIDs (they lower renin). **False negative**? Diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, MRAs, hypokalaemia. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`Why AVS rather than CT? **Non-functioning incidentalomas** are common - CT misdirects surgery in up to 40%. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Liddle vs PA**: both low renin, but aldosterone is low in Liddle and it responds to amiloride. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: interpreting ARR **without checking that renin is actually suppressed** or **without correcting potassium**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in primary aldosteronism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Spironolactone`,`12.5-25 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate every 4-8 weeks to 50-100 mg/day; lifelong for bilateral PA`,`Gynaecomastia, breast pain, ED, menstrual upset, hyperkalaemia; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Eplerenone`,`25 mg oral twice daily`,`Up to 50 mg twice daily; lifelong`,`Hyperkalaemia; fewer sexual side effects; CYP3A4 interactions (clarithromycin, ketoconazole)`],[`Amiloride`,`5 mg oral once daily`,`Up to 20 mg/day; add-on or MRA-intolerant`,`Hyperkalaemia; no anti-fibrotic MR blockade`],[`Dexamethasone (FH-I only)`,`0.125-0.25 mg oral at bedtime`,`Lifelong, lowest dose that normalises BP`,`Cushingoid effects if overdosed; growth suppression in children`],[`Potassium chloride`,`Oral 40-80 mmol/day; IV up to 10 mmol/h peripheral`,`Until K 4.0 mmol/L or more`,`Hyperkalaemia, GI irritation; never IV bolus`],[`Verapamil SR / doxazosin / hydralazine`,`Verapamil SR 120-240 mg/day; doxazosin 2-8 mg/day; hydralazine 25-50 mg TDS`,`During ARR washout if needed`,`Constipation, bradycardia (verapamil); postural hypotension (doxazosin); reflex tachycardia (hydralazine)`]]},{heading:`Screening, confirmatory and lateralisation tests with cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`How done`,`Positive / cut-off`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Aldosterone-renin ratio`,`Morning, seated, after 2 h upright; K corrected`,`Renin suppressed (PRA under 1 ng/mL/h or DRC under 8 mU/L), aldosterone 10 ng/dL or more, ARR above lab cut-off (often 20-30)`,`Beta-blockers false positive; diuretics, ACEi/ARB, hypokalaemia false negative`],[`Saline infusion test`,`2 L 0.9% saline over 4 h, seated`,`Aldosterone over about 6-10 ng/dL confirms; under 5 excludes`,`Volume overload in heart failure; avoid in severe uncontrolled BP`],[`Oral salt loading`,`Over 200 mmol Na/day for 3 days`,`24-h urine aldosterone over 12 mcg/day`,`Worsens hypokalaemia; needs K replacement`],[`Captopril challenge`,`25-50 mg oral, sample at 0 and 1-2 h`,`Aldosterone suppressed under 30%, renin stays low`,`Lower sensitivity; equivocal results common`],[`Adrenal CT`,`Thin-slice adrenal protocol`,`Nodule, hyperplasia; mass over 4 cm or HU over 10 suspicious`,`Mislateralises in about 40%`],[`Adrenal venous sampling`,`Simultaneous or sequential, with or without ACTH`,`Selectivity index 2+ (3+ with ACTH); lateralisation index 4+ unilateral, under 3 bilateral`,`Right adrenal vein hard to cannulate; operator-dependent`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of hypertension with hypokalaemia`,columns:[`Condition`,`Renin`,`Aldosterone`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Primary aldosteronism`,`Low`,`High`,`Non-suppressible aldosterone; no oedema`],[`Renovascular / malignant hypertension`,`High`,`High`,`Renal bruit, asymmetric kidneys, severe retinopathy`],[`Diuretic use`,`High`,`High`,`Drug history; resolves on stopping`],[`Liquorice / apparent mineralocorticoid excess`,`Low`,`Low`,`Liquorice history; raised urinary cortisol:cortisone ratio`],[`Liddle syndrome`,`Low`,`Low`,`Family history; responds to amiloride not spironolactone`],[`Cushing syndrome / ectopic ACTH`,`Low`,`Low`,`Cushingoid or catabolic; high cortisol`],[`11-beta- or 17-alpha-hydroxylase CAH`,`Low`,`Low`,`Virilisation or sexual infantilism; raised DOC`]]},{heading:`Subtypes of primary aldosteronism`,columns:[`Subtype`,`Approximate share`,`Key feature`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Bilateral idiopathic hyperplasia`,`About 60-65%`,`Milder, often normokalaemic, older`,`MRA lifelong`],[`Aldosterone-producing adenoma`,`About 30-35%`,`Younger, severe hypokalaemia, KCNJ5 common in Asians`,`Laparoscopic adrenalectomy`],[`Unilateral adrenal hyperplasia`,`About 2%`,`Lateralises on AVS without a discrete adenoma`,`Adrenalectomy`],[`Aldosterone-secreting carcinoma`,`Under 1%`,`Mass usually over 4 cm, co-secretion`,`Open adrenalectomy, oncology`],[`Familial hyperaldosteronism I-IV`,`Under 5%`,`Onset under 20, family history, early stroke (FH-I)`,`FH-I glucocorticoid; others MRA or surgery`]]},{heading:`Who to screen for primary aldosteronism`,columns:[`Group`,`Why`,`Guideline`],rows:[[`All adults with hypertension`,`PA is common and under-diagnosed`,`Endocrine Society 2025`],[`Resistant hypertension (3 drugs incl diuretic) or BP 150/100 or more`,`PA in about 20%`,`AHA/ACC 2025, ESH 2023`],[`Hypertension with spontaneous or diuretic-induced hypokalaemia`,`Classic phenotype`,`AHA/ACC 2025`],[`Hypertension with adrenal incidentaloma`,`APA may present as incidentaloma`,`ESE/ENSAT 2023`],[`Hypertension with OSA or unexplained AF`,`Strong association`,`AHA/ACC 2025`],[`Onset under 40, or family history of PA or early stroke`,`Familial forms`,`Endocrine Society 2025`]]}],redFlags:[`Potassium under 2.5-3.0 mmol/L, U waves or arrhythmia - admit for IV potassium and cardiac monitoring`,`Hypokalaemic periodic paralysis or rhabdomyolysis`,`Hypertensive emergency with target-organ damage (encephalopathy, pulmonary oedema, AKI)`,`Adrenal mass over 4 cm or heterogeneous with high HU - suspect aldosterone-secreting carcinoma`,`New AF, stroke or heart failure in a young hypertensive - screen for PA`,`Hyperkalaemia (over 5.5 mmol/L) on MRA or after adrenalectomy`,`Hypertension in a child or adolescent with family history of early haemorrhagic stroke (FH-I)`],pearls:[`PA is the **commonest curable cause of hypertension** - and most patients have a **normal potassium**.`,`Read **renin first**: no renin suppression, no PA - the ratio alone misleads.`,`**Beta-blockers raise the ARR (false positive); diuretics, ACEi/ARBs and hypokalaemia lower it (false negative).**`,`**No oedema in PA** because of aldosterone escape.`,`CT shows the anatomy, **AVS shows the function** - never operate on CT alone after 35.`,`Titrate MRA until **renin un-suppresses** - that is the cardioprotective target.`,`After adrenalectomy **stop potassium and MRA on day 1** and watch for hyperkalaemia.`,`**Liddle = amiloride; PA = spironolactone; GRA = dexamethasone.**`,`Mnemonic **SALT** for screening: **S**leep apnoea, **A**F/adrenal mass, **L**ow potassium, **T**hree-drug resistance (plus young onset).`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old man has BP 168/104 mmHg on amlodipine, telmisartan and chlorthalidone. Serum potassium is 3.1 mmol/L. Which is the most appropriate initial investigation for primary aldosteronism?`,options:[`24-hour urinary catecholamines`,`Morning plasma aldosterone and renin after correcting potassium`,`Adrenal venous sampling`,`Contrast CT of the adrenals`],answer:1,explanation:`The screening test is the aldosterone-renin ratio drawn in the morning after potassium is corrected; the 2025 Endocrine Society guideline allows testing on most antihypertensives with interpretation for drug effects. AVS and adrenal CT come only after biochemical confirmation, and catecholamines screen for phaeochromocytoma, not PA.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-q2`,stem:`Which antihypertensive is most likely to cause a FALSE-POSITIVE aldosterone-renin ratio?`,options:[`Spironolactone`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Atenolol`,`Ramipril`],answer:2,explanation:`Beta-blockers such as atenolol suppress renin release from the juxtaglomerular cells more than they lower aldosterone, so the ratio rises and may be falsely positive. Spironolactone, thiazides and ACE inhibitors such as ramipril raise renin and therefore produce false-negative results instead.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-q3`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman with confirmed primary aldosteronism has a 1.2 cm left adrenal nodule on CT and wants surgery. What is the next step?`,options:[`Left laparoscopic adrenalectomy`,`Bilateral adrenalectomy`,`Lifelong dexamethasone`,`Adrenal venous sampling`],answer:3,explanation:`At 52 a small nodule may be a non-functioning incidentaloma while the true source is bilateral hyperplasia or the opposite gland; CT mislateralises in about 40%, so AVS is required before surgery. Proceeding straight to left adrenalectomy risks removing the wrong gland, bilateral adrenalectomy causes permanent adrenal insufficiency, and dexamethasone is only for familial hyperaldosteronism type I.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-q4`,stem:`A hypertensive man with hypokalaemia has low plasma renin and LOW plasma aldosterone. His BP and potassium respond to amiloride but not to spironolactone. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Liddle syndrome`,`Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia`,`Aldosterone-producing adenoma`,`Renovascular hypertension`],answer:0,explanation:`Liddle syndrome is a gain-of-function mutation of the epithelial sodium channel, so sodium is retained without aldosterone; renin and aldosterone are both low, and only an ENaC blocker such as amiloride works. Both forms of primary aldosteronism have high aldosterone and respond to spironolactone, and renovascular hypertension has high renin.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-q5`,stem:`On the first day after a right adrenalectomy for an aldosterone-producing adenoma, which instruction is correct?`,options:[`Continue spironolactone for 3 months to protect the heart`,`Double the potassium supplement because aldosterone falls`,`Stop spironolactone and potassium supplements and monitor potassium weekly`,`Start fludrocortisone routinely in all patients`],answer:2,explanation:`After removal of the adenoma the suppressed contralateral zona glomerulosa can cause hyperkalaemia, so MRAs and potassium supplements are stopped on day 1 and potassium is checked weekly for about 4 weeks. Continuing spironolactone or increasing potassium invites dangerous hyperkalaemia, and fludrocortisone is reserved for the minority with persistent hyperkalaemia.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, clinical features, diagnostic approach and management of primary aldosteronism. Add a note on whom to screen.`,openingLines:[`Primary aldosteronism is renin-independent, non-suppressible aldosterone secretion from an adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia, and is the commonest curable cause of hypertension (5-10% of hypertensives, about 20% of resistant hypertension).`,`Diagnosis proceeds in three steps - screen (aldosterone-renin ratio), confirm (saline or captopril test) and subtype (CT plus adrenal venous sampling) - because treatment differs: adrenalectomy for unilateral and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist for bilateral disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Bilateral idiopathic hyperplasia about 60-65%; aldosterone-producing adenoma about 30-35% (KCNJ5 somatic mutations).`,`Unilateral hyperplasia, carcinoma (rare), familial hyperaldosteronism types I-IV.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Aldosterone acts on MR in collecting duct: ENaC and Na-K-ATPase up - sodium retention, K and H loss.`,`Volume expansion suppresses renin; aldosterone escape explains absence of oedema; MR-mediated fibrosis explains excess cardiovascular damage.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Resistant or young-onset hypertension; weakness, cramps, polyuria, nocturia; periodic paralysis.`,`Complications: stroke, AF, LVH, heart failure, CKD; most patients normokalaemic.`]},{heading:`Whom to screen`,points:[`Endocrine Society 2025: all hypertensives; priority to resistant HTN, hypokalaemia, incidentaloma, OSA, AF, onset under 40, family history of early stroke.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic approach`,points:[`Screen: morning ARR after correcting potassium; renin suppressed and aldosterone 10 ng/dL or more; note drug interference.`,`Confirm: saline infusion (aldosterone over 6-10 ng/dL after 2 L), oral salt load, captopril challenge; skip if florid.`,`Subtype: adrenal CT to exclude carcinoma; AVS - selectivity index 2+ and lateralisation index 4+ = unilateral.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Unilateral: laparoscopic adrenalectomy after MRA pre-treatment; stop MRA and K on day 1; monitor for hyperkalaemia.`,`Bilateral or non-surgical: spironolactone 12.5-25 mg titrated to 50-100 mg; eplerenone 25-50 mg BD if gynaecomastia; amiloride add-on; salt under 5 g.`,`Target renin un-suppression; FH-I - low-dose dexamethasone.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prognosis`,points:[`Potassium and creatinine at 1 and 4 weeks then 3-6 monthly; PASO outcome at 6-12 months; surgical cure of BP 35-60%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspect - ARR - confirm - CT - AVS - adrenalectomy vs MRA`,`Table of drugs causing false-positive and false-negative ARR`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, aetiology and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and whom to screen`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic approach with cut-offs and flow chart`,marks:3},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:3}],keywords:[`ARR`,`renin suppression`,`saline infusion test`,`AVS`,`adrenalectomy`,`spironolactone`,`Endocrine Society 2025`]},{id:`endocrine-primary-aldosteronism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the aldosterone-renin ratio: how to perform it and how to interpret it.`,openingLines:[`The aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR) is the screening test for primary aldosteronism; it detects autonomous aldosterone secretion with a suppressed renin.`,`Its value depends on patient preparation, drug effects and on reading renin and aldosterone individually, not only the ratio.`],answer:[{heading:`Preparation and sampling`,points:[`Correct potassium to 4.0 mmol/L or more; unrestricted salt.`,`Morning sample after 2 hours upright and 5-15 minutes seated.`,`Endocrine Society 2025: test on current antihypertensives; washout (verapamil SR, hydralazine, doxazosin) only if equivocal.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`Positive: renin suppressed (PRA under 1 ng/mL/h or DRC under 8 mU/L) with aldosterone 10 ng/dL or more and ARR above lab cut-off (often 20-30).`,`Very low renin with low aldosterone gives a high ratio but is not PA.`]},{heading:`Interfering factors`,points:[`False positive: beta-blockers, clonidine, NSAIDs, CKD, older age.`,`False negative: diuretics, ACEi/ARB, DHP-CCB, MRA, hypokalaemia, low-salt diet, pregnancy.`]},{heading:`Next step`,points:[`Positive screen - confirmatory test (saline or captopril) unless florid, then CT and AVS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: false positive vs false negative causes`],markSplit:[{part:`Preparation`,marks:1},{part:`Cut-offs and interpretation`,marks:2},{part:`Interfering factors and next step`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ARR`,`PRA`,`DRC`,`beta-blocker`,`hypokalaemia`,`false negative`]}],references:[`Adler GK, Stowasser M, et al. Primary Aldosteronism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline. J Clin Endocrinol Metab, 2025`,`Funder JW, et al. The Management of Primary Aldosteronism: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2016`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the adrenal cortex`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`2025 AHA/ACC/multisociety Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults`,`2023 ESH Guidelines for the management of arterial hypertension`,`Williams TA, et al. Primary Aldosteronism Surgical Outcome (PASO) consensus. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol, 2017`,`MoHFW. National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) operational guidelines, 2023`]}),s.push({id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma`,title:`Phaeochromocytoma and paraganglioma`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Phaeochromocytoma** is a **catecholamine-secreting tumour of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells** and **paraganglioma** its extra-adrenal sympathetic or parasympathetic counterpart (together **PPGL**); diagnose with **plasma free or urinary fractionated metanephrines**, then image, offer **germline genetic testing to all**, and resect only after **7-14 days of alpha-blockade (then beta-blockade), salt and fluid loading**.`,keywords:[`PPGL`,`pheochromocytoma`,`paraganglioma`,`metanephrines`,`normetanephrine`,`3-methoxytyramine`,`phenoxybenzamine`,`doxazosin`,`phentolamine`,`metyrosine`,`MIBG`,`68Ga-DOTATATE`,`SDHB`,`VHL`,`MEN2`,`NF1`,`catecholamine crisis`,`clonidine suppression test`,`belzutifan`,`rule of 10s`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Phaeochromocytoma** arises from adrenal medullary chromaffin cells; **paraganglioma (PGL)** arises from extra-adrenal paraganglia - **sympathetic** (thoraco-abdominal, pelvic, usually secretory) or **parasympathetic** (head and neck, usually non-secretory). [WHO 2022]`,`Incidence about **0.6 per 100,000 per year**; found in **0.2-0.6% of hypertensives** and in about **5% of adrenal incidentalomas**; peak in the 4th-5th decades. [Harrison 22e]`,`Up to **50% are now found incidentally** on imaging and about **10% on genetic surveillance** rather than with symptoms. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Autopsy series show that **a large share were never diagnosed in life** - many deaths attributed to MI or stroke were catecholamine crises. [Harrison 22e]`,`**About 35-40% carry a germline mutation** - the highest heritability of any human tumour. [Endocrine Society 2014; Lenders 2023 review]`,`**India**: tertiary series (AIIMS, PGIMER, SGPGI, CMC Vellore) report **late presentation with large tumours, a high share of paragangliomas and frequent hereditary disease (VHL, SDHx)**; population incidence data are lacking. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab]`,`**WHO 2022**: all PPGLs are considered to have **metastatic potential**; the term 'malignant' is replaced by **metastatic** PPGL (about 10-15% overall, **up to 40% with SDHB**). [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and genetics`,points:[`**Cluster 1 - pseudohypoxia**: **SDHx (SDHA, B, C, D, AF2), VHL, EPAS1 (HIF-2-alpha), FH** - noradrenergic or dopaminergic, often extra-adrenal, **SDHB most often metastatic**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Cluster 2 - kinase signalling**: **RET (MEN2), NF1, TMEM127, MAX** - adrenergic (adrenaline-secreting), adrenal, often bilateral in MEN2. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Cluster 3 - Wnt signalling**: somatic MAML3 fusion and CSDE1 - sporadic, aggressive. [WHO 2022]`,`**MEN2A/2B**: phaeochromocytoma in about **50%** with medullary thyroid carcinoma - see the MEN topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VHL**: phaeochromocytoma in 10-20% with retinal and CNS haemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma and pancreatic cysts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NF1**: phaeochromocytoma in about 1-3% (café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas). [Harrison 22e]`,`The old **'rule of 10s'** (10% bilateral, extra-adrenal, malignant, familial, paediatric) is **outdated** - familial disease is about 35-40%. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Tumours synthesise catecholamines from tyrosine (rate-limiting **tyrosine hydroxylase** - target of metyrosine) and metabolise them **continuously within the tumour** by **COMT to metanephrines** - so metanephrines are raised even when release is episodic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PNMT** (adrenaline synthesis) needs high cortisol from the adrenal cortex, so **adrenaline-secreting tumours are adrenal**; extra-adrenal tumours secrete **noradrenaline or dopamine**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alpha-1 stimulation**: vasoconstriction, hypertension, pallor, **volume contraction** (pressure natriuresis) and orthostatic hypotension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-1 stimulation**: tachycardia, palpitations, arrhythmia; chronic excess causes **catecholamine cardiomyopathy and takotsubo**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Unopposed alpha**: giving a beta-blocker first removes beta-2 vasodilatation and precipitates a **hypertensive crisis** - hence alpha before beta. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic**: catecholamines inhibit insulin and drive glycogenolysis - **hyperglycaemia** that may resolve after surgery; raised metabolic rate causes sweating and weight loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`After tumour removal, **sudden catecholamine withdrawal plus alpha-blockade** causes post-operative **hypotension and rebound hypoglycaemia**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**By site**: adrenal phaeochromocytoma (about 80-85%) vs paraganglioma (15-20%) - sympathetic (organ of Zuckerkandl, bladder) or parasympathetic (carotid body, glomus jugulare). [WHO 2022]`,`**By biochemical phenotype**: **adrenergic** (metanephrine raised - MEN2, NF1), **noradrenergic** (normetanephrine - VHL, SDHx), **dopaminergic** (3-methoxytyramine - SDHx, higher metastatic risk). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**By genetics**: cluster 1, 2, 3 (as above) - drives screening of other organs and follow-up intensity. [WHO 2022]`,`**Metastatic PPGL** is defined only by **chromaffin tissue where it is normally absent** (bone, liver, lung, lymph nodes) - histology scores (PASS, GAPP) do not reliably predict it. [WHO 2022]`,`**Size and risk**: tumours **over 5 cm**, extra-adrenal site, SDHB mutation and high 3-methoxytyramine predict metastasis. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Classic triad: **episodic headache, sweating and palpitations** - all three together have about 90% specificity in a hypertensive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertension**: sustained in about half, paroxysmal in about a third; **normotensive in 5-15%** (especially incidental or dopaminergic tumours). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paroxysms** last minutes to an hour: pallor (not flushing), tremor, anxiety, sense of impending doom, chest or abdominal pain, nausea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Triggers**: exertion, posture, abdominal pressure, **micturition (bladder PGL)**, anaesthesia, surgery, labour, and drugs. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Drugs that provoke crises**: **beta-blockers without alpha-blockade, metoclopramide, glucocorticoids (high-dose dexamethasone)**, tricyclics, MAO inhibitors, sympathomimetics, droperidol, opioids, cocaine. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Atypical presentations**: unexplained heart failure, takotsubo, shock, stroke, new-onset diabetes in a lean patient, **crisis during unrelated surgery or labour**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Family history** of PPGL, MTC, renal cancer, retinal haemangioblastoma or sudden death. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Hypertension with postural drop** (volume contraction) - a hypertensive with orthostatic hypotension is a clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tachycardia, tremor, sweating, pallor** during a paroxysm; often lean. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertensive retinopathy** and signs of cardiomyopathy (S3, crackles). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Syndromic stigmata**: café-au-lait spots, axillary freckling, neurofibromas (**NF1**); marfanoid habitus, mucosal neuromas, thyroid nodule (**MEN2B**); retinal angiomas (**VHL**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck mass** that moves sideways but not vertically (carotid body tumour - Fontaine sign), pulsatile tinnitus (glomus). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Avoid palpating an abdominal mass deeply** - it can trigger a paroxysm. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Panic disorder / anxiety**: flushing or trembling with hyperventilation, triggered by stress; metanephrines normal (mildly raised catecholamines possible). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis**: sustained (not paroxysmal) symptoms, warm moist skin, goitre; TSH suppressed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** (insulinoma, sulfonylurea): adrenergic attacks relieved by eating; low glucose during attack. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced**: clonidine withdrawal, cocaine, amphetamine, MAOI with tyramine, decongestants. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Labile essential hypertension, menopause flushes, migraine, carcinoid syndrome (flushing, diarrhoea)**, baroreflex failure, autonomic epilepsy. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Pseudo-phaeochromocytoma**: severe paroxysmal hypertension driven by repressed emotion; biochemistry normal. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - biochemistry`,points:[`**First-line**: **plasma free metanephrines** (best drawn **supine after 30 minutes**) or **24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines** - sensitivity about **95-99%**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Interpretation**: values **over 3-4 times the upper limit** (or rises in two or more metabolites) are nearly diagnostic; mild elevations (under 2 times) are mostly false positives and need repeat supine sampling. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Plasma 3-methoxytyramine** identifies dopaminergic tumours and predicts metastasis (SDHB). [Lenders, Lancet 2023 review]`,`**False positives**: **tricyclics, SNRIs, phenoxybenzamine, levodopa, MAOIs, sympathomimetics, cocaine**, acetaminophen (some assays), seated sampling, acute illness, OSA, renal failure. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Clonidine suppression test**: **0.3 mg oral**; normetanephrine that **fails to fall by 40% or stays above the reference** at 3 hours confirms PPGL. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Plain catecholamines and VMA are obsolete** as screening tests (low sensitivity). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Supportive**: glucose (hyperglycaemia), ECG and echo (LVH, cardiomyopathy), haematocrit (haemoconcentration), calcium and calcitonin if MEN2 suspected. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - imaging and genetics`,points:[`**Image only after biochemical confirmation** (except incidental masses) - **CT abdomen-pelvis with contrast** first: heterogeneous, **unenhanced HU over 10**, often cystic or haemorrhagic. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Non-ionic contrast is safe**; alpha-blockade before CT is not needed. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**MRI** (T2 **'light-bulb' bright**) for skull base and neck PGL, metastases, children, pregnancy, contrast allergy. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Functional imaging**: **68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT** is the most sensitive for SDHx, head-neck and metastatic disease; **18F-DOPA PET** for VHL and EPAS1; **123I-MIBG** to select patients for 131I-MIBG therapy. [EANM/SNMMI 2019]`,`Functional imaging is indicated for **tumours over 5 cm, paragangliomas, SDHx carriers and suspected metastasis** before surgery. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Germline genetic testing in all patients** (next-generation panel) - shared decision-making; results guide surveillance of relatives. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Fine-needle biopsy of a suspected phaeochromocytoma is contraindicated** - it can trigger a crisis and seed the tumour. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Management - preoperative preparation (OPD)`,points:[`**Surgery is the only cure**; **laparoscopic adrenalectomy** for most adrenal tumours under 6-8 cm; **cortical-sparing** surgery for bilateral hereditary disease (MEN2, VHL). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Alpha-blockade for 7-14 days** before surgery in all hormonally active tumours: **phenoxybenzamine** (non-selective, irreversible) **10 mg twice daily**, raised by 10-20 mg every 2-3 days to about **1 mg/kg/day**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Selective alpha-1 blocker alternative**: **doxazosin 2 mg daily, titrated to 8-32 mg/day** (or prazosin, terazosin) - less reflex tachycardia, no post-op prolonged hypotension, cheaper and widely available in India. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**PRESCRIPT trial**: phenoxybenzamine and doxazosin gave **similar intraoperative haemodynamic instability** overall, with slightly more instability on doxazosin. [Buitenwerf, JCEM 2020]`,`**High-sodium diet (over 5 g salt/day) and 2.5 L fluid/day** from day 2-3 of alpha-blockade to reverse volume contraction. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Beta-blocker only after at least 3-4 days of adequate alpha-blockade** - **propranolol 20 mg TDS or atenolol/metoprolol** - for tachycardia over 90 or arrhythmia. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Calcium channel blocker** (**nifedipine SR or amlodipine**) as add-on or for mild hypertension/normotensive patients. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Targets**: seated **BP under 130/80**, standing **systolic over 90**, seated **HR 60-70** and standing **70-80**; mild stuffy nose and orthostasis show adequate blockade. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Metyrosine** (tyrosine hydroxylase inhibitor, 250 mg QID, up to 4 g/day) for large or metastatic tumours where available - not marketed in India. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency (crisis and perioperative)`,points:[`**PPGL crisis** (hypertensive emergency, shock, pulmonary oedema, MI, arrhythmia, multi-organ failure): **ICU**, arterial line. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phentolamine 1-5 mg IV bolus** repeated every 5 minutes (or infusion) is the classic agent; alternatives - **sodium nitroprusside, nicardipine or clevidipine infusion**, **magnesium sulphate**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Never give a beta-blocker first**; labetalol (alpha:beta about 1:7 IV) is **not preferred** because it can paradoxically raise BP. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`Catecholamine-induced **shock or cardiomyopathy**: cautious fluids, mechanical support (ECMO) in specialised centres; emergency surgery during crisis has high mortality - **stabilise first**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Intraoperative**: anaesthetist aware; surges during intubation and tumour handling treated with **phentolamine, nitroprusside, nicardipine, esmolol, magnesium**; **ligate the adrenal vein early**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Post-operative (first 24-48 h)**: watch for **hypotension** (fluids, vasopressors), **hypoglycaemia** (glucose every 1-2 hours for 24 hours), and **adrenal insufficiency after bilateral surgery** (hydrocortisone cover). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Metastatic PPGL**: resection or debulking, **131I-MIBG or 177Lu-DOTATATE (PRRT)**, CVD chemotherapy (cyclophosphamide, vincristine, dacarbazine), sunitinib, and **belzutifan (HIF-2-alpha inhibitor, FDA 2025)**. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: rare but maternal and fetal mortality is high if missed; mimics pre-eclampsia (but **no proteinuria early, paroxysms, postural hypotension**); use plasma metanephrines and **MRI without gadolinium**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`In pregnancy: **alpha-blockade (doxazosin or phenoxybenzamine)**, then **laparoscopic surgery before 24 weeks**, or after delivery by **caesarean section** (vaginal labour risks crisis). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Children**: higher proportion of **hereditary (VHL, SDHx), bilateral and extra-adrenal** tumours - test genetics in every child. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**CKD**: urinary metanephrines unreliable - use **plasma free metanephrines**; normetanephrine rises with advanced CKD. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Diabetes**: catecholamine-driven hyperglycaemia often resolves after surgery; reduce glucose-lowering drugs post-op to avoid hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Incidental adrenal mass with HU over 10**: measure metanephrines before any biopsy or surgery - see the Adrenal incidentaloma topic. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: doxazosin or prazosin plus salt and fluids is an acceptable, inexpensive preparation; refer to a high-volume adrenal surgical centre. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular**: hypertensive crisis, MI with normal coronaries, **takotsubo or dilated catecholamine cardiomyopathy**, arrhythmia, sudden death. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Neurological**: stroke, hypertensive encephalopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic**: diabetes, weight loss; **renal**: AKI from rhabdomyolysis or shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertension persists** after surgery in about **25%** (coexisting essential hypertension). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Recurrence** in about **10-15% over 10 years**, higher with hereditary and extra-adrenal tumours - hence lifelong follow-up. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Metastatic disease**: 5-year survival about **60-70%** with wide variation (slower with SDHB-negative bone-only disease). [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Refer every suspected PPGL** (raised metanephrines or adrenal mass with HU over 10) to an endocrine centre; **never start a beta-blocker alone**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Post-op metanephrines at 2-6 weeks** to document cure, then **yearly for at least 10 years**; **lifelong** in hereditary, young, large or extra-adrenal disease. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**SDHx carriers**: whole-body **MRI every 2-3 years** plus yearly metanephrines; **VHL**: yearly metanephrines from age 5; **MEN2**: from age 11 (high-risk RET) or 16. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Cascade genetic testing** of first-degree relatives once a pathogenic variant is found. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`Counsel to **carry a card** listing the diagnosis and drugs to avoid (metoclopramide, unopposed beta-blockers, dexamethasone) before any procedure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Programme link: hypertensives detected under **NP-NCD** with paroxysms, postural drop or onset under 40 need referral for secondary-cause work-up. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2022/2023 classification**: no benign/malignant split - all PPGLs have metastatic potential; use 'metastatic PPGL'. [WHO 2022]`,`**ESE/ENSAT 2023 incidentaloma guideline**: metanephrines needed only for adrenal masses **not lipid-rich (HU over 10)**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Belzutifan** (HIF-2-alpha inhibitor) approved by the **FDA in 2025** for advanced or metastatic PPGL (LITESPARK-015). [FDA 2025]`,`**131I-MIBG (Azedra) withdrawn in 2024**; PRRT with 177Lu-DOTATATE is increasingly used for SSTR-positive metastatic disease. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Selective omission of preoperative alpha-blockade** in normotensive or small tumours is being debated (observational data), but the **Endocrine Society 2014 recommendation for blockade remains standard**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Plasma 3-methoxytyramine** now recommended alongside metanephrines in expert reviews to detect dopaminergic and metastatic disease. [Lenders, Lancet 2023 review]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic triad: **headache, sweating, palpitations** with hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Why **alpha before beta**? Beta-blockade removes beta-2 vasodilatation, leaving unopposed alpha vasoconstriction - crisis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Why **metanephrines, not catecholamines**? Tumours metabolise catecholamines continuously to metanephrines - sensitivity is far higher. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`Why is **adrenaline secretion adrenal**? PNMT needs high local cortisol. [Harrison 22e]`,`Why is **FNAC contraindicated**? Crisis and seeding. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Common mistakes: quoting the **rule of 10s** as current, starting a beta-blocker first, and forgetting **post-op hypoglycaemia**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in phaeochromocytoma and paraganglioma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Phenoxybenzamine`,`10 mg oral BD`,`Increase 10-20 mg every 2-3 days to about 1 mg/kg/day; 7-14 days pre-op`,`Orthostasis, reflex tachycardia, nasal stuffiness, post-op hypotension; costly`],[`Doxazosin`,`2 mg oral OD`,`Titrate to 8-32 mg/day; 7-14 days pre-op`,`First-dose hypotension, dizziness; less tachycardia`],[`Prazosin`,`1 mg oral at bedtime`,`Up to 2-5 mg TDS`,`First-dose syncope; short-acting`],[`Propranolol / metoprolol`,`Propranolol 20 mg TDS; metoprolol 25-50 mg BD`,`Only after 3-4 days of alpha-blockade`,`Crisis if given first; asthma, bradycardia`],[`Nifedipine SR / amlodipine`,`Nifedipine SR 30-60 mg OD; amlodipine 5-10 mg OD`,`Add-on pre-op`,`Oedema, headache`],[`Phentolamine`,`1-5 mg IV bolus, repeat every 5 min, or infusion`,`During crisis or surgery`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia, hypotension`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`0.3-10 mcg/kg/min IV infusion`,`Crisis; titrate to BP`,`Cyanide toxicity with prolonged use; light-sensitive`],[`Metyrosine`,`250 mg oral QID, up to 4 g/day`,`Pre-op for large or metastatic tumours`,`Sedation, extrapyramidal effects, crystalluria; not marketed in India`]]},{heading:`Hereditary PPGL syndromes`,columns:[`Gene / syndrome`,`Cluster and phenotype`,`Associated features`,`Metastatic risk`],rows:[[`RET (MEN2A/2B)`,`Cluster 2; adrenergic, adrenal, often bilateral`,`Medullary thyroid carcinoma; hyperparathyroidism (2A); marfanoid, neuromas (2B)`,`Low`],[`VHL`,`Cluster 1; noradrenergic, adrenal, bilateral`,`Haemangioblastomas, RCC, pancreatic cysts`,`Low`],[`NF1`,`Cluster 2; adrenergic, adrenal`,`Café-au-lait, neurofibromas, Lisch nodules`,`Low-moderate`],[`SDHB`,`Cluster 1; noradrenergic or dopaminergic, extra-adrenal`,`RCC, GIST`,`High (up to 40%)`],[`SDHD / SDHAF2`,`Cluster 1; head-neck PGL, multifocal`,`Paternal transmission`,`Low`],[`MAX / TMEM127`,`Cluster 2; adrenal`,`Bilateral adrenal tumours`,`Low-moderate`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of paroxysmal symptoms`,columns:[`Condition`,`Distinguishing clinical clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Phaeochromocytoma`,`Pallor, headache, sweating, postural drop`,`Plasma metanephrines 3-4 times ULN`],[`Panic disorder`,`Hyperventilation, fear, stress trigger`,`Metanephrines normal`],[`Thyrotoxicosis`,`Sustained symptoms, goitre, warm skin`,`Suppressed TSH`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Relief with food, diabetes drugs`,`Glucose during attack`],[`Carcinoid syndrome`,`Flushing, diarrhoea, wheeze`,`Urine 5-HIAA`],[`Drug-induced (cocaine, clonidine withdrawal)`,`Drug history`,`Urine toxicology`],[`Menopausal flushes`,`Age, menstrual change, flushing`,`FSH`]]},{heading:`Biochemical and imaging tests with cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Interpretation`,`Use / pitfall`],rows:[[`Plasma free metanephrines (supine)`,`Over 3-4 times ULN nearly diagnostic; under 2 times often false positive`,`Best first test; seated sampling raises normetanephrine`],[`24-h urine fractionated metanephrines`,`Over 2-3 times ULN suspicious`,`Alternative first test; unreliable in CKD`],[`Plasma 3-methoxytyramine`,`Raised in dopaminergic tumours`,`Predicts SDHB and metastasis`],[`Clonidine suppression (0.3 mg)`,`Normetanephrine fails to fall 40% = PPGL`,`For borderline results`],[`CT adrenal`,`Unenhanced HU over 10, heterogeneous`,`HU 10 or less essentially excludes`],[`MRI`,`T2 bright 'light bulb'`,`Head-neck, pregnancy, children`],[`68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT`,`Most sensitive for SDHx and metastases`,`Staging before surgery in high-risk tumours`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypertensive crisis with headache, chest pain, pulmonary oedema or encephalopathy in a known or suspected PPGL`,`Shock, takotsubo or cardiomyopathy with unexplained hypertension swings`,`Severe hypertension triggered by anaesthesia, labour, metoclopramide, steroids or a beta-blocker`,`Pregnant woman with paroxysmal hypertension and postural hypotension`,`Adrenal mass with HU over 10 being planned for biopsy - stop and measure metanephrines`,`Post-adrenalectomy hypotension or hypoglycaemia in the first 48 hours`,`Known SDHB carrier with bone pain or new mass (metastasis)`],pearls:[`**Alpha first, beta later - never beta alone.**`,`**Metanephrines, not catecholamines or VMA**, are the screening test.`,`Draw plasma metanephrines **supine after 30 minutes** to avoid false positives.`,`**Adrenaline-secreting means adrenal** - PNMT needs cortisol.`,`The **rule of 10s is dead** - about 35-40% are hereditary; test genes in everyone.`,`**Never biopsy** an adrenal mass until phaeochromocytoma is excluded.`,`**Salt and fluids** with alpha-blockade - a stuffy nose and mild postural drop mean adequate blockade.`,`**SDHB = extra-adrenal, noradrenergic or dopaminergic, metastatic.**`,`Watch for **hypoglycaemia and hypotension** after the tumour is out.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-q1`,stem:`The most sensitive initial biochemical test for phaeochromocytoma is:`,options:[`24-hour urinary vanillylmandelic acid`,`Plasma catecholamines drawn seated`,`Plasma free metanephrines drawn supine`,`Serum chromogranin A`],answer:2,explanation:`Tumours metabolise catecholamines continuously to metanephrines inside the tumour, so plasma free metanephrines (sampled supine) have sensitivity of about 95-99%. VMA and plasma catecholamines miss episodic secretors, seated sampling raises false positives, and chromogranin A is non-specific (raised by PPIs and renal failure).`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old with a confirmed 4 cm left phaeochromocytoma has BP 170/100 and heart rate 110/min. Which is the correct preoperative plan?`,options:[`Start doxazosin, titrate over 10-14 days with high salt and fluids, then add a beta-blocker if tachycardia persists`,`Start propranolol immediately to control heart rate, then add phenoxybenzamine`,`Proceed to urgent adrenalectomy within 24 hours without drugs`,`Start labetalol alone as it blocks both alpha and beta receptors`],answer:0,explanation:`Alpha-blockade for 7-14 days with salt and fluid loading restores volume and prevents intraoperative crisis; a beta-blocker is added only after adequate alpha-blockade. Giving propranolol first leaves unopposed alpha vasoconstriction, unprepared surgery risks crisis, and labetalol has far more beta than alpha activity and can raise BP.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-q3`,stem:`Which gene mutation in paraganglioma carries the highest risk of metastatic disease?`,options:[`RET`,`NF1`,`VHL`,`SDHB`],answer:3,explanation:`SDHB-related tumours are typically extra-adrenal, noradrenergic or dopaminergic, and metastasise in up to 40%, so carriers need lifelong imaging surveillance. RET (MEN2), NF1 and VHL tumours are mostly adrenal and rarely metastasise.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-q4`,stem:`A patient with an undiagnosed adrenal mass develops a severe hypertensive crisis after being given an antiemetic before CT. Which drug is most likely responsible?`,options:[`Ondansetron`,`Metoclopramide`,`Domperidone`,`Promethazine`],answer:1,explanation:`Metoclopramide, a dopamine D2 antagonist, releases catecholamines from chromaffin tumours and is a recognised trigger of PPGL crisis. Ondansetron is considered safe, and although domperidone and promethazine have dopamine or other receptor actions, metoclopramide is the classic named culprit in guidelines.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-q5`,stem:`Six hours after laparoscopic removal of a phaeochromocytoma, a patient is drowsy with BP 86/50 mmHg. The first two things to check and treat are:`,options:[`Serum calcium and give calcium gluconate`,`Serum potassium and give potassium chloride`,`Plasma metanephrines and restart phenoxybenzamine`,`Blood glucose and circulating volume - give dextrose and IV fluids`],answer:3,explanation:`Sudden catecholamine withdrawal with residual alpha-blockade causes hypotension, and the removal of catecholamine-induced insulin suppression causes rebound hypoglycaemia; both are common in the first 24-48 hours. Calcium and potassium are not the typical problem, and restarting alpha-blockade would worsen hypotension; metanephrines are rechecked at 2-6 weeks.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and preoperative management of phaeochromocytoma. Add a note on the genetics of PPGL.`,openingLines:[`Phaeochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting tumour of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells; extra-adrenal tumours are paragangliomas, and together they are called PPGL.`,`About 35-40% are hereditary, all have metastatic potential (WHO 2022), and safe surgery depends on 7-14 days of alpha-blockade before any beta-blocker.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Triad of episodic headache, sweating, palpitations; sustained or paroxysmal hypertension with postural drop; pallor, tremor, anxiety.`,`Triggers: exertion, micturition, anaesthesia, metoclopramide, steroids, beta-blockers; atypical - cardiomyopathy, shock, crisis in labour.`]},{heading:`Biochemical diagnosis`,points:[`Plasma free metanephrines (supine) or 24-h urine fractionated metanephrines; over 3-4 times ULN nearly diagnostic.`,`Borderline: repeat supine, stop interfering drugs, clonidine suppression test; 3-methoxytyramine for dopaminergic tumours.`]},{heading:`Localisation`,points:[`CT (HU over 10, heterogeneous) or MRI (T2 bright); 68Ga-DOTATATE PET for PGL, SDHx and metastasis; no FNAC.`]},{heading:`Genetics`,points:[`Cluster 1 pseudohypoxia (SDHx, VHL, EPAS1), cluster 2 kinase (RET, NF1, TMEM127, MAX), cluster 3 Wnt.`,`Germline testing for all; cascade screening of relatives.`]},{heading:`Preoperative management`,points:[`Phenoxybenzamine 10 mg BD to 1 mg/kg/day or doxazosin 2-32 mg/day for 7-14 days.`,`High-salt diet and 2.5 L fluids; beta-blocker after 3-4 days if HR over 90; CCB add-on.`,`Targets: BP under 130/80 seated, systolic over 90 standing, HR 60-70 seated.`]},{heading:`Perioperative and follow-up`,points:[`Intraoperative phentolamine, nitroprusside, magnesium; early adrenal vein ligation.`,`Post-op hypotension and hypoglycaemia; metanephrines at 2-6 weeks then yearly at least 10 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspicion - metanephrines - imaging - genetics - alpha then beta - surgery - follow-up`,`Catecholamine synthesis pathway showing tyrosine hydroxylase, PNMT and COMT`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Biochemical and imaging diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Genetics`,marks:1},{part:`Preoperative and perioperative management`,marks:4}],keywords:[`metanephrines`,`alpha before beta`,`phenoxybenzamine`,`doxazosin`,`SDHB`,`68Ga-DOTATATE`,`WHO 2022`]},{id:`endocrine-pheochromocytoma-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of a phaeochromocytoma crisis.`,openingLines:[`A PPGL crisis is an acute catecholamine surge causing hypertensive emergency or shock with end-organ damage; mortality is high.`,`Stabilise with an alpha-blocker or vasodilator in ICU, never a beta-blocker first, and operate only after stabilisation.`],answer:[{heading:`Recognition`,points:[`Severe hypertension or labile BP, tachyarrhythmia, pulmonary oedema, MI, encephalopathy, shock, multi-organ failure.`,`Precipitants: anaesthesia, metoclopramide, steroids, beta-blocker, labour, tumour handling.`]},{heading:`Immediate management`,points:[`ICU, arterial line, oxygen, stop triggering drugs.`,`Phentolamine 1-5 mg IV every 5 min or infusion; or nitroprusside, nicardipine, magnesium sulphate.`,`Beta-blocker (esmolol) only after alpha-blockade for tachyarrhythmia.`]},{heading:`Shock / cardiomyopathy`,points:[`Cautious fluids, inotropes or mechanical support (ECMO) in specialised centres.`]},{heading:`After stabilisation`,points:[`Oral alpha-blockade 7-14 days, salt and fluids, then elective adrenalectomy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Crisis flow chart: recognise - ICU - phentolamine - add beta later - stabilise - elective surgery`],markSplit:[{part:`Recognition and triggers`,marks:1},{part:`Drug management with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Definitive plan`,marks:1}],keywords:[`phentolamine`,`nitroprusside`,`magnesium`,`crisis`,`no beta first`]}],references:[`Lenders JWM, et al. Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2014`,`WHO Classification of Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`,`Fassnacht M, et al. ESE/ENSAT Clinical Practice Guideline on the management of adrenal incidentalomas, 2023`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pheochromocytoma`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Neuroendocrine and Adrenal Tumors, 2025`,`Buitenwerf E, et al. PRESCRIPT randomised trial of phenoxybenzamine vs doxazosin. J Clin Endocrinol Metab, 2020`]}),s.push({id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma`,title:`Adrenal incidentaloma`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`An **adrenal incidentaloma** is an **adrenal mass of 1 cm or more found on imaging done for another reason**; every one needs two questions answered - **is it malignant** (unenhanced CT attenuation **10 HU or less** means a benign lipid-rich adenoma) and **is it functioning** (**1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test in all**, metanephrines if not lipid-rich, aldosterone-renin ratio if hypertensive or hypokalaemic) - per **ESE/ENSAT 2023**.`,keywords:[`adrenal mass`,`adrenal incidentaloma`,`AI`,`adrenocortical adenoma`,`Hounsfield units`,`HU`,`lipid-rich adenoma`,`contrast washout`,`chemical shift MRI`,`1 mg dexamethasone suppression test`,`mild autonomous cortisol secretion`,`MACS`,`subclinical Cushing`,`adrenocortical carcinoma`,`ACC`,`myelolipoma`,`adrenal metastasis`,`ESE/ENSAT 2023`,`urine steroid metabolomics`,`bilateral adrenal masses`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Adrenal incidentaloma (AI)**: an adrenal mass **1 cm or more** in diameter detected on imaging performed **not for suspected adrenal disease**; masses found on cancer staging or screening of hereditary syndromes are excluded from the strict definition. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Found on about **1-5% of abdominal CT scans**, rising with age to about **7% over 70 years**; rare under 30. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Rising CT use has made AI one of the **commonest endocrine referrals**; most are **benign non-functioning adenomas (about 80%)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Approximate composition in referral series: **non-functioning adenoma 40-70%, mild autonomous cortisol secretion 20-50% of adenomas**, phaeochromocytoma **1-5%**, aldosteronoma **2-5%**, overt Cushing **1-4%**, **adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) under 2%**, metastasis (higher in known cancer), myelolipoma and cysts. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**India**: no population registry; tertiary series (PGIMER, AIIMS, KEM) report a higher share of **phaeochromocytoma and of infective causes** (tuberculosis, histoplasmosis) among referred masses, especially **bilateral** ones. [Indian J Endocrinol Metab]`,`**Bilateral incidentalomas** occur in about **10-15%** - most are bilateral adenomas, but infiltrative disease and CAH must be considered. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - what the mass can be`,points:[`**Benign cortical**: adrenocortical adenoma (non-functioning or cortisol/aldosterone secreting), nodular hyperplasia, **bilateral macronodular adrenal hyperplasia**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Medullary**: **phaeochromocytoma** (up to half of phaeochromocytomas are now found incidentally), ganglioneuroma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant**: **adrenocortical carcinoma**, **metastasis** (lung, breast, kidney, melanoma, GI), lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other benign**: **myelolipoma** (macroscopic fat), cyst, haemangioma, haematoma, schwannoma. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Infective / infiltrative** (often bilateral): **tuberculosis, histoplasmosis**, amyloid, sarcoid - important in India because they cause **adrenal insufficiency**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital adrenal hyperplasia** can present as uni- or bilateral adrenal enlargement or myelolipoma - see the CAH topic. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Pseudo-adrenal masses**: splenic lobule, pancreatic tail, varices, renal upper-pole lesions. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the imaging and the tests work`,points:[`Adenomas are rich in **intracytoplasmic lipid (cholesterol esters for steroid synthesis)**, which lowers X-ray attenuation - hence **unenhanced CT 10 HU or less** identifies a lipid-rich adenoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`On **chemical-shift MRI**, the same lipid causes **signal loss on opposed-phase images**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Adenomas take up and **wash out contrast quickly** (absolute washout 60% or more, relative 40% or more at 15 minutes), while carcinomas, metastases and phaeochromocytomas retain it - but washout is now considered **less accurate than believed**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Cortisol-secreting adenomas** produce cortisol independent of ACTH, so ACTH is suppressed and **dexamethasone fails to suppress cortisol** - the basis of the 1 mg DST. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MACS** is a continuum: small, ACTH-independent cortisol excess without the catabolic signs of Cushing, yet sufficient to raise **hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, osteoporosis, cardiovascular events and mortality**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`ACTH suppression from MACS causes **atrophy of the contralateral adrenal** - the reason for **post-operative adrenal insufficiency** after removing the adenoma. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`ACC often secretes **steroid precursors and androgens** (DHEAS, 11-deoxycortisol), detected by **urine steroid metabolomics**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**By imaging**: **benign (homogeneous, 10 HU or less)** vs **indeterminate (over 10 HU)** vs **suspicious** (heterogeneous, irregular, over 4 cm, calcification, necrosis, growth). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**By function**: non-functioning; **MACS** (post-DST cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL / 50 nmol/L without Cushing signs); **overt Cushing**; primary aldosteronism; phaeochromocytoma; androgen-secreting. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**ESE/ENSAT 2023 dropped the 'possible ACS' (1.9-5.0 mcg/dL) vs 'ACS' (over 5.0) split** - all values above 1.8 mcg/dL without overt features are now called **MACS**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**By size**: risk of ACC is about **2% under 4 cm, 6% at 4-6 cm and 25% over 6 cm** - size is a weaker discriminator than HU. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Unilateral vs bilateral** - bilateral needs each side characterised plus tests for CAH, adrenal insufficiency and bilateral macronodular hyperplasia. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most are **asymptomatic**; the history is a search for **subtle hormone excess and malignancy**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Cortisol excess clues**: new or worsening **hypertension, diabetes, weight gain, osteoporotic or vertebral fractures**, easy bruising, proximal weakness, mood change. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Catecholamine clues**: episodic headache, sweating, palpitations, labile BP. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Aldosterone clues**: resistant hypertension, muscle weakness, cramps, hypokalaemia. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Androgen excess** (rapid-onset hirsutism, virilisation, voice change) or feminisation in men suggests **ACC**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Malignancy history**: known cancer (lung, breast, kidney, melanoma), weight loss, flank pain, recent rapid growth. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Infective history** for bilateral masses: TB contact, past TB, fatigue, salt craving, pigmentation (adrenal insufficiency). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**BP lying and standing** (hypertension, postural drop in phaeochromocytoma or adrenal insufficiency). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cushingoid signs that discriminate**: **wide purple striae over 1 cm, proximal myopathy, easy bruising, facial plethora** - their absence with abnormal DST = MACS. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Virilisation**: hirsutism, clitoromegaly, frontal balding - suggests androgen-secreting ACC. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Hyperpigmentation of palmar creases and buccal mucosa** - primary adrenal insufficiency with bilateral infiltration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal mass, hepatomegaly, lymph nodes** - malignancy. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Syndromic stigmata**: café-au-lait spots (NF1), mucosal neuromas (MEN2B), retinal angiomas (VHL). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Lipid-poor adenoma** vs **phaeochromocytoma**: both over 10 HU - distinguished by metanephrines. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Adenoma vs ACC**: ACC is usually over 4 cm, heterogeneous, over 20 HU, irregular, with androgen or precursor excess. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Metastasis**: known primary, often bilateral, over 10 HU, may grow quickly; FDG-avid. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Myelolipoma**: macroscopic fat (**below -20 HU**), benign, no follow-up unless large and symptomatic. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Tuberculosis / histoplasmosis**: bilateral, enlarged with necrosis or calcification, with adrenal insufficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adrenal haemorrhage**: anticoagulation, sepsis, antiphospholipid syndrome - high HU acutely, shrinks on follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - imaging`,points:[`**Unenhanced CT is the first test**: **10 HU or less and homogeneous = benign adenoma, no further imaging needed regardless of size**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Indeterminate (over 10 HU)**: options are **immediate additional imaging** (contrast washout CT, chemical-shift MRI, FDG-PET-CT), **interval imaging in 6-12 months**, or **surgery** - chosen in a multidisciplinary meeting by size, HU and patient factors. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Homogeneous, under 4 cm, 11-20 HU**: risk of malignancy is low - **one repeat CT/MRI at 12 months** or immediate additional imaging is reasonable. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Over 4 cm and over 20 HU, or heterogeneous**: high risk - **surgery** after excluding phaeochromocytoma, without biopsy. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Significant growth** on interval imaging (about **20% and at least 5 mm in a year**) suggests malignancy and favours surgery. [ESE/ENSAT 2016]`,`**MRI preferred** in young people, children and pregnancy (no radiation). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Adrenal biopsy is rarely indicated**: only for suspected metastasis or infection where the result changes management, **after excluding phaeochromocytoma**, and **never if ACC is suspected** (seeding). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - hormonal work-up`,points:[`**1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test in ALL** (dexamethasone 1 mg at 11 pm, cortisol at 8-9 am): **1.8 mcg/dL (50 nmol/L) or less excludes** cortisol excess. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Post-DST cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL without Cushing features = MACS**; over 5 mcg/dL (138 nmol/L) indicates more marked autonomy. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Confirm MACS** with **morning ACTH (suppressed), DHEAS (low)**, repeat DST, and if needed late-night salivary cortisol or 24-h UFC (often normal in MACS); check **dexamethasone level** where available to exclude rapid metabolism. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**False-positive DST**: enzyme inducers (**rifampicin - relevant in TB**, carbamazepine, phenytoin), **oral oestrogens** (raise CBG), missed dose, depression, alcohol, obesity. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Plasma or urine metanephrines** in all masses **not lipid-rich (over 10 HU)** - ESE/ENSAT 2023 says no need for masses 10 HU or less. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Aldosterone-renin ratio** in every patient with **hypertension or hypokalaemia**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Sex hormones and steroid precursors** (DHEAS, androstenedione, testosterone, 17-OHP, 11-deoxycortisol; **urine steroid metabolomics**) when imaging or clinical features suggest ACC. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Bilateral masses**: add **17-OHP** (CAH) and **morning cortisol with ACTH or Synacthen test** (adrenal insufficiency). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Benign (10 HU or less), non-functioning**: reassure; **no further imaging and no repeat hormonal testing** unless new clinical features. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**MACS**: screen and treat **hypertension, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia and osteoporosis (DXA, vertebral fractures)** with standard drugs; reassess comorbidities **yearly**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Adrenalectomy for MACS** is individualised - consider in **younger patients with cortisol-related comorbidities that are worsening or hard to control**; RCT and cohort data show improved BP and glycaemia after surgery. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Statins** for dyslipidaemia, **bisphosphonates** for osteoporosis, and BP and glucose control per usual targets in MACS. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Primary aldosteronism or phaeochromocytoma** on work-up - manage as in those topics. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Indeterminate mass**: refer for multidisciplinary review; do not order biopsy from primary care. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Medical therapy for overt Cushing awaiting surgery (rarely for MACS): **metyrapone 250 mg QID** or **osilodrostat** - specialist only. [Endocrine Society 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / surgery`,points:[`**Indications for adrenalectomy**: overt hormone excess (Cushing, phaeochromocytoma, unilateral PA), **suspected ACC**, MACS with progressive comorbidity, significant growth. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Laparoscopic adrenalectomy** for benign-looking masses up to about 6 cm; **open adrenalectomy** for suspected ACC with local invasion, by an experienced surgeon (R0 resection matters for survival). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma must be excluded** before any adrenal surgery or biopsy; if positive, 7-14 days of alpha-blockade first. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Perioperative glucocorticoid cover** for **MACS or overt Cushing**: hydrocortisone **100 mg IV at induction**, then 50 mg 6-8 hourly for 24 h, tapering to oral replacement. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Post-op adrenal insufficiency** occurs in a large share after MACS surgery - **test morning cortisol or Synacthen at intervals** and taper hydrocortisone over weeks to months. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Bilateral infiltrative disease with adrenal crisis**: hydrocortisone 100 mg IV, saline; treat TB with standard ATT (remember rifampicin increases hydrocortisone clearance - **double the replacement dose**). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children, adolescents, under 40 and pregnancy**: higher chance of malignancy and functioning lesions - **MRI**, urgent evaluation, lower threshold for surgery. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Known extra-adrenal cancer**: mass is metastasis in a substantial share; **FDG-PET-CT**; biopsy only if it changes management and phaeochromocytoma is excluded. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Elderly and frail**: weigh benefit of work-up against comorbidity; a **10 HU or less mass needs nothing more** than a DST. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Diabetes and hypertension**: these are the comorbidities MACS worsens - check DST in poorly controlled patients with a known adrenal nodule. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Patients on rifampicin (ATT) or oral contraceptives**: DST unreliable - use late-night salivary cortisol or repeat after stopping. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: unenhanced CT HU, 1 mg DST and serum potassium/BP are cheap and answer most questions; refer indeterminate or functioning masses. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Non-functioning adenomas rarely become malignant** (risk close to zero) and **under 1% become hormonally active** per year. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**MACS** is associated with **higher cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality**, independent of overt Cushing. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**ACC** carries a poor prognosis - 5-year survival about **60-80% for stage I-II** and **under 15% for stage IV**. [ESE/ENSAT 2018 ACC guideline]`,`**Missed phaeochromocytoma** may cause **crisis during unrelated surgery**, biopsy or contrast-free procedures. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Post-operative adrenal insufficiency** after removing a cortisol-secreting adenoma - can be fatal if steroid cover is not given. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Refer urgently** (2 weeks): mass **over 4 cm**, heterogeneous or **over 20 HU**, rapid growth, virilisation, overt Cushing, or positive metanephrines. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Benign, non-functioning**: **discharge** after one assessment - ESE/ENSAT 2023 removed routine repeat imaging and hormone tests. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Indeterminate small mass** not operated: **one repeat imaging at 6-12 months**; stop if stable. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**MACS**: **yearly review** of BP, HbA1c, lipids and bone health; repeat DST not routinely needed. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Counsel that most masses are **harmless 'bystanders'**, explain why tests are needed, and advise informing doctors before surgery or steroid therapy. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Encourage **radiologists to report HU** on every adrenal mass - a simple step that saves repeat imaging. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (ESE/ENSAT 2023)`,points:[`**Size threshold removed**: a homogeneous mass **10 HU or less needs no further imaging whatever its size**. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**MACS replaces 'subclinical Cushing' and 'possible/autonomous cortisol secretion'** for post-DST cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL without overt features. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Metanephrines only for non-lipid-rich masses** (over 10 HU) - not routinely for clear adenomas. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**No routine follow-up imaging or repeat hormone testing** for benign non-functioning adenomas. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Urine steroid metabolomics** recommended as an adjunct when ACC is suspected (EURINE-ACT data). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Multidisciplinary expert team** review for indeterminate masses and all candidates for surgery. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Two questions for every AI**: is it **malignant** (HU, size, heterogeneity) and is it **functioning** (DST, metanephrines, ARR). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Magic numbers: **1 cm** (definition), **10 HU** (benign), **4 cm** (malignancy risk), **1.8 mcg/dL / 50 nmol/L** (DST). [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Why **never biopsy first**? Phaeochromocytoma crisis and ACC seeding; biopsy rarely distinguishes adenoma from carcinoma. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Why **steroid cover** after removing a cortisol-secreting adenoma? Contralateral adrenal is suppressed. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`Common mistakes: ordering **metanephrines for every 8 HU adenoma**, doing DST on rifampicin, and **repeat CTs every year** for benign adenomas. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Imaging features: benign vs malignant adrenal masses`,columns:[`Feature`,`Lipid-rich adenoma`,`Phaeochromocytoma`,`Adrenocortical carcinoma / metastasis`],rows:[[`Unenhanced CT HU`,`10 or less`,`Over 10 (often over 20)`,`Over 20`],[`Size`,`Usually under 4 cm`,`Variable, often 3-5 cm`,`ACC usually over 4-6 cm`],[`Texture and margins`,`Homogeneous, smooth`,`Heterogeneous, cystic, haemorrhage`,`Heterogeneous, irregular, necrosis, calcification`],[`Contrast washout`,`Absolute 60%+, relative 40%+`,`Variable`,`Slow washout`],[`MRI`,`Signal loss on opposed-phase`,`T2 bright`,`No signal loss; T2 intermediate-high`],[`Growth`,`Stable`,`Slow`,`Rapid (over 20% and 5 mm a year)`]]},{heading:`Hormonal work-up of an adrenal incidentaloma`,columns:[`Test`,`Who`,`Cut-off / interpretation`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`1 mg overnight DST`,`All`,`1.8 mcg/dL (50 nmol/L) or less excludes; above = MACS if no Cushing signs`,`Rifampicin, carbamazepine, oral oestrogen give false positives`],[`ACTH and DHEAS`,`Abnormal DST`,`Suppressed ACTH and low DHEAS support ACTH-independent cortisol`,`Assay variability`],[`Plasma or urine metanephrines`,`Masses over 10 HU`,`Over 3-4 times ULN = phaeochromocytoma`,`TCAs, SNRIs, seated sampling`],[`Aldosterone-renin ratio`,`Hypertension or hypokalaemia`,`Suppressed renin with aldosterone 10 ng/dL or more`,`Beta-blockers, diuretics; correct potassium`],[`Androgens and precursors, urine steroid metabolomics`,`Suspected ACC`,`Raised DHEAS, 11-deoxycortisol, testosterone`,`Limited availability in India`],[`17-OHP and Synacthen test`,`Bilateral masses`,`High 17-OHP = CAH; low cortisol = adrenal insufficiency`,`Timing in women (follicular phase)`]]},{heading:`Drugs and steroid cover relevant to adrenal incidentaloma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone (test)`,`1 mg oral at 11 pm`,`Single dose; cortisol at 8-9 am`,`False result with CYP3A4 inducers, missed dose`],[`Hydrocortisone (peri-op cover for MACS/Cushing)`,`100 mg IV at induction, then 50 mg IV 6-8 hourly`,`24 h, then oral 15-25 mg/day tapered over weeks-months`,`Hyperglycaemia; under-dosing causes adrenal crisis`],[`Metyrapone`,`250 mg oral QID, titrate to cortisol`,`Pre-op or non-surgical Cushing`,`Hirsutism, hypertension, hypokalaemia (precursor excess)`],[`Osilodrostat`,`2 mg oral BD, titrate`,`Specialist use`,`Adrenal insufficiency, QT prolongation`],[`Doxazosin (if phaeochromocytoma)`,`2 mg oral OD, titrate to 8-32 mg`,`7-14 days pre-op`,`First-dose hypotension`],[`Spironolactone (if PA)`,`12.5-25 mg oral OD, titrate to 50-100 mg`,`Lifelong if not operated`,`Gynaecomastia, hyperkalaemia`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of an adrenal mass`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Key imaging feature`,`Key biochemical feature`,`Action`],rows:[[`Non-functioning adenoma`,`10 HU or less, homogeneous`,`All tests normal`,`Discharge`],[`MACS adenoma`,`Adenoma imaging`,`DST cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL, low ACTH`,`Treat comorbidities; consider surgery`],[`Phaeochromocytoma`,`Over 10 HU, T2 bright`,`Metanephrines raised`,`Alpha-blockade then surgery`],[`Aldosteronoma`,`Small, low HU`,`Low renin, high aldosterone`,`AVS then surgery`],[`Adrenocortical carcinoma`,`Over 4 cm, heterogeneous, over 20 HU`,`Androgens, precursors raised`,`Open surgery at expert centre`],[`Myelolipoma`,`Macroscopic fat below -20 HU`,`Normal`,`No follow-up unless symptomatic`],[`TB / histoplasmosis`,`Bilateral, necrosis or calcification`,`Low cortisol, high ACTH`,`ATT or antifungal; steroid replacement`]]}],redFlags:[`Adrenal mass over 4 cm, heterogeneous or over 20 HU - suspect carcinoma`,`Rapid growth on interval imaging or new virilisation in a woman`,`Raised metanephrines - do not biopsy or operate without alpha-blockade`,`Bilateral adrenal masses with hypotension, hyponatraemia or pigmentation - adrenal insufficiency`,`Overt Cushing features (proximal myopathy, wide purple striae, bruising)`,`Spontaneous hypokalaemia with severe hypertension`,`Any adrenal mass in a child, adolescent or pregnant woman`],pearls:[`Two questions for every incidentaloma: **is it bad (malignant), is it busy (functioning)?**`,`**10 HU or less on plain CT = benign adenoma - stop imaging, whatever the size.**`,`**1 mg DST for everyone**; 1.8 mcg/dL (50 nmol/L) is the line.`,`**Metanephrines only if HU over 10; ARR only if hypertensive or hypokalaemic.**`,`**Never biopsy** before excluding phaeochromocytoma, and never if ACC is suspected.`,`MACS hurts **heart, glucose and bones** - screen yearly.`,`**Steroid cover** after removing a cortisol-secreting adenoma.`,`In India think **TB and histoplasmosis** for bilateral masses with adrenal insufficiency.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old has a 3.2 cm homogeneous right adrenal mass measuring 6 HU on unenhanced CT. Which statement about further imaging is correct according to ESE/ENSAT 2023?`,options:[`Repeat CT every year for 5 years`,`No further imaging is needed`,`MRI with chemical shift is mandatory`,`CT-guided biopsy to confirm benignity`],answer:1,explanation:`A homogeneous mass of 10 HU or less is a lipid-rich benign adenoma, and the 2023 guideline recommends no further imaging regardless of size. Annual CT was the older practice now abandoned, MRI adds nothing once HU is diagnostic, and biopsy is not indicated and risks complications.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-q2`,stem:`Which single hormonal test is recommended for EVERY patient with an adrenal incidentaloma?`,options:[`Aldosterone-renin ratio`,`Plasma free metanephrines`,`Serum DHEAS`,`1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test`],answer:3,explanation:`Cortisol autonomy (MACS) is the commonest functional abnormality and has cardiometabolic consequences, so a 1 mg DST is done in all. The ARR is for patients with hypertension or hypokalaemia, metanephrines for masses over 10 HU, and DHEAS mainly supports MACS or suspected carcinoma.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with a 2.5 cm adenoma (8 HU) has post-dexamethasone cortisol 3.4 mcg/dL, suppressed ACTH, hypertension and type 2 diabetes but no Cushingoid features. What is the correct term for her condition?`,options:[`Overt Cushing syndrome`,`Pseudo-Cushing state`,`Mild autonomous cortisol secretion`,`Non-functioning adenoma`],answer:2,explanation:`ESE/ENSAT 2023 uses mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS) for post-DST cortisol above 1.8 mcg/dL without overt Cushing signs, replacing 'subclinical Cushing'. Overt Cushing needs catabolic features such as proximal myopathy and wide striae, pseudo-Cushing is ACTH-dependent and non-adrenal, and the abnormal DST rules out a non-functioning adenoma.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has a 5.5 cm heterogeneous left adrenal mass of 38 HU with irregular margins. Plasma metanephrines are normal. What is the next best step?`,options:[`Referral for adrenalectomy at an expert centre`,`CT-guided percutaneous biopsy`,`Repeat CT in 12 months`,`Reassure and discharge`],answer:0,explanation:`A large, heterogeneous, high-attenuation mass is suspicious for adrenocortical carcinoma; once phaeochromocytoma is excluded, the guideline advises surgery by an experienced adrenal surgeon. Biopsy cannot reliably separate adenoma from carcinoma and risks seeding, while watchful waiting or discharge delays treatment of a possible cancer.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-q5`,stem:`A man on rifampicin-based ATT has a 2 cm adrenal adenoma and a post-dexamethasone cortisol of 4 mcg/dL. The most likely explanation for this result is:`,options:[`Adrenal tuberculosis`,`Accelerated dexamethasone metabolism`,`Ectopic ACTH secretion`,`Primary aldosteronism`],answer:1,explanation:`Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, speeding dexamethasone clearance, so cortisol is not suppressed and the DST is falsely positive; the test should be repeated off the drug or replaced by salivary cortisol. Adrenal TB causes low cortisol, ectopic ACTH would give florid catabolic Cushing, and aldosteronism does not affect the DST.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define adrenal incidentaloma. Discuss its evaluation and management as per the current ESE/ENSAT guideline.`,openingLines:[`An adrenal incidentaloma is an adrenal mass 1 cm or more found on imaging performed for reasons other than suspected adrenal disease; it is seen on about 1-5% of abdominal CTs and in about 7% of people over 70.`,`Evaluation answers two questions - is it malignant (unenhanced CT HU, size, heterogeneity) and is it hormonally active (1 mg DST in all, metanephrines if over 10 HU, ARR if hypertensive) - per ESE/ENSAT 2023.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Non-functioning adenoma (commonest), MACS adenoma, phaeochromocytoma, aldosteronoma, ACC, metastasis, myelolipoma, cyst, TB/histoplasmosis (bilateral).`]},{heading:`Imaging evaluation`,points:[`Unenhanced CT: 10 HU or less homogeneous = benign, no further imaging.`,`Over 10 HU: washout CT, chemical-shift MRI, FDG-PET, or repeat imaging at 6-12 months; over 4 cm and heterogeneous - surgery.`,`Biopsy only for suspected metastasis/infection after excluding phaeochromocytoma.`]},{heading:`Hormonal evaluation`,points:[`1 mg DST in all: 1.8 mcg/dL or less excludes; above = MACS (confirm with ACTH, DHEAS).`,`Metanephrines if over 10 HU; ARR if hypertension or hypokalaemia; androgens and steroid metabolomics if ACC suspected; 17-OHP and Synacthen test if bilateral.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Benign non-functioning: discharge.`,`MACS: treat HTN, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, osteoporosis; individualised adrenalectomy; perioperative hydrocortisone.`,`Overt hormone excess or suspected ACC: adrenalectomy (open for ACC at expert centre).`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`No routine imaging or hormone repeats for benign non-functioning; one repeat image for indeterminate; yearly comorbidity review for MACS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm: incidentaloma - unenhanced CT HU - DST in all - metanephrines/ARR as indicated - discharge / follow-up / surgery`,`Table comparing adenoma, phaeochromocytoma and ACC on imaging`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Hormonal evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`10 HU`,`1 mg DST`,`MACS`,`metanephrines`,`ACC`,`ESE/ENSAT 2023`]},{id:`endocrine-adrenal-incidentaloma-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS).`,openingLines:[`MACS is ACTH-independent cortisol secretion from an adrenal adenoma, defined by post-1 mg dexamethasone cortisol above 1.8 mcg/dL (50 nmol/L) without clinical features of overt Cushing syndrome (ESE/ENSAT 2023).`,`It occurs in 20-50% of adrenal adenomas and raises cardiometabolic risk and mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`1 mg DST cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL; supporting low ACTH and DHEAS; exclude rifampicin, oral oestrogen, depression, alcohol.`]},{heading:`Associations`,points:[`Hypertension, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, osteoporosis and vertebral fractures, cardiovascular events.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Screen and treat comorbidities yearly.`,`Adrenalectomy for younger patients with worsening comorbidities; hydrocortisone cover and post-op adrenal insufficiency testing.`]},{heading:`What changed in 2023`,points:[`Single term MACS replaces subclinical Cushing and possible/autonomous cortisol secretion.`]}],mustDraw:[`Spectrum diagram: non-functioning - MACS - overt Cushing with DST values`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Associations`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`MACS`,`1 mg DST`,`ACTH`,`DHEAS`,`adrenalectomy`,`steroid cover`]}],references:[`Fassnacht M, et al. European Society of Endocrinology clinical practice guidelines on the management of adrenal incidentalomas, in collaboration with ENSAT. Eur J Endocrinol, 2023`,`Fassnacht M, et al. ESE/ENSAT adrenal incidentaloma guideline, 2016`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the adrenal cortex`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Nieman LK, et al. The Diagnosis of Cushing's Syndrome: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2008`,`Lenders JWM, et al. Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2014`,`Adler GK, et al. Primary Aldosteronism: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2025`,`Fassnacht M, et al. ESE/ENSAT guideline on adrenocortical carcinoma, 2018`]});var c=[];c.push({id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease`,title:`Osteomalacia, rickets and Paget disease`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Osteomalacia** (adults) and **rickets** (children, at the open growth plate) are **defective mineralisation of newly formed osteoid**, most often from **vitamin D or calcium deficiency** and less often from **phosphate wasting (FGF23 excess, Fanconi)**; **Paget disease** is a focal disorder of **excessive osteoclastic resorption with disorganised new bone**, flagged by an **isolated raised ALP** and treated with a **single 5 mg zoledronic acid infusion**.`,keywords:[`osteomalacia`,`rickets`,`nutritional rickets`,`hypophosphataemic rickets`,`XLH`,`X-linked hypophosphataemia`,`FGF23`,`burosumab`,`tumour-induced osteomalacia`,`Looser zones`,`pseudofracture`,`Milkman syndrome`,`rachitic rosary`,`Harrison sulcus`,`Paget disease of bone`,`osteitis deformans`,`zoledronic acid`,`alkaline phosphatase`,`renal osteodystrophy`,`CKD-MBD`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Osteomalacia** is impaired mineralisation of the **bone matrix (osteoid)** in mature bone, so bone becomes soft and bends or develops pseudofractures; **rickets** is the same defect in a child plus failed mineralisation and disordered chondrocyte maturation at the **growth plate**, so children also get widened, cupped metaphyses and deformity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osteoporosis differs**: in osteoporosis bone is normally mineralised but reduced in amount; in osteomalacia the amount of matrix may be normal but it is under-mineralised - which is why osteomalacia hurts, weakens muscles and has abnormal biochemistry while osteoporosis is silent with normal biochemistry. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin D deficiency is very common in India**: community studies report serum 25(OH)D below 20 ng/mL in roughly **50-90%** of adults across regions, despite abundant sunlight, because of skin pigmentation, covered clothing, indoor work, air pollution and low-calcium, high-phytate vegetarian diets. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`The **Comprehensive National Nutrition Survey (CNNS 2016-18)** found vitamin D deficiency (25(OH)D below 12 ng/mL) in about **14% of preschool children, 18% of school-age children and 24% of adolescents** - biochemical deficiency is far commoner than clinical rickets. [CNNS 2019]`,`**Clinical nutritional rickets** still occurs in India in exclusively breastfed infants of deficient mothers, in toddlers on low-calcium diets and in adolescents (especially girls) at the pubertal growth spurt; **osteomalacia** is seen in purdah-observing and multiparous women, the elderly housebound and patients on long-term anticonvulsants. [IAP 2017]`,`**Paget disease of bone** affects about **1-2% of white people over 55** (commonest in the UK and in migrants from it), is uncommon but reported in Indians, is slightly commoner in men, and its incidence and severity have **fallen** over the last 30 years. [Ralston NEJM 2013]`,`**Renal osteodystrophy** is the bone component of **CKD-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD)** - it includes osteitis fibrosa (high-turnover, PTH-driven), adynamic bone disease and osteomalacia; its management is covered in the CKD topic and KDIGO guidance. [KDIGO CKD-MBD 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Vitamin D deficiency** (commonest): poor sun exposure (housebound, veiled, indoor workers, high latitude, dark skin, sunscreen), low intake, **exclusive prolonged breastfeeding without supplement**, obesity (sequestration in fat) and **maternal deficiency** in the newborn. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Impaired vitamin D absorption or metabolism**: coeliac disease, pancreatic insufficiency, bariatric surgery, cholestatic liver disease, **CKD** (loss of 1-alpha hydroxylation), and **enzyme-inducing drugs - phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, rifampicin** - that catabolise 25(OH)D. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calcium-deficiency rickets**: children on very low-calcium diets (cereal-based, little dairy) develop rickets even with normal 25(OH)D - described in Nigeria and in parts of India. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**FGF23-mediated phosphate wasting**: **X-linked hypophosphataemia (XLH, PHEX mutation)** - the commonest heritable rickets, about 1 in 20,000; autosomal dominant (FGF23) and recessive (DMP1, ENPP1) forms; and acquired **tumour-induced osteomalacia (TIO)** from small phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic FGF23 excess**: **ferric carboxymaltose** infusion raises intact FGF23 and causes hypophosphataemia, and repeated doses have caused osteomalacia - check phosphate in patients receiving repeated infusions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal tubular phosphate loss (Fanconi syndrome)**: **tenofovir disoproxil**, adefovir, ifosfamide, multiple myeloma light chains, Wilson disease, cystinosis and heavy metals - glycosuria, aminoaciduria and acidosis accompany it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rare inherited vitamin D disorders**: **VDDR type 1A** (CYP27B1, 1-alpha hydroxylase defect - low calcitriol) and **VDDR type 2A** (vitamin D receptor defect - very high calcitriol, often **alopecia**); **hypophosphatasia** (ALPL mutation) is the one rickets-like disease with a **low ALP**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other mineralisation inhibitors**: aluminium (old dialysis fluids, antacids), etidronate, excess **fluoride (skeletal fluorosis)** and severe metabolic acidosis (renal tubular acidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease risk factors**: age over 55, **family history** (first-degree relative raises risk about 7-10 fold), **SQSTM1 (p62) mutations** in about 40% of familial and 10% of sporadic cases; a slow-virus (paramyxovirus) trigger has been proposed but is unproven. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Mineralisation needs an adequate **calcium x phosphate product** at the osteoid surface plus normal ALP to clear the mineralisation inhibitor **pyrophosphate** - any shortage of calcium, phosphate or ALP leaves osteoid unmineralised. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **vitamin D deficiency** gut calcium absorption falls from about 30-40% to 10-15%; the fall in ionised calcium drives **secondary hyperparathyroidism**, which keeps calcium near normal but causes **phosphaturia and low phosphate** - so the product falls and osteoid is not mineralised. [Harrison 22e]`,`The sequence explains the stages: early - low 25(OH)D, high PTH, normal calcium; later - low phosphate, high ALP; late - **low calcium with tetany** once bone and PTH can no longer compensate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTH also stimulates 1-alpha hydroxylase**, so serum **1,25(OH)2D is often normal or even high** in nutritional deficiency - measuring calcitriol to diagnose vitamin D deficiency is a classic error. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**FGF23** (from osteocytes) removes NaPi-2a/2c phosphate transporters from the proximal tubule and **suppresses 1-alpha hydroxylase** - so FGF23 excess (XLH, TIO) gives **low phosphate with low or inappropriately normal calcitriol** and normal calcium and PTH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Proximal myopathy** comes from low phosphate (reduced muscle ATP) and loss of vitamin D receptor signalling in type 2 muscle fibres; bone pain comes from **hydrated osteoid expanding under the periosteum**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Looser zones (pseudofractures)** are linear bands of unmineralised osteoid at points where arteries cross bone or stress concentrates - the femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs, lateral scapular border and ulna. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease** starts with large, multinucleated **hyperactive osteoclasts** (containing nuclear inclusions; enhanced RANKL sensitivity in SQSTM1 mutation) that resorb bone; osteoblasts respond with rapid, disorganised **woven bone** - bigger, more vascular, weaker and more deformed than normal, with a mosaic of cement lines on histology. [Harrison 22e]`,`Paget phases: **lytic** (advancing 'blade of grass' or flame-shaped front; osteoporosis circumscripta in the skull), **mixed**, and **sclerotic** (cotton-wool skull, picture-frame or ivory vertebra) - ALP tracks the osteoblastic activity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Calciopenic rickets/osteomalacia** (low calcium supply): nutritional vitamin D deficiency, calcium deficiency, malabsorption, liver disease, anticonvulsants, CKD, VDDR 1A and 2A - **PTH high, phosphate low or normal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phosphopenic rickets/osteomalacia** (phosphate loss): FGF23-mediated (XLH, ADHR, ARHR, TIO, iron infusion) and FGF23-independent (Fanconi, hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets with hypercalciuria - SLC34A3) - **PTH normal, calcium normal, phosphate low**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mineralisation-inhibitor causes**: hypophosphatasia (low ALP), aluminium, fluoride, etidronate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin D status thresholds** (Global Consensus 2016, also used by IAP): **sufficient above 20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L)**, insufficient 12-20 ng/mL (30-50 nmol/L), **deficient below 12 ng/mL (30 nmol/L)**; the Endocrine Society 2011 used deficiency below 20 and insufficiency 21-29 ng/mL. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Thacher Rickets Severity Score** grades wrist and knee radiographs from 0 to 10 - a score of **1.5 or more** supports active rickets and is used to monitor healing. [Thacher 2000]`,`**Paget disease** is classified as **monostotic** (one bone, about 30%) or **polyostotic** (about 70%) and by phase (lytic, mixed, sclerotic); it does not spread to new bones after diagnosis. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Adult osteomalacia**: diffuse, dull **bone pain** (lower back, pelvis, hips, ribs), **difficulty climbing stairs or rising from squatting** (proximal weakness), a **waddling gait**, fatigue and fractures after minor trauma - often misdiagnosed as fibromyalgia, polymyositis or 'body ache'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infant rickets**: delayed sitting, standing and walking, irritability, sweating, poor growth, frequent chest infections ('rachitic lung'), and **hypocalcaemic seizures or tetany** - which may be the first presentation under 6 months. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Toddler and child**: bowing of legs after walking starts (genu varum), knock-knees later (genu valgum), wide wrists, delayed dentition and dental caries. [IAP 2017]`,`**Adolescent**: knee and leg pain, genu valgum, waddling gait and tetany during the pubertal growth spurt. [IAP 2017]`,`**Clues to hypophosphataemic rickets**: **family history** (often mother or father with short stature and bowed legs), leg bowing that does not respond to vitamin D, short stature, **dental abscesses without caries**, and **no tetany or myopathy** (calcium is normal). [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**Tumour-induced osteomalacia**: an adult with progressive bone pain, severe weakness, multiple fractures and height loss over years, **low phosphate** and no family history - a small soft tissue or bone tumour (often in the leg or head and neck) is the cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease**: about 70% are **asymptomatic** and found by an isolated raised ALP or an X-ray; symptomatic patients have deep, constant **bone pain worse at night and at rest**, increasing hat size, bowing of a leg, deafness, or secondary hip and knee arthritis. [Ralston NEJM 2013]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Craniotabes** (ping-pong ball feel of the parieto-occipital skull) - an early sign in infants, though it can be normal in the first 3 months; **frontal and parietal bossing** and **delayed closure of the anterior fontanelle** (beyond 18 months). [IAP 2017]`,`**Rachitic rosary** - palpable beading of the costochondral junctions; **Harrison sulcus** - a horizontal groove at the lower rib cage where the diaphragm pulls on soft ribs; pigeon chest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Widened wrists and ankles** (metaphyseal flaring, double malleoli), **genu varum or valgum** (measure intercondylar or intermalleolar distance), windswept deformity, coxa vara and short stature. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Hypocalcaemia signs**: **Chvostek sign** (facial twitch on tapping the facial nerve), **Trousseau sign** (carpal spasm with BP cuff above systolic for 3 minutes), carpopedal spasm and laryngospasm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adult osteomalacia**: **bone tenderness on pressing the sternum, ribs or tibia**, proximal muscle weakness with **normal or brisk reflexes** and no wasting (unlike neuropathy), waddling gait and kyphosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease**: **warmth over affected bone** (increased vascularity), enlarged skull with frontal bossing, **anterolateral bowing of the femur or anterior bowing of the tibia (sabre tibia)**, kyphosis, and conductive or sensorineural deafness. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for clues to the cause: **dental abscesses** (XLH), **alopecia** (VDDR 2A), signs of malabsorption, liver disease or CKD, a palpable soft tissue tumour (TIO), and **mottled teeth with genu valgum** in a fluorosis-endemic area. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Osteoporosis**: no pain until fracture, normal muscle strength and **normal calcium, phosphate and ALP** - an abnormal ALP or phosphate in an 'osteoporotic' patient demands exclusion of osteomalacia before starting a bisphosphonate (see the Osteoporosis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Polymyositis and other myopathies**: raised **CK** and muscle pain; osteomalacic myopathy has **normal CK** and high ALP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary hyperparathyroidism**: high calcium with high or inappropriately normal PTH and low-normal phosphate; in vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia **calcium is low or normal** while PTH is high (secondary). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bone metastases and myeloma**: high ALP and bone pain - but metastases often have high calcium, and myeloma has anaemia, high ESR, a normal ALP and lytic lesions; prostate cancer gives sclerotic lesions like Paget. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Physiological bowing**: genu varum is normal up to about 2 years and genu valgum from 3 to 7 years; it is symmetrical, with normal height, wrists and biochemistry. [IAP 2017]`,`**Skeletal fluorosis**: genu valgum, dental mottling, **osteosclerosis** and ligament calcification in an endemic area (Rajasthan, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Gujarat) - water fluoride above 1.5 mg/L. [NPPCF]`,`**Raised ALP from the liver**: check **GGT** (raised in liver disease, normal in bone disease) or bone-specific ALP; a growing child or adolescent normally has an ALP two to three times the adult range. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line (district hospital)**: serum **calcium (albumin-corrected), phosphate, ALP**, creatinine, albumin and **25(OH)D**; add **PTH** when available - this five-test panel separates almost all causes (see the biochemical pattern table). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nutritional rickets** is diagnosed by history, examination and biochemistry, **confirmed by radiographs**: wrist (under 3 years) or knee - **cupping, fraying and splaying** of the metaphysis with a widened growth plate. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Typical vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia**: low 25(OH)D (usually under 10-12 ng/mL), **high ALP (in about 95%)**, high PTH, low or normal calcium and phosphate, and **low 24-hour urine calcium**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypophosphataemic rickets** work-up: fasting phosphate (compared with the age-specific range - children's normal values are higher), **TmP/GFR** (low), urine calcium, **intact FGF23** (high or inappropriately normal), 1,25(OH)2D (low or normal) and PHEX gene testing. [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**Tumour-induced osteomalacia**: once FGF23 is high and there is no family history, localise the tumour with **68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT** (the tumours express somatostatin receptors), then MRI of the lesion; venous sampling for FGF23 if needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**X-rays in adults**: **Looser zones** (symmetrical, perpendicular to the cortex), codfish (biconcave) vertebrae, a triradiate pelvis in severe cases; a **bone biopsy after tetracycline double labelling** is the gold standard but rarely needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease**: **raised total ALP with normal calcium, phosphate and GGT**; plain X-rays of symptomatic sites show expansion, cortical thickening and coarse trabeculae; an **isotope bone scan** maps the extent; P1NP or bone-specific ALP for monostotic disease with a normal total ALP. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`,`**Do not** measure 1,25(OH)2D to diagnose vitamin D deficiency; measure it only when inherited or renal disorders of activation, granulomatous disease or hypophosphataemia are being evaluated. [Endocrine Society 2011]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Nutritional rickets** (Global Consensus 2016): **cholecalciferol at least 2000 IU daily for at least 12 weeks** at every age, or a single stoss dose - **50,000 IU (3-12 months), 150,000 IU (1-12 years), 300,000 IU (over 12 years)** - always with **elemental calcium 500 mg/day** (diet or supplement). [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**IAP 2017 schedule** used widely in India: infants under 1 year 2000 IU daily for 12 weeks; children 1-18 years **3000-6000 IU daily for 12 weeks** or **60,000 IU weekly for 6 weeks**; then maintenance **400 IU (under 1 year) or 600 IU daily**. [IAP 2017]`,`**Adult osteomalacia** from deficiency: **cholecalciferol 60,000 IU orally once weekly for 8 weeks** (Endocrine Society: 50,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks or 6000 IU daily), then maintenance **1500-2000 IU daily or 60,000 IU monthly**, plus **elemental calcium 1000-1200 mg/day** in divided doses. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Calcium carbonate** (40% elemental) is cheap and on NLEM but needs food and acid; **calcium citrate** (21% elemental) suits achlorhydria, PPI users and post-bariatric patients; give no more than **500 mg elemental per dose** for absorption. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malabsorption, obesity and enzyme-inducing drugs** need **2-3 times the usual dose** (for example 6000-10,000 IU/day); in cholestatic liver disease or poor response, **calcifediol** (25(OH)D3, 50 mcg weekly capsules are sold in India) is better absorbed. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Disorders of activation** (CKD stage 4-5, VDDR 1A, hypoparathyroidism) need **active vitamin D - calcitriol 0.25-1 mcg/day or alfacalcidol 0.5-2 mcg/day** - with calcium and phosphate monitoring; ordinary cholecalciferol will not heal VDDR 1A. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Expected response**: bone pain eases in weeks, **ALP may rise transiently** as healing osteoid mineralises then falls over 3-6 months; radiological healing of rickets (a white **zone of provisional calcification**) appears in about 3-4 weeks and is near complete by 3 months. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Avoid single mega-doses in adults** (300,000-600,000 IU injections): annual 500,000 IU oral doses **increased falls and fractures** in older women, and injectable depot forms give erratic levels - weekly or daily oral dosing is preferred. [Sanders JAMA 2010]`,`**Paget disease - symptomatic**: **zoledronic acid 5 mg IV over at least 15 minutes, single dose**, after confirming 25(OH)D is replete and calcium normal; give **calcium 500 mg twice daily and vitamin D for 10-14 days after** to prevent hypocalcaemia; paracetamol or NSAID for pain. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`,`**Paget alternatives**: **risedronate 30 mg daily for 2 months** or **alendronate 40 mg daily for 6 months** (oral bisphosphonates taken fasting with a full glass of water, upright for 30 minutes); **calcitonin is no longer recommended** (less effective, cancer signal). [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: hypocalcaemic seizures, tetany or laryngospasm; infants with **rickets and heart failure (hypocalcaemic dilated cardiomyopathy)**; severe hypophosphataemia (under 1 mg/dL) with weakness; and fractures needing surgery. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Symptomatic hypocalcaemia**: **10% calcium gluconate 10-20 mL (adults) or 1-2 mL/kg (children, max 10 mL) diluted, IV over 10 minutes** with ECG monitoring, then an infusion of elemental calcium 0.5-1.5 mg/kg/hour; start oral calcium and vitamin D the same day. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Correct magnesium** if low (magnesium sulphate) - hypocalcaemia will not correct while hypomagnesaemia persists; avoid giving calcium through the same line as bicarbonate or phosphate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hungry-bone risk**: starting high-dose vitamin D in severe osteomalacia can drop calcium further as bones take up mineral - always co-prescribe calcium and recheck calcium at 1-2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease before orthopaedic surgery** (hip or knee arthroplasty, osteotomy) on actively pagetic bone: treat with zoledronic acid beforehand to reduce bleeding from the hypervascular bone (expert opinion, weak evidence). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Immobilised Paget patients** can develop **hypercalcaemia** because resorption continues without formation - check calcium, hydrate and give a bisphosphonate. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and lactation**: give **cholecalciferol 1000-2000 IU daily** to deficient women, plus calcium (the national programme supplies **calcium 500 mg twice daily from 14 weeks** for pre-eclampsia prevention); avoid stoss doses; supplement the newborn with 400 IU daily. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**Infants**: **400 IU vitamin D daily from birth to 12 months** for all breastfed infants and those taking under 1 litre of formula - the single most effective rickets prevention. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Preterm infants** develop **metabolic bone disease of prematurity** (low phosphate, ALP over 900 IU/L) - needs fortified milk with phosphate and calcium, not just vitamin D. [IAP 2017]`,`**CKD**: 25(OH)D can be repleted with cholecalciferol in all stages; active vitamin D analogues are reserved for **severe and progressive secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD 4-5** - routine calcitriol in CKD 3-4 is no longer advised. [KDIGO CKD-MBD 2017]`,`**Elderly**: daily or weekly dosing, falls assessment and calcium from diet; treat deficiency before starting osteoporosis drugs, especially **zoledronic acid or denosumab** (hypocalcaemia risk). [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Antiepileptics and anti-TB drugs**: patients on long-term phenytoin, carbamazepine or phenobarbital, and those on rifampicin with low intake, should receive maintenance cholecalciferol and have ALP checked yearly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV on tenofovir disoproxil (TDF)**: unexplained bone pain or low phosphate with glycosuria means **Fanconi syndrome** - switch to a TDF-free regimen per NACO guidance. [NACO 2021]`,`**Paget disease in the elderly**: renal function must allow bisphosphonate use - zoledronic acid is **avoided if creatinine clearance is under 35 mL/min**. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Rickets**: permanent leg deformity (genu varum/valgum may need guided growth or osteotomy if persisting after 2 years of treatment), short stature, **contracted pelvis** in girls (later obstructed labour), dental defects and hypocalcaemic cardiomyopathy. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Osteomalacia**: fractures (femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs), kyphosis, height loss and disability - pain and myopathy usually resolve within months of treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**XLH in adults**: enthesopathy, early osteoarthritis, spinal stenosis, pseudofractures, dental abscesses and hearing loss; **nephrocalcinosis and tertiary hyperparathyroidism** are complications of phosphate and calcitriol therapy. [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**Paget disease - skeletal**: bone deformity, **pathological fracture** (fissure fractures on the convex side), secondary osteoarthritis, spinal stenosis and basilar invagination. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease - neurological and other**: **deafness** (ossicle involvement and cochlear damage), cranial nerve palsies, hydrocephalus, **high-output heart failure** with extensive disease, and hypercalcaemia on immobilisation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osteosarcoma** arises in **under 1%** of Paget patients - suspect it when pain becomes **new, severe and localised with swelling** or ALP rises sharply; prognosis is poor. [Ralston NEJM 2013]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Nutritional rickets**: check calcium, phosphate and ALP at **4 weeks** (healing begins) and at **3 months** with a repeat radiograph; 25(OH)D at 3 months; persistent deformity at 2 years goes to orthopaedics. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`**Refer to an endocrinologist**: rickets that does **not heal after 3 months** of adequate vitamin D, **normal 25(OH)D with rickets**, low phosphate with normal PTH, a family history, alopecia, low ALP, or adult-onset hypophosphataemia (suspect TIO). [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**Adult osteomalacia**: recheck calcium and ALP at 4-8 weeks, 25(OH)D at **3 months** (target above 20-30 ng/mL), and stop high-dose loading once replete; check urine calcium if levels rise high. [Endocrine Society 2011]`,`**Vitamin D toxicity** (25(OH)D usually above 150 ng/mL) causes hypercalcaemia, vomiting, polyuria, nephrocalcinosis and AKI - commonly from **unsupervised 60,000 IU sachets taken daily** instead of weekly in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget disease**: review symptoms and **ALP 3-6 months after zoledronic acid**, then yearly; retreat for **recurrent pain** with a rising ALP - a single infusion often gives remission for years. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`,`**XLH**: specialist follow-up every 3-6 months for growth, phosphate, ALP, PTH, urine calcium and **renal ultrasound for nephrocalcinosis**; dental review every 6 months. [XLH Consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Sun exposure**: about **15-30 minutes of midday sun on face and arms** several days a week produces adequate vitamin D in most Indians; darker skin and pollution need longer. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**ICMR-NIN RDA 2020** recommends **600 IU (15 mcg) vitamin D daily** for children and adults (400 IU for infants) and **calcium about 1000 mg/day** for adults - most Indian diets fall short of both. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**Food fortification**: FSSAI standards permit fortification of **edible oil and milk with vitamins A and D** (+F logo) - encourage fortified products. [FSSAI 2018]`,`**RBSK** (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram) screens children from birth to 18 years for the '4 Ds', which include **vitamin D deficiency/rickets** under deficiencies, with referral to District Early Intervention Centres. [RBSK, MoHFW]`,`**Calcium-rich Indian foods**: milk and curd, paneer, ragi (finger millet - about 340 mg/100 g), sesame, green leafy vegetables and small fish with bones. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**Fluorosis areas**: the **National Programme for Prevention and Control of Fluorosis (NPPCF)** provides defluoridated water and calcium/vitamin D supplementation - consider fluorosis in endemic districts. [NPPCF]`,`**Paget disease counselling**: first-degree relatives may be screened with ALP after 40 if there is a strong family history; genetic testing for SQSTM1 is research-level in India. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Endocrine Society 2024 guideline (vitamin D for prevention of disease)**: advises **against routine 25(OH)D testing** in the general population, suggests **empiric supplementation** for children and adolescents 1-18 years, pregnancy, **adults 75 and over** and high-risk prediabetes, and **no longer defines a target level** - treatment of osteomalacia itself is unchanged. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`The 2024 guideline suggests **daily lower-dose rather than intermittent high-dose** vitamin D in adults over 50 who need it - consistent with the falls data from mega-doses. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**ZiPP trial (2024)**: a single prophylactic zoledronic acid infusion in **SQSTM1 mutation carriers** reduced the development of new Paget lesions - promising but not yet a routine recommendation. [Ralston JBMR 2024]`,`**Paget international guideline (2019)**: bisphosphonates are for **bone pain** from active disease; **'treat-to-target' to normalise ALP is not recommended** (PRISM-EZ showed no benefit in fractures or quality of life). [Ralston JBMR 2019]`,`**Burosumab** (anti-FGF23 antibody) is now recommended in many guidelines as **first-line for children with XLH** (and for symptomatic adults and TIO when the tumour cannot be removed), but its cost limits use in India - conventional phosphate plus calcitriol remains standard. [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**ICMR-NIN 2020 RDA** raised the vitamin D recommendation to **600 IU/day** and is the current Indian reference for intake counselling. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Biochemical differentiation of osteomalacia, osteoporosis and Paget disease'** - draw the calcium, phosphate, ALP, PTH table. [Exam pattern]`,`**Viva**: 'Which rickets has a low ALP?' - **hypophosphatasia**; 'Which has very high calcitriol and alopecia?' - **VDDR type 2A**; 'Which has normal PTH and low phosphate?' - **XLH**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling a patient with bone pain, proximal weakness and high ALP 'osteoporosis' and starting alendronate - bisphosphonates worsen untreated osteomalacia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: treating XLH with large doses of cholecalciferol alone - it does not heal and causes hypercalciuria; it needs **phosphate plus calcitriol or burosumab**. [XLH Consensus 2019]`,`**Looser zone vs stress fracture**: a Looser zone is a bilateral, symmetrical, cortical lucent band perpendicular to the surface without callus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paget viva**: 'Increasing hat size, deafness and a warm bowed tibia in an elderly man with isolated high ALP' - treat with **zoledronic acid 5 mg once**. [Ralston JBMR 2019]`,`**Earliest radiological sign of healing rickets**: a dense white line at the metaphysis (zone of provisional calcification) in about 3-4 weeks. [IAP 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Biochemical patterns in metabolic bone disease`,columns:[`Condition`,`Calcium`,`Phosphate`,`ALP`,`PTH`,`25(OH)D`,`1,25(OH)2D`,`Other clue`],rows:[[`Nutritional vitamin D deficiency`,`Low or normal`,`Low or normal`,`High`,`High`,`Low`,`Normal or high`,`Low urine calcium`],[`Calcium-deficiency rickets`,`Low or normal`,`Low or normal`,`High`,`High`,`Normal`,`High`,`Low dietary calcium`],[`XLH / TIO (FGF23 excess)`,`Normal`,`Low`,`High`,`Normal or mildly high`,`Normal`,`Low or inappropriately normal`,`Low TmP/GFR, high FGF23`],[`VDDR type 1A (1-alpha hydroxylase)`,`Low`,`Low`,`High`,`High`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Responds to calcitriol only`],[`VDDR type 2A (receptor defect)`,`Low`,`Low`,`High`,`High`,`Normal`,`Very high`,`Alopecia in many`],[`Hypophosphatasia`,`Normal or high`,`Normal or high`,`Low`,`Normal or low`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`High pyridoxal 5-phosphate`],[`Renal osteodystrophy (CKD)`,`Low`,`High`,`High`,`High`,`Often low`,`Low`,`Raised creatinine`],[`Paget disease`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`High`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal GGT, bone scan hot spots`],[`Osteoporosis`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal or low`,`Normal`,`Low T-score on DXA`]]},{heading:`Drugs for osteomalacia, rickets and Paget disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cholecalciferol (child, nutritional rickets)`,`2000 IU oral daily (global); IAP: 3000-6000 IU daily or 60,000 IU weekly over 1 year; stoss 50,000-300,000 IU once by age`,`12 weeks, then 400-600 IU daily`,`Hypercalcaemia if overdosed; always give calcium 500 mg/day`],[`Cholecalciferol (adult osteomalacia)`,`60,000 IU oral weekly (or 6000 IU daily)`,`8 weeks, then 1500-2000 IU daily or 60,000 IU monthly`,`Toxicity if sachets taken daily; check calcium at 1-2 weeks in severe disease`],[`Calcium carbonate / citrate`,`Elemental 500-1200 mg/day oral in doses of 500 mg or less`,`During repletion and long term if diet is low`,`Constipation, bloating; carbonate needs food; separate from iron and thyroxine`],[`Calcifediol`,`50 mcg oral weekly (or 20-30 mcg daily)`,`8-12 weeks, recheck 25(OH)D`,`Faster rise; useful in malabsorption and liver disease; hypercalcaemia`],[`Calcitriol / alfacalcidol`,`Calcitriol 0.25-1 mcg/day; alfacalcidol 0.5-2 mcg/day oral`,`Titrate to calcium, PTH and urine calcium`,`Hypercalcaemia, hypercalciuria, nephrocalcinosis`],[`Oral phosphate (XLH)`,`20-60 mg/kg/day elemental in 4-6 divided doses (children)`,`Long term until growth complete, with calcitriol`,`Diarrhoea, secondary/tertiary hyperparathyroidism, nephrocalcinosis`],[`Burosumab (XLH, TIO)`,`Children 0.8 mg/kg SC every 2 weeks; adults 1 mg/kg SC every 4 weeks`,`Long term; titrate to fasting phosphate`,`Injection-site reactions, restless legs; very expensive; stop oral phosphate and calcitriol`],[`Zoledronic acid (Paget)`,`5 mg IV infusion over at least 15 minutes`,`Single dose; repeat only for relapse`,`Acute-phase flu-like reaction, hypocalcaemia (replete vitamin D first), avoid if CrCl under 35 mL/min, osteonecrosis of jaw (rare)`],[`Risedronate (Paget)`,`30 mg oral daily, fasting, upright 30 min`,`2 months`,`Oesophagitis, hypocalcaemia; avoid if CrCl under 30 mL/min`],[`Calcium gluconate 10% (tetany)`,`10-20 mL (adults) or 1-2 mL/kg (children) IV over 10 min, then infusion`,`Until oral therapy takes over`,`Extravasation necrosis, arrhythmia on rapid injection, digoxin toxicity`]]},{heading:`Classification of rickets and osteomalacia`,columns:[`Group`,`Causes`,`Mechanism`,`Treatment principle`],rows:[[`Calciopenic - nutritional`,`Vitamin D deficiency, calcium deficiency`,`Low calcium absorption, secondary hyperparathyroidism`,`Cholecalciferol plus calcium`],[`Calciopenic - malabsorption/metabolism`,`Coeliac, bariatric, liver disease, anticonvulsants, rifampicin`,`Low absorption or high catabolism of vitamin D`,`High-dose cholecalciferol or calcifediol`],[`Calciopenic - activation defect`,`CKD 4-5, VDDR 1A`,`No 1-alpha hydroxylation`,`Calcitriol or alfacalcidol`],[`Calciopenic - end-organ resistance`,`VDDR 2A (VDR mutation)`,`Receptor defect`,`Very high-dose calcitriol, IV calcium`],[`Phosphopenic - FGF23 excess`,`XLH, ADHR, ARHR, TIO, ferric carboxymaltose`,`Renal phosphate wasting, low calcitriol`,`Phosphate plus calcitriol, burosumab, tumour resection`],[`Phosphopenic - tubular`,`Fanconi (tenofovir, myeloma, Wilson), HHRH`,`Proximal tubular phosphate loss`,`Treat cause, phosphate`],[`Mineralisation inhibitors`,`Hypophosphatasia, aluminium, fluoride, etidronate`,`Pyrophosphate excess or toxic inhibition`,`Remove toxin; asfotase alfa for hypophosphatasia`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of bone pain with a raised ALP`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key clinical clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Osteomalacia`,`Diffuse pain, proximal weakness, waddling gait`,`Low 25(OH)D, high PTH, low phosphate, Looser zones`],[`Paget disease`,`Older, warm bowed bone, deafness, often asymptomatic`,`Isolated high ALP, normal calcium; bone scan hot spots`],[`Primary hyperparathyroidism`,`Stones, bones, groans, moans`,`High calcium with non-suppressed PTH`],[`Bone metastases`,`Known primary (breast, prostate, lung), weight loss, focal pain`,`Bone scan, PSA, imaging of primary; calcium often high`],[`Healing fracture`,`Recent trauma`,`X-ray shows callus; ALP falls in weeks`],[`Liver disease (not bone)`,`Jaundice, alcohol, cholestasis`,`High GGT, high bilirubin; normal bone-specific ALP`],[`Normal growth`,`Child or adolescent`,`Age-specific ALP range; normal calcium, phosphate, X-ray`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Serum 25(OH)D`,`Below 12 ng/mL deficient; 12-20 insufficient; above 20 sufficient (Global 2016); toxicity above 150`,`Best marker of vitamin D stores`],[`Serum ALP`,`Raised in about 95% of osteomalacia; low in hypophosphatasia`,`Check GGT to confirm bone origin`],[`Serum PTH`,`High in calciopenic, normal in phosphopenic`,`Separates calcium from phosphate disorders`],[`Fasting phosphate and TmP/GFR`,`Low TmP/GFR with low phosphate`,`Renal phosphate wasting (FGF23 or Fanconi)`],[`Intact FGF23`,`High or inappropriately normal with hypophosphataemia`,`XLH, TIO, iron-induced`],[`Wrist/knee X-ray`,`Cupping, fraying, splaying; RSS 1.5 or more`,`Active rickets; monitor healing`],[`Pelvis/long-bone X-ray`,`Looser zones; Paget expansion, blade-of-grass lesion`,`Osteomalacia vs Paget`],[`68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT`,`Focal uptake in soft tissue or bone`,`Localises TIO tumour`],[`Isotope bone scan`,`Intense uptake in affected bones`,`Extent of Paget disease`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypocalcaemic seizure, laryngospasm or tetany in an infant or adult`,`Infant with rickets and breathlessness or heart failure (hypocalcaemic cardiomyopathy)`,`Rickets not healing after 3 months of adequate vitamin D and calcium (suspect hypophosphataemic or inherited rickets)`,`Adult with progressive bone pain, fractures and low phosphate without family history (tumour-induced osteomalacia)`,`Paget disease with new severe localised pain, swelling or a sharp ALP rise (osteosarcoma)`,`Paget disease of the skull with headache, cranial nerve palsy or long tract signs (basilar invagination, hydrocephalus)`,`Vomiting, polyuria and confusion in a patient taking 60,000 IU sachets (vitamin D toxicity with hypercalcaemia)`],pearls:[`**Osteomalacia hurts, osteoporosis does not** - bone pain plus proximal weakness plus high ALP is osteomalacia until proved otherwise.`,`**Low phosphate + normal PTH = FGF23 problem**; low phosphate + high PTH = vitamin D or calcium problem.`,`**Never diagnose vitamin D deficiency with 1,25(OH)2D** - it is often normal or high because PTH drives 1-alpha hydroxylase.`,`**Low ALP rickets = hypophosphatasia**; **alopecia + very high calcitriol = VDDR 2A**.`,`**Replete vitamin D before zoledronic acid or denosumab** - or expect symptomatic hypocalcaemia.`,`**Paget = big, warm, bent bone with isolated high ALP** - one zoledronic acid infusion controls it for years.`,`Looser zones sit where **arteries cross bone**: femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs, lateral scapula.`,`**60,000 IU is a weekly dose, not daily** - vitamin D toxicity in India usually comes from misread sachets.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman who wears a burqa has had low back and hip pain for a year and now has difficulty rising from squatting. Calcium is 8.2 mg/dL, phosphate 2.1 mg/dL, ALP 410 IU/L and PTH 168 pg/mL. Which result would you expect next?`,options:[`Serum 25(OH)D of 6 ng/mL`,`Serum 1,25(OH)2D markedly low`,`Raised intact FGF23 with low TmP/GFR`,`Low serum ALP isoenzyme from bone`],answer:0,explanation:`Bone pain, proximal myopathy, low-normal calcium, low phosphate, high ALP and high PTH in a sun-deprived woman is nutritional vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia, so 25(OH)D will be very low. Calcitriol is usually normal or high because secondary hyperparathyroidism stimulates 1-alpha hydroxylase. A raised FGF23 belongs to phosphopenic rickets, where PTH is normal, and a low bone ALP is hypophosphatasia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-q2`,stem:`A 6-year-old boy has bowed legs and short stature despite three courses of vitamin D. His mother is short with bowed legs. Calcium is normal, phosphate low, ALP high, PTH normal and 25(OH)D 32 ng/mL. The most appropriate treatment is:`,options:[`Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for another 12 weeks`,`Calcium carbonate 1 g daily alone`,`Oral phosphate in divided doses with calcitriol, or burosumab`,`Zoledronic acid infusion`],answer:2,explanation:`Low phosphate with normal calcium and PTH, normal 25(OH)D, vitamin D resistance and an affected parent point to X-linked hypophosphataemia, an FGF23-mediated phosphate wasting disorder. It needs phosphate replacement with calcitriol, or burosumab where affordable. More cholecalciferol does not heal it and risks hypercalciuria, calcium alone does not address phosphate loss, and bisphosphonates have no role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old man has an ALP of 520 IU/L found on a routine check. GGT, calcium, phosphate and creatinine are normal. He complains of a warm, bowed right shin and needs a bigger hat. The best single treatment is:`,options:[`Calcitonin injections for 6 months`,`Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV once after confirming vitamin D sufficiency`,`Alendronate 70 mg weekly for 5 years`,`Calcitriol 0.5 mcg daily`],answer:1,explanation:`An isolated high ALP with normal GGT, calcium and phosphate, a warm bowed tibia and enlarging skull is symptomatic Paget disease. A single 5 mg zoledronic acid infusion gives the longest remission, after vitamin D and calcium are replete. Calcitonin is no longer recommended; the osteoporosis alendronate dose of 70 mg weekly is inadequate for Paget (40 mg daily for 6 months is used); calcitriol has no role.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has had progressive bone pain, two rib fractures and severe weakness over 3 years. There is no family history. Phosphate is 1.4 mg/dL with low TmP/GFR, calcium and PTH are normal, 1,25(OH)2D is low and intact FGF23 is high. The next investigation is:`,options:[`PHEX gene sequencing`,`Bone marrow biopsy`,`Serum protein electrophoresis`,`68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT`],answer:3,explanation:`Adult-onset FGF23-mediated hypophosphataemia without a family history is tumour-induced osteomalacia until proved otherwise. The small phosphaturic mesenchymal tumours express somatostatin receptors and are found with 68Ga-DOTATATE PET-CT; removing the tumour cures the disease. PHEX testing suits childhood-onset familial disease, while myeloma causes Fanconi-type phosphate loss with a normal FGF23 and would be screened with electrophoresis.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-q5`,stem:`In a 2-year-old with nutritional rickets treated with cholecalciferol and calcium, the earliest radiological sign of healing is:`,options:[`Disappearance of the rachitic rosary`,`Normalisation of genu varum`,`A dense line of provisional calcification at the metaphysis`,`Closure of the anterior fontanelle`],answer:2,explanation:`A dense white zone of provisional calcification appears at the metaphysis within about 3-4 weeks of effective treatment and is the earliest radiological sign of healing. The rachitic rosary and limb bowing resolve only over months to years and are clinical, not radiological, markers. Fontanelle closure depends on age and is not a measure of healing.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Classify rickets. Describe the clinical features, investigations and management of nutritional rickets. Add a note on hypophosphataemic rickets.`,openingLines:[`Rickets is defective mineralisation of osteoid and of the growth plate cartilage in a growing child, causing bone deformity, growth failure and, at times, hypocalcaemia.`,`Nutritional vitamin D and calcium deficiency is the commonest cause in India; phosphopenic (FGF23-mediated) rickets is the commonest heritable form.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Calciopenic**: nutritional vitamin D or calcium deficiency, malabsorption, liver disease, anticonvulsants, CKD, VDDR 1A and 2A.`,`**Phosphopenic**: FGF23-mediated (XLH, ADHR, ARHR, TIO) and tubular (Fanconi, HHRH).`,`**Mineralisation inhibitors**: hypophosphatasia, aluminium, fluoride.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology in brief`,points:[`Low vitamin D lowers calcium absorption; **secondary hyperparathyroidism** causes phosphaturia and a low calcium-phosphate product.`,`Unmineralised osteoid and disordered chondrocytes widen and cup the growth plate.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Skull: **craniotabes**, frontal bossing, delayed fontanelle closure; delayed dentition.`,`Chest: **rachitic rosary, Harrison sulcus**, pigeon chest; recurrent chest infections.`,`Limbs: **wrist widening, genu varum/valgum**, double malleoli; delayed motor milestones, hypotonia.`,`Hypocalcaemia: **tetany, seizures, laryngospasm**, cardiomyopathy in infants.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Calcium, phosphate, **ALP (high)**, PTH (high), **25(OH)D (below 12 ng/mL deficient)**, creatinine.`,`Wrist/knee X-ray: **cupping, fraying, splaying**, widened growth plate; Thacher score.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`**Cholecalciferol 2000 IU daily for 12 weeks** or stoss 150,000 IU once (1-12 years); IAP: 60,000 IU weekly for 6 weeks.`,`**Calcium 500 mg/day**; then maintenance 400-600 IU daily.`,`Tetany: **10% calcium gluconate 1-2 mL/kg IV over 10 minutes**, then infusion.`,`Monitor calcium, phosphate, ALP at 4 weeks; X-ray and 25(OH)D at 3 months; orthopaedic referral for deformity persisting at 2 years.`]},{heading:`Hypophosphataemic rickets`,points:[`**XLH (PHEX)**: high FGF23, low phosphate, normal calcium and PTH, low TmP/GFR, low or normal calcitriol.`,`No tetany or myopathy; dental abscesses; family history; does not respond to vitamin D.`,`Treat with **oral phosphate 20-60 mg/kg/day in 4-6 doses plus calcitriol**, or **burosumab 0.8 mg/kg SC 2-weekly**; watch for nephrocalcinosis.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`**400 IU vitamin D daily for all infants** in the first year; maternal supplementation; sunlight; fortified foods; RBSK screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Biochemical table: calciopenic vs phosphopenic rickets (calcium, phosphate, ALP, PTH, 25(OH)D, 1,25(OH)2D)`,`Labelled sketch of a rachitic wrist X-ray (cupping, fraying, splaying)`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management of nutritional rickets`,marks:2},{part:`Hypophosphataemic rickets`,marks:2}],keywords:[`rickets`,`vitamin D`,`stoss therapy`,`XLH`,`FGF23`,`rachitic rosary`,`cupping fraying splaying`]},{id:`endocrine-metabolic-bone-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Paget disease of bone.`,openingLines:[`Paget disease is a focal disorder of bone remodelling in which overactive osteoclasts resorb bone and osteoblasts lay down enlarged, disorganised, weak woven bone.`,`It affects 1-2% of people over 55 in Western populations, is often asymptomatic and is suspected from an isolated raised ALP.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and pathology`,points:[`Age over 55, family history, **SQSTM1 (p62) mutation**; lytic, mixed and sclerotic phases.`,`Sites: pelvis, spine, skull, femur, tibia; monostotic or polyostotic.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and complications`,points:[`Bone pain, **warm bowed bone (sabre tibia)**, enlarging skull, **deafness**, secondary OA.`,`Fracture, spinal stenosis, cranial nerve palsy, high-output failure, **osteosarcoma under 1%**.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**High ALP with normal calcium, phosphate and GGT**.`,`X-ray: blade-of-grass lesion, cotton-wool skull, ivory vertebra; **bone scan** for extent.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`**Zoledronic acid 5 mg IV once** after vitamin D repletion; or risedronate 30 mg daily for 2 months.`,`Analgesics, orthopaedic surgery, hearing aids; treat-to-target ALP not recommended (2019 guideline).`]}],mustDraw:[`Labelled diagram of the three phases of Paget disease (lytic, mixed, sclerotic) with typical X-ray signs`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and complications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`Paget disease`,`osteitis deformans`,`ALP`,`zoledronic acid`,`SQSTM1`,`osteosarcoma`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on Paget disease and other dysplasias of bone, and on vitamin D and bone mineral metabolism`,`Munns CF et al. Global Consensus Recommendations on Prevention and Management of Nutritional Rickets, J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2016`,`Indian Academy of Pediatrics Guidelines on Prevention and Treatment of Vitamin D Deficiency and Rickets, Indian Pediatrics 2017`,`Demay MB et al. Vitamin D for the Prevention of Disease: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2024 (and Holick et al. Endocrine Society guideline on vitamin D deficiency, 2011)`,`Ralston SH et al. Diagnosis and Management of Paget's Disease of Bone in Adults: A Clinical Guideline, J Bone Miner Res 2019; Singer FR et al. Endocrine Society Paget guideline 2014`,`Haffner D et al. Clinical practice recommendations for the diagnosis and management of X-linked hypophosphataemia, Nat Rev Nephrol 2019`,`ICMR-NIN Nutrient Requirements for Indians (RDA and EAR), 2020; Comprehensive National Nutrition Survey 2016-18, MoHFW 2019`,`KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline Update for CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder, 2017`]}),c.push({id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism`,title:`Male hypogonadism and testosterone therapy`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Male hypogonadism** is a clinical syndrome of **androgen deficiency symptoms with an unequivocally and consistently low morning fasting testosterone** (below about **264-300 ng/dL** on two occasions), classified by LH/FSH into **primary (testicular, high gonadotrophins - e.g. Klinefelter)** and **secondary (hypothalamic-pituitary, low or normal gonadotrophins)**; testosterone replacement treats symptoms but **suppresses fertility** and needs **haematocrit and PSA monitoring** - TRAVERSE 2023 showed no excess major cardiovascular events.`,keywords:[`hypogonadism`,`androgen deficiency`,`testosterone replacement therapy`,`TRT`,`Klinefelter syndrome`,`47,XXY`,`hypogonadotropic hypogonadism`,`hypergonadotropic hypogonadism`,`late-onset hypogonadism`,`functional hypogonadism`,`SHBG`,`free testosterone`,`testosterone undecanoate`,`testosterone enanthate`,`hCG`,`TRAVERSE`,`erythrocytosis`,`anabolic steroid`,`micro-TESE`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hypogonadism** in men is failure of the testes to produce **physiological testosterone (androgen deficiency)** and/or a **normal number of sperm**, due to disruption at one or more levels of the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Diagnosis needs **both** consistent symptoms/signs **and** an unequivocally low **morning fasting total testosterone on two separate days** - a low level without symptoms, or symptoms with a normal level, is not hypogonadism. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Thresholds differ**: Endocrine Society uses the CDC-harmonised lower limit **264 ng/dL (9.2 nmol/L)**; AUA uses **300 ng/dL**; EAU and EMAS use **12 nmol/L (about 350 ng/dL)** total or **free testosterone below 225-243 pmol/L** - quote the guideline whose number you use. [EAU 2025]`,`The **European Male Ageing Study (EMAS)** found symptomatic **late-onset hypogonadism** (three sexual symptoms plus total testosterone below 11 nmol/L and free below 220 pmol/L) in only about **2% of men aged 40-79** - far lower than the number on testosterone. [EMAS, NEJM 2010]`,`Testosterone falls about **1% a year after 40** and free testosterone faster because **SHBG rises with age**; obesity and diabetes, not age itself, explain most low levels in middle-aged men. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)** occurs in about **1 in 500-1000 male births** and is the commonest chromosomal cause of primary hypogonadism and male infertility, yet most cases are never diagnosed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: hospital-based studies report low testosterone in roughly one-fifth to one-third of men with type 2 diabetes or obesity - largely **functional secondary hypogonadism**; national prevalence data are lacking. [Indian clinic series]`,`**Testosterone prescribing and gym anabolic steroid misuse** have risen sharply in Indian cities - steroid withdrawal is now a recognised cause of secondary hypogonadism in young men. [EAU 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Primary (testicular) - congenital**: **Klinefelter syndrome**, cryptorchidism, anorchia, Y-chromosome microdeletion (spermatogenesis only), myotonic dystrophy and defects of testosterone synthesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary - acquired**: **mumps orchitis** (post-pubertal), testicular torsion or trauma, **chemotherapy (alkylating agents) and radiotherapy**, orchidectomy, TB or leprosy orchitis, alcohol, ketoconazole, and HIV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary (hypothalamic-pituitary) - congenital**: **congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (CHH)** - with anosmia it is **Kallmann syndrome (ANOS1/KAL1, FGFR1)**; Prader-Willi syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary - organic acquired**: **pituitary adenoma and hyperprolactinaemia**, craniopharyngioma, pituitary surgery or radiotherapy, **haemochromatosis** (iron in gonadotrophs), infiltrative disease (sarcoid, histiocytosis, TB), head injury. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary - functional (reversible)**: **obesity, type 2 diabetes, obstructive sleep apnoea**, chronic opioids, **glucocorticoids**, anabolic steroid withdrawal, acute or chronic illness (CKD, cirrhosis, HIV, COPD), heavy exercise and undernutrition. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Combined primary and secondary**: ageing, alcohol, sickle cell disease, cirrhosis, CKD and glucocorticoids affect both testis and pituitary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Androgen resistance** (androgen insensitivity syndrome, 5-alpha reductase deficiency) gives high testosterone and high LH with under-virilisation - it is not testosterone deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Pulsatile hypothalamic GnRH** drives pituitary **LH**, which makes **Leydig cells** secrete testosterone, and **FSH**, which with high intratesticular testosterone drives **Sertoli cells** to support spermatogenesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Feedback**: testosterone (directly and after aromatisation to **oestradiol**) suppresses GnRH and LH; **inhibin B** from Sertoli cells suppresses FSH - so testicular failure raises FSH first. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intratesticular testosterone is about 100 times the serum level** - exogenous testosterone suppresses LH and FSH, collapses intratesticular testosterone and causes **azoospermia in most men within months**, the basis of its contraceptive effect and fertility warning. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Circulating testosterone is about **44% bound to SHBG** (tightly, inactive), about **50% to albumin** (loosely, bioavailable) and **2-3% free**; altered SHBG makes total testosterone misleading. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SHBG low** (total testosterone falsely low): obesity, type 2 diabetes, nephrotic syndrome, hypothyroidism, glucocorticoids, androgens; **SHBG high** (total falsely normal): ageing, HIV, cirrhosis, hyperthyroidism, anticonvulsants, oestrogens. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Testosterone is converted by **5-alpha reductase** to **DHT** (prostate, hair follicle, external genitalia) and by **aromatase** to oestradiol (bone, libido, fat) - so a high oestrogen-to-androgen ratio causes gynaecomastia in Klinefelter. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obesity hypogonadism**: aromatase in fat raises oestradiol, and leptin, insulin resistance and inflammation suppress GnRH - weight loss reverses it, which is why it is called **functional**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Testosterone stimulates erythropoiesis** by suppressing **hepcidin** (more iron available) and raising erythropoietin - explaining both correction of anaemia and **erythrocytosis**, the commonest adverse effect. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Primary hypogonadism (hypergonadotropic)**: low testosterone, **high LH and FSH**, small testes - testicular failure; fertility usually cannot be restored with hormones. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Secondary hypogonadism (hypogonadotropic)**: low testosterone with **low or inappropriately normal LH and FSH** - fertility **can be induced with gonadotrophins**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Compensated (subclinical) hypogonadism**: normal testosterone with **raised LH** - seen in ageing and early testicular damage; replacement is not routinely recommended. [EAU 2025]`,`**Organic vs functional**: organic disease (structural or genetic) is permanent and justifies testosterone; functional (obesity, illness, drugs) should first be treated by **removing the cause**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**By age of onset**: **pre-pubertal** onset gives eunuchoid proportions, infantile genitalia, high-pitched voice and absent beard; **post-pubertal** onset gives low libido, ED, fatigue and slow loss of body hair, with normal voice and skeleton. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late-onset hypogonadism (LOH)** is the EMAS/EAU term for age-associated, symptomatic low testosterone in men over 40, usually with comorbidity. [EAU 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Most specific symptoms**: **reduced libido**, **reduced spontaneous and morning erections**, erectile dysfunction, gynaecomastia, loss of body hair and reduced shaving, very small or shrinking testes, infertility, hot flushes and low-trauma fracture. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Less specific symptoms**: fatigue, low mood, poor concentration, sleep disturbance, reduced muscle bulk and strength, and increased body fat - these alone do not justify testing. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Adolescent**: **delayed puberty** (no testicular enlargement by 14 years), poor growth spurt or long limbs - see the Puberty topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**History for cause**: mumps after puberty, testicular surgery or torsion, chemotherapy or radiotherapy, **anosmia** (Kallmann), headaches and visual loss (pituitary), galactorrhoea, opioids, steroids, **gym 'supplements' or injections**, alcohol and snoring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fertility intention** must be asked before any treatment - it decides between testosterone and gonadotrophins. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Comorbidity screen**: diabetes, obesity, OSA, CKD, liver disease, HIV and TB, depression - and **prostate symptoms** (IPSS) before starting testosterone. [AUA 2018]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Testicular volume** with a **Prader orchidometer**: adult normal about **15-25 mL**; under 15 mL is reduced; **under 4 mL, small and firm suggests Klinefelter**; soft small testes suggest post-pubertal damage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eunuchoid proportions**: **arm span exceeds height by more than 5 cm** and lower segment exceeds upper - epiphyses close late without testosterone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gynaecomastia** (palpable glandular disc under the areola), sparse facial, axillary and pubic hair with a female pattern, fine skin and wrinkling around the eyes and mouth in long-standing deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smell test** (anosmia in Kallmann), **visual fields by confrontation** (bitemporal hemianopia with a pituitary tumour), galactorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues to cause**: bronze skin and hepatomegaly (**haemochromatosis**), cushingoid features, acromegaly, signs of liver disease, **acne and muscle bulk with testicular atrophy in a gym-goer** (anabolic steroids). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Before treatment**: digital rectal examination (men over 40 or at high risk), BMI, waist and blood pressure. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Depression**: low libido, fatigue and poor concentration - screen with PHQ-9; testosterone does not treat depression (TRAVERSE depressive substudy). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Vasculogenic or diabetic erectile dysfunction**: libido and morning testosterone are normal - treat with a PDE5 inhibitor and risk factors. [EAU 2025]`,`**Hypothyroidism and hyperprolactinaemia**: fatigue, low libido and ED - check TSH and prolactin; prolactin causes secondary hypogonadism that reverses with cabergoline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstructive sleep apnoea**: fatigue, low testosterone and erythrocytosis - treating OSA can raise testosterone, and testosterone can worsen untreated OSA. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Acute illness or non-fasting sample**: testosterone falls after meals and in illness - never diagnose during acute illness or from an afternoon sample. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Altered SHBG**: an obese diabetic man with total testosterone just below range may have a normal **free testosterone** - measure free testosterone before labelling him. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Total testosterone between 7 and 10 am (or within 3 hours of waking), fasting**, by a reliable assay; **repeat** to confirm because about 30% of low values are normal on retesting. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Free testosterone** by equilibrium dialysis or calculated from total testosterone, SHBG and albumin (**Vermeulen formula**) when total is near the lower limit or SHBG is altered. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**LH and FSH** in every confirmed case - **high means primary**, **low or normal means secondary**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Primary hypogonadism**: **karyotype** (Klinefelter, especially if testes are under 6 mL), semen analysis; Y-microdeletion testing in severe oligospermia or azoospermia. [EAU 2025]`,`**Secondary hypogonadism**: **serum prolactin**, **iron saturation and ferritin** (haemochromatosis), and assessment of other pituitary axes (morning cortisol, TSH with free T4). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Pituitary MRI** in secondary hypogonadism when **testosterone is under 150 ng/dL**, there is panhypopituitarism, persistent hyperprolactinaemia, or symptoms of a mass (headache, visual field loss). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Baseline before therapy**: **haematocrit**, **PSA** in men 55-69 (or 40-69 at high risk) after shared decision, lipids, HbA1c, and **DXA** if long-standing deficiency or low-trauma fracture. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Semen analysis** (two samples) when fertility matters - WHO 2021 lower reference: concentration 16 million/mL, total motility 42%. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Treat the cause first in functional hypogonadism**: weight loss (lifestyle, GLP-1 agonists or bariatric surgery), CPAP for OSA, withdraw opioids and steroids, and stop anabolic steroids - testosterone often normalises. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Testosterone therapy** is recommended for men with **organic hypogonadism** and symptomatic men with confirmed low testosterone after discussion of benefits and risks; in **men over 65 it is individualised** and mainly for sexual symptoms (ACP 2020). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Testosterone enanthate or cypionate** (available in India as 250 mg/mL ampoules): **100-200 mg IM every 1-2 weeks** (Indian practice often 250 mg every 2-3 weeks) - peaks and troughs cause mood swings and more erythrocytosis. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Testosterone undecanoate depot 1000 mg IM** (deep gluteal, slowly over 2 minutes): at **0 and 6 weeks, then every 10-14 weeks** - steady levels, fewer injections; **observe 30 minutes** for oil microembolism (POME) and anaphylaxis. [EAU 2025]`,`**Testosterone gel 1% (50-100 mg/day)** applied to shoulders or abdomen once daily - most physiological levels; risk of **transfer to women and children** (wash hands, cover the site). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Oral testosterone undecanoate 40 mg capsules**: 120-160 mg/day in two divided doses **with a fat-containing meal** - variable absorption; **avoid methyltestosterone** (hepatotoxic). [EAU 2025]`,`**Target**: testosterone in the **mid-normal range (about 400-700 ng/dL)** - measured midway between enanthate injections, **just before the next undecanoate injection**, 2-8 hours after gel application. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Contraindications**: **breast or prostate cancer**, palpable prostate nodule or **PSA over 4 ng/mL (over 3 in high-risk men)** without urology evaluation, **haematocrit over 48%** (over 50% at high altitude), untreated severe OSA, severe LUTS (IPSS over 19), uncontrolled heart failure, **MI or stroke in the last 6 months**, thrombophilia, and **wish for fertility soon**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Do not prescribe testosterone** for low testosterone without symptoms, for 'age-related' fatigue alone, for weight loss, or to men planning a pregnancy with their partner. [AUA 2018]`]},{heading:`Fertility and IPD / emergency care`,points:[`**Secondary hypogonadism wanting fertility**: **hCG 1500-2000 IU SC or IM two to three times weekly** for 6 months, adding **FSH or hMG 75-150 IU three times weekly** if still azoospermic; spermatogenesis takes **6-24 months**; pulsatile GnRH is an alternative. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Primary hypogonadism (including Klinefelter)**: gonadotrophins do not help; **micro-dissection TESE with ICSI** retrieves sperm in about **40-50% of Klinefelter men** - refer before starting testosterone. [EAU 2025]`,`**After anabolic steroid or testosterone use**: stop, and allow **6-12 months** for the axis to recover; **hCG or clomiphene (off-label)** may speed recovery under specialist care. [EAU 2025]`,`**Admission is rarely needed** - indications are pituitary apoplexy (sudden headache, visual loss), adrenal crisis in hypopituitarism, and severe testosterone complications (DVT/PE, stroke, haematocrit over 54% with symptoms). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypopituitarism**: replace **glucocorticoid before thyroxine and testosterone** - starting thyroxine first can precipitate adrenal crisis (see the Adrenal disorders topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**POME reaction** after undecanoate injection (cough, dyspnoea, throat tightening within minutes): stop the injection, give oxygen and supportive care; treat anaphylaxis with IM adrenaline 0.5 mg. [EAU 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Type 2 diabetes and obesity**: the **T4DM trial (2021)** showed testosterone plus lifestyle reduced progression to diabetes (**12% vs 21% at 2 years**) in men with low-normal testosterone - but weight loss remains first line. [T4DM, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2021]`,`**Elderly**: TRAVERSE (mean age 63) showed modest benefit in sexual activity and anaemia but **more fractures, atrial fibrillation and pulmonary embolism**; use for sexual symptoms only and stop if no benefit at 12 months (ACP). [ACP 2020]`,`**Adolescent boys with hypogonadism**: induce puberty with **low-dose testosterone enanthate 50 mg IM monthly**, rising by 25-50 mg every 6 months over 2-3 years - see the Puberty topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV with weight loss**: short-term testosterone can improve lean mass; SHBG is high, so measure free testosterone. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Opioid users** (including methadone and buprenorphine maintenance): screen for hypogonadism; testosterone or opioid reduction is appropriate. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**CKD and cirrhosis**: low testosterone is common; replacement is not routinely recommended because benefits are unproven. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Prostate**: treated low-risk prostate cancer with undetectable PSA - testosterone may be considered with urology after shared decision (AUA); untreated cancer remains a contraindication. [AUA 2018]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Untreated hypogonadism**: **osteoporosis and fractures**, anaemia, sarcopenia, increased fat mass, insulin resistance, sexual dysfunction and reduced quality of life. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Erythrocytosis** (haematocrit over 54%) is the **commonest adverse effect** - highest with short-acting IM injections and in older men; it raises the risk of thrombosis. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Other adverse effects**: acne, oily skin, gynaecomastia, fluid retention, worsening OSA, testicular atrophy, **infertility**, raised PSA and LUTS, and rises in blood pressure. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**TRAVERSE safety signals**: more **atrial fibrillation, acute kidney injury and pulmonary embolism** and, in the fracture substudy, **more clinical fractures** than placebo. [TRAVERSE, NEJM 2023-2024]`,`**Klinefelter**: increased risk of **breast cancer**, mediastinal germ cell tumours, osteoporosis, type 2 diabetes, venous thromboembolism, autoimmune disease and learning and psychosocial difficulties - life expectancy is a few years shorter. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anabolic steroid abuse**: cardiomyopathy, dyslipidaemia, liver tumours (oral 17-alpha-alkylated agents), aggression, prolonged hypogonadism and dependence. [EAU 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Review at 3-6 months, then every 12 months**: symptoms, adverse effects and adherence. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Testosterone level** at 3-6 months, aiming for mid-normal, with dose or interval adjustment. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Haematocrit** at baseline, **3-6 months**, then yearly; if **over 54%**, stop therapy until it falls, look for hypoxia and OSA, and restart at a lower dose (or switch to gel). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**PSA and DRE** (men 55-69, or 40-69 at high risk) at baseline and **3-12 months**, then per screening practice; **urology referral** if PSA rises **more than 1.4 ng/mL in 12 months**, exceeds **4 ng/mL**, or DRE is abnormal, or IPSS exceeds 19. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**DXA** at 1-2 years in men with osteoporosis or fracture. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Refer to endocrinology**: secondary hypogonadism (pituitary work-up), suspected Klinefelter or Kallmann, delayed puberty, fertility wish, and testosterone under 150 ng/dL. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Weight loss, exercise, sleep and alcohol reduction** are the first prescription in functional hypogonadism - a 10% weight loss can raise testosterone meaningfully. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Warn gym-goers** that anabolic steroids and 'testosterone boosters' cause testicular atrophy, infertility and prolonged hypogonadism. [EAU 2025]`,`**Mumps vaccination** (MMR under the Universal Immunisation Programme in some states) prevents mumps orchitis; **orchidopexy by 12-18 months** for undescended testis protects later function. [EAU Paediatric Urology 2024]`,`**Sperm banking** before chemotherapy or pelvic radiotherapy in adolescents and men. [EAU 2025]`,`**NP-NCD screening** for diabetes and obesity at Ayushman Arogya Mandir identifies men at risk of functional hypogonadism. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Counsel on transfer** of testosterone gel to partners and children, and that testosterone is **not a contraceptive to rely on** without semen analysis. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**TRAVERSE (NEJM 2023)**: 5246 men aged 45-80 with or at high risk of cardiovascular disease and testosterone under 300 ng/dL; transdermal testosterone was **non-inferior to placebo for major adverse cardiac events (7.0% vs 7.3%)**, with more AF, AKI and PE. [TRAVERSE 2023]`,`**TRAVERSE substudies (2023-2024)**: no increase in high-grade prostate cancer; modest improvement in sexual activity; correction of anaemia; **no improvement in depressive symptoms**; **increased clinical fractures**. [TRAVERSE 2024]`,`**FDA class labelling change (2025)**: added TRAVERSE results, removed the earlier language suggesting increased cardiovascular risk, kept the limitation against use for age-related low testosterone alone, and added a **blood pressure warning for all products**. [FDA 2025]`,`**AUA testosterone deficiency guideline (amended 2024)** incorporates TRAVERSE in counselling on cardiovascular risk; its 300 ng/dL threshold is unchanged. [AUA 2024]`,`**EAU Sexual and Reproductive Health guideline (2025)** keeps the **12 nmol/L total / 225 pmol/L free** thresholds and emphasises treating functional causes and protecting fertility. [EAU 2025]`,`**WHO semen manual 6th edition (2021)** reference limits are now used for infertility work-up. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: **'Approach to a man with low testosterone'** - two morning samples, LH/FSH split, cause-specific tests, treatment and monitoring. [Exam pattern]`,`**Viva**: 'Small firm testes, gynaecomastia, tall and infertile' - **Klinefelter, 47,XXY**; confirm with **karyotype**, FSH very high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Delayed puberty with anosmia' - **Kallmann syndrome**: low GnRH, low LH/FSH, absent or hypoplastic olfactory bulbs on MRI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving testosterone to a man who wants children - it causes azoospermia; give **hCG** for secondary hypogonadism. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing on an afternoon, non-fasting or single sample, or during acute illness. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Common mistake**: not measuring LH/FSH, prolactin and iron studies before replacing - a prolactinoma or haemochromatosis is then missed. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Monitoring triad to quote: **testosterone level, haematocrit, PSA** at 3-6 months then yearly. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Testosterone and fertility drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Testosterone enanthate/cypionate`,`100-200 mg IM every 1-2 weeks (India: 250 mg every 2-3 weeks)`,`Long term; trough or mid-interval level at 3-6 months`,`Peaks and troughs, erythrocytosis (highest), acne, mood swings`],[`Testosterone undecanoate depot`,`1000 mg deep IM over 2 minutes`,`0 and 6 weeks, then every 10-14 weeks; pre-dose level`,`POME and anaphylaxis - observe 30 min; erythrocytosis`],[`Testosterone gel 1%`,`50-100 mg (5-10 g gel) transdermal once daily`,`Level 2-8 h after application at 2-4 weeks`,`Skin irritation, transfer to women and children`],[`Oral testosterone undecanoate`,`40 mg capsules, 120-160 mg/day in 2 doses with fatty meal`,`Level 3-5 h after dose`,`Variable absorption, raised BP; avoid methyltestosterone`],[`Human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG)`,`1500-2000 IU SC/IM two to three times weekly`,`6 months, then add FSH if azoospermic; total 12-24 months`,`Gynaecomastia, acne; for secondary hypogonadism only`],[`FSH (recombinant) or hMG`,`75-150 IU SC three times weekly`,`Added to hCG for 6-18 months`,`Cost; ineffective in primary hypogonadism`],[`Cabergoline (prolactinoma)`,`0.25-0.5 mg oral twice weekly`,`Titrate to prolactin`,`Nausea, postural hypotension, valvulopathy at high dose`],[`Clomiphene citrate (off-label)`,`25 mg oral daily or 50 mg alternate days`,`Specialist use in functional secondary hypogonadism`,`Visual symptoms, mood change; not licensed for men`]]},{heading:`Classification of male hypogonadism`,columns:[`Type`,`Testosterone`,`LH / FSH`,`Examples`,`Fertility treatable?`],rows:[[`Primary (hypergonadotropic)`,`Low`,`High / high`,`Klinefelter, mumps orchitis, chemotherapy, cryptorchidism, torsion`,`No - micro-TESE with ICSI only`],[`Secondary - organic`,`Low`,`Low or normal`,`Kallmann/CHH, pituitary tumour, prolactinoma, haemochromatosis, radiotherapy`,`Yes - hCG plus FSH`],[`Secondary - functional`,`Low or low-normal`,`Low or normal`,`Obesity, T2DM, OSA, opioids, steroids, illness, anabolic withdrawal`,`Usually reverses with cause`],[`Compensated`,`Normal`,`High LH`,`Ageing, early testicular damage`,`Variable`],[`Androgen resistance`,`Normal or high`,`High LH`,`Androgen insensitivity, 5-alpha reductase deficiency`,`Limited`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of low libido and erectile dysfunction`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Organic hypogonadism`,`Small testes, loss of body hair, low morning erections`,`Two morning testosterone, LH/FSH`],[`Functional hypogonadism`,`Obesity, diabetes, OSA, opioids`,`Low-normal total testosterone, low SHBG, normal free testosterone often`],[`Hyperprolactinaemia`,`Galactorrhoea, headache, visual loss`,`Prolactin, pituitary MRI`],[`Vasculogenic ED`,`Normal libido, vascular risk factors`,`Normal testosterone`],[`Depression`,`Anhedonia, sleep and appetite change`,`PHQ-9`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Cold intolerance, weight gain`,`TSH`],[`Drug-induced`,`SSRIs, beta-blockers, spironolactone, finasteride`,`Drug history`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / interpretation`,`When`],rows:[[`Total testosterone (7-10 am, fasting, x2)`,`Below 264 ng/dL (ES), 300 (AUA), 12 nmol/L / 350 ng/dL (EAU)`,`All suspected cases`],[`Free testosterone (calculated or dialysis)`,`Below about 225 pmol/L (6.5 ng/dL)`,`Borderline total or altered SHBG`],[`LH and FSH`,`High = primary; low/normal = secondary`,`Every confirmed low testosterone`],[`Prolactin, ferritin and transferrin saturation`,`Raised prolactin; saturation over 45%`,`Secondary hypogonadism`],[`Pituitary MRI`,`Indicated if testosterone under 150 ng/dL, panhypopituitarism, mass symptoms`,`Secondary hypogonadism`],[`Karyotype`,`47,XXY or mosaic`,`Primary, testes under 6 mL, azoospermia`],[`Haematocrit`,`Do not start if over 48%; stop if over 54% on therapy`,`Baseline, 3-6 months, yearly`],[`PSA`,`Refer if over 4 ng/mL or rise over 1.4 ng/mL in 12 months`,`Men 55-69 (40-69 high risk)`],[`Semen analysis`,`Below 16 million/mL (WHO 2021)`,`Fertility wish`]]}],redFlags:[`Secondary hypogonadism with headache, visual field loss or very low testosterone (under 150 ng/dL) - pituitary tumour`,`Sudden severe headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia - pituitary apoplexy`,`Haematocrit over 54% on testosterone, or symptoms of thrombosis (DVT, PE, stroke)`,`PSA over 4 ng/mL, rise over 1.4 ng/mL in a year, or prostate nodule on testosterone`,`Testicular mass in a man being evaluated for hypogonadism (germ cell tumour)`,`Breast lump that is hard, eccentric or fixed in a man with Klinefelter syndrome (breast cancer)`,`No testicular enlargement by 14 years, or anosmia with absent puberty`],pearls:[`**Two morning fasting samples** - never diagnose on one, in the afternoon or during illness.`,`**LH and FSH split the diagnosis**: high = primary (testis), low/normal = secondary (brain).`,`**Testosterone is a contraceptive**: it causes azoospermia - fertility wish means hCG, not testosterone.`,`**Small firm testes + gynaecomastia + tall = Klinefelter (47,XXY)** - the commonest cause of primary hypogonadism.`,`**Monitoring triad**: testosterone level, haematocrit, PSA at 3-6 months then yearly.`,`**Haematocrit over 54% - stop**; over 48% at baseline - do not start.`,`**TRAVERSE 2023**: no excess MACE, but more AF, AKI, PE and fractures.`,`**Obesity hypogonadism is functional** - fix weight, sleep apnoea and opioids before reaching for testosterone.`,`In secondary hypogonadism, always check **prolactin and ferritin**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-q1`,stem:`A 23-year-old tall man presents with infertility. He has bilateral gynaecomastia and firm testes of 3 mL each. Arm span exceeds height by 7 cm. Testosterone is 210 ng/dL, LH 28 IU/L and FSH 42 IU/L. The most useful confirmatory test is:`,options:[`Pituitary MRI`,`Serum prolactin`,`Peripheral blood karyotype`,`GnRH stimulation test`],answer:2,explanation:`Small firm testes, gynaecomastia, eunuchoid proportions and infertility with low testosterone and very high LH and FSH indicate primary testicular failure typical of Klinefelter syndrome, confirmed by a 47,XXY karyotype. Pituitary MRI and prolactin are for secondary (low gonadotrophin) hypogonadism, and a GnRH stimulation test adds nothing when gonadotrophins are already high.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old man with idiopathic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism wishes to father a child within the next year. Which treatment is most appropriate?`,options:[`Testosterone undecanoate 1000 mg IM every 12 weeks`,`hCG 1500-2000 IU two to three times weekly, adding FSH if still azoospermic`,`Testosterone gel 50 mg daily with clomiphene`,`Micro-dissection testicular sperm extraction as first step`],answer:1,explanation:`In secondary hypogonadism the testes are normal but unstimulated, so hCG (acting like LH) raises intratesticular testosterone and FSH is added if sperm do not appear after about 6 months. Exogenous testosterone in any form suppresses LH and FSH and causes azoospermia, so it is wrong for a man seeking fertility. Micro-TESE is reserved for primary testicular failure such as Klinefelter syndrome.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-q3`,stem:`A 56-year-old man on testosterone enanthate injections for 6 months has a haematocrit of 56%. The best next step is:`,options:[`Continue therapy and repeat haematocrit in 12 months`,`Start aspirin and continue the same dose`,`Withhold testosterone, assess for hypoxia and sleep apnoea, and restart at a lower dose once haematocrit is safe`,`Switch to a double dose of oral testosterone undecanoate`],answer:2,explanation:`Erythrocytosis is the commonest adverse effect of testosterone and a haematocrit above 54% requires stopping therapy until it falls, evaluating for hypoxia and obstructive sleep apnoea, and restarting at a reduced dose or with a gel, which causes less erythrocytosis than short-acting injections. Continuing unchanged or adding aspirin leaves the thrombotic risk, and doubling any formulation would worsen it.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-q4`,stem:`Regarding the TRAVERSE trial (NEJM 2023) of transdermal testosterone in men aged 45-80 with hypogonadism and high cardiovascular risk, which statement is correct?`,options:[`Testosterone was non-inferior to placebo for major adverse cardiac events, but atrial fibrillation and pulmonary embolism were more frequent`,`Testosterone doubled the rate of myocardial infarction`,`Testosterone significantly reduced major adverse cardiac events`,`Testosterone increased high-grade prostate cancer`],answer:0,explanation:`TRAVERSE randomised 5246 men and found MACE in 7.0% with testosterone and 7.3% with placebo, meeting non-inferiority, so it neither increased nor reduced events. Atrial fibrillation, acute kidney injury and pulmonary embolism were more frequent, and a later substudy showed more fractures. The prostate substudy found no increase in high-grade prostate cancer.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-q5`,stem:`A 48-year-old man with BMI 34 kg/m2 and type 2 diabetes complains of fatigue. An afternoon total testosterone is 270 ng/dL. What should be done next?`,options:[`Start testosterone undecanoate injections`,`Order a pituitary MRI`,`Order a karyotype`,`Repeat a fasting morning total testosterone with SHBG to calculate free testosterone`],answer:3,explanation:`An afternoon single value cannot diagnose hypogonadism, and obesity and diabetes lower SHBG, making total testosterone falsely low. Guidelines require two morning fasting values and free testosterone when SHBG is altered, and fatigue alone is a non-specific symptom. Starting testosterone is premature, MRI is reserved for confirmed secondary hypogonadism with very low levels or mass features, and karyotype is for primary hypogonadism.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define male hypogonadism. Classify its causes. Outline the evaluation of a man with suspected hypogonadism and discuss testosterone replacement therapy and its monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Male hypogonadism is a clinical syndrome of androgen deficiency and/or impaired spermatogenesis, diagnosed by consistent symptoms with an unequivocally low morning fasting testosterone on two occasions.`,`It is classified by gonadotrophins into primary (testicular, high LH/FSH) and secondary (hypothalamic-pituitary, low or normal LH/FSH).`],answer:[{heading:`Physiology in one line`,points:[`GnRH - LH (Leydig, testosterone) and FSH (Sertoli, spermatogenesis); negative feedback by testosterone, oestradiol and inhibin B.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Primary**: Klinefelter, cryptorchidism, mumps orchitis, chemoradiation, torsion, trauma.`,`**Secondary organic**: Kallmann, pituitary tumour, hyperprolactinaemia, haemochromatosis.`,`**Secondary functional**: obesity, T2DM, OSA, opioids, glucocorticoids, anabolic steroid withdrawal.`,`**Combined**: ageing, alcohol, cirrhosis, CKD.`]},{heading:`Clinical evaluation`,points:[`Specific symptoms: low libido, reduced morning erections, gynaecomastia, loss of body hair, infertility, fractures.`,`Examination: testicular volume (orchidometer), eunuchoid proportions, gynaecomastia, smell, visual fields.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Two fasting 7-10 am total testosterone** (below 264-300 ng/dL); free testosterone if SHBG altered.`,`**LH/FSH**: high = primary - karyotype, semen analysis; low/normal = secondary - prolactin, iron studies, other pituitary axes, MRI if under 150 ng/dL.`,`Baseline haematocrit, PSA, DXA.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Functional: weight loss, CPAP, stop opioids/steroids.`,`Testosterone: **enanthate 100-200 mg IM 1-2 weekly**, **undecanoate 1000 mg IM at 0, 6 weeks then 10-14 weekly**, or **gel 50-100 mg daily**.`,`Fertility wish: **hCG plus FSH** (secondary); micro-TESE with ICSI (primary).`,`Contraindications: prostate/breast cancer, PSA over 4, haematocrit over 48%, untreated OSA, recent MI/stroke, fertility wish.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`At 3-6 months then yearly: **testosterone (mid-normal), haematocrit (stop if over 54%), PSA/DRE**, symptoms; DXA at 1-2 years.`,`TRAVERSE 2023: no excess MACE; more AF, PE, AKI, fractures.`]}],mustDraw:[`Hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis with feedback loops`,`Flow chart: low testosterone - repeat - LH/FSH - primary vs secondary work-up`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Testosterone therapy and fertility`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring and safety`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hypogonadism`,`testosterone`,`LH`,`FSH`,`Klinefelter`,`haematocrit`,`PSA`,`TRAVERSE`,`hCG`]},{id:`endocrine-male-hypogonadism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate primary from secondary male hypogonadism.`,openingLines:[`Primary hypogonadism is testicular failure with high LH and FSH; secondary hypogonadism is hypothalamic or pituitary failure with low or inappropriately normal LH and FSH.`,`The distinction decides the work-up and whether fertility can be restored.`],answer:[{heading:`Primary (hypergonadotropic)`,points:[`Causes: Klinefelter, mumps orchitis, cryptorchidism, chemoradiation, torsion.`,`LH/FSH high; testes small (firm in Klinefelter); gynaecomastia common.`,`Work-up: karyotype, semen analysis.`,`Fertility: not restored by hormones; micro-TESE with ICSI.`]},{heading:`Secondary (hypogonadotropic)`,points:[`Causes: Kallmann, pituitary tumour, prolactinoma, haemochromatosis, obesity, opioids, steroids.`,`LH/FSH low or normal; anosmia, visual field defects, other pituitary deficits.`,`Work-up: prolactin, ferritin, pituitary axes, MRI.`,`Fertility: **restorable with hCG plus FSH** or pulsatile GnRH.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: primary vs secondary hypogonadism`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Causes and features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and fertility treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`primary hypogonadism`,`secondary hypogonadism`,`hypergonadotropic`,`hypogonadotropic`,`Kallmann`,`Klinefelter`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the Testes and Male Reproductive System`,`Bhasin S et al. Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2018`,`Lincoff AM et al. Cardiovascular Safety of Testosterone-Replacement Therapy (TRAVERSE), N Engl J Med 2023; and TRAVERSE fracture, prostate and sexual function substudies 2023-2024`,`Mulhall JP et al. Evaluation and Management of Testosterone Deficiency: AUA Guideline 2018 (amended 2024)`,`European Association of Urology Guidelines on Sexual and Reproductive Health, 2025`,`Qaseem A et al. Testosterone Treatment in Adult Men With Age-Related Low Testosterone: American College of Physicians Guideline, 2020`,`Wittert G et al. Testosterone treatment to prevent or revert type 2 diabetes in men (T4DM), Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2021`,`WHO Laboratory Manual for the Examination and Processing of Human Semen, 6th edition, 2021`]}),c.push({id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia`,title:`Gynaecomastia`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Gynaecomastia** is **benign proliferation of glandular breast tissue in a male**, felt as a **rubbery, concentric subareolar disc**, caused by an **imbalance of oestrogen over androgen action**; it is **physiological in neonates, at puberty and in old age**, and otherwise due to **drugs, liver or renal disease, hypogonadism, hyperthyroidism or hormone-secreting tumours (hCG, testicular, adrenal)** - assess with **testosterone, oestradiol, LH, FSH and hCG**, treat the cause, and use **tamoxifen** for recent painful disease or **surgery** for long-standing fibrotic tissue.`,keywords:[`gynaecomastia`,`gynecomastia`,`male breast enlargement`,`pseudogynaecomastia`,`lipomastia`,`pubertal gynaecomastia`,`spironolactone`,`tamoxifen`,`raloxifene`,`hCG`,`oestradiol`,`Leydig cell tumour`,`Klinefelter syndrome`,`male breast cancer`,`Simon classification`,`subcutaneous mastectomy`,`liposuction`,`refeeding gynaecomastia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Gynaecomastia** is benign enlargement of the male breast from **proliferation of ductal epithelium and periductal stroma**, palpable as a **firm or rubbery disc at least 0.5-2 cm** extending concentrically from beneath the nipple. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudogynaecomastia (lipomastia)** is fat without glandular tissue, seen in obesity - soft, without a subareolar disc, and needs no endocrine work-up. [EAA 2019]`,`**Trimodal age distribution**: **neonatal** (60-90% of newborns, from maternal oestrogen; resolves in weeks), **pubertal** (up to about 50-60% of boys, peak at 13-14 years), and **older men** (about 25-65% over 50, rising with age, adiposity and drugs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pubertal gynaecomastia resolves within 6 months to 2 years in about 90%** - persistence beyond 17 years or after full puberty is uncommon and needs evaluation. [EAA 2019]`,`**Approximate causes in adults presenting for evaluation**: persistent pubertal or idiopathic about 50%, **drugs 10-25%**, cirrhosis or malnutrition about 8%, primary hypogonadism about 8%, testicular tumours about 3%, secondary hypogonadism about 2%, hyperthyroidism about 1.5%, CKD about 1%. [Braunstein NEJM 2007]`,`**India**: no national prevalence survey; clinic series show many adolescents seeking care for cosmetic distress, and **drug-induced** cases from **spironolactone, anti-TB drugs (isoniazid, ethionamide), efavirenz and anabolic steroids** are common in practice. [Indian clinic series]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Physiological**: neonatal (maternal and placental oestrogens), **pubertal** (oestradiol rises earlier than testosterone), ageing (fall in testosterone, more fat aromatase, higher SHBG). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Increased oestrogen production**: **hCG-secreting tumours** (testicular germ cell, extragonadal - lung, liver, stomach), **Leydig or Sertoli cell tumours**, **feminising adrenal carcinoma**, aromatase excess syndrome, and **obesity** (fat aromatase). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Decreased androgen**: **primary hypogonadism (Klinefelter**, orchitis, castration) and **secondary hypogonadism** (pituitary, prolactinoma). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Altered metabolism or binding**: **cirrhosis** (more androstenedione aromatised, SHBG rises, spironolactone use), **hyperthyroidism** (raised SHBG and aromatase), **CKD and dialysis** (Leydig dysfunction), and **refeeding after starvation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Androgen resistance**: androgen insensitivity syndrome (partial), 5-alpha reductase deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs - anti-androgenic**: **spironolactone**, bicalutamide, flutamide, cyproterone, **GnRH agonists**, **finasteride and dutasteride**, ketoconazole, cimetidine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs - oestrogenic or hormonal**: oestrogens (including transfer from a partner's cream), **hCG**, **anabolic steroids and testosterone** (aromatised), digoxin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs - other or uncertain mechanism**: **isoniazid, ethionamide**, **efavirenz**, metronidazole, **PPIs (omeprazole)**, calcium channel blockers, ACE inhibitors, methyldopa, tricyclics, **antipsychotics (via prolactin)**, alcohol, marijuana, opioids, and lavender or tea-tree oil products. [Braunstein NEJM 2007]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Male breast tissue has **oestrogen receptors (stimulate)** and **androgen receptors (inhibit)** - gynaecomastia results from a **rise in the oestrogen-to-androgen ratio** or in local oestrogen sensitivity, whatever the absolute levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oestrogen in men** comes about 15-20% directly from the testes and **80% from peripheral aromatisation** of testosterone and androstenedione in fat, muscle, skin and liver - so obesity and liver disease raise it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**hCG** acts on Leydig LH receptors and **stimulates aromatase**, raising oestradiol more than testosterone - the reason any adult with unexplained gynaecomastia needs an hCG level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SHBG binds testosterone more avidly than oestradiol** - when SHBG rises (cirrhosis, hyperthyroidism, ageing) free testosterone falls more than free oestradiol and the ratio shifts toward oestrogen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spironolactone** blocks the androgen receptor, displaces testosterone from SHBG and increases aromatisation - it causes gynaecomastia in up to about **10% at low doses and over 50% at high doses** used in cirrhosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Histological phases**: the **florid (proliferative) phase** in the first months has epithelial hyperplasia, oedema and inflammation - tender and drug-responsive; by **about 12 months** it becomes **fibrotic** with hyalinised stroma - painless and unresponsive to drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refeeding gynaecomastia**: starvation suppresses gonadotrophins; refeeding restarts the axis like a 'second puberty' with a transient oestrogen excess - it resolves spontaneously. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Simon classification (surgical)**: **grade I** small enlargement without skin excess; **grade IIa** moderate enlargement without skin excess; **grade IIb** moderate enlargement with skin excess; **grade III** marked enlargement with skin excess (ptosis, a pendulous female-like breast). [Simon 1973]`,`**Aetiological**: physiological (neonatal, pubertal, senescent), pathological (endocrine, systemic disease, tumours), **drug-induced**, and idiopathic. [EAA 2019]`,`**By hormonal mechanism**: oestrogen excess, androgen deficiency, androgen resistance, and altered oestrogen-androgen ratio. [Harrison 22e]`,`**By duration**: **under 12 months (florid, potentially reversible)** and **over 12 months (fibrotic, needs surgery)** - the most useful clinical split for treatment. [EAA 2019]`,`**Laterality**: usually **bilateral** but often asymmetric; **unilateral** gynaecomastia occurs (about one-third) and still needs breast cancer exclusion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Duration and tempo**: recent, rapidly progressive, painful enlargement suggests an active cause (drug, tumour); long-standing, painless enlargement suggests persistent pubertal or fibrotic disease. [EAA 2019]`,`**Breast tenderness** is common in the first 6-12 months; nipple discharge (especially **bloody**) is **not** a feature of gynaecomastia and suggests cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Detailed drug history**: prescribed, over-the-counter, herbal, **gym supplements and anabolic steroids**, **topical oestrogen use by a partner**, alcohol and recreational drugs. [EAA 2019]`,`**Symptoms of hypogonadism**: reduced libido, erectile dysfunction, infertility, reduced shaving - suggest testicular failure or a tumour suppressing LH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic disease**: jaundice and alcohol (cirrhosis), weight loss and tremor (**hyperthyroidism**), uraemic symptoms (CKD), cough or haemoptysis (hCG-secreting lung cancer), recent recovery from starvation or illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Testicular symptoms**: swelling, heaviness or a lump - a testicular tumour can present with gynaecomastia before a palpable mass. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychosocial impact**: adolescents often avoid swimming, sport and changing rooms; ask about teasing, low mood and body image. [EAA 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Technique**: patient supine with hands behind the head; bring thumb and forefinger together from the periphery toward the nipple - a **rubbery disc concentric to the areola** is gynaecomastia; fingers meeting without resistance is fat (pseudogynaecomastia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measure** the glandular disc diameter in centimetres on each side and record Simon grade - for follow-up and surgical planning. [EAA 2019]`,`**Features of breast cancer**: **hard, eccentric, irregular mass fixed to skin or chest wall**, nipple retraction or bloody discharge, skin ulceration or peau d'orange, and **axillary lymphadenopathy**. [ACR 2021]`,`**Testicular examination is mandatory**: a **mass** suggests tumour; **small firm testes** suggest Klinefelter; small soft testes suggest acquired failure; asymmetry needs ultrasound. [EAA 2019]`,`**Virilisation and Tanner stage**: in adolescents, confirm normal pubertal progression; eunuchoid proportions suggest hypogonadism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic signs**: stigmata of **chronic liver disease** (spider naevi, palmar erythema, ascites), thyroid signs (goitre, tremor, tachycardia), uraemia, cachexia, and abdominal mass (adrenal carcinoma). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pseudogynaecomastia (lipomastia)**: obese, soft, no subareolar disc, bilateral; treat with weight loss. [EAA 2019]`,`**Male breast cancer**: under 1% of all breast cancers; **older men, BRCA2 carriers, Klinefelter, prior chest radiation**; hard eccentric mass, skin or nipple changes - mammography or ultrasound and core biopsy. [ACR 2021]`,`**Lipoma, epidermoid cyst, dermoid**: discrete, not concentric with the nipple, soft or cystic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breast abscess or mastitis**: acute, red, hot, fluctuant, febrile - often in diabetics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurofibroma or lymphangioma** and chest wall lesions: consider in syndromic patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastasis** to the breast (lymphoma, prostate cancer on hormone therapy) - rare but suspected with systemic features. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pubertal boy with a normal examination** (normal testes, normal puberty, no drugs): **no tests needed** - reassure and review in 6 months. [EAA 2019]`,`**Adults with recent or unexplained gynaecomastia** after excluding drugs and systemic disease: **total testosterone, oestradiol, LH, FSH, SHBG, beta-hCG**, plus **TSH, liver function and creatinine**; **prolactin** only if hypogonadism or galactorrhoea. [EAA 2019]`,`**High hCG**: **testicular ultrasound** - if negative, look for extragonadal germ cell or hCG-secreting non-trophoblastic tumour (**chest X-ray/CT** for lung, abdominal CT). [Harrison 22e]`,`**High oestradiol with low or normal LH**: **testicular ultrasound** (Leydig or Sertoli tumour, may be impalpable); if normal, **DHEAS and adrenal CT** for a feminising adrenal tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low testosterone with high LH**: primary hypogonadism - **karyotype**; **low testosterone with low or normal LH**: secondary hypogonadism - prolactin and pituitary assessment. [EAA 2019]`,`**High testosterone with high LH**: **androgen resistance** or **hyperthyroidism** - check TSH and free T4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breast imaging** only when clinically suspicious for cancer: **ultrasound first under 25 years**, **mammography first at 25 or over**; BI-RADS 4-5 lesions need **core biopsy**. [ACR 2021]`,`**Normal all tests** = idiopathic gynaecomastia - common in older men; recheck in 6 months. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Pubertal gynaecomastia**: **reassurance and observation** for up to 2 years; most regress spontaneously; address psychological distress and weight. [EAA 2019]`,`**Stop or substitute the offending drug**: spironolactone - **eplerenone** in heart failure or **amiloride** in cirrhosis; cimetidine - another acid suppressant; efavirenz - a **dolutegravir-based regimen (TLD)**; regression is usually seen within about **1 month** if the tissue is not fibrotic. [EAA 2019]`,`**Treat the cause**: hypogonadism with testosterone (may initially worsen because of aromatisation), hyperthyroidism with antithyroid drugs, prolactinoma with cabergoline, tumours with surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tamoxifen 10-20 mg orally once daily for 3-6 months** (off-label) for **painful or recent (under 12 months)** gynaecomastia: partial or complete regression in most patients; pain often settles within a month. [EAA 2019]`,`**Raloxifene 60 mg daily** is an alternative SERM, used mainly in pubertal boys in small studies. [EAA 2019]`,`**Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole) are not effective** for established gynaecomastia - an RCT in pubertal boys showed no benefit over placebo. [EAA 2019]`,`**Tamoxifen adverse effects**: hot flushes, gastrointestinal upset, reduced libido, and rarely venous thromboembolism - avoid in men with thrombosis history. [EAA 2019]`,`**Testosterone, danazol and DHT** are not recommended for eugonadal men with gynaecomastia. [EAA 2019]`]},{heading:`Surgery, IPD and procedures`,points:[`**Indications for surgery**: **fibrotic gynaecomastia (over 12 months)**, failed medical therapy, persistent pubertal gynaecomastia **after about 17-18 years** or 2 years of observation, marked size (Simon IIb-III), and significant psychosocial distress. [EAA 2019]`,`**Subcutaneous mastectomy through a periareolar (Webster) incision** removes glandular tissue; **liposuction** treats the fatty component; combined techniques give the best contour. [EAA 2019]`,`**Skin excision** (with nipple repositioning) is needed for Simon grade IIb-III with redundant skin. [Simon 1973]`,`**Send excised tissue for histopathology** - occasional occult carcinoma or atypia is found. [EAA 2019]`,`**Surgical complications**: haematoma, seroma, nipple necrosis or numbness, contour irregularity (saucer deformity) and asymmetry. [EAA 2019]`,`**Admission** is needed only for surgery or for the cause (testicular tumour, adrenal carcinoma, decompensated cirrhosis); gynaecomastia itself is never an emergency. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Prostate cancer on anti-androgen monotherapy (bicalutamide)**: up to about **70%** develop gynaecomastia; **prophylactic tamoxifen 10-20 mg daily** or a single low-dose **breast bud radiotherapy** reduces it - tamoxifen is more effective. [EAU Prostate 2024]`,`**Cirrhosis**: gynaecomastia results from the disease and spironolactone; switching to amiloride or eplerenone often helps painful cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: gynaecomastia with **efavirenz** (true glandular), versus **lipohypertrophy** from older antiretrovirals - switching to dolutegravir-based therapy per NACO is effective. [NACO 2021]`,`**Tuberculosis**: **isoniazid and ethionamide** cause gynaecomastia (MDR regimens especially); continue treatment and reassure - it regresses after completion. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Dialysis and refeeding**: gynaecomastia is usually transient as the axis recovers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Klinefelter syndrome**: gynaecomastia is common and breast cancer risk is **about 20-50 times** that of other men - examine breasts at each review. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly men**: usually multifactorial (drugs, obesity, low testosterone); review the medication list before investigating extensively. [EAA 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Psychological morbidity**: low self-esteem, social withdrawal, anxiety and eating disorders in adolescents. [EAA 2019]`,`**Persistent fibrotic tissue** after about 12 months will not respond to drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Missed testicular or adrenal tumour** is the most serious error - gynaecomastia can be the presenting sign of a curable germ cell tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gynaecomastia itself is **not premalignant**, except that the underlying condition (Klinefelter, BRCA2) may raise breast cancer risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prognosis**: excellent for pubertal and drug-induced cases; surgical results are good, with recurrence uncommon if glandular tissue is removed. [EAA 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Urgent referral**: hard, eccentric, fixed mass, nipple bloody discharge or skin change (suspected cancer); testicular mass; high hCG or oestradiol; rapidly growing breast tissue in an adult. [ACR 2021]`,`**Endocrinology referral**: abnormal hormones, hypogonadism, persistent pubertal gynaecomastia beyond 2 years, prepubertal gynaecomastia (before puberty - suspect a tumour or exogenous oestrogen). [EAA 2019]`,`**Surgery referral**: fibrotic disease, failed medical therapy or major distress. [EAA 2019]`,`**Follow-up**: re-measure disc size every **3-6 months**; after stopping a drug review at 1 month; on tamoxifen review at 3 months. [EAA 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Reassure adolescents** that pubertal gynaecomastia is common and usually self-limiting; **Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK)** adolescent-friendly health clinics offer counselling. [RKSK, MoHFW]`,`**Warn against anabolic steroids, 'mass gainers' and unlabelled gym supplements** - a common cause in young Indian men. [EAA 2019]`,`**Weight reduction** reduces pseudogynaecomastia and aromatisation. [EAA 2019]`,`**Prescribers**: choose eplerenone over spironolactone in HF when gynaecomastia is a concern, and counsel men starting anti-androgens or anti-TB drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Teach breast awareness** in men at risk (Klinefelter, BRCA2, prior chest irradiation). [ACR 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**European Academy of Andrology (EAA) clinical practice guideline on gynaecomastia evaluation and management (2019)** remains the main guideline: hormone panel including **hCG**, testicular ultrasound for hormonal abnormality, tamoxifen for recent painful disease, no aromatase inhibitors, surgery for long-standing disease. [EAA 2019]`,`**ACR Appropriateness Criteria (symptomatic male breast)**: imaging is **not needed for typical gynaecomastia**; for an indeterminate mass use **ultrasound under 25 years and mammography at 25 or over**. [ACR 2021]`,`**NACO 2021 shift to dolutegravir-based TLD first line** has reduced efavirenz-related gynaecomastia in India. [NACO 2021]`,`**EAU prostate cancer guideline (2024)** supports prophylactic tamoxifen for men on long-term bicalutamide monotherapy. [EAU Prostate 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Causes and evaluation of gynaecomastia'** - draw the hormone algorithm (hCG, oestradiol, LH, testosterone). [Exam pattern]`,`**Viva**: 'Which test must never be missed in adult gynaecomastia?' - **serum hCG and testicular examination/ultrasound** for germ cell tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Commonest drug cause?' - **spironolactone**; anti-androgens in prostate cancer have the highest incidence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: investigating every pubertal boy - a normal examination needs only reassurance. [EAA 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: giving tamoxifen to long-standing fibrotic gynaecomastia - it will not shrink; refer for surgery. [EAA 2019]`,`**Pseudogynaecomastia vs gynaecomastia**: the **finger-pinch test** for a subareolar disc. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in gynaecomastia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tamoxifen (off-label)`,`10-20 mg oral once daily`,`3-6 months; review at 3 months`,`Hot flushes, GI upset, VTE (avoid with thrombosis history)`],[`Raloxifene (off-label)`,`60 mg oral once daily`,`3-6 months`,`Hot flushes, leg cramps, VTE`],[`Tamoxifen prophylaxis with bicalutamide`,`10-20 mg oral daily`,`Throughout anti-androgen therapy`,`As above`],[`Eplerenone (substitute for spironolactone in HF)`,`25 mg oral daily, up to 50 mg`,`Long term`,`Hyperkalaemia; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Amiloride (substitute in cirrhosis)`,`5-10 mg oral daily`,`Long term`,`Hyperkalaemia; weaker diuretic`],[`Anastrozole`,`1 mg oral daily`,`Not recommended - ineffective in RCT`,`Bone loss, arthralgia`],[`Testosterone (only if hypogonadal)`,`As for hypogonadism`,`Long term`,`May initially worsen gynaecomastia by aromatisation`]]},{heading:`Causes of gynaecomastia`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Physiological`,`Neonatal, pubertal, senescent`,`Transient oestrogen excess or falling testosterone`],[`Oestrogen excess - tumours`,`Testicular germ cell (hCG), Leydig/Sertoli cell, adrenal carcinoma, hCG-secreting lung or liver cancer`,`Direct oestrogen secretion or hCG-driven aromatase`],[`Androgen deficiency`,`Klinefelter, orchitis, castration, pituitary disease`,`Low testosterone`],[`Systemic disease`,`Cirrhosis, hyperthyroidism, CKD, refeeding, obesity`,`Raised SHBG, aromatisation, Leydig dysfunction`],[`Drugs - anti-androgen`,`Spironolactone, bicalutamide, finasteride, GnRH agonists, ketoconazole, cimetidine`,`Receptor block or reduced androgen synthesis`],[`Drugs - oestrogenic`,`Oestrogens, hCG, anabolic steroids, digoxin`,`Oestrogen or aromatisable androgen`],[`Drugs - other`,`Isoniazid, ethionamide, efavirenz, PPIs, calcium channel blockers, antipsychotics`,`Various or unknown`],[`Idiopathic`,`No cause found`,`Local sensitivity`]]},{heading:`Interpreting the hormone panel`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Likely cause`,`Next step`],rows:[[`High hCG`,`Testicular germ cell tumour or hCG-secreting non-testicular tumour`,`Testicular ultrasound; if negative, CT chest and abdomen`],[`High oestradiol, low or normal LH`,`Leydig/Sertoli cell tumour or feminising adrenal tumour`,`Testicular ultrasound; then DHEAS and adrenal CT`],[`Low testosterone, high LH`,`Primary hypogonadism (Klinefelter)`,`Karyotype`],[`Low testosterone, low or normal LH`,`Secondary hypogonadism`,`Prolactin, pituitary hormones, MRI`],[`High testosterone, high LH`,`Androgen resistance or hyperthyroidism`,`TSH and free T4`],[`All normal`,`Idiopathic, persistent pubertal or drug-related`,`Observe; review drugs; recheck in 6 months`]]},{heading:`Gynaecomastia vs pseudogynaecomastia vs breast cancer`,columns:[`Feature`,`Gynaecomastia`,`Pseudogynaecomastia`,`Male breast cancer`],rows:[[`Consistency`,`Rubbery to firm disc`,`Soft fat, no disc`,`Hard, irregular`],[`Position`,`Concentric under the areola`,`Diffuse`,`Often eccentric`],[`Laterality`,`Usually bilateral`,`Bilateral`,`Usually unilateral`],[`Tenderness`,`Common when recent`,`Absent`,`Usually painless`],[`Skin and nipple`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Retraction, bloody discharge, ulceration`],[`Nodes`,`None`,`None`,`Axillary nodes may be palpable`],[`Imaging`,`Not routinely needed`,`Not needed`,`Mammography or ultrasound, then core biopsy`]]},{heading:`Simon classification`,columns:[`Grade`,`Description`,`Usual treatment`],rows:[[`I`,`Small enlargement, no skin excess`,`Observation, medical therapy or liposuction`],[`IIa`,`Moderate enlargement, no skin excess`,`Subcutaneous mastectomy with or without liposuction`],[`IIb`,`Moderate enlargement with skin excess`,`Mastectomy with possible skin excision`],[`III`,`Marked enlargement with skin excess (ptosis)`,`Mastectomy with skin excision and nipple repositioning`]]}],redFlags:[`Hard, eccentric, fixed breast mass, nipple retraction, bloody discharge or skin ulceration (male breast cancer)`,`Axillary lymphadenopathy with a breast mass`,`Testicular mass or asymmetry in a man with gynaecomastia (testicular tumour)`,`Rapidly enlarging, painful gynaecomastia in an adult with no drug cause`,`Raised beta-hCG or markedly raised oestradiol`,`Gynaecomastia with virilisation loss and an abdominal mass (feminising adrenal carcinoma)`,`Gynaecomastia in a prepubertal boy (exogenous oestrogen or tumour)`],pearls:[`**Gynaecomastia = oestrogen-to-androgen ratio rises** - think of every cause as raising oestrogen or lowering androgen action.`,`**Pinch from the periphery**: a rubbery subareolar disc is gynaecomastia; nothing but fat is pseudogynaecomastia.`,`**Always examine the testes and check hCG** in unexplained adult gynaecomastia.`,`**Pubertal gynaecomastia**: 90% regress within 2 years - reassure, do not investigate a normal boy.`,`**Spironolactone** is the classic drug; switch to eplerenone (HF) or amiloride (cirrhosis).`,`**Under 12 months: tamoxifen works; over 12 months: fibrosis - surgery.**`,`**Aromatase inhibitors do not work** for established gynaecomastia.`,`**Klinefelter + breast lump** - think cancer (20-50 times risk).`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-q1`,stem:`A 14-year-old boy at Tanner stage 3 has a 2 cm tender subareolar disc on both sides for 4 months. Testes are 8 mL each and the rest of the examination is normal. He takes no drugs. The best management is:`,options:[`Serum hCG, oestradiol and testicular ultrasound`,`Reassurance and review in 6 months`,`Tamoxifen 20 mg daily for 6 months`,`Referral for subcutaneous mastectomy`],answer:1,explanation:`Bilateral tender gynaecomastia in mid-puberty with normal testes and no drugs is physiological pubertal gynaecomastia, which regresses within 2 years in about 90%, so reassurance and review are correct. Hormone tests and ultrasound are not needed with a normal examination; tamoxifen is reserved for severe or persistent painful cases; surgery is for fibrotic disease persisting after puberty.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old man has 3 months of painful bilateral breast enlargement. He takes no drugs. Testes feel normal. Beta-hCG is markedly raised, LH is suppressed and oestradiol is high. The next step is:`,options:[`Scrotal ultrasound`,`Pituitary MRI`,`Start tamoxifen and recheck hCG in 3 months`,`Karyotype`],answer:0,explanation:`A raised hCG in a young man with recent gynaecomastia indicates an hCG-secreting germ cell tumour until proved otherwise; hCG drives testicular aromatase, raising oestradiol and suppressing LH. Testicular tumours may be impalpable, so scrotal ultrasound comes first, with CT chest and abdomen if negative. Pituitary MRI and karyotype address hypogonadism, and tamoxifen would delay diagnosis of a curable cancer.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-q3`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis on spironolactone 200 mg daily develops painful gynaecomastia. The most appropriate first step is:`,options:[`Start anastrozole 1 mg daily`,`Add testosterone gel`,`Replace spironolactone with amiloride`,`Refer for mastectomy`],answer:2,explanation:`Spironolactone causes gynaecomastia by blocking androgen receptors and increasing aromatisation, especially at the high doses used in ascites; replacing it with amiloride often relieves pain and halts progression. Aromatase inhibitors are ineffective for gynaecomastia, testosterone can be aromatised and worsen it, and surgery is reserved for long-standing fibrotic tissue after the cause is addressed.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-q4`,stem:`A 70-year-old man starting bicalutamide 150 mg monotherapy for prostate cancer asks how to prevent breast enlargement. The most effective prophylaxis is:`,options:[`Anastrozole 1 mg daily`,`Finasteride 5 mg daily`,`Testosterone undecanoate injections`,`Tamoxifen 10-20 mg daily`],answer:3,explanation:`High-dose bicalutamide monotherapy causes gynaecomastia in most men because it blocks androgen receptors while testosterone and oestradiol rise. Prophylactic tamoxifen blocks breast oestrogen receptors and is more effective than anastrozole or breast bud radiotherapy. Finasteride itself causes gynaecomastia, and testosterone is contraindicated in prostate cancer.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-q5`,stem:`A 32-year-old man has had painless bilateral gynaecomastia for 3 years after taking anabolic steroids at a gym, which he stopped 2 years ago. Hormones are now normal. He is distressed by the appearance. The best treatment is:`,options:[`Tamoxifen 20 mg daily for 6 months`,`Subcutaneous mastectomy with liposuction`,`Raloxifene 60 mg daily for 1 year`,`Danazol 200 mg daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Gynaecomastia present for over 12 months has passed the florid phase into fibrosis, so drug therapy no longer shrinks it. With normal hormones and significant distress, surgical removal of glandular tissue with liposuction for the fatty component is the effective option. Tamoxifen and raloxifene act only on recent, proliferative tissue, and danazol is not recommended because of androgenic side effects.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define gynaecomastia. Discuss its causes, pathophysiology, evaluation and management.`,openingLines:[`Gynaecomastia is benign proliferation of glandular breast tissue in a male, felt as a rubbery subareolar disc, from an increased oestrogen-to-androgen action ratio.`,`It is physiological in neonates, at puberty and in old age; otherwise drugs, systemic disease, hypogonadism and hormone-secreting tumours are the causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Oestrogen stimulates, androgen inhibits breast tissue; ratio shifts by more oestrogen, less androgen or receptor block.`,`Florid phase under 12 months (tender, reversible); fibrotic after 12 months.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Physiological: neonatal, pubertal, senescent.`,`Tumours: testicular germ cell (hCG), Leydig/Sertoli, adrenal, hCG-secreting lung cancer.`,`Hypogonadism: Klinefelter, secondary.`,`Systemic: cirrhosis, hyperthyroidism, CKD, refeeding.`,`Drugs: spironolactone, anti-androgens, finasteride, isoniazid, efavirenz, cimetidine, anabolic steroids.`]},{heading:`Clinical evaluation`,points:[`Duration, pain, drugs, hypogonadal and systemic symptoms.`,`Pinch test for disc; measure size; Simon grade; signs of cancer; **testicular examination**; liver and thyroid signs.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Pubertal with normal exam: none.`,`Adult: **testosterone, oestradiol, LH, FSH, SHBG, hCG**, TSH, LFT, creatinine.`,`Testicular ultrasound for high hCG or oestradiol; adrenal CT if DHEAS high; mammography/US only if cancer suspected.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Reassure pubertal; stop or switch drugs; treat cause.`,`**Tamoxifen 10-20 mg daily for 3-6 months** for recent painful disease; aromatase inhibitors ineffective.`,`Surgery (subcutaneous mastectomy, liposuction) for fibrotic, persistent or Simon IIb-III disease.`,`Prophylactic tamoxifen for men on bicalutamide.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm: hCG, oestradiol, LH and testosterone pattern to cause`,`Simon classification table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`gynaecomastia`,`oestrogen-androgen ratio`,`hCG`,`spironolactone`,`tamoxifen`,`Simon classification`]},{id:`endocrine-gynaecomastia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate gynaecomastia, pseudogynaecomastia and male breast cancer.`,openingLines:[`Gynaecomastia is glandular proliferation, pseudogynaecomastia is fat deposition, and male breast cancer is a malignant epithelial tumour.`,`Examination alone separates most cases; imaging and biopsy are reserved for suspicious masses.`],answer:[{heading:`Gynaecomastia`,points:[`Rubbery concentric subareolar disc, often bilateral, tender when recent; hormonal or drug cause.`]},{heading:`Pseudogynaecomastia`,points:[`Obese man, soft fat, no disc on pinch test; treat with weight loss.`]},{heading:`Male breast cancer`,points:[`Older man, BRCA2 or Klinefelter; hard, eccentric, fixed lump, nipple retraction or bloody discharge, axillary nodes.`,`Mammography (25 or over) or ultrasound, then core biopsy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of the three conditions`],markSplit:[{part:`Gynaecomastia features`,marks:2},{part:`Pseudogynaecomastia`,marks:1},{part:`Breast cancer features and work-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`gynaecomastia`,`pseudogynaecomastia`,`male breast cancer`,`pinch test`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the Testes and Male Reproductive System (gynaecomastia)`,`Kanakis GA et al. European Academy of Andrology (EAA) clinical practice guidelines on gynecomastia evaluation and management, Andrology 2019`,`Braunstein GD. Gynecomastia, N Engl J Med 2007`,`American College of Radiology Appropriateness Criteria: Evaluation of the Symptomatic Male Breast, revised 2021`,`EAU-EANM-ESTRO-ESUR-ISUP-SIOG Guidelines on Prostate Cancer, 2024`,`National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, NACO, 2021`,`Simon BE, Hoffman S, Kahn S. Classification and surgical correction of gynecomastia, Plast Reconstr Surg 1973`]}),c.push({id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders`,title:`Precocious and delayed puberty`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Precocious puberty** is the onset of secondary sexual characteristics **before 8 years in girls (breast budding) or 9 years in boys (testicular volume 4 mL or more)** - **central (GnRH-dependent, pubertal LH, treated with GnRH agonists)** or **peripheral (GnRH-independent, suppressed LH)**; **delayed puberty** is **no breast development by 13 years in girls or testes under 4 mL by 14 years in boys**, most often **constitutional delay**, which must be separated from **hypogonadotropic (e.g. Kallmann) or hypergonadotropic (Turner, Klinefelter) hypogonadism**.`,keywords:[`precocious puberty`,`central precocious puberty`,`peripheral precocious puberty`,`GnRH-dependent`,`delayed puberty`,`constitutional delay of growth and puberty`,`CDGP`,`Tanner staging`,`sexual maturity rating`,`bone age`,`GnRH agonist`,`leuprolide`,`triptorelin`,`McCune-Albright syndrome`,`Kallmann syndrome`,`congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism`,`Turner syndrome`,`premature thelarche`,`premature adrenarche`,`orchidometer`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Puberty** begins with reactivation of **pulsatile hypothalamic GnRH secretion**; the first sign is **breast budding (thelarche, B2) in girls** at a mean of about 10-11 years and **testicular volume of 4 mL (G2) in boys** at a mean of about 11.5-12 years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precocious puberty**: onset **before 8 years in girls** and **before 9 years in boys** (2-2.5 SD below the mean); menarche before 9-10 years is also precocious. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Delayed puberty**: **no breast development by 13 years** in girls or **testicular volume under 4 mL by 14 years** in boys; also **primary amenorrhoea at 15** or no menarche 3 years after thelarche, and **arrested puberty** (no progress for over 2 years). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central precocious puberty (CPP)** is **about 10 times commoner in girls**, and in girls **about 90% is idiopathic**; in boys a pathological (often CNS) cause is found in a large minority, so boys need fuller evaluation. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP)** is the commonest cause of delayed puberty - about **60-65% of boys** and about 30% of girls with delay; there is usually a **family history of late bloomers**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secular trend**: age at thelarche has fallen in many populations, including Indian urban girls, linked to childhood obesity; **menarche in Indian girls** averages about 12-13 years. Clinics worldwide, including India, reported a **rise in CPP referrals during the COVID-19 lockdowns**. [Indian paediatric endocrine series]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**CPP - idiopathic**: most girls; **genetic** causes include loss-of-function **MKRN3** (paternally inherited, the commonest familial cause) and **DLK1**, and rare gain-of-function **KISS1/KISS1R**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CPP - organic CNS**: **hypothalamic hamartoma** (commonest lesion, may cause gelastic seizures), gliomas (optic glioma in **NF1**), hydrocephalus, cranial irradiation, head injury, **post-meningitic, including tuberculous meningitis** in India, and arachnoid cysts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary CPP**: prolonged exposure to sex steroids (late-treated **CAH**, McCune-Albright) matures the hypothalamus and switches on central puberty. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peripheral precocious puberty (PPP) - girls**: **functional ovarian cysts**, **McCune-Albright syndrome** (GNAS mutation), granulosa cell tumour, **exogenous oestrogen** (creams, contaminated food, lavender/tea-tree oils), and severe **primary hypothyroidism** (Van Wyk-Grumbach syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPP - boys**: **congenital adrenal hyperplasia** (21-hydroxylase - see the CAH topic), adrenal or **Leydig cell tumour**, **hCG-secreting tumours** (hepatoblastoma, intracranial or mediastinal germ cell tumour), **familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis, activating LHCGR)**, and exposure to a parent's **testosterone gel**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed puberty - functional hypogonadotropic**: **chronic illness** (coeliac disease, IBD, CKD, thalassaemia, TB, poorly controlled type 1 diabetes, HIV), **undernutrition**, anorexia, excessive exercise, hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinaemia and steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed puberty - permanent hypogonadotropic**: **congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (CHH)** - **Kallmann syndrome** when anosmic (ANOS1, FGFR1, PROKR2, CHD7 in CHARGE); **craniopharyngioma** and other sellar tumours; cranial irradiation; transfusional iron overload in thalassaemia. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`,`**Delayed puberty - hypergonadotropic**: **Turner syndrome (45,X and variants)** in girls, **Klinefelter** in boys (usually starts but stalls), gonadal damage from **chemotherapy or radiotherapy**, bilateral torsion or anorchia, galactosaemia, autoimmune ovarian failure. [Turner Guideline 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The hypothalamic **GnRH pulse generator** is active in fetal life and **'mini-puberty'** (first 3-6 months after birth), then restrained through childhood by **inhibitory signals (MKRN3, GABA)** and released at puberty by **kisspeptin, neurokinin B** and nutritional signals such as **leptin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pubertal GnRH pulses** begin at night, raise **LH more than FSH**, and drive gonadal sex steroids - so a **pubertal (raised) LH** separates **central** from **peripheral** puberty, where LH is suppressed by feedback. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oestrogen** causes breast development, the growth spurt and **epiphyseal fusion** in both sexes (testosterone acts on the plate after aromatisation) - early puberty gives a tall child but a **short adult** because bone age advances faster than height. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adrenarche** (adrenal androgen rise, DHEAS) is independent of gonadarche and causes pubic and axillary hair, body odour and acne - so pubic hair alone does not mean true puberty. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GnRH agonists** work by **continuous receptor stimulation** - after an initial flare, pituitary GnRH receptors downregulate and LH and FSH fall; they treat only GnRH-dependent (central) puberty. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Kallmann syndrome**: GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode with olfactory axons during fetal life, explaining the combination of **anosmia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**McCune-Albright**: post-zygotic activating **GNAS** mutation makes Gs-alpha constitutively active - autonomous ovarian (or testicular), thyroid, adrenal and GH hypersecretion with **cafe-au-lait macules (coast of Maine)** and **polyostotic fibrous dysplasia**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and Tanner staging`,points:[`**Tanner (sexual maturity rating) - girls breast**: **B1** prepubertal; **B2** breast bud with areolar enlargement; **B3** breast and areola enlarge without contour separation; **B4** areola and papilla form a **secondary mound**; **B5** adult contour with recession of the areola. [Tanner 1969]`,`**Tanner - boys genital**: **G1** prepubertal, testes under 4 mL; **G2** testes **4 mL or more**, scrotal reddening and thinning; **G3** penile lengthening; **G4** penile breadth and glans development, scrotal darkening; **G5** adult; adult testes about **15-25 mL**. [Tanner 1969]`,`**Pubic hair (both sexes)**: **PH1** none; **PH2** sparse long hair at the base of penis or labia; **PH3** darker, coarser, curlier over pubis; **PH4** adult type but limited area; **PH5** adult with spread to medial thighs. [Tanner 1969]`,`**Sequence**: girls - thelarche, pubarche, **peak height velocity early (B2-B3)**, **menarche about 2-2.5 years after thelarche (at B4)**; boys - testicular enlargement, pubarche, **peak height velocity late (G4)**, voice break and facial hair. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precocious puberty classification**: **central (GnRH-dependent)**, **peripheral (GnRH-independent)**, and **benign variants** - isolated premature thelarche, premature adrenarche and isolated premature menarche. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Delayed puberty classification**: **CDGP**, **functional hypogonadotropic** (illness, nutrition), **permanent hypogonadotropic** (CHH, Kallmann, tumours), and **hypergonadotropic** (Turner, Klinefelter, gonadal failure). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isosexual vs contrasexual**: virilisation of a girl or feminisation of a boy is **contrasexual** and always pathological (CAH or androgen tumour in girls; oestrogen tumour or aromatase excess in boys). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Precocious puberty**: age at first sign, **tempo of progression**, growth spurt (outgrowing clothes and shoes), vaginal bleeding, body odour, acne, and behavioural change. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**CNS clues**: headache, vomiting, visual disturbance, **gelastic (laughing) seizures** (hamartoma), polyuria (germinoma with diabetes insipidus), previous meningitis, head injury or cranial radiotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposure history**: parental **testosterone gel or oestrogen creams**, lavender or tea-tree oil products, contraceptive pills in the house, and unlabelled 'tonics'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed puberty**: family history of **late puberty in parents** (mother's menarche, father's growth spurt), **sense of smell**, chronic diarrhoea, weight loss, abdominal pain (coeliac, IBD), diet, exercise, eating behaviour, and previous chemotherapy or radiotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neonatal clues to CHH**: **micropenis and cryptorchidism** in boys, previous surgery for undescended testes, cleft lip or palate, deafness, and **mirror movements (synkinesia)** in Kallmann. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`,`**Psychosocial**: early puberty causes embarrassment, early menstruation hygiene problems and risk of abuse; late puberty causes bullying, low self-esteem and school avoidance - both deserve treatment for distress alone. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Accurate height, weight and BMI** plotted on **IAP 2015 growth charts** (5-18 years) with **parental target height** (mid-parental height plus 6.5 cm for boys, minus 6.5 cm for girls); **height velocity** over 4-6 months is the key dynamic sign. [IAP 2015]`,`**Tanner staging** of breast or genitals and pubic hair; **testicular volume by Prader orchidometer** - **4 mL or more = central puberty** in a boy; **small testes with virilisation = adrenal or exogenous androgen (peripheral)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asymmetric testicular enlargement** suggests a **Leydig cell tumour**; bilateral modest enlargement with marked virilisation suggests testotoxicosis or hCG-secreting tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: **cafe-au-lait macules** - smooth-edged 'coast of California' in **NF1**, irregular 'coast of Maine' in **McCune-Albright**; acne, oily skin, axillary hair. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fundoscopy and visual fields** for papilloedema or a chiasmal lesion; **smell testing** (anosmia in Kallmann); neurological signs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Turner stigmata**: short stature, **webbed neck, low posterior hairline, wide-spaced nipples, cubitus valgus, short 4th metacarpal**, lymphoedema history, and coarctation (absent femoral pulses). [Turner Guideline 2024]`,`**Body proportions**: **eunuchoid** (arm span exceeding height) suggests long-standing hypogonadism; **thyroid signs**, goitre, and signs of chronic illness (pallor, wasting, clubbing). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Premature thelarche**: isolated breast development, usually **under 2-3 years**, non-progressive, **normal height velocity and bone age**, prepubertal LH - observe; a minority progress to CPP. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Premature adrenarche**: pubic or axillary hair and body odour from **age 6-8 years**, **no breast or testicular development**, DHEAS raised for age, bone age slightly advanced - benign but linked to later PCOS and insulin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-classic CAH**: looks like premature adrenarche but with **rapid growth, clitoromegaly or markedly advanced bone age** - check **17-hydroxyprogesterone**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary hypothyroidism (Van Wyk-Grumbach)**: breast development or menstrual bleeding with **delayed bone age and slow growth** - the only precocious puberty with retarded bone age; it reverses with levothyroxine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CDGP vs CHH** (the hardest distinction): CDGP has **delayed bone age matching height age, normal growth for bone age, family history** and spontaneous start by about 18 years; CHH suggests itself with **anosmia, micropenis, cryptorchidism or very low inhibin B**. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`,`**Turner syndrome** in any girl with **short stature and delayed puberty**, even without stigmata - only a karyotype excludes it. [Turner Guideline 2024]`,`**Obesity in girls (lipomastia)** mimics breast development - palpate for glandular tissue; **obesity in boys** buries a normal penis (concealed penis) and is mistaken for hypogonadism. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bone age** (X-ray of the left hand and wrist, Greulich-Pyle or TW3): **advanced over 1-2 years** in precocious puberty, **delayed** in CDGP, hypothyroidism and hypogonadism. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Basal LH by an ultrasensitive assay (ICMA/ECLIA)**: **above about 0.3 IU/L** strongly suggests **central** puberty; a prepubertal LH does not exclude it in early stages. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**GnRH or GnRH-agonist stimulation test** (for example leuprolide 20 mcg/kg SC, LH at 30-60 minutes): **peak LH above about 5 IU/L** confirms central puberty; **LH:FSH ratio above about 0.66** supports it. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Sex steroids and adrenal markers**: oestradiol, testosterone, **17-OHP** (CAH), **DHEAS** (adrenal tumour or adrenarche), **beta-hCG** in boys with PPP, **TSH and free T4**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pelvic ultrasound in girls**: **uterine length above about 3.5 cm**, a pear shape, endometrial echo and **ovarian volume above 2 mL** indicate oestrogen exposure; also shows ovarian cysts or tumours; **testicular ultrasound** in boys with asymmetric testes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MRI brain (hypothalamic-pituitary)** in **all boys with CPP**, **girls with onset under 6 years**, and any child with neurological signs; it is debated in girls aged 6-8 years without features (yield under 5%). [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Delayed puberty screen**: **LH, FSH, testosterone or oestradiol**, **karyotype** if gonadotrophins are high or the girl is short, CBC, ESR, creatinine, LFT, **tTG-IgA** (coeliac), TSH, free T4, prolactin, IGF-1, and MRI for suspected CHH or a sellar tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CDGP vs CHH tests**: **basal inhibin B** (very low in CHH boys - about 35 pg/mL or less), GnRH-agonist stimulated LH and smell testing; often only **time or a short course of sex steroids** separates them. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Benign variants** (premature thelarche, premature adrenarche): **reassure** and review height velocity and Tanner stage every **3-6 months**; refer if progression or growth acceleration appears. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Indications for GnRH agonist in CPP**: **progressive puberty** (advancing Tanner stage and bone age over 3-6 months), **predicted adult height loss**, and **psychosocial distress** or developmental delay where early menarche will be hard to manage; height gain is greatest when treatment starts **before 6 years in girls**. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Leuprolide acetate depot**: **3.75-7.5 mg IM every 4 weeks** (or 0.3 mg/kg), **11.25 or 22.5 mg IM every 3 months**, or **45 mg SC every 6 months**; **triptorelin 3.75 mg IM monthly, 11.25 mg 3-monthly or 22.5 mg 6-monthly**; **histrelin 50 mg implant yearly**. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**When to stop GnRH agonist**: individualised, usually around a **chronological age of about 11 years in girls and 12 years in boys** or bone age about 12-12.5 years; menarche typically follows **12-18 months after stopping**. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**CDGP in boys**: watchful waiting with reassurance, or a **'jump start' of testosterone enanthate 50-100 mg IM every 4 weeks for 3-6 months** (from about 14 years) - it improves self-esteem and does not compromise adult height; **letrozole** (off-label) is an alternative under specialist care. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CDGP or hypogonadism in girls**: **low-dose oestradiol** - transdermal **3.1-6.2 mcg/day** (a fraction of a 25 mcg patch) or **oral 17-beta-oestradiol about 5 mcg/kg/day** - increased every 6 months towards adult doses over 2-3 years; add a **progestogen** after about 2 years or when breakthrough bleeding occurs. [Turner Guideline 2024]`,`**Treat functional causes**: gluten-free diet in coeliac disease, nutrition and calorie repletion, control of IBD, CKD, thalassaemia (iron chelation) and diabetes - puberty often follows. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peripheral precocious puberty** is treated by its cause: hydrocortisone for CAH, levothyroxine for hypothyroidism, removal of exogenous sex steroids or tumours; **aromatase inhibitors (letrozole) or tamoxifen** for McCune-Albright, and **bicalutamide plus anastrozole** for testotoxicosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Specialist, IPD and emergency care`,points:[`**Admission is uncommon**; admit for **raised intracranial pressure**, visual loss or seizures from an intracranial lesion, **adrenal crisis in salt-wasting CAH**, or tumour surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypothalamic hamartoma** with only CPP is treated medically with GnRH agonists; surgery or laser ablation is reserved for **intractable gelastic seizures**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Craniopharyngioma and germinoma** need neurosurgery, oncology and lifelong pituitary replacement - check cortisol, thyroid and **AVP deficiency (diabetes insipidus)** before and after surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Permanent hypogonadism in boys**: **testosterone enanthate 50 mg IM monthly**, increased by about 50 mg every 6-12 months to the **adult dose over 2-3 years**; **FSH priming followed by hCG** can induce testicular growth and later fertility in CHH. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`,`**Fertility in adults with CHH**: men - **hCG plus FSH**; women - **gonadotrophins or pulsatile GnRH** for ovulation induction (see the Male hypogonadism topic). [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`,`**Turner syndrome**: **growth hormone** from early childhood for height, **oestrogen induction at 11-12 years**, cardiac (MRI/echo for bicuspid valve and coarctation), renal ultrasound, hearing and thyroid screening - multidisciplinary care. [Turner Guideline 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children with neurodevelopmental disability or prior CNS insult (hydrocephalus, cerebral palsy)** have higher rates of CPP - treatment eases menstrual hygiene and care burden. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Childhood cancer survivors**: cranial radiotherapy causes CPP (low dose) or hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (high dose); alkylating chemotherapy and gonadal radiation cause hypergonadotropic failure - long-term follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thalassaemia major** (common in India): delayed puberty from **pituitary iron overload** - optimise chelation and induce puberty with sex steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adopted children and migrants** (from low- to high-income settings) have a higher risk of early puberty after catch-up growth. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Girls with obesity**: earlier thelarche is common; distinguish lipomastia by palpation and use bone age and LH before diagnosing CPP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: Tanner staging, height velocity, bone age X-ray and basal LH/oestradiol are enough to refer; do not start GnRH agonists without confirming central puberty. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Untreated progressive CPP**: **short adult height** (from early epiphyseal fusion), early menarche, psychosocial stress, and possibly higher later risk of metabolic disease and breast cancer. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**GnRH agonist adverse effects**: **withdrawal vaginal bleed after the first dose** (flare), injection-site reactions and **sterile abscess**, headache, hot flushes, mood change, and rarely **idiopathic intracranial hypertension** and slipped capital femoral epiphysis. [FDA 2022]`,`**Long-term outcomes after GnRH agonists**: reproductive function, **bone density** and BMI are normal in adulthood; treatment does **not** increase PCOS or obesity. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**CDGP**: puberty eventually completes spontaneously; **adult height is usually within the genetic range**, sometimes slightly below target. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated hypogonadism**: **osteoporosis**, eunuchoid habitus, infertility and psychosocial harm - timely induction protects bone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CHH reversal**: about **10-20%** of men with CHH recover reproductive function after stopping treatment - retest off therapy in adulthood. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a paediatric endocrinologist**: any girl with breast development before 8 years or menarche before 9-10, any boy with pubertal signs before 9, contrasexual signs, and delayed puberty at 13 (girls) or 14 (boys). [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**On GnRH agonist**: clinical review every **3-6 months** (Tanner stage, height velocity), **LH (basal or 30-60 minutes after the depot dose) to confirm suppression**, and **bone age every 6-12 months**. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Signs of inadequate suppression**: progressing breasts or testes, continuing fast growth or bone age advance, or menses - shorten the interval or increase the dose. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**CDGP**: review every **6 months** - if no spontaneous progress after a testosterone or oestrogen course, investigate for permanent hypogonadism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Puberty induction**: monitor height, Tanner stage, bone age, uterine size (girls) and haemoglobin and lipids (boys on testosterone). [Turner Guideline 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Prevent childhood obesity** (diet, activity, screen time) - linked with earlier thelarche in girls. [IAP 2015]`,`**Store testosterone gels and oestrogen creams safely** and wash hands after applying; avoid unprescribed tonics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RBSK** screens children for defects, deficiencies, diseases and developmental delays at Anganwadi and school level - early growth and pubertal concerns can be referred to **District Early Intervention Centres**. [RBSK, MoHFW]`,`**Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK)** adolescent-friendly health clinics provide counselling on puberty, menstruation and body image. [RKSK, MoHFW]`,`**Counselling for CPP**: explain menstrual hygiene, appropriate sex education for the child's developmental age, and protection from abuse. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**Newborn screening for CAH** (17-OHP, being expanded in some Indian states) prevents salt-wasting crisis and later peripheral precocity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**International Consortium update on GnRH analogue use in children (2019)** remains the reference: basal ultrasensitive LH as the first test, long-acting 3- and 6-monthly depots acceptable, MRI for all boys and girls under 6. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**FDA 2022 safety labelling**: GnRH agonists used for CPP now carry a warning for **pseudotumour cerebri (idiopathic intracranial hypertension)** - ask about headache and visual symptoms and check fundi. [FDA 2022]`,`**Six-monthly depots** (leuprolide 45 mg SC and triptorelin 22.5 mg IM) are now standard options, cutting injection burden. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`,`**International Turner syndrome guideline (2024)**: start **transdermal oestradiol at 11-12 years**, increase to adult dose over 2-3 years, karyotype for any girl with unexplained short stature or delayed puberty. [Turner Guideline 2024]`,`**European consensus on CHH and Kallmann** guides genetic testing, **FSH priming before hCG** in boys, and retesting for reversal. [ESPE CHH Consensus 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Tanner staging'** - draw the B1-5, G1-5 and PH1-5 table, and give the onset ages (8 girls, 9 boys; delay at 13 and 14). [Exam pattern]`,`**Viva**: 'Precocious puberty with delayed bone age?' - **primary hypothyroidism (Van Wyk-Grumbach)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Precocious puberty, cafe-au-lait macules and fractures?' - **McCune-Albright syndrome** (peripheral; GnRH agonists do not work initially). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Delayed puberty with anosmia?' - **Kallmann syndrome**; 'short girl with delayed puberty?' - **Turner until karyotype says otherwise**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Testicular volume rule in boys**: **enlarged testes = central** (or hCG/testotoxicosis); **prepubertal testes with virilisation = adrenal or exogenous androgen**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistakes**: calling isolated pubic hair 'precocious puberty' (it is usually adrenarche), starting GnRH agonists for peripheral puberty, and not doing MRI in a boy with CPP. [GnRHa Consortium 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for puberty disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Leuprolide acetate depot (CPP)`,`3.75-7.5 mg IM every 4 weeks; 11.25 or 22.5 mg IM every 3 months; 45 mg SC every 6 months`,`Until about 11 years (girls) / 12 years (boys); check LH suppression`,`Withdrawal bleed after first dose, sterile abscess, headache, IIH (rare)`],[`Triptorelin (CPP)`,`3.75 mg IM monthly, 11.25 mg 3-monthly or 22.5 mg 6-monthly`,`As above`,`As above`],[`Histrelin implant (CPP)`,`50 mg SC implant`,`Replace every 12 months`,`Implant breakage, local reactions`],[`Testosterone enanthate (boys, CDGP)`,`50-100 mg IM every 4 weeks`,`3-6 months; may repeat once`,`Acne, mood change; check bone age before`],[`Testosterone enanthate (permanent hypogonadism)`,`Start 50 mg IM monthly, raise 50 mg every 6-12 months`,`Adult dose over 2-3 years`,`Erythrocytosis, priapism (rare), early epiphyseal fusion if too fast`],[`Oestradiol (girls)`,`Transdermal 3.1-6.2 mcg/day or oral 17-beta-oestradiol about 5 mcg/kg/day`,`Increase every 6 months to adult dose over 2-3 years; add progestogen after about 2 years`,`Breast tenderness, nausea, VTE (rare); avoid ethinyl oestradiol`],[`Letrozole (off-label; McCune-Albright, CDGP boys)`,`2.5 mg oral daily`,`Specialist-guided`,`Vertebral changes, raised testosterone in boys`],[`Bicalutamide plus anastrozole (testotoxicosis)`,`Bicalutamide 50 mg plus anastrozole 1 mg oral daily`,`Until central puberty takes over`,`Gynaecomastia, liver enzyme rise`]]},{heading:`Tanner staging`,columns:[`Stage`,`Girls - breast`,`Boys - genitalia`,`Pubic hair (both)`],rows:[[`1`,`Prepubertal, papilla elevation only`,`Prepubertal; testes under 4 mL`,`None (vellus only)`],[`2`,`Breast bud, areola enlarges`,`Testes 4 mL or more, scrotal reddening and thinning`,`Sparse long hair at base of penis or labia`],[`3`,`Breast and areola enlarge, single contour`,`Penis lengthens; testes 8-12 mL`,`Darker, coarser, curly over pubis`],[`4`,`Areola and papilla form secondary mound`,`Penis broadens, glans develops, scrotum darkens; testes 12-15 mL`,`Adult type, smaller area, not on thighs`],[`5`,`Adult contour, areola recedes`,`Adult size; testes 15-25 mL`,`Adult, spreads to medial thighs`]]},{heading:`Central vs peripheral precocious puberty vs benign variants`,columns:[`Feature`,`Central (GnRH-dependent)`,`Peripheral (GnRH-independent)`,`Premature thelarche / adrenarche`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Early activation of GnRH pulse generator`,`Autonomous gonadal/adrenal steroids or exogenous`,`Isolated breast or adrenal androgen effect`],[`Basal / stimulated LH`,`Pubertal (basal over 0.3; peak over 5 IU/L)`,`Suppressed`,`Prepubertal`],[`Testes (boys)`,`Enlarged bilaterally`,`Small (adrenal/exogenous); asymmetric (Leydig tumour)`,`Prepubertal`],[`Bone age`,`Advanced`,`Advanced (delayed in hypothyroidism)`,`Normal or slightly advanced`],[`Height velocity`,`Accelerated`,`Accelerated`,`Normal`],[`Consonance`,`Normal sequence`,`Often out of sequence or contrasexual`,`Single feature only`],[`Treatment`,`GnRH agonist`,`Treat cause; aromatase inhibitor or anti-androgen`,`Observe`]]},{heading:`Causes of delayed puberty and how to tell them apart`,columns:[`Category`,`Examples`,`LH / FSH`,`Clues`],rows:[[`Constitutional delay (CDGP)`,`Familial late bloomer`,`Low (prepubertal)`,`Family history, delayed bone age matching height age, healthy`],[`Functional hypogonadotropic`,`Coeliac, IBD, CKD, malnutrition, anorexia, hypothyroidism, thalassaemia`,`Low`,`Weight loss, chronic illness, abnormal screening tests`],[`Permanent hypogonadotropic`,`Kallmann/CHH, craniopharyngioma, irradiation`,`Low`,`Anosmia, micropenis, cryptorchidism, headache, visual loss, low inhibin B`],[`Hypergonadotropic - girls`,`Turner, chemotherapy, autoimmune ovarian failure, galactosaemia`,`High`,`Short stature, Turner stigmata, karyotype 45,X`],[`Hypergonadotropic - boys`,`Klinefelter, anorchia, chemotherapy, orchitis`,`High`,`Small firm testes, karyotype 47,XXY`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Use`],rows:[[`Bone age (left hand-wrist)`,`Advanced over 1-2 years vs delayed`,`Precocious vs delayed; growth prediction`],[`Basal LH (ultrasensitive)`,`Above about 0.3 IU/L`,`Suggests central puberty`],[`GnRH/GnRH-agonist stimulation`,`Peak LH above about 5 IU/L`,`Confirms central puberty`],[`Pelvic ultrasound`,`Uterus above about 3.5 cm, ovarian volume above 2 mL`,`Oestrogen exposure; cysts and tumours`],[`17-OHP, DHEAS, hCG`,`Raised for age`,`CAH, adrenal tumour, hCG-secreting tumour`],[`TSH, free T4`,`High TSH, low FT4`,`Van Wyk-Grumbach; delayed puberty screen`],[`Karyotype`,`45,X or 47,XXY`,`High gonadotrophins; short girl with delay`],[`Inhibin B (boys)`,`About 35 pg/mL or less`,`Suggests CHH rather than CDGP`],[`MRI hypothalamic-pituitary`,`Hamartoma, glioma, tumour, absent olfactory bulbs`,`All boys with CPP, girls under 6, CHH, neurological signs`]]}],redFlags:[`Pubertal signs in a boy before 9 years (high chance of CNS or tumour cause)`,`Breast development or vaginal bleeding in a girl under 6 years, or rapid progression at any age`,`Headache, vomiting, visual loss, polyuria or gelastic seizures with abnormal puberty (intracranial lesion)`,`Contrasexual signs - virilisation in a girl or feminisation in a boy (CAH or tumour)`,`Asymmetric testicular enlargement or an abdominal mass (Leydig or adrenal tumour)`,`Delayed puberty with short stature in a girl (Turner) or with anosmia, micropenis or cryptorchidism in a boy (CHH)`,`Delayed puberty with weight loss, diarrhoea or poor growth (chronic disease such as coeliac or IBD)`],pearls:[`**8 for girls, 9 for boys** = precocious; **13 for girls, 14 for boys** = delayed.`,`**First sign**: breast bud in girls, **testes 4 mL** in boys - not pubic hair.`,`**Pubertal LH = central; suppressed LH = peripheral** - GnRH agonists treat only central.`,`**Precocious puberty with delayed bone age = hypothyroidism (Van Wyk-Grumbach).**`,`**Every boy with CPP gets an MRI brain.**`,`**Big testes = central; small testes with virilisation = adrenal or exogenous androgen.**`,`**Short girl with delayed puberty = karyotype for Turner.**`,`**Kallmann = hypogonadotropic hypogonadism + anosmia** (failed GnRH neuron migration).`,`**CDGP**: family history, delayed bone age, normal growth for bone age - reassure or short testosterone course.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-q1`,stem:`A 6.5-year-old girl has Tanner B3 breasts, pubic hair and height velocity of 9 cm/year. Bone age is 9 years. Ultrasensitive basal LH is 1.2 IU/L. The most appropriate treatment after confirming the diagnosis is:`,options:[`Letrozole 2.5 mg daily`,`Medroxyprogesterone injections`,`Leuprolide depot 11.25 mg IM every 3 months`,`Observation with review in 1 year`],answer:2,explanation:`Progressive breast development, rapid growth, advanced bone age and a pubertal basal LH above 0.3 IU/L indicate central precocious puberty; a GnRH agonist such as 3-monthly leuprolide depot suppresses the axis and preserves adult height. Letrozole is used for peripheral causes such as McCune-Albright, medroxyprogesterone does not protect height, and observing a rapidly progressive case loses growth potential.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-q2`,stem:`A 7-year-old boy has pubic hair, penile enlargement and rapid growth. Both testes measure 2 mL. Bone age is 11 years. Which test is most likely to be diagnostic?`,options:[`Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone`,`GnRH stimulation test`,`MRI of the pituitary`,`Testicular biopsy`],answer:0,explanation:`Virilisation with prepubertal (2 mL) testes means the androgens are not coming from gonadotrophin-driven testes, so this is peripheral precocious puberty, most commonly from non-classic or simple virilising 21-hydroxylase CAH, diagnosed by a raised 17-OHP. A GnRH test and pituitary MRI investigate central puberty, where both testes would be enlarged, and biopsy has no role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-q3`,stem:`A 15-year-old boy has testes of 3 mL and no pubic hair. He has never been able to smell coffee and had orchidopexy for an undescended testis. LH and FSH are low. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Klinefelter syndrome`,`Kallmann syndrome`,`Constitutional delay of growth and puberty`,`Primary hypothyroidism`],answer:1,explanation:`Absent puberty with low gonadotrophins and anosmia is Kallmann syndrome, in which GnRH neurons fail to migrate with olfactory neurons; cryptorchidism further supports congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Klinefelter gives high LH and FSH and usually starts puberty; constitutional delay has a normal sense of smell and often a family history; hypothyroidism would show other features and abnormal thyroid tests.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-q4`,stem:`A 7-year-old girl has breast development and occasional vaginal bleeding. She is short, has gained weight and her bone age is 5 years. Which investigation will most likely explain the findings?`,options:[`Pelvic ultrasound for ovarian tumour`,`MRI brain for hypothalamic hamartoma`,`Serum DHEAS`,`Serum TSH and free T4`],answer:3,explanation:`Precocious puberty with delayed bone age, slow growth and weight gain is the hallmark of severe primary hypothyroidism (Van Wyk-Grumbach syndrome), where very high TSH acts on FSH receptors and stimulates the ovaries; TSH and free T4 make the diagnosis, and levothyroxine reverses it. Ovarian tumours, hamartomas and adrenal causes all advance rather than delay bone age.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-q5`,stem:`A healthy 14.5-year-old boy is the shortest in his class with testes of 3 mL. His father had his growth spurt at 17. Height velocity is normal for bone age, which is 12 years. Smell is normal, and blood tests are normal. The best management is:`,options:[`Pituitary MRI and GH stimulation test`,`Lifelong testosterone undecanoate`,`Reassurance, or a short course of testosterone enanthate 50-100 mg IM monthly for 3-6 months`,`Karyotype and hCG stimulation test`],answer:2,explanation:`A healthy boy with a family history of late puberty, delayed bone age matching his height and normal smell and screening tests has constitutional delay of growth and puberty. Management is reassurance or, for distress, a short 'jump start' of low-dose testosterone, which does not harm adult height. MRI and GH testing, lifelong testosterone and karyotype are for permanent or hypergonadotropic causes not suggested here.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define precocious puberty. Classify its causes. Describe the evaluation and management of central precocious puberty.`,openingLines:[`Precocious puberty is the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before 8 years in girls or 9 years in boys.`,`It is central (GnRH-dependent, pubertal LH) or peripheral (GnRH-independent, suppressed LH), and must be separated from benign variants such as premature thelarche and adrenarche.`],answer:[{heading:`Physiology and Tanner staging`,points:[`Kisspeptin-driven GnRH pulses raise LH and FSH; first sign is thelarche (B2) or testes 4 mL (G2).`,`Tanner staging of breast, genitals and pubic hair.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Central**: idiopathic (most girls), MKRN3, hypothalamic hamartoma, tumours, hydrocephalus, post-meningitis, irradiation, secondary to CAH.`,`**Peripheral**: ovarian cyst, McCune-Albright, granulosa tumour, CAH, Leydig tumour, hCG tumour, testotoxicosis, exogenous steroids, hypothyroidism.`,`**Benign variants**: premature thelarche, adrenarche, isolated menarche.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History: age of onset, tempo, CNS symptoms, exposures, family history.`,`Examination: height velocity, Tanner stage, testicular volume, cafe-au-lait spots, fundi, neurological signs.`,`**Bone age**, **basal ultrasensitive LH (over 0.3 IU/L)**, GnRH stimulation (**peak LH over 5**), oestradiol/testosterone, TSH, 17-OHP, hCG.`,`Pelvic ultrasound; **MRI brain for all boys and girls under 6**.`]},{heading:`Management of CPP`,points:[`Indications: progressive puberty, height loss, psychosocial distress.`,`**Leuprolide 3.75-7.5 mg IM monthly or 11.25 mg 3-monthly**, triptorelin 3.75/11.25/22.5 mg, histrelin implant.`,`Treat cause: hamartoma, tumours.`,`Monitor every 3-6 months: Tanner, growth, LH suppression; bone age yearly; stop at about 11 years (girls), 12 (boys).`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Treatment preserves adult height when started early; adult fertility and bone density are normal.`,`Adverse effects: withdrawal bleed, sterile abscess, IIH (FDA 2022).`]}],mustDraw:[`Hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis showing site of central vs peripheral puberty`,`Flow chart: early puberty - bone age and LH - central vs peripheral vs benign variant`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:3},{part:`Evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Management and monitoring`,marks:3},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`precocious puberty`,`GnRH agonist`,`bone age`,`LH`,`hamartoma`,`McCune-Albright`,`Tanner`]},{id:`endocrine-puberty-disorders-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the evaluation of delayed puberty in a boy.`,openingLines:[`Delayed puberty in a boy is testicular volume under 4 mL at 14 years.`,`Constitutional delay is commonest, but chronic illness, hypogonadotropic and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism must be excluded.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1 - clinical`,points:[`Height, velocity, target height, Tanner stage, testicular volume, smell, micropenis, cryptorchidism, chronic illness signs.`]},{heading:`Step 2 - first tests`,points:[`Bone age, LH, FSH, testosterone, CBC, ESR, creatinine, LFT, tTG-IgA, TSH, prolactin.`]},{heading:`Step 3 - branch on LH/FSH`,points:[`**High**: primary - karyotype (Klinefelter), history of chemotherapy or orchitis.`,`**Low with abnormal screen**: functional - treat illness.`,`**Low with normal screen**: CDGP vs CHH - family history, inhibin B, smell; MRI if CHH suspected.`]},{heading:`Step 4 - treat`,points:[`CDGP: reassure or testosterone 50-100 mg IM monthly for 3-6 months.`,`Permanent hypogonadism: escalating testosterone over 2-3 years, or hCG plus FSH for fertility.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart branching on LH/FSH into hypergonadotropic, functional, CDGP and CHH`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and clinical step`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Branching and treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`delayed puberty`,`CDGP`,`Kallmann`,`Klinefelter`,`inhibin B`,`testosterone`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of Puberty; Disorders of the Testes and Female Reproductive System`,`Bangalore Krishna K et al. Use of Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone Analogs in Children: Update by an International Consortium, Horm Res Paediatr 2019`,`Boehm U et al. Expert consensus document: European Consensus Statement on congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, Nat Rev Endocrinol 2015`,`Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Care of Girls and Women with Turner Syndrome (International Turner Syndrome Consensus Group), Eur J Endocrinol 2024`,`US FDA Drug Safety Labeling Change: GnRH agonists for central precocious puberty - pseudotumor cerebri warning, 2022`,`Indian Academy of Pediatrics revised growth charts for height, weight and BMI for 5-18 year old Indian children, Indian Pediatrics 2015`,`Tanner JM. Growth at Adolescence, and Marshall WA, Tanner JM. Variations in the pattern of pubertal changes, Arch Dis Child 1969-1970`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`endocrine-hirsutism`,title:`Hirsutism and virilisation`,oneLiner:`**Hirsutism** is excess **terminal hair in a male pattern** in a woman (modified **Ferriman-Gallwey score** at or above the ethnic cut-off, conventionally **8 or more**), caused in about **70-80% by PCOS**; **virilisation** (clitoromegaly, deep voice, temporal balding, rising muscle mass) or **rapid onset with testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL** points to an **androgen-secreting tumour** until proved otherwise.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`hirsutism`,`virilisation`,`virilization`,`hyperandrogenism`,`Ferriman-Gallwey`,`mFG score`,`PCOS`,`non-classic CAH`,`NCCAH`,`17-hydroxyprogesterone`,`DHEAS`,`androgen-secreting tumour`,`ovarian hyperthecosis`,`HAIR-AN`,`spironolactone`,`cyproterone acetate`,`finasteride`,`eflornithine`,`laser hair removal`,`hypertrichosis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hirsutism** is excessive growth of **terminal (thick, pigmented) hair** in androgen-dependent, male-pattern sites in a woman - upper lip, chin, chest, linea alba, back, buttocks and inner thighs. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Hypertrichosis** is different: diffuse growth of **vellus or terminal hair in a non-sexual pattern**, not androgen-dependent, caused by drugs (minoxidil, phenytoin, ciclosporin), anorexia, hypothyroidism or porphyria - antiandrogens do not help it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Virilisation** is hirsutism plus signs of high androgen action on other tissues - **clitoromegaly, deepening of the voice, temporal balding, increased muscle mass, breast atrophy** and loss of female body contour - and implies very high androgen levels. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Hirsutism affects about **5-15% of women of reproductive age** worldwide, varying with ethnicity and the cut-off used; it is the commonest reason women with hyperandrogenism present to a doctor. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**PCOS causes about 70-80%** of hirsutism; **idiopathic hirsutism** (normal androgens, regular ovulatory cycles, normal ovaries) about 5-20%; **non-classic CAH** about 1-5%; **androgen-secreting tumours** about 0.2%. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`In India, community studies report **PCOS in roughly 8-20% of young women** depending on the criteria used, and hirsutism is its commonest visible feature in Indian series - so hirsutism is a daily OPD problem. [FOGSI PCOS 2020]`,`South Asian women have **more terminal hair at baseline** than East Asian women but similar to or less than Mediterranean women - which is why one mFG cut-off does not fit every population. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Ovarian**: PCOS (commonest), **ovarian hyperthecosis** (postmenopausal, severe, nests of luteinised theca cells), and ovarian androgen-secreting tumours (**Sertoli-Leydig cell tumour**, Leydig/hilus cell tumour, steroid cell tumour). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Adrenal**: **non-classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency (NCCAH)**, rarely 11-beta-hydroxylase or 3-beta-HSD deficiency, **adrenocortical carcinoma**, and Cushing syndrome (especially ACTH-dependent with high adrenal androgens). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Other endocrine**: hyperprolactinaemia, acromegaly and hypothyroidism cause mild hirsutism through lower SHBG or altered androgen metabolism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe insulin resistance**: the **HAIR-AN syndrome** (hyperandrogenism, insulin resistance, acanthosis nigricans) - insulin acts on ovarian theca cells as a co-gonadotrophin and lowers SHBG. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Drugs and exogenous androgens**: danazol, anabolic steroids (gym users), testosterone gels used by a partner (skin transfer), DHEA supplements and valproate (via a PCOS-like state). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Pregnancy**: **luteoma of pregnancy** and **hyperreactio luteinalis** (theca-lutein cysts) cause new hirsutism or virilisation in the mother and can virilise a female fetus. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Idiopathic hirsutism** reflects increased skin **5-alpha-reductase activity** or androgen receptor sensitivity at normal serum androgen levels - a diagnosis made only after excluding the rest. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Risk factors for PCOS-related hirsutism: **central obesity, family history** of PCOS or type 2 diabetes, and **Asian-Indian insulin-resistant phenotype**. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why hair grows and why the drugs work`,points:[`Androgens convert fine **vellus hair into terminal hair** in sensitive follicles; once converted, the follicle stays terminal even after androgens fall - so treatment **stops new conversion and slows growth but rarely removes existing hair**, and mechanical or laser removal is still needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Testosterone** is converted in the pilosebaceous unit by **5-alpha-reductase** to **dihydrotestosterone (DHT)**, the potent ligand - the reason **finasteride** (5-alpha-reductase inhibitor) works. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In women about **25% of testosterone comes from the ovary, 25% from the adrenal and 50% from peripheral conversion** of androstenedione; **DHEAS is almost entirely adrenal**, so a high DHEAS localises the source to the adrenal. [Harrison 22e]`,`Only **1-2% of testosterone is free**; most is bound to **SHBG**, which falls with obesity, insulin resistance, hypothyroidism and androgens - so free testosterone rises even when total testosterone is normal. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`In **PCOS**, increased GnRH pulse frequency raises **LH**, which drives theca-cell androgen production, while **hyperinsulinaemia** amplifies LH action and lowers SHBG - so **weight loss and metformin** lower free androgens. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Combined oral contraceptives** work two ways: the oestrogen **raises hepatic SHBG** and the progestin **suppresses LH**, cutting ovarian androgen output; cyproterone and drospirenone add **androgen-receptor blockade**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Spironolactone** blocks the androgen receptor and weakly inhibits androgen synthesis; antiandrogens **feminise a male fetus**, so they must be combined with reliable contraception. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Hair grows in cycles (anagen, catagen, telogen) of several months, so any drug needs **at least 6 months** before its effect can be judged. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`In **NCCAH**, partial 21-hydroxylase deficiency diverts **17-hydroxyprogesterone** into adrenal androgens; cortisol output is maintained by mild ACTH drive, so patients are not salt-wasting and present with PCOS-like features. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`The **modified Ferriman-Gallwey (mFG) score** grades **9 body areas** (upper lip, chin, chest, upper abdomen, lower abdomen, upper arm, thigh, upper back, lower back) from **0 to 4** each, total 0-36. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Cut-offs: **8 or more** in US/UK White and Black women; **4-6 or more** in East Asian women; **9-10 or more** in Mediterranean, Hispanic and Middle Eastern women. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Indian cut-off**: there is no single validated national value; most Indian clinics use **mFG 8 or more**, and some Indian population data suggest a lower threshold of about 6-7 - so treat a lower score with distress or a focal pattern as clinically significant. [FOGSI PCOS 2020]`,`The **2023 International PCOS Guideline** states an mFG of **4-6 or more** indicates hirsutism overall, while acknowledging ethnic variation; self-treatment (shaving, threading, waxing) lowers the score, so ask what was done before the visit. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`Severity bands in common use: **mild 8-15, moderate 16-25, severe above 25** - useful for choosing between cosmetic measures alone and drug therapy. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Patient-important hirsutism**: the Endocrine Society advises treating **any degree of hirsutism that distresses the woman**, even with a low score - the score guides investigation, distress guides treatment. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Aetiological grouping for the viva: **functional ovarian (PCOS)**, **adrenal enzymatic (NCCAH)**, **neoplastic (ovarian or adrenal tumour)**, **other endocrine**, **drug-induced** and **idiopathic**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Tempo of onset** is the single most useful question: PCOS and idiopathic hirsutism begin **around puberty and progress slowly**; onset over **months**, after the mid-20s or **after menopause** suggests a tumour. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Menstrual history**: oligomenorrhoea (cycles over 35 days or fewer than 8 a year) suggests PCOS; regular cycles with hirsutism suggest idiopathic hirsutism, but **one-third of women with regular cycles are anovulatory** - check a mid-luteal progesterone. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Symptoms of virilisation**: voice change, receding hairline, clitoral enlargement, increased libido, breast shrinkage - ask directly because women rarely volunteer them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Associated features**: acne, seborrhoea, female-pattern hair loss, weight gain, acanthosis, infertility and **features of Cushing, acromegaly or hyperprolactinaemia** (galactorrhoea). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Drug and supplement history**: danazol, anabolic steroids, partner's testosterone gel, valproate, DHEA - and minoxidil, phenytoin or ciclosporin, which cause hypertrichosis rather than hirsutism. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Family history** of hirsutism, PCOS, diabetes or CAH, and **ethnic background** (NCCAH is commoner in some communities with consanguinity). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychosocial impact**: hirsutism causes significant depression, anxiety and social withdrawal - screen for mood and eating disorders, and ask about **how much time she spends removing hair**. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Reproductive plans**: desire for pregnancy changes the treatment completely (no antiandrogens, ovulation induction instead of COCP). [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Score **all 9 mFG areas** with the patient undressed and note recent hair removal; photograph if possible to document the baseline. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Clitoromegaly**: a **clitoral index (length x width) above 35 mm2** or a glans width over 10 mm indicates significant androgen excess and virilisation. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Temporal recession, deep voice, increased muscle bulk and breast atrophy** - each points to severe hyperandrogenism and a possible tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**BMI, waist circumference and acanthosis nigricans** in the neck and axillae - markers of insulin resistance and PCOS. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Cushingoid signs**: proximal myopathy, wide purple striae over 1 cm, easy bruising, facial plethora, dorsocervical pad - the discriminating ones are the myopathy, striae and bruising. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Abdominal and pelvic examination**: a palpable adnexal mass suggests an ovarian tumour; an abdominal mass may be an adrenocortical carcinoma; check blood pressure (11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, Cushing). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Galactorrhoea, visual fields and acral enlargement** to screen for prolactinoma or acromegaly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**PCOS**: peripubertal onset, slow progression, oligomenorrhoea, polycystic ovarian morphology, mildly raised testosterone (usually below 150 ng/dL), obesity and acanthosis. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Idiopathic hirsutism**: regular ovulatory cycles, **normal androgens** and normal ovaries; **idiopathic hyperandrogenaemia** has raised androgens but regular ovulatory cycles and normal ovaries. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**NCCAH**: indistinguishable clinically from PCOS; **raised morning follicular-phase 17-OHP**; family history and consanguinity raise suspicion. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`**Androgen-secreting ovarian tumour**: rapid virilisation, **testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL** with a normal DHEAS, adnexal mass (often small Leydig tumours under 1 cm that are hard to image). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Adrenal tumour (usually carcinoma)**: rapid virilisation, **DHEAS above 700 mcg/dL**, often co-secreting cortisol, large adrenal mass on CT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ovarian hyperthecosis**: postmenopausal, progressive severe hirsutism or virilisation with testosterone that may reach tumour range but **bilateral enlarged solid ovaries** and no discrete mass. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypertrichosis**: diffuse, non-sexual pattern, drug history (minoxidil, phenytoin, ciclosporin) - not an androgen problem. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`Test androgens when the **mFG is abnormal** or hirsutism is **focal but distressing with other features**, and always when onset is sudden, rapid or with virilisation; mild isolated hirsutism with regular cycles can be treated empirically after a total testosterone. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Total testosterone**, early morning, **days 3-10 of the cycle** (or any day in amenorrhoea), off COCP for **3 months**, ideally by **LC-MS/MS** - direct immunoassays are unreliable at female levels. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Free testosterone** by equilibrium dialysis or **calculated free testosterone / free androgen index** (from total testosterone and SHBG) is more sensitive than total testosterone in PCOS. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Tumour thresholds**: **total testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL (5.2-6.9 nmol/L)** or above 2.5 times the upper limit, or **DHEAS above 700 mcg/dL** - these call for imaging regardless of symptoms, though some tumours have lower levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Morning 17-hydroxyprogesterone** in the follicular phase: below **200 ng/dL** makes NCCAH unlikely, **above 1000 ng/dL** is diagnostic, and **200-1000 ng/dL** needs an **ACTH (250 mcg cosyntropin) stimulation test** - a 60-minute 17-OHP above about 1000 ng/dL confirms NCCAH. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`**Screen for other causes**: TSH, prolactin, and when suspected **IGF-1** (acromegaly) and **1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test** (Cushing - cortisol above 1.8 mcg/dL is abnormal). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Metabolic screen in PCOS**: 75 g OGTT or HbA1c, fasting lipids, BMI and BP - Indian women with PCOS have high rates of prediabetes even when lean. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Imaging**: **transvaginal ultrasound** (PCOM: 20 or more follicles per ovary or ovarian volume 10 mL or more) and for suspected tumour **MRI pelvis**, **CT adrenal** (adrenal carcinoma is usually over 4-6 cm); **selective ovarian and adrenal venous sampling** in a tertiary centre when imaging is negative. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Postmenopausal virilisation**: a **GnRH agonist suppression test** - testosterone suppression favours hyperthecosis or LH-dependent tissue, persistence favours an autonomous tumour (not absolute). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Explain and set expectations**: drugs prevent new terminal hair and slow growth; visible benefit takes **6 months**, and most women still need hair removal - this single conversation decides adherence. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Weight loss of 5-10%** in overweight women with PCOS lowers free testosterone and improves cycles; combine diet and **150 minutes a week** of exercise. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Direct hair removal** for all who want it: shaving (does not make hair thicker), threading (common in India), waxing, depilatory creams and electrolysis (permanent, slow, for small areas). [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Photoepilation (laser or IPL)** gives long-term reduction; in **darker Indian skin (Fitzpatrick IV-VI)** use the **long-pulse Nd:YAG 1064 nm** or diode laser to reduce burns and pigmentation; it works poorly on white, grey or red hair. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**First-line drug: combined oral contraceptive** for most premenopausal women not seeking pregnancy - e.g. ethinylestradiol **20-30 mcg** with **levonorgestrel, desogestrel or drospirenone 3 mg** once daily for 21 days with a 7-day break, continued long term. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Add an antiandrogen after 6 months** if the response to COCP is not enough: **spironolactone 50-100 mg twice daily** (start 25-50 mg/day, maximum 200 mg/day) - the commonest choice in India. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Alternatives**: **cyproterone acetate** 2 mg in the COCP (ethinylestradiol 35 mcg) - effective but higher **VTE risk**, so not first choice; **finasteride 2.5-5 mg daily** - always with contraception. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Topical eflornithine 13.9% cream twice daily** to the face slows facial hair (ornithine decarboxylase inhibitor) - an adjunct to laser, where available; hair regrows within 8 weeks of stopping. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Metformin** is not recommended for hirsutism alone; use it for **insulin resistance, IGT or BMI 25 or more** in PCOS, starting 500 mg daily and building to 1500-2000 mg/day. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**NCCAH**: COCP plus antiandrogen as for PCOS; **glucocorticoids** (hydrocortisone 10-20 mg/day in divided doses or prednisolone 2.5-5 mg at night) are reserved for infertility or when the woman prefers them. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / tertiary care`,points:[`Hirsutism itself is never an emergency; **admission or urgent referral** is for suspected **adrenocortical carcinoma** (large mass, cortisol co-secretion, hypokalaemia) or an ovarian tumour needing surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ovarian androgen-secreting tumour**: unilateral **salpingo-oophorectomy** (fertility-sparing) or total hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy after childbearing; testosterone normalises within days. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Adrenocortical carcinoma**: open adrenalectomy at a high-volume centre, **perioperative glucocorticoid cover** if cortisol co-secretion, and mitotane adjuvant therapy as decided by the tumour board. [ESE-ENSAT 2018]`,`**Ovarian hyperthecosis**: bilateral oophorectomy after menopause, or **GnRH agonist** depot (e.g. leuprolide 3.75 mg monthly) if surgery is unsuitable. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**GnRH agonists** for severe hyperandrogenaemia not responding to COCP plus antiandrogen - with oestrogen add-back to protect bone. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Flutamide is avoided** because of dose-related **hepatotoxicity** (including fatal hepatitis). [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: stop all antiandrogens (risk of **feminising a male fetus**); new virilisation in pregnancy suggests **luteoma** (solid, often bilateral, regresses after delivery) or hyperreactio luteinalis - manage expectantly unless torsion or uncertainty. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Women seeking pregnancy**: no COCP or antiandrogen; use cosmetic measures and treat anovulation with **letrozole 2.5-7.5 mg on days 2-6**, the first-line ovulation inducer in PCOS. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Adolescents**: diagnose PCOS only with **both** irregular cycles at least 2-3 years after menarche and hyperandrogenism - ultrasound is **not used** within 8 years of menarche; COCP can still treat symptoms. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Postmenopausal women**: new or worsening hirsutism needs **testosterone, DHEAS and imaging** - think hyperthecosis or tumour, not PCOS. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**COCP contraindications** (WHO MEC category 4): migraine with aura, smoker over 35 years (15+ cigarettes a day), previous VTE, uncontrolled hypertension, breast cancer - use spironolactone with a non-oestrogen method (copper IUD or LNG-IUS). [WHO MEC 2015]`,`**CKD or hyperkalaemia risk**: spironolactone can raise potassium - check potassium if eGFR is reduced or ACE inhibitor/ARB is co-prescribed; routine potassium checks are not needed in healthy young women. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Diabetes or prediabetes with PCOS**: add metformin; GLP-1 agonists help weight and may lower androgens. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Psychological morbidity** - depression, anxiety, poor body image and reduced quality of life - is the main burden and is often missed. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**PCOS-linked metabolic risk**: type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, MASLD, sleep apnoea and endometrial hyperplasia from unopposed oestrogen. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Laser complications** in darker skin: burns, **post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation** and paradoxical hair growth. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Drug adverse effects**: COCP - VTE; spironolactone - polyuria, irregular bleeding (without COCP), hyperkalaemia; finasteride - teratogenicity and mood change. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Prognosis**: about **60-70%** of women achieve a meaningful reduction in hair growth on COCP plus antiandrogen over 6-12 months; hirsutism recurs when treatment stops. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Adrenocortical carcinoma** carries a poor prognosis (5-year survival under 40% in advanced stages); ovarian Sertoli-Leydig tumours are mostly early stage with good outcome. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** for rapid onset, virilisation, **testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL**, **DHEAS above 700 mcg/dL**, adnexal or adrenal mass, or Cushingoid features. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Refer routinely** to endocrinology for confirmed NCCAH (genetic counselling), and to gynaecology for infertility. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`**Review at 6 months** with a repeat mFG score and patient-reported change; change or add drugs only after this interval. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`On **spironolactone**, check potassium and creatinine at baseline and after starting if there is renal impairment or interacting drugs. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`In PCOS, repeat **glucose testing every 1-3 years** depending on risk, and lipids, BP and weight yearly. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`Attempt a **trial off therapy** after about 1-2 years of good control if the woman wishes; restart if hair growth returns. [Endocrine Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Counsel that **threading, waxing and shaving are safe** and do not worsen hirsutism - a common Indian myth. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Lifestyle and weight control from adolescence** reduce insulin resistance and PCOS expression; schools and **Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK)** adolescent clinics are the place to start. [MoHFW RKSK]`,`Use the PCOS visit for **NCD risk screening** - BP, glucose, waist - in line with **NP-NCD** opportunistic screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs for those 30 or older. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Genetic counselling for NCCAH**: about 2/3 of affected women are compound heterozygous carrying one severe allele - **partner screening** helps assess the risk of classic CAH in offspring. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`Address **body image and mental health** - validated distress and depression screening (PHQ-9) is part of PCOS care. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**International Evidence-based PCOS Guideline 2023** (Monash/ESHRE/ASRM): mFG **4-6 or more** indicates hirsutism; **AMH** may be used instead of ultrasound for PCOM in adults (not adolescents); calculated free testosterone, FAI or bioavailable testosterone preferred for biochemical hyperandrogenism. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**PCOS Guideline 2023 on COCP**: no specific COCP preferred; use the **lowest effective ethinylestradiol dose (20-30 mcg)**; **35 mcg EE plus cyproterone** is second-line due to VTE risk; antiandrogens added after **6 months** of suboptimal response. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`**Endocrine Society 2018 hirsutism guideline** (still current): test androgens in abnormal mFG; COCP first-line; **against antiandrogen monotherapy without contraception**; **against insulin-lowering drugs** for hirsutism alone; photoepilation for those who want it. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`**Endocrine Society 2018 CAH guideline**: morning 17-OHP screen with cosyntropin stimulation for borderline values and genotyping for counselling; glucocorticoids not first-line for NCCAH hirsutism. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`**LC-MS/MS** is now the recommended method for female androgen measurement; many Indian labs still use immunoassays - interpret borderline values cautiously. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to a woman with hirsutism'** - write definition, mFG, causes in a table, history (tempo), examination (virilisation), investigation algorithm, and treatment ladder. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Common mistake: **confusing hirsutism with hypertrichosis** - antiandrogens do nothing for drug-induced hypertrichosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **spironolactone without contraception** - the examiner wants the fetal feminisation risk stated. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Common mistake: judging treatment at 6 weeks - **hair needs 6 months**. [Endocrine Society 2018]`,`Viva: 'Which single hormone localises an adrenal source?' - **DHEAS** (above 700 mcg/dL). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Why is cyproterone not first-line?' - its COCP partner carries **higher VTE risk** than levonorgestrel-containing pills. [International PCOS Guideline 2023]`,`Viva: 'What does rapid virilisation with normal DHEAS and high testosterone suggest?' - an **ovarian androgen-secreting tumour**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for hirsutism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Combined oral contraceptive (EE 20-30 mcg + levonorgestrel/desogestrel/drospirenone)`,`1 tablet orally daily (21/7 or continuous)`,`Assess at 6 months; continue long term`,`VTE, migraine with aura, smoker over 35, uncontrolled HTN, breast cancer are contraindications`],[`EE 35 mcg + cyproterone acetate 2 mg`,`1 tablet orally daily`,`Second line; stop when control achieved if possible`,`Higher VTE risk than levonorgestrel pills; hepatotoxicity at high doses`],[`Spironolactone`,`50-100 mg orally twice daily (start 25-50 mg/day; max 200 mg/day)`,`Add after 6 months of COCP; review at 6 months`,`Teratogenic (feminises male fetus) - needs contraception; hyperkalaemia in CKD; menstrual spotting; polyuria`],[`Finasteride`,`2.5-5 mg orally daily`,`6-12 months`,`Teratogenic - needs contraception; mood change; less effective than spironolactone in some trials`],[`Eflornithine 13.9% cream`,`Topically to face twice daily`,`Effect in 6-8 weeks; regrowth 8 weeks after stopping`,`Burning, acne; facial use only; availability limited in India`],[`Metformin`,`500 mg daily, build to 1500-2000 mg/day with meals`,`Long term for metabolic indications`,`Not for hirsutism alone; GI upset, B12 deficiency`],[`GnRH agonist (leuprolide depot)`,`3.75 mg IM monthly with oestrogen add-back`,`Severe hyperandrogenaemia, hyperthecosis`,`Bone loss, menopausal symptoms, cost`],[`Hydrocortisone / prednisolone (NCCAH)`,`Hydrocortisone 10-20 mg/day divided or prednisolone 2.5-5 mg at night`,`Mainly for fertility in NCCAH`,`Cushingoid effects, bone loss; needs stress-dose education`]]},{heading:`Causes of hirsutism and the discriminating clue`,columns:[`Cause`,`Share of cases`,`Clinical clue`,`Biochemical clue`],rows:[[`PCOS`,`70-80%`,`Peripubertal onset, oligomenorrhoea, obesity, acanthosis`,`Mildly high testosterone/FAI, testosterone usually under 150 ng/dL`],[`Idiopathic hirsutism`,`5-20%`,`Regular ovulatory cycles, normal ovaries`,`Normal androgens`],[`Non-classic CAH`,`1-5%`,`PCOS-like, family history, consanguinity`,`Morning 17-OHP over 200 ng/dL; post-ACTH over 1000`],[`Ovarian tumour`,`Under 0.5%`,`Rapid virilisation, adnexal mass`,`Testosterone over 150-200 ng/dL, normal DHEAS`],[`Adrenal carcinoma`,`Rare`,`Rapid virilisation, Cushingoid, abdominal mass`,`DHEAS over 700 mcg/dL, cortisol co-secretion`],[`Ovarian hyperthecosis`,`Rare`,`Postmenopausal, progressive, bilateral solid enlarged ovaries`,`Testosterone may be tumour range; suppresses with GnRH agonist`],[`Cushing, acromegaly, prolactinoma, hypothyroidism`,`Rare`,`Features of the underlying disease`,`DST, IGF-1, prolactin, TSH`],[`Drugs (danazol, anabolic steroids, partner's testosterone gel)`,`Variable`,`Drug history`,`Suppressed LH/FSH with exogenous androgens`]]},{heading:`Modified Ferriman-Gallwey scoring and cut-offs`,columns:[`Item`,`Detail`],rows:[[`Areas scored`,`9: upper lip, chin, chest, upper abdomen, lower abdomen, upper arm, thigh, upper back, lower back`],[`Score per area`,`0 (none) to 4 (frank virile); total 0-36`],[`White and Black women (US/UK)`,`8 or more abnormal`],[`East Asian women`,`4-6 or more abnormal`],[`Mediterranean, Hispanic, Middle Eastern women`,`9-10 or more abnormal`],[`Indian practice`,`8 or more conventionally; treat lower scores with distress`],[`International PCOS Guideline 2023`,`4-6 or more indicates hirsutism, with ethnic variation`],[`Severity (common usage)`,`Mild 8-15, moderate 16-25, severe over 25`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Total testosterone (LC-MS/MS)`,`All with abnormal mFG; early morning, day 3-10, off COCP 3 months`,`Over 150-200 ng/dL or over 2.5x ULN suggests tumour`],[`SHBG with calculated free testosterone / FAI`,`Normal total testosterone but suspected PCOS`,`FAI raised in PCOS; low SHBG in insulin resistance`],[`DHEAS`,`Rapid onset or virilisation`,`Over 700 mcg/dL suggests adrenal tumour`],[`17-OHP (morning, follicular)`,`All hirsute women at risk, or PCOS-like picture`,`Under 200 ng/dL unlikely; 200-1000 do ACTH test; over 1000 diagnostic of NCCAH`],[`TSH, prolactin`,`All`,`Exclude hypothyroidism and hyperprolactinaemia`],[`1 mg overnight DST`,`Cushingoid features`,`Cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL needs further tests`],[`Transvaginal ultrasound / AMH`,`PCOS work-up in adults`,`20 or more follicles per ovary or volume 10 mL or more`],[`CT adrenal / MRI pelvis; venous sampling`,`Tumour-range androgens`,`Locate adrenal or ovarian source`]]}],redFlags:[`Rapid onset or progression of hirsutism over months`,`Signs of virilisation: clitoromegaly, deep voice, temporal balding, breast atrophy`,`Total testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL or DHEAS above 700 mcg/dL`,`New hirsutism after menopause`,`Palpable adnexal or abdominal mass`,`Cushingoid features (proximal myopathy, wide purple striae, bruising) with hirsutism`,`New virilisation in pregnancy`],pearls:[`Hirsutism = male-pattern terminal hair (androgen-dependent); hypertrichosis = diffuse hair (not androgen-dependent).`,`Tempo tells the tumour: slow since puberty = PCOS; fast over months = tumour.`,`Testosterone over 200 ng/dL = ovary; DHEAS over 700 mcg/dL = adrenal.`,`PCOS causes 70-80% of hirsutism; always measure a morning 17-OHP to catch NCCAH.`,`Six months before you judge any hirsutism drug - that is one hair cycle.`,`COCP first, add spironolactone at 6 months - and never an antiandrogen without contraception.`,`Laser for darker Indian skin: long-pulse Nd:YAG 1064 nm.`,`Metformin treats the metabolism, not the hair.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old postmenopausal woman reports rapidly progressive facial hair growth, frontal balding and a hoarse voice over 6 months. Total testosterone is 310 ng/dL and DHEAS is normal. Adrenal CT is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Polycystic ovary syndrome`,`Non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia`,`Ovarian androgen-secreting tumour or hyperthecosis`,`Adrenocortical carcinoma`],answer:2,explanation:`Rapid virilisation after menopause with testosterone above 200 ng/dL and a normal DHEAS localises the source to the ovary - a Leydig or steroid-cell tumour or ovarian hyperthecosis. PCOS begins around puberty and does not cause tumour-range testosterone; NCCAH also starts early and raises 17-OHP; adrenocortical carcinoma typically raises DHEAS and shows a mass on CT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-q2`,stem:`According to the Endocrine Society 2018 guideline, what is the first-line drug therapy for most premenopausal women with hirsutism who do not wish to conceive?`,options:[`Combined oral contraceptive pill`,`Spironolactone monotherapy`,`Metformin`,`Flutamide`],answer:0,explanation:`The COCP raises SHBG and suppresses LH-driven ovarian androgen production and also provides the contraception needed if an antiandrogen is added later. Spironolactone alone is advised against without reliable contraception because it can feminise a male fetus; metformin is not recommended for hirsutism alone; flutamide is avoided because of hepatotoxicity.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-q3`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman with hirsutism and oligomenorrhoea has a morning follicular-phase 17-hydroxyprogesterone of 480 ng/dL. What is the next step?`,options:[`Diagnose PCOS and start a COCP`,`Start prednisolone for congenital adrenal hyperplasia`,`Order CT of the adrenal glands`,`Perform an ACTH (cosyntropin) stimulation test`],answer:3,explanation:`A basal 17-OHP between 200 and 1000 ng/dL is indeterminate for non-classic CAH, so a 250 mcg cosyntropin stimulation test is done; a stimulated value above about 1000 ng/dL confirms it. Labelling PCOS now would miss NCCAH; starting glucocorticoids before confirmation is premature and they are not first-line for hirsutism; adrenal CT is for tumour-range DHEAS or androgens, not for an enzyme defect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-q4`,stem:`A woman with hirsutism has been on a COCP for 3 months and is disappointed with the result. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Switch immediately to a cyproterone-containing pill`,`Continue for at least 6 months before judging, using cosmetic measures meanwhile`,`Stop the COCP and start finasteride alone`,`Add metformin 2 g daily for hirsutism`],answer:1,explanation:`Hair follicles cycle over several months, so any hirsutism treatment needs at least 6 months before its effect can be judged; only then is an antiandrogen added. Switching to cyproterone at 3 months is premature and carries higher VTE risk; finasteride alone without contraception is unsafe in a woman who may conceive; metformin has little effect on hirsutism.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-q5`,stem:`A woman with Fitzpatrick type V skin asks about laser hair removal for chin hirsutism. Which laser is the safest choice?`,options:[`Ruby laser 694 nm`,`Alexandrite laser 755 nm`,`Long-pulse Nd:YAG laser 1064 nm`,`Intense pulsed light at short wavelengths`],answer:2,explanation:`The longer 1064 nm wavelength of the Nd:YAG laser penetrates deeper and is absorbed less by epidermal melanin, reducing burns and pigmentation in dark skin. Ruby and alexandrite lasers and short-wavelength IPL are absorbed strongly by epidermal melanin and are better suited to fair skin with dark hair.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define hirsutism. Discuss the causes, evaluation and management of a 26-year-old woman presenting with hirsutism.`,openingLines:[`Hirsutism is excess terminal hair in a male, androgen-dependent pattern in a woman, graded by the modified Ferriman-Gallwey score (abnormal 8 or more conventionally; 4-6 or more by the 2023 PCOS guideline).`,`PCOS causes 70-80% of cases; rapid onset, virilisation or testosterone above 150-200 ng/dL indicates a possible tumour.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and scoring`,points:[`Distinguish from hypertrichosis (diffuse, non-androgenic) and virilisation (clitoromegaly, deep voice, balding, muscle bulk).`,`mFG: 9 areas, 0-4 each; cut-off varies with ethnicity - 8 or more in Indian practice.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Ovarian: PCOS, hyperthecosis, androgen-secreting tumour.`,`Adrenal: NCCAH, adrenocortical carcinoma, Cushing.`,`Other: hyperprolactinaemia, acromegaly, hypothyroidism, drugs (danazol, anabolic steroids), idiopathic.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Tempo of onset, menstrual history, virilising symptoms, drugs, family history, fertility plans.`,`mFG score, clitoral index, BMI, waist, acanthosis, Cushingoid signs, galactorrhoea, pelvic and abdominal mass.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Morning day 3-10 total testosterone (LC-MS/MS), SHBG/FAI, 17-OHP, TSH, prolactin.`,`DHEAS and imaging if rapid onset or virilisation; 1 mg DST and IGF-1 if suspected.`,`Ultrasound/AMH for PCOS; OGTT and lipids as metabolic screen.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Counsel: 6-month time frame; weight loss 5-10%; cosmetic removal and laser (Nd:YAG for dark skin).`,`COCP first-line; add spironolactone 50-100 mg twice daily at 6 months if needed, with contraception.`,`Eflornithine cream for face; metformin only for metabolic indications.`,`Treat cause: surgery for tumours, glucocorticoids for NCCAH infertility, GnRH agonist for hyperthecosis.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Re-score at 6 months; screen for mood disorder; metabolic review yearly in PCOS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: hirsutism - testosterone/17-OHP/DHEAS - PCOS vs NCCAH vs tumour`,`Table of causes with discriminating clue`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mFG`,marks:1},{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with flow chart`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Ferriman-Gallwey`,`PCOS`,`NCCAH`,`17-OHP`,`DHEAS`,`spironolactone`,`COCP`,`laser`]},{id:`endocrine-hirsutism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate hirsutism due to PCOS from hirsutism due to an androgen-secreting tumour.`,openingLines:[`Both cause androgen excess, but PCOS is a slow functional disorder beginning at puberty while a tumour causes rapid virilisation with tumour-range androgens.`,`The key discriminators are tempo, virilisation and the testosterone/DHEAS level.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`PCOS: peripubertal, slow, oligomenorrhoea, obesity, acanthosis, no virilisation.`,`Tumour: any age (often older or postmenopausal), months of progression, clitoromegaly, deep voice, balding.`]},{heading:`Biochemistry`,points:[`PCOS: testosterone mildly raised (usually under 150 ng/dL), raised FAI, low SHBG.`,`Ovarian tumour: testosterone over 150-200 ng/dL; adrenal tumour: DHEAS over 700 mcg/dL.`]},{heading:`Imaging and treatment`,points:[`PCOS: polycystic ovarian morphology or high AMH; treat with lifestyle, COCP, antiandrogen.`,`Tumour: MRI pelvis or CT adrenal, venous sampling if negative; surgical removal.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: PCOS vs androgen-secreting tumour`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Biochemical cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging and treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PCOS`,`tumour`,`testosterone 200`,`DHEAS 700`,`virilisation`]}],references:[`Martin KA et al. Evaluation and Treatment of Hirsutism in Premenopausal Women: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2018`,`Teede HJ et al. International Evidence-based Guideline for the Assessment and Management of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome, 2023`,`Speiser PW et al. Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia Due to Steroid 21-Hydroxylase Deficiency: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2018`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`FOGSI Good Clinical Practice Recommendations on PCOS, 2020`,`WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use, 5th edition, 2015`]}),l.push({id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia`,title:`Hypoglycaemia in people without diabetes: insulinoma and others`,oneLiner:`In a person without diabetes, hypoglycaemia is investigated only when **Whipple's triad** is documented (symptoms, **plasma glucose below 55 mg/dL**, relief with glucose); **endogenous hyperinsulinism** is proved by **insulin 3 microU/mL or more, C-peptide 0.6 ng/mL or more and proinsulin 5 pmol/L or more** at that low glucose with a **negative sulfonylurea screen**, and **insulinoma** is then localised (EUS, CT/MRI, GLP-1 receptor PET) and enucleated.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`non-diabetic hypoglycaemia`,`Whipple triad`,`insulinoma`,`72-hour fast`,`C-peptide`,`proinsulin`,`beta-hydroxybutyrate`,`sulfonylurea screen`,`factitious hypoglycaemia`,`post-bariatric hypoglycaemia`,`dumping syndrome`,`NIPHS`,`insulin autoimmune syndrome`,`Hirata disease`,`IGF-2 tumour hypoglycaemia`,`NICTH`,`diazoxide`,`selective arterial calcium stimulation`,`GLP-1 receptor PET`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Whipple's triad**: (1) symptoms or signs consistent with hypoglycaemia, (2) a **low plasma glucose measured** at the time (by a reliable method, not a glucometer), and (3) **relief of symptoms when glucose is raised** - only this justifies evaluation in a non-diabetic person. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Threshold**: in non-diabetics, investigate when plasma glucose is **below 55 mg/dL (3.0 mmol/L)** with symptoms; healthy people, especially young women and children, can have fasting values in the 50s without disease. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`Hypoglycaemia without diabetes is **uncommon**; in hospitals it is mostly due to **drugs, sepsis, liver or renal failure, malnutrition and alcohol**, while a well-looking outpatient needs a search for **endogenous hyperinsulinism**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Insulinoma** is the commonest functional pancreatic NET, with an incidence of about **1-4 per million per year**, median age about 50, slight female excess. [ENETS 2023]`,`The **'rule of 10s'**: about 90% are **benign, solitary and under 2 cm**, about 10% malignant, about 10% multiple and **5-10% part of MEN1**; they are evenly distributed through head, body and tail. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-bariatric hypoglycaemia** after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass or sleeve gastrectomy is increasingly seen in India as bariatric surgery grows; severe forms affect under 1% but milder symptoms are much commoner. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`In India, **malaria (falciparum and quinine), sepsis, alcohol, starvation and drugs** remain important causes of hypoglycaemia in emergency departments. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Ill or medicated person** - drugs (insulin, sulfonylureas, alcohol, **quinine, pentamidine, cibenzoline, fluoroquinolones** such as gatifloxacin and levofloxacin, beta-blockers, tramadol, indomethacin), critical illness (sepsis, hepatic, renal or cardiac failure, starvation), hormone deficiency (cortisol, GH in children) and **non-islet cell tumour hypoglycaemia**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Seemingly well person** - **endogenous hyperinsulinism**: insulinoma, **non-insulinoma pancreatogenous hypoglycaemia syndrome (NIPHS)**, post-bariatric hypoglycaemia, **insulin autoimmune syndrome**, and **insulin secretagogue** ingestion (accidental, pharmacy dispensing error or factitious). [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Accidental or factitious** hypoglycaemia: commoner in **health-care workers** and relatives of people with diabetes; dispensing errors (look-alike drug names) also occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Insulin autoimmune syndrome (Hirata disease)**: autoantibodies to insulin release bound insulin late after meals; linked to **HLA-DRB1*04:06** and to sulfhydryl drugs - **methimazole, alpha-lipoic acid** (a common Indian supplement), clopidogrel. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Non-islet cell tumour hypoglycaemia (NICTH)**: large mesenchymal tumours (solitary fibrous tumour, sarcoma) or hepatocellular carcinoma secrete **big IGF-2**, which activates the insulin receptor. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Alcohol** inhibits gluconeogenesis; hypoglycaemia occurs 6-36 hours after a binge in a person with depleted glycogen - a common Indian emergency picture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adrenal insufficiency and hypopituitarism** cause fasting hypoglycaemia mainly in children; in adults it is a late, uncommon feature. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the counter-regulatory cascade`,points:[`As glucose falls, the body defends it in order: **insulin secretion falls** (around 80 mg/dL), **glucagon** rises (65-70 mg/dL), **adrenaline** rises (65-70 mg/dL), then **cortisol and GH** - symptoms begin around **50-55 mg/dL** and cognition fails below about 50. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurogenic (autonomic) symptoms** - adrenergic tremor, palpitations, anxiety and cholinergic sweating, hunger, paraesthesia - warn the patient; **neuroglycopenic symptoms** - confusion, behaviour change, seizures, coma - reflect brain glucose deprivation. [Harrison 22e]`,`In insulinoma, insulin release is **autonomous**, so insulin stays inappropriately detectable even when glucose is low - this is the basis of the **fast and the biochemical criteria**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Insulin suppresses lipolysis and ketogenesis**, so beta-hydroxybutyrate stays low (**2.7 mmol/L or less**) and **glycogen is preserved** - glucagon then releases it, raising glucose by **25 mg/dL or more**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**C-peptide** is co-secreted with endogenous insulin in equimolar amounts; injected insulin contains no C-peptide - so **high insulin with low C-peptide means exogenous insulin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Repeated hypoglycaemia causes **hypoglycaemia-associated autonomic failure** - warning symptoms are lost and the patient presents with confusion or seizures, often misdiagnosed as **epilepsy or psychiatric illness**. [Harrison 22e]`,`After gastric bypass, rapid nutrient delivery causes an **exaggerated GLP-1 and insulin surge** 1-3 hours after meals with **beta-cell hyperfunction**, producing late postprandial hypoglycaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**IGF-2** from tumours suppresses insulin, GH and IGF-1 while activating insulin receptors - hence **low insulin, low C-peptide, low beta-hydroxybutyrate** with a positive glucagon response. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Endocrine Society clinical classification**: hypoglycaemia in the **ill or medicated** person versus the **seemingly well** person - the most examined framework. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**By timing**: **fasting** (insulinoma, NICTH, hormone deficiency, liver failure, drugs) versus **postprandial** (post-bariatric, NIPHS, insulin autoimmune syndrome) - insulinoma can be both in about 20%. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**By insulin status**: **insulin-mediated** (insulinoma, sulfonylurea, exogenous insulin, NIPHS, post-bariatric, autoimmune) versus **non-insulin-mediated** (IGF-2, alcohol, hormone deficiency, starvation, organ failure). [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Insulinoma by function and syndrome**: sporadic (about 90%) versus **MEN1-associated** (younger, often multiple, higher recurrence). [ENETS 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 grading** of pancreatic NETs applies to insulinoma: **G1 Ki-67 under 3%, G2 3-20%, G3 above 20%**. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Typical insulinoma history: **episodes on fasting, after missed meals or exercise**, early morning confusion, and relief with food - patients **eat frequently and gain weight**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuroglycopenia dominates** in insulinoma: odd behaviour, amnesia, diplopia, seizures, 'drunken' episodes - average diagnostic delay is years and many are first labelled epileptic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postprandial symptoms 1-3 hours after meals**, with a history of gastric bypass or sleeve, suggest post-bariatric hypoglycaemia; **early dumping** (within 30 minutes: bloating, palpitations, flushing) is a different, volume-driven syndrome. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Drug history in detail**: diabetic family members' tablets at home, recent antibiotic (fluoroquinolone), antimalarial (quinine), pentamidine, supplements (alpha-lipoic acid), occupation in health care. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Alcohol**: binge drinking with little food in the preceding day. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptoms of a large tumour** (abdominal or chest mass, weight loss) suggest NICTH; features of **MEN1** (renal stones, hypercalcaemia, peptic ulcers, galactorrhoea, family history) suggest MEN1-insulinoma. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Adrenergic-only symptoms** after meals with normal measured glucose are **not hypoglycaemia** ('idiopathic postprandial syndrome') - avoid the label of reactive hypoglycaemia without Whipple's triad. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`During an episode: **sweating, tremor, tachycardia, pallor**, confusion, focal neurological deficit (can mimic stroke), seizures or coma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Between episodes, insulinoma patients usually look well and are often **overweight** from frequent eating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatomegaly, jaundice, ascites** - liver failure; **cachexia with a large mass** - NICTH. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Hyperpigmentation, hypotension** - primary adrenal insufficiency; features of hypopituitarism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bariatric scars** and weight history; injection marks or hidden tablets in factitious cases. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Bedside glucometer** reading during symptoms is the first step, but a glucometer is unreliable below 70 mg/dL - **draw a venous sample** before giving glucose. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Epilepsy**: insulinoma presenting with seizures; check glucose in every seizure and in every 'resistant epilepsy'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychiatric or functional episodes, panic attacks**: adrenergic symptoms with normal glucose during the event. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Pseudohypoglycaemia**: falsely low glucose from **delayed sample processing** (glycolysis in a tube without fluoride), **leucocytosis, polycythaemia** or poor peripheral perfusion - patient is asymptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stroke or TIA**: focal deficit that resolves with dextrose is hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Factitious insulin**: high insulin with **suppressed C-peptide**; **sulfonylurea**: biochemically identical to insulinoma until the **sulfonylurea screen** is positive. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Insulin autoimmune syndrome**: **very high insulin (often over 100 microU/mL)**, insulin/C-peptide molar ratio over 1, and positive insulin antibodies. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Critical sample** during a spontaneous episode (before treatment): plasma glucose, **insulin, C-peptide, proinsulin, beta-hydroxybutyrate**, sulfonylurea/meglitinide screen and insulin antibodies; then give **glucagon 1 mg IV** and measure glucose at 10, 20 and 30 minutes. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Diagnostic criteria for endogenous hyperinsulinism** at glucose **below 55 mg/dL**: insulin **3 microU/mL or more**, C-peptide **0.6 ng/mL (0.2 nmol/L) or more**, proinsulin **5 pmol/L or more**, beta-hydroxybutyrate **2.7 mmol/L or less**, and glucose rise **25 mg/dL or more after glucagon**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**72-hour supervised fast** in hospital if no spontaneous episode is captured: stop non-essential drugs, allow calorie-free fluids, check glucose every 6 hours and every 1-2 hours once below 60 mg/dL; **end the fast at glucose 45 mg/dL or less with symptoms** (or below 55 mg/dL if Whipple's triad was documented earlier), or at 72 hours. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`About **95-99% of insulinomas** become hypoglycaemic within 72 hours, and about two-thirds within 24 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mixed-meal test** (the meal that provokes symptoms, or a liquid mixed meal) for **postprandial** symptoms, sampling for 5 hours - **never use an OGTT**, which is unvalidated and causes false positives. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Patterns**: insulinoma and sulfonylurea - high insulin, high C-peptide, high proinsulin; exogenous insulin - high insulin, **low C-peptide**; IGF-2 - all low, positive glucagon response, **IGF-2:IGF-1 ratio above 10**; non-insulin causes - low insulin with **high beta-hydroxybutyrate**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Localisation** after biochemical proof only: triple-phase **contrast CT** or **MRI** pancreas (sensitivity about 70-80%), **endoscopic ultrasound** (over 90% in experienced hands, permits FNA), and **GLP-1 receptor PET/CT (68Ga-exendin-4)** - the most sensitive, for occult tumours. [ENETS 2023]`,`**68Ga-DOTATATE PET** is **less useful** for benign insulinoma because many lack SSTR2, but better for **malignant** insulinoma. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Selective arterial calcium stimulation test (SACST)** with hepatic vein sampling: a **two-fold or greater insulin rise** localises the arterial territory; also separates NIPHS (diffuse response) from insulinoma; plus **intraoperative ultrasound** at surgery. [ENETS 2023]`,`Screen every insulinoma patient for **MEN1** (calcium, PTH, prolactin; germline MEN1 testing if under 30 or multiple tumours). [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Conscious patient**: **15-20 g oral glucose** (4 glucose tablets, 150-200 mL fruit juice or 3-4 teaspoons of sugar in water), recheck in 15 minutes, then a complex-carbohydrate snack. [ADA 2026]`,`**Before treating, draw blood** (a grey-top fluoride tube for glucose and a plain tube for insulin, C-peptide, sulfonylurea) whenever safely possible - this critical sample saves a 72-hour fast. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Insulinoma awaiting surgery**: frequent small meals including a bedtime snack, uncooked cornstarch, and **diazoxide 50-100 mg twice or thrice daily (3-8 mg/kg/day)** - it opens beta-cell K-ATP channels and inhibits insulin release. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Post-bariatric hypoglycaemia**: dietitian-led **low-glycaemic, small, frequent meals** with protein and fat, no simple sugars or liquids with meals; then **acarbose 25-100 mg with meals**, then diazoxide or **octreotide**; reversal surgery or feeding gastrostomy in refractory cases. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Drug-induced**: stop or change the culprit (fluoroquinolone, quinine, beta-blocker); counsel on alcohol with food. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Insulin autoimmune syndrome**: stop the triggering drug (methimazole, alpha-lipoic acid); frequent small low-carbohydrate meals; most remit within 3-6 months; **glucocorticoids or rituximab** for severe cases. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Advise **no driving** until hypoglycaemia is controlled, and teach family to give glucose or glucagon. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Unconscious or unable to swallow**: **25 g IV dextrose** (50 mL of 50% via a large vein, or **100-250 mL of 10% dextrose**, preferred to avoid extravasation injury), then a **10% dextrose infusion** with hourly glucose. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**No IV access**: **glucagon 1 mg IM/SC** (or 3 mg intranasal) - ineffective in **alcohol, starvation and liver failure** where glycogen is depleted, and can worsen sulfonylurea hypoglycaemia by stimulating insulin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thiamine 100-200 mg IV before or with glucose** in alcoholics or malnourished patients to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Sulfonylurea overdose**: admit for at least **24 hours** (longer with glibenclamide or CKD), dextrose infusion, and **octreotide 50-100 mcg SC every 6-12 hours** to stop recurrent insulin release. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV** when adrenal insufficiency or hypopituitarism is suspected; **treat sepsis and malaria** (prefer artesunate, which avoids quinine-induced hyperinsulinaemia). [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Surgery**: **laparoscopic enucleation** for small, superficial insulinomas away from the main pancreatic duct, **distal pancreatectomy** for deep body/tail lesions and pancreatoduodenectomy rarely; cure rate **over 90%** for benign tumours. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Non-surgical**: **EUS-guided radiofrequency ablation** or ethanol ablation for small insulinomas in poor surgical candidates - an accepted option in ENETS 2023. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Malignant insulinoma**: debulking, liver-directed therapy, **everolimus 10 mg daily** (also raises glucose), **177Lu-DOTATATE PRRT** if SSTR-positive, sunitinib, streptozocin or capecitabine-temozolomide; somatostatin analogues only if SSTR-positive (can **worsen** hypoglycaemia by suppressing glucagon). [ENETS 2023]`,`**NICTH**: tumour resection is curative; otherwise **glucocorticoids (prednisolone 30-60 mg/day)**, GH, or continuous dextrose and feeds. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: insulinoma may be unmasked in the first trimester or postpartum; diagnose with the same criteria, localise with **MRI or EUS** (no CT or PET), and operate in the second trimester or after delivery. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Children**: persistent hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia of infancy (congenital hyperinsulinism) and hormone deficiencies dominate; glucose requirement **over 8 mg/kg/min** suggests hyperinsulinism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly and CKD**: sulfonylurea (especially **glibenclamide**) accumulation, reduced renal gluconeogenesis and insulin clearance make hypoglycaemia prolonged. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver failure and sepsis**: reduced gluconeogenesis and increased use; treat cause and continuous dextrose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if a 72-hour fast or C-peptide assay is unavailable, **capture a critical sample during an episode**, freeze serum and send to a referral laboratory; check malaria and drug history first. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Post-bariatric**: avoid labelling dumping as hypoglycaemia; use **CGM** to document lows and guide dietary change. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Neurological injury** from recurrent or prolonged neuroglycopenia - cognitive decline, seizures and rarely permanent brain damage or death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Road traffic accidents and falls** during unrecognised episodes. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Weight gain** from defensive eating in insulinoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Surgical complications**: **pancreatic fistula in up to about 30-40%** after enucleation (usually minor), pancreatitis, and diabetes after larger resections. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Prognosis**: benign insulinoma resected has near-normal survival; MEN1-associated tumours recur more often; malignant insulinoma with liver metastases has a median survival of a few years, improved with modern therapies. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** any non-diabetic adult with documented Whipple's triad to an endocrinologist for a supervised fast and localisation. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Admit** all sulfonylurea overdoses, recurrent severe episodes, and patients needing a 72-hour fast. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`After insulinoma resection, confirm cure by **resolution of symptoms and a normal fast** if doubt remains; monitor for diabetes after large resections. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MEN1 surveillance** lifelong for MEN1-related insulinoma (yearly calcium, PTH, gastrin, prolactin; periodic pancreatic MRI). [ENETS 2023]`,`**Malignant insulinoma**: imaging every 3-6 months with chromogranin and glucose monitoring; CGM helps. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Safe storage of diabetes medicines** at home and correct dispensing - accidental sulfonylurea ingestion is preventable. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Rational use of fluoroquinolones** - prescribers should avoid gatifloxacin and use caution with others in elderly and diabetic patients. [FDA 2018]`,`**Bariatric patients**: pre-operative counselling about late hypoglycaemia and dietitian follow-up for life. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Alcohol counselling** and nutrition support; malaria treatment with **artesunate** under the **National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control**. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`Teach families to recognise neuroglycopenia and give oral glucose; keep a **medical alert card**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Endocrine Society 2009 guideline on adult hypoglycaemic disorders** remains the current reference for Whipple's triad, the 72-hour fast and the biochemical cut-offs. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**ENETS 2023 guidance on functional pancreatic NETs**: **GLP-1 receptor PET/CT** recommended for occult insulinoma; **EUS-guided ablation** accepted for small benign insulinomas in selected patients; parenchyma-sparing surgery preferred. [ENETS 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 classification** of endocrine and neuroendocrine tumours applies Ki-67 grading to insulinomas. [WHO 2022]`,`**FDA 2018** strengthened warnings on fluoroquinolone-related hypoglycaemic coma, especially in older adults and diabetics. [FDA 2018]`,`**Hypoglycaemia levels** (level 1 below 70, level 2 below 54, level 3 severe) are defined for diabetes; for non-diabetics the investigation threshold stays at 55 mg/dL. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to hypoglycaemia in a non-diabetic'** - Whipple's triad, ill vs well, critical sample, 72-hour fast, interpretation table, localisation, treatment. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`Viva: 'High insulin, low C-peptide?' - **exogenous insulin**. 'High insulin, high C-peptide, positive screen?' - **sulfonylurea**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`Common mistake: **OGTT for reactive hypoglycaemia** - it is not valid; use a mixed meal. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`Common mistake: **imaging before biochemistry** - incidental pancreatic lesions mislead. [ENETS 2023]`,`Viva: 'Why does glucagon raise glucose in insulinoma?' - insulin preserves **liver glycogen**. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`Viva: 'Drug of choice for medical control of insulinoma?' - **diazoxide** (oedema and hirsutism as side effects). [ENETS 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in non-diabetic hypoglycaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dextrose`,`25 g IV (50 mL of 50% or 100-250 mL of 10%), then 10% infusion`,`Until cause corrected; hourly glucose`,`Phlebitis and extravasation with 50%; give thiamine first in alcoholics`],[`Glucagon`,`1 mg IM/SC (3 mg intranasal); 1 mg IV in diagnostic test`,`Single dose; repeat once`,`Ineffective with depleted glycogen (alcohol, starvation, liver failure); nausea`],[`Diazoxide`,`50-100 mg orally 2-3 times daily (3-8 mg/kg/day)`,`Until surgery or long term if inoperable`,`Oedema (add thiazide), hirsutism, nausea, hypotension`],[`Octreotide`,`50-100 mcg SC every 6-12 h (SU overdose); LAR 20-30 mg monthly`,`24-48 h in overdose; long term in SSTR-positive tumour`,`Can worsen insulinoma hypoglycaemia; gallstones, diarrhoea`],[`Acarbose`,`25-100 mg orally with meals`,`Long term (post-bariatric)`,`Flatulence, diarrhoea`],[`Everolimus`,`10 mg orally daily`,`Malignant insulinoma until progression`,`Hyperglycaemia (useful), stomatitis, pneumonitis, infections`],[`Prednisolone`,`30-60 mg/day orally`,`NICTH, insulin autoimmune syndrome`,`Steroid side effects`],[`177Lu-DOTATATE (PRRT)`,`7.4 GBq IV x4 cycles every 8 weeks`,`SSTR-positive metastatic tumour`,`Marrow suppression, nephrotoxicity (amino acid infusion); transient hypoglycaemia flare`]]},{heading:`Biochemical patterns at glucose below 55 mg/dL`,columns:[`Cause`,`Insulin`,`C-peptide`,`Proinsulin`,`Beta-hydroxybutyrate`,`Glucagon response`,`Other`],rows:[[`Insulinoma / NIPHS / post-bariatric`,`High (3 or more)`,`High (0.6 or more)`,`High (5 or more)`,`Low (2.7 or less)`,`Rise 25 mg/dL or more`,`SU screen negative`],[`Sulfonylurea / meglitinide`,`High`,`High`,`High`,`Low`,`Rise`,`SU screen positive`],[`Exogenous insulin`,`Very high`,`Low`,`Low`,`Low`,`Rise`,`Insulin/C-peptide ratio high`],[`Insulin autoimmune syndrome`,`Very high (often over 100)`,`High`,`High or normal`,`Low`,`Rise`,`Insulin antibodies positive`],[`IGF-2 (NICTH)`,`Low`,`Low`,`Low`,`Low`,`Rise`,`IGF-2:IGF-1 over 10`],[`Non-insulin (alcohol, starvation, adrenal, liver failure)`,`Low`,`Low`,`Low`,`High`,`No rise`,`Clinical context`]]},{heading:`Classification: ill or medicated versus seemingly well`,columns:[`Group`,`Causes`,`First step`],rows:[[`Ill or medicated`,`Drugs (SU, insulin, alcohol, quinine, fluoroquinolones, pentamidine), sepsis, liver/renal/heart failure, starvation`,`Treat and remove cause`],[`Ill or medicated`,`Hormone deficiency (cortisol, GH), NICTH`,`Cortisol, IGF-2/IGF-1`],[`Seemingly well`,`Insulinoma, NIPHS, post-bariatric hypoglycaemia`,`Critical sample or 72-h fast / mixed-meal test`],[`Seemingly well`,`Insulin autoimmune syndrome, accidental or factitious secretagogue or insulin`,`Antibodies, SU screen, C-peptide`]]},{heading:`Insulinoma localisation`,columns:[`Test`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Triple-phase CT / MRI pancreas`,`First line, widely available`,`Misses many tumours under 1 cm`],[`Endoscopic ultrasound`,`Over 90% sensitivity; FNA; ablation`,`Operator dependent; tail lesions harder`],[`GLP-1 receptor PET/CT (68Ga-exendin-4)`,`Most sensitive for benign occult insulinoma`,`Limited availability in India`],[`68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT`,`Good for malignant insulinoma`,`Benign tumours often SSTR2-negative`],[`Selective arterial calcium stimulation`,`Regionalises occult tumour; separates NIPHS`,`Invasive, tertiary only`],[`Intraoperative ultrasound`,`Finds tumour and duct relation at surgery`,`Needs laparotomy/laparoscopy`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypoglycaemia with coma or seizures in a non-diabetic`,`Recurrent fasting neuroglycopenia with documented Whipple's triad`,`Sulfonylurea ingestion (accidental or overdose) - admit for at least 24 hours`,`Hypoglycaemia with sepsis, malaria, liver failure or suspected adrenal crisis`,`Hypoglycaemia with a large abdominal or thoracic tumour (IGF-2)`,`Insulinoma with liver metastases or features of MEN1`],pearls:[`No Whipple's triad, no work-up.`,`Draw the critical sample before you give glucose.`,`Insulin 3, C-peptide 0.6, proinsulin 5, BOHB 2.7, glucagon +25 - the insulinoma numbers.`,`High insulin, low C-peptide = someone is injecting insulin.`,`Sulfonylurea mimics insulinoma - always send the SU screen.`,`Biochemistry first, imaging second.`,`Insulinoma: 90% benign, solitary, under 2 cm; 5-10% MEN1.`,`Never OGTT for reactive hypoglycaemia - mixed meal only.`,`Glucagon fails when glycogen is gone: alcohol, starvation, liver failure.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old nurse is brought with confusion; plasma glucose is 38 mg/dL. Insulin is 85 microU/mL, C-peptide is below 0.1 ng/mL, and proinsulin is low. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Insulinoma`,`Sulfonylurea ingestion`,`Exogenous insulin administration`,`IGF-2-secreting tumour`],answer:2,explanation:`High insulin with suppressed C-peptide and proinsulin means the insulin did not come from the beta cell, so it was injected - commoner in health workers. Insulinoma and sulfonylurea both raise C-peptide and proinsulin with insulin; an IGF-2 tumour suppresses insulin itself.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-q2`,stem:`During a supervised 72-hour fast, a patient has symptoms with plasma glucose 42 mg/dL, insulin 9 microU/mL, C-peptide 1.4 ng/mL, proinsulin 22 pmol/L and beta-hydroxybutyrate 0.3 mmol/L. Which single test must be checked before diagnosing insulinoma?`,options:[`Serum cortisol`,`Plasma sulfonylurea and meglitinide screen`,`Insulin antibodies`,`Serum IGF-2`],answer:1,explanation:`Sulfonylureas and meglitinides stimulate the beta cell, producing exactly the same pattern of raised insulin, C-peptide and proinsulin with low ketones, so a negative drug screen is required before calling it insulinoma. Cortisol and IGF-2 are relevant when insulin is suppressed, and insulin antibodies matter when insulin is extremely high, often over 100 microU/mL.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman develops sweating and confusion 2 hours after meals, 2 years after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. What is the most appropriate diagnostic test?`,options:[`75 g oral glucose tolerance test`,`72-hour supervised fast`,`Mixed-meal tolerance test with sampling during symptoms`,`68Ga-DOTATATE PET/CT`],answer:2,explanation:`Post-bariatric hypoglycaemia is postprandial, so it is reproduced with a mixed meal or documented with CGM and a critical sample. The OGTT is not validated for postprandial hypoglycaemia and produces false positives; the 72-hour fast tests fasting hypoglycaemia; imaging is never the first step before biochemical proof.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-q4`,stem:`A man with a large retroperitoneal sarcoma has recurrent fasting hypoglycaemia. Insulin and C-peptide are undetectable, beta-hydroxybutyrate is low, and glucose rises by 40 mg/dL after glucagon. What is the mechanism?`,options:[`Secretion of incompletely processed big IGF-2 by the tumour`,`Tumour consumption of glucose alone`,`Adrenal metastases causing cortisol deficiency`,`Ectopic insulin secretion by the sarcoma`],answer:0,explanation:`Non-islet cell tumour hypoglycaemia is driven by big IGF-2 acting on insulin receptors, giving an insulin-like picture (low ketones, preserved glycogen with a glucagon response) while insulin and C-peptide are suppressed. Ectopic insulin would raise insulin; adrenal failure and pure glucose consumption would not suppress ketones or preserve glycogen.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-q5`,stem:`Which drug is used for medical control of hypoglycaemia in a patient with a benign insulinoma awaiting surgery?`,options:[`Glibenclamide`,`Metformin`,`Propranolol`,`Diazoxide`],answer:3,explanation:`Diazoxide opens beta-cell K-ATP channels and suppresses insulin secretion, controlling hypoglycaemia in most insulinomas; its side effects are oedema and hirsutism. Glibenclamide closes the same channels and would worsen hypoglycaemia; metformin has no role; propranolol masks warning symptoms.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the approach to a 45-year-old non-diabetic man with recurrent episodes of confusion relieved by eating. How will you diagnose and manage an insulinoma?`,openingLines:[`Hypoglycaemia in a non-diabetic is evaluated only when Whipple's triad is documented: symptoms, plasma glucose below 55 mg/dL and relief with glucose.`,`Insulinoma is the commonest cause of endogenous hyperinsulinism in a well adult; it is diagnosed biochemically first and localised second.`],answer:[{heading:`Confirm hypoglycaemia`,points:[`Whipple's triad with a venous plasma glucose; exclude pseudohypoglycaemia.`,`Classify: ill/medicated vs seemingly well; fasting vs postprandial.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Timing, neuroglycopenia, weight gain, drugs at home, occupation, alcohol, bariatric surgery, MEN1 features.`,`Liver, adrenal, tumour signs.`]},{heading:`Biochemistry`,points:[`Critical sample: glucose, insulin, C-peptide, proinsulin, BOHB, SU screen, insulin antibodies; glucagon 1 mg IV response.`,`72-hour fast if no spontaneous episode; end at glucose 45 mg/dL or less with symptoms.`,`Insulinoma: insulin 3 or more, C-peptide 0.6 or more, proinsulin 5 or more, BOHB 2.7 or less, glucagon rise 25 or more, SU screen negative.`]},{heading:`Localisation`,points:[`CT/MRI pancreas, EUS; GLP-1R PET or SACST for occult tumours; intraoperative ultrasound.`,`Screen for MEN1.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Acute: oral glucose or 25 g IV dextrose; glucagon 1 mg IM.`,`Pre-operative: frequent meals, diazoxide 3-8 mg/kg/day.`,`Enucleation or distal pancreatectomy; EUS-RFA in unfit patients.`,`Malignant: everolimus, PRRT, sunitinib, chemotherapy.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Confirm cure; MEN1 surveillance; driving advice.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: Whipple's triad - critical sample/72-h fast - interpretation - localisation`,`Table of biochemical patterns (insulinoma, SU, exogenous insulin, IGF-2)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Biochemical diagnosis with table`,marks:3},{part:`Localisation`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Whipple triad`,`72-hour fast`,`C-peptide`,`proinsulin`,`diazoxide`,`EUS`,`GLP-1R PET`,`enucleation`]},{id:`endocrine-nondiabetic-hypoglycaemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the 72-hour fast.`,openingLines:[`The 72-hour supervised fast is the gold-standard test to reproduce fasting hypoglycaemia and prove endogenous hyperinsulinism.`,`About 95-99% of insulinomas become hypoglycaemic within 72 hours.`],answer:[{heading:`Indication and preparation`,points:[`Suspected fasting hypoglycaemia without a captured spontaneous episode.`,`Admit; stop non-essential drugs; calorie-free fluids only; IV access.`]},{heading:`Procedure`,points:[`Glucose every 6 h, every 1-2 h once below 60 mg/dL; insulin, C-peptide, proinsulin, BOHB with each low.`,`End at glucose 45 mg/dL or less with symptoms (55 if Whipple documented) or at 72 h.`,`At the end: draw samples, give glucagon 1 mg IV, glucose at 10, 20, 30 min; then feed.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`Insulin 3 or more, C-peptide 0.6 or more, proinsulin 5 or more, BOHB 2.7 or less, glucagon rise 25 or more = endogenous hyperinsulinism.`,`Negative SU screen needed before diagnosing insulinoma.`]}],mustDraw:[`Timeline of the 72-hour fast with end points`],markSplit:[{part:`Indication and preparation`,marks:1},{part:`Procedure and end points`,marks:2},{part:`Interpretation`,marks:2}],keywords:[`72-hour fast`,`Whipple`,`glucagon test`,`C-peptide`,`beta-hydroxybutyrate`]}],references:[`Cryer PE et al. Evaluation and Management of Adult Hypoglycemic Disorders: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2009`,`Hofland J et al. European Neuroendocrine Tumor Society (ENETS) 2023 guidance paper for functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour syndromes`,`WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India, 2023`,`American Diabetes Association Standards of Care in Diabetes, 2026`]}),l.push({id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine`,title:`MEN syndromes and neuroendocrine tumours`,oneLiner:`**Multiple endocrine neoplasia** is autosomal dominant tumour predisposition - **MEN1 (MENIN gene: parathyroid, pancreatic NET, pituitary)** and **MEN2 (RET proto-oncogene: medullary thyroid carcinoma, phaeochromocytoma, plus hyperparathyroidism in 2A or mucosal neuromas and marfanoid habitus in 2B)**; **neuroendocrine tumours** are graded by **Ki-67 (G1 under 3%, G2 3-20%, G3 over 20%)**, diagnosed with **24-h urinary 5-HIAA, chromogranin A and Ga-68 DOTATATE PET**, and treated with surgery, **somatostatin analogues**, PRRT and targeted drugs.`,frequency:`uncommon`,keywords:[`MEN1`,`MEN2A`,`MEN2B`,`MEN4`,`menin`,`RET proto-oncogene`,`medullary thyroid carcinoma`,`prophylactic thyroidectomy`,`carcinoid syndrome`,`carcinoid heart disease`,`5-HIAA`,`chromogranin A`,`Ga-68 DOTATATE`,`octreotide`,`lanreotide`,`PRRT`,`177Lu-DOTATATE`,`gastrinoma`,`Zollinger-Ellison`,`VIPoma`,`glucagonoma`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**MEN1** prevalence is about **1 in 30,000**, with near-complete penetrance (over 95%) by age 50; **MEN2** about **1 in 35,000**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MEN1 clinical diagnosis**: **two or more** of the three main tumours (parathyroid, pancreatic/duodenal NET, pituitary); **familial MEN1**: one main tumour plus a first-degree relative with MEN1; **genetic MEN1**: a germline MEN1 mutation in an asymptomatic carrier. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**MEN2A** accounts for about **95%** of MEN2; **MEN2B** about 5% (about 75% de novo mutations), and **familial MTC** is now considered a variant of MEN2A. [ATA 2015]`,`**Neuroendocrine neoplasms (NENs)** arise from diffuse neuroendocrine cells; incidence has risen about **6-fold since 1973** to roughly **7-8 per 100,000** per year (SEER), largely from better imaging and endoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Commonest primary sites: **small intestine (ileum)**, rectum, lung/bronchus, pancreas, stomach and appendix; **prevalence exceeds that of gastric and pancreatic cancer** combined because many grow slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: no population registry for NETs; hospital series from Tata Memorial, AIIMS and PGIMER show **gastroenteropancreatic NETs** commonest, often at a **metastatic stage at diagnosis**; Ga-68 DOTATATE PET and **Lu-177 PRRT** (isotope produced indigenously by BARC/BRIT) are now available in many Indian centres. [Indian NET series]`,`About **5-10% of medullary thyroid cancers** are part of MEN2 in older series, and **up to 25% of 'sporadic-looking' MTC carry a germline RET mutation** - so every MTC needs RET testing. [ATA 2015]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and genetics`,points:[`**MEN1** results from inactivating mutations of the **MEN1 tumour-suppressor gene on 11q13** encoding **menin**; tumours arise after a 'second hit' (Knudson two-hit model) in the remaining allele. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MEN2** results from **activating (gain-of-function) mutations of the RET proto-oncogene on 10q11.2**, a receptor tyrosine kinase - one mutated allele is enough, so it is a dominant oncogene, not a tumour suppressor. [ATA 2015]`,`**Genotype-phenotype**: **codon 634** (exon 11) is the commonest MEN2A mutation and carries high pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism risk; **M918T** (exon 16) causes **over 95% of MEN2B**. [ATA 2015]`,`**MEN4** - **CDKN1B (p27)** mutation, MEN1-like phenotype with negative MEN1 testing. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other inherited NET syndromes: **von Hippel-Lindau** (pancreatic NET, phaeochromocytoma), **neurofibromatosis type 1** (duodenal somatostatinoma, phaeochromocytoma), **tuberous sclerosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RET** is also mutated in **Hirschsprung disease** (loss of function) - and some MEN2A codon 609/618/620 families have both. [ATA 2015]`,`Most sporadic NETs have no clear environmental cause; small-intestinal NETs have few mutations (CDKN1B loss in some); pancreatic NETs often show **MEN1, DAXX/ATRX** and **mTOR pathway** alterations - the basis for **everolimus**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - hormones explain the syndromes`,points:[`**Carcinoid syndrome** results from **serotonin, tachykinins, histamine, kallikrein and prostaglandins** reaching the systemic circulation; gut NET hormones are normally inactivated by the liver, so the syndrome usually needs **liver metastases** (or a bronchial/ovarian primary draining directly into systemic veins). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flushing** is from kinins, tachykinins and histamine; **diarrhoea** from serotonin-driven gut motility and secretion; **bronchospasm** from histamine and kinins. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carcinoid heart disease**: serotonin causes **fibrous plaques on right-sided valves** - **tricuspid regurgitation** and pulmonary stenosis - because the lung metabolises serotonin before it reaches the left heart. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pellagra**: tumours divert up to 60% of dietary **tryptophan** to serotonin, causing **niacin deficiency** - dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serotonin is metabolised to 5-HIAA**, excreted in urine - the basis of the 24-hour urinary 5-HIAA test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Somatostatin receptor type 2 (SSTR2)** is expressed on most well-differentiated NETs, which is why **octreotide/lanreotide** control hormone release and growth and why **Ga-68 DOTATATE** images and **177Lu-DOTATATE** treats them. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MTC** arises from **parafollicular C cells**, secretes **calcitonin and CEA**; RET-driven C-cell hyperplasia precedes cancer, which is why **prophylactic thyroidectomy by genotype** works. [ATA 2015]`,`**Gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison)**: gastrin drives parietal cell acid hypersecretion - multiple, distal or refractory ulcers and **diarrhoea** (acid inactivates pancreatic lipase). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**MEN1 (Wermer)**: **parathyroid hyperplasia (about 95%)**, **pancreatic/duodenal NETs (30-80%)** - non-functioning commonest, gastrinoma commonest functioning, then insulinoma - and **anterior pituitary adenoma (30-40%, prolactinoma commonest)**; also adrenal cortical tumours, thymic and bronchial carcinoids, lipomas, angiofibromas and collagenomas. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**MEN2A (Sipple)**: **MTC (about 95-100%)**, **phaeochromocytoma (up to 50%)**, **primary hyperparathyroidism (20-30%)**; variants with **cutaneous lichen amyloidosis** and **Hirschsprung disease**. [ATA 2015]`,`**MEN2B**: **MTC (100%, earliest and most aggressive)**, **phaeochromocytoma (about 50%)**, **mucosal neuromas** (lips, tongue), **marfanoid habitus**, intestinal ganglioneuromatosis, medullated corneal nerves - **no hyperparathyroidism**. [ATA 2015]`,`**ATA 2015 RET risk categories**: **highest (ATA-HST)** - M918T; **high (ATA-H)** - codon 634 and A883F; **moderate (ATA-MOD)** - all others. [ATA 2015]`,`**WHO 2019/2022 grading of GEP-NENs**: well-differentiated **NET G1 (Ki-67 under 3%, under 2 mitoses/2 mm2)**, **G2 (Ki-67 3-20%)**, **G3 (over 20%)**; poorly differentiated **neuroendocrine carcinoma (NEC)**, small or large cell, Ki-67 over 20%; **MiNEN** for mixed tumours. [WHO 2022]`,`**Functional vs non-functional**: functional tumours cause hormone syndromes (carcinoid, gastrinoma, insulinoma, VIPoma, glucagonoma, somatostatinoma); **non-functional** tumours (about 60-90% of pancreatic NETs) present with mass effects or incidentally. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Staging**: AJCC/ENETS TNM staging by site; **lung NETs** are typical carcinoid (under 2 mitoses/2 mm2, no necrosis) and atypical carcinoid (2-10 mitoses or necrosis). [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**MEN1**: kidney stones, bone pain, fatigue (hypercalcaemia), **recurrent peptic ulcers or diarrhoea** (gastrinoma), fasting confusion (insulinoma), galactorrhoea or amenorrhoea (prolactinoma) - often in the **20s-40s** with a family history. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**MEN2**: neck lump (MTC), **paroxysmal headache, sweating, palpitations** (phaeochromocytoma), diarrhoea (calcitonin), and in 2B constipation or megacolon from ganglioneuromatosis. [ATA 2015]`,`**Carcinoid syndrome** (in about 10-20% of NETs, mostly ileal with liver metastases): **episodic flushing** of face and upper chest (seconds to minutes; precipitated by alcohol, stress, food), **watery diarrhoea**, wheeze and **right heart failure** symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Small bowel NET** often presents with **intermittent abdominal pain or obstruction** from the **intense desmoplastic mesenteric reaction**, or mesenteric ischaemia - years of misdiagnosis as IBS are typical. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastrinoma**: refractory or distal duodenal/jejunal ulcers, ulcers without H. pylori or NSAIDs, chronic diarrhoea responding to PPI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VIPoma** (Verner-Morrison, WDHA): **profuse watery diarrhoea over 3 L/day**, hypokalaemia, achlorhydria; **glucagonoma**: **necrolytic migratory erythema**, diabetes, weight loss, DVT, glossitis; **somatostatinoma**: diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Appendiceal NETs** are usually found incidentally at appendicectomy; **rectal NETs** at colonoscopy as small yellow submucosal nodules. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Flushing**: bright red-purple of face and upper trunk; long-standing disease gives **telangiectasia and facial venous engorgement**; bronchial carcinoid flushes are prolonged (hours) with facial oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatomegaly**, often nodular, from liver metastases; **pellagra** dermatitis in sun-exposed skin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carcinoid heart disease**: raised JVP with large v waves, **pansystolic murmur of TR** increasing on inspiration, pulsatile liver, oedema. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**MEN2B phenotype**: **bumpy lips and tongue** (mucosal neuromas), everted eyelids, **marfanoid habitus** without lens or aortic changes - recognising it in childhood allows early thyroidectomy. [ATA 2015]`,`**MEN1 skin**: facial **angiofibromas** and **collagenomas**, lipomas - clues in a patient with hyperparathyroidism. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**Thyroid nodule with cervical lymphadenopathy** in a young person with family history - MTC. [ATA 2015]`,`**Glucagonoma rash**: annular, migratory, erythematous, blistering and crusting lesions in the groin, perineum and lower limbs. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Flushing**: **menopause** (no diarrhoea), alcohol and drugs (niacin, calcium-channel blockers), **rosacea**, mastocytosis (urticaria pigmentosa, tryptase), MTC, phaeochromocytoma (pallor more than flush), anaphylaxis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic diarrhoea**: IBS, IBD, coeliac disease, microscopic colitis, VIPoma, gastrinoma, MTC, hyperthyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sporadic vs MEN1 hyperparathyroidism**: MEN1 presents **younger (20-25 years)**, with **multigland hyperplasia** and high recurrence after surgery. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**Sporadic vs hereditary MTC**: hereditary is **bilateral and multifocal** with C-cell hyperplasia; RET testing decides. [ATA 2015]`,`**Hypergastrinaemia**: much commoner causes are **PPI use, atrophic gastritis (pernicious anaemia), H. pylori, renal failure** - acid is low in these, high in gastrinoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised chromogranin A** without NET: **PPIs, atrophic gastritis, renal and heart failure, liver disease**, IBD - CgA is a poor screening test. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**24-hour urinary 5-HIAA**: normal about 2-8 mg/24 h (laboratory dependent); **over 25 mg/24 h** is diagnostic of carcinoid syndrome; avoid for 48-72 h **bananas, pineapple, kiwi, plums, avocado, tomatoes, aubergine, walnuts** and drugs such as paracetamol or guaifenesin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chromogranin A** for monitoring known NET burden rather than diagnosis; **stop PPIs for 1-2 weeks** if feasible before testing; ENETS 2023 limits its role. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Histology** with **synaptophysin and chromogranin** immunostaining and **Ki-67 index** is essential for diagnosis and grade. [WHO 2022]`,`**Ga-68 DOTATATE / DOTATOC / DOTANOC PET/CT**: **sensitivity over 90%** for well-differentiated NETs, defines SSTR status for SSA and PRRT eligibility; it has replaced octreotide (In-111) scintigraphy. [ENETS 2023]`,`**18F-FDG PET** for **G3 NET and NEC** (high-grade lose SSTR, gain glucose uptake); dual tracer imaging predicts prognosis. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Cross-sectional imaging**: multiphase CT or **MRI liver** (best for liver metastases); **EUS** for small pancreatic NETs; capsule endoscopy or enteroscopy for small bowel primaries. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Gastrinoma**: fasting gastrin **over 10 times ULN (about 1000 pg/mL) with gastric pH below 2** is diagnostic; otherwise a **secretin stimulation test** (rise of 120 pg/mL or more); stop PPI cautiously under specialist care. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** in every patient with carcinoid syndrome; **NT-proBNP** screens for carcinoid heart disease. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MEN2 work-up**: **calcitonin, CEA**, neck ultrasound, **plasma free metanephrines** (always exclude phaeochromocytoma before any surgery), calcium and PTH, and **germline RET testing** in all MTC. [ATA 2015]`,`**MEN1 work-up**: calcium, PTH, prolactin, IGF-1, gastrin, fasting glucose/insulin, pancreatic polypeptide, **MRI pituitary and pancreas**, and **MEN1 gene testing** in index cases and first-degree relatives. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - medical therapy`,points:[`**Octreotide LAR 20-30 mg IM every 4 weeks** or **lanreotide autogel 120 mg deep SC every 28 days** controls flushing and diarrhoea in about **70-80%** and slows growth (PROMID, CLARINET) in SSTR-positive G1/G2 tumours. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Short-acting octreotide 100-500 mcg SC 2-3 times daily** for breakthrough symptoms or before starting the depot. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Telotristat ethyl 250 mg orally three times daily** (tryptophan hydroxylase inhibitor) for carcinoid diarrhoea not controlled by SSAs. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Gastrinoma**: high-dose **PPI** (e.g. omeprazole 40-60 mg twice daily, titrated to acid output) - never stop abruptly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic NET progression**: **everolimus 10 mg daily** (RADIANT-3) or **sunitinib 37.5 mg daily** (pancreatic); **everolimus** also for lung and GI non-functional NET (RADIANT-4); **capecitabine-temozolomide (CAPTEM)** for pancreatic NET. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Cabozantinib 60 mg daily** improved progression-free survival in previously treated pancreatic and extra-pancreatic NETs (CABINET trial). [NCCN 2025]`,`**Supportive**: **niacin/nicotinamide** supplements, anti-diarrhoeals, avoid triggers (alcohol, spicy food); pancreatic enzymes for SSA-induced steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - surgery, PRRT and carcinoid crisis`,points:[`**Surgery** is the only cure: resection of the primary and regional nodes for localised NETs; **appendiceal NET under 2 cm** - appendicectomy alone; **rectal NET under 1 cm** - endoscopic resection. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Small bowel NET**: resect primary and **mesenteric nodes** even with liver metastases, to prevent obstruction and ischaemia; **cholecystectomy** at surgery if long-term SSA planned. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Liver metastases**: resection, ablation, **transarterial embolisation/chemoembolisation** or radioembolisation; liver transplant in highly selected young patients. [ENETS 2023]`,`**PRRT with 177Lu-DOTATATE 7.4 GBq IV x 4 cycles every 8 weeks** with renal-protective amino acid infusion for progressive SSTR-positive midgut NETs (**NETTER-1**: markedly longer PFS); **NETTER-2 (2024)** supports **first-line PRRT** for advanced G2/G3 GEP-NETs with Ki-67 10-55%. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Carcinoid crisis** (severe flushing, bronchospasm, **hypotension**, arrhythmia) triggered by anaesthesia, surgery, embolisation or tumour handling: prevent with **octreotide 100-500 mcg SC/IV** before the procedure or an **infusion of 50-100 mcg/h** starting about 12 h before and continued 24-48 h; treat with **IV octreotide bolus 500-1000 mcg**, fluids, and **avoid adrenaline and sympathomimetics** (they release more mediators). [ENETS 2023]`,`**Carcinoid heart disease**: diuretics, SSAs, and **valve replacement** (tricuspid and pulmonary) in experienced centres. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**NEC (poorly differentiated)**: treat like small-cell carcinoma - **cisplatin or carboplatin with etoposide**. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Management of MEN syndromes`,points:[`**MEN2 prophylactic total thyroidectomy by RET risk**: **ATA-HST (M918T) within the first year of life**; **ATA-H (codon 634, A883F) at or before age 5**, guided by calcitonin; **ATA-MOD** when calcitonin rises, usually in childhood. [ATA 2015]`,`**Exclude phaeochromocytoma before any surgery** in MEN2 with plasma free or urine fractionated metanephrines; remove phaeochromocytoma first under **alpha-blockade**. [ATA 2015]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma screening start age**: **11 years for ATA-HST and ATA-H**, **16 years for ATA-MOD**. [ATA 2015]`,`**Advanced MTC**: **selpercatinib or pralsetinib** (selective RET inhibitors) are preferred over vandetanib/cabozantinib for RET-mutant disease. [NCCN 2025]`,`**MEN1 hyperparathyroidism**: **subtotal (3.5-gland) parathyroidectomy** or total parathyroidectomy with autotransplantation plus **transcervical thymectomy** (supernumerary glands and thymic carcinoid). [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**MEN1 pancreatic NETs**: operate on **functional tumours** and non-functional tumours **over 2 cm** or growing; small non-functional tumours are watched. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MEN1 gastrinoma**: usually **multiple small duodenal** tumours; treat with PPI, and correct hypercalcaemia first (calcium drives gastrin). [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: undiagnosed **phaeochromocytoma in MEN2** is lethal in labour - test metanephrines in any MEN2 woman planning pregnancy; SSAs are generally stopped. [ATA 2015]`,`**Children**: genetic testing of at-risk relatives of MEN2 **before age 5 (before 1 year if M918T family)**, and of MEN1 relatives **by age 5**. [ATA 2015]`,`**Elderly**: many G1 NETs are indolent - watchful waiting with imaging may be better than aggressive surgery. [ENETS 2023]`,`**CKD**: chromogranin falsely high; PRRT needs adequate renal function (dose adjustments, amino acid protection). [ENETS 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: send 24-h urinary 5-HIAA and histology with Ki-67; refer for PET and PRRT to centres covered under **PM-JAY** or state schemes; short-acting octreotide is cheaper than LAR. [Indian NET series]`,`**Diabetes**: SSAs and everolimus raise glucose; check glucose regularly. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Carcinoid heart disease** in about **20-50%** of carcinoid syndrome patients - the main cause of death in them. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mesenteric fibrosis** causing bowel obstruction or ischaemia; **liver failure** from extensive metastases. [ENETS 2023]`,`**SSA side effects**: **gallstones**, steatorrhoea, abdominal cramps, hyperglycaemia, bradycardia. [ENETS 2023]`,`**PRRT risks**: marrow suppression, **myelodysplasia or leukaemia (about 2-3%)**, renal toxicity. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Prognosis by grade and site**: 5-year survival is **over 90% for localised G1** NETs but much lower for metastatic disease; **NEC has median survival around 1 year** when metastatic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MEN1**: leading causes of death are **malignant pancreatic NETs and thymic carcinoids**; **MEN2**: MTC metastasis and undiagnosed phaeochromocytoma. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer all suspected NETs and MEN** to a centre with a **NET multidisciplinary tumour board** (surgery, oncology, nuclear medicine, endocrinology). [ENETS 2023]`,`**NET surveillance**: CT/MRI every **3-6 months** for G2/G3 or metastatic disease, every **6-12 months** for G1; Ga-68 PET at progression or for PRRT eligibility. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Echo yearly** (or with symptoms) in carcinoid syndrome. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MEN1 surveillance (Endocrine Society 2012)**: **calcium/PTH yearly from 8 years**, **prolactin and IGF-1 yearly from 5 years**, **pituitary MRI every 3 years from 5 years**, **gastrin from 20 years**, **abdominal MRI/CT yearly from under 10 years**, thoracic CT or MRI every 1-2 years from 15. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`**MTC after thyroidectomy**: calcitonin and CEA at 3 months then 6-12-monthly; **doubling times** predict outcome. [ATA 2015]`,`**Cascade screening**: offer genetic testing to all first-degree relatives once a familial mutation is found. [ATA 2015]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ENETS 2023 guidance papers** (functional pancreatic NETs, small-intestinal NETs, NEC, carcinoid syndrome): Ga-68 SSTR PET as standard; chromogranin A's diagnostic role limited; PRRT and telotristat positions defined. [ENETS 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 classification** (5th edition) keeps NET G1-G3 and NEC, and separates well-differentiated G3 NET from NEC. [WHO 2022]`,`**NETTER-2 (Lancet 2024)**: first-line 177Lu-DOTATATE with octreotide improved PFS in newly diagnosed high-grade (Ki-67 10-55%) G2/G3 GEP-NETs. [NCCN 2025]`,`**CABINET (NEJM 2025)**: cabozantinib prolonged PFS in progressive pancreatic and extra-pancreatic NETs after prior therapy. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Selective RET inhibitors** (selpercatinib, pralsetinib) now preferred for RET-mutant advanced MTC (LIBRETTO-531 2023 showed superiority over cabozantinib/vandetanib). [NCCN 2025]`,`**ATA 2015 MTC guideline** risk categories remain the basis for prophylactic thyroidectomy; **Endocrine Society 2012 MEN1 guideline** still current for surveillance. [ATA 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Mnemonic **MEN1 = 3 Ps** (parathyroid, pancreas, pituitary); **MEN2A = 2 Ms and 1 P** (MTC, medulla (phaeo), parathyroid); **MEN2B = 1 P, 2 Ms** (phaeo, MTC, marfanoid/mucosal neuromas). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Why is carcinoid syndrome rare without liver metastases?' - **hepatic first-pass metabolism** of serotonin. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Why are right heart valves affected?' - **lungs inactivate serotonin**; left-sided disease suggests bronchial primary or a PFO. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Common mistake: **operating on MTC before excluding phaeochromocytoma**. [ATA 2015]`,`Common mistake: **interpreting chromogranin A on PPIs**. [ENETS 2023]`,`Viva: 'Commonest functional pancreatic NET in MEN1?' - **gastrinoma** (duodenal, multiple); 'commonest overall pancreatic NET in MEN1' - non-functioning. [Endocrine Society MEN1 2012]`,`Viva: 'Drug to avoid in carcinoid crisis?' - **adrenaline and other sympathomimetics**. [ENETS 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in neuroendocrine tumours and MEN`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Octreotide LAR`,`20-30 mg IM every 4 weeks (short-acting 100-500 mcg SC 2-3 times daily)`,`Long term; escalate dose or frequency for symptoms`,`Gallstones, steatorrhoea, hyperglycaemia, bradycardia`],[`Lanreotide autogel`,`120 mg deep SC every 28 days`,`Long term (CLARINET antiproliferative)`,`As octreotide; injection-site nodules`],[`Telotristat ethyl`,`250 mg orally three times daily`,`Add-on for refractory diarrhoea`,`Nausea, depression, constipation`],[`177Lu-DOTATATE (PRRT)`,`7.4 GBq IV every 8 weeks x4`,`Progressive SSTR-positive NET`,`Marrow suppression, MDS/leukaemia, renal toxicity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Everolimus`,`10 mg orally daily`,`Until progression`,`Stomatitis, pneumonitis, hyperglycaemia, infections`],[`Sunitinib`,`37.5 mg orally daily`,`Pancreatic NET until progression`,`Hypertension, hand-foot syndrome, hypothyroidism`],[`Capecitabine + temozolomide`,`Capecitabine 750 mg/m2 twice daily days 1-14 + temozolomide 200 mg/m2 days 10-14, 28-day cycle`,`Pancreatic NET`,`Myelosuppression, nausea`],[`Selpercatinib`,`160 mg orally twice daily (120 mg if under 50 kg)`,`RET-mutant advanced MTC`,`Hypertension, QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity`],[`Cisplatin/carboplatin + etoposide`,`Standard small-cell regimens IV`,`NEC, 4-6 cycles`,`Myelosuppression, nephrotoxicity, neuropathy`]]},{heading:`MEN syndromes compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`MEN1`,`MEN2A`,`MEN2B`],rows:[[`Gene`,`MEN1 (menin), 11q13, tumour suppressor`,`RET, 10q11.2, oncogene (codon 634 commonest)`,`RET M918T`],[`Parathyroid`,`About 95%, multigland`,`20-30%`,`Absent`],[`Pancreatic/duodenal NET`,`30-80% (gastrinoma, insulinoma, non-functioning)`,`No`,`No`],[`Pituitary`,`30-40% (prolactinoma)`,`No`,`No`],[`MTC`,`No`,`About 95-100%`,`100%, early and aggressive`],[`Phaeochromocytoma`,`Rare`,`Up to 50%`,`About 50%`],[`Other`,`Angiofibromas, collagenomas, lipomas, thymic carcinoid`,`Lichen amyloidosis, Hirschsprung`,`Mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus, ganglioneuromatosis`],[`Key prevention`,`Surveillance from childhood`,`Prophylactic thyroidectomy by age 5 (codon 634)`,`Thyroidectomy in first year`]]},{heading:`WHO 2022 grading of GEP neuroendocrine neoplasms`,columns:[`Category`,`Differentiation`,`Ki-67 index`,`Mitotic count (per 2 mm2)`],rows:[[`NET G1`,`Well`,`Under 3%`,`Under 2`],[`NET G2`,`Well`,`3-20%`,`2-20`],[`NET G3`,`Well`,`Over 20%`,`Over 20`],[`NEC (small or large cell)`,`Poor`,`Over 20% (often over 55%)`,`Over 20`],[`MiNEN`,`Mixed with non-NE component (each at least 30%)`,`Variable`,`Variable`]]},{heading:`Functional NET syndromes`,columns:[`Tumour`,`Hormone`,`Key features`,`Diagnostic test`],rows:[[`Small bowel NET with liver metastases`,`Serotonin, tachykinins`,`Flushing, diarrhoea, wheeze, TR`,`24-h urine 5-HIAA over 25 mg`],[`Gastrinoma`,`Gastrin`,`Refractory ulcers, diarrhoea`,`Gastrin over 10x ULN with pH under 2; secretin test`],[`Insulinoma`,`Insulin`,`Fasting neuroglycopenia`,`72-h fast`],[`VIPoma`,`VIP`,`Watery diarrhoea over 3 L/day, hypokalaemia, achlorhydria`,`Plasma VIP`],[`Glucagonoma`,`Glucagon`,`Necrolytic migratory erythema, diabetes, DVT, weight loss`,`Plasma glucagon`],[`Somatostatinoma`,`Somatostatin`,`Diabetes, gallstones, steatorrhoea`,`Plasma somatostatin`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`24-h urine 5-HIAA`,`Carcinoid syndrome; over 25 mg/24 h diagnostic`,`Serotonin-rich foods and some drugs raise it`],[`Chromogranin A`,`Tumour burden and follow-up`,`False positive with PPI, atrophic gastritis, CKD, heart failure`],[`Ga-68 DOTATATE PET/CT`,`Staging, SSA and PRRT eligibility`,`Less sensitive for G3/NEC and benign insulinoma`],[`FDG PET/CT`,`G3 NET and NEC`,`Low uptake in G1 NET`],[`Calcitonin, CEA`,`MTC diagnosis and follow-up`,`Calcitonin raised in CKD, PPI use`],[`Plasma free metanephrines`,`Phaeochromocytoma in MEN2`,`Must be done before any MEN2 surgery`],[`Germline RET / MEN1 testing`,`Index case and relatives`,`Negative MEN1 test may be MEN4 (CDKN1B)`]]}],redFlags:[`Carcinoid crisis: flushing, bronchospasm and hypotension during surgery or embolisation`,`Bowel obstruction or mesenteric ischaemia in a known small-bowel NET`,`Severe watery diarrhoea with hypokalaemia (VIPoma) or dehydration`,`Paroxysmal hypertension in a MEN2 patient - exclude phaeochromocytoma before any procedure`,`Right heart failure in carcinoid syndrome`,`Rapidly growing tumour or Ki-67 over 20% (G3/NEC)`,`Hypercalcaemia or hypoglycaemia in a MEN1 family member`],pearls:[`MEN1 = 3 Ps; MEN2A = MTC + phaeo + parathyroid; MEN2B = MTC + phaeo + marfanoid + mucosal neuromas.`,`MEN1 is a lost tumour suppressor (menin); MEN2 is a gained oncogene (RET).`,`Every MTC gets germline RET testing; every MEN2 surgery waits for metanephrines.`,`M918T - thyroidectomy in year one; codon 634 - by age 5.`,`Carcinoid syndrome means liver metastases (unless bronchial or ovarian primary).`,`Serotonin scars right heart valves - TR and PS.`,`5-HIAA over 25 mg/24 h is diagnostic; stop bananas and pineapples first.`,`Ga-68 DOTATATE both finds the tumour and selects it for Lu-177 - 'see it, treat it' (theranostics).`,`No adrenaline in carcinoid crisis - give octreotide.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-q1`,stem:`A 4-year-old child's father has medullary thyroid carcinoma with a RET codon 634 mutation. The child carries the same mutation and has a normal calcitonin. What is the recommended management?`,options:[`Annual neck ultrasound until a nodule appears`,`Prophylactic total thyroidectomy at or before age 5`,`Thyroidectomy only when calcitonin exceeds 150 pg/mL`,`Start vandetanib to prevent cancer`],answer:1,explanation:`Codon 634 is an ATA high-risk mutation, so total thyroidectomy is advised at or before age 5, timed by calcitonin, before MTC spreads. Waiting for a nodule or a calcitonin of 150 allows metastatic disease; vandetanib is for advanced MTC, not prevention.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old man has episodic facial flushing, watery diarrhoea and a pansystolic murmur increasing with inspiration. CT shows multiple liver lesions. Which test is most useful to confirm the syndrome?`,options:[`Serum chromogranin A while on pantoprazole`,`Plasma free metanephrines`,`24-hour urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid`,`Serum tryptase`],answer:2,explanation:`Flushing, diarrhoea and tricuspid regurgitation with liver metastases is carcinoid syndrome, confirmed by a 24-hour urine 5-HIAA over 25 mg/day. Chromogranin A is non-specific and falsely raised by PPIs; metanephrines test for phaeochromocytoma; tryptase tests for mastocytosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-q3`,stem:`During hepatic artery embolisation in a patient with metastatic ileal NET, the patient develops severe flushing, wheeze and BP 70/40 mmHg. What is the most appropriate drug?`,options:[`Intravenous octreotide bolus`,`Intravenous adrenaline infusion`,`Nebulised salbutamol alone`,`Intravenous phenylephrine`],answer:0,explanation:`This is a carcinoid crisis, treated with an IV octreotide bolus (500-1000 mcg) and fluids. Adrenaline and other sympathomimetics such as phenylephrine can provoke further mediator release and worsen the crisis; salbutamol alone does not address the vasoactive mediators.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-q4`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has nephrolithiasis with hypercalcaemia, recurrent jejunal ulcers and galactorrhoea. Which gene is most likely mutated?`,options:[`RET proto-oncogene`,`VHL`,`CDKN1B`,`MEN1 (menin)`],answer:3,explanation:`Hyperparathyroidism, a gastrinoma (jejunal ulcers) and a prolactinoma are the three Ps of MEN1, caused by loss of the menin tumour-suppressor gene on 11q13. RET causes MEN2 (MTC, phaeochromocytoma); VHL causes phaeochromocytoma, renal cancer and pancreatic NETs but not this triad; CDKN1B causes the rarer MEN4, considered only when MEN1 testing is negative.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-q5`,stem:`A pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour has a Ki-67 index of 12% and is well differentiated. How is it graded in the WHO 2022 classification?`,options:[`NET G1`,`NET G2`,`NET G3`,`Neuroendocrine carcinoma`],answer:1,explanation:`A well-differentiated NET with Ki-67 between 3% and 20% is G2. G1 requires Ki-67 below 3%; G3 NET is well differentiated with Ki-67 above 20%; neuroendocrine carcinoma is poorly differentiated, regardless of the precise Ki-67 above 20%.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`uncommon`,question:`Classify multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes. Discuss the genetics, clinical features and screening of MEN1 and MEN2.`,openingLines:[`MEN syndromes are autosomal dominant disorders with tumours in two or more endocrine glands: MEN1 from loss of the menin tumour suppressor and MEN2 from activating RET mutations.`,`Genetic diagnosis allows surveillance in MEN1 and prophylactic thyroidectomy in MEN2.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`MEN1 (parathyroid, pancreatic/duodenal NET, pituitary), MEN2A (MTC, phaeochromocytoma, parathyroid), MEN2B (MTC, phaeochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid), MEN4 (CDKN1B).`]},{heading:`Genetics`,points:[`MEN1: 11q13, menin, two-hit tumour suppressor.`,`MEN2: RET on 10q11.2, gain-of-function; codon 634 (2A), M918T (2B); ATA risk categories.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`MEN1: hypercalcaemia (95%), gastrinoma, insulinoma, prolactinoma; skin angiofibromas.`,`MEN2: neck mass, diarrhoea, paroxysmal hypertension; 2B phenotype.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`MEN1: calcium, PTH, gastrin, prolactin, IGF-1, MRI pituitary/pancreas, gene test.`,`MEN2: calcitonin, CEA, metanephrines, calcium, neck ultrasound, RET test.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`MEN1: 3.5-gland parathyroidectomy with thymectomy; PPI for gastrinoma; surgery for functional or over 2 cm pancreatic NETs.`,`MEN2: exclude/remove phaeo first; thyroidectomy by RET risk (year 1 for M918T, by 5 for codon 634).`,`Advanced MTC: selpercatinib or pralsetinib.`]},{heading:`Screening of relatives`,points:[`Cascade genetic testing; MEN1 biochemical surveillance from age 5; MEN2 phaeo screening from 11 or 16.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of MEN1, MEN2A and MEN2B`,`Timeline of prophylactic thyroidectomy by ATA risk`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Genetics`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management and screening`,marks:3}],keywords:[`MEN1`,`menin`,`RET`,`codon 634`,`M918T`,`prophylactic thyroidectomy`,`metanephrines`]},{id:`endocrine-men-neuroendocrine-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on carcinoid syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Carcinoid syndrome is flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm and right-sided valve disease caused by serotonin and other vasoactive amines from a NET, usually ileal with liver metastases.`,`It is diagnosed by a 24-hour urinary 5-HIAA over 25 mg and treated with somatostatin analogues.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Serotonin, tachykinins, histamine bypass hepatic metabolism via liver metastases.`,`Tryptophan diversion causes pellagra; serotonin fibrosis affects tricuspid and pulmonary valves.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Flushing, diarrhoea, wheeze, TR murmur, hepatomegaly, pellagra.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`24-h urine 5-HIAA, chromogranin A, Ga-68 DOTATATE PET, CT/MRI liver, echo.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Octreotide LAR 20-30 mg monthly or lanreotide 120 mg; telotristat for diarrhoea.`,`Surgery, liver-directed therapy, PRRT; octreotide cover for procedures.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: serotonin - liver metastasis - systemic effects - right heart valves`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management including crisis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`carcinoid syndrome`,`5-HIAA`,`serotonin`,`octreotide`,`tricuspid regurgitation`]}],references:[`Thakker RV et al. Clinical Practice Guidelines for Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN1), Endocrine Society, 2012`,`Wells SA Jr et al. Revised American Thyroid Association Guidelines for the Management of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma, 2015`,`European Neuroendocrine Tumor Society (ENETS) 2023 Guidance Papers for GEP neuroendocrine neoplasms`,`WHO Classification of Tumours: Endocrine and Neuroendocrine Tumours, 5th edition, 2022`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Neuroendocrine and Adrenal Tumors, 2025`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`]}),l.push({id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies`,title:`Endocrine emergencies: adrenal crisis, hypercalcaemic crisis and pituitary apoplexy`,oneLiner:`**Adrenal crisis** is acute cortisol deficiency with hypotension or shock, treated immediately - **before tests return** - with **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM, then 200 mg/24 h** (infusion or 50 mg 6-hourly) and **1 L 0.9% saline in the first hour**; **hypercalcaemic crisis** (calcium usually **over 14 mg/dL** with dehydration and confusion) needs **saline, calcitonin, then zoledronate or denosumab**; **pituitary apoplexy** is sudden haemorrhage or infarction of a pituitary adenoma - **thunderclap headache, visual loss, ophthalmoplegia** - needing **hydrocortisone first** and urgent MRI with neurosurgical review.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`adrenal crisis`,`Addisonian crisis`,`acute adrenal insufficiency`,`hydrocortisone 100 mg`,`stress dosing`,`sick day rules`,`steroid emergency card`,`hypercalcaemic crisis`,`hypercalcaemia of malignancy`,`zoledronic acid`,`denosumab`,`calcitonin`,`vitamin D toxicity`,`pituitary apoplexy`,`Pituitary Apoplexy Score`,`thyroid storm`,`myxoedema coma`,`Waterhouse-Friderichsen`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis**: acute deterioration with **absolute hypotension (systolic below 100 mmHg) or relative hypotension (20 mmHg or more below usual)** that resolves within 1-2 hours of parenteral glucocorticoid - the working definition used in trials and guidelines. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`Adrenal crises occur in about **6-8 per 100 patient-years** in people with known adrenal insufficiency, with a mortality of about **0.5 per 100 patient-years** - many deaths are from delayed hydrocortisone. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency** is now the commonest cause: anyone on **prednisolone 5 mg/day or more (or equivalent) for 4 weeks or more**, and many on high-dose inhaled, intra-articular or topical steroids, can develop a crisis when steroids stop or illness strikes. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Hypercalcaemic crisis** (severe hypercalcaemia): serum calcium usually **over 14 mg/dL (3.5 mmol/L)** with **multi-organ dysfunction** - dehydration, AKI, altered sensorium, arrhythmia; most often from **malignancy** or **primary hyperparathyroidism**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hypercalcaemia of malignancy occurs in about **20-30% of patients with cancer** at some time and carries a median survival of a few months; in India, **vitamin D toxicity from repeated mega-dose injections (600,000 IU)** is a well-recognised cause. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy** affects about **2-12% of pituitary adenomas**, often as the **first presentation** of an unknown (frequently non-functioning) macroadenoma; peak age 40-60 years with slight male excess. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`Indian data: Indian series of adrenal insufficiency show **tuberculosis** still a leading cause of primary adrenal insufficiency, alongside autoimmune adrenalitis; Indian PHPT registry data show many patients present with **bone disease, stones and higher calcium** than in the West. [Indian PHPT Registry]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and precipitants`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis precipitants**: **gastroenteritis (commonest)**, other infections, surgery, trauma, **abrupt steroid withdrawal**, missed doses, vomiting preventing oral intake, and strenuous exertion or heat. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Primary adrenal insufficiency**: autoimmune adrenalitis, **adrenal TB**, fungal infections (histoplasmosis), HIV-related, metastases, **adrenal haemorrhage** (anticoagulants, antiphospholipid syndrome, **meningococcal sepsis - Waterhouse-Friderichsen**), CAH, adrenoleukodystrophy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary/tertiary**: exogenous glucocorticoids (commonest overall), pituitary tumours or apoplexy, **Sheehan syndrome**, opioids, **immune checkpoint inhibitor hypophysitis**. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Drugs that precipitate crisis**: starting **levothyroxine before hydrocortisone** in combined deficiency (accelerates cortisol clearance), **CYP3A4 inducers** (rifampicin - relevant in TB, phenytoin), and ketoconazole, etomidate, mitotane, osilodrostat. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia causes**: **PTH-dependent** - primary or tertiary hyperparathyroidism, lithium, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia; **PTH-independent** - **PTHrP (humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy, about 80%)**, osteolytic metastases (breast, myeloma), **calcitriol-mediated** (lymphoma, TB, sarcoidosis), vitamin D toxicity, thiazides, immobilisation, milk-alkali syndrome, thyrotoxicosis. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy precipitants** (present in about 40%): **hypertension**, major surgery (especially **cardiac bypass**), **anticoagulants/antiplatelets**, dopamine agonists, GnRH agonists, **dynamic pituitary testing**, pregnancy, head trauma, radiotherapy. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Cortisol is permissive for vascular tone**: it up-regulates **alpha-adrenergic receptors and angiotensin II responsiveness**, so its absence causes **catecholamine-resistant vasodilatory shock** - pressors fail until hydrocortisone is given. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aldosterone deficiency** in primary disease causes sodium loss, volume depletion and **hyperkalaemia**; secondary disease spares aldosterone, so **potassium is usually normal** but **hyponatraemia** still occurs from ADH excess (cortisol normally suppresses ADH). [Harrison 22e]`,`Low cortisol impairs gluconeogenesis - **hypoglycaemia**, especially in children and secondary insufficiency; high ACTH in primary disease stimulates melanocortin-1 receptors - **pigmentation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydrocortisone at 50 mg or more per day has enough mineralocorticoid activity** - so fludrocortisone is not needed during crisis treatment. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hypercalcaemia** causes **nephrogenic diabetes insipidus** (calcium-sensing receptor in the thick ascending limb and collecting duct reduces aquaporin-2), so polyuria worsens dehydration, GFR falls, calcium excretion falls - a **vicious cycle** that saline breaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`High calcium **shortens the QT** (shortened ST segment), slows conduction, reduces neuronal excitability (**confusion, coma**) and smooth muscle tone (**constipation, ileus**), and precipitates **pancreatitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bisphosphonates and denosumab** stop osteoclastic bone resorption - the main driver of malignant hypercalcaemia - but act over **2-4 days**, while **calcitonin** works within **hours** but escapes in **48 hours (tachyphylaxis)**. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy**: a macroadenoma outgrows its blood supply in a tight sella; haemorrhage or infarction suddenly expands it upward (**chiasm - bitemporal field loss**) and laterally into the **cavernous sinus (III, IV, VI nerves)**; blood in the subarachnoid space gives **meningism**; **ACTH deficiency** in about 70% causes the life-threatening adrenal crisis. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`]},{heading:`Classification and scoring`,points:[`**Adrenal insufficiency by level**: **primary** (adrenal; high ACTH, low aldosterone, hyperkalaemia, pigmentation), **secondary** (pituitary; low ACTH, aldosterone intact), **tertiary** (hypothalamic, including exogenous steroids). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hypercalcaemia severity**: **mild** - corrected calcium under 12 mg/dL; **moderate** - 12-14 mg/dL; **severe** - over 14 mg/dL; symptoms and the speed of rise matter as much as the number. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Corrected calcium** = measured calcium (mg/dL) + 0.8 x (4.0 - albumin g/dL); **ionised calcium** is preferred when albumin is very abnormal or in myeloma (paraprotein binding). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pituitary Apoplexy Score (PAS)** sums level of consciousness (GCS), visual acuity, visual field defect and ocular palsy, from 0 to 10 - used to track the patient and guide surgery decisions. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Hypercalcaemia mechanism groups** (for the viva): PTH-mediated, PTHrP-mediated, calcitriol-mediated, osteolytic, and other (drugs, immobilisation, endocrine). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Related endocrine emergencies to name**: thyroid storm (**Burch-Wartofsky score 45 or more** highly suggestive), myxoedema coma, DKA/HHS, hypoglycaemia, phaeochromocytoma crisis, hypocalcaemic tetany and severe hyponatraemia - each has its own topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis**: profound weakness, **vomiting, abdominal pain** (can mimic an acute abdomen), fever, confusion, collapse - in a patient on steroids, with known Addison or hypopituitarism, or after stopping long-term prednisolone. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Clues to undiagnosed Addison**: months of fatigue, weight loss, salt craving, postural dizziness, **pigmentation**, and previous **TB** - patients are often misdiagnosed as gastroenteritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia ('bones, stones, groans, thrones and psychic moans')**: polyuria, thirst, constipation, nausea, abdominal pain, bone pain, renal colic, **confusion, drowsiness, depression**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia history**: weight loss, cough, haemoptysis, breast lump, bone pain (malignancy); kidney stones over years (PHPT); **vitamin D injections, calcium supplements, antacids**, thiazides, lithium. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy**: **sudden severe headache** (often retro-orbital, thunderclap) in over 80%, vomiting, **visual blurring or loss**, **diplopia or ptosis**, drowsiness; prior symptoms of a pituitary tumour (amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea, acromegaly) may be elicited. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`Ask every collapsed or vomiting patient: **'Do you take steroids, or have you stopped them recently?'** - and look for a steroid card or bracelet. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis**: hypotension or shock **unresponsive to fluids and pressors**, tachycardia, dehydration, fever, confusion; abdominal tenderness without peritonism. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Pigmentation** of palmar creases, buccal mucosa, gums, scars and knuckles - primary disease only; **pallor and absent pigmentation** with loss of axillary/pubic hair - pituitary cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Purpuric rash with shock** - meningococcaemia with adrenal haemorrhage (Waterhouse-Friderichsen). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia**: dehydration, **reduced reflexes**, confusion, band keratopathy (chronic), **short QT on ECG**, bradycardia or heart block; look for lymphadenopathy, breast lump, hepatomegaly, bone tenderness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy**: **bitemporal hemianopia** or reduced acuity, **third nerve palsy (ptosis, down-and-out eye, dilated pupil)** - the commonest ocular palsy, sixth and fourth nerve palsies, meningism, reduced GCS; **hypotension** if cortisol-deficient. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Cushingoid habitus in a shocked patient** - suspect steroid withdrawal adrenal crisis rather than excluding adrenal insufficiency. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis** is confused with **septic shock, acute abdomen, gastroenteritis and DKA**; the clue is **hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia** and shock that does not respond to pressors. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hypercalcaemia vs other causes of confusion with dehydration**: HHS, hyponatraemia, uraemia, sepsis - always check calcium in a confused elderly patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTH distinguishes hypercalcaemia**: **raised or inappropriately normal PTH** - PHPT (or FHH, lithium); **suppressed PTH** - malignancy, vitamin D, granulomas, milk-alkali. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy vs subarachnoid haemorrhage**: both cause thunderclap headache and meningism; **visual field loss, ophthalmoplegia** and a sellar mass point to apoplexy; CT/CTA and MRI decide. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Apoplexy vs other mimics**: bacterial meningitis (CSF can show pleocytosis in apoplexy too), cavernous sinus thrombosis, basilar artery thrombosis, ophthalmoplegic migraine, posterior communicating aneurysm (third nerve palsy). [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Thyroid storm** (fever, tachycardia, AF, agitation, heart failure) and **myxoedema coma** (hypothermia, bradycardia, hyponatraemia) - covered in the Thyroid storm and myxoedema coma topic; both need hydrocortisone cover. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Adrenal crisis**: draw **serum cortisol and plasma ACTH** (plus renin/aldosterone) before hydrocortisone if it causes **no delay** - then treat; **never wait for results**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Random cortisol in a sick patient**: **below about 3-5 mcg/dL (80-140 nmol/L) strongly supports** adrenal insufficiency; values above 18 mcg/dL (500 nmol/L, assay-dependent - lower with newer specific assays) make it unlikely; intermediate values need a **250 mcg Synacthen test** once stable. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Crisis labs**: sodium (low in about 70-80%), potassium (high in primary), glucose, urea/creatinine, full blood count (eosinophilia), and cultures; **chest X-ray and abdominal CT** if TB or adrenal haemorrhage suspected (enlarged or calcified adrenals). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia**: repeat **albumin-corrected or ionised calcium**, then **intact PTH** first; then **PTHrP, 25-OH vitamin D, 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D**, phosphate, magnesium, creatinine, ALP, **serum protein electrophoresis and free light chains**, TSH, and ECG. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**25-OH vitamin D above 150 ng/mL** with suppressed PTH indicates **vitamin D toxicity**; high **1,25-(OH)2D** with suppressed PTH suggests granulomatous disease or lymphoma. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy**: **MRI pituitary is the investigation of choice** (sensitivity about 90%); **urgent CT** first if MRI is unavailable or SAH is suspected (CT detects haemorrhage in about 80% but often misses infarction). [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Apoplexy labs**: urgent **cortisol**, sodium, glucose, and baseline **TSH/free T4, prolactin, IGF-1, LH/FSH and testosterone or oestradiol**; **formal visual fields and acuity** by ophthalmology as soon as possible. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Low prolactin** after apoplexy predicts more severe pituitary damage; a **very high prolactin** suggests an underlying prolactinoma that may respond to dopamine agonists. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care - prevention of crisis`,points:[`**Maintenance in primary adrenal insufficiency**: **hydrocortisone 15-25 mg/day in 2-3 divided doses** (largest on waking, e.g. 10 + 5 + 2.5 mg) plus **fludrocortisone 50-100 mcg once daily**; prednisolone 3-5 mg/day is an alternative. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Sick day rule 1**: **double the oral dose (triple in some protocols)** during fever over 38 C, infection needing antibiotics or bed rest, until recovered (usually 2-3 days). [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`**Sick day rule 2**: if **vomiting, diarrhoea or unable to take tablets**, inject **hydrocortisone 100 mg IM** (children 50 mg/m2, or by age: infants 25 mg, school age 50 mg) and **go to hospital**. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`**Every patient** should carry a **steroid emergency card**, wear a medical alert bracelet, and keep an **emergency hydrocortisone injection kit** (100 mg vial) at home, with family trained to give it. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`**Patients on long-term glucocorticoids** (prednisolone 5 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more): **never stop abruptly**; taper, and give sick-day rules and stress cover - see the Glucocorticoid therapy topic. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Mild hypercalcaemia in OPD**: stop **thiazides, lithium, calcium and vitamin D supplements**, encourage **oral fluids 2-3 L/day**, and investigate with PTH; **refer** calcium over 12 mg/dL or any symptoms. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Known pituitary macroadenoma**: warn about sudden headache and visual change, and caution with anticoagulation and major surgery. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - adrenal crisis`,points:[`**Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV (or IM) bolus immediately**, then **200 mg/24 h** as a **continuous IV infusion** or **50 mg IV/IM every 6 hours**; children **50 mg/m2** bolus then **50-100 mg/m2/24 h**. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Fluids**: **1 L of 0.9% saline over the first hour**, then guided by haemodynamics (often 3-4 L in 24 h); children **20 mL/kg** bolus. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hypoglycaemia**: **dextrose** (e.g. 5-10% dextrose in saline, or 25 g IV if glucose below 70 mg/dL). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**If hydrocortisone is unavailable**: **dexamethasone 4 mg IV** (no mineralocorticoid action) or **methylprednisolone 40 mg IV**; hydrocortisone remains preferred. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Treat the precipitant** (antibiotics for infection, anti-TB therapy - remembering **rifampicin doubles the hydrocortisone requirement**); monitor BP, urine output, sodium, potassium and glucose; HDU/ICU if shocked. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Avoid over-rapid correction of sodium** in chronic hyponatraemia: aim no more than **8-10 mmol/L in 24 hours** - hydrocortisone itself causes a brisk water diuresis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Taper** to oral **double maintenance dose over 1-3 days** once the patient is stable and eating, then to usual dose; restart **fludrocortisone** when hydrocortisone falls below 50 mg/day in primary disease. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Surgical stress cover**: major surgery - **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction then 200 mg/24 h**, halving daily as recovery allows; minor procedures - double the usual dose on the day. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - hypercalcaemic crisis and pituitary apoplexy`,points:[`**Hypercalcaemia step 1 - rehydrate**: **0.9% saline 200-300 mL/h** (or 1-2 L in the first hours if very dry), adjusted to keep **urine output 100-150 mL/h**; caution in heart failure and CKD; **loop diuretics are not routine** - furosemide only **after volume repletion** for fluid overload. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Step 2 - calcitonin 4 IU/kg SC/IM every 12 hours** (increase to 8 IU/kg every 6-12 h if needed) for **calcium over 14 mg/dL** or symptoms - lowers calcium by about 1-2 mg/dL within 4-6 hours; stop after **48 hours** (tachyphylaxis). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Step 3 - antiresorptive**: **zoledronic acid 4 mg IV over at least 15 minutes** (dose-reduce for CrCl 30-60; avoid if CrCl below 30 or creatinine over 4.5 mg/dL) or **pamidronate 60-90 mg IV over 2-4 hours**; effect starts in 2-4 days and lasts 2-4 weeks. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Denosumab 120 mg SC** - the Endocrine Society 2023 guideline **suggests denosumab over bisphosphonate** in hypercalcaemia of malignancy, and it is the choice when **bisphosphonates fail or renal function is poor** (start 60 mg or less in severe CKD with close monitoring - severe hypocalcaemia risk). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Cause-specific**: **glucocorticoids** (prednisolone 40-60 mg/day) for **calcitriol-mediated** hypercalcaemia (lymphoma, TB, sarcoidosis, vitamin D toxicity); **cinacalcet** for parathyroid carcinoma; **urgent parathyroidectomy** for PHPT crisis once stable; treat the cancer. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Haemodialysis** with low or zero calcium dialysate for **calcium over 18 mg/dL with neurological signs**, or severe hypercalcaemia with **renal failure or heart failure** precluding saline. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy**: **hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV bolus** then **50-100 mg IV/IM every 6 hours** (or 2-4 mg/h infusion) **in any patient with haemodynamic instability, altered consciousness, reduced vision or severe hypopituitarism** - after drawing cortisol. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Apoplexy surgery vs conservative**: **urgent transsphenoidal decompression** (ideally within 7 days) for **severely reduced acuity, persistent or worsening field defects, or deteriorating consciousness**; isolated **ocular palsy** usually recovers with **conservative care**; decision by a pituitary multidisciplinary team, monitoring visual fields, acuity and **sodium (SIADH or diabetes insipidus may follow)** daily. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Pointer - thyroid storm**: propranolol 60-80 mg 4-hourly, **PTU 200 mg 4-hourly** (or methimazole 20 mg 4-6-hourly), **iodine 1 hour after thionamide**, **hydrocortisone 100 mg 8-hourly** - full details in the Thyroid storm topic. [ATA 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: adrenal crisis can occur in hyperemesis, labour and postpartum - **hydrocortisone 100 mg IM/IV at the onset of active labour**, then 200 mg/24 h until delivery; hydrocortisone does not cross to the fetus significantly (placental 11-beta-HSD2). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Pregnancy and apoplexy**: pituitary enlarges physiologically; apoplexy in pregnancy and **Sheehan syndrome postpartum** - see the Hypopituitarism topic. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Hypercalcaemia in pregnancy**: usually PHPT; saline and calcitonin are safe; **bisphosphonates and denosumab are avoided**; parathyroidectomy in the second trimester. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Elderly**: hypercalcaemia presents as **confusion or constipation alone**; adrenal crisis as falls and delirium; cautious fluids for heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: zoledronate dose reduced or avoided; denosumab causes **profound hypocalcaemia** in advanced CKD; dialysis is often the safest calcium-lowering method. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Children**: crisis presents with **hypoglycaemia** and seizures; **CAH neonates** with salt-wasting crisis at 1-3 weeks (vomiting, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia) - hydrocortisone 50 mg/m2 IV. [Endocrine Society CAH 2018]`,`**Septic shock**: hydrocortisone **200 mg/day** is used for vasopressor-dependent shock regardless of adrenal testing - do not confuse this with crisis treatment, though doses overlap. [SSC 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if hydrocortisone is unavailable, **dexamethasone 4 mg IV/IM** or **prednisolone** may be used; keep hydrocortisone in every PHC emergency tray; **IM** route if no IV access. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Immune checkpoint inhibitor patients**: new fatigue, headache or hypotension suggest **hypophysitis** - check cortisol and treat as adrenal insufficiency. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Untreated adrenal crisis** is rapidly fatal from **shock, hyperkalaemic arrhythmia and hypoglycaemia**; with prompt hydrocortisone most recover within hours. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Over-rapid sodium correction** during crisis treatment risks **osmotic demyelination syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemic crisis** complications: **AKI**, arrhythmia, pancreatitis, coma; malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia predicts **median survival of weeks to months**. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Bisphosphonate and denosumab adverse effects**: acute-phase reaction (fever, myalgia), **hypocalcaemia**, nephrotoxicity (zoledronate), **osteonecrosis of the jaw** with repeated doses. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Pituitary apoplexy outcomes**: **ocular palsies recover in most** patients (conservative or surgical); visual acuity and fields improve in many; **permanent hypopituitarism in about 50-80%**, needing lifelong replacement; mortality is now low but rises with delayed steroid cover. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Recurrent adrenal crises** cluster in patients with poor education and previous crises - each crisis is a prompt to re-educate. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Admit immediately**: suspected adrenal crisis, corrected calcium **over 14 mg/dL or symptomatic at any level**, and every suspected pituitary apoplexy (with neurosurgery and ophthalmology input). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`After adrenal crisis: **endocrine review**, confirm diagnosis (Synacthen test when stable, unless already known), re-teach sick-day rules, prescribe the **IM hydrocortisone kit**, and issue a **steroid card**. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`After hypercalcaemia: calcium and creatinine **daily in hospital**, then weekly; repeat antiresorptive no sooner than **7 days** after the first dose if calcium remains high. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`After apoplexy: **dynamic pituitary testing at 4-8 weeks**, **MRI at 3-6 months** then yearly for 5 years if conservative (tumour regrowth), and visual field checks. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Annual review** of adrenal insufficiency: weight, BP, electrolytes, symptoms of over- or under-replacement, emergency kit expiry date. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Steroid emergency card** (NHS England national card 2020): carries diagnosis, daily dose and the instruction to give **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM** in emergencies; Indian patients can carry an equivalent hospital-issued card and medical alert bracelet. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`**Educate family members** to give IM hydrocortisone; most crisis deaths are in patients who never self-injected. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Rational steroid use** - counter over-the-counter and quack steroid use (including **steroid-laced 'herbal' preparations**), an important cause of iatrogenic Cushing and withdrawal crisis in India. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Vitamin D safety**: avoid repeated **600,000 IU injections**; use oral cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks for deficiency and check levels before repeating courses - see the Vitamin D topic. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**TB control** under **NTEP** prevents adrenal TB; remember **rifampicin** when dosing hydrocortisone in TB patients. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESE/Endocrine Society 2024 guideline on glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency**: no routine testing while on supraphysiological doses; taper to physiological dose, then test or continue tapering; **stress dosing and steroid card for all at risk**; adrenal crisis treatment hydrocortisone 100 mg then 200 mg/24 h. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Endocrine Society 2023 (Fuleihan) hypercalcaemia of malignancy guideline**: **denosumab suggested over bisphosphonates**; calcitonin plus antiresorptive for severe; glucocorticoids for calcitriol-mediated; cinacalcet for parathyroid carcinoma. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**NHS England/Society for Endocrinology 2020** national steroid emergency card and patient safety alert; updated 2024 sick-day guidance continues double-dose rules. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`,`**UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011** (Rajasekaran) remain the main reference; recent series support **conservative management** with similar visual and endocrine outcomes in selected patients without severe visual loss. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`**Endocrine Society 2016 primary adrenal insufficiency guideline** remains current for diagnosis and maintenance replacement. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Note on conflicts**: some protocols give hydrocortisone **50 mg 6-hourly** and others a **200 mg/24 h infusion** - both are accepted; the infusion avoids troughs. [ESE/Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Management of adrenal crisis'** - draw sample, **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV**, 1 L saline in 1 hour, glucose, 200 mg/24 h, find precipitant, taper, educate. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`Common mistake: **waiting for cortisol results** or giving dexamethasone when hydrocortisone is available. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`Common mistake: **thyroxine before hydrocortisone** in hypopituitarism - precipitates crisis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **furosemide before rehydration** in hypercalcaemia. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`Viva: 'Why does calcitonin stop working?' - receptor down-regulation (**tachyphylaxis in 48 h**). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`Viva: 'Commonest cranial nerve palsy in apoplexy?' - **third (oculomotor)**. [UK Pituitary Apoplexy Guidelines 2011]`,`Viva: 'Why no fludrocortisone during crisis?' - hydrocortisone **50 mg/day or more** gives enough mineralocorticoid effect. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Emergency drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Hydrocortisone (adrenal crisis)`,`100 mg IV/IM bolus, then 200 mg/24 h infusion or 50 mg 6-hourly; child 50 mg/m2 then 50-100 mg/m2/24 h`,`Taper over 1-3 days to double oral dose`,`Hyperglycaemia, rapid sodium rise; none in emergency`],[`0.9% saline`,`1 L over first hour (child 20 mL/kg), then by response; hypercalcaemia 200-300 mL/h`,`Guided by BP and urine output 100-150 mL/h`,`Fluid overload in heart failure or CKD`],[`Calcitonin (salmon)`,`4 IU/kg SC/IM 12-hourly, up to 8 IU/kg 6-12-hourly`,`48 hours only`,`Tachyphylaxis, nausea, flushing, rare allergy`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV over at least 15 min (reduce for CrCl 30-60)`,`Single dose; repeat after 7 days if needed`,`Acute-phase reaction, hypocalcaemia, nephrotoxicity, jaw osteonecrosis; avoid CrCl under 30`],[`Pamidronate`,`60-90 mg IV over 2-4 h`,`Single dose`,`As zoledronate; slower onset`],[`Denosumab`,`120 mg SC (lower dose in severe CKD)`,`Repeat at intervals per response`,`Severe hypocalcaemia (especially CKD, low vitamin D), jaw osteonecrosis`],[`Prednisolone (calcitriol-mediated)`,`40-60 mg/day orally`,`3-7 days then taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection`],[`Hydrocortisone (pituitary apoplexy)`,`100-200 mg IV bolus, then 50-100 mg 6-hourly or 2-4 mg/h`,`Until stable, then oral replacement`,`As above`],[`Fludrocortisone (maintenance, primary AI)`,`50-100 mcg orally daily`,`Restart when hydrocortisone below 50 mg/day`,`Hypertension, hypokalaemia, oedema`]]},{heading:`Three emergencies compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Adrenal crisis`,`Hypercalcaemic crisis`,`Pituitary apoplexy`],rows:[[`Typical setting`,`Known AI, steroid withdrawal, infection, TB adrenals`,`Cancer, PHPT, vitamin D toxicity`,`Unknown macroadenoma, hypertension, surgery, anticoagulants`],[`Key symptoms`,`Vomiting, abdominal pain, collapse, confusion`,`Polyuria, constipation, confusion, coma`,`Thunderclap headache, visual loss, diplopia`],[`Key signs`,`Shock resistant to pressors, pigmentation`,`Dehydration, short QT, hyporeflexia`,`Bitemporal field loss, III nerve palsy, meningism`],[`Key labs`,`Low Na, high K, low glucose, low cortisol`,`High corrected Ca, PTH low or high`,`Low cortisol, low Na, MRI haemorrhage`],[`First drug`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV`,`0.9% saline, then calcitonin`,`Hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV`],[`Definitive`,`Treat precipitant, education`,`Zoledronate or denosumab, treat cause`,`Decompression if vision threatened`]]},{heading:`Causes of hypercalcaemia by PTH`,columns:[`PTH`,`Cause`,`Clue`],rows:[[`High or inappropriately normal`,`Primary hyperparathyroidism`,`Chronic, stones, bone disease, low phosphate`],[`High or inappropriately normal`,`Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia`,`Calcium/creatinine clearance ratio under 0.01`],[`High or inappropriately normal`,`Lithium, tertiary hyperparathyroidism`,`Drug history, CKD`],[`Suppressed`,`Humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy`,`High PTHrP; squamous, renal, breast cancers`],[`Suppressed`,`Osteolytic metastases, myeloma`,`Bone pain, paraprotein, anaemia`],[`Suppressed`,`Calcitriol-mediated (lymphoma, TB, sarcoidosis)`,`High 1,25-(OH)2D`],[`Suppressed`,`Vitamin D toxicity`,`25-OH D over 150 ng/mL; injection history`],[`Suppressed`,`Milk-alkali, thiazides, immobilisation, thyrotoxicosis`,`History`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Random cortisol in acute illness`,`Under 3-5 mcg/dL`,`Strongly supports adrenal insufficiency`],[`Random cortisol in acute illness`,`Over 18 mcg/dL (assay dependent)`,`Adrenal insufficiency unlikely`],[`250 mcg Synacthen test (when stable)`,`30 or 60 min cortisol under about 14-18 mcg/dL (assay dependent)`,`Adrenal insufficiency`],[`Plasma ACTH`,`High (over 2x ULN) with low cortisol`,`Primary adrenal insufficiency`],[`Corrected calcium`,`Over 14 mg/dL`,`Severe; crisis if symptomatic`],[`25-OH vitamin D`,`Over 150 ng/mL`,`Vitamin D toxicity`],[`MRI pituitary`,`Haemorrhage or infarct in sellar mass`,`Pituitary apoplexy (investigation of choice)`],[`Pituitary Apoplexy Score`,`0-10 (GCS, acuity, fields, ocular palsy)`,`Higher score favours surgery`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension or shock in a patient on long-term steroids or with known adrenal insufficiency`,`Vomiting and unable to take oral hydrocortisone`,`Hyponatraemia with hyperkalaemia and hypoglycaemia`,`Corrected calcium over 14 mg/dL, or any hypercalcaemia with confusion, AKI or arrhythmia`,`Thunderclap headache with visual loss or ophthalmoplegia`,`Falling visual acuity or consciousness in pituitary apoplexy`,`Purpuric rash with shock (adrenal haemorrhage)`],pearls:[`Adrenal crisis: never wait - 100 mg hydrocortisone, then 200 mg per 24 hours.`,`Shock that pressors cannot fix - think cortisol.`,`Hydrocortisone before thyroxine, always.`,`Rifampicin doubles the hydrocortisone dose.`,`Sick day rules: fever = double; vomiting = inject 100 mg IM and come in.`,`Hypercalcaemia: fill the tank (saline), then calcitonin now, bisphosphonate or denosumab for later.`,`Furosemide only after rehydration.`,`Calcitonin escapes in 48 hours.`,`Apoplexy: steroid first, scan second, scalpel if sight is going.`,`Third nerve palsy is the commonest ocular palsy in apoplexy.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman on prednisolone 10 mg daily for 2 years for rheumatoid arthritis stopped it a week ago. She presents with vomiting, abdominal pain and BP 76/40 mmHg. Sodium is 126 mmol/L and glucose 62 mg/dL. What is the immediate treatment?`,options:[`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV with 0.9% saline, after drawing cortisol if no delay`,`Synacthen test first to confirm adrenal insufficiency`,`Noradrenaline infusion and broad-spectrum antibiotics only`,`Restart oral prednisolone 10 mg and observe`],answer:0,explanation:`This is an adrenal crisis after abrupt steroid withdrawal; hydrocortisone 100 mg IV with a litre of saline in the first hour is given at once, with a cortisol sample only if it causes no delay. Testing first delays life-saving treatment; pressors alone fail in cortisol deficiency; oral prednisolone cannot be relied on in a vomiting, shocked patient.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-q2`,stem:`A 68-year-old man with squamous lung cancer is confused, with corrected calcium 15.2 mg/dL, creatinine 1.8 mg/dL and dry mucosa. What is the first step?`,options:[`Intravenous furosemide 40 mg`,`Zoledronic acid 4 mg IV as the sole treatment`,`Intravenous 0.9% saline to restore volume`,`Oral prednisolone 40 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`Hypercalcaemia causes nephrogenic DI and volume depletion, so saline (about 200-300 mL/h, aiming for urine output 100-150 mL/h) comes first, followed by calcitonin and an antiresorptive. Furosemide before rehydration worsens dehydration; zoledronate takes 2-4 days to act and needs renal dosing; steroids help only calcitriol-mediated hypercalcaemia, not PTHrP-driven squamous cancer.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-q3`,stem:`A 52-year-old hypertensive man develops sudden severe headache, vomiting and a left ptosis with a dilated pupil 2 days after coronary bypass surgery. BP is 92/60 mmHg. CT head shows a hyperdense sellar mass. What should be given first?`,options:[`Cabergoline orally`,`Hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV`,`Mannitol infusion`,`Nimodipine orally`],answer:1,explanation:`This is pituitary apoplexy (sellar haemorrhage, third nerve palsy, hypotension) after cardiac surgery; acute ACTH deficiency is the immediate threat, so hydrocortisone 100-200 mg IV is given first, then MRI and neurosurgical review. Cabergoline helps only prolactinoma and is not first; mannitol is not indicated; nimodipine is for aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-q4`,stem:`Which hypercalcaemia treatment acts within hours but loses effect after about 48 hours because of tachyphylaxis?`,options:[`Zoledronic acid`,`Denosumab`,`Prednisolone`,`Calcitonin`],answer:3,explanation:`Calcitonin inhibits osteoclasts and increases renal calcium excretion within 4-6 hours but its receptors down-regulate within about 48 hours, so it bridges until slower drugs act. Zoledronic acid and denosumab take 2-4 days to act but last weeks; prednisolone works over days and only in calcitriol-mediated disease.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-q5`,stem:`A man with Addison disease due to adrenal tuberculosis is started on rifampicin-based anti-TB therapy. What change is needed in his steroid replacement?`,options:[`Stop fludrocortisone`,`No change, as rifampicin does not interact with hydrocortisone`,`Increase hydrocortisone, typically doubling the dose`,`Switch to dexamethasone 0.5 mg daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Rifampicin induces CYP3A4 and accelerates cortisol clearance, so hydrocortisone usually needs to be doubled to avoid precipitating a crisis. Saying there is no interaction ignores this enzyme induction; stopping fludrocortisone would cause salt wasting in primary disease; dexamethasone is also metabolised faster and lacks mineralocorticoid action.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the causes, clinical features and management of adrenal crisis. Add a note on sick-day rules and prevention.`,openingLines:[`Adrenal crisis is life-threatening acute cortisol deficiency presenting with hypotension or shock that responds to parenteral glucocorticoid within 1-2 hours.`,`Treatment is immediate hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM, then 200 mg/24 h, with 1 L of 0.9% saline in the first hour - never delayed for tests.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes and precipitants`,points:[`Primary: autoimmune, TB, adrenal haemorrhage, metastases, CAH. Secondary/tertiary: exogenous steroids (commonest), pituitary disease, apoplexy, Sheehan, checkpoint inhibitors.`,`Precipitants: gastroenteritis, infection, surgery, steroid withdrawal, thyroxine before hydrocortisone, rifampicin.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Loss of cortisol's permissive vascular effect - pressor-resistant shock; aldosterone loss - hyperkalaemia; hypoglycaemia; ADH excess - hyponatraemia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and investigations`,points:[`Vomiting, abdominal pain, confusion, shock, fever, pigmentation (primary).`,`Draw cortisol and ACTH; Na, K, glucose, creatinine, cultures; Synacthen when stable.`]},{heading:`Emergency management`,points:[`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM, then 200 mg/24 h infusion or 50 mg 6-hourly.`,`0.9% saline 1 L in first hour; dextrose for hypoglycaemia; treat precipitant; monitor in HDU.`,`Taper over 1-3 days to double oral dose; fludrocortisone when hydrocortisone below 50 mg/day.`]},{heading:`Prevention and sick-day rules`,points:[`Double dose for fever or illness; 100 mg IM if vomiting and go to hospital.`,`Steroid emergency card, bracelet, IM kit, family training; surgical stress cover.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of adrenal crisis management`,`Sick-day rules box`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Emergency management`,marks:4},{part:`Sick-day rules and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`adrenal crisis`,`hydrocortisone 100 mg`,`200 mg/24 h`,`sick-day rules`,`steroid card`,`rifampicin`]},{id:`endocrine-endocrine-emergencies-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of hypercalcaemic crisis.`,openingLines:[`Hypercalcaemic crisis is severe hypercalcaemia (usually over 14 mg/dL) with dehydration, AKI or altered sensorium, most often from malignancy or primary hyperparathyroidism.`,`Management is rehydration, rapid calcitonin, an antiresorptive and treatment of the cause.`],answer:[{heading:`Confirm and assess`,points:[`Corrected or ionised calcium, creatinine, ECG; PTH, PTHrP, vitamin D metabolites, SPEP.`]},{heading:`Steps`,points:[`0.9% saline 200-300 mL/h, urine output 100-150 mL/h; stop calcium, vitamin D, thiazides, lithium.`,`Calcitonin 4 IU/kg 12-hourly for 48 h.`,`Zoledronate 4 mg IV over 15 min or denosumab 120 mg SC (preferred in malignancy by Endocrine Society 2023; renal impairment).`,`Glucocorticoids for calcitriol-mediated; cinacalcet for parathyroid carcinoma; parathyroidectomy for PHPT.`,`Haemodialysis for calcium over 18 mg/dL with neurological signs or renal failure.`]},{heading:`Monitor`,points:[`Calcium and creatinine daily; watch for hypocalcaemia after denosumab.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: saline - calcitonin - zoledronate/denosumab - cause-specific - dialysis`],markSplit:[{part:`Assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Flow chart with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Cause-specific treatment and monitoring`,marks:1}],keywords:[`hypercalcaemia`,`saline`,`calcitonin`,`zoledronic acid`,`denosumab`,`dialysis`]}],references:[`Bornstein SR et al. Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2016`,`Beuschlein F et al. European Society of Endocrinology and Endocrine Society Joint Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and therapy of glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency, 2024`,`El-Hajj Fuleihan G et al. Treatment of Hypercalcemia of Malignancy in Adults: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2023`,`Rajasekaran S et al. UK guidelines for the management of pituitary apoplexy (Society for Endocrinology), 2011`,`Society for Endocrinology / NHS England. Steroid Emergency Card and adrenal crisis guidance, 2020`,`Ross DS et al. American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis, 2016`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India, 2023`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric`,title:`Obesity pharmacotherapy and bariatric surgery`,oneLiner:`Obesity is a **chronic, relapsing disease of excess adiposity**; when lifestyle therapy alone fails, **anti-obesity medication** (orlistat, liraglutide 3 mg, **semaglutide 2.4 mg**, **tirzepatide**) is added at BMI 27 or more (25 or more with a complication in Asian Indians), and **metabolic-bariatric surgery** is offered at an Asian BMI of **27.5 or more with metabolic disease and 32.5 or more regardless** (IFSO/ASMBS 2022) - always with lifelong nutritional follow-up.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`anti-obesity medication`,`AOM`,`orlistat`,`semaglutide 2.4 mg`,`Wegovy`,`tirzepatide`,`Mounjaro`,`Zepbound`,`liraglutide 3 mg`,`phentermine`,`naltrexone-bupropion`,`setmelanotide`,`sleeve gastrectomy`,`Roux-en-Y gastric bypass`,`one-anastomosis gastric bypass`,`metabolic surgery`,`dumping syndrome`,`clinical obesity`,`IFSO 2022`,`SELECT trial`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Obesity** is a chronic, relapsing, multifactorial disease of excess or dysfunctional adipose tissue that impairs health; it is treated like hypertension - long term, not as a short course. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asian-Indian BMI bands** (Indian consensus 2009, retained 2025): normal 18-22.9, **overweight 23-24.9, obesity 25 or more** kg/m2; abdominal obesity is waist **90 cm or more in men, 80 cm or more in women** - see the Obesity and metabolic syndrome topic for assessment. [Indian consensus 2009]`,`**Global burden**: in 2022 about **2.5 billion adults were overweight and 890 million had obesity** (1 in 8 people worldwide); adult obesity has more than doubled since 1990. [WHO 2024]`,`**India**: NFHS-5 (2019-21) found BMI 25 or more in **24% of women and 22.9% of men**, and high waist-hip ratio in a much larger share, especially in urban and southern states. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB** (Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023) estimated **generalised obesity in 28.6%** and **abdominal obesity in 39.5%** of Indian adults - about 250 million and 350 million people. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Childhood obesity** is rising in India (NFHS-5: overweight in 3.4% of under-5s), and the Lancet 2024 global analysis ranked India among countries with the largest absolute numbers of adults with obesity. [NCD-RisC Lancet 2024]`,`**Treatment gap**: fewer than 1 in 50 eligible people worldwide receive anti-obesity medication or surgery; cost and stigma are the main barriers in India. [EASO 2025]`,`Indian surgical volume: India performs an estimated **20,000-30,000 bariatric procedures a year**, mostly sleeve gastrectomy and one-anastomosis gastric bypass, almost all paid out of pocket. [OSSI 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors that shape treatment`,points:[`**Energy imbalance** from ultra-processed, energy-dense food, sugar-sweetened beverages, large portions and fried snacks, with low physical activity and urban sedentary work. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic contribution**: heritability of BMI is **40-70%**; over 1,000 common variants (FTO, MC4R) each add a little - so obesity is not a failure of willpower. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Monogenic obesity** (MC4R, leptin, leptin receptor, POMC, PCSK1 deficiency) and syndromes (**Prader-Willi**, Bardet-Biedl) present with severe early-onset obesity and hyperphagia; leptin deficiency responds to metreleptin, POMC/LEPR deficiency and BBS to **setmelanotide**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Drug-induced weight gain**: insulin, sulfonylureas, pioglitazone, glucocorticoids, olanzapine, clozapine, mirtazapine, valproate, gabapentin, pregabalin, beta-blockers (older), depot medroxyprogesterone - review these before prescribing an anti-obesity drug. [Endocrine Society 2015]`,`**Endocrine causes** are rare (under 1%): hypothyroidism, Cushing syndrome, hypothalamic damage, growth hormone deficiency, PCOS - test only when clinical clues exist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sleep deprivation, shift work, smoking cessation, stress and depression** all raise appetite and should be addressed in the plan. [AACE 2016]`,`**Thin-fat Indian phenotype**: more visceral and ectopic (liver, muscle) fat and less muscle at the same BMI, from fetal undernutrition followed by overnutrition - so complications appear at a lower BMI. [Indian obesity definition 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why weight is defended and how the drugs work`,points:[`Body weight is regulated by the **hypothalamic arcuate nucleus**: **POMC/CART neurons** (alpha-MSH acting on MC4R) cut appetite, while **AgRP/NPY neurons** drive hunger; leptin from fat and insulin signal stores, ghrelin from the stomach signals hunger. [Harrison 22e]`,`Obesity brings **leptin resistance** - levels are high but the hypothalamus no longer responds - so leptin itself is useless as a drug except in congenital leptin deficiency. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Metabolic adaptation** after diet: energy expenditure falls more than predicted, **ghrelin rises and GLP-1, PYY and leptin fall**, so hunger increases for years - the biology behind weight regain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GLP-1 receptor agonists** act on the hypothalamus and **area postrema/brainstem** to increase satiety and cut food reward, and slow gastric emptying; weight loss is driven mainly by eating less, not by nausea. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Tirzepatide** is a once-weekly **dual GIP and GLP-1 receptor agonist**; GIP adds central appetite suppression and improves adipose tissue insulin sensitivity, giving larger weight loss than GLP-1 agonism alone. [SURMOUNT-5 2025]`,`**Orlistat** is a reversible inhibitor of **gastric and pancreatic lipase** that blocks absorption of about **30% of dietary fat** - its side effects come from that unabsorbed fat. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phentermine** is a sympathomimetic amine releasing noradrenaline (appetite suppression); **naltrexone-bupropion** stimulates POMC neurons while blocking their auto-inhibition by beta-endorphin. [Endocrine Society 2015]`,`**Bariatric surgery is not just restriction**: sleeve and bypass raise **GLP-1 and PYY**, cut ghrelin (sleeve removes the fundus), change bile acids and the microbiome - which is why diabetes often remits within days, before weight loss. [ADA 2026]`,`**Excess adiposity harms through two routes**: mass effect (osteoarthritis, sleep apnoea, reflux) and metabolic effect (insulin resistance, MASLD, dyslipidaemia, hypertension, inflammation, cancer). [Lancet Commission 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging that decide treatment`,points:[`**WHO BMI classes** (Western): overweight 25-29.9, class I 30-34.9, class II 35-39.9, class III 40 or more kg/m2; **Asian cut-offs** are about 2.5 kg/m2 lower at each step (23, 27.5, 32.5, 37.5). [WHO 2004]`,`**Lancet Commission 2025** redefined obesity: **excess adiposity** must be confirmed (BMI plus waist, waist-hip or waist-height ratio, or direct fat measurement; BMI over 40 alone is enough), then classified as **preclinical obesity** (no organ dysfunction) or **clinical obesity** (organ dysfunction or limitation of daily activities). [Lancet Commission 2025]`,`**Indian obesity definition 2025** (Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol, Misra et al.): **stage 1** = BMI 23 or more without functional impairment or comorbidity; **stage 2** = BMI 23 or more **with** abdominal adiposity and an obesity-related functional impairment or disease - stage 2 needs active treatment including drugs. [Indian obesity definition 2025]`,`**Edmonton Obesity Staging System (EOSS 0-4)** grades metabolic, mechanical and mental burden and predicts mortality better than BMI; stage 2 or more favours drugs or surgery. [Sharma 2009]`,`**Drug classes**: lipase inhibitor (orlistat); incretin-based (liraglutide 3 mg, semaglutide 2.4 mg, tirzepatide); centrally acting (phentermine, phentermine-topiramate, naltrexone-bupropion - not marketed in India); monogenic (setmelanotide, metreleptin). [EASO 2025]`,`**Surgical classes**: restrictive-hormonal (**sleeve gastrectomy**, adjustable band), bypass (**RYGB, OAGB**), malabsorptive (BPD-DS, SADI-S), and endoscopic (intragastric balloon, endoscopic sleeve gastroplasty). [IFSO 2022]`,`**Withdrawn drugs examiners ask**: **sibutramine** (banned in India 2010 after SCOUT showed more MI and stroke), **rimonabant** (depression and suicide), **lorcaserin** (withdrawn 2020, cancer signal), fenfluramine (valvulopathy). [CDSCO 2010]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - history before prescribing`,points:[`**Weight history**: age of onset, maximum weight, previous attempts and what worked, weight gain triggers (pregnancy, job change, drugs, quitting smoking). [Endocrine Society 2015]`,`**Eating pattern**: 24-hour recall, night eating, binge eating (loss of control, eating alone, distress) - **binge-eating disorder** needs psychological treatment and may respond to lisdexamfetamine or topiramate, not to plain dieting. [AACE 2016]`,`**Complications screen**: snoring and daytime sleepiness (STOP-BANG), knee pain, breathlessness, reflux, irregular periods, infertility, urinary incontinence, erectile dysfunction, low mood - these define **clinical obesity**. [Lancet Commission 2025]`,`**Contraindication screen before GLP-1 or tirzepatide**: personal or family history of **medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2**, past pancreatitis, gallstones, proliferative retinopathy, gastroparesis, pregnancy plans. [FDA label 2024]`,`**Psychiatric and substance history**: depression, suicidal ideation, eating disorders, alcohol use - essential before surgery and before centrally acting drugs. [ASMBS 2022]`,`**Readiness and resources**: motivation (5 As - Ask, Assess, Advise, Agree, Assist), family support, ability to afford a weekly injection for at least 1-2 years. [Obesity Canada 2020]`,`**Women of reproductive age**: contraception and pregnancy plans - **tirzepatide reduces oral contraceptive absorption**, and all incretin drugs must stop before conception. [FDA label 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Weight, height, BMI, waist** (midpoint between costal margin and iliac crest) and **waist-to-height ratio** (0.5 or more is excess central fat) at every visit. [Indian obesity definition 2025]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans and skin tags** mark hyperinsulinaemia; check fasting glucose and HbA1c. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Purple striae over 1 cm, proximal myopathy, thin skin, easy bruising** suggest Cushing syndrome rather than simple obesity - white or pink striae are common in ordinary obesity. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Neck circumference** over 43 cm (men) or 40 cm (women) and crowded oropharynx (Mallampati 3-4) predict obstructive sleep apnoea and a difficult airway before surgery. [ASMBS 2022]`,`**BP with a large cuff**: a standard cuff on an arm over 32 cm circumference overestimates BP. [ESC 2024]`,`**Hirsutism and acne** suggest PCOS; **goitre, bradycardia and delayed reflexes** suggest hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperpigmented, lax abdominal pannus with intertrigo**, lymphoedema and venous ulcers are mechanical complications seen in class III obesity. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of weight gain`,points:[`**Hypothyroidism**: weight gain is modest (usually under 5 kg, mostly fluid); obesity is never the only sign - check TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cushing syndrome**: central fat with thin limbs, wide purple striae, proximal myopathy, osteoporosis and hypertension - screen with 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Oedematous states** (heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis with ascites) gain weight from fluid - look for pitting oedema, raised JVP, ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced weight gain** (antipsychotics, steroids, insulin, valproate) - switching drugs may be the best anti-obesity measure. [Endocrine Society 2015]`,`**Hypothalamic obesity** after craniopharyngioma surgery or radiation: severe hyperphagia, low energy expenditure, poor response to diet. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Monogenic obesity and Prader-Willi syndrome**: onset before 5 years, extreme hyperphagia, developmental delay or hypogonadism - refer to genetics. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Lipoedema** (symmetric painful fat on legs sparing feet, women) and **lipodystrophy** - resistant to diet and to anti-obesity drugs. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - before drugs or surgery`,points:[`**Baseline for all**: fasting glucose and **HbA1c**, fasting lipids, ALT/AST, creatinine with eGFR, urine albumin-creatinine ratio, TSH, CBC. [ADA 2026]`,`**MASLD screen**: FIB-4 score from age, AST, ALT and platelets; FIB-4 1.3 or more needs elastography (FibroScan) - semaglutide is now approved for MASH with F2-F3 fibrosis. [EASL-EASD-EASO 2024]`,`**Sleep study** (or validated home testing) if STOP-BANG is 3 or more; OSA should be treated before surgery. [ASMBS 2022]`,`**Before tirzepatide or semaglutide**: lipase only if abdominal pain history; **fundus examination** in diabetics with retinopathy (rapid glucose lowering can worsen it); no routine calcitonin. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pre-bariatric work-up**: upper GI endoscopy (hiatus hernia, Barrett, H. pylori - routine in India because of high gastric pathology), abdominal ultrasound (gallstones), ECG, echo if cardiac symptoms, PFT, vitamin D, B12, iron studies, folate. [IFSO-APC 2023]`,`**Psychological assessment** before surgery (depression, binge eating, alcohol use, understanding of the lifelong changes). [ASMBS 2022]`,`**Hormonal tests only on suspicion**: 1 mg dexamethasone suppression or late-night salivary cortisol for Cushing, testosterone in men with symptoms, LH/FSH/androgens for PCOS. [Endocrine Society 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - lifestyle foundation and drug selection`,points:[`**Lifestyle therapy is the base for every patient**: an energy deficit of **500-750 kcal/day**, protein 1-1.2 g/kg, **150-300 minutes of moderate activity a week** plus resistance training twice weekly - resistance training preserves muscle lost on GLP-1 drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**Indication for drugs**: BMI **30 or more**, or **27 or more with a weight-related complication** (drug labels); Asian-Indian practice uses **BMI 27 or more, or 25 or more with a complication**. [Indian consensus 2009]`,`**First choice when affordable**: **semaglutide 2.4 mg or tirzepatide**, because they give 15-20% weight loss and treat complications (diabetes, MASH, OSA, heart failure with preserved EF, knee OA); choose by complication, as EASO 2025 recommends. [EASO 2025]`,`**Semaglutide 2.4 mg** SC once weekly: 0.25 mg for 4 weeks, then 0.5, 1.0, 1.7 and **2.4 mg** at 4-weekly steps; STEP-1 gave **14.9% vs 2.4%** weight loss at 68 weeks. [STEP-1 NEJM 2021]`,`**Tirzepatide** SC once weekly: **2.5 mg for 4 weeks**, then increase by 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to a maintenance of **5, 10 or 15 mg**; SURMOUNT-1 gave **20.9%** weight loss at 72 weeks on 15 mg, and SURMOUNT-5 showed it beat semaglutide (20.2% vs 13.7%). [SURMOUNT-5 NEJM 2025]`,`**Liraglutide 3 mg** SC daily: start 0.6 mg, increase by 0.6 mg weekly to 3.0 mg; about 8% weight loss (SCALE); cheaper biosimilars are available in India. [SCALE NEJM 2015]`,`**Orlistat 120 mg orally three times a day with fat-containing meals** (60 mg OTC strength); about **3 kg more than placebo** at 1 year and 37% fewer new diabetes cases (XENDOS); give a **multivitamin at bedtime, 2 hours apart**. [XENDOS 2004]`,`**Stopping rule**: if weight loss is **under 5% after 12 weeks on the maintenance dose**, stop the drug and reconsider - non-responders rarely improve. [Endocrine Society 2015]`,`**Phentermine** (15-37.5 mg once daily, short-term in the US) and **phentermine-topiramate or naltrexone-bupropion are not approved in India**; do not import or compound them. [CDSCO 2024]`,`**Long-term use is the rule**: after stopping semaglutide, **two-thirds of lost weight returns within a year** (STEP-1 extension) and SURMOUNT-4 showed regain on switching to placebo - counsel that the drug treats a chronic disease. [SURMOUNT-4 JAMA 2024]`]},{heading:`Bariatric surgery - indications, procedures and outcomes`,points:[`**ASMBS/IFSO 2022 indications**: BMI **35 or more** regardless of comorbidity; BMI **30-34.9 with metabolic disease**; in Asians, **BMI 27.5 or more** should be offered surgery and **32.5 or more** is the equivalent of 35. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Diabetes Surgery Summit-2 (DSS-II)**: metabolic surgery recommended in T2DM with Asian BMI **37.5 or more**, or **32.5-37.4 with poor control**, and considered at **27.5-32.4** if uncontrolled on optimal medical therapy. [DSS-II 2016]`,`**Sleeve gastrectomy** (resects about 80% of stomach along greater curve): commonest worldwide and in India; **25-30% total weight loss**; worsens or causes **GERD and Barrett oesophagus**. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB)**: small pouch plus 100-150 cm Roux limb; **30-35% total weight loss**, best for GERD and diabetes; risks marginal ulcer, internal hernia, dumping, iron and B12 deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**One-anastomosis gastric bypass (OAGB)**: long pouch with a single loop anastomosis; popular in India for diabetes; risks bile reflux and protein-calorie malnutrition if the biliopancreatic limb is too long. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Mortality benefit**: the **Swedish Obese Subjects (SOS)** study showed about **3 years longer life** and fewer cardiovascular deaths and cancers; STAMPEDE showed durable diabetes control at 5 years versus medical therapy. [SOS NEJM 2012]`,`**Diabetes remission** after surgery is predicted by short duration (under 8 years), no insulin, and good C-peptide - captured in the **DiaRem and ABCD scores** (ABCD used in Asians). [ADA 2026]`,`**Contraindications**: uncontrolled psychiatric illness, active alcohol or substance misuse, inability to follow up lifelong, prohibitive anaesthetic risk, active cancer, current pregnancy; age alone is not a contraindication. [ASMBS 2022]`,`**Adolescents**: surgery can be offered from 13 years for class II obesity with comorbidity or class III; **semaglutide 2.4 mg** is approved from 12 years (STEP TEENS, 16% BMI reduction). [AAP 2023]`]},{heading:`IPD and emergencies - perioperative care and complications`,points:[`**Pre-op**: 2-week **low-calorie liver-shrinking diet** (800-1,200 kcal), stop smoking 6 weeks before, CPAP for OSA, and **hold GLP-1 agonists** per anaesthetic policy (weekly agents 1 week before; liquid diet 24 hours before is an accepted alternative) to reduce aspiration risk. [ASA multisociety 2024]`,`**Thromboprophylaxis**: early ambulation, mechanical prophylaxis and **LMWH (enoxaparin 40 mg SC 12-hourly** for BMI 40 or more, per protocol) after surgery. [ASMBS 2013]`,`**Anastomotic or staple-line leak** (1-2%): tachycardia **over 120/min** is often the only early sign - treat persistent tachycardia after bariatric surgery as a leak until CT with oral contrast says otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Persistent vomiting after any bariatric surgery** needs **thiamine 100 mg IV before glucose** - Wernicke encephalopathy can appear within weeks. [ASMBS 2019]`,`**Internal hernia** after RYGB (Petersen space): colicky pain with normal labs and normal X-ray can still be closed-loop obstruction - needs urgent CT and surgical review. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute pancreatitis or gallstone disease on GLP-1/tirzepatide**: stop the drug, manage conventionally, do not restart if pancreatitis is confirmed. [FDA label 2024]`,`**Severe dehydration and acute kidney injury** from vomiting and diarrhoea on incretin drugs - hold the drug, rehydrate, check creatinine. [FDA label 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: all anti-obesity drugs are contraindicated; stop **semaglutide 2 months before conception**; tirzepatide - use non-oral or add barrier contraception for **4 weeks after starting and after each dose increase**. [FDA label 2024]`,`**After bariatric surgery**, delay pregnancy for **12-18 months**; avoid the OGTT for GDM screening (dumping) and use glucose monitoring instead. [ACOG 2021]`,`**Type 2 diabetes**: weight loss of **10-15%** is a primary treatment goal and can produce remission; tirzepatide or semaglutide is preferred - see the GLP-1 receptor agonists topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD**: semaglutide slows kidney decline (FLOW 2024); orlistat can cause **oxalate nephropathy** - avoid in CKD and stones. [FLOW NEJM 2024]`,`**Elderly (sarcopenic obesity)**: aim for modest loss (5-10%) with high protein and resistance exercise to protect muscle and bone. [ESPEN-EASO 2022]`,`**Cardiovascular disease without diabetes**: **SELECT** showed semaglutide 2.4 mg reduced **MACE by 20%** in people with BMI 27 or more and established CVD. [SELECT NEJM 2023]`,`**Heart failure with preserved EF and obesity**: semaglutide (STEP-HFpEF) and tirzepatide (SUMMIT 2025) improve symptoms and reduce HF events. [SUMMIT NEJM 2025]`,`**Obstructive sleep apnoea**: tirzepatide reduced apnoea-hypopnoea index by about 50-60% (SURMOUNT-OSA) and is FDA-approved for moderate-severe OSA with obesity. [SURMOUNT-OSA NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications of drugs and surgery, and prognosis`,points:[`**GLP-1/tirzepatide adverse effects**: nausea (20-45%), vomiting, diarrhoea, constipation - mostly during dose escalation; reduce by slow titration, small meals and stopping eating when full. [STEP-1 NEJM 2021]`,`**Serious incretin risks**: gallstones and cholecystitis (rapid weight loss), pancreatitis, dehydration-related AKI, **loss of lean mass** (about a quarter to a third of weight lost), and a possible signal for NAION (optic neuropathy) with semaglutide. [EMA PRAC 2025]`,`**Orlistat adverse effects**: oily spotting, faecal urgency and incontinence, flatus with discharge, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency; interacts with **levothyroxine (4 hours apart), warfarin and ciclosporin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dumping syndrome** after bypass: **early (10-30 min)** - osmotic fluid shift with cramps, diarrhoea, palpitations; **late (1-3 h)** - reactive hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia; treat with small low-sugar meals and separating fluids from food; acarbose for late dumping. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Micronutrient deficiencies** after surgery: **iron, B12, folate, vitamin D, calcium, thiamine, copper, zinc** - copper deficiency causes a myelopathy that mimics B12 deficiency. [ASMBS 2019]`,`**Late surgical complications**: marginal ulcer (smoking, NSAIDs), stricture, internal hernia, gallstones, GERD after sleeve, **alcohol use disorder** (faster alcohol absorption) and a higher self-harm risk. [ASMBS 2022]`,`**Weight regain**: 20-30% regain some weight 2-5 years after surgery; adding semaglutide or tirzepatide is an accepted rescue. [IFSO 2024]`,`**Prognosis**: 5-10% weight loss improves BP, glucose and lipids; **10-15%** improves MASH, OSA and diabetes remission; **15% or more** reduces cardiovascular events and mortality. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and post-operative nutrition`,points:[`**On drugs**: review every 4 weeks during dose escalation, then every 3 months - weight, waist, BP, side effects, glucose; reduce antihypertensives, sulfonylureas and insulin as weight falls to avoid hypotension and hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Refer for surgery** when the Asian BMI threshold is met and medical therapy has not achieved goals, or earlier in uncontrolled diabetes - to an accredited multidisciplinary centre. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Post-op diet stages**: clear liquids day 1-2, full liquids (protein shakes) to about 2 weeks, pureed 2-4 weeks, soft 4-6 weeks, then regular textures by about 8 weeks. [ASMBS 2019]`,`**Protein 60-80 g/day** (up to 1.5 g/kg ideal body weight), eaten first at each meal; fluids **1.5-2 L/day sipped between meals**, not within 30 minutes of eating. [ASMBS 2019]`,`**Lifelong supplements** (ASMBS micronutrient update): adult multivitamin with minerals (twice daily after bypass), **calcium citrate 1,200-1,500 mg/day** in divided doses, **vitamin D3 3,000 IU/day** (target 25-OH D over 30 ng/mL), **B12 350-1,000 microgram oral daily or 1,000 microgram IM monthly**, elemental iron 45-60 mg/day, thiamine 12 mg or more/day. [ASMBS 2019]`,`**Gallstone prophylaxis**: **ursodeoxycholic acid 500-600 mg/day for 6 months** after surgery reduces gallstone formation. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Laboratory monitoring** after surgery: CBC, iron studies, B12, folate, calcium, vitamin D, PTH, albumin, liver and kidney tests at **3, 6 and 12 months, then yearly**; add zinc and copper after bypass. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**NP-NCD** (National Programme for Prevention and Control of NCDs) screens adults over 30 at **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** for BMI, waist, BP and glucose - the entry point for weight counselling in primary care. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024** advise limiting added sugar to under 25 g/day, cutting ultra-processed (HFSS) foods, and getting most calories from whole grains, pulses, vegetables and fruit. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Cooking oil**: the 2025 national call to **cut edible oil use by 10%** and FSSAI's **Eat Right India** campaign target the main hidden calorie source in Indian diets. [FSSAI 2025]`,`**Fit India Movement** and school health under Ayushman Bharat promote daily physical activity; WHO advises at least **60 minutes a day for children** and 150-300 minutes a week for adults. [WHO 2020]`,`**Use person-first, non-stigmatising language** (a person with obesity) and ask permission before discussing weight - stigma worsens weight gain and avoidance of care. [Joint international consensus 2020]`,`**Set realistic goals**: 5-10% loss over 6 months is clinically meaningful; celebrate improvements in BP, glucose, sleep and mobility, not just kilograms. [Obesity Canada 2020]`,`**Counsel on drug realities**: weekly injections, gradual titration, cost, side effects, need for long-term use, and that eating less protein and skipping exercise on these drugs causes muscle loss. [EASO 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Lancet Diabetes & Endocrinology Commission 2025** introduced **clinical vs preclinical obesity** - diagnosis needs confirmed excess adiposity plus organ dysfunction or limited daily activity, not BMI alone. [Lancet Commission 2025]`,`**Indian obesity definition 2025** (Misra et al., Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol): two-stage Asian-Indian classification at BMI 23 or more, with stage 2 tied to abdominal adiposity and complications. [Indian obesity definition 2025]`,`**EASO 2025 pharmacotherapy framework**: choose the drug by complication; **semaglutide 2.4 mg or tirzepatide** first-line for most complications (diabetes, MASH, OSA, HFpEF, knee OA). [EASO 2025]`,`**WHO guideline (December 2025)** issued a conditional recommendation for GLP-1 therapies in adults with obesity as part of comprehensive care. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cardiovascular and organ indications**: SELECT (2023) MACE reduction; FDA approvals of tirzepatide for **OSA (2024)** and semaglutide for **MASH with F2-F3 fibrosis (2025)**. [FDA 2025]`,`**India access**: **tirzepatide (Mounjaro, March 2025)** and **semaglutide 2.4 mg (Wegovy, June 2025)** were launched in India; lower-cost generic semaglutide followed patent expiry in 2026. [CDSCO 2025]`,`**ADA Standards 2026** state weight loss of 10-15% or more as a primary treatment target in T2DM, and recommend continuing anti-obesity medication beyond goal to maintain weight. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pipeline**: oral semaglutide 25 mg, oral non-peptide **orforglipron**, cagrilintide-semaglutide and the triple agonist **retatrutide** (phase 3) - examiners may ask the mechanism. [Lancet 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'Discuss the pharmacotherapy of obesity' - write indications with Asian cut-offs, a drug table with dose, weight loss and side effects, the 12-week stopping rule, and withdrawn drugs. [Exam pattern]`,`**IFSO 2022 Asian surgery thresholds**: offer at **27.5**, recommend at **32.5** - candidates wrongly quote 35/40 for Indians. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Orlistat dose trap**: 120 mg three times daily **with meals**; no benefit if a meal contains no fat, so the dose can be skipped. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sibutramine** was banned in India in 2010 - do not list it as a current drug. [CDSCO 2010]`,`**Tirzepatide is a dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist**, not a GLP-1 agonist alone; retatrutide adds glucagon receptor agonism. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Commonest bariatric procedure** worldwide and in India is **sleeve gastrectomy**; RYGB is preferred when GERD coexists. [IFSO 2022]`,`**Thiamine before glucose** in any vomiting post-bariatric patient - a favourite viva point. [ASMBS 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anti-obesity drugs: dose, efficacy and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Orlistat (lipase inhibitor)`,`120 mg orally three times daily with fat-containing meals`,`Long term; stop if under 5% loss at 12 weeks; about 3 kg over placebo`,`Oily spotting, faecal urgency, fat-soluble vitamin loss, oxalate nephropathy; space levothyroxine 4 h; avoid in cholestasis and malabsorption`],[`Liraglutide 3 mg (GLP-1 RA)`,`0.6 mg SC daily, increase 0.6 mg weekly to 3.0 mg`,`Stop if under 5% loss after 12 weeks at 3 mg; about 8% loss`,`Nausea, vomiting, gallstones, pancreatitis; MTC/MEN2 history; pregnancy`],[`Semaglutide 2.4 mg (GLP-1 RA)`,`SC once weekly: 0.25 mg, 0.5, 1.0, 1.7, 2.4 mg`,`Step up every 4 weeks over 16 weeks; long term; about 15% loss; 20% MACE reduction (SELECT)`,`GI effects, gallstones, pancreatitis, AKI from dehydration, retinopathy worsening in diabetics; stop 2 months before conception`],[`Tirzepatide (GIP/GLP-1 RA)`,`SC once weekly: 2.5 mg, then add 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 5, 10 or 15 mg`,`Long term; about 20% loss on 15 mg; approved for OSA`,`GI effects, gallstones, pancreatitis; MTC/MEN2; reduces oral contraceptive efficacy - barrier method for 4 weeks after each step`],[`Phentermine (sympathomimetic)`,`15-37.5 mg orally once daily (US)`,`Short term (12 weeks) in US labels; not approved in India`,`Tachycardia, hypertension, insomnia, anxiety; avoid in CVD, glaucoma, hyperthyroidism, MAOI use`],[`Naltrexone-bupropion`,`8/90 mg tablets, titrated to 2 tablets twice daily (US)`,`Stop if under 5% at 12 weeks; not available in India`,`Nausea, seizures, raised BP; avoid with opioids, epilepsy, eating disorders; suicidality warning`],[`Setmelanotide (MC4R agonist)`,`SC daily, specialist dosing`,`Lifelong in POMC, PCSK1, LEPR deficiency and Bardet-Biedl syndrome`,`Skin hyperpigmentation, injection reactions, nausea; not for common obesity`]]},{heading:`Indications by BMI: Western vs Asian-Indian thresholds`,columns:[`Intervention`,`Western threshold (kg/m2)`,`Asian / Indian threshold (kg/m2)`,`Source`],rows:[[`Lifestyle therapy`,`25 or more`,`23 or more`,`WHO 2004; Indian consensus 2009`],[`Anti-obesity drugs`,`30 or more, or 27 or more with complication`,`27 or more, or 25 or more with complication`,`Drug labels; Indian consensus 2009`],[`Metabolic surgery - consider`,`30-34.9 with metabolic disease`,`27.5 or more (offer)`,`ASMBS/IFSO 2022`],[`Metabolic surgery - recommend`,`35 or more regardless of comorbidity`,`32.5 or more`,`ASMBS/IFSO 2022`],[`Surgery for T2DM (DSS-II)`,`40 or more; 35-39.9 uncontrolled; consider 30-34.9`,`37.5 or more; 32.5-37.4 uncontrolled; consider 27.5-32.4`,`DSS-II 2016`],[`Adolescents`,`Class II with comorbidity or class III`,`Same percentile-based criteria, age 13 or more`,`AAP 2023`]]},{heading:`Bariatric procedures compared`,columns:[`Procedure`,`Mechanism`,`Total weight loss`,`Typical complications`],rows:[[`Sleeve gastrectomy`,`Restriction plus lower ghrelin, faster gastric emptying`,`25-30%`,`Staple-line leak, GERD, Barrett oesophagus, stricture`],[`Roux-en-Y gastric bypass`,`Small pouch, bypass of duodenum and proximal jejunum, incretin rise`,`30-35%`,`Marginal ulcer, internal hernia, dumping, iron/B12/calcium deficiency`],[`One-anastomosis gastric bypass`,`Long pouch, single loop anastomosis`,`30-35%`,`Bile reflux, protein malnutrition, anaemia`],[`BPD-DS / SADI-S`,`Mainly malabsorptive`,`35-45%`,`Protein malnutrition, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, diarrhoea`],[`Adjustable gastric band`,`Restriction`,`15-20%`,`Slippage, erosion, high reoperation rate - largely abandoned`],[`Endoscopic sleeve gastroplasty / balloon`,`Endoscopic restriction`,`10-15%`,`Pain, vomiting; balloon removed at 6-12 months`]]},{heading:`Post-bariatric micronutrient supplements and monitoring`,columns:[`Nutrient`,`Routine daily dose`,`Deficiency picture`,`Monitor`],rows:[[`Multivitamin with minerals`,`1-2 adult tablets daily (2 after bypass)`,`Mixed deficiencies`,`Adherence at every visit`],[`Calcium citrate`,`1,200-1,500 mg/day in divided doses`,`Secondary hyperparathyroidism, osteoporosis`,`Calcium, PTH, DXA at 2 years`],[`Vitamin D3`,`3,000 IU/day, titrate`,`Osteomalacia, bone pain`,`25-OH D over 30 ng/mL`],[`Vitamin B12`,`350-1,000 microgram oral daily or 1,000 microgram IM monthly`,`Megaloblastic anaemia, neuropathy`,`B12 yearly (methylmalonic acid if borderline)`],[`Iron`,`45-60 mg elemental daily (menstruating women more)`,`Microcytic anaemia, fatigue`,`Ferritin and CBC at 3, 6, 12 months then yearly`],[`Thiamine`,`12 mg or more daily; 100 mg IV if vomiting`,`Wernicke encephalopathy, beriberi`,`Clinical; thiamine level if vomiting`],[`Copper and zinc`,`Copper 2 mg, zinc 8-22 mg (in multivitamin)`,`Copper: anaemia, neutropenia, myelopathy`,`Yearly after bypass`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of weight gain`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clue`,`First test`],rows:[[`Simple (polygenic) obesity`,`Gradual gain, family history, no specific signs`,`None beyond complications screen`],[`Cushing syndrome`,`Purple striae over 1 cm, proximal myopathy, thin skin, osteoporosis`,`1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Modest gain, cold intolerance, bradycardia, slow-relaxing reflexes`,`TSH`],[`Drug-induced`,`Gain after antipsychotic, steroid, insulin, valproate`,`Drug history`],[`Hypothalamic / monogenic`,`Early onset, extreme hyperphagia, past brain tumour or syndrome features`,`Genetic testing, MRI`],[`Fluid retention`,`Pitting oedema, raised JVP, ascites, proteinuria`,`Urine protein, echo, liver tests`]]}],redFlags:[`Persistent tachycardia over 120/min, fever or abdominal pain after bariatric surgery (leak)`,`Persistent vomiting after bariatric surgery - give thiamine and admit (Wernicke risk)`,`Severe epigastric pain radiating to the back on a GLP-1 agonist or tirzepatide (pancreatitis)`,`Colicky abdominal pain after gastric bypass (internal hernia, closed-loop obstruction)`,`Dizziness, oliguria and rising creatinine from vomiting on incretin therapy (AKI)`,`Neck lump or hoarseness on a GLP-1 agonist in someone with MTC/MEN2 family history`,`Suicidal thoughts, binge-purge behaviour or rapid unexplained weight loss on therapy`,`Hypoglycaemic symptoms 1-3 hours after meals post-bypass (late dumping or post-bariatric hypoglycaemia)`],pearls:[`Asian thresholds are about 2.5 lower: drugs at 27 (25 with complication), surgery offered at 27.5 and recommended at 32.5.`,`Stop any anti-obesity drug that gives under 5% loss after 12 weeks on the maintenance dose.`,`Tirzepatide beats semaglutide: about 20% vs 14% weight loss (SURMOUNT-5).`,`SELECT: semaglutide 2.4 mg cut MACE by 20% in non-diabetic people with obesity and CVD.`,`Orlistat: 120 mg TDS with fatty meals, multivitamin at bedtime, levothyroxine 4 hours apart.`,`Sleeve worsens reflux; bypass cures it - pick RYGB for GERD.`,`After stopping incretin drugs, two-thirds of the weight returns in a year - obesity is chronic.`,`Post-bariatric vomiting: thiamine before glucose, always.`,`Mnemonic for post-bypass deficiencies - 'I Bought Fresh Dahi, Curd, Too': Iron, B12, Folate, vitamin D, Calcium/Copper, Thiamine.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old Indian man with type 2 diabetes has BMI 29 kg/m2 and HbA1c 9.2% despite metformin, an SGLT2 inhibitor and basal insulin. According to the ASMBS/IFSO 2022 recommendations adapted for Asians, which statement about metabolic surgery is correct?`,options:[`He should not be considered until BMI reaches 35 kg/m2`,`He should be offered metabolic surgery, as the Asian threshold is BMI 27.5 kg/m2`,`Surgery is contraindicated because he is on insulin`,`Only an intragastric balloon is appropriate below BMI 30 kg/m2`],answer:1,explanation:`ASMBS/IFSO 2022 lowered thresholds for Asians: metabolic surgery should be offered at BMI 27.5 or more (and is recommended at 32.5 or more), especially with uncontrolled T2DM. The BMI 35 cut-off is the Western value; insulin use reduces the chance of remission but is not a contraindication; a balloon gives modest, temporary loss and is not the recommended choice.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-q2`,stem:`Tirzepatide produces weight loss mainly through which mechanism?`,options:[`Inhibition of pancreatic lipase in the gut lumen`,`Release of noradrenaline from hypothalamic neurons`,`Agonism at the melanocortin-4 receptor`,`Dual agonism at GIP and GLP-1 receptors reducing appetite`],answer:3,explanation:`Tirzepatide is a once-weekly dual GIP and GLP-1 receptor agonist that acts on hypothalamic and brainstem centres to cut appetite and food intake. Lipase inhibition is orlistat's mechanism, noradrenaline release is phentermine's, and MC4R agonism is setmelanotide's, which is reserved for rare monogenic obesity.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-q3`,stem:`A woman 6 weeks after sleeve gastrectomy has had persistent vomiting for 10 days and now presents confused with nystagmus and an unsteady gait. Capillary glucose is 62 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate first step?`,options:[`Intravenous thiamine before giving dextrose`,`50 mL of 25% dextrose immediately, then thiamine the next day`,`Urgent MRI brain before any treatment`,`Oral multivitamin and review in clinic`],answer:0,explanation:`Persistent vomiting after bariatric surgery depletes thiamine, and confusion, nystagmus and ataxia indicate Wernicke encephalopathy; intravenous thiamine must be given before (or with) glucose because dextrose alone can precipitate or worsen it. Delaying thiamine for imaging is wrong, and oral multivitamins are inadequate for an established neurological deficit.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-q4`,stem:`A patient started on orlistat asks how to take it. Which instruction is correct?`,options:[`120 mg once daily at bedtime with the multivitamin`,`120 mg before breakfast on an empty stomach`,`120 mg three times daily with each main meal containing fat, and a multivitamin at bedtime`,`360 mg once weekly with a high-fat meal`],answer:2,explanation:`Orlistat blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase only when food is present, so it is taken as 120 mg with each fat-containing main meal (up to an hour after); a dose can be skipped if a meal has no fat. A multivitamin is taken at bedtime, at least 2 hours away from orlistat, because orlistat reduces fat-soluble vitamin absorption. Bedtime or empty-stomach dosing has no fat to act on.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-q5`,stem:`Which statement about anti-obesity drug therapy is supported by trial evidence?`,options:[`Weight lost on semaglutide is maintained after the drug is stopped at 68 weeks`,`Semaglutide 2.4 mg reduced major adverse cardiovascular events in people with obesity and CVD but without diabetes`,`Sibutramine is the preferred low-cost option in India`,`Orlistat produces more weight loss than tirzepatide`],answer:1,explanation:`The SELECT trial (2023) showed a 20% reduction in MACE with semaglutide 2.4 mg in non-diabetic adults with BMI 27 or more and established CVD. The STEP-1 extension showed two-thirds of weight returned within a year of stopping; sibutramine was banned in India in 2010 after SCOUT; orlistat gives about 3 kg extra loss, far less than tirzepatide's 20%.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the indications, drugs and monitoring for pharmacotherapy of obesity in Asian Indians. Add a note on the indications for bariatric surgery.`,openingLines:[`Obesity is a chronic, relapsing disease of excess adiposity; in Asian Indians it is defined at BMI 25 or more (overweight 23-24.9) with abdominal obesity at waist 90/80 cm.`,`Drugs are added to lifestyle therapy at BMI 27 or more, or 25 or more with a complication, and surgery is offered at 27.5 and recommended at 32.5 (ASMBS/IFSO 2022).`],answer:[{heading:`1. Definition and staging`,points:[`Asian-Indian BMI bands and waist cut-offs; **Lancet Commission 2025** clinical vs preclinical obesity. [Lancet Commission 2025]`,`**Indian obesity definition 2025**: stage 1 (BMI 23 or more, no complication) and stage 2 (with abdominal adiposity and complication). [Indian obesity definition 2025]`]},{heading:`2. Indications for drug therapy`,points:[`BMI **27 or more, or 25 or more with a complication**, after 3-6 months of lifestyle therapy, or alongside it. [Indian consensus 2009]`,`Choose by complication: T2DM, MASH, OSA, HFpEF, CVD favour **semaglutide 2.4 mg or tirzepatide**. [EASO 2025]`]},{heading:`3. Drugs (draw the table)`,points:[`**Orlistat** 120 mg TDS with meals; about 3 kg over placebo. [XENDOS 2004]`,`**Liraglutide** 3 mg daily; about 8%. **Semaglutide** 2.4 mg weekly; about 15%. **Tirzepatide** 5-15 mg weekly; about 20%. [SURMOUNT-5 NEJM 2025]`,`Not in India: phentermine, phentermine-topiramate, naltrexone-bupropion; banned: sibutramine. [CDSCO 2010]`]},{heading:`4. Monitoring and stopping rule`,points:[`Monthly during titration, then 3-monthly: weight, waist, BP, glucose, side effects; de-escalate insulin and sulfonylureas. [ADA 2026]`,`Stop if **under 5% loss at 12 weeks** on maintenance dose; otherwise continue long term - regain follows withdrawal. [Endocrine Society 2015]`]},{heading:`5. Bariatric surgery`,points:[`Asian thresholds: **offer at 27.5, recommend at 32.5** kg/m2 (ASMBS/IFSO 2022); DSS-II for T2DM. [IFSO 2022]`,`Procedures: sleeve, RYGB, OAGB; lifelong supplements and laboratory follow-up. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`6. National link and counselling`,points:[`Screening under **NP-NCD** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; ICMR-NIN 2024 diet; Eat Right India; Fit India. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]}],mustDraw:[`Drug table: drug, dose, weight loss, key adverse effects`,`Treatment ladder: lifestyle, drugs, surgery with Asian BMI thresholds`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and Asian cut-offs`,marks:1},{part:`Indications for drugs`,marks:1.5},{part:`Drug table with doses`,marks:3.5},{part:`Monitoring and stopping rule`,marks:1.5},{part:`Bariatric surgery indications`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`semaglutide`,`tirzepatide`,`orlistat`,`IFSO 2022`,`Asian BMI`,`stopping rule`]},{id:`endocrine-obesity-pharmacotherapy-bariatric-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on nutritional care and complications after bariatric surgery.`,openingLines:[`Bariatric surgery changes how food is absorbed for life, so nutritional care is lifelong: staged diet, high protein, and daily micronutrient supplements.`,`Deficiencies of iron, B12, calcium, vitamin D and thiamine are the commonest preventable complications.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Staged diet`,points:[`Clear liquids, full liquids to 2 weeks, pureed 2-4 weeks, soft 4-6 weeks, regular by 8 weeks; **protein 60-80 g/day**; fluids sipped between meals. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`2. Supplements`,points:[`Multivitamin (twice daily after bypass), **calcium citrate 1,200-1,500 mg**, **vitamin D3 3,000 IU**, B12, iron 45-60 mg, thiamine. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`3. Complications`,points:[`Early: leak, bleeding, VTE; late: dumping (early and late), marginal ulcer, internal hernia, gallstones, GERD after sleeve. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wernicke encephalopathy** with vomiting - thiamine before glucose. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`4. Monitoring`,points:[`Labs at **3, 6, 12 months then yearly**; ursodeoxycholic acid 6 months; delay pregnancy 12-18 months. [ASMBS 2019]`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of supplements with dose and deficiency sign`],markSplit:[{part:`Staged diet and protein`,marks:1.5},{part:`Supplements`,marks:1.5},{part:`Complications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Monitoring`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`dumping`,`thiamine`,`calcium citrate`,`B12`,`staged diet`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Evaluation and management of obesity`,`Rubino F et al. Definition and diagnostic criteria of clinical obesity - Lancet Diabetes & Endocrinology Commission, 2025`,`Misra A et al. Definition and diagnostic criteria for obesity in Asian Indians - Lancet Diabetes & Endocrinology, 2025`,`Eisenberg D et al. ASMBS/IFSO Guidelines on Indications for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery, 2022`,`EASO framework for the pharmacological treatment of obesity, 2025`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 8: Obesity and weight management`,`ASMBS Integrated Health Nutritional Guidelines for the Surgical Weight Loss Patient (micronutrient update), 2019`,`ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians, 2024`]}),u.push({id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise`,title:`Medical nutrition therapy and exercise in diabetes: Indian diet planning`,oneLiner:`**Medical nutrition therapy (MNT)** is an individualised, dietitian-supported eating plan that lowers HbA1c by **1-2%**; for Indians it means **cutting refined carbohydrate (white rice, maida, sugar) to about 50% of energy, raising protein and fibre**, using the **plate method** and whole grains or millets, plus **150 minutes a week of aerobic activity and resistance training 2-3 times a week** - and a **10-15 kg weight loss can put early type 2 diabetes into remission** (DiRECT).`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`MNT`,`diet in diabetes`,`diabetic diet`,`plate method`,`My Plate for the Day`,`ICMR-NIN 2024`,`glycaemic index`,`glycaemic load`,`millets`,`Shree Anna`,`carbohydrate counting`,`DiRECT trial`,`diabetes remission`,`total diet replacement`,`exercise prescription`,`resistance training`,`intermittent fasting`,`non-sugar sweeteners`,`DSMES`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Medical nutrition therapy** is nutrition assessment, diagnosis, intervention and monitoring delivered by a registered dietitian or trained team; in T2DM it lowers **HbA1c by 1.0-1.9%**, comparable to a drug. [ADA 2026]`,`**Lifestyle therapy** (MNT plus physical activity plus behaviour support) is the foundation at every stage of diabetes - drugs are added to it, never instead of it. [ADA 2026]`,`**India**: ICMR-INDIAB estimates **101 million adults with diabetes (11.4%) and 136 million with prediabetes (15.3%)**; most live where dietitians are scarce, so the physician must give the first diet advice. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**The Indian diet problem**: ICMR-INDIAB diet data show Indians get about **62% of energy from carbohydrate**, mostly white rice and refined wheat, with low protein (about 12%) - the pattern linked to diabetes. [ICMR-INDIAB Nat Med 2023]`,`**Physical inactivity**: ICMR-INDIAB found about **55% of Indians physically inactive**; women and urban residents are least active. [ICMR-INDIAB 2014]`,`**Diabetes self-management education and support (DSMES)** should be given at four times: diagnosis, yearly, when complications or new barriers arise, and at transitions of care. [ADA 2026]`,`**Remission of T2DM** is defined as **HbA1c under 6.5% at least 3 months after stopping all glucose-lowering drugs** (ADA/EASD/Diabetes UK consensus 2021). [ADA/EASD 2021]`]},{heading:`Dietary risk factors in Indian patients`,points:[`**Refined grains**: white rice intake was associated with diabetes in CURES (Chennai); replacing it with brown rice, parboiled rice or millets lowers glycaemic load. [CURES 2009]`,`**Added sugar and sugary drinks**: tea and coffee with sugar several times a day, sweets, fruit juice and soft drinks deliver large rapid glucose loads. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Fried snacks and reused oils** (samosa, pakora, namkeen) raise trans and saturated fat and calories; **vanaspati** contains trans fat. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Low protein and low vegetable intake**: dal portions are small and vegetables are often overcooked or fried; fruit and vegetable intake is below 400 g/day in most Indians. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Ultra-processed HFSS foods** (high fat, sugar, salt), bakery items and packaged snacks are rising fastest in urban and rural diets. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Meal timing**: late heavy dinners, skipped breakfasts, festive feasts and long fasts followed by large meals worsen glucose variability. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Alcohol**: adds empty calories, causes delayed hypoglycaemia on insulin or sulfonylureas, raises triglycerides and BP. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Physiology - why diet and exercise lower glucose`,points:[`**Postprandial glucose** depends on the amount of carbohydrate and how fast it is absorbed; in early T2DM loss of first-phase insulin release makes the post-meal spike the main contributor to HbA1c. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Glycaemic index (GI)** ranks a carbohydrate by its 2-hour glucose response against glucose (100): **low 55 or less, medium 56-69, high 70 or more**. [Atkinson 2021]`,`**Glycaemic load (GL)** = GI x grams of available carbohydrate / 100; it accounts for portion size - **low 10 or less, high 20 or more** - so a large plate of a medium-GI food can still be high-GL. [Atkinson 2021]`,`**Fibre, protein, fat and acid** (curd, lemon) slow gastric emptying and absorption; eating **vegetables and protein before rice or roti** (food sequencing) lowers the post-meal peak. [Shukla 2015]`,`**Weight loss reverses ectopic fat**: the **twin cycle hypothesis** (Taylor) - losing about 15 kg removes fat from the liver and pancreas, restoring hepatic insulin sensitivity and first-phase insulin secretion. [DiRECT Lancet 2018]`,`**Muscle contraction moves GLUT4 to the membrane through AMPK**, independent of insulin - so exercise lowers glucose even in insulin resistance, and the effect on insulin sensitivity lasts **24-72 hours**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resistance training increases muscle mass**, the main site of glucose disposal - important in Indians, who have less muscle at the same BMI. [ADA 2026]`,`**Starch processing matters**: grinding, overcooking and polishing raise GI; parboiling, cooling (retrograded resistant starch) and whole-grain forms lower it. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification of eating patterns and diet approaches`,points:[`**No single ideal macronutrient split** fits all; ADA endorses Mediterranean, DASH, plant-based, low-carbohydrate and vegetarian patterns, individualised to culture and cost. [ADA 2026]`,`**Indian macronutrient target**: ICMR-INDIAB modelling found remission of new diabetes needs carbohydrate cut to **49-54% of energy**, protein raised to **19-20%** and fat kept at **21-26%** - a shift away from the usual 62% carbohydrate. [ICMR-INDIAB Nat Med 2023]`,`**Low-carbohydrate (26-45% energy) and very-low-carbohydrate (under 26%, ketogenic) diets** lower HbA1c and weight in the short term; avoid very-low-carb diets in pregnancy, eating disorders, CKD and with **SGLT2 inhibitors** (euglycaemic DKA). [ADA 2026]`,`**Total diet replacement / very-low-energy diet** (about 825-853 kcal/day formula for 12-20 weeks) followed by stepped food reintroduction is the remission strategy used in DiRECT. [DiRECT Lancet 2018]`,`**Intermittent fasting and time-restricted eating** give weight loss similar to daily calorie restriction; they need drug adjustment on insulin or sulfonylureas. [ADA 2026]`,`**Carbohydrate counting** (1 exchange = 15 g carbohydrate) matches prandial insulin to meals - essential in type 1 diabetes (see the Type 1 diabetes topic), optional in T2DM. [ADA 2026]`,`**Diet exchange lists**: Indian foods grouped so that 1 cereal exchange (about 20 g raw rice or atta, one small phulka) gives roughly 15 g carbohydrate and 70 kcal. [ICMR 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - diet and activity history`,points:[`**24-hour diet recall** with portions in household measures (katori, ladle, number of rotis), plus a typical weekend and festival day. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Cooking oil**: litres used by the family per month divided by members - more than about **0.5 L per person per month** is excess. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Sugar sources**: number of cups of tea with sugar, sweets, juices, biscuits; 'sugar-free' sweets often contain refined flour and fat. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Food security, cost and who cooks**: involve the person who cooks; ask about food insecurity, which ADA now asks clinicians to screen for. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fasting and religious practices** (Ramadan, Navratri, Ekadashi, weekly fasts) - plan drug timing in advance (see Diabetes in special situations). [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Activity history**: steps or minutes walked, occupation, sitting time, barriers (joint pain, safety, heat, time, women's access to public spaces). [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia history** with skipped meals or exercise on insulin or sulfonylureas, and alcohol pattern. [ADA 2026]`,`**Screen for disordered eating** (binge eating, insulin omission for weight loss in type 1). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Examination and assessment before an exercise prescription`,points:[`**Weight, BMI, waist** and ideally body composition; **grip strength or chair-stand test** to detect sarcopenia in the elderly. [ADA 2026]`,`**Feet**: monofilament and vibration testing, deformity, ulcers - loss of protective sensation means good footwear and daily foot inspection, and **non-weight-bearing exercise if an ulcer is present**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fundus**: **proliferative or severe non-proliferative retinopathy** contraindicates vigorous aerobic or resistance exercise, heavy lifting and Valsalva (risk of vitreous haemorrhage, retinal detachment). [ADA 2026]`,`**Autonomic neuropathy**: resting tachycardia, postural drop - needs cardiac evaluation before raising intensity; heart-rate targets are unreliable, use perceived exertion. [ADA 2026]`,`**Cardiac evaluation**: routine stress ECG is **not** needed before low or moderate activity in asymptomatic people; assess those with symptoms or planning vigorous exercise from a sedentary state. [ADA 2026]`,`**Joints and balance**: knee osteoarthritis favours cycling, swimming or chair exercises; falls risk in the elderly calls for balance training. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Differential - why diet 'is not working'`,points:[`**Hidden carbohydrate and oil**: portion creep, sugar in tea, fruit juice, extra rice at dinner - a repeat diet recall usually finds it. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Disease progression**: falling beta-cell function means rising HbA1c despite good diet - add or intensify drugs rather than blame the patient. [ADA 2026]`,`**Insulin deficiency** (LADA, type 1, pancreatic diabetes): weight loss and rising glucose on a strict diet suggest insulin is needed, not more restriction. [ADA 2026]`,`**Drug-induced hyperglycaemia**: steroids, antipsychotics, thiazides at high dose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Intercurrent illness**: infection, TB, thyrotoxicosis raise glucose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Over-restriction**: very low intake with sulfonylureas causes hypoglycaemia and rebound eating; a starving patient may show ketonuria. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring targets`,points:[`**HbA1c every 3 months** until target, then 6-monthly; MNT effect is visible by **3-6 months**. [ADA 2026]`,`**SMBG pairs** (before and 2 hours after the same meal) show which meal causes the spike and teach the patient the effect of each food; aim for a post-meal rise under about 40-50 mg/dL. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Lipid profile**: high triglycerides respond to cutting sugar, refined carbohydrate and alcohol, and to weight loss. [ADA 2026]`,`**Renal function and urine ACR** before prescribing protein targets; protein **0.8 g/kg/day** in CKD not on dialysis. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Haemoglobin, B12 (on metformin), vitamin D** - common deficiencies in vegetarian Indian diets. [ADA 2026]`,`**CGM** (where affordable) shows food-specific excursions and post-exercise lows - see the Glucose monitoring topic. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - writing an Indian diet plan`,points:[`**Energy**: about **20-25 kcal/kg ideal body weight** for overweight sedentary adults, 30 for moderately active and 35 for heavy work; a 500-750 kcal/day deficit for weight loss. [ICMR 2018]`,`**Plate method** (ADA Diabetes Plate): **half non-starchy vegetables, quarter protein** (dal, paneer, egg, fish, chicken, soya), **quarter carbohydrate** (rice, roti, millet), with water - teachable in 2 minutes with no counting. [ADA 2026]`,`**ICMR-NIN 'My Plate for the Day' (about 2,000 kcal)**: vegetables including greens **400 g**, fruit **100 g**, cereals and millets **250 g**, pulses/eggs/flesh **85 g**, nuts and seeds **35 g**, milk or curd **300 mL**, fats and oils **27 g**. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**ICMR-NIN limits**: cereals no more than **45% of energy**, added sugar under **5% of energy (about 25 g/day)**, salt under **5 g/day**, total fat under 30% with saturated fat under 10%. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Carbohydrate quality**: whole grains, **millets** (ragi, jowar, bajra, foxtail), parboiled or brown rice, whole dals and legumes; avoid maida, polished rice in large portions, sugar and juices. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Fibre**: at least **14 g per 1,000 kcal** (25-40 g/day) from vegetables, whole pulses, whole grains, methi seeds. [ADA 2026]`,`**Protein**: 1-1.2 g/kg/day if kidneys are normal (more in the elderly and on GLP-1 drugs to protect muscle); include a protein source at every meal. [ADA 2026]`,`**Fats**: use a mix of oils (groundnut, mustard, rice bran, sesame), avoid vanaspati and reheated oil; nuts 30 g/day help lipids. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Fruit**: 1-2 whole fruits a day (about 100-150 g each), not juice; mango and banana in small portions as part of the carbohydrate allowance. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Meal pattern**: 3 meals and 1-2 small snacks, no long gaps on insulin or sulfonylureas; take vegetables and protein first, carbohydrate last. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Exercise prescription (FITT) and weight-loss remission`,points:[`**Aerobic**: at least **150 minutes a week of moderate-to-vigorous activity** over 3 or more days, with **no more than 2 consecutive days off**; younger fit people may do 75 minutes of vigorous activity. [ADA 2026]`,`**Resistance**: **2-3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days**, 8-10 exercises for major muscle groups, 1-3 sets of 10-15 repetitions, progressing load. [ADA 2026]`,`**Break up sitting**: every **30 minutes stand or walk for about 3 minutes** - improves post-meal glucose. [ADA 2026]`,`**Post-meal walk of 10-15 minutes** lowers the post-meal peak more than the same walk at another time. [ADA 2026]`,`**Indian consensus**: **60 minutes of activity daily** - 30 minutes aerobic, 15 minutes work-related and 15 minutes muscle-strengthening - because Asian Indians need more activity for the same benefit. [Indian PA consensus 2012]`,`**Older adults**: add flexibility and **balance training 2-3 times a week** (yoga, tai chi) to prevent falls. [ADA 2026]`,`**Benefit**: structured exercise lowers HbA1c by about **0.6-0.7%**, more with combined aerobic plus resistance training and with more than 150 min/week. [Umpierre JAMA 2011]`,`**DiRECT**: total diet replacement (825-853 kcal/day for 12-20 weeks) then food reintroduction gave **46% remission at 1 year and 36% at 2 years**; **86% of those who lost 15 kg or more** were in remission. [DiRECT Lancet 2018]`,`**DiRECT 5-year follow-up**: remission persisted in about **13% vs 5%** of controls, with fewer serious adverse events - remission needs lasting weight maintenance. [DiRECT 5-year 2024]`,`**Remission is more likely** with diabetes under 6 years, no insulin, weight loss of 10-15% or more; ReTUNE showed remission in people with normal BMI after losing 10-15% too. [ReTUNE 2023]`]},{heading:`Safety - hypoglycaemia, illness and inpatient nutrition`,points:[`**Before exercise on insulin or sulfonylurea**: if glucose is **under 90 mg/dL**, take **15-30 g carbohydrate** first; consider reducing the pre-exercise insulin dose. [ADA 2026]`,`**If glucose is over 250 mg/dL with ketones**, avoid exercise (type 1); without ketones and feeling well, light exercise is safe. [ADA 2026]`,`**Delayed (nocturnal) hypoglycaemia** can occur up to **24 hours after** prolonged exercise - check bedtime glucose and add a snack. [ADA 2026]`,`**Alcohol**: limit to **1 drink a day for women and 2 for men** if taken at all, always with food; it blocks gluconeogenesis and causes delayed hypoglycaemia. ICMR-NIN 2024 advises avoiding alcohol altogether. [ADA 2026]`,`**Sick-day nutrition**: keep taking fluids and easily digested carbohydrate (about 150-200 g/day, e.g. khichdi, dal water, curd rice) and never skip insulin; see the sick-day rules topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**Inpatient diet**: consistent-carbohydrate meal plan, meals timed with prandial insulin; enteral or parenteral nutrition needs basal plus correction insulin - see the in-hospital hyperglycaemia topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**Hypoglycaemia treatment**: **15-20 g glucose** (3-4 glucose tablets, 150 mL juice, 3 teaspoons sugar in water), recheck in 15 minutes - not chocolate, whose fat slows absorption. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and GDM**: minimum **175 g carbohydrate/day**, 71 g protein and 28 g fibre; no weight-loss diets or very-low-carb diets - see the GDM topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD**: protein **0.8 g/kg/day** (not lower) in stages 3-5 not on dialysis, 1.0-1.2 g/kg on dialysis; sodium under 2 g/day; potassium and phosphate limits only when raised. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Elderly**: avoid restrictive diets; prioritise protein (1-1.2 g/kg), calcium, vitamin D and resistance exercise; malnutrition is commoner than obesity in frail elders. [ADA 2026]`,`**Children and adolescents with T2DM**: 60 minutes of daily activity, screen time under 2 hours, family-based nutrition change. [ADA 2026]`,`**On GLP-1 agonists or tirzepatide**: small meals, stop at fullness, high protein and resistance training to limit muscle loss; avoid fatty meals that worsen nausea. [ADA 2026]`,`**Vegetarians**: combine cereal and pulse (dal-rice, khichdi, idli) for complete protein; include milk, curd, paneer, soya, nuts; check B12. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: use locally available low-cost foods - seasonal vegetables, whole dals, millets distributed through the **PDS** in several states, groundnuts. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications of poor nutrition practice and prognosis`,points:[`**Unsupervised crash diets** with insulin or sulfonylureas cause hypoglycaemia; very-low-carb diets on SGLT2 inhibitors cause **euglycaemic DKA**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Over-restriction** leads to malnutrition, sarcopenia, gallstones (rapid weight loss), and eventual binge eating and weight regain. [ADA 2026]`,`**Excess protein supplements** without kidney assessment may hasten CKD progression in DKD. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Exercise injuries**: foot ulcers from poor footwear in neuropathy, vitreous haemorrhage with heavy lifting in proliferative retinopathy, hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Prognosis**: each 1 kg weight lost lowers diabetes risk by about 16% in prediabetes; 5% weight loss improves glucose, BP and lipids; 15 kg can give remission. [DPP 2006]`,`**Remission is not cure**: glucose often rises again with weight regain, so continue annual HbA1c and complication screening even in remission. [ADA/EASD 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Refer to a dietitian** at diagnosis and when not at target, with **3-4 visits in the first 3-6 months**, then at least yearly. [ADA 2026]`,`**Use motivational interviewing**: elicit the patient's own reasons, agree one or two SMART goals per visit (e.g. replace sugar in tea, 20-minute walk after dinner). [ADA 2026]`,`**Involve the family** - especially whoever cooks - because Indian meals are shared and food is tied to hospitality and festivals. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Check at each visit**: weight, waist, HbA1c trend, hypoglycaemia, steps or minutes of exercise, foot status and a short diet recall. [ADA 2026]`,`**Remission programme**: refer suitable patients (diabetes under 6 years, BMI 25 or more in Asians, motivated) to a supervised low-calorie programme; deprescribe drugs at its start to avoid hypoglycaemia. [Diabetes UK 2024]`,`**Refer to a cardiologist or ophthalmologist** before vigorous exercise if there is angina, autonomic neuropathy or proliferative retinopathy. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme link`,points:[`**NP-NCD** screens all adults over 30 at **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** and offers lifestyle counselling by community health officers. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians 2024**: 17 guidelines including a diverse diet, limiting salt, sugar and ultra-processed foods, reading food labels and staying active. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**International Year of Millets 2023** and the 'Shree Anna' campaign promote millets; note that processed millet products (cookies, flakes) can still have a high GI. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Eat Right India (FSSAI)** and **Fit India Movement** support reduced oil, sugar and salt and daily activity. [FSSAI 2025]`,`**Diabetes Prevention Program**: 7% weight loss and 150 minutes of activity cut progression to diabetes by **58%**; the Indian Diabetes Prevention Programme (IDPP-1) showed 28.5% reduction - see the Prediabetes topic. [IDPP 2006]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024** replaced the 2011 edition: 'My Plate for the Day', cereals no more than 45% of energy, added sugar under 5%, avoid protein supplements and alcohol. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB macronutrient study (Nature Medicine 2023)**: Indian-specific targets for remission - carbohydrate 49-54%, protein 19-20%, fat 21-26% of energy. [ICMR-INDIAB Nat Med 2023]`,`**WHO 2023 guideline on non-sugar sweeteners** advises against using them for weight control (no long-term benefit, possible cardiometabolic risk); ADA accepts them only as a short-term replacement for sugar, with water preferred. [WHO 2023]`,`**ADA Standards 2026 Section 5**: screen for food insecurity, individualise eating patterns, prioritise weight management, and protein and resistance training for those on incretin drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**DiRECT 5-year results (Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2024)** confirm durable remission in a minority and fewer serious adverse events. [DiRECT 5-year 2024]`,`**KDIGO 2022 diabetes in CKD**: protein 0.8 g/kg/day and sodium under 2 g/day in diabetes with CKD. [KDIGO 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'Diet in a 50-year-old diabetic' - calculate calories from ideal body weight, give the plate method, a sample Indian day menu, and exercise prescription. [Exam pattern]`,`**GI vs GL**: GI is quality, GL is quality times quantity - examiners want both definitions with cut-offs. [Atkinson 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing a 'no rice, no fruit' list; the modern advice is portion and quality, not banning foods. [ADA 2026]`,`**Remission definition**: HbA1c under 6.5% at least 3 months off all drugs - not 'cure'. [ADA/EASD 2021]`,`**Exercise caution trio**: proliferative retinopathy (no heavy lifting), active foot ulcer (non-weight-bearing), hypoglycaemia on insulin (check glucose first). [ADA 2026]`,`**Ideal body weight shortcut**: height in cm minus 100 (men) or minus 105 (women) gives a rough Indian ideal weight in kg for diet calculation. [ICMR 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Glycaemic index of common Indian foods (approximate)`,columns:[`Category`,`GI range`,`Examples`,`Advice`],rows:[[`High GI`,`70 or more`,`Glucose, polished white rice in large portion, cornflakes, boiled potato, watermelon, maida bread`,`Small portion, combine with dal and vegetables`],[`Medium GI`,`56-69`,`Whole-wheat chapati, parboiled rice, brown rice, upma, ripe banana`,`Main carbohydrate in a quarter plate`],[`Low GI`,`55 or less`,`Rajma, chana, moong and other whole pulses, milk, curd, apple, guava, most non-starchy vegetables`,`Use freely as protein and fibre sources`],[`Millets`,`Varies with processing, often medium`,`Ragi, jowar, bajra, foxtail, little millet as whole-grain roti or rice substitute`,`Prefer whole forms; processed millet snacks can be high GI`]]},{heading:`ICMR-NIN 'My Plate for the Day' (about 2,000 kcal) and diabetes adaptation`,columns:[`Food group`,`ICMR-NIN 2024 amount`,`Diabetes adaptation`,`Note`],rows:[[`Vegetables and green leafy`,`400 g`,`Increase; half of each plate`,`Raw salad plus cooked; limit potato`],[`Fruits`,`100 g`,`1-2 whole fruits, not juice`,`Counted within carbohydrate`],[`Cereals and millets`,`250 g raw`,`Reduce in overweight; whole grains and millets`,`Cereals no more than 45% of energy`],[`Pulses, eggs, flesh foods`,`85 g`,`Increase to a protein source at each meal`,`Check kidney function first`],[`Nuts and seeds`,`35 g`,`30 g unsalted daily`,`Energy dense - portion matters`],[`Milk and curd`,`300 mL`,`Toned or double-toned`,`Low GI, protein, calcium`],[`Fats and oils`,`27 g`,`20-25 g visible oil`,`Mix oils; no vanaspati or reheated oil`]]},{heading:`Exercise prescription in diabetes (FITT)`,columns:[`Type`,`Frequency`,`Intensity and time`,`Precautions`],rows:[[`Aerobic (brisk walk, cycling, swimming)`,`3-7 days/week, no more than 2 days off`,`Moderate (can talk, not sing); 150 min/week or more`,`Check glucose on insulin/SU; foot care`],[`Resistance (bands, weights, body weight)`,`2-3 non-consecutive days`,`8-10 exercises, 1-3 sets of 10-15 reps`,`Avoid heavy lifting and Valsalva in proliferative retinopathy`],[`Flexibility and balance (yoga, tai chi)`,`2-3 days/week`,`As tolerated`,`Older adults, neuropathy`],[`Breaking sitting time`,`Every 30 minutes`,`3 minutes of light walking or standing`,`Useful for desk workers`],[`Post-meal walk`,`After main meals`,`10-15 minutes light-moderate`,`Lowers postprandial peak`]]},{heading:`Contraindications and cautions for exercise`,columns:[`Condition`,`Risk`,`Modification`],rows:[[`Proliferative or severe NPDR`,`Vitreous haemorrhage, retinal detachment`,`No vigorous exercise, heavy lifting, head-down positions`],[`Peripheral neuropathy with ulcer`,`Non-healing ulcer, Charcot foot`,`Non-weight-bearing (swimming, cycling, chair exercise)`],[`Autonomic neuropathy`,`Silent ischaemia, hypotension, poor thermoregulation`,`Cardiac evaluation; use perceived exertion`],[`Insulin or sulfonylurea`,`Hypoglycaemia during and up to 24 h after`,`Glucose check; 15-30 g carbohydrate if under 90 mg/dL`],[`Type 1 with glucose over 250 and ketones`,`Worsening ketosis`,`Postpone exercise`],[`Known CAD or angina`,`Ischaemia, arrhythmia`,`Stress test and supervised programme`]]},{heading:`Diet approaches compared`,columns:[`Approach`,`What it involves`,`Best for`,`Caution`],rows:[[`Plate method`,`Half vegetables, quarter protein, quarter carbohydrate`,`All, especially low literacy`,`Portions still matter`],[`Calorie-counted exchange plan`,`Calories from ideal weight, exchanges`,`Motivated, dietitian access`,`Time-consuming`],[`Low-carbohydrate (26-45%)`,`Less rice/roti, more protein and vegetables`,`Overweight T2DM`,`Adjust insulin/SU; caution with SGLT2i`],[`Total diet replacement`,`825-853 kcal formula for 12-20 weeks`,`Remission in early T2DM`,`Supervision; stop SU/insulin at start`],[`Time-restricted eating`,`Eating window of 8-10 hours`,`Those who prefer simple rules`,`Hypoglycaemia on SU/insulin`]]}],redFlags:[`Weight loss and rising glucose despite strict diet (insulin deficiency - LADA, type 1, pancreatic diabetes)`,`Hypoglycaemia during or after exercise on insulin or sulfonylurea, especially at night`,`Chest pain, breathlessness or syncope on exertion`,`Proliferative retinopathy in someone starting weight training`,`Foot ulcer or new foot deformity in a patient walking for exercise`,`Ketonuria with nausea in someone on a very-low-carbohydrate diet and an SGLT2 inhibitor (euglycaemic DKA)`,`Binge eating, purging or insulin omission for weight control`],pearls:[`MNT lowers HbA1c by 1-2% - as much as a drug.`,`Plate method: half vegetables, quarter protein, quarter carbohydrate.`,`Indians eat about 62% of energy as carbohydrate; aim for about 50% with more protein (ICMR-INDIAB 2023).`,`GI: low 55 or less, high 70 or more; GL: low 10 or less, high 20 or more.`,`150 minutes aerobic over 3 days plus resistance twice weekly; never 2 days off in a row.`,`DiRECT: 15 kg lost gave 86% remission; 46% overall at 1 year.`,`Exercise effect on insulin sensitivity lasts 24-72 hours - hence daily activity.`,`WHO 2023: do not use non-sugar sweeteners for weight control.`,`FITT mnemonic: Frequency, Intensity, Time, Type.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-q1`,stem:`According to the ICMR-INDIAB macronutrient analysis (2023), which dietary change was associated with remission of newly diagnosed diabetes in Asian Indians?`,options:[`Increasing carbohydrate to 70% of energy from whole grains`,`Reducing carbohydrate to about 49-54% of energy and raising protein to about 19-20%`,`Replacing all carbohydrate with fat in a ketogenic diet`,`Lowering protein below 10% of energy to protect the kidneys`],answer:1,explanation:`ICMR-INDIAB (Nature Medicine 2023) found the usual Indian diet supplies about 62% of energy from carbohydrate; remission was associated with cutting carbohydrate to 49-54%, raising protein to 19-20% and keeping fat at 21-26%. Raising carbohydrate worsens control, a ketogenic diet is not the recommended long-term pattern, and protein restriction below 0.8 g/kg is not advised even in CKD.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes wants to start weight training at a gym. Fundoscopy shows proliferative diabetic retinopathy. What is the best advice?`,options:[`No restriction; resistance exercise improves glucose control`,`Avoid all physical activity until laser treatment is complete and then never exercise again`,`Avoid heavy lifting, Valsalva and vigorous exercise; continue moderate aerobic activity after ophthalmology review`,`Only exercise if HbA1c is under 7%`],answer:2,explanation:`Proliferative or severe non-proliferative retinopathy is a contraindication to vigorous aerobic and heavy resistance exercise because raised intraocular and BP surges can cause vitreous haemorrhage or retinal detachment. Moderate activity is still encouraged after ophthalmology clearance. Unrestricted training is unsafe, lifelong avoidance is unnecessary, and HbA1c does not decide exercise safety.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-q3`,stem:`In the DiRECT trial, what predicted remission of type 2 diabetes most strongly?`,options:[`The amount of weight lost, with most patients losing 15 kg or more achieving remission`,`Use of metformin during the diet phase`,`Baseline HbA1c above 10%`,`A vegetarian diet`],answer:0,explanation:`In DiRECT, total diet replacement followed by food reintroduction gave 46% remission at 1 year, and 86% of those who lost 15 kg or more were in remission - weight loss was the key determinant. All glucose-lowering drugs were stopped at the start, so metformin was not a factor; higher baseline HbA1c and longer duration predict lower, not higher, remission; diet type was not tested.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-q4`,stem:`A food has a glycaemic index of 60 and a serving contains 50 g of available carbohydrate. What is its glycaemic load and category?`,options:[`12 - medium`,`60 - high`,`8 - low`,`30 - high`],answer:3,explanation:`Glycaemic load = GI x grams of available carbohydrate / 100 = 60 x 50 / 100 = 30, which is high (20 or more). The value 60 is the GI itself, not the load; 12 and 8 come from miscalculation. This shows why a medium-GI food eaten in a large portion can still produce a large glucose rise.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-q5`,stem:`A patient on glimepiride and basal insulin plans a 1-hour evening brisk walk. Pre-exercise capillary glucose is 82 mg/dL. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Skip the walk and take an extra dose of glimepiride`,`Take 15-30 g of carbohydrate before starting and check glucose at bedtime`,`Walk without eating, since exercise raises glucose`,`Inject rapid-acting insulin before walking`],answer:1,explanation:`ADA advises taking 15-30 g of carbohydrate before exercise if glucose is under 90 mg/dL in people on insulin or sulfonylureas, because muscle glucose uptake rises and hypoglycaemia can occur during and up to 24 hours after activity - so bedtime glucose should be checked. Extra glimepiride or rapid insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia, and moderate exercise lowers, not raises, glucose.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe medical nutrition therapy for a 50-year-old Indian man with type 2 diabetes and obesity. Add a note on the exercise prescription and on remission of type 2 diabetes.`,openingLines:[`Medical nutrition therapy is individualised, dietitian-supported nutrition care that lowers HbA1c by 1-2% and is the foundation of diabetes treatment at every stage.`,`For Indians it means less refined carbohydrate, more protein and fibre, the plate method and millets, with 150 minutes of weekly activity and resistance training.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Assessment`,points:[`24-hour recall, oil and sugar use, meal timing, fasting, alcohol, food security; weight, BMI, waist, feet, fundus. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`2. Calorie and macronutrient plan`,points:[`Ideal weight (height minus 100) x 20-25 kcal; 500-750 kcal deficit. [ICMR 2018]`,`Carbohydrate about 50% (49-54%), protein 19-20%, fat 21-26%; sugar under 25 g, salt under 5 g. [ICMR-INDIAB Nat Med 2023]`]},{heading:`3. Practical Indian diet`,points:[`**Plate method**; ICMR-NIN 'My Plate for the Day'; millets and whole grains; dal at each meal; whole fruit; vegetables first. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`Sample day: vegetable-dal-millet roti meals, curd, fruit, nuts; tea without sugar. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`4. Exercise prescription`,points:[`150 min/week aerobic over 3 or more days; resistance 2-3 times a week; break sitting every 30 min; post-meal walk. [ADA 2026]`,`Precautions: retinopathy, foot ulcer, autonomic neuropathy, hypoglycaemia on insulin/SU. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`5. Remission`,points:[`Definition: HbA1c under 6.5% at 3 months off drugs. DiRECT: 46% at 1 year, 86% with 15 kg loss. [DiRECT Lancet 2018]`]},{heading:`6. Follow-up and programme`,points:[`Dietitian 3-4 visits in 6 months, HbA1c 3-monthly; NP-NCD and ICMR-NIN 2024 guidance. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]}],mustDraw:[`Diabetes plate diagram`,`FITT exercise table`],markSplit:[{part:`Assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Calorie and macronutrient plan`,marks:2},{part:`Practical Indian diet and plate`,marks:3},{part:`Exercise prescription with precautions`,marks:2.5},{part:`Remission and follow-up`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`MNT`,`plate method`,`ICMR-NIN 2024`,`millets`,`FITT`,`DiRECT`,`remission`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-nutrition-exercise-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate glycaemic index and glycaemic load, with examples from Indian foods.`,openingLines:[`Glycaemic index ranks the quality of a carbohydrate by its 2-hour glucose response compared with glucose.`,`Glycaemic load multiplies GI by the amount of carbohydrate eaten, so it reflects both quality and quantity.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Definitions and formula`,points:[`GI = area under glucose curve for 50 g available carbohydrate of the food / that of 50 g glucose x 100. [Atkinson 2021]`,`GL = GI x grams of available carbohydrate per serving / 100. [Atkinson 2021]`]},{heading:`2. Cut-offs`,points:[`GI: low 55 or less, medium 56-69, high 70 or more. GL: low 10 or less, medium 11-19, high 20 or more. [Atkinson 2021]`]},{heading:`3. Indian examples`,points:[`High GI: polished rice, potato, maida bread; low GI: rajma, chana, milk, curd, apple. [Atkinson 2021]`,`Watermelon has high GI but low GL per serving; a large plate of rice has high GL. [Atkinson 2021]`]},{heading:`4. Factors altering GI`,points:[`Processing, cooking time, ripeness, fibre, fat, protein and acid; parboiling and cooling lower GI. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: GI vs GL`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and formula`,marks:1.5},{part:`Cut-offs`,marks:1},{part:`Examples`,marks:1.5},{part:`Modifying factors`,marks:1}],keywords:[`glycaemic index`,`glycaemic load`,`parboiled rice`,`pulses`]}],references:[`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 5: Facilitating positive health behaviours`,`ICMR-National Institute of Nutrition. Dietary Guidelines for Indians, 2024`,`Anjana RM et al. Macronutrient recommendations for remission and prevention of diabetes in Asian Indians (ICMR-INDIAB) - Nature Medicine, 2023`,`Lean MEJ et al. Primary care-led weight management for remission of type 2 diabetes (DiRECT) - Lancet, 2018; 5-year follow-up, Lancet Diabetes & Endocrinology, 2024`,`ICMR Guidelines for Management of Type 2 Diabetes, 2018`,`RSSDI-ESI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, 2022`,`WHO Guideline on use of non-sugar sweeteners, 2023`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`]}),u.push({id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy`,title:`Glucocorticoid therapy: equivalence, side effects, tapering and steroid cover`,oneLiner:`**Supraphysiological glucocorticoid** (above about **prednisolone 5 mg or hydrocortisone 20 mg a day**) given for more than **3-4 weeks** by any route suppresses the **HPA axis** and causes predictable dose- and time-dependent toxicity (Cushingoid features, hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, infection); it must be **tapered**, not stopped, covered with **stress doses** for illness and surgery, and the patient given a **steroid emergency card** (ESE/ES 2024).`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`corticosteroids`,`prednisolone`,`hydrocortisone`,`dexamethasone`,`methylprednisolone`,`deflazacort`,`steroid equivalence`,`HPA axis suppression`,`glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency`,`steroid taper`,`steroid cover`,`stress dosing`,`sick day rules`,`steroid emergency card`,`glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis`,`iatrogenic Cushing syndrome`,`steroid withdrawal syndrome`,`pulse steroids`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Glucocorticoid (GC) therapy** is the use of natural or synthetic corticosteroids for their anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive action (pharmacological dose) or to replace deficient cortisol (physiological dose). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Physiological cortisol output** is about **8-10 mg/m2/day**, equivalent to **hydrocortisone 15-25 mg** or **prednisolone 4-5 mg** a day; anything above this, for long enough, suppresses the HPA axis. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Prevalence**: about **1-3% of adults** in population studies take systemic GCs at any time and about 1% take them long-term; use is higher in the elderly and in rheumatology, respiratory and renal clinics. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency (GI-AI)** occurred in about **49% after oral**, 52% after intra-articular, 8% after inhaled and 5% after topical GC in a meta-analysis - it is common, not rare. [Broersen JCEM 2015]`,`**India-specific misuse**: over-the-counter dexamethasone and betamethasone for pain and 'weakness', adulterated traditional remedies, and topical steroid misuse in fairness creams lead to iatrogenic Cushing syndrome and adrenal crises. [IJEM 2019]`,`**COVID-19 lesson**: inappropriate high-dose steroid use with uncontrolled diabetes drove India's **2021 mucormycosis epidemic** - a reminder that steroid harm is dose- and glucose-dependent. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`**Iatrogenic Cushing syndrome** from exogenous GC is the **commonest cause of Cushing syndrome** overall. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Indications and risk factors for toxicity`,points:[`**Common indications**: asthma and COPD exacerbations, rheumatoid arthritis, **polymyalgia rheumatica**, giant cell arteritis, SLE and vasculitis, nephrotic syndrome, IBD, autoimmune hepatitis, transplant, cerebral oedema, severe allergy, TB meningitis and pericarditis, sepsis with refractory shock, COVID-19 needing oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Replacement indications**: primary and secondary adrenal insufficiency and congenital adrenal hyperplasia - see the Adrenal disorders topic. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Toxicity rises with dose and duration**: cumulative dose drives osteoporosis, cataract and diabetes; peak dose drives psychosis and infection. [ACR 2022]`,`**HPA suppression risk** is high with prednisolone **20 mg or more for 3 weeks or more**, any Cushingoid patient, and **evening or bedtime dosing**; low with any dose for under 3 weeks; intermediate in between. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Long-acting agents** (dexamethasone, betamethasone) and **split or night doses** suppress the axis more than a single morning dose of prednisolone. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Drug interactions**: **CYP3A4 inhibitors** (ritonavir, cobicistat, itraconazole, clarithromycin) raise GC levels - even **inhaled fluticasone or budesonide** can cause Cushing syndrome and AI; **rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine** speed GC clearance (double the steroid dose with rifampicin). [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Host factors**: elderly, postmenopausal women, diabetes, low BMI, previous fracture, liver disease and hypoalbuminaemia (more free drug) increase toxicity. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - mechanism of action, side effects and HPA suppression`,points:[`GCs cross the cell membrane and bind the cytoplasmic **glucocorticoid receptor (GR)**, which moves to the nucleus and acts by **transactivation** (switching on genes such as lipocortin/annexin-1) and **transrepression** (blocking NF-kappa-B and AP-1). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-inflammatory effect**: transrepression cuts cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF), COX-2, adhesion molecules and phospholipase A2 activity; **lymphopenia, eosinopenia and neutrophilia** (demargination) follow within hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic side effects come mainly from transactivation**: hepatic **gluconeogenesis** and insulin resistance (hyperglycaemia), muscle protein breakdown (myopathy), lipolysis with central fat redistribution (Cushingoid habitus). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Bone**: GCs cause **osteoblast and osteocyte apoptosis**, raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin, reduce gut calcium absorption and sex steroids - fracture risk rises within **3-6 months**, even at 2.5-7.5 mg/day. [ACR 2022]`,`**Mineralocorticoid effect**: cortisol binds the MR equally to aldosterone but is normally inactivated by **11-beta-HSD2** in the kidney; high hydrocortisone doses saturate it, causing sodium retention, hypertension and hypokalaemia - synthetic agents like dexamethasone have almost no MR activity. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**HPA suppression**: exogenous GC feeds back on hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH; without ACTH the **zona fasciculata atrophies**, so on withdrawal the adrenal cannot respond - **tertiary (secondary) adrenal insufficiency**. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Mineralocorticoid axis is preserved** (renin-angiotensin control of zona glomerulosa), so GI-AI does not cause hyperkalaemia and is usually less severe than primary AI - but adrenal crisis still occurs. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Recovery order**: hypothalamus and pituitary recover first (ACTH rises), then the adrenal regrows; full recovery takes **weeks to 12 months or more** after long courses. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Prednisone is a prodrug** converted to prednisolone by hepatic 11-beta-HSD1 - prefer prednisolone in liver disease (prednisolone is what is marketed in India). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - potency, half-life and dose bands`,points:[`**Equivalent anti-inflammatory doses**: hydrocortisone **20 mg** = cortisone acetate 25 mg = **prednisolone 5 mg** = methylprednisolone 4 mg = triamcinolone 4 mg = **dexamethasone 0.75 mg** = betamethasone 0.6 mg = deflazacort 6 mg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative glucocorticoid potency**: hydrocortisone 1, prednisolone 4, methylprednisolone 5, dexamethasone 25-30; **mineralocorticoid potency** falls from hydrocortisone 1 to prednisolone 0.8, methylprednisolone 0.5 and dexamethasone 0. [Harrison 22e]`,`**By biological half-life**: short (8-12 h) hydrocortisone and cortisone; intermediate (12-36 h) prednisolone, methylprednisolone, triamcinolone; long (36-54 h) dexamethasone and betamethasone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EULAR dose nomenclature** (prednisolone-equivalent): low **7.5 mg/day or less**, medium over 7.5-30 mg, high over 30-100 mg, very high over 100 mg, **pulse 250 mg or more** for 1-5 days. [EULAR 2002]`,`**Fludrocortisone** is a pure mineralocorticoid replacement (50-200 microgram/day) for primary AI and orthostatic hypotension, not an anti-inflammatory. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**By route**: systemic (oral, IV, IM depot), inhaled, intranasal, topical (graded by potency class from mild hydrocortisone 1% to super-potent clobetasol 0.05%), intra-articular and epidural - **all can suppress the HPA axis**. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Topical potency (Indian/UK class)**: mild (hydrocortisone 1%), moderate (clobetasone butyrate 0.05%), potent (betamethasone valerate 0.1%, mometasone 0.1%), very potent (clobetasol propionate 0.05%). [BNF 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history in a patient on steroids`,points:[`**Exact steroid history**: drug, dose, route, timing (morning or split), duration, cumulative courses in the last year, and **non-prescribed sources** (local injections, 'pain powders', traditional remedies, skin creams). [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Cushingoid symptoms**: weight gain, round face, easy bruising, poor wound healing, proximal weakness (difficulty climbing stairs or rising from squatting), mood change, insomnia, menstrual disturbance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptoms of adrenal insufficiency after a taper or abrupt stop**: fatigue, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, weight loss, dizziness, arthralgia and myalgia - often non-specific and missed. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Glucocorticoid withdrawal syndrome**: myalgia, arthralgia, fatigue, low mood and sleep disturbance during dose reduction even when the HPA axis is adequate - mimics AI and disease flare. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Infection history**: past TB or TB contact, hepatitis B, herpes zoster, recurrent infections; strongyloides exposure in endemic rural areas. [ACR 2022]`,`**Bone history**: previous fragility fracture, height loss, back pain, falls, menopausal status. [ACR 2022]`,`**Eye symptoms**: blurred vision (posterior subcapsular cataract, central serous chorioretinopathy), eye pain or haloes (raised intraocular pressure). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Moon face, plethora, dorsocervical (buffalo) and supraclavicular fat pads, central obesity** - Cushingoid habitus from fat redistribution. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thin skin, purple striae over 1 cm, easy bruising, poor wound healing** - collagen loss from inhibited fibroblasts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Proximal myopathy** with normal CK (steroid myopathy) - test by asking the patient to rise from a squat or chair without using the arms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertension, oedema and hypokalaemia** - mineralocorticoid effect, mostly with hydrocortisone or high doses. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Acne, hirsutism, oral or genital candidiasis, tinea** - androgenic and immunosuppressive effects. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postural hypotension and hyperpigmentation**: hypotension may occur in GI-AI but **hyperpigmentation does not** (ACTH is low) - pigmentation points to primary AI. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Growth chart in children** - height velocity falls on chronic systemic or high-dose inhaled GC. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Iatrogenic vs endogenous Cushing syndrome**: iatrogenic has low ACTH and low serum cortisol (except with hydrocortisone or prednisolone, which cross-react in immunoassays); endogenous has high cortisol. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Adrenal insufficiency vs glucocorticoid withdrawal syndrome**: AI has low morning cortisol and may cause hypotension, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia; withdrawal syndrome has adequate cortisol and no biochemical abnormality. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**AI vs flare of the underlying disease** (e.g. PMR returning on taper): disease flare shows raised ESR/CRP and disease-specific signs. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Steroid myopathy vs inflammatory myositis flare**: steroid myopathy has normal CK and painless proximal weakness; myositis has raised CK. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Steroid psychosis vs CNS lupus or infection**: psychosis appears early, at high doses (over 40 mg/day), and settles with dose reduction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary vs GC-induced (tertiary) AI**: primary has high ACTH, pigmentation, hyperkalaemia and needs fludrocortisone; GC-induced has low ACTH and normal potassium. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations - baseline, monitoring and HPA testing`,points:[`**Before long-term GC** (over 3 months or high dose): weight, BP, HbA1c/FPG, lipids, potassium, **chest X-ray and TB symptom screen**, latent TB test (IGRA or TST) where indicated, hepatitis B serology, BMD (DXA) and fracture risk (FRAX). [ACR 2022]`,`**Glucose monitoring**: morning prednisolone causes **afternoon and evening hyperglycaemia** with normal fasting glucose - check pre-lunch or pre-dinner capillary glucose, not fasting, for steroid-induced hyperglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Do not test the HPA axis while the patient is still on supraphysiological doses** - the result is predictably suppressed and adds nothing. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Morning (8 am) serum cortisol** once the dose is at a physiological level: omit hydrocortisone for **24 hours** (longer for prednisolone; dexamethasone suppresses for days) before the test. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Interpretation (assay-dependent)**: cortisol **over 300 nmol/L (about 10 microgram/dL)** - axis recovered, stop GC; **under 150 nmol/L (about 5 microgram/dL)** - continue physiological GC and retest in weeks to months; **150-300** - continue tapering and repeat, or do a short Synacthen test. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Short Synacthen test** (250 microgram ACTH IV or IM, cortisol at 0 and 30-60 min) is used for equivocal morning cortisol - see the Dynamic endocrine tests topic. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Assay pitfalls**: prednisolone and hydrocortisone cross-react in cortisol immunoassays; **oral oestrogen raises CBG** and total cortisol, masking AI. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Eye check**: slit lamp and intraocular pressure yearly on long-term GC, earlier with symptoms or family history of glaucoma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - prescribing, prevention of harm and tapering`,points:[`**Prescribing principles**: lowest effective dose, shortest duration, **single morning dose** (mimics the diurnal peak, less HPA suppression), use steroid-sparing agents early (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, biologics). [ACR 2022]`,`**Short courses under 3 weeks** (e.g. prednisolone 40-50 mg for 5-7 days in asthma or COPD exacerbation) can be **stopped without a taper** unless repeated courses or longer prior exposure. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Bone protection** for prednisolone **2.5 mg or more for 3 months or more**: calcium 800-1,000 mg/day and vitamin D 600-800 IU/day, BMD within 6 months, and **alendronate 70 mg weekly** (or risedronate) for moderate or high fracture risk; zoledronic acid, denosumab or teriparatide for very high risk. [ACR 2022]`,`**GI protection**: a PPI only if also on NSAIDs or aspirin, an anticoagulant, or with past peptic ulcer - GC alone causes little ulcer risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infection prevention**: inactivated vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal, zoster recombinant) before starting; **live vaccines contraindicated** at prednisolone **20 mg/day or more for 14 days or more**; **PJP prophylaxis** (co-trimoxazole 960 mg three times weekly) for 20 mg or more for 4 weeks or more with another immunosuppressant or haematological malignancy. [ACR 2022]`,`**TB in India**: screen for TB symptoms and give TB preventive treatment where latent TB is found in patients starting prolonged immunosuppressive therapy, as per national TPT guidance. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Strongyloides**: give **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg single dose** (repeat at 2 weeks in some protocols) before high-dose GC in people from endemic areas with eosinophilia, to prevent hyperinfection. [CDC 2024]`,`**Typical taper of prednisolone** (adjust to disease activity): above 40 mg - reduce 5-10 mg every 1-2 weeks; **20-40 mg - reduce 5 mg** every 1-2 weeks; **10-20 mg - reduce 2.5 mg** every 2-3 weeks; **5-10 mg - reduce 1 mg** every 2-4 weeks. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**At the physiological dose** (prednisolone 4-5 mg), either continue a slow taper (e.g. 1 mg each month) with clinical monitoring, or check 8 am cortisol; switching to **hydrocortisone 15-20 mg/day in divided doses** is an option. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Steroid emergency card** and patient education for every patient on long-term GC, with sick day rules and an emergency hydrocortisone injection kit for those at highest risk. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`Steroid cover - sick day rules, surgery and adrenal crisis`,points:[`**Who needs cover**: anyone on prednisolone **5 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more**, anyone who stopped such therapy within the last 12 months, and those with proven GI-AI. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Sick day rule 1**: with fever over 38 C or illness needing bed rest or antibiotics, **double the usual daily GC dose** (or take hydrocortisone 20 mg 6-hourly) until recovered, usually 2-3 days. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Sick day rule 2**: if vomiting, diarrhoea or unable to take tablets, give **hydrocortisone 100 mg IM** and attend hospital at once. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Minor procedures under local anaesthesia** (dental extraction, skin biopsy, most imaging): usually no extra GC; take the usual dose. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Major surgery under general anaesthesia, labour and delivery, major trauma, ICU**: **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction**, then **200 mg/24 h by continuous infusion** (or 50 mg IV every 6 hours), then double the oral dose for 48 hours or longer and return to the usual dose as recovery allows. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Intermediate procedures** (e.g. endoscopy with sedation, minor surgery under GA): hydrocortisone 50-100 mg IV before the procedure and a doubled oral dose for 24-48 hours. [AAGBI 2020]`,`**Adrenal crisis**: **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV or IM immediately**, then 200 mg/24 h (infusion or 50 mg 6-hourly), **0.9% saline 1 L in the first hour** then by need, dextrose if hypoglycaemic; treat the trigger; do not wait for cortisol results. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Patients already on high doses** (above stress-dose equivalent, e.g. prednisolone over 40 mg) usually need no extra cover beyond continuing their dose parenterally if nil by mouth. [AAGBI 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **prednisolone is preferred** - placental 11-beta-HSD2 inactivates about 90%; **dexamethasone and betamethasone cross the placenta** and are used deliberately for fetal lung maturity (betamethasone 12 mg IM, two doses 24 hours apart). [RCOG 2022]`,`**Breastfeeding**: prednisolone is compatible; above 40 mg/day, feed just before the dose or wait 4 hours. [BNF 2025]`,`**Children**: GC slows growth - use alternate-day dosing or the lowest dose, monitor height velocity, and remember high-dose inhaled fluticasone can cause adrenal crisis. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Elderly**: higher risk of fracture, delirium, hyperglycaemia, cataract and infection - prefer the lowest dose and start bone protection early. [ACR 2022]`,`**Diabetes**: expect a rise mainly in afternoon-evening glucose; morning **NPH insulin** matched to prednisolone dose is the usual fix - see the Steroid-induced hyperglycaemia topic. [ADA 2026]`,`**TB on rifampicin**: rifampicin halves prednisolone exposure - **double the GC dose** (including replacement doses in AI). [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Liver disease and hypoalbuminaemia**: use prednisolone, not prednisone; more free drug means more side effects at the same dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coexisting hypothyroidism and AI**: start glucocorticoid **before** levothyroxine to avoid precipitating adrenal crisis. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Endocrine/metabolic**: hyperglycaemia and new diabetes, Cushingoid habitus, weight gain, dyslipidaemia, HPA suppression and adrenal crisis, growth retardation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Musculoskeletal**: osteoporosis and **vertebral fractures** (risk doubles), **avascular necrosis of the femoral head** (even after high-dose short courses), proximal myopathy. [ACR 2022]`,`**Cardiovascular**: hypertension, fluid retention, hypokalaemia, and higher risk of MI, stroke and heart failure at higher cumulative doses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infections**: bacterial, TB reactivation, herpes zoster, PJP, fungal (candida, **mucormycosis** with diabetes), strongyloides hyperinfection; fever and signs may be masked. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eye**: **posterior subcapsular cataract**, open-angle glaucoma, central serous chorioretinopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuropsychiatric**: insomnia, mood swings, euphoria, depression, **psychosis** (dose over 40 mg/day), cognitive change. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GI and skin**: peptic ulcer with NSAIDs, pancreatitis, fatty liver; thin skin, striae, bruising, acne, poor wound healing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prognosis of GI-AI**: most patients recover HPA function within **6-12 months**, but some take years and a few never recover; adrenal crisis deaths are preventable with education and cards. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Every visit on long-term GC**: weight, BP, glucose (afternoon), symptoms of infection, mood, sleep, muscle strength. [ACR 2022]`,`**Every 3-6 months**: HbA1c, lipids, potassium; **every 1-3 years**: DXA (more often if high risk); **yearly**: eye check for cataract and glaucoma. [ACR 2022]`,`**Refer to endocrinology** for equivocal or persistently low morning cortisol, repeated adrenal crises, iatrogenic Cushing from non-prescribed steroids, or difficult taper. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Refer to rheumatology, pulmonology or nephrology** to add steroid-sparing agents when GC cannot be tapered below about 7.5 mg/day. [ACR 2022]`,`**Retest morning cortisol** every 1-3 months during a slow taper at physiological dose until recovery. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Admit** with vomiting and hypotension, suspected adrenal crisis, sepsis on high-dose GC, steroid psychosis, or DKA/HHS triggered by steroids. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Counsel**: never stop suddenly; carry the steroid card; follow sick day rules; inform any doctor, dentist or anaesthetist; take the dose in the morning with food. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Discourage non-prescribed steroids**: ask directly about local injections, powders and fairness creams; topical steroids are prescription-only (Schedule H) yet widely sold over the counter. [IADVL 2023]`,`**Rational use**: avoid steroids for viral fevers, non-specific aches and 'tonic' use; COVID-19 guidance limited steroids to patients needing oxygen. [ICMR COVID guidance 2021]`,`**NLEM 2022** lists prednisolone, hydrocortisone, dexamethasone and methylprednisolone - low-cost generics are available through **Jan Aushadhi** stores. [NLEM 2022]`,`**Diabetes and TB screening** under NP-NCD and NTEP for patients on long-term GC, as both are common in India and worsened by steroids. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Bone health**: calcium and vitamin D and weight-bearing exercise for all on long-term GC; fall prevention in the elderly. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESE/Endocrine Society 2024 guideline on glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency**: no routine HPA testing on supraphysiological doses; taper to physiological dose then use **morning cortisol (150/300 nmol/L thresholds)** to decide; stress dosing and emergency cards for all at risk. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**ESE/ES 2024** recognised the **glucocorticoid withdrawal syndrome** as distinct from AI - manage by temporarily raising the dose and slowing the taper. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**ESE/ES 2024** advised switching long-acting agents (dexamethasone) to shorter-acting ones (prednisolone, hydrocortisone) when approaching physiological doses, to allow HPA recovery. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**ACR 2022 GIOP guideline** extends bone protection to doses of **2.5 mg/day or more for 3 months or more**, with oral bisphosphonate first-line and sequential therapy after anabolic agents. [ACR 2022]`,`**GINA 2024-2025** emphasises reducing **oral corticosteroid burden** in asthma (cumulative OCS harm begins at about 500-1,000 mg lifetime) using ICS-formoterol and biologics. [GINA 2025]`,`**UK national steroid emergency card (2020)** and the ESE/ES 2024 guideline both stress emergency card use - widely adopted in Indian endocrinology practice. [Society for Endocrinology 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Equivalence chain to memorise**: hydrocortisone 20 = prednisolone 5 = methylprednisolone 4 = dexamethasone 0.75 mg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why taper?** Not because of the disease - because the suppressed HPA axis needs time; short courses under 3 weeks need no taper. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Why no hyperkalaemia in GI-AI?** The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis is intact. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Why prednisolone in pregnancy?** Placental 11-beta-HSD2 inactivates it; dexamethasone crosses. [RCOG 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: checking fasting glucose for steroid diabetes - the rise is afternoon and evening. [ADA 2026]`,`**Mnemonic for side effects - CUSHINGOID**: Cataract, Ulcers, Skin thinning and Striae, Hypertension and Hyperglycaemia, Infection, Necrosis of femoral head, Glucose intolerance, Osteoporosis, Immunosuppression, Diabetes and Depression. [Exam pattern]`,`**Rifampicin trap**: patients on steroids starting ATT need a doubled steroid dose. [ESE/ES 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Glucocorticoid equivalence and potency`,columns:[`Drug`,`Equivalent dose (mg)`,`Glucocorticoid potency`,`Mineralocorticoid potency`,`Biological half-life`],rows:[[`Hydrocortisone`,`20`,`1`,`1`,`Short, 8-12 h`],[`Cortisone acetate`,`25`,`0.8`,`0.8`,`Short, 8-12 h`],[`Prednisolone / prednisone`,`5`,`4`,`0.8`,`Intermediate, 12-36 h`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`4`,`5`,`0.5`,`Intermediate, 12-36 h`],[`Triamcinolone`,`4`,`5`,`0`,`Intermediate, 12-36 h`],[`Deflazacort`,`6`,`4`,`Minimal`,`Intermediate`],[`Dexamethasone`,`0.75`,`25-30`,`0`,`Long, 36-54 h`],[`Betamethasone`,`0.6`,`25-30`,`0`,`Long, 36-54 h`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`Not used for anti-inflammatory effect`,`10`,`125-250`,`Intermediate`]]},{heading:`Glucocorticoid drugs: doses, use and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone`,`Low 7.5 mg or less; medium up to 30 mg; high 30-100 mg orally once in the morning`,`Taper if over 3 weeks; 1 mg steps below 10 mg`,`All systemic GC effects; double dose with rifampicin`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`Replacement 15-25 mg/day in 2-3 doses; stress 100 mg IV then 200 mg/24 h`,`Stress doses for 24-72 h then taper`,`Fluid retention and hypokalaemia at high dose`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`Pulse 500-1,000 mg IV daily for 3-5 days`,`Follow with oral prednisolone`,`Hyperglycaemia, arrhythmia with rapid infusion, psychosis, infection`],[`Dexamethasone`,`Cerebral oedema 8-16 mg/day; COVID-19 with oxygen 6 mg/day for up to 10 days`,`Switch to shorter-acting agent before final taper`,`Strong HPA suppression; crosses placenta; hyperglycaemia`],[`Betamethasone`,`Fetal lung maturity 12 mg IM, 2 doses 24 h apart`,`Single course`,`Neonatal hypoglycaemia; maternal hyperglycaemia`],[`Deflazacort`,`6-90 mg/day orally (6 mg = 5 mg prednisolone)`,`Taper as prednisolone`,`Claimed bone and glucose sparing - weak evidence`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`50-200 microgram/day orally`,`Long term in primary AI`,`Hypertension, hypokalaemia, oedema`]]},{heading:`Steroid cover for illness and surgery (ESE/ES 2024 and AAGBI 2020)`,columns:[`Situation`,`Glucocorticoid cover`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Minor illness with fever over 38 C`,`Double the usual oral dose`,`Until recovered, usually 2-3 days`],[`Vomiting or unable to take tablets`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IM and go to hospital`,`Then IV regimen`],[`Minor procedure under local anaesthesia`,`Usual dose; no extra`,`-`],[`Intermediate procedure or minor surgery under GA`,`Hydrocortisone 50-100 mg IV before procedure`,`Double oral dose for 24-48 h`],[`Major surgery, labour, trauma, ICU`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction, then 200 mg/24 h infusion or 50 mg 6-hourly`,`Until eating; double oral dose 48 h or more, then usual`],[`Adrenal crisis`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV/IM stat, then 200 mg/24 h; saline 1 L in first hour`,`Taper to oral over 1-3 days after recovery`]]},{heading:`Interpreting morning cortisol during taper (ESE/ES 2024)`,columns:[`8 am cortisol`,`Interpretation`,`Action`],rows:[[`Over 300 nmol/L (about 10 microgram/dL)`,`HPA axis recovered`,`Stop glucocorticoid`],[`150-300 nmol/L (5-10 microgram/dL)`,`Partial recovery`,`Continue taper at physiological dose; repeat in weeks or do short Synacthen test`],[`Under 150 nmol/L (about 5 microgram/dL)`,`Adrenal insufficiency likely`,`Continue physiological GC with stress dosing; retest in 1-3 months`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of symptoms during steroid taper`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key features`,`Cortisol`,`Action`],rows:[[`GC-induced adrenal insufficiency`,`Fatigue, nausea, hypotension, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia`,`Low morning cortisol`,`Physiological replacement, stress dosing`],[`GC withdrawal syndrome`,`Myalgia, arthralgia, fatigue, low mood`,`Adequate`,`Temporarily raise dose, slow taper`],[`Flare of underlying disease`,`Disease-specific signs, raised ESR/CRP`,`Variable`,`Raise dose, add steroid-sparing agent`],[`Primary adrenal insufficiency (coexistent)`,`Pigmentation, hyperkalaemia, salt craving`,`Low with high ACTH`,`Hydrocortisone plus fludrocortisone`]]}],redFlags:[`Vomiting, hypotension or collapse in a patient on or recently off long-term steroids (adrenal crisis)`,`Fever or sepsis in a patient on high-dose steroids - signs may be masked`,`Sudden hip or groin pain on steroids (avascular necrosis of femoral head)`,`Acute psychosis, severe agitation or suicidal thoughts on high-dose steroids`,`Blood glucose over 300 mg/dL or ketones after starting steroids`,`Painful red eye, visual loss or haloes on long-term steroids (glaucoma)`,`Black necrotic nasal or palatal lesion in a diabetic on steroids (mucormycosis)`],pearls:[`Hydrocortisone 20 = prednisolone 5 = methylprednisolone 4 = dexamethasone 0.75 mg.`,`Under 3 weeks: no taper needed; over 3-4 weeks above 5 mg prednisolone: taper.`,`Morning cortisol over 300 nmol/L - stop; under 150 nmol/L - continue replacement (ESE/ES 2024).`,`Sick day rule: double the dose for fever; vomiting means 100 mg IM hydrocortisone and hospital.`,`Major surgery: hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction, then 200 mg/24 h.`,`GI-AI spares aldosterone - no hyperkalaemia, no pigmentation.`,`Bone protection starts at 2.5 mg/day for 3 months (ACR 2022).`,`Rifampicin doubles the steroid requirement.`,`Steroid diabetes peaks in the afternoon - check pre-dinner glucose.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-q1`,stem:`A patient takes dexamethasone 1.5 mg daily. What is the approximately equivalent daily dose of prednisolone?`,options:[`1.5 mg`,`5 mg`,`10 mg`,`40 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`Dexamethasone 0.75 mg is equivalent to prednisolone 5 mg (and hydrocortisone 20 mg), so 1.5 mg dexamethasone is about 10 mg prednisolone. Treating the doses as equal (1.5 mg) ignores dexamethasone's 25-30-fold potency; 5 mg is the equivalent of only 0.75 mg; 40 mg overestimates it fourfold.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-q2`,stem:`A woman with polymyalgia rheumatica has been tapered to prednisolone 5 mg daily after 14 months. According to the ESE/Endocrine Society 2024 guideline, an 8 am serum cortisol (taken 24 hours after the last dose) of 340 nmol/L means:`,options:[`Her HPA axis has recovered and the glucocorticoid can be stopped`,`She has adrenal insufficiency and needs lifelong hydrocortisone`,`She must have a short Synacthen test before any change`,`The result is invalid because cortisol cannot be measured during a taper`],answer:0,explanation:`ESE/ES 2024 suggests that, once at a physiological dose, a morning cortisol above about 300 nmol/L (10 microgram/dL) indicates HPA recovery, so GC can be stopped (watching for PMR relapse). Values under 150 nmol/L suggest AI and 150-300 nmol/L are indeterminate, where a Synacthen test may help; measurement is valid at physiological doses once the dose has been withheld long enough.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-q3`,stem:`A man on prednisolone 10 mg daily for 2 years for interstitial lung disease is scheduled for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy under general anaesthesia. What is the appropriate steroid cover?`,options:[`Omit the prednisolone on the day of surgery`,`Continue oral prednisolone only; no extra cover is needed`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction, then 200 mg/24 h until eating, then double oral dose for 48 hours`,`Dexamethasone 20 mg IV daily for one week`],answer:2,explanation:`Anyone on 5 mg prednisolone or more for over 4 weeks is at risk of adrenal insufficiency under surgical stress; for surgery under general anaesthesia ESE/ES 2024 advises hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at induction then 200 mg/24 h, followed by a doubled oral dose before returning to usual. Omitting the dose risks crisis, usual dose alone may be inadequate for major stress, and high-dose dexamethasone is excessive and lacks mineralocorticoid action.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-q4`,stem:`Which feature helps distinguish glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency from primary adrenal insufficiency?`,options:[`Hyponatraemia`,`Absence of hyperpigmentation and normal potassium`,`Fatigue and anorexia`,`Postural hypotension`],answer:1,explanation:`In GC-induced (tertiary) AI, ACTH is suppressed so there is no pigmentation, and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis is intact so potassium stays normal. Primary AI has high ACTH (pigmentation) and aldosterone deficiency (hyperkalaemia). Hyponatraemia, fatigue and postural hypotension can occur in both and do not discriminate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman is to start prednisolone 15 mg daily for at least 6 months. Which preventive measure is recommended by the ACR 2022 glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis guideline?`,options:[`Bone protection only if the dose exceeds 30 mg`,`Routine PPI to prevent osteoporosis`,`Wait for the first fracture before starting treatment`,`Calcium and vitamin D, BMD and fracture risk assessment within 6 months, and an oral bisphosphonate if risk is moderate or high`],answer:3,explanation:`ACR 2022 recommends optimising calcium and vitamin D for anyone on prednisolone 2.5 mg or more for 3 months or more, BMD and fracture risk assessment within 6 months, and an oral bisphosphonate (e.g. alendronate 70 mg weekly) for moderate or high risk - postmenopausal women on 15 mg usually qualify. Waiting for a fracture or a 30 mg threshold is wrong, and PPIs do not protect bone.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the adverse effects of long-term glucocorticoid therapy and their prevention. How will you taper steroids and provide steroid cover for surgery?`,openingLines:[`Glucocorticoids given above physiological dose (prednisolone 5 mg or hydrocortisone 20 mg daily) for more than 3-4 weeks cause dose- and time-dependent toxicity and suppress the HPA axis.`,`They must therefore be used at the lowest dose for the shortest time, with bone, infection and metabolic protection, a planned taper and stress-dose cover (ESE/ES 2024).`],answer:[{heading:`1. Equivalence and mechanism`,points:[`Draw the equivalence table; GR transrepression (benefit) vs transactivation (metabolic harm). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`2. Adverse effects by system`,points:[`Metabolic, bone (osteoporosis, AVN), muscle, CVS, infection (TB, PJP, mucor), eye, CNS, skin, GI, growth, HPA suppression. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`3. Prevention`,points:[`Lowest dose, morning single dose, steroid-sparing agents. [ACR 2022]`,`Calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate (ACR 2022); TB screen and TPT; vaccines; PJP prophylaxis; glucose monitoring afternoon. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`4. Tapering`,points:[`No taper if under 3 weeks; stepwise reduction; at 5 mg, slow taper or 8 am cortisol (over 300 stop, under 150 continue). [ESE/ES 2024]`,`Withdrawal syndrome vs AI vs flare. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`5. Steroid cover`,points:[`Sick day rules; minor procedures - usual dose; major surgery - hydrocortisone 100 mg IV then 200 mg/24 h; adrenal crisis protocol. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`6. Education`,points:[`Steroid emergency card, never stop abruptly, inform all doctors. [ESE/ES 2024]`]}],mustDraw:[`Equivalence and potency table`,`Tapering flow with morning cortisol thresholds`,`Steroid cover table by procedure`],markSplit:[{part:`Equivalence and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Adverse effects`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:2},{part:`Tapering`,marks:2},{part:`Steroid cover and education`,marks:2}],keywords:[`HPA suppression`,`taper`,`steroid cover`,`ESE/ES 2024`,`GIOP`,`emergency card`]},{id:`endocrine-glucocorticoid-therapy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency.`,openingLines:[`Glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency is tertiary AI from suppression of CRH and ACTH by exogenous steroid, with atrophy of the zona fasciculata.`,`It follows about half of long oral courses, and also intra-articular, inhaled and topical use.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Who is at risk`,points:[`Over 3-4 weeks above physiological dose, evening dosing, long-acting agents, CYP3A4 inhibitors with inhaled steroids. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`2. Features`,points:[`Fatigue, nausea, hypotension, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia; no pigmentation, normal potassium. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`3. Diagnosis`,points:[`8 am cortisol at physiological dose: over 300 nmol/L recovered, under 150 AI; Synacthen if equivocal. [ESE/ES 2024]`]},{heading:`4. Management`,points:[`Physiological GC, slow taper, stress dosing, emergency card; adrenal crisis - hydrocortisone 100 mg IV and saline. [ESE/ES 2024]`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: taper to physiological dose, morning cortisol, decision`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk`,marks:1},{part:`Features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`tertiary adrenal insufficiency`,`morning cortisol`,`stress dose`,`emergency card`]}],references:[`Beuschlein F et al. European Society of Endocrinology and Endocrine Society Joint Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and therapy of glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency, 2024`,`Humphrey MB et al. 2022 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Prevention and Treatment of Glucocorticoid-Induced Osteoporosis, 2023`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Glucocorticoid therapy and disorders of the adrenal cortex`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Woodcock T et al. Guidelines for the management of glucocorticoids during the peri-operative period for patients with adrenal insufficiency - AAGBI/RCP/Society for Endocrinology, 2020`,`Broersen LH et al. Adrenal insufficiency in corticosteroid use: systematic review and meta-analysis - JCEM, 2015`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, MoHFW/NTEP, 2021`]}),u.push({id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests`,title:`Dynamic endocrine function tests: how to do and interpret them`,oneLiner:`**Dynamic endocrine tests** challenge a hormonal axis because single basal levels fluctuate: **stimulate when deficiency is suspected** (short Synacthen, insulin tolerance, glucagon, water deprivation with desmopressin) and **suppress when excess is suspected** (dexamethasone suppression for cortisol, **OGTT for GH**, saline infusion for aldosterone) - each with a fixed protocol, drug preparation, safety precautions and assay-specific cut-offs.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`dynamic function tests`,`short Synacthen test`,`ACTH stimulation test`,`tetracosactide`,`overnight dexamethasone suppression test`,`LDDST`,`HDDST`,`OGTT GH suppression`,`insulin tolerance test`,`glucagon stimulation test`,`water deprivation test`,`copeptin`,`saline infusion test`,`clomiphene stimulation test`,`TRH test`,`72-hour fast`,`aldosterone-renin ratio`,`biotin interference`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, principles and epidemiology of the conditions tested`,points:[`**Dynamic testing** measures a hormone's response to a stimulus or suppressant, because basal levels are pulsatile, diurnal or affected by stress and binding proteins - a single random value often cannot confirm or exclude disease. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Rule of thumb**: **'If it is low, stimulate it; if it is high, suppress it'** - deficiency is confirmed by a failed response to stimulation, excess by failure to suppress. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sequence**: clinical suspicion first, then basal screening test, then a dynamic confirmatory test, and only then **imaging** - imaging first finds incidentalomas and misleads. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adrenal insufficiency** affects about 100-140 per million (primary) and 150-280 per million (secondary), plus a far larger number with glucocorticoid-induced AI - see the Glucocorticoid therapy topic. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Endogenous Cushing syndrome** is rare (about 2-3 per million per year) but **mild autonomous cortisol secretion** is found in up to a third of adrenal incidentalomas. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Primary aldosteronism** is present in about **5-10% of all hypertensives** and up to 20% of resistant hypertension - the commonest endocrine cause of hypertension and the main reason for dynamic tests in primary care. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Acromegaly** (about 60-80 per million) and **AVP deficiency** (diabetes insipidus, about 1 in 25,000) are uncommon, but their diagnosis rests entirely on dynamic tests. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Indications - when to order a dynamic test`,points:[`**Suspected adrenal insufficiency** with an indeterminate morning cortisol (roughly 100-450 nmol/L, assay-dependent) - short Synacthen test. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Suspected Cushing syndrome** (purple striae, proximal myopathy, easy bruising, unexplained osteoporosis or diabetes in the young) and every **adrenal incidentaloma** - 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Suspected acromegaly** with raised or equivocal IGF-1 - OGTT with GH suppression. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Suspected GH deficiency** in adults with pituitary disease, or children with short stature and low height velocity - ITT, glucagon, clonidine or arginine tests. [AACE 2019]`,`**Polyuria over 3 L/day** (adults) with dilute urine after excluding diabetes mellitus, hypercalcaemia and hypokalaemia - water deprivation or copeptin-based tests. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Positive aldosterone-renin ratio** in a hypertensive - confirmatory saline infusion, oral salt loading, captopril or fludrocortisone suppression test. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Fasting hypoglycaemia in a non-diabetic** (Whipple triad documented) - supervised 72-hour fast. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Hypogonadism, puberty disorders and TSH-secreting tumour vs thyroid hormone resistance** - clomiphene, GnRH/GnRH-agonist and TRH tests in specialist centres. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Physiology - why each test works`,points:[`**Negative feedback** underlies suppression tests: dexamethasone (not measured in cortisol assays) suppresses pituitary ACTH, so cortisol falls in normal people but not with autonomous secretion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cushing disease keeps partial feedback**: a pituitary corticotroph adenoma suppresses with **high-dose** dexamethasone, while ectopic ACTH and adrenal tumours do not - the logic of the HDDST. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Synacthen (tetracosactide, ACTH 1-24)** stimulates the adrenal directly; a chronically ACTH-deprived adrenal (secondary AI) atrophies and cannot respond - but **within 4-6 weeks of a pituitary insult** the adrenal has not yet atrophied, so the test can be falsely normal. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Hypoglycaemia (ITT)** is a powerful stress acting at the hypothalamus, releasing CRH-ACTH-cortisol and GHRH-GH - so it tests the whole axis, making it the gold standard. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Glucose suppresses GH** via somatostatin release; autonomous somatotroph adenomas fail to suppress - and may show a paradoxical rise. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Water deprivation** raises plasma osmolality, which normally releases AVP and concentrates urine; failure to concentrate that is corrected by **desmopressin** means AVP deficiency, while no correction means **AVP resistance** (renal). [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Copeptin** is the stable C-terminal fragment of pre-pro-AVP, released 1:1 with AVP - a reliable surrogate for the unstable AVP molecule. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Sodium loading** normally suppresses renin and aldosterone; autonomous aldosterone production (adenoma, bilateral hyperplasia) fails to suppress. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Clomiphene** blocks hypothalamic oestrogen receptors, removing negative feedback so GnRH, LH and FSH rise - an intact response shows the hypothalamic-pituitary axis works. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification of dynamic tests`,points:[`**Stimulation tests (suspected deficiency)**: short Synacthen, insulin tolerance, glucagon stimulation, clonidine and arginine (GH), macimorelin, GnRH/GnRH agonist, clomiphene, TRH, hCG stimulation (testes), water deprivation with desmopressin, arginine- or hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Suppression tests (suspected excess)**: 1 mg overnight, low-dose 48-hour and high-dose dexamethasone suppression; OGTT for GH; saline infusion, oral salt loading, captopril and fludrocortisone tests for aldosterone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Provocation tests for neoplasia**: 72-hour fast (insulinoma), calcium stimulation of calcitonin (MTC), CRH or desmopressin stimulation of ACTH (Cushing disease), mixed-meal test (post-bariatric hypoglycaemia). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Localisation dynamic tests** (tertiary centres): **bilateral inferior petrosal sinus sampling** with CRH or desmopressin (central-to-peripheral ACTH ratio 2 or more basal, 3 or more after stimulation = pituitary source) and **adrenal venous sampling** (lateralisation of aldosterone). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**By axis**: adrenal cortex (SST, DST, ITT), GH (OGTT, ITT, glucagon), posterior pituitary (water deprivation, copeptin), mineralocorticoid (saline infusion), gonadal (clomiphene, GnRH, hCG), thyroid (TRH), pancreas (72-h fast). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical preparation - history before the test`,points:[`**Drug history is the commonest cause of a wrong result**: steroids by any route, oral oestrogen (raises CBG), enzyme inducers, CYP3A4 inhibitors, antihypertensives, diuretics, biotin. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Hydrocortisone**: omit for at least 12-24 hours before an SST or morning cortisol; **prednisolone** cross-reacts in assays and suppresses the axis - withhold longer; **dexamethasone** does not cross-react and can be used as cover while testing. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Oral oestrogen** (combined pill, HRT) raises cortisol-binding globulin and total cortisol - stop for **6 weeks** before cortisol tests, or interpret with caution; transdermal oestrogen does not. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Aldosterone tests**: correct hypokalaemia; stop **spironolactone and eplerenone for 4-6 weeks**; ACE inhibitors, ARBs, diuretics and dihydropyridine CCBs raise renin (false negative); beta-blockers and clonidine lower renin (false positive) - switch to **verapamil SR, hydralazine, prazosin or doxazosin**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Biotin** (hair and nail supplements) interferes with streptavidin-biotin immunoassays for TSH, thyroid hormones, cortisol, PTH and others - stop it at least **2-3 days** before testing. [FDA 2019]`,`**Comorbidity screen for ITT**: ischaemic heart disease, abnormal ECG, epilepsy, age over 60-65, untreated hypothyroidism, and morning cortisol under 100 nmol/L are contraindications. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Timing and stress**: acute illness, night shifts, alcohol excess, depression and severe obesity alter cortisol rhythm - delay elective cortisol testing until recovery. [Endocrine Society 2008]`]},{heading:`Patient preparation, consent and safety`,points:[`**Explain the purpose, duration and risks** and obtain consent; many tests need fasting from midnight and a supine rest of 30 minutes before baseline sampling. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Cannula placed 30 minutes before baseline** to avoid stress-related cortisol and GH rise; label every sample with time. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Weigh the patient** (water deprivation, ITT and glucagon dosing depend on weight). [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Bedside resuscitation equipment, 10% and 25% dextrose, and hydrocortisone** must be ready for ITT, glucagon and Synacthen tests. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Doctor present throughout** an insulin tolerance test and at intervals in water deprivation - these tests can be dangerous. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Sample handling**: ACTH in **chilled EDTA tube on ice** and separated quickly; renin and aldosterone at room temperature (cold activates prorenin); copeptin and cortisol are stable. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After the test**: give a meal (ITT, glucagon, 72-h fast), resume withheld drugs, and give a glucocorticoid stress dose if adrenal insufficiency is likely. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Pitfalls and differential interpretation`,points:[`**Cortisol-binding globulin (CBG)**: high in pregnancy and oral oestrogen (falsely high cortisol, missed AI); low in nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, critical illness (falsely low cortisol, overdiagnosed AI). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Assay-specific cut-offs**: newer monoclonal immunoassays and LC-MS/MS read cortisol about 20% lower than older polyclonal assays - the classic 500 nmol/L SST cut-off overcalls AI with them. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**False-positive dexamethasone suppression**: enzyme inducers (rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine) speed dexamethasone breakdown; oral oestrogen; non-adherence; **pseudo-Cushing states** (depression, alcohol, severe obesity, uncontrolled diabetes). [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**False-negative dexamethasone suppression**: CYP3A4 inhibitors (itraconazole, ritonavir) and liver failure slow dexamethasone clearance; cyclical Cushing syndrome tested in a quiet phase. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**OGTT-GH pitfalls**: GH may fail to suppress in uncontrolled diabetes, liver or renal failure, anorexia, adolescence and with oral oestrogen - false positives for acromegaly. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Water deprivation pitfalls**: chronic polyuria washes out the renal medullary gradient, so even normal or primary polydipsia patients may concentrate poorly - accuracy of the classic test is only about **70%**. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Aldosterone-renin ratio pitfalls**: hypokalaemia lowers aldosterone (false negative); age, CKD and beta-blockers lower renin (false positive); the ratio is meaningless without minimum aldosterone levels. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Adrenal tests - morning cortisol, short Synacthen and ITT`,points:[`**8-9 am serum cortisol**: under **100 nmol/L (about 3 microgram/dL)** strongly suggests AI; over about **400-450 nmol/L (15 microgram/dL)** makes it unlikely; values between need a stimulation test. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Paired ACTH** distinguishes primary AI (ACTH over 2 times upper limit, high renin) from secondary AI (low or inappropriately normal ACTH). [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Short Synacthen test (SST)**: **tetracosactide 250 microgram IV or IM**, cortisol at **0, 30 and 60 minutes**; can be done any time of day; normal if peak cortisol is at or above the cut-off. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**SST cut-off**: classic **500 nmol/L (18 microgram/dL)**; with newer immunoassays or LC-MS/MS, about **420-450 nmol/L (14-15 microgram/dL)** at 30 minutes - use the local laboratory's assay-specific value. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**Low-dose (1 microgram) SST** may detect milder secondary AI but dilution and adsorption to plastic make it less reliable; the 250 microgram test is standard. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Insulin tolerance test**: soluble insulin **0.1-0.15 U/kg IV** (0.05 U/kg if hypopituitarism likely, 0.2-0.3 U/kg if insulin resistant); adequate hypoglycaemia = glucose **under 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L) with symptoms**; samples at 0, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120 min. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**ITT interpretation**: peak cortisol at or above about **500 nmol/L (assay-dependent)** excludes AI; peak GH under **3 microgram/L** in adults (under 10, or 7 in some protocols, in children) indicates GH deficiency. [AACE 2019]`,`**Stopping an ITT**: if severe neuroglycopenia (seizure, unconsciousness) - give **IV dextrose (e.g. 10% 100-150 mL or 25% 50 mL)** but continue sampling; finish with a meal and observe. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Cushing tests - dexamethasone suppression and localisation`,points:[`**1 mg overnight DST**: dexamethasone **1 mg orally at 11 pm-midnight**, serum cortisol at **8-9 am**; cortisol **under 50 nmol/L (1.8 microgram/dL)** is normal suppression and excludes Cushing syndrome with high sensitivity. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Adrenal incidentaloma (ESE/ENSAT 2023)**: 1 mg DST cortisol **over 50 nmol/L without overt Cushing features = mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS)**; screen for hypertension, diabetes and osteoporosis rather than labelling by a higher threshold. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**Measure serum dexamethasone with the post-DST cortisol** where available to confirm adequate absorption and exclude rapid metabolism. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Low-dose 48-hour DST (LDDST)**: **0.5 mg every 6 hours for 48 hours** (eight doses, e.g. 9 am day 1 to 3 am day 3), cortisol at 48 hours; under 50 nmol/L is normal - more specific than the overnight test and less affected by pseudo-Cushing. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Other first-line screens**: **late-night salivary cortisol** (two samples above the assay's upper limit) and **24-hour urinary free cortisol** (two collections, over 3 times upper limit strongly positive); two abnormal tests are needed for diagnosis. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**High-dose DST** (2 mg every 6 hours for 48 hours, or 8 mg overnight): cortisol suppression **over 50%** of baseline favours pituitary Cushing disease over ectopic ACTH - poor accuracy, now largely replaced by CRH/desmopressin tests and IPSS. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**ACTH after confirmation**: under 10 pg/mL (2 pmol/L) means adrenal cause - adrenal CT; over 20 pg/mL means ACTH-dependent - pituitary MRI, then IPSS if the MRI is negative or the adenoma under 6 mm. [Pituitary Society 2021]`]},{heading:`GH axis tests - OGTT suppression and stimulation tests`,points:[`**Acromegaly**: measure **IGF-1 first** (age- and sex-adjusted); if raised or equivocal, do a **75 g OGTT** with GH at 0, 30, 60, 90 and 120 minutes. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**OGTT-GH interpretation**: failure of GH to fall **below 1 microgram/L** (below 0.4 microgram/L with ultrasensitive assays) confirms acromegaly; a paradoxical rise is seen in some. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Glucagon stimulation test** (when ITT is contraindicated): **glucagon 1 mg IM** (1.5 mg if over 90 kg), GH and glucose every 30 min for 240 min; peak GH **3 microgram/L or less** in BMI under 25 (or 25-30 with high pre-test probability), **1 microgram/L or less** in BMI over 30 indicates GHD. [AACE 2019]`,`**Macimorelin oral test** (ghrelin agonist, 0.5 mg/kg): peak GH under **2.8 microgram/L** indicates adult GHD; simple and safe but costly and not widely available in India. [AACE 2019]`,`**Children**: two different stimulation tests (clonidine **0.15 mg/m2 orally**, glucagon, arginine, ITT) must both fail, with peak GH under **10 microgram/L** (7 in many guidelines) - **sex-steroid priming** before testing peripubertal children avoids false positives. [PES 2016]`,`**Clonidine test side effects**: drowsiness and hypotension for several hours - monitor BP and keep the child lying down. [PES 2016]`,`**Adults with three or more other pituitary deficiencies and low IGF-1** have GHD without a stimulation test. [AACE 2019]`]},{heading:`Posterior pituitary - water deprivation and copeptin tests`,points:[`**First confirm polyuria**: 24-hour urine over 3 L (adults) with osmolality under 300 mOsm/kg; exclude hyperglycaemia, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia and lithium. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Water deprivation protocol**: fluids withheld (from early morning; overnight only if polyuria is mild); hourly weight, urine volume and osmolality; plasma sodium and osmolality every 1-2 hours; maximum about **8 hours**. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Stop deprivation** if weight falls **over 3%**, plasma sodium exceeds **145-150 mmol/L** or osmolality over 300-305 mOsm/kg, or urine osmolality reaches **over 600-800 mOsm/kg** (normal concentrating ability). [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Desmopressin phase**: give **desmopressin 2 microgram IM or SC (or 10-20 microgram intranasal)**, allow limited drinking, and measure urine osmolality over the next 2-4 hours. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Interpretation**: urine osmolality stays **under 300** after deprivation then rises **over 50%** with desmopressin = **AVP deficiency** (central DI); stays under 300 with little rise = **AVP resistance** (nephrogenic); **over 800** after deprivation = normal or primary polydipsia; 300-800 is indeterminate. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Hypertonic saline-stimulated copeptin**: 3% saline infused to plasma sodium **150 mmol/L or more**; copeptin **over 4.9 pmol/L** = primary polydipsia, lower = AVP deficiency (accuracy about 97%); needs sodium monitoring every 30 minutes. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Arginine-stimulated copeptin**: arginine infusion over 30 min, copeptin at 60 min **over 3.8 pmol/L** favours primary polydipsia - simpler and safer but less accurate than hypertonic saline. [Refardt Lancet 2023]`,`**Baseline copeptin over 21.4 pmol/L** without prior fluid restriction indicates **AVP resistance** - no deprivation test needed. [Endocrine Society 2022]`]},{heading:`Aldosterone tests - screening and confirmation`,points:[`**Screen** with a morning **aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR)** after 2 hours upright and 5-15 minutes seated, potassium corrected; a raised ARR with aldosterone over about 10 ng/dL (277 pmol/L) and suppressed renin is positive. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Saline infusion test (SIT)**: **2 L of 0.9% saline over 4 hours** (8 am to 12 noon), seated preferred to recumbent; aldosterone at 0 and 4 h; post-infusion aldosterone **under 5 ng/dL makes PA unlikely**, **over 10 ng/dL (recumbent) or over 6 ng/dL (seated) confirms it**. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**SIT contraindications**: uncontrolled severe hypertension, heart failure, renal insufficiency, severe hypokalaemia, arrhythmia - monitor BP and potassium during the infusion. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Oral salt loading test**: over 200 mmol sodium/day for 3 days with potassium replacement; 24-hour urine aldosterone **over 12 microgram/day** (with urine sodium over 200 mmol/day) confirms PA. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Captopril challenge**: 25-50 mg orally seated; aldosterone normally falls by over 30% at 1-2 hours - persistent high aldosterone with suppressed renin confirms PA; safe in heart failure and CKD. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Confirmation can be skipped** with spontaneous hypokalaemia, undetectable renin and aldosterone over 20 ng/dL - proceed to CT and adrenal venous sampling if surgery is considered. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Other stimulation and provocation tests`,points:[`**Clomiphene stimulation test (men)**: clomiphene **100 mg (or up to 3 mg/kg, maximum 200 mg) daily for 7 days**; a rise (roughly doubling) of LH, FSH and testosterone shows an intact hypothalamic-pituitary axis - a failed response suggests hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Clomiphene citrate challenge test (women)**: 100 mg on days 5-9 with FSH on day 3 and day 10; a raised day-10 FSH indicated poor ovarian reserve - now replaced by **AMH and antral follicle count**. [ASRM 2020]`,`**TRH test**: **TRH 200 microgram IV**, TSH at 0, 20 and 60 minutes; a normal rise at 20 min; a **delayed 60-minute peak** suggests hypothalamic disease; **TSH rises in thyroid hormone resistance but not in TSH-secreting adenoma** - TRH is rarely available in India. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**GnRH or GnRH-agonist stimulation** for precocious puberty: LH peak **over 5 IU/L** indicates central (gonadotropin-dependent) puberty; leuprolide is used where gonadorelin is unavailable - see the Precocious puberty topic. [ESPE 2019]`,`**hCG stimulation test** in boys with undescended testes or ambiguous genitalia: testosterone rise shows functioning Leydig cells; a raised testosterone-to-DHT ratio suggests 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Supervised 72-hour fast** for suspected insulinoma: end when glucose is **55 mg/dL (3.0 mmol/L) or less** with symptoms; insulin **3 microU/mL or more**, C-peptide **0.6 ng/mL or more**, proinsulin 5 pmol/L or more, beta-hydroxybutyrate 2.7 mmol/L or less, negative sulfonylurea screen = endogenous hyperinsulinism; glucagon 1 mg IV at the end raises glucose by 25 mg/dL or more. [Endocrine Society 2009]`,`**Mixed-meal test** (not OGTT) is used to provoke **post-bariatric hypoglycaemia**, because the OGTT itself causes dumping and false positives. [Endocrine Society 2009]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: CBG and total cortisol rise 2-3 fold and DST suppression is blunted - **24-hour urinary free cortisol** (with trimester-specific cut-offs) is preferred for Cushing; avoid ITT, water deprivation and saline loading. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Critical illness**: avoid formal dynamic tests; a random cortisol under about **275 nmol/L (10 microgram/dL)** or a rise under 250 nmol/L (9 microgram/dL) after Synacthen suggests critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency - but treatment of shock should not wait. [SCCM/ESICM 2024]`,`**Children**: weight-based doses, sex-steroid priming for GH tests, and avoid ITT in young children because of seizure risk; use glucagon or clonidine. [PES 2016]`,`**Elderly and cardiac patients**: ITT is contraindicated; use SST for the adrenal axis and glucagon or macimorelin for GH. [AACE 2019]`,`**CKD and liver disease**: low CBG and altered dexamethasone and GH handling give false results - prefer late-night salivary cortisol in CKD with caution, and interpret OGTT-GH with care. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**Resource-limited settings in India**: Synacthen supply is intermittent and TRH and GnRH are hard to obtain; a well-timed **8 am cortisol**, the **1 mg overnight DST** and the **75 g OGTT** are cheap, available everywhere and answer most primary-care questions. [ESI 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications of testing, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**ITT**: severe neuroglycopenia, seizures, arrhythmia and myocardial ischaemia; deaths have occurred in children given excessive dextrose rescue (cerebral oedema) - do it only in experienced units. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Water deprivation**: dangerous dehydration and hypernatraemia in true AVP deficiency - stop at 3% weight loss; never leave the patient unsupervised (risk of secret drinking falsifying the result as well). [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Hypertonic saline test**: osmotic symptoms, and risk of overshooting sodium - check sodium every 30 minutes and stop at 150 mmol/L. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`,`**Saline infusion test**: volume overload, hypertension and hypokalaemia in susceptible patients. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Synacthen and glucagon**: rare allergy to tetracosactide (more with atopy); nausea, vomiting and late hypoglycaemia after glucagon. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Referral**: primary care can do the 8 am cortisol, 1 mg DST, ARR and OGTT; **refer ITT, water deprivation, copeptin, LDDST/HDDST, IPSS and adrenal venous sampling** to an endocrinology unit. [ESI 2023]`,`**Follow-up**: repeat an abnormal screening test before acting, interpret with the clinical picture, and repeat dynamic tests after treatment (e.g. OGTT-GH after acromegaly surgery, SST months after pituitary surgery). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026) and exam angle`,points:[`**ESE/ES 2024 glucocorticoid-induced AI guideline** endorses lower, **assay-specific SST cut-offs** and morning cortisol thresholds (150/300 nmol/L) for deciding whether to stop steroids. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`**ESE/ENSAT 2023 adrenal incidentaloma update** replaced 'possible autonomous cortisol secretion' with **MACS** for 1 mg DST cortisol over 50 nmol/L without overt Cushing features. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`,`**2022 international nomenclature** renamed central DI as **AVP deficiency** and nephrogenic DI as **AVP resistance**; copeptin-based tests (hypertonic saline, arginine) are preferred over classic water deprivation where available. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`**Endocrine Society 2025 primary aldosteronism guideline** recommends screening all adults with hypertension with ARR and allows skipping confirmatory tests when the biochemistry is unequivocal. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Exam favourite**: 'If it is low, stimulate; if high, suppress' and the cut-off list - SST peak 500 (or assay-specific about 450) nmol/L, 1 mg DST under 50 nmol/L, OGTT-GH under 1 microgram/L, SIT aldosterone over 10 ng/dL. [Exam pattern]`,`**Viva trap**: a normal SST does not exclude secondary AI within 4-6 weeks of pituitary surgery or apoplexy - the adrenal has not yet atrophied. [Endocrine Society 2016]`,`**Viva trap 2**: dexamethasone, not prednisolone, is used in suppression tests because it is potent and **not measured in cortisol assays**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Test agents: dose, protocol and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tetracosactide (Synacthen)`,`250 microgram IV or IM (1 microgram in low-dose test)`,`Cortisol at 0, 30, 60 min`,`Rare allergy; false normal within 4-6 weeks of pituitary insult`],[`Dexamethasone (overnight DST)`,`1 mg orally at 11 pm-midnight`,`Cortisol at 8-9 am next day`,`Enzyme inducers and oral oestrogen cause false positives`],[`Dexamethasone (LDDST / HDDST)`,`0.5 mg 6-hourly (LDDST) or 2 mg 6-hourly (HDDST) orally`,`48 hours; cortisol at 48 h`,`Adherence; same drug interactions`],[`Soluble insulin (ITT)`,`0.1-0.15 U/kg IV (0.05 if hypopituitary; 0.2-0.3 if insulin resistant)`,`Samples 0-120 min; target glucose under 40 mg/dL`,`Contraindicated in IHD, epilepsy, elderly, cortisol under 100 nmol/L`],[`Glucagon (GH stimulation)`,`1 mg IM (1.5 mg if over 90 kg)`,`GH every 30 min for 240 min`,`Nausea, vomiting, late hypoglycaemia`],[`Glucose 75 g (OGTT-GH)`,`75 g anhydrous glucose orally in 250-300 mL water`,`GH at 0, 30, 60, 90, 120 min`,`False positive in diabetes, liver or renal failure, adolescence`],[`Desmopressin (water deprivation)`,`2 microgram IM/SC or 10-20 microgram intranasal`,`Urine osmolality over 2-4 h`,`Hyponatraemia if patient drinks freely after`],[`0.9% saline (SIT)`,`2 L IV over 4 h`,`Aldosterone at 0 and 4 h`,`Avoid in heart failure, uncontrolled hypertension, severe hypokalaemia`],[`Clomiphene citrate`,`100 mg (up to 200 mg) orally daily`,`7 days; LH, FSH, testosterone before and after`,`Visual disturbance, mood change, hot flushes`],[`TRH (protirelin)`,`200 microgram IV`,`TSH at 0, 20, 60 min`,`Flushing, nausea, urge to micturate; poor availability`]]},{heading:`Which test for which question`,columns:[`Clinical question`,`Screening test`,`Dynamic confirmatory test`,`Abnormal result`],rows:[[`Adrenal insufficiency`,`8 am cortisol with ACTH`,`Short Synacthen test (ITT gold standard)`,`Peak cortisol under 500 nmol/L (assay-specific about 420-450)`],[`Cushing syndrome`,`1 mg DST, late-night salivary cortisol, 24-h UFC`,`LDDST 48 h`,`Cortisol over 50 nmol/L after dexamethasone`],[`Acromegaly`,`IGF-1`,`75 g OGTT with GH`,`GH nadir not below 1 microgram/L`],[`Adult GH deficiency`,`IGF-1 (often normal)`,`ITT, glucagon or macimorelin`,`ITT peak GH under 3; glucagon 3 or less (1 if obese) microgram/L`],[`AVP deficiency vs primary polydipsia`,`24-h urine volume and osmolality, plasma sodium`,`Hypertonic saline-copeptin or water deprivation with desmopressin`,`Copeptin 4.9 pmol/L or less at sodium 150`],[`Primary aldosteronism`,`Aldosterone-renin ratio`,`Saline infusion, salt loading or captopril test`,`Post-saline aldosterone over 10 (recumbent) or 6 (seated) ng/dL`],[`Insulinoma`,`Glucose, insulin, C-peptide during symptoms`,`Supervised 72-hour fast`,`Glucose 55 or less with insulin 3 or more and C-peptide 0.6 or more`]]},{heading:`Water deprivation test interpretation`,columns:[`Diagnosis`,`Urine osmolality after deprivation (mOsm/kg)`,`Rise after desmopressin`,`Plasma osmolality at end`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Over 800`,`Under 10%`,`Normal`],[`Primary polydipsia`,`Usually over 600 (may be lower from medullary washout)`,`Under 10%`,`Normal or low`],[`Complete AVP deficiency (central DI)`,`Under 300`,`Over 50%`,`High`],[`Partial AVP deficiency`,`300-800`,`10-50%`,`High-normal`],[`AVP resistance (nephrogenic DI)`,`Under 300`,`Under 10-50%, often none`,`High`]]},{heading:`Common interferences and pitfalls`,columns:[`Factor`,`Test affected`,`Effect`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Oral oestrogen (pill, HRT), pregnancy`,`Cortisol (SST, DST)`,`Raised CBG - falsely high cortisol, false-positive DST, missed AI`,`Stop oral oestrogen 6 weeks or use urinary/salivary tests`],[`Rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine`,`DST`,`Fast dexamethasone clearance - false failure to suppress`,`Measure dexamethasone level or use other tests`],[`Hydrocortisone, prednisolone`,`Cortisol assays`,`Cross-reactivity and HPA suppression`,`Withhold 12-24 h or more; cover with dexamethasone`],[`Spironolactone, diuretics, ACEi/ARB, DHP-CCB`,`ARR`,`Renin raised - false negative`,`Switch to verapamil SR, hydralazine, alpha-blockers`],[`Beta-blockers, clonidine, NSAIDs`,`ARR`,`Renin lowered - false positive`,`Switch where safe or interpret with caution`],[`Biotin supplements`,`Immunoassays (TSH, FT4, cortisol, PTH)`,`Spuriously high or low values`,`Stop 2-3 days before testing`],[`Hypokalaemia`,`ARR, aldosterone tests`,`Aldosterone suppressed - false negative`,`Correct potassium first`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of ACTH-dependent Cushing syndrome by dynamic tests`,columns:[`Test`,`Cushing disease (pituitary)`,`Ectopic ACTH`,`Adrenal tumour`],rows:[[`Plasma ACTH`,`Normal or mildly raised`,`Often very high`,`Suppressed (under 10 pg/mL)`],[`High-dose DST`,`Suppresses over 50% (most)`,`Usually no suppression`,`No suppression`],[`CRH or desmopressin test`,`ACTH and cortisol rise`,`Usually no rise`,`No rise`],[`IPSS central-to-peripheral ACTH ratio`,`2 or more basal, 3 or more after CRH/desmopressin`,`Under 2 / under 3`,`Not indicated`],[`Hypokalaemia`,`Uncommon`,`Common, severe`,`Occasional`]]}],redFlags:[`Morning cortisol under 100 nmol/L with vomiting or hypotension - treat as adrenal crisis, do not wait for a Synacthen test`,`Seizure or unconsciousness during an insulin tolerance test`,`Weight loss over 3% or plasma sodium over 150 mmol/L during water deprivation`,`Hypernatraemia with altered sensorium in a polyuric patient who cannot drink`,`Chest pain or arrhythmia during ITT or saline infusion`,`Severe hypokalaemia (under 3.0 mmol/L) found during aldosterone work-up`,`Glucose 55 mg/dL or less with neuroglycopenia during a supervised fast`],pearls:[`If it is low, stimulate it; if it is high, suppress it.`,`Biochemistry first, imaging last - or you will find incidentalomas.`,`SST: 250 microgram tetracosactide, cortisol at 0, 30, 60 min; peak 500 nmol/L classic, about 450 with newer assays.`,`1 mg overnight DST: cortisol under 50 nmol/L (1.8 microgram/dL) is normal.`,`OGTT-GH: nadir under 1 microgram/L excludes acromegaly.`,`ITT is the gold standard for both cortisol and GH, but contraindicated in IHD and epilepsy.`,`Water deprivation: under 300 then over 50% rise with desmopressin means AVP deficiency.`,`Copeptin over 4.9 pmol/L after hypertonic saline means primary polydipsia.`,`Stop spironolactone 4-6 weeks and correct potassium before the aldosterone-renin ratio.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with an incidental 2.5 cm adrenal adenoma has an 8 am cortisol of 95 nmol/L after 1 mg dexamethasone at midnight. She has hypertension but no Cushingoid features. How is this best classified under the ESE/ENSAT 2023 guideline?`,options:[`Normal suppression - no further evaluation`,`Overt Cushing syndrome needing urgent adrenalectomy`,`Mild autonomous cortisol secretion`,`Primary aldosteronism`],answer:2,explanation:`A post-dexamethasone cortisol above 50 nmol/L (1.8 microgram/dL) without overt clinical Cushing features is termed mild autonomous cortisol secretion (MACS) in the 2023 ESE/ENSAT update, and should prompt screening for hypertension, diabetes and osteoporosis. Normal suppression requires under 50 nmol/L; overt Cushing needs clinical features and further confirmation; the DST says nothing about aldosterone.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-q2`,stem:`Ten days after transsphenoidal surgery for a pituitary macroadenoma, a patient has a normal short Synacthen test. What is the correct interpretation?`,options:[`The test may be falsely normal because the adrenals have not yet atrophied; cover with glucocorticoid and retest after 6 weeks`,`Secondary adrenal insufficiency is excluded permanently`,`The test proves primary adrenal insufficiency`,`An insulin tolerance test is contraindicated for life`],answer:0,explanation:`The SST tests adrenal responsiveness to ACTH; after acute loss of pituitary ACTH, the adrenal cortex takes about 4-6 weeks to atrophy, so the response can still be normal early on. A morning cortisol or glucocorticoid cover with later retesting is advised. It neither excludes secondary AI permanently nor indicates primary AI, and ITT can be used later if there are no contraindications.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-q3`,stem:`A patient with polyuria undergoes water deprivation. Urine osmolality reaches only 210 mOsm/kg with plasma sodium 148 mmol/L; after desmopressin it rises to 520 mOsm/kg. The diagnosis is:`,options:[`Primary polydipsia`,`Arginine vasopressin resistance (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus)`,`Normal response`,`Arginine vasopressin deficiency (central diabetes insipidus)`],answer:3,explanation:`Failure to concentrate urine (under 300 mOsm/kg) despite rising plasma sodium, followed by a rise of over 50% after desmopressin, shows the kidney responds to AVP but none is being secreted - AVP deficiency. In AVP resistance the urine does not concentrate after desmopressin; in primary polydipsia and normal people urine concentrates well during deprivation itself.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-q4`,stem:`A hypertensive man with a raised aldosterone-renin ratio is due for a saline infusion test. Which preparation is most appropriate?`,options:[`Continue spironolactone to protect against hypokalaemia`,`Correct hypokalaemia and stop spironolactone 4-6 weeks before, switching to verapamil SR or an alpha-blocker if needed`,`Start a beta-blocker to control BP during the test`,`Restrict dietary salt for a week before the test`],answer:1,explanation:`Spironolactone raises renin and invalidates aldosterone testing, and hypokalaemia suppresses aldosterone, so both must be dealt with first; verapamil SR, hydralazine and alpha-blockers have minimal effect on the axis. Beta-blockers suppress renin and give false positives, and salt restriction stimulates renin-aldosterone - the opposite of the liberal sodium intake required.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-q5`,stem:`Which finding during a 75 g OGTT supports a diagnosis of acromegaly in a patient with a raised IGF-1?`,options:[`GH nadir of 0.2 microgram/L`,`Glucose at 2 hours of 150 mg/dL`,`GH failing to fall below 1 microgram/L`,`Insulin level rising fivefold`],answer:2,explanation:`Glucose normally suppresses GH via somatostatin; failure of GH to fall below 1 microgram/L (below 0.4 with ultrasensitive assays) confirms autonomous secretion in acromegaly. A nadir of 0.2 microgram/L is normal suppression; the 2-hour glucose shows impaired tolerance, common in acromegaly but not diagnostic; insulin rise is not a criterion.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`What are dynamic endocrine function tests? Describe the procedure, interpretation and pitfalls of the short Synacthen test, the dexamethasone suppression tests and the water deprivation test.`,openingLines:[`Dynamic endocrine tests assess a hormonal axis by its response to a stimulus or suppressant, because basal hormone levels fluctuate.`,`The principle is to stimulate a gland suspected to be deficient and to suppress a gland suspected to be overactive, using standard protocols and assay-specific cut-offs.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Principles and classification`,points:[`Stimulation vs suppression vs provocation tests; biochemistry before imaging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`2. Short Synacthen test`,points:[`Tetracosactide 250 microgram IV/IM, cortisol 0, 30, 60 min; peak 500 nmol/L classic, about 420-450 with newer assays. [ESE/ES 2024]`,`Pitfalls: oral oestrogen, recent pituitary insult, steroid cross-reactivity, low CBG. [Endocrine Society 2016]`]},{heading:`3. Dexamethasone suppression tests`,points:[`1 mg overnight: cortisol under 50 nmol/L normal; LDDST 0.5 mg 6-hourly 48 h; HDDST 2 mg 6-hourly - over 50% suppression favours Cushing disease. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`Pitfalls: enzyme inducers, oestrogen, pseudo-Cushing, non-adherence; MACS in incidentaloma. [ESE/ENSAT 2023]`]},{heading:`4. Water deprivation test`,points:[`Protocol with stopping rules (3% weight loss, sodium over 145-150); desmopressin 2 microgram. [Endocrine Society 2022]`,`Interpretation table; copeptin tests as preferred alternatives. [Fenske NEJM 2018]`]},{heading:`5. Safety and general pitfalls`,points:[`Drug history, biotin, timing, supervision, emergency drugs. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: test, protocol, normal response, abnormal response`,`Water deprivation interpretation table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and principles`,marks:1.5},{part:`Short Synacthen test`,marks:2.5},{part:`Dexamethasone suppression tests`,marks:3},{part:`Water deprivation test`,marks:2.5},{part:`Pitfalls and safety`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`Synacthen`,`dexamethasone suppression`,`water deprivation`,`copeptin`,`MACS`,`assay cut-off`]},{id:`endocrine-dynamic-endocrine-tests-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the insulin tolerance test.`,openingLines:[`The insulin tolerance test uses insulin-induced hypoglycaemia as a central stress to test the whole hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal and GH axes.`,`It is the gold standard for both, but needs strict supervision and has firm contraindications.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Indications and contraindications`,points:[`Suspected secondary AI or adult GHD; contraindicated in IHD, abnormal ECG, epilepsy, elderly, cortisol under 100 nmol/L, untreated hypothyroidism. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`2. Procedure`,points:[`Fasting, cannula, doctor present; soluble insulin 0.1-0.15 U/kg IV; samples 0-120 min; target glucose under 40 mg/dL with symptoms. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`3. Interpretation`,points:[`Peak cortisol about 500 nmol/L or more (assay-dependent) normal; peak GH under 3 microgram/L = adult GHD. [AACE 2019]`]},{heading:`4. Safety`,points:[`Dextrose for severe neuroglycopenia, continue sampling; meal after; alternatives glucagon test or SST. [AACE 2019]`]}],mustDraw:[`Timeline of sampling with glucose curve`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications and contraindications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Procedure`,marks:1.5},{part:`Interpretation`,marks:1},{part:`Safety and alternatives`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ITT`,`hypoglycaemia`,`GH deficiency`,`cortisol peak`,`glucagon test`]}],references:[`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024 - Endocrine testing`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Bornstein SR et al. Diagnosis and treatment of primary adrenal insufficiency: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2016`,`Nieman LK et al. The diagnosis of Cushing's syndrome: Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline, 2008`,`Fassnacht M et al. ESE clinical practice guideline on the management of adrenal incidentalomas, in collaboration with ENSAT, 2023 update`,`Beuschlein F et al. ESE/Endocrine Society guideline on glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency, 2024`,`Fenske W et al. A copeptin-based approach in the diagnosis of diabetes insipidus - NEJM, 2018; Arima H et al. Changing the name of diabetes insipidus, 2022`,`Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline on Primary Aldosteronism, 2025; Yuen KCJ et al. AACE Clinical Practice Guidelines for Growth Hormone Deficiency in Adults, 2019`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes`,title:`Fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes and pancreatogenic (type 3c) diabetes`,oneLiner:`**Pancreatogenic (type 3c) diabetes** is diabetes caused by disease of the exocrine pancreas - chronic or acute pancreatitis, pancreatectomy, pancreatic cancer, cystic fibrosis or haemochromatosis - with loss of insulin **and glucagon and pancreatic polypeptide**; **fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes (FCPD)** is its tropical Indian form: diabetes in a **young, lean person with tropical calcific pancreatitis (pain, calculi, steatorrhoea)** who needs insulin yet rarely develops ketosis.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`FCPD`,`type 3c diabetes`,`T3cDM`,`pancreatogenic diabetes`,`tropical calcific pancreatitis`,`TCP`,`tropical chronic pancreatitis`,`post-pancreatitis diabetes mellitus`,`PPDM-A`,`malnutrition-related diabetes mellitus`,`MRDM`,`SPINK1`,`pancreatic calculi`,`pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy`,`PERT`,`faecal elastase`,`pancreatic cancer-related diabetes`,`ENDPAC`,`cystic fibrosis-related diabetes`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Type 3c (pancreatogenic) diabetes** is diabetes secondary to disease of the exocrine pancreas; the ADA classifies it under **'specific types - diseases of the exocrine pancreas'**. [ADA 2026]`,`In Western series type 3c accounts for about **5-10% of adult diabetes**, and in one UK primary-care cohort about **88% were misclassified as type 2**. [Woodmansey, Diabetes Care 2017]`,`**Chronic pancreatitis** is its commonest cause (about 75-80%), followed by pancreatic cancer, acute pancreatitis, pancreatectomy, cystic fibrosis and haemochromatosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**FCPD** is the diabetic stage of **tropical calcific pancreatitis (TCP)**, an idiopathic, non-alcoholic chronic pancreatitis of the young described mainly from **Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha**, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka and tropical Africa. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`FCPD now forms **under 1% of diabetes in India** and is declining as nutrition improves; most Indian chronic pancreatitis today is idiopathic, with alcohol rising in men. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**WHO 1985** called it **malnutrition-related diabetes mellitus (MRDM)** with two subtypes - FCPD and protein-deficient pancreatic diabetes; **WHO 1999** dropped MRDM and moved FCPD to diseases of the exocrine pancreas. [WHO 1999]`,`After a first attack of **acute pancreatitis**, about **15-20% develop prediabetes or diabetes within 3-5 years** - a group called **post-pancreatitis diabetes mellitus (PPDM-A)**. [Das, Gut 2014]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Genetic**: **SPINK1 N34S** mutation is found in about **30-50% of Indian TCP**; CTRC, CFTR and CEL variants also contribute, and PRSS1 causes hereditary pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cassava (tapioca) cyanogen toxicity** was the classic hypothesis for Kerala TCP but is now considered **unproven** - TCP occurs in areas where cassava is not eaten. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Oxidative stress and micronutrient deficiency** (low selenium, zinc, vitamin C, carotenoids) in childhood malnutrition are proposed co-factors. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Other type 3c causes: **alcoholic chronic pancreatitis**, recurrent acute pancreatitis, **pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma**, **distal or total pancreatectomy**, cystic fibrosis, **haemochromatosis** and thalassaemia iron overload, trauma. [ADA 2026]`,`Risk of diabetes in chronic pancreatitis rises with **duration**, **calcification**, **distal pancreatectomy**, smoking and early onset; cumulative risk reaches **about 80% at 25 years**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Risk after acute pancreatitis is higher after **necrotising pancreatitis**, recurrent attacks, alcohol aetiology and obesity, but even mild attacks raise it. [Das, Gut 2014]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the phenotype differs from type 1 and type 2`,points:[`Fibrosis and calculi destroy **acinar tissue first and islets later**, so exocrine insufficiency (steatorrhoea) usually precedes diabetes by years. [Harrison 22e]`,`Loss of **beta cells** gives insulin deficiency, but loss of **alpha cells** gives **glucagon deficiency** - hence **brittle control and severe, prolonged hypoglycaemia** with insulin. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Ketosis resistance** in FCPD is explained by **residual C-peptide secretion**, low glucagon (less ketogenesis), **reduced fat stores** and possibly low carnitine. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Loss of **pancreatic polypeptide (PP)** causes **hepatic insulin resistance** (fewer hepatic insulin receptors), so fasting hyperglycaemia is prominent. [Ewald and Bretzel 2013]`,`Maldigestion of fat reduces **incretin (GLP-1, GIP) secretion**, and **pancreatic enzyme replacement partly restores** the incretin response. [Ewald and Bretzel 2013]`,`In **pancreatic cancer-related diabetes**, tumour-derived factors (adrenomedullin, exosomes) cause beta-cell dysfunction and **paraneoplastic insulin resistance** often before the tumour is visible. [Harrison 22e]`,`Peripheral insulin sensitivity is usually **normal or increased**, which is why type 3c patients are often lean and need modest insulin doses. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Mohan criteria for FCPD**: (1) residence in a tropical country, (2) diabetes by WHO criteria, (3) evidence of chronic pancreatitis - **pancreatic calculi** on imaging or at least 3 of abnormal morphology, recurrent abdominal pain from childhood, steatorrhoea, abnormal pancreatic function test, (4) **no other cause** (alcohol, gallstones, hyperparathyroidism). [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Ewald-Bretzel major criteria for type 3c**: **exocrine pancreatic insufficiency** (faecal elastase-1 or direct function test), **pathological pancreatic imaging** (US, CT, MRI, EUS) and **absence of type 1 autoantibodies** - all three required. [Ewald and Bretzel 2013]`,`**Ewald-Bretzel minor criteria**: absent PP response to a mixed meal, impaired incretin secretion, no excessive insulin resistance (normal HOMA-IR), impaired beta-cell function, low fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K). [Ewald and Bretzel 2013]`,`Natural history of TCP in three stages: **pain (childhood) - calculi and steatorrhoea - diabetes**, diabetes usually appearing about a decade after pain onset. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**PPDM-A** (after acute pancreatitis) needs diabetes diagnosed **at least 90 days after the attack**, with no diabetes before - glucose during the attack is stress hyperglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic cancer-related diabetes (PCRD)** is new-onset diabetes within about **3 years before** a cancer diagnosis, characteristically with **weight loss**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Typical FCPD patient: **under 30 years at diabetes onset**, **lean (BMI often under 18.5)**, from a poor rural background, with **recurrent upper abdominal pain since childhood**. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Steatorrhoea** - bulky, pale, oily, foul-smelling stools that float and are hard to flush - may be masked by low fat intake; ask specifically. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pain often **lessens as the gland burns out**, so many FCPD patients present with diabetes alone and calculi are found on an X-ray. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Presentation with **marked hyperglycaemia (often over 300 mg/dL) without ketonuria** in a young lean person should prompt a plain abdominal film. [RSSDI 2022]`,`History in an adult type 3c: alcohol, smoking, previous **acute pancreatitis admissions**, gallstones, pancreatic surgery, family history of pancreatitis or cancer. [ADA 2026]`,`New diabetes after 50 with **weight loss, anorexia, back pain or jaundice** is pancreatic cancer until proven otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **recurrent hypoglycaemia** on modest insulin doses - a clue to glucagon deficiency and malabsorption. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Emaciation**, muscle wasting and signs of malnutrition (angular stomatitis, pallor, hair changes) are common in FCPD. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Classic but non-specific descriptions: **bilateral parotid enlargement**, a **cyanotic hue of the lips** and a pot belly. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Signs of **fat-soluble vitamin deficiency**: night blindness (A), bone tenderness and proximal myopathy (D), easy bruising (K). [Harrison 22e]`,`Epigastric tenderness or a palpable mass suggests a pseudocyst or cancer; **jaundice** suggests biliary stricture or a head-of-pancreas tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for complications at diagnosis: **retinopathy and neuropathy** occur at rates similar to type 2 diabetes in FCPD. [RSSDI 2022]`,`In haemochromatosis-related diabetes: **bronze skin, hepatomegaly, hypogonadism, arthropathy** of the 2nd-3rd MCP joints. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Type 1 diabetes**: also young and lean but **ketosis-prone**, GAD/IA-2/ZnT8 antibodies positive, no calculi, C-peptide very low. [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 2 diabetes**: overweight, acanthosis, family history, insulin resistance; no steatorrhoea or calculi - but type 2 can coexist with chronic pancreatitis. [ADA 2026]`,`**MODY**: autosomal dominant three-generation family history, antibody negative, preserved C-peptide, no pancreatic disease (HNF1B-MODY can show pancreatic atrophy). [ADA 2026]`,`**Ketosis-prone (Flatbush) diabetes**: DKA at onset in an obese adult, later insulin-independent; no pancreatic disease. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Alcoholic chronic pancreatitis**: older man, heavy alcohol, smaller and more peripheral calculi; FCPD calculi are **large, dense and intraductal**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Pancreatic adenocarcinoma** must be excluded in any new diabetes after 50 with weight loss, and in a TCP patient whose pain or control suddenly worsens. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Plain X-ray abdomen (AP)**: discrete **calculi to the right of L1-L2** along the pancreatic duct - the cheapest district-hospital test for FCPD. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Ultrasound** shows ductal dilatation, calculi and atrophy; **CT** is the best test for calcification and to exclude a mass; **MRCP** maps the duct; **EUS** is the most sensitive for early disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Faecal elastase-1**: **under 200 microg/g** suggests exocrine insufficiency and **under 100 microg/g** severe insufficiency; watery stool falsely lowers it. [HaPanEU 2017]`,`**Fasting C-peptide** (often 0.2-0.6 nmol/L, residual) and negative **GAD antibodies** separate FCPD from type 1. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**HbA1c** can mislead in anaemia, iron deficiency or after transfusion; pair with fasting glucose and, where possible, CGM. [ADA 2026]`,`Nutritional panel: **25-OH vitamin D**, vitamin B12, albumin, prealbumin, magnesium, haemoglobin and a **DEXA scan** (osteoporosis common). [ESPEN 2024]`,`Cancer work-up when suspected: **contrast CT pancreas protocol** or EUS-guided biopsy; **CA 19-9** supports but does not diagnose (false positives with cholestasis, false negatives in Lewis-negative people). [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Stop alcohol and smoking** completely - both accelerate fibrosis, diabetes and cancer risk. [HaPanEU 2017]`,`Diet: **do not restrict fat** in exocrine insufficiency; give small frequent meals with normal fat content plus enzymes, and adequate calories to regain weight. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**Metformin** is first line in mild type 3c with preserved weight (start 500 mg once daily with food, up to 1 g twice daily if tolerated); it may lower pancreatic cancer risk. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`Most FCPD patients need **insulin** at presentation; usual requirement is **about 0.5-1 unit/kg/day**, often as basal-bolus or premixed twice daily. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Start insulin low and titrate cautiously because of **glucagon deficiency and erratic absorption** - teach hypoglycaemia recognition and give glucose, as glucagon rescue may be less effective. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`**Pancreatic enzyme replacement (PERT)**: **25,000-50,000 units lipase with each main meal and half with snacks**, taken with the first bite; increase stepwise to a maximum of about **10,000 units lipase/kg/day**. [HaPanEU 2017]`,`If steatorrhoea persists on adequate PERT, add a **PPI** (for example pantoprazole 40 mg once daily) to protect enzymes from acid, then look for SIBO. [HaPanEU 2017]`,`Supplement **vitamin D** (for example cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks then monthly) and calcium if deficient. [ESPEN 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`Admit for **severe hypoglycaemia with neuroglycopenia**, uncontrolled hyperglycaemia with vomiting, intractable pain, jaundice or GI bleeding. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`Treat **hypoglycaemia** with **25% dextrose 100 mL IV** (or 50 mL of 50%) then a 10% dextrose infusion, and reduce insulin by 10-20%. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`DKA is uncommon but occurs, especially with infection; manage with standard **fixed-rate IV insulin 0.1 unit/kg/hour** - see the DKA and HHS topic. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Pain of chronic pancreatitis**: paracetamol and NSAIDs, then tramadol; for large ductal stones, **ESWL with ERCP** (pioneered at AIG Hyderabad) or surgery (**Frey procedure, lateral pancreaticojejunostomy**). [ESGE 2018]`,`After **total pancreatectomy**, diabetes is absolute and brittle; patients need basal-bolus insulin (often under 0.5 unit/kg/day), full-dose PERT, and ideally **CGM**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Total pancreatectomy with islet autotransplantation (TPIAT)** is offered in specialised centres for intractable pain to preserve some beta-cell mass. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: insulin only; continue PERT (not absorbed systemically, safe) and correct fat-soluble vitamins. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Children and adolescents** with TCP: growth failure and pubertal delay - monitor growth charts and optimise calories and PERT. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**Incretin drugs**: **GLP-1 RA and DPP-4 inhibitors** are generally avoided in pancreatitis because of labelled pancreatitis warnings and GI side effects that worsen weight loss. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** carry a higher risk of **euglycaemic DKA** in insulin-deficient type 3c, and cause unwanted weight loss - use only with caution. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`**Sulfonylureas** can work while C-peptide is preserved but add hypoglycaemia risk; thiazolidinediones are seldom useful because insulin resistance is not the main defect. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Cystic fibrosis-related diabetes**: screen annually from age 10 with an OGTT; treat with insulin. [ADA 2026]`,`Resource-limited setting: **human premixed insulin 30/70** twice daily from NP-NCD/Jan Aushadhi, plus generic pancreatin capsules, is a workable regimen. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Microvascular complications** (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy) occur in FCPD at rates **similar to type 2 diabetes**; macrovascular disease is less common because patients are young and lean. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Pancreatic cancer**: the risk in TCP/FCPD is up to **100-fold** that of the general population and in chronic pancreatitis overall about **13-fold**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** is a leading cause of morbidity and death, due to glucagon deficiency, malabsorption and alcohol. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`Malnutrition complications: **osteoporosis and fractures**, sarcopenia, night blindness, infections. [ESPEN 2024]`,`Local complications: pseudocyst, **biliary or duodenal stricture**, splenic vein thrombosis with gastric varices, pancreatic ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`Survival in FCPD has improved greatly with insulin; leading causes of death now are **diabetic nephropathy, pancreatic cancer and infections**. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer every suspected FCPD/type 3c to a **diabetologist and gastroenterologist** for confirmation, endoscopic therapy and cancer surveillance planning. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Screen people with **chronic pancreatitis annually** with **FPG and HbA1c**, and after acute pancreatitis at **3 months, then yearly** for at least 5 years. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`Monitor **HbA1c every 3 months** with SMBG or CGM; target HbA1c **under 7%** but relax to under 7.5-8% if hypoglycaemia is frequent. [ADA 2026]`,`Yearly: weight and BMI, stool frequency on PERT, **vitamin D, fat-soluble vitamins**, eye, feet, urine ACR; DEXA every 2 years if osteopenic. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**Cancer surveillance**: no proven screening programme for TCP, so keep a **low threshold for CT or EUS** with new weight loss, change in pain, jaundice or sudden loss of glycaemic control. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hereditary pancreatitis (PRSS1) carriers: CAPS consortium advises surveillance with **EUS or MRI from age 40**. [CAPS 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Counsel that diabetes is **due to pancreatic damage**, will need lifelong insulin in most, and that enzymes must be taken **with every meal**. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Total **abstinence from alcohol and tobacco**; refer to tobacco cessation (National Tobacco Control Programme quitline **1800-11-2356**). [MoHFW]`,`Insulin, glucometers and screening are delivered through **NP-NCD** (National Programme for Prevention and Control of NCDs) at Ayushman Arogya Mandir and district NCD clinics. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Hospital costs of ESWL, ERCP and pancreatic surgery are covered under **AB PM-JAY** packages. [NHA]`,`Childhood nutrition improvement (ICDS, PM POSHAN) is thought to be part of why TCP is declining. [RSSDI 2022]`,`Teach **sick-day rules** and safe injection storage; FCPD patients are often poor and rural, so give a simple fixed regimen they can afford. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2025-2026** keep pancreatic diabetes under specific types and advise considering it in anyone with a history of pancreatitis, pancreatic surgery or cystic fibrosis. [ADA 2026]`,`**ESPEN 2024 practical guideline** on nutrition in pancreatitis: no fat restriction, PERT with meals, screen for and treat **sarcopenia and osteoporosis**. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**PancreasFest 2021 consensus**: annual diabetes screening in chronic pancreatitis, **metformin first line** when insulin is not needed, caution with incretins and SGLT2 inhibitors, insulin for significant hyperglycaemia. [PancreasFest 2021]`,`**ADA 2026** supports **CGM** for people on insulin from diagnosis - especially valuable in brittle type 3c with glucagon deficiency. [ADA 2026]`,`Growing evidence (2023-2025 cohorts) that **new-onset diabetes after 50 with weight loss** warrants pancreatic imaging; scores such as **ENDPAC** are being validated but are not yet guideline standard. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: 'Young, lean, diabetic, abdominal pain, X-ray shows calculi' - answer **FCPD**, and name the **Mohan criteria**.`,`Remember the triad of TCP: **pain, calculi, diabetes** - in that order over time.`,`Why ketosis-resistant? **Residual C-peptide, low glucagon, low fat stores** - examiners love this.`,`Why brittle with hypoglycaemia? **Glucagon deficiency** plus malabsorption.`,`Common mistake: **restricting dietary fat** - correct practice is normal fat with adequate PERT.`,`Common mistake: labelling type 3c as type 2 and starting a **DPP-4 inhibitor or GLP-1 RA** in someone with pancreatitis.`,`Always mention **pancreatic cancer surveillance** - up to 100-fold risk in TCP.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - FCPD and type 3c diabetes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`500 mg oral once daily with food`,`Increase by 500 mg every 1-2 weeks to 1 g twice daily`,`GI upset worsens weight loss; avoid eGFR under 30, alcohol excess`],[`Human premixed insulin 30/70`,`About 0.5-1 unit/kg/day SC, two-thirds before breakfast and one-third before dinner`,`Adjust 2 units every 3 days to FPG 80-130 mg/dL`,`Hypoglycaemia (glucagon deficiency) - start low`],[`Basal-bolus (glargine plus regular or rapid-acting)`,`Glargine 0.2 unit/kg SC bedtime plus prandial insulin with meals`,`Titrate basal to fasting, bolus to post-meal values`,`Hypoglycaemia; needs SMBG or CGM`],[`Pancreatin (PERT) capsules`,`25,000-50,000 units lipase oral with each main meal; 10,000-25,000 with snacks`,`Lifelong; maximum about 10,000 units lipase/kg/day`,`Do not crush; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses; take with first bite`],[`Pantoprazole (adjunct)`,`40 mg oral once daily before breakfast`,`When steatorrhoea persists on adequate PERT`,`Long-term: hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency, C. difficile`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU oral weekly for 8 weeks`,`Then 60,000 IU monthly; recheck 25-OH D at 3 months`,`Hypercalcaemia if overdosed`],[`Tramadol (pain)`,`50-100 mg oral every 6-8 hours`,`Short courses; reassess for endotherapy`,`Seizures, serotonin syndrome, dependence`]]},{heading:`FCPD vs type 1 vs type 2 vs MODY`,columns:[`Feature`,`FCPD`,`Type 1`,`Type 2`,`MODY`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`10-30 years`,`Childhood-young adult`,`Usually over 30 (Indians earlier)`,`Under 25-35`],[`Body build`,`Very lean, malnourished`,`Lean`,`Overweight, central obesity`,`Usually normal weight`],[`Ketosis`,`Rare (ketosis-resistant)`,`Prone`,`Uncommon`,`Rare`],[`Abdominal pain, steatorrhoea`,`Yes`,`No`,`No`,`No`],[`Pancreatic calculi`,`Yes - large intraductal`,`No`,`No`,`No`],[`Islet antibodies`,`Negative`,`Positive`,`Negative`,`Negative`],[`C-peptide`,`Low but detectable`,`Very low or absent`,`Normal or high`,`Preserved`],[`Treatment`,`Insulin plus PERT`,`Insulin`,`Lifestyle, metformin, others`,`Sulfonylurea (HNF1A/4A) or none (GCK)`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria - FCPD (Mohan) and type 3c (Ewald-Bretzel)`,columns:[`Set`,`Criteria`,`Rule`],rows:[[`Mohan criteria (FCPD)`,`Tropical country; diabetes by WHO criteria; pancreatic calculi or 3 of: abnormal morphology, childhood pain, steatorrhoea, abnormal pancreatic function; no other cause of pancreatitis`,`All four needed`],[`Ewald-Bretzel major (type 3c)`,`Exocrine insufficiency (FE-1 or direct test); pathological pancreatic imaging; no type 1 autoantibodies`,`All three needed`],[`Ewald-Bretzel minor`,`Absent PP response; impaired incretin secretion; no excess insulin resistance; impaired beta-cell function; low fat-soluble vitamins`,`Supportive`],[`PPDM-A`,`No diabetes before; diabetes diagnosed at least 90 days after acute pancreatitis`,`Exclude stress hyperglycaemia`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding / cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Plain X-ray abdomen`,`Calculi right of L1-L2`,`Chronic calcific pancreatitis - FCPD`],[`Faecal elastase-1`,`Under 200 microg/g; under 100 severe`,`Exocrine insufficiency`],[`Fasting C-peptide`,`Low but detectable`,`Residual secretion - explains ketosis resistance`],[`GAD / IA-2 antibodies`,`Negative`,`Against type 1`],[`CT pancreas (pancreas protocol)`,`Calcification, ductal dilatation, atrophy or mass`,`Confirms CP; excludes cancer`],[`EUS`,`Rosemont criteria features`,`Earliest changes; biopsy if mass`],[`CA 19-9`,`Raised`,`Supports cancer; false positives with cholestasis`],[`25-OH vitamin D`,`Under 20 ng/mL`,`Deficiency - treat; check bone density`]]},{heading:`Causes of type 3c diabetes`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Specific point`],rows:[[`Chronic pancreatitis (incl. TCP)`,`Pain, calculi, steatorrhoea`,`Commonest cause; cancer surveillance`],[`Acute pancreatitis (PPDM-A)`,`Previous attack`,`Screen at 3 months then yearly`],[`Pancreatic adenocarcinoma`,`New diabetes over 50 with weight loss`,`Image the pancreas`],[`Pancreatectomy`,`Surgical history`,`Total pancreatectomy - absolute insulin deficiency`],[`Cystic fibrosis`,`Lung disease, young`,`Annual OGTT from 10 years`],[`Haemochromatosis / thalassaemia`,`Bronze skin, transfusions`,`Ferritin, transferrin saturation; chelation`]]}],redFlags:[`New diabetes after 50 with unexplained weight loss, back pain or jaundice - image the pancreas urgently`,`Recurrent severe or nocturnal hypoglycaemia on insulin`,`Sudden loss of glycaemic control or new weight loss in a known TCP patient - exclude pancreatic cancer`,`Obstructive jaundice or a palpable epigastric mass`,`Haematemesis or melaena (splenic vein thrombosis, pseudoaneurysm)`,`Persistent vomiting with abdominal pain - acute-on-chronic pancreatitis or gastric outlet obstruction`,`Severe malnutrition (BMI under 16) or vertebral fracture`],pearls:[`**Pain, calculi, diabetes** - the three stages of tropical calcific pancreatitis.`,`FCPD is **insulin-requiring but ketosis-resistant** - residual C-peptide, low glucagon, low fat stores.`,`Glucagon deficiency makes type 3c **brittle** - start insulin low, teach hypoglycaemia care.`,`FCPD calculi are **large, dense and intraductal**; alcoholic calculi smaller and peripheral.`,`**Do not restrict fat** - give PERT with the first bite of every meal.`,`PERT: **25,000-50,000 units lipase per main meal**, half for snacks.`,`New diabetes over 50 plus weight loss = **think pancreatic cancer**.`,`WHO 1985 MRDM (FCPD and PDPD) was **abolished in 1999** - FCPD is now 'disease of exocrine pancreas'.`,`Avoid **DPP-4 inhibitors and GLP-1 RA** in a patient with pancreatitis.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old lean man from Kerala has had recurrent epigastric pain since childhood and bulky oily stools. Fasting glucose is 310 mg/dL, urine ketones are negative and a plain abdominal X-ray shows dense calculi to the right of L1-L2. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Type 1 diabetes with coincident gallstones`,`Fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes`,`Maturity-onset diabetes of the young`,`Ketosis-prone type 2 diabetes`],answer:1,explanation:`A young lean patient from the tropics with childhood pancreatic pain, steatorrhoea, marked hyperglycaemia without ketosis and intraductal pancreatic calculi meets the Mohan criteria for FCPD. Type 1 diabetes would be ketosis-prone and gallstones lie in the right upper quadrant, not along the pancreatic duct; MODY has no pancreatic disease; ketosis-prone diabetes presents with DKA in an obese adult.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-q2`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains the frequent severe hypoglycaemia seen in patients with type 3c diabetes on insulin?`,options:[`Excess pancreatic polypeptide secretion`,`Increased hepatic insulin receptors`,`Loss of alpha-cell glucagon secretion`,`High insulin antibody titres`],answer:2,explanation:`Pancreatic destruction removes alpha cells as well as beta cells, so counter-regulatory glucagon is lost and hypoglycaemia is severe and prolonged. Pancreatic polypeptide is deficient, not in excess, and its loss reduces hepatic insulin receptors, causing hepatic insulin resistance rather than hypoglycaemia; insulin antibodies are not a feature.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-q3`,stem:`A patient with chronic pancreatitis and diabetes has persistent steatorrhoea and weight loss. What is the correct dietary and enzyme advice?`,options:[`Restrict dietary fat to under 20 g/day and avoid enzymes until pain settles`,`Take pancreatic enzymes once daily at bedtime`,`Use a GLP-1 receptor agonist to slow gastric emptying`,`Normal fat diet with 25,000-50,000 units lipase taken with each main meal`],answer:3,explanation:`Current ESPEN and HaPanEU guidance is a normal-fat diet with pancreatic enzyme replacement of 25,000-50,000 units lipase taken with the first bite of each main meal, increased as needed. Fat restriction worsens malnutrition and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency; bedtime enzymes miss the meal; GLP-1 agonists worsen weight loss and carry a pancreatitis warning.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-q4`,stem:`In the WHO classification of 1985, fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes was placed under which category?`,options:[`Malnutrition-related diabetes mellitus`,`Gestational diabetes`,`Insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus`,`Impaired glucose tolerance`],answer:0,explanation:`WHO 1985 created malnutrition-related diabetes mellitus with two subtypes, FCPD and protein-deficient pancreatic diabetes. WHO 1999 abolished MRDM and moved FCPD to diseases of the exocrine pancreas. It was never classed as insulin-dependent (type 1) diabetes despite needing insulin, nor as IGT or gestational diabetes.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-q5`,stem:`A 62-year-old non-smoker develops new diabetes (HbA1c 8.4%) and has lost 7 kg in 4 months without trying. He has no family history of diabetes. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Start metformin and review in 3 months`,`Contrast-enhanced pancreas-protocol CT`,`Check GAD antibodies to diagnose LADA`,`Start a sulfonylurea because of weight loss`],answer:1,explanation:`New-onset diabetes after 50 with unexplained weight loss is a recognised early sign of pancreatic adenocarcinoma, so pancreatic imaging with pancreas-protocol CT is the priority. Starting metformin alone delays a possible cancer diagnosis; GAD antibodies may be checked later but LADA does not explain this risk profile; a sulfonylurea does nothing to find the cause.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define type 3c (pancreatogenic) diabetes. Describe the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes.`,openingLines:[`Type 3c diabetes is diabetes secondary to disease of the exocrine pancreas, with loss of insulin, glucagon and pancreatic polypeptide.`,`Fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes is its tropical form - diabetes in a young lean person with tropical calcific pancreatitis who needs insulin yet resists ketosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`ADA: specific type - diseases of the exocrine pancreas; causes chronic/acute pancreatitis, cancer, pancreatectomy, CF, haemochromatosis.`,`WHO 1985 MRDM (FCPD, PDPD) abolished in 1999.`,`Type 3c is 5-10% of adult diabetes in the West; FCPD under 1% of diabetes in India, mainly Kerala and Tamil Nadu.`]},{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis`,points:[`SPINK1 N34S in 30-50%; CTRC, CFTR variants; oxidative stress and micronutrient deficiency; cassava hypothesis unproven.`,`Acinar loss first (steatorrhoea), islet loss later.`,`Beta-cell loss - insulin deficiency; alpha-cell loss - glucagon deficiency and hypoglycaemia; PP loss - hepatic insulin resistance.`,`Ketosis resistance: residual C-peptide, low glucagon, low fat stores.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Young (under 30), lean, poor background; childhood abdominal pain; steatorrhoea; marked hyperglycaemia without ketosis.`,`Signs: emaciation, parotid enlargement, vitamin deficiency signs.`,`Stages: pain, calculi, diabetes.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Mohan criteria: tropical country, WHO diabetes, calculi or 3 features of CP, no other cause.`,`X-ray abdomen for calculi; USG, CT, MRCP, EUS; faecal elastase under 200 microg/g; C-peptide, GAD antibodies negative.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Insulin 0.5-1 unit/kg/day (premixed or basal-bolus), start low; metformin in mild cases.`,`PERT 25,000-50,000 units lipase per main meal, normal-fat diet; PPI adjunct; vitamin D, calcium.`,`Pain: analgesics, ESWL plus ERCP, Frey or lateral pancreaticojejunostomy.`,`Avoid incretins; caution with SGLT2 inhibitors.`]},{heading:`Complications and follow-up`,points:[`Microvascular complications like type 2; pancreatic cancer risk up to 100-fold; hypoglycaemia; osteoporosis.`,`3-monthly HbA1c, annual eye, kidney, feet, vitamin D; low threshold for CT/EUS with weight loss or change in pain.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: pain to calculi to diabetes natural history`,`Table comparing FCPD, type 1 and type 2 diabetes`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Aetiopathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:3}],keywords:[`FCPD`,`type 3c`,`Mohan criteria`,`PERT`,`ketosis resistance`,`SPINK1`]},{id:`endocrine-pancreatogenic-diabetes-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes from type 1 diabetes.`,openingLines:[`Both present in young lean people needing insulin, but FCPD is secondary to tropical calcific pancreatitis while type 1 is autoimmune beta-cell destruction.`,`The key discriminators are ketosis, islet antibodies, pancreatic calculi and steatorrhoea.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and pathogenesis`,points:[`FCPD: chronic pancreatitis (SPINK1, malnutrition); type 1: T-cell autoimmunity, HLA-DR3/DR4.`,`FCPD loses alpha and beta cells; type 1 loses beta cells only.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`FCPD: abdominal pain, steatorrhoea, ketosis-resistant, brittle hypoglycaemia; type 1: osmotic symptoms, DKA-prone.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`FCPD: calculi on X-ray, low faecal elastase, C-peptide low but detectable, antibodies negative.`,`Type 1: no calculi, antibodies (GAD, IA-2, ZnT8) positive, C-peptide absent.`]},{heading:`Treatment and prognosis`,points:[`FCPD: insulin plus PERT, cancer surveillance; type 1: intensive insulin, screen for other autoimmunity.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`FCPD`,`type 1 diabetes`,`ketosis`,`calculi`,`GAD antibodies`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on diabetes classification and chronic pancreatitis`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Section 2: Diagnosis and Classification`,`RSSDI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the Management of Type 2 Diabetes, 2022 (FCPD section)`,`Ewald N, Bretzel RG. Diabetes mellitus secondary to pancreatic diseases (type 3c). Eur J Intern Med 2013`,`Hart PA et al. PancreasFest 2021 consensus: diagnosis and management of diabetes in chronic pancreatitis`,`HaPanEU/UEG evidence-based guidelines for chronic pancreatitis, 2017; ESPEN practical guideline on clinical nutrition in acute and chronic pancreatitis, 2024`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, 2023 - diabetes and hypoglycaemia`]}),d.push({id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth`,title:`Short stature and growth disorders`,oneLiner:`**Short stature** is height **below -2 SD (about the 3rd centile)** for age and sex on the appropriate chart (**WHO 2006 under 5 years, IAP 2015 from 5-18 years**), or height well below the **mid-parental target range**, or a **subnormal growth velocity crossing centiles**; most short children are **normal variants (familial or constitutional delay)**, and the pathological ones are found by **growth velocity, proportions and bone age**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`short stature`,`growth failure`,`stunting`,`IAP 2015 growth charts`,`WHO growth standards`,`mid-parental height`,`target height`,`growth velocity`,`bone age`,`Greulich-Pyle`,`constitutional delay of growth and puberty`,`CDGP`,`familial short stature`,`growth hormone deficiency`,`Turner syndrome`,`IGF-1`,`somatropin`,`somapacitan`,`SGA`,`upper segment lower segment ratio`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Short stature**: height **below -2 SD** (roughly below the 3rd centile) for age and sex; **severe short stature** is below -3 SD. [Harrison 22e]`,`Also pathological: height **more than 2 SD below the mid-parental target**, or **growth velocity below the 25th centile** for 6-12 months, or **crossing two major centile lines** after age 2 years. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`By definition about **2.3% of normal children** fall below -2 SD, so most short children are **normal variants**, not disease. [Nelson 22e]`,`India: **stunting in 35.5% of under-5 children** (NFHS-5, 2019-21) - chronic undernutrition is the commonest pathological cause of short stature in India. [NFHS-5]`,`In Indian paediatric endocrine clinic series the commonest causes are **constitutional delay, familial short stature, undernutrition, hypothyroidism, GH deficiency, celiac disease and Turner syndrome**. [IAP 2015]`,`Use **WHO 2006 standards under 5 years** and **IAP 2015 revised charts from 5-18 years** for Indian children; CDC or UK charts mislabel Indian children. [IAP 2015]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - causes`,points:[`**Normal variants (commonest)**: familial (genetic) short stature, **constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP)**, or both together. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Nutritional and chronic systemic disease**: protein-energy malnutrition, **celiac disease**, IBD, CKD, renal tubular acidosis, chronic liver disease, cyanotic heart disease, thalassaemia, TB, HIV, poorly controlled type 1 diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endocrine**: **hypothyroidism**, **GH deficiency** (isolated, or multiple pituitary hormone deficiency from craniopharyngioma or septo-optic dysplasia), **Cushing syndrome** including iatrogenic steroids, precocious puberty (tall child, short adult), pseudohypoparathyroidism, rickets. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Genetic syndromes**: **Turner (45,X)**, Noonan (PTPN11), Down, Prader-Willi, Russell-Silver, SHOX deficiency (Leri-Weill), 22q11 deletion. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Skeletal dysplasias** (disproportionate): **achondroplasia** (FGFR3), hypochondroplasia, spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, mucopolysaccharidoses. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Small for gestational age (SGA)**: about **10% do not catch up by 2-4 years** and stay short. [Clayton, JCEM 2007 consensus]`,`**Psychosocial deprivation** (psychosocial dwarfism) causes reversible functional GH deficiency. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Physiology of growth and pathophysiology`,points:[`Growth phases follow the **Karlberg ICP model**: **infancy** (nutrition-driven), **childhood** (GH and thyroid-driven) and **puberty** (sex steroids plus GH). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Normal velocity: about **25 cm in year 1**, 12 cm in year 2, 8 cm in year 3, then **5-6 cm/year** until puberty; **under 4 cm/year** in a prepubertal child after age 3 is abnormal. [Nelson 22e]`,`Peak pubertal velocity is about **8-9 cm/year in girls (Tanner B2-3)** and **10-11 cm/year in boys (G4)**; girls peak about 2 years earlier. [Nelson 22e]`,`**GH** acts mainly through hepatic and local **IGF-1**, which drives growth-plate chondrocyte proliferation; IGF-1 is low in GHD, malnutrition, hypothyroidism and liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oestrogen** (from aromatised androgen in boys too) closes the epiphyses - which is why **precocious puberty** gives a tall child but a short adult. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Thyroid hormone** is needed for epiphyseal maturation - hypothyroidism gives **marked growth arrest with very delayed bone age** and infantile proportions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Glucocorticoids** inhibit GH secretion, IGF-1 action and chondrocytes, so steroid-treated children (asthma, nephrotic syndrome) grow poorly. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**SHOX haploinsufficiency** explains short stature in Turner syndrome, which is why GH helps despite normal GH levels. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - the one examiners ask`,points:[`Classify first as **proportionate vs disproportionate**: disproportionate points to **skeletal dysplasia or rickets**. [Nelson 22e]`,`Proportionate is split into **prenatal onset** (SGA, syndromes, intrauterine infection, fetal alcohol) and **postnatal onset** (nutritional, systemic, endocrine, psychosocial). [Nelson 22e]`,`**Normal variant vs pathological**: normal variants have **normal growth velocity**; pathological causes have **falling velocity** (crossing centiles). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weight-for-height** splits postnatal causes: **underweight for height** suggests nutritional or systemic disease; **overweight for height ('short and fat')** suggests endocrine disease (hypothyroid, Cushing, GHD). [Nelson 22e]`,`**Bone age** groups causes: **equal to chronological age** - familial, genetic syndromes, skeletal dysplasia; **delayed** - CDGP, nutritional, systemic, endocrine; **advanced** - precocious puberty, CAH, obesity. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Upper segment : lower segment ratio** is about **1.7 at birth, 1.3 at 3 years and 1.0 by 10 years**; increased ratio with short limbs (achondroplasia, rickets, hypothyroidism), decreased with short trunk (spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, Morquio). [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Get **previous heights** from school or anganwadi records, the MCP card and photographs - **one height is a snapshot, velocity is the story**. [IAP 2015]`,`Birth history: **birth weight and gestation** (SGA), neonatal hypoglycaemia, prolonged jaundice and **micropenis** (congenital hypopituitarism). [Nelson 22e]`,`Dietary recall, chronic diarrhoea, abdominal distension (**celiac**), polyuria and polydipsia (RTA, diabetes insipidus, CKD), recurrent infections, cough (TB). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Headache, vomiting, visual disturbance** - craniopharyngioma until proven otherwise. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Family history: **parents' heights (measure them, do not ask)** and **age at menarche of mother and age at growth spurt of father** - late timing suggests CDGP. [Nelson 22e]`,`Drug history: **inhaled or oral steroids**, stimulants for ADHD, anticonvulsants; also school performance, home environment and neglect. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Measure accurately: **infantometer under 2 years**, **stadiometer** over 2 years, plot on the right chart, and calculate **height SDS and BMI**. [IAP 2015]`,`**Mid-parental height (MPH)**: boys (father + mother + 13)/2, girls (father + mother - 13)/2 cm; target range **MPH +/- 8.5 cm**. [IAP 2015]`,`Measure **arm span** (normally about equal to height in adults) and **sitting height / US:LS ratio** for proportions. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Turner stigmata**: webbed neck, low posterior hairline, **cubitus valgus**, shield chest, widely spaced nipples, short 4th metacarpal, lymphoedema at birth, multiple naevi, coarctation murmur. [Turner guideline 2024]`,`**GHD features**: chubby **cherubic face**, frontal bossing, mid-face hypoplasia, truncal adiposity, high-pitched voice, micropenis. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Other clues: **goitre, dry skin, delayed relaxation of ankle jerk** (hypothyroid); **rickets** (wrist widening, rachitic rosary, bow legs); **Cushingoid** features; Tanner staging; **fundus and visual fields** for a pituitary tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pseudohypoparathyroidism: **round face, short 4th and 5th metacarpals (knuckle-knuckle-dimple-dimple sign)**, subcutaneous calcification. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - the normal variants vs disease`,points:[`**Familial short stature**: short parents, normal velocity, **bone age equals chronological age**, normal puberty timing, final height within target range. [Nelson 22e]`,`**CDGP**: normal birth size, velocity slows in the first 2-3 years then runs parallel below the centile, **bone age delayed and equal to height age**, late puberty, family history of 'late bloomers', **final height near target**. [Nelson 22e]`,`**GH deficiency**: falling velocity, **severely delayed bone age**, overweight for height, low IGF-1, failed stimulation tests. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: growth arrest with weight gain, **bone age very delayed**, infantile US:LS ratio, raised TSH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Celiac disease** may present with short stature alone, with no gut symptoms - always test **tTG-IgA with total IgA**. [ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**Turner syndrome** may have no visible stigmata - **karyotype every girl with unexplained short stature**. [Turner guideline 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line (district hospital)**: CBC, ESR or CRP, creatinine, electrolytes with **venous blood gas** (RTA), calcium, phosphate, **ALP**, albumin, LFT, urine routine and pH, stool for ova and fat. [IAP 2015]`,`**TSH and free T4**, and **tTG-IgA with total serum IgA** in every short child without an obvious cause. [ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**Karyotype** (at least 30 cells to detect mosaicism) in **every short girl** without an explanation. [Turner guideline 2024]`,`**Bone age**: X-ray of the **left hand and wrist**, read by **Greulich-Pyle atlas** or TW3; a delay of more than 2 years is significant. [Nelson 22e]`,`**IGF-1 and IGFBP-3** screen for GHD, but IGF-1 is also low in malnutrition, hypothyroidism and in young children - interpret by age, sex and pubertal stage. [PES 2016]`,`**GH stimulation tests**: two tests (clonidine 0.15 mg/m2 oral, glucagon, arginine, insulin tolerance), **peak GH under 10 ng/mL** traditionally (many now use **under 7 ng/mL** with modern assays); **sex-steroid priming** in peripubertal children avoids false positives. [PES 2016]`,`**MRI pituitary** with contrast in confirmed GHD (ectopic posterior pituitary, hypoplasia, tumour); skeletal survey for dysplasia; **SHOX/gene panels** where suspected. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Normal variants need **reassurance and 6-monthly height monitoring** - show the family the plotted chart and predicted adult height. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Nutritional rehabilitation**: age-appropriate calories and protein, deworming (**albendazole 400 mg single dose** over 2 years), iron and micronutrients; treat the underlying disease. [IAP 2015]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: **levothyroxine about 4-6 microg/kg/day in children 1-5 years**, 3-5 microg/kg/day at 6-12 years, adjusted to TSH - catch-up growth follows. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Celiac disease**: lifelong **strict gluten-free diet** - catch-up growth occurs over 1-2 years. [ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**CDGP in boys** distressed by delay (13-14 years or more): short course of **testosterone enanthate 50-100 mg IM monthly for 3-6 months** - does not compromise final height. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Rickets**: vitamin D (for example **stoss 300,000-600,000 IU** in older children, or daily 2,000 IU for 3 months) with calcium - see the Vitamin D topic. [Global Rickets Consensus 2016]`,`Avoid giving **anabolic steroids, cyproheptadine or 'height tonics'** - they advance bone age or do nothing. [IAP 2015]`]},{heading:`Growth hormone therapy - indications and practice`,points:[`**Approved paediatric indications**: GH deficiency, **Turner syndrome**, **Prader-Willi syndrome**, **CKD**, **SGA without catch-up** (by 2 years US, 4 years EU), **Noonan syndrome**, **SHOX deficiency** and **idiopathic short stature** (height below -2.25 SD, US only). [PES 2016]`,`**Doses (daily SC at bedtime)**: GHD **0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day**; Turner **0.045-0.05 mg/kg/day**; SGA 0.035 mg/kg/day; CKD 0.045-0.05 mg/kg/day; Prader-Willi **1 mg/m2/day**. [PES 2016]`,`**Long-acting weekly GH**: **somapacitan 0.16 mg/kg/week**, **lonapegsomatropin 0.24 mg/kg/week**, **somatrogon 0.66 mg/kg/week** - approved for paediatric GHD; availability in India is limited. [FDA 2021-2023]`,`Monitor every **3-6 months**: height velocity, IGF-1 (keep within normal range), TSH, cortisol in MPHD, glucose, spine and hips. [PES 2016]`,`**Stop** when **velocity falls below 2-2.5 cm/year** or **bone age exceeds about 14 years in girls and 16 years in boys**; retest GHD at transition for adult GH therapy. [PES 2016]`,`**Adverse effects**: idiopathic intracranial hypertension, **slipped capital femoral epiphysis**, scoliosis progression, oedema, glucose intolerance; **contraindications** - active malignancy, closed epiphyses, active proliferative retinopathy, and **Prader-Willi with severe obesity or sleep apnoea** (sudden death). [PES 2016]`,`Cost: GH costs lakhs of rupees a year in India - check coverage under state schemes and RBSK before starting. [IAP 2015]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Turner syndrome**: start GH early (about **4-6 years** or when growth falters), **oestrogen at 11-12 years** (low-dose transdermal oestradiol, titrated over 2-3 years); screen heart (echo, MRI), kidney ultrasound, hearing, thyroid, celiac. [Turner guideline 2024]`,`**Achondroplasia**: **vosoritide** (a CNP analogue, daily SC) increases growth velocity by about 1.6 cm/year; watch for foramen magnum stenosis and sleep apnoea. [FDA 2021/2023]`,`**Steroid-treated children**: use the lowest dose, alternate-day regimens or inhaled steroid with spacer; growth velocity recovers after stopping. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Precocious puberty**: tall now but short later - refer for GnRH analogue (leuprolide depot) - see the Precocious and delayed puberty topic. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Adolescent with stunting** (common in India): catch-up is limited once bone age is advanced; focus on nutrition, anaemia and self-esteem. [NFHS-5]`,`**Primary IGF-1 deficiency** (Laron syndrome, GH insensitivity): high GH, very low IGF-1; treat with **mecasermin**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated **Turner syndrome** final height is about **20 cm below target** (about 143-147 cm); GH adds about 5-8 cm. [Turner guideline 2024]`,`Early-treated **GH deficiency** usually reaches target height; late diagnosis loses height permanently. [PES 2016]`,`**Congenital hypothyroidism** missed beyond the neonatal period causes irreversible intellectual disability - newborn screening prevents it. [IAP 2015]`,`**CDGP** reaches near-target height, but some lose a few centimetres; psychosocial distress and bullying are common. [Nelson 22e]`,`Stunting in the first 1000 days is linked to **poor cognition, lower earnings and adult cardiometabolic disease**. [Lancet Maternal and Child Nutrition Series]`,`Short stature by itself carries **psychosocial burden**; counselling is part of treatment. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer to a **paediatric endocrinologist** for height below -3 SD, velocity below 4 cm/year, height more than 2 SD below target, disproportion, dysmorphism or a suspected pituitary lesion. [IAP 2015]`,`Measure every **6 months** (velocity over less than 6 months is unreliable) on the same stadiometer, ideally by the same person. [Nelson 22e]`,`Repeat bone age **every 1-2 years** in CDGP and on GH therapy. [PES 2016]`,`On GH: **3-monthly height, 6-monthly IGF-1 and TSH**, annual glucose and HbA1c, examine hips and spine. [PES 2016]`,`Urgent referral for **headache with visual field loss**, papilloedema or polyuria - craniopharyngioma. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**RBSK (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram)** screens children 0-18 years for 4 Ds including deficiencies and growth delay, with referral to District Early Intervention Centres. [MoHFW]`,`**Poshan Abhiyaan / Mission Poshan 2.0** and the **Poshan Tracker** monitor growth at anganwadis; **ICDS** supplementary nutrition and PM POSHAN mid-day meals target stunting. [MoWCD]`,`**Newborn screening for congenital hypothyroidism** (TSH) prevents both short stature and intellectual disability. [IAP 2015]`,`**First 1000 days**: maternal nutrition, exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months, complementary feeding, WASH and deworming prevent stunting. [WHO]`,`Counsel families that GH is **not a height tonic** for normal short children and that predicted adult height is usually within the family range. [PES 2016]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**International Turner syndrome guideline 2024**: karyotype every short girl, GH from about 4-6 years at 0.045-0.05 mg/kg/day, puberty induction with **transdermal oestradiol at 11-12 years**, lifelong cardiac surveillance. [Turner guideline 2024]`,`**Long-acting weekly GH** (somapacitan and somatrogon FDA-approved in 2023, lonapegsomatropin 2021) - same efficacy, better adherence; check IGF-1 at the right day after injection. [FDA 2023]`,`**Vosoritide** label extended to **infants and young children with achondroplasia** (2023). [FDA 2023]`,`**IAP 2015 charts** remain the Indian standard for 5-18 years, with BMI cut-offs equivalent to **adult 23 and 27**. [IAP 2015]`,`**PES 2016 guideline** still underpins GH practice: lower GH stimulation cut-offs for new assays and sex-steroid priming. [PES 2016]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to a child with short stature'** - answer with definition, MPH, velocity, proportions, bone age and the cause table.`,`Remember: **short and fat = endocrine; short and thin = nutritional or systemic**.`,`**Bone age = height age < chronological age** in CDGP; **bone age = chronological age** in familial short stature.`,`Common mistake: diagnosing short stature from **one measurement** without velocity or mid-parental height.`,`Common mistake: missing **Turner** in a short girl without stigmata - always do a karyotype.`,`Common mistake: forgetting **celiac and RTA** - both are frequent hidden causes in India.`,`Know the **MPH formula** and the **+/- 8.5 cm** target range.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - treating causes of short stature`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Somatropin (daily GH) - GHD`,`0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day SC at bedtime`,`Until velocity under 2-2.5 cm/year or bone age 14 (girls) / 16 (boys); titrate to IGF-1`,`IIH, SCFE, scoliosis, glucose intolerance; not with active cancer`],[`Somatropin - Turner / CKD`,`0.045-0.05 mg/kg/day SC`,`From about 4-6 years until near-final height`,`As above; monitor glucose and BP`],[`Somatropin - Prader-Willi`,`1 mg/m2/day SC`,`Sleep study before and during treatment`,`Contraindicated with severe obesity or sleep apnoea - sudden death`],[`Somapacitan (long-acting GH)`,`0.16 mg/kg/week SC`,`Weekly, adjust by IGF-1 and growth`,`As daily GH; injection-site reactions`],[`Levothyroxine`,`4-6 microg/kg/day oral (1-5 y); 3-5 microg/kg/day (6-12 y)`,`TSH every 6-8 weeks after change, then 6-monthly`,`Over-treatment advances bone age; palpitations`],[`Testosterone enanthate (CDGP boys)`,`50-100 mg IM every 4 weeks`,`3-6 months, then reassess`,`Acne, priapism; avoid excessive bone age advance`],[`Transdermal oestradiol (Turner)`,`Start about 3-7 microg/day patch (or oral 0.25 mg) at 11-12 years`,`Increase over 2-3 years; add progestogen after breakthrough bleed`,`Thrombosis (low), breast tenderness`]]},{heading:`Familial short stature vs CDGP vs pathological`,columns:[`Feature`,`Familial short stature`,`CDGP`,`Pathological (GHD, hypothyroid)`],rows:[[`Parents' height`,`Short`,`Average; late bloomers`,`Usually average`],[`Growth velocity`,`Normal`,`Normal (slows early, then parallel)`,`Low, crossing centiles`],[`Bone age`,`Equal to chronological age`,`Delayed, equals height age`,`Markedly delayed`],[`Puberty`,`Normal timing`,`Delayed`,`Delayed or absent`],[`Weight for height`,`Normal`,`Normal or thin`,`Often overweight ('short and fat')`],[`Final height`,`Short but within target range`,`Near target`,`Below target if untreated`],[`Action`,`Reassure, monitor`,`Reassure; testosterone if distressed`,`Treat cause, GH or thyroxine`]]},{heading:`Bone age and proportion classification`,columns:[`Finding`,`Group`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Bone age = chronological age`,`Genetic, familial`,`Familial short stature, Turner, skeletal dysplasia, SGA`],[`Bone age delayed`,`Constitutional, nutritional, systemic, endocrine`,`CDGP, malnutrition, celiac, CKD, hypothyroidism, GHD, Cushing`],[`Bone age advanced`,`Sex-steroid excess`,`Precocious puberty, CAH, obesity`],[`Increased US:LS (short limbs)`,`Disproportionate`,`Achondroplasia, rickets, hypothyroidism`],[`Decreased US:LS (short trunk)`,`Disproportionate`,`Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia, Morquio, vertebral disease`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Height SDS`,`Below -2 SD (below -3 SD severe)`,`Short stature; refer if below -3`],[`Growth velocity (prepubertal, over 3 years)`,`Under 4 cm/year`,`Pathological until proven otherwise`],[`Mid-parental height`,`Child more than 8.5 cm (2 SD) below MPH`,`Look for pathology`],[`Venous blood gas`,`Normal anion-gap acidosis, urine pH over 5.5`,`Distal RTA`],[`tTG-IgA (with total IgA)`,`Over 10 times upper limit`,`Celiac disease (biopsy-sparing route if EMA positive)`],[`Karyotype`,`45,X or mosaic`,`Turner syndrome`],[`IGF-1`,`Below -2 SD for age and puberty`,`Supports GHD; also low in malnutrition`],[`GH stimulation (two tests)`,`Peak under 10 ng/mL (under 7 with newer assays)`,`GH deficiency`]]},{heading:`Syndromic causes at a glance`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`Key clue`,`Test`,`GH approved?`],rows:[[`Turner`,`Girl, webbed neck, cubitus valgus, delayed puberty`,`Karyotype`,`Yes`],[`Noonan`,`Either sex, pulmonary stenosis, ptosis, webbed neck`,`PTPN11 gene`,`Yes`],[`Prader-Willi`,`Neonatal hypotonia, hyperphagia, obesity`,`Methylation test 15q11-13`,`Yes`],[`Russell-Silver`,`SGA, triangular face, body asymmetry`,`11p15 methylation`,`Yes (as SGA)`],[`SHOX deficiency`,`Madelung deformity, mesomelia`,`SHOX analysis`,`Yes`],[`Achondroplasia`,`Short limbs, large head, trident hand`,`Clinical, FGFR3`,`No - vosoritide`]]}],redFlags:[`Headache, vomiting, visual field loss or papilloedema - craniopharyngioma or pituitary tumour`,`Growth velocity under 4 cm/year or crossing two centile lines`,`Height below -3 SD or more than 2 SD below mid-parental target`,`Neonatal hypoglycaemia with micropenis or prolonged jaundice - congenital hypopituitarism`,`Polyuria and polydipsia with growth failure - diabetes insipidus, RTA or CKD`,`Disproportionate short stature or dysmorphic features`,`Severe acute malnutrition (weight-for-height below -3 SD or MUAC under 11.5 cm)`],pearls:[`**Velocity beats a single height** - plot at least two measurements 6 months apart.`,`**MPH**: (father + mother +/- 13)/2; target **+/- 8.5 cm**.`,`**Short and fat = endocrine; short and thin = nutritional**.`,`**CDGP**: bone age delayed and **equal to height age**; final height near target.`,`**Familial**: bone age = chronological age, short parents, normal velocity.`,`**Karyotype every short girl** - Turner can be stigmata-free.`,`Always test **TSH and tTG-IgA** - hypothyroidism and celiac hide behind short stature.`,`US:LS ratio **1.7 at birth, 1.3 at 3 years, 1.0 at 10 years**.`,`Precocious puberty: **tall child, short adult**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-q1`,stem:`A 14-year-old boy is below the 3rd centile for height. His velocity has been 5 cm/year for 3 years, he is Tanner stage 1, his father had his growth spurt at 17 years, and bone age is 11 years. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Growth hormone deficiency`,`Familial short stature`,`Constitutional delay of growth and puberty`,`Hypothyroidism`],answer:2,explanation:`Normal prepubertal velocity, delayed bone age, delayed puberty and a family history of late growth spurt are classic for constitutional delay of growth and puberty, with final height near target. Familial short stature has bone age equal to chronological age and normal puberty timing; GH deficiency and hypothyroidism show a falling velocity below 4 cm/year.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-q2`,stem:`A 9-year-old girl is at -2.6 SD for height with normal intelligence and no dysmorphic features. TSH, tTG-IgA and blood gas are normal. Which investigation is mandatory next?`,options:[`Karyotype`,`Insulin tolerance test`,`MRI brain`,`Skeletal survey`],answer:0,explanation:`Turner syndrome may have no visible stigmata, so every girl with unexplained short stature needs a karyotype (at least 30 cells to detect mosaicism). Insulin tolerance testing comes only after GHD is suspected and is hazardous; MRI is done after GHD is confirmed; a skeletal survey is for disproportionate short stature.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-q3`,stem:`What is the recommended starting dose of daily growth hormone for a child with confirmed growth hormone deficiency?`,options:[`0.1 mg/kg/day`,`0.005 mg/kg/day`,`1 mg/kg/week`,`0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day`],answer:3,explanation:`The Pediatric Endocrine Society recommends 0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day subcutaneously for GH deficiency, titrated to growth and IGF-1. Turner syndrome needs a higher 0.045-0.05 mg/kg/day. A dose of 0.1 mg/kg/day or 1 mg/kg/week is far too high and raises IGF-1 and adverse effects, while 0.005 mg/kg/day is an adult-range dose that will not drive growth.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-q4`,stem:`In which of the following conditions is bone age typically equal to chronological age?`,options:[`Constitutional delay of growth and puberty`,`Familial short stature`,`Primary hypothyroidism`,`Growth hormone deficiency`],answer:1,explanation:`In familial (genetic) short stature, maturation is normal, so bone age equals chronological age and the child finishes short within the family target range. Constitutional delay has bone age delayed to match height age, while hypothyroidism and GH deficiency produce marked bone age delay.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-q5`,stem:`Growth hormone therapy in Prader-Willi syndrome requires extra caution because of which risk?`,options:[`Hypoglycaemic seizures`,`Premature epiphyseal fusion`,`Sudden death in children with severe obesity or obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Hypothyroid crisis`],answer:2,explanation:`Sudden deaths, often during respiratory infection, have been reported in Prader-Willi children with severe obesity or upper airway obstruction on GH, so a sleep study and weight control are needed before and during treatment. GH tends to cause hyperglycaemia, not hypoglycaemia; it does not fuse epiphyses; it can unmask central hypothyroidism but not cause crisis.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define short stature. Describe the causes, clinical approach and investigation of a child with short stature. Add a note on indications for growth hormone therapy.`,openingLines:[`Short stature is height below -2 SD (3rd centile) for age and sex on the appropriate chart, or height more than 2 SD below the mid-parental target, or subnormal growth velocity.`,`Most short children are normal variants - familial short stature or constitutional delay - and pathology is detected by velocity, proportions and bone age.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and growth norms`,points:[`Below -2 SD; severe below -3 SD; velocity under 4 cm/year after age 3 abnormal.`,`Charts: WHO 2006 under 5 years, IAP 2015 for 5-18 years; NFHS-5 stunting 35.5%.`,`MPH = (father + mother +/- 13)/2; target +/- 8.5 cm.`]},{heading:`Causes (classification)`,points:[`Normal variants: familial, CDGP.`,`Proportionate prenatal: SGA, Turner, Noonan, Russell-Silver, Prader-Willi.`,`Proportionate postnatal: malnutrition, celiac, CKD, RTA, heart disease; hypothyroidism, GHD, Cushing; psychosocial.`,`Disproportionate: achondroplasia, rickets, SED, MPS.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Previous heights, birth weight, neonatal hypoglycaemia, diet, diarrhoea, polyuria, headache, steroids, parental heights and puberty timing.`,`Accurate stadiometer height, weight, BMI, arm span, US:LS ratio, Tanner stage, dysmorphism, thyroid, rickets, fundus and fields.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, ESR, RFT, VBG, Ca, P, ALP, LFT, urine pH, stool; TSH, FT4; tTG-IgA with total IgA; karyotype in girls; bone age.`,`Second line: IGF-1, IGFBP-3, two GH stimulation tests (peak under 10 ng/mL), MRI pituitary, skeletal survey, gene tests.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`BA = CA: familial, genetic; BA delayed: CDGP, systemic, endocrine; BA advanced: precocious puberty, CAH.`,`Short and fat: endocrine; short and thin: nutritional or systemic.`]},{heading:`Growth hormone therapy`,points:[`Indications: GHD, Turner, Prader-Willi, CKD, SGA without catch-up, Noonan, SHOX, ISS (US).`,`Dose GHD 0.025-0.035 mg/kg/day SC; Turner 0.045-0.05; stop at velocity under 2-2.5 cm/year or BA 14/16.`,`Adverse: IIH, SCFE, scoliosis, glucose intolerance; contraindicated in active cancer.`]}],mustDraw:[`Growth chart showing familial short stature vs CDGP vs GHD curves`,`Flow chart: short stature - velocity - proportions - bone age`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and norms`,marks:1},{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`History and examination`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and interpretation`,marks:3},{part:`GH therapy`,marks:2}],keywords:[`short stature`,`MPH`,`bone age`,`CDGP`,`GHD`,`Turner`,`IAP 2015`]},{id:`endocrine-short-stature-growth-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate familial short stature from constitutional delay of growth and puberty.`,openingLines:[`Both are normal variants with normal growth velocity and no disease.`,`They are separated by parental height, bone age and timing of puberty.`],answer:[{heading:`History`,points:[`Familial: short parents; CDGP: average parents with late puberty ('late bloomers').`]},{heading:`Growth pattern`,points:[`Familial: grows parallel below the centile from infancy; CDGP: slows in the first 2-3 years then parallel, with late spurt.`]},{heading:`Bone age and puberty`,points:[`Familial: bone age = chronological age, puberty on time; CDGP: bone age delayed = height age, puberty delayed.`]},{heading:`Outcome and management`,points:[`Familial: short adult within target range - reassure; CDGP: near target height - reassure, short testosterone course in distressed boys.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table with growth curves`],markSplit:[{part:`History`,marks:1},{part:`Growth pattern`,marks:1},{part:`Bone age and puberty`,marks:2},{part:`Outcome and management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`familial short stature`,`CDGP`,`bone age`,`late bloomer`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd edition, 2024 - disorders of growth`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`Indian Academy of Pediatrics (IAP) Growth Charts Committee. Revised IAP growth charts for height, weight and BMI for 5-18-year-old Indian children. Indian Pediatrics 2015`,`Grimberg A et al. Pediatric Endocrine Society guidelines for growth hormone and insulin-like growth factor-I treatment in children and adolescents, Horm Res Paediatr 2016`,`Gravholt CH et al. Clinical practice guidelines for the care of girls and women with Turner syndrome, Eur J Endocrinol 2024`,`National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5), 2019-21, MoHFW`,`ESPGHAN guidelines for the diagnosis of coeliac disease, 2020`]}),d.push({id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach`,title:`Approach to hypercalcaemia`,oneLiner:`**Hypercalcaemia** is an albumin-corrected serum calcium **above about 10.5 mg/dL** (or ionised calcium above 1.32 mmol/L); **primary hyperparathyroidism and malignancy cause about 90%**, and the single most useful test is an **intact PTH** - **raised or inappropriately normal PTH** means PTH-dependent disease, **suppressed PTH** sends you to **PTHrP, vitamin D metabolites, myeloma screen and granulomas (TB in India)** - while treatment is graded by severity: **saline, then zoledronic acid or denosumab, with calcitonin for a rapid fall and steroids for calcitriol-driven causes**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`hypercalcaemia`,`hypercalcemia`,`corrected calcium`,`ionised calcium`,`primary hyperparathyroidism`,`PTHrP`,`humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy`,`multiple myeloma`,`granulomatous hypercalcaemia`,`sarcoidosis`,`tuberculosis`,`vitamin D toxicity`,`milk-alkali syndrome`,`familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia`,`FHH`,`zoledronic acid`,`denosumab`,`calcitonin`,`stones bones groans moans`,`hypercalcaemic crisis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`Normal total serum calcium is about **8.5-10.5 mg/dL** (2.15-2.6 mmol/L) and ionised calcium **1.12-1.32 mmol/L**; about 40% is albumin-bound, so always **correct for albumin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Corrected calcium (mg/dL) = measured calcium + 0.8 x (4.0 - albumin g/dL)**; measure **ionised calcium** when albumin is very abnormal, in acid-base disturbance, myeloma (paraprotein binding) or CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity**: **mild under 12 mg/dL**, **moderate 12-14 mg/dL**, **severe over 14 mg/dL** (3.5 mmol/L); symptoms depend as much on the **rate of rise** as the level. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT)** is the commonest cause in **outpatients**, **malignancy** the commonest in **inpatients** - together about **90%** of all cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hypercalcaemia of malignancy occurs in up to **20-30% of patients with cancer** at some time and signals a poor prognosis (median survival often weeks to months). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`India: PHPT is more often **symptomatic** (bone disease, stones, pancreatitis) than in the West, per the **Indian PHPT Registry**; **vitamin D toxicity** from repeated high-dose injections and **tuberculosis** are distinctive Indian causes. [Indian PHPT Registry]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - causes grouped by PTH`,points:[`**PTH-dependent**: **PHPT** (single adenoma about 85%, hyperplasia, carcinoma under 1%), **tertiary hyperparathyroidism** in CKD or after transplant, **lithium**, **FHH** (CaSR loss-of-function), MEN 1 and 2A. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignancy - humoral (PTHrP)**, about **80%** of cancer cases: squamous cancers (lung, head and neck, oesophagus, cervix), renal, bladder, breast and ovarian cancers, adult T-cell lymphoma. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Malignancy - local osteolytic**, about **20%**: **multiple myeloma**, breast cancer with bone metastases, lymphoma - via RANKL, IL-6 and MIP-1 alpha. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calcitriol-mediated**: **lymphomas** (especially Hodgkin), **granulomas - sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, leprosy, histoplasmosis, fungal infection**, silicone granulomas - macrophage **1-alpha-hydroxylase** escapes PTH control. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Vitamin D toxicity**: 25-OH D usually **over 150 ng/mL**, mostly from **repeated 6 lakh IU injections** or mislabelled supplements; also **vitamin A excess**, calcitriol or alfacalcidol overdose. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Milk-alkali syndrome**: high **calcium carbonate** intake (antacids, osteoporosis supplements) with alkali, causing hypercalcaemia, metabolic alkalosis and AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Others**: **thiazides** (reduce urinary calcium; unmask PHPT), thyrotoxicosis, **immobilisation** (young, Paget disease), adrenal insufficiency, phaeochromocytoma, theophylline, **teriparatide**, rebound after stopping **denosumab**, Williams syndrome in infants. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the symptoms and the treatments`,points:[`Calcium enters the ECF from **bone resorption, gut absorption and renal reabsorption**; hypercalcaemia means one of these inflows exceeds renal excretion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hypercalcaemia activates the renal **CaSR** in the thick ascending limb and collecting duct, causing **nephrogenic diabetes insipidus** - polyuria, then **volume depletion**, falling GFR and still higher calcium: a vicious cycle broken by **saline**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Raised extracellular calcium **reduces neuromuscular excitability** (stabilises sodium channels), causing weakness, hyporeflexia, constipation, ileus and confusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`On the heart it **shortens the plateau phase**, giving a **short QT interval**, bradycardia, AV block and, at very high levels, Osborn J waves and arrhythmia; it potentiates **digoxin toxicity**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PTHrP** shares the N-terminal of PTH and binds the PTH1 receptor, so it raises calcium and lowers phosphate - but **does not raise 1,25-(OH)2D** much, unlike PTH. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`Bone resorption drives most cancer and PHPT hypercalcaemia, so **antiresorptives (bisphosphonates, denosumab)** are the backbone; gut-driven causes (granulomas, vitamin D) respond to **glucocorticoids**, which block 1-alpha-hydroxylase. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Calcitonin** inhibits osteoclasts and increases urinary calcium within hours but receptors down-regulate - **tachyphylaxis in 48 hours**. [Endocrine Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`Grade by level: **mild under 12 mg/dL** - often asymptomatic, no urgent treatment; **moderate 12-14** - treat if symptomatic or rising; **severe over 14** - treat urgently regardless of symptoms. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Hypercalcaemic crisis**: calcium usually **over 14 mg/dL** with dehydration, AKI and altered sensorium - an emergency; see the Endocrine emergencies topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mechanistic classification for malignancy: **humoral (PTHrP)**, **local osteolytic**, **calcitriol-mediated** and **ectopic PTH** (rare). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`Clinical classification: **PTH-dependent** (high or inappropriately normal PTH) vs **PTH-independent** (suppressed PTH, usually under 20 pg/mL). [Harrison 22e]`,`PHPT phenotypes: **symptomatic**, **asymptomatic** (most in the West) and **normocalcaemic PHPT** (raised PTH with normal calcium after excluding vitamin D deficiency and CKD). [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Classic mnemonic: **'stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans and thrones'** - renal stones, bone pain and fractures, abdominal pain and constipation, depression or confusion, polyuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal**: polyuria, polydipsia, nocturia (nephrogenic DI), renal colic, nephrocalcinosis, AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GI**: anorexia, nausea, vomiting, constipation, **peptic ulcer** and **acute pancreatitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuropsychiatric**: fatigue, poor concentration, depression, confusion, drowsiness and coma at high levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tempo** is a clue: long-standing mild hypercalcaemia over years suggests **PHPT or FHH**; rapid rise with weight loss suggests **malignancy**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Drug and supplement history is essential: **vitamin D injections, calcium carbonate, antacids, thiazides, lithium**, vitamin A, teriparatide, recent denosumab withdrawal. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`Ask about **cough, fever, night sweats** (TB), smoking and haemoptysis (squamous lung cancer), bone pain and back pain (myeloma, metastases), breast lump, and family history of hypercalcaemia (FHH, MEN). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Dehydration**: dry tongue, tachycardia, postural hypotension - often severe because of nephrogenic DI and vomiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Band keratopathy** (calcium deposits at the limbus of the cornea, seen on slit lamp) indicates long-standing hypercalcaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hyporeflexia, proximal weakness, altered sensorium; **hypertension** is common in PHPT. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Look for a **cause**: clubbing and chest signs (lung cancer, TB), lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly (lymphoma, TB), breast lump, bone tenderness, **lupus pernio or erythema nodosum** (sarcoidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`A **palpable neck mass** with calcium over 14 mg/dL and PTH more than 3-5 times normal suggests **parathyroid carcinoma** (parathyroid adenomas are rarely palpable). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`In PHPT with bone disease (common in India): **brown tumours**, bone deformity, pathological fractures, proximal myopathy. [Indian PHPT Registry]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - discriminating features`,points:[`**PHPT vs FHH**: both have high calcium with normal-high PTH; FHH has **calcium/creatinine clearance ratio under 0.01**, lifelong mild hypercalcaemia and family history; parathyroidectomy does not help FHH. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`**PHPT vs malignancy**: PHPT has raised PTH, mild long-standing calcium; malignancy has **suppressed PTH, high PTHrP**, rapid rise and a sick patient. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Granulomatous vs vitamin D toxicity**: both suppress PTH; granulomas have **high 1,25-(OH)2D with normal 25-OH D**, toxicity has **very high 25-OH D (over 150 ng/mL)**. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Milk-alkali syndrome**: suppressed PTH, **metabolic alkalosis**, AKI and a history of calcium carbonate or antacids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudohypercalcaemia**: high albumin from dehydration, or **paraprotein binding** in myeloma - ionised calcium is normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lithium**: raises the calcium set-point of the parathyroid; mild hypercalcaemia with normal-high PTH that may resolve on stopping lithium. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - the algorithm`,points:[`Step 1: **repeat and confirm** - corrected or ionised calcium, plus creatinine, electrolytes, phosphate, magnesium, ALP, albumin and **ECG** (short QT). [Harrison 22e]`,`Step 2: **intact PTH** - the pivotal test. **Raised or inappropriately normal (over about 20-25 pg/mL)**: PHPT, FHH, lithium or tertiary HPT. **Suppressed (under 20 pg/mL)**: PTH-independent. [Harrison 22e]`,`PTH-dependent: **24-hour urinary calcium** and **calcium/creatinine clearance ratio** (under 0.01 FHH; over 0.02 PHPT), **25-OH D**, eGFR, then **neck ultrasound and sestamibi or 4D-CT/18F-choline PET** only to localise before surgery. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`PTH-independent: **PTHrP**, **25-OH D**, **1,25-(OH)2D**, **serum protein electrophoresis, immunofixation and serum free light chains** (myeloma), TSH, chest X-ray, and CT chest-abdomen as directed. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`If 1,25-(OH)2D is high: look for **TB** (chest X-ray, sputum CBNAAT, lymph node biopsy), **sarcoidosis** (ACE, HRCT, biopsy) and **lymphoma** (LDH, CT, biopsy). [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Other tests as needed: **vitamin A level**, cortisol (adrenal insufficiency), serum bicarbonate (milk-alkali alkalosis), bone scan, DEXA and renal ultrasound for stones and nephrocalcinosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`PHPT bone: **DEXA at lumbar spine, hip and distal one-third radius** (cortical bone loss is earliest at radius); vertebral fracture assessment. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Mild (under 12 mg/dL), asymptomatic**: no urgent treatment; avoid dehydration, **stop thiazides, lithium (if possible), calcium and vitamin D supplements**, and find the cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`Encourage **oral fluids 2-3 L/day**, keep a **normal dietary calcium intake (about 1000-1200 mg/day)** - restricting calcium raises PTH further in PHPT. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`**PHPT**: refer for **parathyroidectomy** if symptomatic or meets surgical criteria - see the Primary hyperparathyroidism topic; **cinacalcet 30 mg twice daily** if surgery is not possible. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`**Vitamin D toxicity**: stop all vitamin D, hydrate; give **prednisolone 20-40 mg/day** if calcium stays high - 25-OH D falls slowly over months because it is stored in fat. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`**Milk-alkali**: stop calcium carbonate and antacids and rehydrate - it usually corrects within days; **avoid bisphosphonates** (risk of rebound hypocalcaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Granulomatous (TB, sarcoidosis)**: treat the disease (ATT per NTEP; steroids for sarcoid), **avoid sunlight and vitamin D**, and use **prednisolone 20-40 mg/day** for persistent hypercalcaemia. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - by severity`,points:[`Admit **calcium over 14 mg/dL**, any symptomatic hypercalcaemia, **altered sensorium, AKI, arrhythmia** or vomiting with dehydration. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**IV 0.9% saline** first: a bolus if hypotensive, then **200-300 mL/hour** adjusted to keep **urine output 100-150 mL/hour**; watch for overload in heart failure and CKD - it lowers calcium by only about 1-2 mg/dL. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calcitonin 4 IU/kg SC or IM every 12 hours** (can increase to 8 IU/kg every 6-12 hours) for a rapid fall in severe cases; useful for only **48 hours** (tachyphylaxis). [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Zoledronic acid 4 mg IV over at least 15 minutes** (onset 2-4 days, nadir 4-7 days, lasts 2-4 weeks); alternative **pamidronate 60-90 mg IV over 2-4 hours**; reduce dose in CKD and check creatinine before each dose. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Denosumab 120 mg SC** (repeat days 8 and 15 then monthly if needed) for **bisphosphonate-refractory hypercalcaemia or significant renal impairment**; risk of hypocalcaemia - check vitamin D. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Glucocorticoids** (prednisolone 40-60 mg/day, or hydrocortisone 200-400 mg/day IV) for calcitriol-mediated causes: **lymphoma, granulomas, vitamin D toxicity**. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Haemodialysis** with low or zero calcium dialysate for calcium **over 18 mg/dL** with neurological symptoms, or severe hypercalcaemia with renal failure or heart failure that cannot take saline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loop diuretics are no longer routine** - give furosemide only after volume repletion to treat fluid overload; monitor calcium every 6-12 hours, plus potassium, magnesium and ECG. [Endocrine Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: PHPT causes miscarriage, pre-eclampsia and **neonatal hypocalcaemia** (suppressed fetal parathyroids); surgery in the **second trimester** if indicated; bisphosphonates and denosumab are avoided. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**CKD**: tertiary hyperparathyroidism and calcium-based binders or calcitriol cause hypercalcaemia; use **calcimimetics (cinacalcet, etelcalcetide)** or parathyroidectomy; reduce bisphosphonate doses. [KDIGO 2017]`,`**Elderly**: present with confusion or constipation alone; thiazides and calcium supplements are frequent culprits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children and infants**: Williams syndrome, FHH, **neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism** (homozygous CaSR), immobilisation, vitamin D overdose from mislabelled drops. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Cancer patients**: hypercalcaemia is often terminal - discuss goals of care; treat to relieve symptoms (confusion, nausea) even in palliation. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**TB in India**: hypercalcaemia can appear **after starting ATT** or with vitamin D supplementation in TB; check calcium in TB patients on vitamin D. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Renal**: nephrogenic DI, AKI, **nephrolithiasis, nephrocalcinosis** and CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac**: short QT, bradyarrhythmias, heart block, hypertension, valvular and vascular calcification. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**GI**: acute pancreatitis, peptic ulcer, severe constipation and ileus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bone**: osteoporosis, fractures and **osteitis fibrosa cystica** in severe PHPT. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`**Neurological**: coma and death in hypercalcaemic crisis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Prognosis depends on cause: PHPT is curable by surgery (about 95% cure); **malignancy-associated hypercalcaemia** carries a median survival of a few months. [Endocrine Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer to **endocrinology** for PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia, and to **oncology or haematology** for PTHrP-mediated or myeloma-related disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`During acute treatment: calcium (corrected or ionised) every **6-12 hours**, creatinine, electrolytes, magnesium, phosphate, fluid balance and ECG. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`After bisphosphonate or denosumab: watch for **hypocalcaemia** at day 4-7; give vitamin D if deficient before denosumab. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`Untreated mild PHPT: **serum calcium and creatinine yearly**, DEXA every 1-2 years, renal imaging if symptoms. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`Vitamin D toxicity: calcium every 1-2 weeks until stable, 25-OH D every 1-3 months until under 100 ng/mL. [Endocrine Society 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Do not prescribe 6 lakh IU vitamin D injections** repeatedly or without a reason; daily or weekly oral dosing is safer. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`The **Endocrine Society 2024 vitamin D guideline** advises **against routine 25-OH D testing** in healthy adults - a measure that also prevents overtreatment and toxicity. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`Counsel patients on **calcium carbonate supplements** not to exceed recommended doses, especially with antacids. [Harrison 22e]`,`Patients with TB or sarcoidosis should **avoid vitamin D supplements** unless deficiency is proven and calcium is monitored. [Williams Endocrinology 15e]`,`Family screening (serum calcium) for **FHH and MEN 1** in relatives of affected patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`TB treatment is free under **NTEP**, and cancer care is covered under **AB PM-JAY**. [MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Endocrine Society 2023 guideline on hypercalcaemia of malignancy**: suggests **denosumab or IV bisphosphonate** (denosumab favoured where available), **calcitonin added** for severe hypercalcaemia, **glucocorticoids** for calcitriol-mediated cases, and **cinacalcet** for parathyroid carcinoma. [Endocrine Society 2023]`,`**Fifth International Workshop on asymptomatic PHPT (2022)**: surgery if calcium over 1 mg/dL above upper limit, T-score -2.5 or less or vertebral fracture, **eGFR under 60**, **hypercalciuria over 250 mg/day (women) or 300 mg/day (men)**, stones or nephrocalcinosis, or **age under 50**. [Fifth International Workshop 2022]`,`**Endocrine Society 2024 vitamin D guideline**: no routine 25-OH D screening and no target threshold in the general population - fewer prescriptions of mega-doses. [Endocrine Society 2024]`,`Routine **loop diuretic** use in hypercalcaemia is discouraged in current guidance. [Endocrine Society 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Approach to hypercalcaemia'** - start with correction for albumin, then PTH-dependent vs independent.`,`**PHPT plus malignancy = 90%**: PHPT in OPD, malignancy in wards.`,`Short QT on ECG = **hypercalcaemia**; long QT = hypocalcaemia.`,`FHH: **Ca/Cr clearance ratio under 0.01** - do not operate.`,`**Steroids work for gut-driven (calcitriol) causes**: lymphoma, granulomas, vitamin D toxicity - not PHPT.`,`Common mistake: giving **furosemide before fluid** - it worsens dehydration and calcium.`,`Common mistake: forgetting to ask about **vitamin D injections** - a top Indian cause.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - treatment of hypercalcaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`0.9% sodium chloride`,`Bolus if hypotensive, then 200-300 mL/hour IV`,`Titrate to urine output 100-150 mL/hour; 24-72 hours`,`Fluid overload in heart failure, CKD`],[`Calcitonin (salmon)`,`4 IU/kg SC/IM every 12 hours; up to 8 IU/kg every 6-12 hours`,`Effect within 4-6 hours; useful for 48 hours only`,`Nausea, flushing; tachyphylaxis`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV over at least 15 minutes`,`Onset 2-4 days; repeat after at least 7 days if needed`,`Acute-phase reaction, AKI, hypocalcaemia, osteonecrosis of jaw`],[`Pamidronate`,`60-90 mg IV over 2-4 hours`,`Single dose; repeat after 7 days if needed`,`Fever, AKI, hypocalcaemia`],[`Denosumab`,`120 mg SC; repeat on days 8 and 15, then monthly`,`For bisphosphonate-refractory or renal impairment`,`Hypocalcaemia (check vitamin D), osteonecrosis of jaw`],[`Prednisolone`,`40-60 mg/day oral (or hydrocortisone 200-400 mg/day IV)`,`3-10 days, then taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection; ineffective in PHPT`],[`Cinacalcet`,`30 mg oral twice daily, up to 90 mg four times daily (carcinoma)`,`Titrate every 2-4 weeks to calcium`,`Nausea, hypocalcaemia; QT effects`]]},{heading:`PTH-dependent vs PTH-independent causes`,columns:[`Group`,`PTH`,`Other key labs`,`Causes`],rows:[[`PTH-dependent`,`High or inappropriately normal`,`Low-normal phosphate; high 1,25-(OH)2D`,`PHPT, tertiary HPT, lithium`],[`FHH`,`Normal or mildly high`,`Ca/Cr clearance ratio under 0.01`,`CaSR (or GNA11, AP2S1) mutation`],[`Humoral malignancy`,`Suppressed`,`High PTHrP, low phosphate`,`Squamous lung, head-neck, renal, breast cancer`],[`Osteolytic malignancy`,`Suppressed`,`Paraprotein, free light chains, lytic lesions`,`Myeloma, breast metastases`],[`Calcitriol-mediated`,`Suppressed`,`High 1,25-(OH)2D, normal 25-OH D`,`Lymphoma, TB, sarcoidosis, fungal infection`],[`Vitamin D toxicity`,`Suppressed`,`25-OH D over 150 ng/mL`,`Repeated mega-dose injections`],[`Milk-alkali`,`Suppressed`,`Metabolic alkalosis, AKI`,`Calcium carbonate plus antacids`]]},{heading:`Severity and treatment`,columns:[`Corrected calcium`,`Severity`,`Typical symptoms`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`10.5-12 mg/dL`,`Mild`,`Usually none`,`Treat cause, hydrate, stop offending drugs`],[`12-14 mg/dL`,`Moderate`,`Polyuria, constipation, nausea`,`Saline plus bisphosphonate if symptomatic or rising`],[`Over 14 mg/dL`,`Severe`,`Confusion, AKI, vomiting, arrhythmia`,`Saline plus calcitonin plus zoledronic acid or denosumab`],[`Over 18 mg/dL with coma or renal failure`,`Crisis`,`Coma, arrhythmia`,`Add haemodialysis with low-calcium dialysate`]]},{heading:`Investigation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Corrected calcium`,`Measured + 0.8 x (4 - albumin)`,`Over 10.5 mg/dL = hypercalcaemia`],[`Ionised calcium`,`Over 1.32 mmol/L`,`True hypercalcaemia`],[`Intact PTH`,`Over 20-25 pg/mL with high calcium`,`PTH-dependent`],[`Ca/Cr clearance ratio`,`Under 0.01`,`FHH`],[`25-OH vitamin D`,`Over 150 ng/mL`,`Vitamin D toxicity`],[`1,25-(OH)2D`,`High with normal 25-OH D`,`Granuloma or lymphoma`],[`24-hour urine calcium`,`Over 250 mg (women) / 300 mg (men)`,`Hypercalciuria - surgical criterion in PHPT`]]},{heading:`ECG and clinical clues`,columns:[`Clue`,`Points to`],rows:[[`Short QT, bradycardia`,`Hypercalcaemia (any cause)`],[`Rapid rise, weight loss, smoker`,`PTHrP-mediated malignancy`],[`Back pain, anaemia, high ESR, renal failure`,`Multiple myeloma`],[`Fever, cough, lymph nodes`,`Tuberculosis or lymphoma`],[`Metabolic alkalosis, antacid use`,`Milk-alkali syndrome`],[`Lifelong mild hypercalcaemia, relatives affected`,`FHH`]]}],redFlags:[`Corrected calcium over 14 mg/dL`,`Confusion, drowsiness or coma with high calcium`,`AKI or oliguria with hypercalcaemia`,`Bradycardia, heart block or arrhythmia on ECG`,`Persistent vomiting and dehydration`,`Rapidly rising calcium with weight loss - likely malignancy`,`Hypercalcaemia with acute pancreatitis`],pearls:[`**Correct for albumin first**: + 0.8 mg/dL per 1 g/dL albumin below 4.`,`**PTH is the pivot**: high or normal = PTH-dependent; suppressed = look elsewhere.`,`**PHPT in OPD, malignancy in IPD** - together 90%.`,`**Stones, bones, groans, moans, thrones**.`,`**Fluids first, furosemide never first**.`,`Calcitonin is fast but fades in **48 hours**; zoledronic acid is slow (2-4 days) but lasts weeks.`,`**Steroids for calcitriol causes**: lymphoma, granulomas, vitamin D toxicity.`,`FHH: **Ca/Cr clearance ratio under 0.01** - surgery does not help.`,`In India, always ask about **vitamin D injections** and **TB**.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old smoker has weight loss and confusion. Corrected calcium is 14.8 mg/dL, phosphate is low and intact PTH is 6 pg/mL. Which mediator is most likely responsible?`,options:[`Excess 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D from a granuloma`,`Ectopic intact PTH secretion`,`Parathyroid hormone-related peptide`,`Calcium-sensing receptor mutation`],answer:2,explanation:`Severe, rapidly symptomatic hypercalcaemia with suppressed PTH and low phosphate in a smoker suggests humoral hypercalcaemia of malignancy from a squamous lung cancer secreting PTHrP. Ectopic PTH would make PTH high and is very rare; granulomatous disease raises calcitriol but phosphate tends to be normal-high; a CaSR mutation (FHH) gives mild lifelong hypercalcaemia with normal PTH.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-q2`,stem:`What is the first step in treating a dehydrated patient with corrected calcium of 15 mg/dL?`,options:[`IV 0.9% saline to restore volume`,`IV furosemide 40 mg`,`Oral cinacalcet`,`Calcium-free diet`],answer:0,explanation:`Hypercalcaemia causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and volume depletion, so IV isotonic saline is the first step to restore GFR and calcium excretion. Furosemide before volume repletion worsens dehydration and is no longer routine; cinacalcet is for parathyroid disease and works slowly; dietary restriction has no role in an emergency.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with mild hypercalcaemia (10.9 mg/dL) and PTH of 62 pg/mL has a 24-hour urine calcium/creatinine clearance ratio of 0.005. His mother also has 'high calcium'. What is the best management?`,options:[`Urgent parathyroidectomy`,`Zoledronic acid infusion`,`Sestamibi scan to localise an adenoma`,`Reassurance and family counselling`],answer:3,explanation:`A calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01 with mild lifelong hypercalcaemia and an affected parent indicates familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia, a benign CaSR disorder that does not improve with surgery. Parathyroidectomy and localisation scans are for primary hyperparathyroidism, and a bisphosphonate is unnecessary for benign mild hypercalcaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-q4`,stem:`A patient with pulmonary tuberculosis has calcium of 12.6 mg/dL, suppressed PTH, normal 25-OH vitamin D and raised 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D. Which drug most specifically targets the mechanism?`,options:[`Calcitonin`,`Prednisolone`,`Cinacalcet`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`],answer:1,explanation:`Granulomatous hypercalcaemia is driven by macrophage 1-alpha-hydroxylase producing calcitriol, which glucocorticoids suppress, so prednisolone with ATT is the specific therapy. Calcitonin works only briefly; cinacalcet acts on the parathyroid, which is already suppressed; thiazides reduce urinary calcium and would worsen hypercalcaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-q5`,stem:`In hypercalcaemia of malignancy that is refractory to zoledronic acid in a patient with eGFR 25 mL/min, which agent does the Endocrine Society 2023 guideline favour?`,options:[`Repeat zoledronic acid at double dose`,`High-dose furosemide`,`Denosumab`,`Oral phosphate`],answer:2,explanation:`Denosumab is not renally cleared and is effective in bisphosphonate-refractory hypercalcaemia of malignancy, so it is preferred with significant renal impairment, with monitoring for hypocalcaemia. Repeating zoledronic acid at a higher dose risks further renal injury; furosemide is not a treatment for hypercalcaemia itself; oral phosphate can cause metastatic calcification and is not recommended.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Enumerate the causes of hypercalcaemia. Describe the clinical features, the investigative approach and the management of severe hypercalcaemia.`,openingLines:[`Hypercalcaemia is an albumin-corrected serum calcium above about 10.5 mg/dL (ionised over 1.32 mmol/L); severe is over 14 mg/dL.`,`Primary hyperparathyroidism and malignancy cause about 90% of cases, and intact PTH separates PTH-dependent from PTH-independent causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`PTH-dependent: PHPT, tertiary HPT, lithium, FHH, MEN.`,`Malignancy: PTHrP (squamous, renal, breast), osteolytic (myeloma, metastases), calcitriol (lymphoma).`,`Granulomatous: TB, sarcoidosis, fungal; vitamin D or A toxicity; milk-alkali.`,`Others: thiazides, thyrotoxicosis, immobilisation, adrenal insufficiency.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans, thrones (polyuria).`,`Dehydration, band keratopathy, hyporeflexia, short QT, bradycardia.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Confirm: corrected/ionised calcium, creatinine, phosphate, ALP, ECG.`,`Intact PTH: high/normal - urine calcium, Ca/Cr clearance ratio, 25-OH D, neck imaging.`,`Suppressed - PTHrP, 25-OH D, 1,25-(OH)2D, SPEP, free light chains, chest X-ray, CT.`]},{heading:`Management of severe hypercalcaemia`,points:[`IV saline 200-300 mL/hour to urine output 100-150 mL/hour.`,`Calcitonin 4 IU/kg every 12 hours for 48 hours.`,`Zoledronic acid 4 mg IV over 15 minutes, or denosumab 120 mg SC if refractory or renal failure.`,`Steroids for lymphoma, granulomas, vitamin D toxicity; dialysis if over 18 mg/dL or renal failure.`,`Loop diuretic only for fluid overload; monitor calcium 6-12 hourly.`]},{heading:`Treat the cause and follow up`,points:[`PHPT - parathyroidectomy; malignancy - oncology; TB - ATT; stop vitamin D, thiazides, calcium carbonate.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm: hypercalcaemia - PTH high/normal vs suppressed - next tests`,`Table of drugs with dose, onset and duration`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigation algorithm`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`hypercalcaemia`,`PTH`,`PTHrP`,`zoledronic acid`,`denosumab`,`calcitonin`,`saline`]},{id:`endocrine-hypercalcaemia-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the laboratory evaluation of hypercalcaemia.`,openingLines:[`Confirm true hypercalcaemia by correcting for albumin or measuring ionised calcium.`,`Intact PTH is the branch point of the algorithm.`],answer:[{heading:`Confirm`,points:[`Repeat corrected or ionised calcium; check creatinine, phosphate, ALP, ECG.`]},{heading:`PTH high or inappropriately normal`,points:[`24-hour urine calcium and Ca/Cr clearance ratio: under 0.01 FHH, over 0.02 PHPT.`,`Lithium history, CKD (tertiary HPT); localise with ultrasound and sestamibi before surgery.`]},{heading:`PTH suppressed`,points:[`PTHrP high - humoral malignancy.`,`25-OH D over 150 ng/mL - vitamin D toxicity.`,`1,25-(OH)2D high - granuloma (TB, sarcoid) or lymphoma.`,`SPEP, free light chains - myeloma; TSH, vitamin A, cortisol, bicarbonate (milk-alkali).`]}],mustDraw:[`Branching flow chart from PTH`],markSplit:[{part:`Confirmation`,marks:1},{part:`PTH-dependent arm`,marks:2},{part:`PTH-independent arm`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PTH`,`PTHrP`,`FHH`,`calcitriol`,`vitamin D toxicity`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - hypercalcaemia and disorders of the parathyroid`,`Williams Textbook of Endocrinology, 15th edition, 2024`,`El-Hajj Fuleihan G et al. Treatment of hypercalcemia of malignancy in adults: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline, J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2023`,`Bilezikian JP et al. Evaluation and management of primary hyperparathyroidism: summary statement and guidelines from the Fifth International Workshop, J Bone Miner Res 2022`,`Demay MB et al. Vitamin D for the prevention of disease: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline, 2024`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - ECG in electrolyte disorders`,`Indian Primary Hyperparathyroidism Registry (PGIMER Chandigarh) publications`,`KDIGO 2017 Clinical Practice Guideline Update for CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder`]}),d.push({id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections`,title:`Infections in diabetes: mucormycosis, emphysematous infections, malignant otitis externa, TB and melioidosis`,oneLiner:`Diabetes impairs **neutrophil chemotaxis, phagocytosis and killing**, and with **ketoacidosis, neuropathy and vasculopathy** predisposes to infections that are **more frequent, more severe and sometimes almost unique to diabetes** - **rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis, emphysematous pyelonephritis and cholecystitis, malignant (necrotising) otitis externa and melioidosis** - and to a **three-fold higher risk of tuberculosis**; management is **source control plus the right antimicrobial plus tight glucose and ketoacidosis control**, and prevention is **vaccination and bidirectional TB-DM screening**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis`,`ROCM`,`COVID-associated mucormycosis`,`black fungus`,`liposomal amphotericin B`,`isavuconazole`,`posaconazole`,`emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Huang-Tseng classification`,`emphysematous cholecystitis`,`emphysematous cystitis`,`malignant otitis externa`,`necrotising otitis externa`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`TB-DM`,`bidirectional screening`,`melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`Klebsiella liver abscess`,`Fournier gangrene`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`People with diabetes have about **1.5-2 times the overall infection risk** and higher infection-related hospitalisation and death, rising with HbA1c. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mucormycosis**: India has the world's highest burden, estimated at about **14 cases per 100,000** - roughly **70 times the global rate** - and **diabetes is the main risk factor in about 70-80%** of Indian cases. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`During the 2021 delta wave India reported more than **45,000 cases of COVID-associated mucormycosis (CAM)**, driven by **uncontrolled diabetes plus inappropriate steroids**. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Tuberculosis**: diabetes increases TB risk about **three-fold**; in India about **15-20% of people with TB have diabetes** - India carries the largest TB-DM burden worldwide. [NTEP]`,`**Melioidosis**: endemic in India (coastal **Karnataka, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry**, Assam); diabetes is present in **about 50-80%** of cases; India is modelled to have the largest number of cases globally. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: about **90-95% of patients have diabetes**, mostly women with poor control. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: over **90% occur in elderly people with diabetes**, nearly always due to **Pseudomonas aeruginosa**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Glycaemic control**: risk rises steeply with HbA1c over 8-9%; **DKA** is the key factor for mucormycosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Glucocorticoids** (especially high doses or long courses), **iron overload and deferoxamine**, neutropenia and transplants add mucormycosis risk. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Obstruction** (stones, papillary necrosis, neurogenic bladder) and **gas-forming organisms** (E. coli, Klebsiella) drive emphysematous pyelonephritis. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Microangiopathy of the ear canal** and water irrigation of the ear (wax removal) precede malignant otitis externa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Soil and water exposure** in the monsoon - rice farmers, construction workers - is the risk for melioidosis; also alcohol, CKD and thalassaemia. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Smoking, undernutrition, alcohol** and crowding compound TB risk in diabetes. [NTEP]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** cause genital mycotic infections and very rarely **Fournier gangrene**. [FDA 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why diabetes invites these infections`,points:[`Hyperglycaemia impairs **neutrophil chemotaxis, adherence, phagocytosis and oxidative burst**, and glycation reduces complement and antibody function. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mucorales and acidosis**: low pH releases **free iron from transferrin**; Mucorales take up iron avidly, and high glucose and ketones up-regulate endothelial **GRP78**, which binds fungal **CotH** spores - enabling **angioinvasion, thrombosis and black necrosis**. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`Mucorales also have a **ketone reductase** that lets them thrive in ketoacidosis, which is why DKA patients are hit hardest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gas formation** in tissue comes from **mixed-acid fermentation of high tissue glucose** by E. coli and Klebsiella in poorly perfused tissue. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Neuropathy** causes a neurogenic bladder (urinary stasis) and painless foot injury; **vasculopathy** limits antibiotic delivery and healing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB**: diabetes impairs macrophage function and T-cell (Th1, IFN-gamma) responses, giving higher bacillary load, more **lower-lobe and cavitary disease**, slower culture conversion and more relapse. [NTEP]`,`**B. pseudomallei** survives inside phagocytes; impaired neutrophil function in diabetes allows dissemination to lung, liver, spleen, prostate and bone. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Mucormycosis forms**: **rhino-orbito-cerebral (commonest in diabetes)**, pulmonary, cutaneous, gastrointestinal, disseminated. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**ROCM staging (Honavar 2021)**: stage 1 - nasal mucosa; stage 2 - paranasal sinuses; stage 3 - orbit (3a nasolacrimal/medial, 3b diffuse, 3c with vision loss, 3d bilateral); stage 4 - CNS (4a focal, 4b diffuse, 4c-4d with cavernous sinus or vascular involvement). [Honavar, IJO 2021]`,`**Huang-Tseng classification of emphysematous pyelonephritis**: class 1 - gas in collecting system only; class 2 - gas in renal parenchyma; **3A - extension to perinephric space**; **3B - to pararenal space**; **class 4 - bilateral or solitary kidney**. [Huang-Tseng 2000]`,`**Malignant otitis externa grading**: stage I - soft tissue and cartilage; stage II - temporal bone osteomyelitis or cranial nerve palsy; stage III - intracranial spread or multiple nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: acute (most), subacute or chronic (over 2 months, mimics TB); localised or **bacteraemic** (with or without septic shock). [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Diabetic foot infection** is graded by **IWGDF/IDSA**: uninfected, mild, moderate, severe, with **'O' for osteomyelitis** - see the Diabetic foot topic. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**ROCM early warning signs**: unilateral **facial pain or numbness**, nasal block, **blackish or bloody nasal discharge**, toothache, loosening of teeth, periorbital swelling, headache - often within weeks of DKA or steroid therapy. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`Late ROCM: **proptosis, ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, diplopia, sudden vision loss**, altered sensorium and contralateral hemiparesis (carotid thrombosis). [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: fever, flank pain, vomiting, sepsis and confusion, often with **AKI and severe hyperglycaemia**; pneumaturia is uncommon. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Emphysematous cholecystitis**: like acute cholecystitis but in an older diabetic man, often **acalculous**, with rapid toxicity and a high risk of **gangrene and perforation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: **severe ear pain worse at night**, out of proportion to the findings, with purulent discharge, failure of topical drops for weeks, then **facial palsy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB in diabetes**: may be atypical - **lower-zone shadows**, fewer symptoms, or worsening glucose control as the only clue. [NTEP]`,`**Melioidosis**: fever for weeks with pneumonia, **multiple liver and spleen abscesses**, prostate abscess, septic arthritis or parotid abscess, in a diabetic farmer after the monsoon. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Black eschar** on the nasal turbinate, palate or face is the hallmark of mucormycosis - but it is a **late** sign; do nasal endoscopy early. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`Orbital apex or cavernous sinus syndrome: **proptosis, chemosis, ophthalmoplegia (III, IV, VI), corneal anaesthesia (V1)** and loss of vision. [Harrison 22e]`,`Malignant otitis externa: **granulation tissue on the floor of the ear canal at the bony-cartilaginous junction**, tender periauricular swelling, and palsy of **VII** (commonest), then IX, X, XI (jugular foramen) and XII. [Harrison 22e]`,`Emphysematous infections: sepsis, flank or right upper quadrant tenderness; **crepitus** is rare. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`Melioidosis: hepatosplenomegaly, abscesses, pneumonia signs, septic shock; **genitourinary examination** for prostatic abscess in men. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Always examine **both feet**, perineum (Fournier gangrene), skin folds (candida, erythrasma), teeth and gums in an unwell diabetic. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Mucormycosis vs invasive aspergillosis**: Mucorales have **broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae with right-angle branching**; Aspergillus has **narrow, septate hyphae with acute-angle (45 degree) branching** and responds to voriconazole (which is **inactive against Mucorales**). [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Mucormycosis vs bacterial orbital cellulitis or cavernous sinus thrombosis**: black eschar, necrosis and a non-response to antibiotics point to fungus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis vs acute pyelonephritis**: gas on imaging and severe sepsis; **CT is the only reliable way** to tell them apart. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Malignant vs simple otitis externa**: severe night pain, granulation tissue, raised ESR or CRP, cranial nerve palsy and failure of topical therapy; exclude **squamous cell carcinoma** of the ear canal by biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis vs TB**: both cause cavitating upper-lobe pneumonia and chronic abscesses; melioidosis grows in ordinary culture and is **resistant to gentamicin and colistin**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**Melioidosis liver abscess vs amoebic or Klebsiella abscess**: multiple small 'honeycomb' abscesses in liver and spleen favour melioidosis; hypervirulent **Klebsiella** gives a single liver abscess with metastatic endophthalmitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Every diabetic with a serious infection**: CBG, venous gas, **ketones**, electrolytes, creatinine, CBC, CRP, blood cultures before antibiotics, HbA1c, urine culture. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Mucormycosis**: urgent **nasal endoscopy with deep scrapings** for **KOH and calcofluor-white mount** (broad aseptate hyphae), **histopathology** (tissue invasion) and culture; **contrast MRI** of paranasal sinuses, orbit and brain (**'black turbinate' sign**) and CT for bone. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: **non-contrast CT abdomen** is the test of choice for gas and extent; ultrasound may show shadowing echoes; X-ray KUB may show gas. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: ear swab culture and sensitivity, **ESR and CRP** (markers for follow-up), **CT temporal bone** (bone erosion), **MRI** (soft tissue and intracranial spread), **technetium-99m bone scan** (sensitive early), gallium or WBC SPECT for response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB-DM**: sputum **CBNAAT/Truenat** for MTB and rifampicin resistance, chest X-ray; every TB patient gets **random blood glucose**, with RBS **200 mg/dL or more** confirmed by FBS or HbA1c. [NTEP]`,`**Melioidosis**: culture of blood, pus, sputum and urine on **Ashdown's medium**; Gram stain shows **'safety-pin' bipolar Gram-negative bacilli**; oxidase positive; ultrasound or CT abdomen for liver, spleen and **prostatic abscesses**. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Tell the laboratory when melioidosis is suspected - **B. pseudomallei is a laboratory biohazard** and is often misidentified as Pseudomonas by automated systems. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Screen every diabetic for TB symptoms** (cough 2 weeks or more, fever, weight loss, night sweats) at each visit, and refer presumptive TB for CBNAAT. [NTEP]`,`**TB-DM**: standard **NTEP regimen 2HRZE/4HRE** (weight-band fixed-dose combinations) with **pyridoxine** to prevent isoniazid neuropathy; check glucose monthly. [NTEP]`,`**Rifampicin lowers sulfonylurea, DPP-4 inhibitor (linagliptin), pioglitazone and repaglinide levels**; **metformin and insulin** are preferred during ATT, and insulin if glucose is very high. [NTEP]`,`**Do not screen for or treat asymptomatic bacteriuria** in non-pregnant diabetic adults; treat symptomatic cystitis per culture (for example **nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days**). [IDSA 2019]`,`**Early ear pain in an elderly diabetic** that fails topical drops in 1-2 weeks needs ESR/CRP and ENT referral - do not keep changing drops. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Teach foot care**, daily inspection and early reporting of any wound; mild foot infection can be treated with oral antibiotics (amoxicillin-clavulanate) for **1-2 weeks**. [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`Review **steroid prescriptions**: use them only when indicated (for example COVID with hypoxia) and monitor glucose while on them. [ICMR advisory 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Mucormycosis - four pillars**: (1) **reverse risk** - correct DKA, control glucose with insulin, stop or taper steroids and deferoxamine; (2) **liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day IV** (**10 mg/kg/day with CNS involvement**); (3) **urgent, repeated surgical debridement** (FESS, maxillectomy, orbital exenteration if needed); (4) step-down. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`If lipid formulations are unavailable, **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1-1.5 mg/kg/day** with saline loading, potassium and magnesium replacement and daily creatinine. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`**Step-down or salvage**: **isavuconazole 200 mg every 8 hours for 6 doses, then 200 mg daily** or **posaconazole delayed-release 300 mg twice on day 1, then 300 mg daily**, for a total course often of **3-6 months** until imaging and clinical resolution. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis**: resuscitate, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics (for example **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g every 6-8 hours** or **meropenem 1 g every 8 hours** in ESBL settings), insulin, and **relieve obstruction (DJ stent or PCN)**; class 1-2 and class 3-4 with fewer than 2 risk factors do well with **percutaneous drainage plus antibiotics**; **nephrectomy** for class 3-4 with **2 or more risk factors** (thrombocytopenia, AKI, altered sensorium, shock) or failed drainage. [Huang-Tseng 2000]`,`**Emphysematous cholecystitis**: IV antibiotics covering anaerobes and Clostridium (for example piperacillin-tazobactam) and **urgent cholecystectomy**, or **percutaneous cholecystostomy** if unfit. [Tokyo Guidelines 2018]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: **anti-pseudomonal therapy for at least 6-8 weeks** - **IV ceftazidime 2 g every 8 hours** or piperacillin-tazobactam (or cefepime), then oral **ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily**; local aural toilet; surgery only for biopsy or debridement of sequestra; antifungal (voriconazole) if Aspergillus is grown. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: intensive phase **IV ceftazidime 40 mg/kg (up to 2 g) every 8 hours** or **meropenem 25 mg/kg (up to 1 g; 2 g for neuromelioidosis) every 8 hours** for **at least 2 weeks** (4-8 weeks for deep abscess, bone or brain), then **eradication with co-trimoxazole** (320/1600 mg twice daily if over 60 kg) for **at least 3 months** with folic acid. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`In all severe infections use **IV insulin infusion** to keep glucose about **140-180 mg/dL**, and drain abscesses - see the Hospital hyperglycaemia and DKA topics. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: amphotericin B is the antifungal of choice; avoid azoles where possible; fluoroquinolones are avoided; TB treatment is standard first-line ATT. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**CKD**: prefer **liposomal amphotericin**; avoid **IV isavuconazole/posaconazole cyclodextrin vehicles** in severe CKD (oral forms are fine); adjust ceftazidime, meropenem and co-trimoxazole doses. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Elderly**: malignant otitis externa and emphysematous cholecystitis affect older people; atypical, afebrile presentations are common. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: melioidosis may present as **parotid abscess** (common in Southeast Asia); type 1 diabetes in DKA can develop mucormycosis. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors**: stop during severe infection, vomiting or sepsis (euglycaemic DKA risk); warn about **perineal pain, swelling and fever** (Fournier gangrene). [FDA 2018]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: start conventional amphotericin with saline loading, refer early for debridement; for melioidosis, ceftazidime and co-trimoxazole are cheap and on the NLEM. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Mucormycosis mortality about 40-50%** overall and **over 80% with cerebral or disseminated disease**; survivors often lose an eye, palate or maxilla and need prosthetic rehabilitation. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`**Amphotericin toxicity**: nephrotoxicity, **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia**, infusion reactions, anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Emphysematous pyelonephritis mortality** has fallen to about **10-25%** with drainage-first strategies; higher with class 4 and shock. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`,`**Malignant otitis externa**: mortality about **10-20%**, higher with multiple cranial nerve palsies; recurrence up to 1 year after therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB-DM** has about **twice the risk of death**, more treatment failure and relapse, and possibly more drug resistance than TB alone. [NTEP]`,`**Melioidosis** mortality is about **10-40%** (higher with septic shock), and relapse is common if eradication therapy is short or incomplete. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Same-day referral to **ENT, ophthalmology and neurosurgery** for any suspected ROCM - every hour of delay costs tissue. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`On amphotericin: daily **creatinine, potassium and magnesium**, twice-weekly CBC; on azoles: **LFT, QT interval** and posaconazole trough levels where available. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`Malignant otitis externa: follow **ESR/CRP** and imaging; continue therapy until symptoms and markers settle; review for relapse for 1 year. [Harrison 22e]`,`TB-DM: **HbA1c at start and end of treatment**, monthly glucose, sputum at end of intensive phase and end of treatment; continue diabetes care after ATT. [NTEP]`,`Melioidosis: check **adherence to co-trimoxazole** (the main reason for relapse), CBC and potassium; follow up for at least a year. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Repeat imaging after **emphysematous pyelonephritis** drainage, and treat underlying stones or obstruction. [Campbell-Walsh Urology 12e]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and vaccines`,points:[`**Glucose control and prompt treatment of DKA** are the single most important preventive measures for mucormycosis and other infections. [ICMR advisory 2021]`,`**Vaccines in diabetes**: **influenza yearly**; **pneumococcal - PCV20 or PCV21 alone, or PCV15 then PPSV23**; **hepatitis B** (19-59 years, consider at 60 and over); **recombinant zoster vaccine 2 doses at 50 years and over**; Td/Tdap; **RSV once at 75 and over or 50-74 at increased risk**; COVID-19 per current advice. [ADA 2026]`,`**Bidirectional TB-DM screening** under the **National Framework for Joint TB-Diabetes Collaborative Activities**: every TB patient tested for diabetes, every diabetic screened for TB symptoms at NCD clinics and Ayushman Arogya Mandir. [NTEP]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** gives nutritional support (Rs 1000/month from 2024) to TB patients - including TB-DM. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Melioidosis prevention**: wear **boots and gloves** in paddy fields and wet soil, drink boiled water, and avoid storm and flood exposure if diabetic. [Darwin guideline 2020]`,`Counsel on **foot care, oral hygiene, safe ear care** (no cotton buds or irrigation in elderly diabetics) and **judicious steroids**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2026** vaccine schedule for diabetes: PCV20/PCV21 single dose, **RSV for 75 and over and 50-74 at increased risk**, and recombinant zoster from 50 years. [ADA 2026]`,`**IWGDF/IDSA 2023 diabetic foot infection guideline**: IWGDF/IDSA classification with 'O' for osteomyelitis, **probe-to-bone plus X-ray**, and **antibiotic durations of 1-2 weeks for soft tissue and about 6 weeks for osteomyelitis** (3 weeks after minor amputation). [IWGDF/IDSA 2023]`,`**NTEP** continues universal diabetes screening of TB patients and raised **Ni-kshay Poshan** support in 2024; WHO consolidated TB guidance on comorbidities (2024) supports integrated TB-diabetes care. [NTEP 2024]`,`The **ECMM/MSG ERC global mucormycosis guideline (2019)** and the **ICMR 2021 advisories** remain the reference; isavuconazole and posaconazole are now widely available in India as step-down agents. [ECMM/MSG ERC 2019]`,`The **Darwin melioidosis guideline** (2020 revision) remains the treatment standard: ceftazidime or meropenem intensive phase, then 3 months of co-trimoxazole. [Darwin guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Why is mucormycosis common in DKA?'** - acidosis frees iron, GRP78 up-regulation, ketone reductase, impaired neutrophils.`,`Microscopy differentiation: **Mucor - broad, aseptate, right-angle branching; Aspergillus - narrow, septate, acute-angle branching**.`,`**Voriconazole does not cover Mucorales** - a favourite trap.`,`Malignant otitis externa: **elderly diabetic + Pseudomonas + night pain + facial palsy**; granulation at the **bony-cartilaginous junction**.`,`Emphysematous pyelonephritis: **CT is diagnostic**; remember **Huang-Tseng** classes and the 'drainage first' approach.`,`Melioidosis: **'safety-pin' Gram-negative bacilli resistant to gentamicin and colistin** in a diabetic farmer.`,`Common mistake: treating asymptomatic bacteriuria in diabetics, or giving **sulfonylureas with rifampicin** and wondering why glucose rises.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - diabetes-associated infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`5 mg/kg/day IV (10 mg/kg/day for CNS)`,`Until stable/improving, usually 3-6 weeks, then step-down`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1-1.5 mg/kg/day IV over 4-6 hours with saline loading`,`If lipid form unavailable`,`Marked nephrotoxicity, rigors, hypokalaemia`],[`Isavuconazole`,`200 mg IV/oral every 8 hours x 6 doses, then 200 mg daily`,`Step-down to complete 3-6 months`,`LFT rise, QT shortening; fewer interactions than posaconazole`],[`Posaconazole DR tablet`,`300 mg twice on day 1, then 300 mg daily oral`,`Step-down; check trough over 1 mg/L`,`QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Ceftazidime`,`2 g IV every 8 hours (40 mg/kg for melioidosis)`,`MOE 6-8 weeks; melioidosis at least 2 weeks`,`Diarrhoea, C. difficile; adjust in CKD`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV every 8 hours (2 g for neuromelioidosis)`,`Severe melioidosis, ESBL emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Seizures (high dose), C. difficile`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`750 mg oral twice daily`,`Early MOE or step-down; total 6-8 weeks`,`Tendinopathy, QT, dysglycaemia; resistance rising`],[`Co-trimoxazole (melioidosis eradication)`,`320/1600 mg oral twice daily (over 60 kg); 240/1200 mg (40-60 kg)`,`At least 3 months (6 months for bone or CNS)`,`Hyperkalaemia, rash, marrow suppression; add folic acid`]]},{heading:`Infections almost unique to diabetes`,columns:[`Infection`,`Organism`,`Key clue`,`Key test`,`Treatment core`],rows:[[`Rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis`,`Rhizopus (Mucorales)`,`DKA or steroids, black eschar, facial pain, proptosis`,`KOH/calcofluor mount, MRI`,`Liposomal amphotericin plus debridement`],[`Emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`E. coli, Klebsiella`,`Diabetic woman, sepsis, flank pain`,`Non-contrast CT`,`Antibiotics plus drainage; nephrectomy if severe`],[`Emphysematous cholecystitis`,`Clostridium, E. coli`,`Older diabetic man, acalculous, toxic`,`CT / ultrasound gas in wall`,`Urgent cholecystectomy or cholecystostomy`],[`Malignant otitis externa`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Elderly, night pain, granulation, VII palsy`,`CT temporal bone, ESR, bone scan`,`Anti-pseudomonal 6-8 weeks`],[`Melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`Farmer, monsoon, abscesses, pneumonia`,`Culture on Ashdown medium`,`Ceftazidime/meropenem then co-trimoxazole`],[`Fournier gangrene`,`Polymicrobial`,`Perineal pain, SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Clinical, CT`,`Urgent debridement plus broad antibiotics`]]},{heading:`Huang-Tseng classification of emphysematous pyelonephritis`,columns:[`Class`,`CT finding`,`Management`],rows:[[`1`,`Gas in collecting system only (emphysematous pyelitis)`,`Antibiotics, relieve obstruction`],[`2`,`Gas in renal parenchyma, no extension`,`Antibiotics plus percutaneous drainage`],[`3A`,`Extension to perinephric space`,`PCD if under 2 risk factors; nephrectomy if 2 or more`],[`3B`,`Extension to pararenal space`,`As 3A`],[`4`,`Bilateral or solitary kidney`,`Bilateral drainage; nephrectomy rarely`]]},{heading:`Mucormycosis vs aspergillosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mucormycosis`,`Aspergillosis`],rows:[[`Main host`,`DKA, uncontrolled diabetes, steroids, iron overload`,`Neutropenia, transplant, steroids`],[`Hyphae`,`Broad (6-15 microm), ribbon-like, pauci-septate`,`Narrow (3-6 microm), septate`],[`Branching`,`Right angle (90 degrees), irregular`,`Acute angle (45 degrees), dichotomous`],[`Drug of choice`,`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`Voriconazole`],[`Voriconazole activity`,`None`,`Yes`]]},{heading:`Vaccines for adults with diabetes`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Who`,`Schedule`],rows:[[`Influenza`,`All with diabetes from 6 months`,`Every year`],[`Pneumococcal`,`19-64 with diabetes; all at 65 and over`,`PCV20 or PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23`],[`Hepatitis B`,`19-59 years; consider 60 and over`,`2-dose (Heplisav-B) or 3-dose series`],[`Recombinant zoster`,`50 years and over`,`2 doses, 2-6 months apart`],[`RSV`,`75 and over; 50-74 at increased risk`,`Single dose`],[`Td/Tdap`,`All adults`,`Tdap once, then Td every 10 years`]]}],redFlags:[`Facial pain or numbness, black nasal discharge or palatal eschar in a diabetic - suspected mucormycosis`,`Proptosis, ophthalmoplegia or sudden visual loss with sinusitis`,`Fever, flank pain and sepsis with gas on imaging - emphysematous pyelonephritis`,`Severe ear pain at night with granulation tissue or a facial nerve palsy in an elderly diabetic`,`Septic shock, multiple liver or spleen abscesses in a diabetic farmer - melioidosis`,`Perineal or scrotal pain, swelling and fever - Fournier gangrene`,`DKA with any of the above, or altered sensorium`,`Foot infection with spreading cellulitis, crepitus or systemic toxicity`],pearls:[`Mucormycosis: **acid frees iron, GRP78 lets the fungus in** - DKA is the soil.`,`**Mucor broad and 90 degrees; Aspergillus narrow and 45 degrees**.`,`**Voriconazole misses Mucor**; liposomal amphotericin 5 mg/kg (10 for brain).`,`**Four pillars** of mucormycosis: reverse risk, amphotericin, debridement, step-down.`,`Emphysematous pyelonephritis: **CT diagnoses, drainage first, nephrectomy if 2+ risk factors**.`,`Malignant otitis externa: **old, diabetic, Pseudomonas, night pain, VII palsy**.`,`Melioidosis: **safety-pin GNB, gentamicin-resistant**, ceftazidime then 3 months co-trimoxazole.`,`**Rifampicin speeds clearance of sulfonylureas and gliptins** - use metformin and insulin in TB-DM.`,`Don't treat **asymptomatic bacteriuria** in non-pregnant diabetics.`],mcqs:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man recovering from DKA develops left facial pain, black nasal discharge and periorbital swelling. Nasal scraping shows broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae branching at right angles. What is the drug of choice?`,options:[`Voriconazole`,`Fluconazole`,`Caspofungin`,`Liposomal amphotericin B`],answer:3,explanation:`Broad, pauci-septate, right-angle-branching hyphae in a patient after DKA indicate mucormycosis, for which liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day (10 with CNS disease) plus surgical debridement is first-line. Voriconazole treats aspergillosis but has no activity against Mucorales; fluconazole and caspofungin are also inactive against them.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-q2`,stem:`A 70-year-old diabetic man has 6 weeks of severe right ear pain worse at night, purulent discharge, granulation tissue on the floor of the ear canal and a new right facial palsy. Which organism is most likely?`,options:[`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Aspergillus niger`,`Streptococcus pneumoniae`],answer:1,explanation:`This is malignant (necrotising) otitis externa, a skull-base osteomyelitis of elderly diabetics caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa in over 90% of cases; the facial nerve is the commonest cranial nerve involved. Staphylococcus aureus causes ordinary otitis externa and furunculosis; Aspergillus is an uncommon cause in non-responders; pneumococcus causes otitis media rather than this picture.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-q3`,stem:`A diabetic woman with sepsis has CT showing gas in the renal parenchyma extending into the perinephric space. She has AKI and thrombocytopenia. Under the Huang-Tseng approach, what is the best management?`,options:[`Oral ciprofloxacin and review in 48 hours`,`IV antibiotics alone without drainage`,`IV antibiotics with nephrectomy if she does not respond rapidly to drainage`,`Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy`],answer:2,explanation:`This is class 3A emphysematous pyelonephritis with two risk factors (AKI and thrombocytopenia), a group in which percutaneous drainage alone often fails, so resuscitation, IV antibiotics and early nephrectomy if drainage fails is advised. Oral antibiotics or IV antibiotics alone are inadequate for gas-forming necrotising infection, and lithotripsy has no role in acute sepsis.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-q4`,stem:`A paddy farmer from coastal Karnataka with diabetes has fever, pneumonia and multiple small liver and spleen abscesses after the monsoon. Blood culture grows an oxidase-positive Gram-negative bacillus with bipolar 'safety-pin' staining, resistant to gentamicin. What is the correct treatment?`,options:[`IV ceftazidime for at least 2 weeks followed by oral co-trimoxazole for at least 3 months`,`IV gentamicin for 7 days`,`Oral doxycycline for 7 days`,`Anti-tubercular therapy for 6 months`],answer:0,explanation:`The picture is melioidosis due to Burkholderia pseudomallei, which needs an intensive phase of IV ceftazidime or meropenem for at least 2 weeks (longer for deep abscesses) and then eradication with co-trimoxazole for at least 3 months to prevent relapse. The organism is intrinsically resistant to gentamicin; a short doxycycline course invites relapse; ATT is wrong because this is not TB despite the similar presentation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-q5`,stem:`A man with type 2 diabetes on glimepiride and linagliptin starts 2HRZE for pulmonary TB. His glucose rises sharply over 2 weeks. What is the best explanation and action?`,options:[`Isoniazid causes insulin resistance; stop isoniazid`,`Pyrazinamide causes pancreatitis; stop pyrazinamide`,`Ethambutol causes hyperglycaemia; switch to streptomycin`,`Rifampicin induces CYP enzymes lowering glimepiride and linagliptin levels; switch to insulin with metformin`],answer:3,explanation:`Rifampicin is a potent CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 inducer that reduces levels of sulfonylureas and linagliptin, so glucose rises; NTEP advises metformin and insulin during ATT. Isoniazid, pyrazinamide and ethambutol do not explain the rise, and stopping a first-line TB drug is not justified.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the predisposing factors, clinical features, diagnosis and management of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis in diabetes. Add a note on lessons from COVID-associated mucormycosis.`,openingLines:[`Mucormycosis is an angioinvasive fungal infection by Mucorales (mainly Rhizopus) that causes tissue necrosis; the rhino-orbito-cerebral form is typical of uncontrolled diabetes and DKA.`,`India has about 70 times the global burden, and diabetes is the risk factor in about 70-80% of Indian cases.`],answer:[{heading:`Predisposing factors and pathogenesis`,points:[`Uncontrolled diabetes, DKA, steroids, iron overload and deferoxamine, neutropenia, transplant.`,`Acidosis frees iron; GRP78-CotH binding; ketone reductase; impaired neutrophils - angioinvasion, thrombosis, necrosis.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and staging`,points:[`Facial pain, numbness, nasal block, black discharge, toothache; eschar on turbinate or palate.`,`Orbit: proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, vision loss; CNS: altered sensorium, hemiparesis.`,`Honavar staging 1-4 (nose, sinus, orbit, brain).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Nasal endoscopy, KOH and calcofluor mount: broad aseptate hyphae with right-angle branching.`,`Histopathology, culture; contrast MRI (black turbinate) and CT.`,`Glucose, ketones, gas, electrolytes, creatinine.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Correct DKA and glucose with insulin; stop steroids.`,`Liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day (10 mg/kg/day CNS); conventional 1-1.5 mg/kg/day if unavailable.`,`Urgent repeated debridement: FESS, maxillectomy, exenteration.`,`Step-down isavuconazole or posaconazole for 3-6 months; monitor potassium, magnesium, creatinine.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Mortality 40-50%, over 80% with cerebral disease; disfigurement needs rehabilitation.`]},{heading:`Lessons from COVID-associated mucormycosis`,points:[`Steroids only for hypoxic COVID, at correct dose and duration; monitor glucose.`,`Early warning signs taught to patients; ENT-eye-neuro teams; ICMR 2021 advisory.`]}],mustDraw:[`Four-pillar management diagram`,`Table: Mucor vs Aspergillus hyphae`],markSplit:[{part:`Predisposing factors and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and staging`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:3},{part:`COVID lessons`,marks:1}],keywords:[`mucormycosis`,`DKA`,`liposomal amphotericin`,`debridement`,`isavuconazole`,`CAM`]},{id:`endocrine-diabetes-infections-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on malignant otitis externa.`,openingLines:[`Malignant (necrotising) otitis externa is an invasive Pseudomonas infection of the external ear canal that spreads to the temporal bone and skull base.`,`Over 90% occur in elderly people with diabetes.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Severe night-time otalgia, discharge, granulation at the bony-cartilaginous junction.`,`Cranial nerve palsy: VII first, then IX-XII.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Swab culture, ESR/CRP, CT temporal bone, MRI, Tc-99m bone scan; biopsy to exclude carcinoma.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`IV ceftazidime 2 g every 8 hours or piperacillin-tazobactam, then ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily; 6-8 weeks total.`,`Glucose control, aural toilet, surgery only for debridement; antifungal if Aspergillus.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Mortality 10-20%; follow ESR/CRP and imaging; relapse possible up to 1 year.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the ear canal showing the bony-cartilaginous junction and cranial nerve spread`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`malignant otitis externa`,`Pseudomonas`,`facial palsy`,`ceftazidime`,`skull-base osteomyelitis`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - infections in diabetes, mucormycosis, otitis externa`,`Cornely OA et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of mucormycosis (ECMM/MSG ERC), Lancet Infect Dis 2019`,`ICMR/MoHFW evidence-based advisory on screening, diagnosis and management of mucormycosis (COVID-associated), 2021`,`Honavar SG. Code Mucor: guidelines for the diagnosis, staging and management of rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis, Indian J Ophthalmol 2021`,`Huang JJ, Tseng CC. Emphysematous pyelonephritis: clinicoradiological classification, management, prognosis and pathogenesis, Arch Intern Med 2000`,`Darwin Melioidosis Treatment Guideline, 2020 revision (Menzies School of Health Research)`,`National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP): National Framework for Joint TB-Diabetes Collaborative Activities and NTEP technical guidance, 2017-2024`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026; IWGDF/IDSA Guideline on the diagnosis and treatment of diabetes-related foot infections, 2023`]});var f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as default};